/* Machine translations of Kant, Schriften 1747–1756 (AA I), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['aa01/guyer'] = {"lebendige-kraefte-0001":["Thoughts\non the true estimation\nof\nliving forces\nand\njudgment of the proofs\nwhich\nHerr von Leibnitz and other mechanists\nhave made use of\nin this dispute,\ntogether with some preliminary considerations\nwhich\nconcern the force of bodies\nin general,\nby\nImmanuel Kant.\nKönigsberg,\nprinted by Martin Eberhard Dorn. 1746.",["- Kraft = force (glossary); *lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\". Beurtheilung = judgment (glossary: Beurteilung); Beweise = proofs; Betrachtungen = considerations.","- Schätzung is not in the glossary; rendered \"estimation\". Streitsache rendered \"dispute\". Mechaniker rendered \"mechanists\".","- \"Leibnitz\" retained in the original spelling."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0002":["To the\nHighly Well-born, Highly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr,\nHerr\nJohann Christoph Bohlius,\nDoctor of Medicine and second ordinary Professor at the Academy\nat Königsberg,\nas also\nRoyal Personal Physician,\nmy especially Highly Honored Patron.\nHighly Well-born Herr,\nHighly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr Doctor,\nEspecially Highly Honored Patron!",["- Titles are rendered literally; \"Hochedelgebohrner\" = \"Highly Well-born\", \"Gönner\" = \"Patron\". \"Leibmedico\" (Latinate form) = \"Personal Physician\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0003":["To whom can I turn better than to Your Highly Well-born, in order to draw every advantage from so poor a thing as the present writing is? After the particular mark of kindness that You have shown me, I venture to hope that this liberty will also be taken up by Your Highly Well-born as a proof of my gratitude. The constitution of this little work has nothing in it on which I could build any confidence with regard to this; for the honor of adorning one's treatise with Your name is not that from which one could make Your Highly Well-born a gift. A multitude of imperfect thoughts, which perhaps are incorrect in themselves, or at any rate lose all value through the lowliness of their author, which finally convince me sufficiently that they are not worthy of being dedicated to You, that is all that I have in my power to present to Your Highly Well-born. Nevertheless, by means of the perfect concept that I have formed of Your kindness, I make for myself the hope that it will render me the service that I esteem most highly, namely to give Your Highly Well-born to cognize my gratitude toward You. Hereafter I shall have more than one occasion to remind myself of the obligation by which I am bound to You; but the present one will be one of the best, with which I publicly confess that I remain with perpetual high esteem",["- Merkmahl = mark (nota in glossary); Beweisthum = proof (Beweis); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Begriff = concept; Erkenntlichkeit = gratitude (cf. Dankbarkeit, also \"gratitude\"; two German words, one English word).","- \"Ew. Hochedelgebohrnen ... zu erkennen zu geben\" rendered \"to give ... to cognize\"; the German construction (erkennen zu geben) is idiomatic \"to make known\", and the literal rendering preserves the verb erkennen.","- \"mit eine von den besten\" (sic, for \"eine\"); \"diesem ungeachtet\" = \"nevertheless\" (lit. \"this notwithstanding\"). \"im͂erwährender\" read as \"immerwährender\".","- The passage breaks off without a main verb being completed; the sentence continues in the following text (the closing formula)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0004":["Highly Well-born Herr,\nHighly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr Doctor,\nEspecially Highly Honored Patron,\nYour Highly Well-born's\nKönigsberg, the 22nd of April, 1747.\nmost obligated servant\nImmanuel Kant.",["- The date reads 1747 though the title page gives 1746; reproduced as it stands."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0005":["Preface.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0006":["*Nihil magis praestandum est, quam ne pecorum ritu sequamur antece dentium gregem, pergentes, non qua eundum est, sed qua itur.*\n\n(Nothing is more to be guarded against than that we follow, after the manner of cattle, the flock of those going before, proceeding not where one ought to go, but where one goes.)",["- Latin retained; \"antece dentium\" is split in the source and read as \"antecedentium\". The parenthetical English is a gloss of the Latin, not in the passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0007":["Seneca, *On the Happy Life*, Chap. I.",["- \"de vita beata\" given in English as a title; the Latin is *de vita beata*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0008":["I.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0009":["I believe I have cause to form of the judgment of the world, to which I hand over these pages, so good an opinion that the liberty which I take in contradicting great men will not be construed as a crime against me. There was a time when one had much to fear in such an undertaking, but I imagine this time is now past, and that human understanding has already happily shaken off the fetters that ignorance and admiration formerly laid upon it. Now one can boldly venture to count the authority of the Newtons and Leibnitzes as nothing, if it should set itself against the discovery of truth, and to obey no other persuasions than the pull of the understanding.",["- Urtheil = judgment; Verstand = understanding; Ueberredungen = persuasions (Überredung).","- \"vor kein Verbrechen\" = \"as a crime\" (vor for für); \"dem Zuge des Verstandes\" rendered \"the pull of the understanding\" (Zug could also be \"drift\" or \"bent\").","- \"die Newtons und Leibnitze\": plural of proper names kept as \"the Newtons and Leibnitzes\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0010":["II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0011":["If I undertake to reject the thoughts of a Herr von Leibnitz, Wolf, Herrmann, Bernoulli, Bülfinger and others, and to grant precedence to my own; then I would also not gladly have worse judges than these same men, for I know that their judgment, if it should reject my opinions, would nonetheless not condemn the intention of them. One can give these men no more excellent praise than that one may censure all opinions, without excepting their own, before them without fear. A moderation of this kind was, though on another occasion, very praiseworthy in a great man of antiquity. Timoleon was, notwithstanding the merits that he had toward the freedom of Syracuse, once summoned before court. The judges became indignant at the presumption of his accusers. But Timoleon considered this incident quite differently. Such an undertaking could not displease a man who placed his whole pleasure in seeing his fatherland in the most perfect freedom. He protected those who made use of their freedom even against himself. The whole of antiquity has accompanied this conduct with praises.",["- Absicht = intention (glossary: aim; intention); Meynungen = opinions (Meinung); Betrachtete = considered (Betrachtung = consideration).","- \"Wolfen\", \"Herrmanns\" are inflected forms of names, rendered as plain names. \"Zufall\" rendered \"incident\" (lit. chance occurrence); \"vor Gericht gefordert\" = \"summoned before court\".","- \"zu sich herunterzulassen\" etc. do not occur here; \"kein vortrefflicher Lob\" kept as \"no more excellent praise\" (comparative form in the German)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0012":["After such great efforts as the greatest men have given themselves for the freedom of human understanding, should one indeed have cause to fear that the outcome of them will displease them?",["- \"Erfolg\" = \"outcome\"; \"derselben\" is ambiguous as to its referent (the efforts), preserved as \"of them\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0013":["III.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0014":["I shall make use of this moderation and fairness to my advantage. But I shall find it only where the mark of merit and of a preeminent science comes to the fore. Apart from these there remains yet a great crowd over which prejudice and the authority of great people still exercise a cruel dominion. These gentlemen, who would gladly be regarded as arbiters in learning, seem to be very skilled at judging a book without having read it. In order to give it up to censure one need only show them its title. If the author is unknown, without character and merits, the book is not worth the time being wasted on it; but still more so if he undertakes great things, to censure famous men, to improve sciences, and to commend his own thoughts to the world. If it came down to number before the judgment seat of the sciences, I should have a very desperate case. But this danger does not make me uneasy. These are those who, as one says, dwell only at the foot of Parnassus, who possess no property and have no voice in the election.",["- Merkmahl = mark (nota); Wissenschaft = science (scientia); Anzahl = number (not Zahl); \"Gelehrsamkeit\" = \"learning\"; urtheilen = judge (v.).","- \"wenn er sich großer Dinge unternimmt\" is irregular in the German (sich with unternimmt); rendered \"if he undertakes great things\".","- \"Tittel\" = \"title\"; \"Richterstuhle\" = \"judgment seat\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0015":["IV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0016":["Prejudice is made just for human beings; it lends support to comfort and to self-love, two properties that one does not lay aside without laying aside humanity. He who is taken up with prejudices raises certain men, whom it would be in vain to belittle and to let down to himself, above all others to an unclimbable height. This preeminence covers everything else with the illusion of a perfect equality, and lets him not become aware of the difference that still reigns among these, and which would otherwise expose him to the vexing observation of seeing how manifoldly one is still surpassed by those who are still found within mediocrity.",["- Scheine = illusion (Schein); Beobachtung = observation; Unterscheid = difference.","- \"Menschheit\" = \"humanity\"; \"ohne die Menschheit\" is literally \"without the humanity\", i.e. rendered \"without laying aside humanity\" only by supplying the verb's sense; the German reads \"nicht ohne die Menschheit ableget\" (\"does not lay aside without humanity\").","- \"Derjenige der von Vorurtheilen eingenommen\" lacks a finite verb \"ist\"; rendered \"He who is taken up with prejudices\". \"vielfach\" = \"manifoldly\"; \"zu sich herunterzulassen\" (\"to let down to himself\") kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0017":["So long, then, as the vanity of human minds shall still be powerful, so long will prejudice also maintain itself, i.e. it will never cease.",["- Gemüther = minds (Gemüt); \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0018":["V.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0019":["In the continuation of this treatise I shall have no hesitation in freely rejecting the proposition of a man however famous, if it presents itself to my understanding as false. This liberty will draw very odious consequences upon me. The world is very inclined to believe: that he who believes himself in one case or another to have a more correct cognition than, say, a great scholar, also places himself in his imagination above him. I venture to say that this illusion is very deceptive, and that here it really deceives.",["- Satz = proposition; Folgen = consequences (Folge: glossary \"sequel; sequence\"; here rendered \"consequences\"); Erkenntniß = cognition; Einbildung = imagination (Einbildungskraft is the faculty; here \"Einbildung\", in the sense of one's fancy); Schein = illusion.","- \"glaubet\" = \"believes\" and \"glauben\" = \"believe\" (Glaube = belief; faith), same English for both verb forms.","- \"betrüglich\"/\"betrüge\" = \"deceptive\"/\"deceives\" (\"würklich\" = \"really\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0020":["There is not found in the perfection of the human understanding any such proportion and similarity as, say, in the structure of the human body; with the latter it is indeed possible to make an inference from the magnitude of one and another member to the magnitude of the whole; but with the capacity of the understanding it is quite otherwise. Science is an irregular body, without symmetry and uniformity. A scholar of dwarf size often surpasses another in this or that part of cognition, who nevertheless towers far above him with the whole domain of his science. The vanity of man, to all appearances, does not extend so far that it should not become aware of this distinction, and should hold the insight into one and another truth to be one and the same with the wide sum total of a superior cognition; at the very least I know that one would do me wrong if one made me this reproach.",["- Verstand = understanding; Wissenschaft = science (scientia); Erkenntniß = cognition; Umfang = domain; Inbegriff = sum total; Fähigkeit = capacity; Schluß = inference.","- \"einem andern\" (dative in the German, after \"übertrift\") is rendered \"another\" as it stands; the case is irregular and is not emended. The German \"übertrift ... einem andern\" is kept as \"surpasses another\".","- \"vor einerley halten\" rendered \"hold ... to be one and the same.\"","- \"Theile\" = part (Teil); \"Ganzen\" = whole."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0021":["VI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0022":["The world is not so absurd as to think a scholar of rank is no longer subject at all to the danger of erring. But that a lowly and unknown writer should have avoided these errors, from which all his acuteness could not rescue a great man, that is the difficulty, which is not so easy to digest. There lies much presumption in these words: The truth, for which the greatest masters of human cognition have competed in vain, has first presented itself to my understanding. I do not venture to justify this thought, but I would also not willingly renounce it.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstand = understanding.","- \"Die Welt ist so ungereimt nicht, zu denken\" is kept in the German order; \"die Welt\" may be read as \"the world\" in the sense of \"people,\" not glossed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0023":["VII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0024":["I stand in the imagination that it is at times not useless to place a certain noble trust in one's own powers. A confidence of this kind enlivens all our efforts, and imparts to them a certain momentum, which is very conducive to the investigation of truth. When one is in the condition of being able to persuade oneself that one may still entrust something to one's consideration, and that it is possible to catch a Herr von Leibnitz in mistakes, then one applies everything to make one's conjecture true. After one has now gone astray a thousand times in an undertaking, the gain that has hereby accrued to the cognition of truths will nevertheless be much more considerable than if one had always kept to the highway.",["- Kräfte = powers (Kraft = force; power); Betrachtung = consideration; überreden = persuade; Erkenntniß = cognition.","- \"Einbildung\" rendered \"imagination\" (not Einbildungskraft)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0025":["On this I base myself. I have already sketched out for myself the path that I want to keep. I will begin my course and nothing shall hinder me from continuing it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0026":["VIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0027":["There is yet a new objection, which one will make to me, and which, as it seems, I must anticipate. One will at times hear me in the tone of a man who is very well assured of the correctness of his propositions, and who does not fear that he will be contradicted, or that his inferences can deceive him. I am not so vain as actually to imagine this to myself, nor do I have reason to take from my propositions the illusion of an error so carefully; for after so many false steps, to which the human understanding has been subject at all times, it is no longer a disgrace to have erred. There lies a quite different intention beneath my procedure. The reader of these pages is without doubt already prepared, through the theorems that are now in vogue concerning the living forces, before he turns to my treatise. He knows what was thought before Leibnitz announced his estimation of forces to the world, and the thought of this man must also be already known to him. He has infallibly let himself be won over by the inferences of one of the two parties, and by all appearances this is the Leibnizian party, for all of Germany has now declared itself for it. In this condition he reads these pages. The defenses of the living forces, under the form of geometrical proofs, have taken possession of his whole soul. He therefore regards my thoughts only as doubts, and if I am very fortunate, still as apparent doubts, the resolution of which he leaves to time, and which nevertheless cannot fall as a hindrance to truth. On the other hand I must apply my whole art in order to hold the reader's attention a little longer with me. I must present myself to him in the whole light of conviction that my proofs afford me, in order to make him attentive to the grounds that instill this confidence in me.",["- Satz/Sätze = proposition(s); Schluß/Schlüsse = inference(s); Lehrsätze = theorems; Beweis = proof; Grund = ground; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Absicht = intention; Verstand = understanding.","- Schein = illusion (in \"den Schein eines Irrthums\"); \"scheinbare\" rendered \"apparent,\" which could also mean \"seeming\"; ambiguity kept.","- \"lebendige Kräfte\" = living forces; \"Kräftenschätzung\" = estimation of forces.","- \"Seele\" = soul. \"Parthey\" = party. \"Leibnitz\" retains the original spelling.","- \"Absehen\" in \"allem Absehen nach\" = \"by all appearances\" (idiom, literal sense \"aim\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0028":["If I were to present my thoughts only under the name of doubts, then the world, which is in any case inclined to regard them as nothing better, would very easily be over them; for an opinion that one has once believed to have been proven will maintain itself for a very long time in approval, even if the doubts by which it is attacked are ever so apparent, and cannot easily be resolved.",["- Meynung = opinion; Zweifel = doubts; scheinbar = apparent (see preceding note on ambiguity: \"apparent\"/\"seeming\").","- \"über dieselbige hinweg seyn\" rendered literally as \"be over them\" (idiom: to be done with them)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0029":["A writer commonly draws his reader imperceptibly along into the very condition in which he himself found himself in the composition of his writing. I would therefore, if it were possible, rather communicate to him the state of conviction than of doubt; for the former would be more advantageous to me, and perhaps also to truth, than the latter. These are the small artifices that I must now not despise, in order to restore even somewhat the equilibrium of the scale, in which the authority of great men gives such a mighty turn.",["- Verfassung = condition; Zustand = state; Ueberzeugung = conviction.","- \"den Zustand der Ueberzeugung, als des Zweifels\" kept with the parallel genitives."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0030":["IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0031":["The last difficulty that I still want to clear away is that which one will make to me on account of discourtesy. It seems: that I could have met the men whom I have undertaken to refute with more reverence than I have really done. I should have pronounced my judgment, which I pass on their propositions, in a much milder tone. I should not have called them errors, falsities or even delusions. The harshness of these expressions seems to be belittling to the great names against which they are directed. In the time of distinctions, which was also the time of the roughness of manners, one would have answered: that one must judge the propositions separated from all personal advantages of their authors. But the courtesy of this century imposes on me quite another law. I would not be excusable if the manner of my expression offended the high esteem that the merit of great men demands of me. But I am assured that this is not so. If we encounter obvious errors alongside the greatest discoveries: so this is not so much a fault of the man as rather of humanity; and one would do this too much honor, in the person of the scholars, if one wanted to exempt it from them entirely. A great man who erects for himself an edifice of propositions cannot turn his attention equally strongly to all possible sides. He is entangled in a certain consideration in particular, and it is no wonder if then from some other side mistakes escape him, which he would infallibly have avoided if, outside this occupation, he had only directed his attention to it.",["- Urtheil = judgment; Ausdruck/Ausdrücke = expression(s); Betrachtung = consideration; Gesetz = law; Satz = proposition; \"beurtheilen\" = judge (v. beurteilen: assess; rendered \"judge\").","- \"Unterscheidungen\" = distinctions; \"Sitten\" = manners.","- \"denenselben\" (in \"von denenselben\") refers back to \"Irrthümer\"/\"Menschheit\" ambiguously; rendered \"from them\" (the scholars/errors left open); \"dieser ... ausnehmen\" rendered \"exempt it from them.\"","- \"wenn er ... auf dieselbe gerichtet hätte\": \"dieselbe\" rendered \"it,\" antecedent left unresolved."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0032":["I will simply confess the truth without circumlocution. I will not be disinclined to hold those propositions to be actual errors and falsities which appear under this shape in my consideration; and why should I impose upon myself the constraint of hiding this thought so anxiously in my writing, in order to seem what I do not think, but what the world would gladly have me think?",["- Sätze = propositions; Betrachtung = consideration; \"würklich\" = actual.","- \"vor\" in \"vor würkliche Irrthümer ... zu halten\" = \"for/as\"; rendered \"to be.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0033":["And to speak in general, I would also get on badly with the ceremony of imparting to all my judgments, which I pronounce on great men, a certain flourish of courtesy, of skillfully softening the expressions, and of letting the mark of reverence be seen everywhere; this effort would, on account of the choice of words, often bring me into a vexatious narrowness, and subject me to the necessity of straying from the footpath of philosophical consideration altogether. I will therefore make use of the occasion of this preface to make a public declaration of the reverence and high esteem that I shall at all times harbor toward the great masters of our cognition, whom I shall now have the honor to call my opponents, and to which the freedom of my poor judgments can do not the slightest detriment.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0034":["X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0035":["After the various prejudices that I have now endeavored to clear away, there nevertheless finally remains a certain rightful prejudice, to which I have in particular to be thankful for whatever in my writing might still be found convincing. When many great men of proven acuteness and power of judgment are led, partly by different, partly by the same ways, to the assertion of precisely the same proposition, it is a far more probable presumption that their proofs are correct than that the understanding of some poor writer should have observed the sharpness in those same proofs more exactly. This writer therefore has great cause to make the object of his consideration especially clear and plain to himself, to analyze and set it out in such a way that, if he should perhaps commit a fallacious inference, it would nevertheless have to shine into his eyes at once; for it is presupposed: that, even if the consideration is intricate, the one who precedes the other in acuteness will sooner discover the truth. He must therefore make his investigation as simple and easy as possible, so that, according to the measure of his power of judgment, he may presume in his consideration just as much light and correctness as the other, according to the measure of his own, in a much more intricate investigation.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0036":["This observation I have let be a law for me in the execution of my undertaking, as will soon be perceived.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0037":["XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0038":["Before we conclude this preface, we want to acquaint ourselves briefly with the present state of the dispute about the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0039":["Herr von Leibnitz, to all appearances, did not first catch sight of the living forces in the cases in which he first presented them to the world. The beginning of an opinion is commonly much simpler, especially of an opinion that carries with it something so bold and wonderful as that of the estimation according to the square. One has certain experiences, which are very common and by which we perceive: that an actual motion, e.g. a blow or impact, always carries more violence with it than a dead pressure, even if it is still strong. This observation was perhaps the seed of a thought that could not remain unfruitful in the hands of Herr von Leibnitz, and that afterwards grew to the magnitude of one of the most famous doctrinal edifices.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0040":["XII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0041":["To speak in general, the matter of the living forces seems, so to speak, to have been made precisely so that the understanding must at one time or another, it might have been at whatever time it would, be seduced by it. The overcome obstacles of gravity, the displaced matters, the compressed springs, the moved masses, the velocities arising in compound motion, everything agrees in a wonderful way to bring about the illusion of the estimation according to the square. There is a time in which the multiplicity of proofs counts for what at another would be accomplished by their sharpness and distinctness. This time is now present among the defenders of the living forces. If they feel little conviction in one or another of their proofs, the semblance of truth, which on the other hand shows itself from so many more sides, fortifies their assent and does not let it become wavering.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0042":["XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0043":["It is harder to say on which side, up to now, in the dispute about the living forces, the presumption of victory has shown itself most. The two Herren Bernoulli, Herr von Leibnitz and Herrmann, who stood at the head of the philosophers of their nation, could not be outweighed by the authority of the remaining scholars of Europe. These men, who had all the weapons of geometry in their power, were alone capable of holding up an opinion that perhaps would not have been allowed to show itself, if it had found itself in the hands of a less famous defender.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0044":["The party of Cartesius as well as that of Herr von Leibnitz have felt for their opinion all the conviction of which one is commonly capable in human cognition. On both sides one has sighed over nothing but the prejudice of the opponents, and each party has believed that its opinion could impossibly be called into doubt, if its opponents would only take the trouble to look at it in a right equilibrium of the inclinations of the mind.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0045":["Meanwhile a certain noteworthy difference does show itself between the way in which the party of the living forces seeks to maintain itself and that in which the estimation of Cartesius defends itself. The latter appeals only to simple cases, in which the decision of truth and error is easy and certain, the former on the contrary makes its proofs as intricate and obscure as possible, and rescues itself, so to speak, with the help of night from a combat in which, perhaps, in a right light of distinctness, it would always draw the shorter straw.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0046":["The Leibnizians also have almost all the experiences on their side; this is perhaps the only thing they have over the Cartesians. The Herren Poleni, s' Gravesande, and van Musschenbroeck have rendered them this service, the consequences of which would perhaps be excellent, if one had made use of them more correctly.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0047":["In this preface I shall give no narration of what I intend to accomplish in the present treatise in the matter of the living forces. This book has no other hope of being read than that which it builds on its brevity; it will therefore be easy for the reader to acquaint himself with its sum total himself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0048":["If I were permitted to trust something to my own imagination: I would say that my opinions could provide some not inconvenient assistance toward settling one of the greatest schisms that now reigns among the geometers of Europe. But this persuasion is vain: The judgment of a human being counts for less nowhere than in his own cause. I am not so very much taken with my own that I would lend an ear, for its benefit, to a prejudice of self-love. Meanwhile, let it be with this as it may, I nevertheless venture to predict with confidence: This dispute will either be settled shortly or it will never cease.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0049":["First Chapter,\non the force of bodies in general.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0050":["§ 1.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0051":["Every body has an essential force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0052":["Because I believe that it can contribute something to the aim that I have, to make the doctrine of the living forces once certain and decisive, if I have previously established some metaphysical concepts of the force of bodies in general; I shall therefore make the beginning with this.",["- Kraft = force (plural lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\"); Vermögen/Urteilskraft: \"power of judgment\" for Urtheilskraft.","- Urtheil = judgment; Vorurtheil = prejudice; Erkenntniß = cognition; Beobachtung/Betrachtung = observation/consideration; Beweis = proof; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Ueberredung = persuasion; Meynung = opinion; Inbegrif = sum total; Merkmahl = mark; Erklärung = declaration (here); Schein = illusion; \"der Schein der Wahrheit\" rendered \"semblance\" (Schein with Wahrheit).","- 0033: \"von allen auszuschweifen\" is obscure (\"von allen\" possibly \"altogether\" or \"from all [paths]\"); rendered \"altogether.\" \"der die Freyheit…\" refers back to Ehrerbietigkeit und Hochachtung (feminine), rendered \"which.\"","- 0035: \"Fehlschluß\" = fallacious inference; \"Vorwurf\" = object (of his consideration); \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"denenselben\" = \"those same.\"","- 0035: \"Es hat dieser\" — \"dieser\" rendered \"This writer\" (the antecedent is Schriftsteller); a minimal clarification of a pronoun.","- 0041: \"Vielheit\" = multiplicity; \"Schärfe\" = sharpness; \"Deutlichkeit\" = distinctness; \"Materien\" = matters (plural).","- 0045: \"den kürzern ziehen\" idiom rendered literally-equivalent \"draw the shorter straw.\" \"Gefecht\" = combat.","- 0048: \"Einbildung\" = imagination (Einbildungskraft is the glossary term; here Einbildung, a different word)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0053":["One says that a body that is in motion has a force. For overcoming obstacles, tensioning springs, displacing masses; all the world calls this working. If one sees no further than the senses teach, one takes this force for something that has been communicated to the body entirely and wholly from outside, and of which it has nothing when it is at rest. The whole crowd of philosophers before Leibnitz was of this opinion, with the sole exception of Aristotle. One believes the obscure entelechy of this man to be the secret behind the effects of bodies. The schoolmen, all of them, who all followed Aristotle, did not comprehend this riddle, and perhaps it was also not made for anyone to comprehend it. Leibnitz, to whom human reason owes so much, taught first that an essential force inheres in the body, which belongs to it even before extension. *Est aliquid praeter extensionem imo extensione prius*; these are his words.",["- Kraft = force; Würkung/würken (old spelling of Wirkung/wirken) = effect / to work; \"working\" is used for würken throughout.","- Welt in \"alle Welt\" = \"the world\" (idiom, literally \"all the world\"); Weltweisen = philosophers (Weltweisheit = philosophy in the glossary).","- begreifen = comprehend; Ausdehnung = extension; Meynung = opinion; Vorstellung = representation.","- Latin quotation left in Latin as in the source; the German \"man glaubt\" is rendered \"one believes.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0054":["§ 2.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0055":["This force of bodies Leibnitz called, in general, the working force.",["- würkende Kraft = \"working force\" (würken = to work; cf. Wirkung = effect)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0056":["The discoverer called this force by the general name of the working force. One ought only to have followed him on his heels in the doctrinal edifices of metaphysics; but one has sought to determine this force somewhat more closely. The body, it is said, has a moving force, for one sees it otherwise do nothing but bring forth motions. When it presses, it strives toward motion, but then the force is in exercise when the motion is actual. But I assert that if one ascribes to the body an essential moving force (*vim motricem*), so that one has an answer ready to the question about the cause of motion, then one practices to a certain extent the artifice that the schoolmen made use of, in that in the investigation of the grounds of warmth or of cold they took refuge in a *vi calorifica* or *frigifaciente*.",["- Lehrgebäuden = \"doctrinal edifices\" (Lehre = doctrine); Grund = ground; würklich = actual (old spelling of wirklich).","- Ambiguity kept: \"auf dem Fusse nachfolgen\" rendered literally \"follow ... on his heels.\"","- Latin forms (vim motricem, vi calorifica, frigifaciente) left as printed in the source.","- beylegen = ascribe; Ursache = cause; Untersuchung = investigation."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0057":["§ 3.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0058":["One ought fairly to call the essential force *vim activam*.",["- \"billig\" = \"fairly\" (in the sense of \"rightly\"); Latin phrase left as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0059":["One does not speak correctly when one makes motion into a kind of effects, and on that account ascribes to it a force of the same name. A body to which infinitely little resistance is made, which consequently hardly works at all, has the most motion. Motion is only the external phenomenon of the state of the body, in which it indeed does not work, but is yet endeavoring to work, but when it suddenly loses its motion through an object, that is, in the moment in which it is brought to rest, it works then. One ought therefore not to name the force of a substance after that which is no effect at all, still less, however, to say of bodies that work in the state of rest (e.g., of a ball that presses the table on which it lies through its heaviness) that they have an endeavor to move themselves. For since they would then not work if they moved themselves, one would have to say: while a body works, it has an endeavor to get into the state in which it does not work. One will therefore much sooner have to name the force of a body a *vim activam* in general than a *vim motricem*.",["- Art = kind; Würkungen = effects; Zustand = state; Gegenstand = object (not Object); Bemühung = endeavor.","- Schweere = \"heaviness\" (old spelling of Schwere); Ruhestand = state of rest.","- The syntax of \"noch viel weniger aber von den Körpern ... sagen daß sie...\" is kept awkward as in the German; the construction depends on \"sollte ... nicht ... benennen ... noch ... sagen.\"","- \"Phänomenon\" rendered \"phenomenon,\" not \"appearance\" (Erscheinung)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0060":["§ 4.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0061":["How motion can be explained from the working force in general.",["- überhaupt = \"in general\"; erklären = explain."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0062":["But nothing is easier than to derive the origin of that which we call motion from the general concepts of the working force. The substance A, whose force is determined to work outside itself, (that is, to alter the inner state of other substances,) finds either in the first moment of its endeavor at once an object that endures its whole force, or it finds none such. If the former befell all substances, we would know no motion at all, we would therefore also not name the force of bodies after it. But if the substance A cannot apply its whole force in the moment of its endeavor, it will apply only a part of it. But with the remaining part of it it cannot remain inactive. It must rather work with its whole force, for it would otherwise cease to be called a force if it were not applied whole. Hence, because the consequences of this exercise are not to be met with in the coexisting state of the world, one will have to find them in the second dimension of the same, namely in the successive series of things. The body will therefore apply its force not at once, but little by little. But in the following moments it cannot work on the same substances on which it worked right at the start, for these endure only the first part of its force, but the rest they are not capable of taking on; thus A works little by little always on other substances. But the substance C on which it works in the second moment must have toward A an entirely different relation of place and position than B, on which it worked right at the beginning, for otherwise there would be no ground why A did not, in the beginning, at once work on the substance C as well as on B. Likewise the substances on which it works in the following moments each have a different position toward the first place of the body A. That is to say, A alters its place while it works successively.",["- Begriff = concept; Zustand = state; Gegenstand = object; Theil = part; Grund = ground; Ort = place; Lage = position.","- Abmessung = \"dimension\" (literally measurement); Folgen = consequences (not Folgerung); succeßive = \"successive.\"","- \"den innern Zustand\" = \"inner state\"; \"coexistirenden\" = \"coexisting.\"","- Pronouns \"er\"/\"sie\" (A as Substanz, then Körper) are kept as in the German: \"it.\"","- \"Relation\" = \"relation\" (Latinate, not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0063":["§ 5.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0064":["What difficulties flow from this into the doctrine of the working of the body on the soul; if one ascribes to them no other force than the *vim motricem*.",["- Lehre = doctrine; \"diesen\" (dative plural) refers back to the bodies; rendered \"them,\" as in the German the antecedent is not restated.","- The semicolon is kept as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0065":["Because we do not become distinctly aware of what a body does when it works in the state of rest, we always think back to the motion that would follow if one removed the resistance. It would be enough to make use of it for this, that one have an external character of that which goes on in the body and which we cannot see. But commonly motion is regarded as that which the force does when it really breaks loose, and which is the only sequel of it. Because it is so easy to find one's way back from this little detour to the right concepts, one should not think that such an error would be of consequence. But it is so in fact, although not in mechanics and natural science. For precisely from this it becomes so hard in metaphysics to represent to oneself how matter is in a position to bring forth representations in the soul of man in a manner that is in fact effective (that is, through physical influx). What else does matter do, one says, than cause motions? Hence all its force will come down to this, that at most it displaces the soul from its place. But how is it possible that the force which alone brings forth motions should generate representations and ideas? These are indeed such different genera of things that it is not comprehensible how one could be the source of the other.",["- deutlich = distinct; gewahr werden = become aware; Folge = sequel (Folgen in \"von Folgen\" = \"of consequence\").","- Character = \"character\" (Latinate, kept); Begriffe = concepts; Vorstellungen = representations; Ideen = ideas.","- Geschlechter = \"genera\" here rendered literally; German \"Geschlecht\" is not Gattung (genus); glossary form used for sense only.","- würksame Art = \"effective manner\" (Art = manner); Sachen = things; begreiflich = comprehensible.","- \"Einfluß\" = \"influx,\" with \"physischen\" = \"physical.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0066":["§ 6.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0067":["The difficulty that arises from this when there is talk of the working of the soul on the body. And how this can be removed through the term of a *vis activa* in general.",["- Benennung = term; \"vis actiuae\" (genitive, in the source) corrected silently as \"vis activa\" in English, Latin spelling \"actiuae\" being old orthography.","- überhaupt = \"in general\"; \"gehoben werden\" = \"be removed.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0068":["A like difficulty manifests itself when the question is whether the soul is also in a position to posit matter in motion. Both difficulties vanish, however, and physical influx acquires no small light, if one posits the force of matter not on the account of motion, but of effects in other substances, which one need not determine more closely. For the question whether the soul can cause motions, that is, whether it has a moving force, transforms itself into this: whether its essential force can be determined to an effect outward, that is, whether it is capable of acting outside itself on other beings and of bringing forth alterations? This question one can answer in a quite decisive way thus: that the soul must be able to act outward for this reason, because it is in a place. For if we analyze the concept of that which we call the place, one finds that it indicates the effects of substances on one another. Thus nothing more has prevented a certain astute writer from making the triumph of physical influx over the pre-established harmony complete than this small confusion of concepts, from which one easily finds one's way out as soon as one only directs one's attention to it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0069":["If one calls the force of bodies in general merely an acting force, one comprehends easily how matter can determine the soul to certain representations.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0070":["It is just as easy to comprehend the way the paradoxical proposition works, namely how it is possible: that matter, of which one nevertheless stands in the imagination that it can cause nothing but motions, impresses certain representations and images on the soul. For matter, which has been posited in motion, acts on everything that is combined with it according to space, hence also on the soul; that is, it alters the inner state of the same, insofar as it relates to the outer. Now the whole inner state of the soul is nothing other than the comprehension together of all its representations and concepts, and insofar as this inner state relates to the outer, it is called the *status repraesentatiuus vniuersi*; hence matter, by means of its force that it has in motion, alters the state of the soul, whereby it represents the world to itself. In this way one comprehends how it can impress representations on the soul.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0071":["§ 7.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0072":["Things can actually exist, yet nevertheless be present nowhere in the world.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0073":["It is difficult not to digress in a matter that is of such wide domain; but I must nevertheless turn back again to what I wanted to remark about the force of bodies. Since all combination and relation of substances existing outside one another stems from the alternating effects that their forces exert against one another, let us see what truths can be derived from this concept of force. Either a substance is in a combination and relation with others outside it, or it is not. Since every self-subsistent being contains in itself the complete source of all its determinations, it is not necessary to its existence that it stand in combination with other things. Hence substances can exist, and nevertheless have no external relation at all to others, or stand in an actual combination with them. Since now without external connections, situations and relations no place takes place, it is quite possible that a thing actually exists, but yet is present nowhere in the whole world. This paradoxical proposition, although it is a consequence, and indeed a very easy consequence, of the best-known truths, has, as far as I know, not yet been remarked by anyone. But still other propositions flow from the same source, which are no less wonderful, and take the understanding captive, so to speak, against its will.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0074":["§ 8.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0075":["It is true in the properly metaphysical sense that more than one world can exist.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0076":["Since one cannot say that something is a part of a whole if it stands in no combination at all with the remaining parts; (for otherwise no difference would be found between an actual union and an imagined one,) but the world is an actually composed being, a substance that is combined with no thing in the whole world will also not belong to the world at all, unless perhaps in thought, that is, it will be no part of the same. If there are many such beings, which stand in no connection with any thing of the world, but have a relation toward one another, there arises from this an entirely particular whole, they constitute an entirely particular world. It is therefore not correctly spoken when one teaches continually in the lecture halls of philosophy that in the metaphysical sense not more than a single world can exist. It is actually possible that God created many millions of worlds, even taken in the quite metaphysical signification; hence it remains undecided whether they also actually exist, or not. The error that has been committed in this has infallibly arisen because one did not pay exact attention to the definition of the world. For the definition reckons to the world only that which stands in an actual combination with the remaining things, * but the theorem forgets this restriction, and speaks of all existing things in general.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0077":["* *Mundus est rerum omnium contingentium simultanearum & successiuarum inter se connexarum series.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0078":["§ 9.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0079":["If substances had no force to act outside themselves, there would be no extension, and also no space.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0080":["It is easy to prove that there would be no space and no extension if substances had no force to act outside themselves. For without this force there is no combination, without this no order, and without this finally no space. But it is somewhat harder to see how from the law according to which this force of substances acts outside itself, the multiplicity of the dimensions of space follows.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0081":["The ground of the threefold dimension of space is still unknown.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0082":["Since I perceive a circular inference in the proof that Herr von Leibniz somewhere in the Theodicy takes from the number of lines that can be drawn at right angles to one another from a point, I thought of proving the threefold dimension of extension from that which one perceives in the powers of numbers. The first three powers of these are quite simple, and let themselves be reduced to no other, but the fourth, as the square-squared, is nothing but a repetition of the second power. As good as this property of numbers seemed to me for explaining the threefold dimension of space from it, in application it nevertheless did not hold water. For the fourth power is, in everything that we can represent to ourselves of space through the power of imagination, a non-thing. One cannot in geometry multiply a square by itself, nor the cube by its root; hence the necessity of the threefold dimension rests not so much on this, that if one posited more, one would do nothing other than that the previous ones would be repeated, (as is the constitution with the powers of numbers,) but rather on a certain other necessity, which I am not yet in a position to explain.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0083":["§ 10.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0084":["It is probable that the threefold dimension of space stems from the law according to which the forces of substances act on one another.",["- Kraft = force throughout; Würkung/würken = effect/act (Wirkung = effect); Veränderung = alteration (not in glossary).","- 0068: \"setzen\" = posit; \"in Bewegung zu setzen\" kept as \"posit in motion\". \"Bestimmen\" = determine; \"Ort\" = place; \"Begriff\" = concept; \"Wesen\" = being.","- 0068: \"der physische Einfluß\" = physical influx (*influxus physicus*); \"vorherbestimmte Harmonie\" = pre-established harmony (literally \"predetermined\").","- 0070: \"begreifen\" = comprehend; \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Einbildung\" = imagination, and \"Einbildungs-Kraft\" (0082) = power of imagination (glossary Einbildungskraft = imagination). \"Art vom paradoxen Satze\" is awkward German and kept so; \"Satz\" = proposition.","- 0070: \"Zusammenfassung\" = comprehension together (not in glossary). \"Raum\" = space; \"Zustand\" = state.","- 0073: \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Relation\" kept as \"relation\"; \"Verstand\" in 0073 = understanding, but in 0076 \"im metaphysischen Verstande\" = \"in the metaphysical sense\" (also \"Bedeutung\" = signification there, \"Sinn\" not used). \"Selbständiges Wesen\" = self-subsistent being. \"Lagen\" = situations (doubtful: could be \"positions\"). \"Ort statt findet\" rendered literally as \"place takes place.\"","- 0076: \"Erklährung\" and \"Definition\" both appear for definition (glossary Erklärung = explanation; definition); rendered \"definition\" in both places. \"Theorem\" kept (cf. Lehrsatz). \"Einschränkung\" = restriction. The parenthesis and the \"*\" footnote mark are kept in place. The sentence beginning \"Since one cannot say...\" has no connective before \"a substance\" in German either (\"so wird\"); the English is kept as a single sentence.","- 0077: footnote Latin kept as printed (*successiuarum*, *connexarum*).","- 0080: \"Vielheit der Abmessungen\" = multiplicity of the dimensions; \"Abmessung\" and \"Dimension\" (0081, 0082) both rendered \"dimension.\" \"Ausdehnung\" = extension.","- 0082: \"Unding\" = non-thing (cf. Ding = thing); \"Potenzen\" = powers; \"Stich halten\" idiom \"hold water\"; \"Zirkelschluß\" = circular inference (glossary Schluß = inference). \"Wie es mit den Potenzen... beschaffen ist\" with Beschaffenheit rendered as \"constitution.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0085":["Because everything that occurs among the properties of a thing must be able to be derived from that which contains in itself the complete ground of the thing itself, the properties of extension, and therefore also its threefold dimension, will also be grounded on the properties of the force which the substances possess, with regard to the things with which they are combined. The force with which a substance acts in the union with others cannot be thought without a certain law, which emerges in the manner of its action. Because the manner of the law according to which the substances act in one another must also determine the manner of the union and composition of many of them, the law according to which an entire collection of substances (that is, a space) is measured, or the dimension of extension, will derive from the laws according to which the substances, by virtue of their essential forces, seek to unite themselves.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0086":["The threefold dimension seems therefore to derive from the fact that the substances in the existing world act in one another in such a way that the strength of the effect is related inversely as the square of the distances.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0087":["In accordance with this, I hold that the substances in the existing world, of which we are a part, have essential forces of such a kind that, in union with one another, they spread out their actions according to the double inverse relation of the distances; second, that the whole which arises from this has, by virtue of this law, the property of the threefold dimension; third, that this law is arbitrary, and that GOD could have chosen another instead, for example the inverse threefold relation; and that finally, fourth, from another law an extension with other properties and dimensions would also have flowed. A science of all these possible kinds of space would be unfailingly the highest geometry that a finite understanding could undertake. The impossibility that we notice in ourselves of representing to ourselves a space of more than three dimensions seems to me to derive from the fact that our soul likewise receives the impressions from outside according to the law of the inverse double relation of the distances, and because its nature itself is made for not only suffering in this way, but also acting outside itself in this way.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0088":["§ 11.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0089":["The condition under which it is probable that there are many worlds.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0090":["If it is possible that there are extensions of other dimensions, then it is also very probable that GOD has actually placed them somewhere. For his works all have the magnitude and manifoldness that they can only hold. Spaces of this kind could now impossibly stand in combination with such as are of entirely different beings; hence spaces of the sort would not belong to our world at all, but would have to constitute worlds of their own. In the foregoing I have shown that more worlds, taken in the metaphysical sense, could exist together, but here is at the same time the condition which, as it seems to me, is the only one on account of which it would also be probable that many worlds actually exist. For if only the single kind of space, which suffers only a threefold dimension, is possible, then the other worlds, which I posit outside of that in which we exist, could be combined with ours according to space; because they are spaces of one and the same kind. Hence it would be asked why GOD has separated the one world from the other, since he would yet by their connection have imparted to his work a greater perfection; for the more combination, the more harmony and agreement there is in the world, whereas gaps and dissolutions violate the laws of order and of perfection. It is therefore not probable that many worlds exist, (even though it is possible in itself,) unless it be that many kinds of space, of which I have now spoken, are possible.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0091":["These thoughts can be the sketch for a consideration that I reserve for myself. But I cannot deny that I communicate them as they occur to me, without procuring their certainty for them through a longer investigation. I am therefore ready to reject them again, as soon as a riper judgment will uncover their weakness to me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0092":["§ 12.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0093":["Some teachers of metaphysics assert that the body, by virtue of its force, strives toward motion in all directions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0094":["The newest philosophy establishes certain concepts of the essential force of bodies which cannot be altogether approved. One calls it a perpetual striving toward motion. Besides the error that this concept carries with it, as I showed at the beginning, there is yet another, of which I now want to speak. If force is a perpetual endeavor toward acting, it would be an obvious contradiction if one wanted to say that this exertion of the force is, with regard to external things, entirely and altogether undetermined. For by virtue of its definition, it is indeed endeavoring to act outside itself in other things; indeed, according to the accepted theorems of the newest teachers of metaphysics, it actually acts into them. Those therefore seem to speak most correctly who say that it is rather directed toward all directions, than that it is, with regard to direction, entirely and altogether undetermined. The renowned Herr Hamberger therefore asserts that the substantial force of the monads strives equally toward motion in all directions, and therefore, like a balance, maintains itself at rest through the equality of the counter-pressures.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0095":["§ 13.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0096":["First objection against this opinion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0097":["According to this system, motion arises when the equilibrium of two oppositely posited tendencies is lifted, and the body moves itself in the direction of the greater tendency, with the excess of force that this has received over the oppositely posited smaller one. This explanation still satisfies the imagination in the case in which the moving body always advances together with the moved. For this case is similar to that in which someone supports with the hand one of two equally weighing scale-pans, and thereby causes the motion of the other. But a body to which its motion has been communicated through an impact continues it to infinity, notwithstanding that the impelling violence ceases to act in it. According to the cited theoretical structure, however, it would not be able to continue its motion, but, as soon as the impelling body left off acting in it, it would also suddenly fall into rest. For because the tendencies of the force of the body, directed toward all directions, are inseparable from its substance, the equilibrium of these inclinations will restore itself in the instant, as soon as the external violence, which had set itself against the one tendency, ceases to act.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0098":["Second objection against the same opinion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0099":["§ 14.",["- *Kraft* = force throughout; *Würkung* / *Wirkung* = effect in 0086 and 0085 (\"Art seiner Würkung\" = \"manner of its action\"); *würken* = act (v.), rendered consistently as \"act.\"","- *Ausdehnung* = extension; *Abmessung* / *Dimension* both appear: *Abmessung* = \"dimension\" in 0085–0087, and *Dimension* also = \"dimension\" (one English word for two German words).","- *Vereinigung* = union; *Verbindung* = combination; *Verknüpfung* = connection; *Zusammensetzung* = composition.","- *Wahrscheinlich* = probable; *Verstand* in 0087 = understanding, in 0090 \"im metaphysischen Verstande\" = \"in the metaphysical sense\" (a locution; the glossary's \"understanding\" is not used there).","- *Wesen* in 0090 (\"von ganz andern Wesen\") = beings; *wesentlich* = essential.","- *Gegenden* = directions (literally \"regions\"); *Richtung* = direction (one English word for two German words); *Tendenzien* = tendencies.","- *Eindrücke* = impressions; *Einbildungskraft* = imagination; *Lehrsätze* = theorems; *Lehrgebäude* = \"theoretical structure\" (literally \"doctrinal edifice\"); *Definition* in 0094 = \"definition\" (Latinate, not *Erklärung*, which appears in 0097 as \"explanation\").","- 0087 \"daß endlich viertens... wäre\" depends on the preceding \"halte ich davor\"; 0090 \"sie\" in \"daß sie GOtt würklich ... angebracht hat\" is ambiguous in German as to number/antecedent only by \"Ausdehnungen\"; left as is. In 0097 \"Gewalt\" = \"violence\" (no glossary entry)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0100":["This, however, is not the only difficulty. Because a thing must be thoroughly determined, the striving toward motion, which the substances exert in all directions, will have to have a certain degree of intensity. For it cannot be infinite; but a finite effort to act, without a certain magnitude of exertion, is impossible: Hence, since the degree of intensity is finite and determined, suppose that a body A of equally great mass runs up against it with a force that is three times stronger than all the effort toward motion that this one has in the essential force of its substance, then it will be able to take from the one running up against it only the third part of its velocity through its *vis inertiae*: But it will also itself attain no greater velocity than that which is equal to the third part of the velocity of the moving body. After the impact has been carried out, therefore, A as the body running up against it is to move on with two degrees of velocity, B however with only one degree, in precisely the same direction. Since now B stands in the way of body A, and does not take on as much velocity as it needs in order not to be an obstacle to the motion of body A; since, in spite of this, it is nevertheless not capable of stopping its motion, A will actually move in the direction A C * with the velocity 2, but B, which is in the way of body A, in just this direction with the velocity as 1, the motions of both, however, will nevertheless proceed unhindered. This, however, is impossible, unless one wanted to posit that B would be penetrated by A, which however is a metaphysical absurdity. **",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0101":["* Fig. I.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0102":["** One comprehends this still more distinctly if one considers that the body A, after the impact has been carried out, will be in C, when B has not yet passed the point D, which divides the line A C in half; consequently the former will have had to penetrate the latter, for otherwise it could not have attained a head start before it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0103":["§ 15.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0104":["Twofold Division of Motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0105":["It is time that I end this metaphysical preparation. I cannot, however, refrain from adding yet a remark, which I hold indispensable for the understanding of what follows. The concepts of dead pressure and of the measure of it, which occur in mechanics, I presuppose in my readers, and in general I shall in these pages present no complete treatise on all that belongs to the doctrine of living and dead forces; but only sketch some slight thoughts that seem to me to be new, and conducive to my principal aim, of improving the Leibnizian measure of forces. Hence I divide all motions into two principal kinds. The one has the property that it maintains itself in the body to which it has been communicated, and continues on to infinity, if no obstacle opposes itself. The other is a perpetual effect of a constantly impelling force, in which not even a resistance is necessary to annihilate it, but which rests only on the external force, and just as soon vanishes as this ceases to maintain it. An example of the first kind are the bullets that are shot and all thrown bodies; of the second kind is the motion of a ball that is pushed gently along by the hand, or otherwise all bodies that are carried, or drawn with moderate velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0106":["§ 16.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0107":["Motion of the second kind is not different from dead pressure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0108":["One comprehends easily, without engaging in a deep consideration of metaphysics, that the force that expresses itself in the motion of the first kind has, in comparison with the force of the second genus, something infinite. For this one in part annihilates itself, and ceases of itself suddenly, as soon as the impelling force withdraws; one can therefore regard it as if it vanished every moment, but also were just as often generated anew. Whereas, on the other hand, that one is an internal source of a force in itself imperishable, which carries out its effect in a continuing time. It therefore relates to that one as a moment to time, or as the point to the line. A motion of this kind is therefore not different from dead pressure, as Herr Baron Wolf has already remarked in his Cosmology.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0109":["§ 17.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0110":["Motion of the first kind presupposes a force that relates as the square of the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0111":["Because I want to speak of motion properly so called, which maintains itself of itself in an empty space in eternity; so I want to regard briefly the nature of it, according to the concepts of metaphysics. If a body, in free motion, runs in an infinitely subtle space, its force can be measured according to the sum of all the effects that it does in eternity. For if this aggregate were not equal to its whole force, one would have to take, in order to find a sum that is equal to the whole intensity of the force, a longer time than the infinite time is, which is absurd. Now one compares two bodies A and B, of which A has a velocity as 2, B however one as 1; so A presses, from the beginning of its motion on, in eternity, the infinitely small masses of the space that it runs through, with twice more velocity than B, but it also covers in this infinite time a space two times greater than B, thus the whole magnitude of the effect that A carries out is proportioned to the product of the force with which it meets the small parts of space, into the multiplicity of these parts, and just so is it constituted with the force of B. Now the effects of both, into the small *moleculas* of space, are proportioned to their velocities, and the multiplicity of these parts are likewise as the velocities, consequently the magnitude of the whole effect of one body to the whole effect of the other is as the square of their velocities, and thus their forces too are in this ratio. *",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0112":["* Because in this writing I properly want to set certain objections against the opinion of Herr von Leibnitz, it seems that I contradict myself, since in this § I offer a proof in confirmation of his opinion. But in the last chapter I shall show that the opinion of Herr von Leibnitz, if only it is limited in a certain way, actually takes place.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0113":["§ 18.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0114":["Second Ground of This.",["- Glossary terms: Grad = degree; Größe = magnitude; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Kraft = force; Bestimmung/bestimmt = determined; Begriff = concept; Betrachtung = consideration; Beweiß = proof; Menge = multiplicity; Grund = ground; Absicht = aim; Meynung = opinion.","- \"Würkung\" (also \"würken\") = effect (to act); \"Bemühung,\" \"Bestrebung,\" \"Anstrengung\" = effort, striving, exertion; all distinct from \"Kraft.\"","- \"Art\" (Haupt-Arten, ersten Art) and \"Geschlechte\" (§16, \"Kraft von dem zweyten Geschlechte\") both rendered by \"kind\"/\"genus\" respectively; \"Geschlecht\" = genus here, not Gattung.","- \"Verstande\" in §15 (\"zum Verstande des folgenden\") = understanding in the sense of the act of understanding.","- \"Ungereimt/Ungereimtheit\" = absurd/absurdity; \"Ein Ding\" = thing; \"Körper\" = body; \"Masse\" = mass.","- The ambiguous \"jener/dieser/diese\" in §16 and \"dieser/er/ihm\" in §14 are kept as in the German (\"this one,\" \"that one,\" \"the former,\" \"the latter\"); referents not resolved.","- \"Dieandere\" in §15 is an OCR run-together, silently corrected to \"Die andere.\" \"Moleculas\" retained as printed and italicized as Latin; \"vim inertiae\" italicized.","- \"Fig. I.\" footnote and the asterisks (* and **) are retained as in the original; \"weil … so\" sentence in §14 (\"Weil … nicht hinderlich sey; weil … aufzuhalten, so wird…\") kept as a single sentence with the doubled \"weil.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0115":["For a better concept of this property of living forces, one may think back to what was said in the 16th §. Dead pressures can have nothing more than simple velocity as their measure, for since their force does not itself rest on the bodies that exert them, but is accomplished through an external violence, the resistance that overpowers it needs, with respect to the strength with which this force seeks to maintain itself in the body, no particular effort of a certain kind (for the force is in no way rooted in the acting substance and endeavors to maintain itself in it), but has to annihilate only the single velocity that the body needs in order to change place. But with living force it is entirely otherwise. Because the state in which the substance finds itself, in that it runs on in free motion with a certain velocity, is grounded completely on the inner determinations; so this same substance is at the same time endeavoring to maintain itself in this state. The external resistance, therefore, besides the force it needs in order to hold the balance to the velocity of this body, must at the same time have a particular violence to break the striving with which the inner force of the body is strained to maintain in itself this state of motion, and the whole strength of the resistance that is to put into rest the bodies that find themselves in free motion must therefore be in compound ratio, from the proportion of the velocity and of the force with which the body is endeavoring to maintain in itself this state of endeavor; i.e. because both ratios are equal to one another, the force that the resistance requires is as the square of the velocity of the bodies running against it.",["- Kräfte/Kraft = force(s); Gewalt rendered \"violence\" (also in the sense of the external agent); Bestimmungen = determinations; Zustand = state; Körper = body.","- Maaß(e) = measure; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Widerstand = resistance; Absicht = \"respect\" in \"in Absicht auf\" (glossary \"aim; intention\" not apt in this idiom).","- Bemühung / bemühen / Bestrebung = effort / endeavor / striving; \"bemühet\" kept as \"endeavoring\" and \"Bemühung\" in \"Zustand der Bemühung\" as \"endeavor.\"","- \"Verhältniß\" / \"Proportion\" = ratio / proportion; \"in zusammengesetzter Verhältniß\" = \"in compound ratio.\"","- \"würkenden\" = \"acting\"; the original spelling \"würken\" is not kept.","- Ambiguity kept: \"die ... Kraft ... auf den Körpern ... selber nicht beruhet\" (the force does not rest on the bodies); \"dieselbe überwältiget\" refers to the force."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0116":["§ 19.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0117":["I may not promise myself to attain anything decisive and incontestable in a consideration that is merely metaphysical, therefore I turn to the following chapter, which through the application of mathematics will perhaps be able to lay more claim to conviction. Our metaphysics is, like many other sciences, in fact only at the threshold of a truly thorough cognition; God knows when one will see it cross that threshold. It is not hard to see its weakness in much of what it undertakes. One very often finds prejudice as the greatest strength of its proofs. Nothing is more to blame for this than the prevailing inclination of those who seek to extend human cognition. They would gladly have a great philosophy, but it would be to be wished that it might also be a thorough one. It is almost the only recompense for a philosopher's effort when, after a laborious investigation, he can finally find repose in the possession of a truly thorough science. Therefore it is a great deal to demand of him that he only seldom trust his own approval, that in his own discoveries he not conceal the imperfections that he is not in a position to remedy, and that he never be so vain as to set aside the pleasure that the imagination of a thorough science produces, in favor of the true utility of cognition. The understanding is very inclined to approval, and it is indeed very hard to hold it back for long; but one should nevertheless finally impose this constraint on oneself, in order to sacrifice to a well-grounded cognition everything that has something extensive and charming about it.",["- Wissenschaft = science; Erkenntniß = cognition; Weltweisheit = philosophy; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Beweise = proofs; Einbildung = \"imagination\" (not Einbildungskraft); Verstand = understanding; Vollkommenheit not present, Unvollkommenheiten = imperfections.","- \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"Beyfall\" = approval.","- \"vor seine Bemühung\" = \"for his effort\" (vor = für).","- \"dem Vergnügen ... dem wahren Nutzen ... hindan zu setzen\": the German has two datives; I render \"set aside the pleasure ... in favor of the true utility,\" preserving the construction as given, which is syntactically irregular.","- \"eine weitläuftige reizendes\" is as printed (probably corrupt or elliptical: \"weitläuftiges, reizendes\"); rendered \"something extensive and charming.\"","- \"sollte\"/\"wollten\" = \"should\"/\"would\"; \"wollten gerne\" in the past subjunctive-like form."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0118":["Second Chapter,\nInvestigation of the Theorems of the Leibnizian Party concerning Living Forces.",["- Hauptstück = chapter; Lehr-Sätze = theorems (Lehrsatz); Parthey = party; Kräfte = forces."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0119":["§ 20.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0120":["I find in the treatise that Herr Bülfinger presented to the Petersburg Academy a consideration that I have at all times made use of as a rule in the investigation of truths. When men of good understanding, among whom either on neither or on both sides the suspicion of foreign intentions is to be found, assert opinions entirely running against one another, then it accords with the logic of probabilities to direct one's attention most of all to a certain middle proposition that leaves both parties in a certain measure in the right.",["- Absichten = intentions; Meynungen = opinions; behaupten = assert; Wahrscheinlichkeiten = probabilities; Verstand = understanding; Mittel-Satz = middle proposition (Satz = proposition).","- \"auf keiner oder auf beyden Theilen\": Theil is masculine/neuter in the plural form \"Theilen\"; rendered \"sides\" (Theil = part/side).","- \"Maaße\" in \"in gewisser Maaße\" = \"measure.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0121":["§ 21.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0122":["I do not know whether I have otherwise been fortunate in this way of thinking, but in the dispute about living forces I hope to be. Never has the world divided itself more equally into certain opinions than in those that concern the measure of forces of moved bodies. The parties are, to all appearances, equally strong and equally fair. Foreign intentions can indeed mix in with them, but of which party should one be able to say that it would be entirely free of this? I choose, therefore, the surest way, in that I seize upon an opinion in which both great parties find their account.",["- Streit-Sache = dispute; Kräften-Maaß = measure of forces; Meynungen = opinions; Absichten = intentions; \"Art zu denken\" = \"way of thinking\" (Art = way).","- \"ihre Rechnung finden\" = \"find their account\" (idiom).","- \"allem Absehen nach\" = \"to all appearances.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0123":["§ 22.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0124":["Leibniz's and Descartes's Estimation of Forces.",["- Schätzung = estimation; Cartesens / Leibnitzens = genitive forms of the names (Cartes = Descartes, Leibnitz = Leibniz, as in the German \"Cartes,\" \"Leibnitz\" retained in the body of the text below)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0125":["Before Leibnitz the world had paid homage to the single proposition of Cartes, which in general assigned to bodies, even to those that find themselves in actual motion, as the measure of their force only the bare velocities. No one let it occur to him that it would be possible to place a doubt in it; but Leibnitz brought human reason suddenly into uprising through the proclamation of a new law, which has since become one of those that have offered to the learned the greatest contest of the understanding. Cartes had estimated the forces of moved bodies simply according to the velocities, but Herr von Leibnitz set as their measure the square of their velocity. This rule of his he did not put forward, as one should think, only under certain conditions that still allow some place to the former; no, rather he denied Cartesens laws absolutely and without limitation, and set his own at once in its place.",["- Satz = proposition; Gesetz = law; Gesetze = laws (singular/plural as printed: \"Cartesens Gesetze\"); Bedingungen = conditions; Einschränkung = limitation; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Wettstreit = contest; Maaße = measure.","- Names rendered as printed (Cartes, Leibnitz, Cartesens), departing from Leibniz/Descartes for consistency with the German; the heading (0124) uses the modern forms.","- \"Niemand liesse es sich beyfallen\": \"liesse\" is subjunctive (lieβe), rendered \"let it occur.\"","- \"dasselbe\" refers to \"Satze\" (the proposition)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0126":["§ 23.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0127":["First Error of the Leibnizian Measure of Forces.",["- Fehler = error; Kräften-Maaß = measure of forces."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0128":["There are properly two pieces that I find to object to in Herr von Leibnitz's rule. That of which I shall now treat draws after it no consequences of importance in the matter of living forces; yet one cannot omit to remark it, so that in so great a proposition nothing may be neglected that can free it from all the small reproaches that one might perhaps make against it:",["- Stücke = pieces; Satz = proposition; Folgen = sequels/consequences (Folge, glossary \"sequel; sequence\"; rendered \"consequences\" here, the plain sense); Vorwürfe = reproaches.","- Passage ends with a colon in the original; the continuation follows in the next passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0129":["The Leibnizian measure of forces has always been presented in this formula: If a body is engaged in actual motion, then its force is as the square of its velocity. Thus, according to this proposition, the mark of this measure of force is nothing but actual motion. But a body can move actually, although its force is no greater than that which it would perhaps exert with this initial velocity merely through pressure. I have already proved this in the previous chapter, and repeat it once more. A ball that I push along quite gently on a smooth surface immediately ceases to move further when I withdraw my hand. In such a motion, then, the force of the body vanishes every moment; but it is restored just as often through a new pressure. In the same moment, therefore, in which the body meets the object, its force from the previous motion is not still its own, no, this is already all annihilated, it possesses only that force which the impelling power communicates to it in just this moment in which it touches the object. One can therefore regard it as if it had not moved at all, and as if it pressed against the resistance merely in a state of rest. Such a body is consequently not different from one that exerts a dead pressure, and therefore its force is not as the square of its velocity, but as the velocity simply. This, then, is the first limitation that I make to the Leibnizian law. He should not have given actual motion alone as the mark of living force, it was also necessary to add a free motion. For if the motion is not free, then the body never has a living force. According to this determination the Leibnizian law, where it is otherwise only correct, will have to appear in this formula: A body that finds itself in actual and free motion has a force that is as the square etc. etc.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0130":["§ 24.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0131":["What an actual motion is.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0132":["I now make the second remark, which will uncover for us the sources of the notorious dispute, and which perhaps also offers the only means of settling it again.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0133":["The defenders of the new estimation of living forces are still in agreement with the Cartesians in this, that bodies, when their motion is only in the beginning, possess a force that is related as their mere velocity. But as soon as the motion can be called actual, the body, in their opinion, has the square of the velocity as its measure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0134":["Let us now investigate what an actual motion properly is. For this word was the cause of the defection from Descartes, but perhaps it can also become a cause of the reunification.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0135":["One calls a motion actual when it does not find itself merely in the point of the beginning, but when, while it lasts, a time has elapsed. This elapsed time, which lies between the beginning of the motion and the moment in which the body acts, is what properly makes it possible to call the motion actual.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0136":["But note well that this time * is not something of a posited and measured magnitude, but that it is entirely undetermined, and can be determined at will. That is to say: one can assume it as small as one wants, if one is to use it to indicate an actual motion thereby. For it is not this or that magnitude of time that properly makes the motion actual, no, it is time in general, be it as small or as large as it likes.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0137":["* In the formula of the Leibnizian measure of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0138":["§ 25.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0139":["Second principal fault of the Leibnizian measure of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0140":["Accordingly the time expended in the motion is the true and sole character of living force; and it alone is that through which this obtains a particular measure as against the dead.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0141":["Let us now make representable, through the line A B, the time that elapses from the beginning of the motion until the body meets an object on which it acts, the beginning of which is in A. ** In B the body thus has a living force, but in the point of beginning A it does not have it, for there it would press an obstacle that stood against it merely with an endeavor toward motion. But let us further infer in the following manner. First",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0142":["** Fig. II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0143":["1st, the time A B is such a determination of the body that finds itself in B, through which a living force is posited in it, and the point of beginning A (namely if I posit the body in it) is a determination that is a ground of dead force. Second",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0144":["2nd. If I make in thought this determination, which is expressed by the line A B, smaller, then I posit the body nearer to the point of beginning, and it is easy to understand that if I continued this, the body would finally find itself in A itself; consequently the determination A B will, through its shortening, always be posited nearer to the determination in A; for if it did not approach this at all, the body could never, through the shortening of the time, even if I continued it infinitely, gain the point A, which is absurd. The determination of the body in C thus comes nearer to the conditions of dead force than in B, in D still nearer than in C, and so on, until in A itself it has all the conditions of dead force, and the conditions for the living have entirely vanished. But if",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0145":["3rdly certain determinations, which are the cause of a property of a body, are gradually transformed into other determinations, which are a ground of an opposite property, then the property that was a consequence of the former conditions must also change along with them, and be gradually transformed into that property which is a consequence of the latter. * Since now, when I shorten in thought the time A B (which is a condition of a living force in B), this condition of living force is necessarily posited nearer to the condition of dead force than it was in B; so the body in C must also actually have a force that comes nearer to the dead than that in B, and still nearer if I posited it in D. Thus a body that possesses a living force under the condition of elapsed time does not possess it in any time whatever, which can be as short as one wants; no, it must be determined and certain, for if it were shorter, it would no longer have this living force. Leibniz's law of the estimation of forces, therefore, cannot take place; for it attributes to bodies that have moved for a time at all (this means as much as those that move actually) a living force without distinction, this time may be as short or as long as one wants. **",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0146":["* According to the rule *posita ratione ponitur rationatum*.",["- Since the passages were given separately, I have returned each id with its own notes; none of the notes below is repeated under the other ids.","- 0129: Kraft = force; Körper = body; Gesetz = law; Bestimmung = determination; Kennzeichen = \"mark\" (not the glossary's Merkmal); würklich = actual (old spelling of wirklich); Druck = pressure.","- 0129: \"Bewegungbegriffen\" read as \"Bewegung begriffen\" (OCR); \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Formul\" = formula.","- 0133: \"Schätzung\" = estimation; \"Maaß\" = measure; \"Meynung\" = opinion.","- 0135–0136: \"würket\" = acts (the old form of wirkt); \"gesetzter\" = posited (setzen); \"undeterminirt\"/\"determinirt\" = undetermined/determined, kept distinct from bestimmen = determine.","- 0141: \"vorstellig machen\" = make representable; \"Vors\" at the break is the start of \"Vorste\" (Vors-te, \"Firstly\"), continued in the next passage as \"1ste\"; rendered \"First\" and \"Second\" as the text stands, with no completion.","- 0143–0145: \"Vors\" at 0143 end similarly begins \"Vors-te\" = \"Second\"; \"Wenn aber\" (0144) and \"3tens\" (0145) join across the break, rendered as given.","- 0145: \"Bedingung\" = condition; \"Grund\" = ground; \"Folge\" = consequence here (Folge is sequel in the glossary, but \"Folge\" with eines Grundes is rendered \"consequence\" for sense; one word for Folge).","- 0146: Latin retained in italics; the Latin wording is Kant's own, with the text's space before the period left out."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0147":["** The brief content of this proof is the following. The time that lies between the beginning of the motion and the moment in which the body strikes can be thought as much shorter as is desired, without it being thereby possible to understand that the condition of the living force will be lost thereby, § 24; now, however, this shortening is a ground from which it can be understood that, if one were to continue it, the body will finally be at the point of beginning, where the living force actually is lost, and the condition for the dead force, on the other hand, presents itself; thus the diminution of this time is no ground that withdraws anything from the condition of the living force, and yet is at the same time a ground for this: which contradicts itself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0148":["§ 26.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0149":["Proof of the same from the law of continuity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0150":["What I have now proved is a quite exact consequence from the law of continuity, whose extensive utility perhaps has not yet been sufficiently come to be known. Herr von Leibnitz, its inventor, made it into a touchstone, on which the laws of Descartes did not hold up under the test. I hold it to be the greatest proof of its excellence that it almost alone offers a means of properly uncovering the most renowned law of the whole of mechanics, and of showing it in its true shape.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0151":["One need only direct one's attention to the manner and way in which Herr von Leibnitz made use of this principle against Descartes, and one will easily perceive how it must be applied here. He proves that the rule that obtains when a body strikes against one that is in motion must also remain when it runs up against one that is at rest; for rest is not different from a very small motion. What holds when unequal bodies run against one another must also hold when the bodies are equal; for a very small inequality can be confused with equality.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0152":["In this way I too conclude: what holds in general when a body has moved for a time must also hold if the motion is only at the beginning, for a very small duration of the motion is not different from the mere beginning of it, or one can fittingly confuse them. From this I conclude: if the body in general then has a living force when it has moved for a time (let it be as short as one likes), it must also have it when it is only beginning to move. For it is all one whether it is just beginning, or has perhaps already continued to move for an uncommonly small time. And so I conclude; because from the Leibnitzian law of the estimation of forces this absurdity follows, that the force would be living in the point of beginning of the motion itself, one cannot assent to it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0153":["It is easy to perceive how greatly the understanding sets itself against it, when this law is presented to it in the proper light of distinctness. It is impossible to persuade oneself that a body that has a dead force at point A should have a living one, which is infinitely many times greater than the dead, if it has only removed itself by an imperceptibly small line from this point. This leap of thoughts is too sudden, there is no path that carries us over from the one determination to the other.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0154":["§ 27.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0155":["The time elapsed in the motion, and hence also the actuality of the motion, is not the true condition under which a living force accrues to the body.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0156":["One should take good heed of what flows from this. The elapsed time, if it is put forward as undetermined, cannot be a condition for living force; and this I have proved before, but even if it is put forward as determined and restricted to a certain magnitude, it still cannot supply the proper condition of living force, and this I now prove in the following way.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0157":["Suppose one could prove that a body that has this velocity will have a living force after one minute, and that this minute is that condition under which this force accrues to it; then, if the magnitude of this time were doubled, everything in the body would be double that previously, taken only singly, already posited a living force in it. But the magnitude of the first minute added a new dimension to the force of the body; (*per hypothesin*) thus the magnitude of two minutes, because it comprehends doubled in itself the conditions that the former contained in itself, will add one dimension more to the force of the body. The body, therefore, that continues its motion freely will indeed have at the point of beginning of it only a force of one dimension, and after the elapse of one minute a force of two dimensions; but at the second minute its force has three dimensions, at the third four, at the fourth five, and so on. That is: its force will, in uniform motion, have as its measure now the velocity simply, now the square of it, now the cube, now the biquadrate, etc.; which are such extravagances as no one will undertake to defend.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0158":["One need not doubt the correctness of these inferences. For if one demands that a time of determinate magnitude, which elapses from the beginning of the motion of a body up to a certain point, comprise the conditions of living force wholly and entirely in itself; then one also cannot deny that in a time twice as great there would also be twice as much of these conditions, for time has no other determinations than its magnitude. And if therefore a simple time is the sufficient ground for bringing a new dimension into the force of a body; then a double time will posit two such dimensions (according to the rule: *rationata sunt in proportione rationum suarum*). One can add further: that time could be a condition for living force only for the reason that the body, in the elapse of it, removes itself from the condition of the dead, which consists in the moment of beginning; and that this time must for this reason have a determinate magnitude, because in less time it would not have removed itself sufficiently from the determinations of the dead force, as the magnitude of a living force requires. Since it now removes itself ever farther, in a greater time, from the moment of beginning, i.e. from the condition of the dead force: so the force of the body would have to attain, to infinity, the longer it moves, more and more dimensions even with its uniform velocity; which is absurd.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0159":["Thus, first, the absence of the actuality of the motion is not the true and proper condition which appropriates to the force of a body the estimation by the simple velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0160":["Second, neither the actuality of the motion in general, and the general and indeterminate consideration of the elapsed time connected with it, nor the determinate and set magnitude of the time, is a sufficient ground of living force, and of the estimation of it according to the square.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0161":["§ 28.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0162":["Mathematics cannot prove the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0163":["From this consideration we wish to draw two consequences of importance.",["- 0147: \"**\" at the start is carried over from the source as printed (apparently a footnote marker); \"§ 24\" kept as in the text.","- 0147: \"Grund\" = ground throughout; \"Bedingung\" = condition; \"verstehen\" = understand; \"lebendige/todte Kraft\" = living/dead force (Kraft = force).","- 0147: \"sie\" in \"wenn man sie fortsetzete\" (feminine) refers to \"Abkürzung\"; \"dagegen\" is rendered \"on the other hand.\"","- 0150: \"Probierstein\" = touchstone; \"kennen gelernet\" rendered \"come to be known\"; \"ihn\" (masc.) after \"Gesetze\" (neut.) is kept as in the German, so \"ihn\" is rendered as \"it\" with the shift unresolved. \"Mittel\" = means.","- 0151/0152: \"Grundsatz\" = principle; \"Gesetz\" = law; \"Cartes\"/\"Cartesen\" rendered \"Descartes.\"","- 0153: \"Deutlichkeit\" = distinctness; \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Überreden\" = persuade; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- 0157: \"Abmessungen\" and \"Dimension\" both rendered \"dimension(s)\" in \"von zwey Abmessungen\" etc. (Abmessung/Dimension); \"Maaß\" = measure; \"Quadratoquadrat\" = biquadrate. \"Würklichkeit\" = actuality (0155, 0159, 0160), where \"Wirklichkeit\" in the glossary is \"actuality.\"","- 0158: \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"einfache Zeit\" = \"simple time\"; \"zureichender Grund\" = sufficient ground. \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" (0159) = \"simple velocity\" (\"schlecht\" in the older sense of \"plain\"; \"schlechthin\" in 0157 = \"simply\"). \"undgesetzte\" (0160) is as printed, probably \"und gesetzte\" (\"and set\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0164":["The first is: that mathematics can never offer any proofs to the advantage of living forces, and that a force estimated in this way, if it otherwise takes place, is nevertheless at the very least outside the territory of mathematical consideration. Everyone knows that, when one wants in this science to estimate the force of a body moved with a certain speed, one is bound to no determinate moment of the time elapsed in the motion, but that, with regard to this limitation, everything is indeterminate and indifferent. The estimation of the force of moved bodies which mathematics provides is therefore of the kind that it extends over all motions in general, the time that has elapsed over it being as short as one likes, and that it sets us no bounds in this respect. An estimation of that kind, however, also goes to the motion of bodies that is at the beginning, § 25. 26. and that is therefore dead, and has the mere speed for its measure. And since the living forces cannot be comprehended together with the dead under one and the same estimation; one sees easily that the former are entirely excluded from a mathematical consideration.",["- Kraft = force; lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\"; todt = \"dead\". Geschwindigkeit rendered \"speed\" (not in glossary).","- Schätzung / schätzen = \"estimation\" / \"estimate\" (not in glossary); Maaß = \"measure\"; Grenzen = \"bounds\" (Grenze = bound(ary)).","- Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; begriffen seyn = \"comprehended\" (begreifen = comprehend).","- Gebiethe = \"territory\"; Absicht = \"regard\" in \"in Absicht auf\" (idiom, not \"aim\").","- \"statt hat\" = \"takes place\"; \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" = \"mere speed\" (schlecht in the older sense \"plain, mere\").","- \"seyn\" in the last clause is the German's own subjunctive-infinitive form (\"ausgeschlossen seyn\"); the German is slightly irregular and is kept as it stands (translated as \"are ... excluded\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0165":["Moreover, mathematics considers in the motion of a body nothing but the speed, the mass, and perhaps also the time, if one wanted to take it in. The speed is never a ground of the living force; for the body, even if according to the opinion of the Leibnizians it possessed a living force, would still not be able to have it in all moments of its motion, but there would be a time after the beginning of the same in which it did not yet have it, although all speed would already be present in it. § 25. 26. The mass is still much less a ground for the same. Finally, we have proved just the same also of time. The motion of each body taken in particular has therefore nothing in itself that in a mathematical consideration would indicate a living force inhering in it. Since now all inferences that one makes from that which a body does that is in motion must be derived from the notions that are comprehended in the consideration of the speed, the mass, and the time, they will, if they are correctly extracted, offer no consequences that establish the living forces. And if it seems that they render these a service, one should not trust this illusion, for there would then be more contained in the consequences than the principles comprised in themselves, i.e. the *rationatum* would be greater than its *ratio*.",["- Grund = \"ground\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"; Folgerungen = \"consequences\"; Schlüsse = \"inferences\" (Schluß = inference).","- Meynung = \"opinion\" (Meinung); Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Erwegung = \"consideration\" (a second German word for \"consideration\", not in glossary; Betrachtung also so rendered).","- Notionen is German-spelled and kept as \"notions\" (not Begriffe).","- *rationatum* and *ratio* are Latin in the original and kept in italics.","- \"darinnen er sie noch nicht hätte\" – \"in which\" refers to \"Zeit\"; \"derselben\" (of the same) is ambiguous between Bewegung and Geschwindigkeit/Kraft; I kept \"the same\" (\"the beginning of the same\"), where the German's antecedent is most naturally \"Bewegung\".","- \"dazu nehmen\" rendered \"take in\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0166":["After such manifold and great efforts as the geometers of these two centuries have made to settle the dispute of Descartes and of Herr von Leibniz through the doctrines of mathematics, it seems very strange that I begin to deny to this science the decision of the same. It is true that for some time it has been disputed whether this science is favorable to Descartes's laws, or whether it defends the party of Herr von Leibniz. But in this discord everyone is agreed on this: that, in order to resolve properly the disputed question of the estimation of forces, one must let it depend on the pronouncement of mathematics. It is wonderful enough: that such great reasoners should have gone astray down such byways, without perceiving, or even merely thinking of, whether this too is the way that could lead them to possession of the truth which they have been tracking. But here it seems to me that I find grounds which compel me to cast to the winds all that is wonderful, and whither should I turn further after its pronouncement?",["- Cartes is rendered \"Descartes\" and Leibnitz \"Leibniz\" (modern spelling of names); Lehren = \"doctrines\"; Kräftenschätzung = \"estimation of forces\".","- Schlußkünstler = \"reasoners\" (lit. \"inference-artists\"); this is a loose rendering of a compound not in the glossary.","- \"nach ihrem Ausspruche\": \"its/their pronouncement\" is ambiguous in the German (mathematics' pronouncement, or that of the grounds/geometers); I kept \"its\" for \"ihrem\" with \"mathematics\" as nearest antecedent, though \"their\" is also possible.","- \"alles das Wunderbare in den Wind zu schlagen\" = idiom \"to cast to the winds / disregard\"; \"wohin ... weiter wenden\" kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0167":["Mathematics already confirms, by its nature, Descartes's laws.",["- Gesetze = \"laws\". \"ihrer Natur nach\" = \"by its nature\" (\"ihrer\" refers to Mathematik)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0168":["The second consequence that I draw from the preceding considerations is this: that the grounds of mathematics, instead of being favorable to the living forces, will rather always confirm Descartes's laws. This must already be clear from the propositions of this §phi, and I can add further: that the mathematical magnitudes, the lines, surfaces, etc., have just the same properties when they are ever so small as when they have who knows what sort of magnitude; and that therefore from the smallest mathematical magnitudes, from the smallest parallelogram, from the fall of a body through the smallest line, just the same properties and consequences must be able to be derived as from the greatest of these genera. If now a line that indicates a motion, as it is constituted immediately after the beginning, has just the same determinations and properties, also just the same consequences, as that line which indicates a motion long after the beginning: then the force that one brings out in a mathematical consideration of the motion of a body will never have other properties than that which is present in the body also in the smallest time, that is in an infinitely small time, from the initial moment on. Since this now is a dead force, and therefore has the measure of the mere speed in itself, all and every mathematically considered motions will present no other estimation than solely and alone that according to the bare speed.",["- Folge = \"consequence\" here (glossary: Folge = sequel; sequence), chosen for context; Folgerung also \"consequence\" — one English word for two German words.","- §phi: \"§phi\" is the OCR/edition form of the section sign with the paragraph; kept as printed.","- Gattungen = \"genera\" (Gattung = genus); Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (Beschaffenheit also \"property\" in glossary); Bestimmungen = \"determinations\".","- \"wer weiß was vor eine Größe\" = \"who knows what sort of magnitude\" (vor = für).","- \"dem größesten\" is dative singular in the German (\"as from the greatest\"), kept as is.","- \"alle und jede ... Bewegungen ... darlegen\" – plural subject, as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0169":["§ 29.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0170":["We know accordingly, even before we enter into a closer investigation of the matter, that Leibniz's adherents, because they want to defend themselves with such weapons as are far removed from the nature of their cause, will succumb in the notorious dispute against Descartes. After this general consideration we want to take into consideration in particular the proofs of which Leibniz's party has chiefly availed itself in this dispute.",["- Sache = \"matter\" in the first sentence and \"cause\" in \"ihrer Sache\"; Streitsache = \"dispute\"/\"disputed matter\". Betrachtung and Erwegung both \"consideration\".","- Beweise = \"proofs\"; \"insbesondere\" = \"in particular\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0171":["Herr von Leibniz was led to his opinion first by that which one perceives in the fall of bodies through their gravity. But it was a wrongly applied principle of Descartes that led him to an error which has perhaps since become the most plausible that has ever crept into human reason. He namely established the following proposition: The same force is necessary to lift a body weighing four pounds one foot high as a one-pound body four feet.",["- Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"; Meynung = \"opinion\".","- scheinbarste = \"most plausible\" (lit. \"most apparent/seeming\"; the German has a tie to Schein); this is an interpretive choice.","- Schuh = \"foot\" (a unit; lit. \"shoe\"); Pfund = \"pound\".","- \"einen ... einen\" (accusative objects): \"a one-pound body four feet\" elliptical, as in the German (\"hoch zu heben\" omitted)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0172":["§ 30.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0173":["The proposition that first brought Herr von Leibniz to the living forces.",["- Satz = \"proposition\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0174":["Because he appeals to the approval of all mechanicians of his time, it seems to me that he inferred this proposition from a rule of Descartes, which the latter made use of to explain the nature of the lever. Descartes assumed that the weights hung on a lever ran through the infinitely small spaces that can be described in their distance from the point of rest. Now two bodies are then in equilibrium when these spaces are, relative to one another, inversely as the weights of the bodies; and so, Leibniz concluded, no more force is necessary to raise a body of one pound to the height four than another whose mass is four to the single height. One easily becomes aware that this conclusion flows from Descartes's basic rule only when the times of the motion are equal. For in the steelyard these times, in which the weights would run through their infinitely small spaces, are equal to one another. Herr von Leibniz left this condition out of account, and also concluded to motion in times that are not equal to one another.",["- Schlußfolge / schloß = \"conclusion\" / \"concluded\" (Schluß = inference; conclusion); folgern = \"infer\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; Grundregel = \"basic rule\".","- Mechaniker = \"mechanicians\"; Schnellwaage = \"steelyard\"; Ruhepuncte = \"point of rest\".","- Gewichter (plural of Gewicht, here used alongside Gewichte) = \"weights\"; kept the same English.","- \"und also schloß Leibnitz\" — the clause \"ist nicht mehr Kraft nöthig\" follows with inverted order; kept in sequence.","- \"aus der Acht ließ\" = \"left out of account\"; \"schloß auch auf die Bewegung\" = \"concluded to motion\" (literal)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0175":["§ 31.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0176":["Herr Hermann's proof that the forces are as the heights that they can reach by them.",["- Beweiß = \"proof\" (Beweis). \"durch dieselben\" (by them) is ambiguous in the German; \"dieselben\" may refer to the forces or to unspecified means; kept as \"by them\". The heading is a fragment."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0177":["The defenders of this man seem to have noticed the objection that could be made to them on account of time. Hence they have sought to arrange their proofs as if the difference of time were to be regarded as nothing at all in the case of the force that bodies acquire through the fall.",["- Einwurf = \"objection\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Unterscheid = \"difference\".","- \"vor nichts anzusehen sey\": rendered literally (\"regarded as nothing\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0178":["Let the infinite spring AB, * which represents gravity, which pursues the body during the fall from A to B; then, says Mr. Herrmann, gravity will communicate to the body at each point of space an equal pressure. These pressures he depicts by the lines AC, DE, BF, etc., which together make up the rectangle AF. The body therefore has, in his opinion, when it has reached the point B, a force that is equal to the sum of all these pressures, i.e., to the rectangle AF. Thus the force in D is related to the force in B as the rectangle AE to the rectangle AF, i.e., as the space traversed AD to the space AB, hence as the squares of the velocities in D and B.",["- \"Es sey ... welche ... verfolget\": the construction is kept as in the German (optative subjunctive, \"Let ... be\").","- Raum = \"space\"; Summe = \"sum\"; Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity\"; Rektangulum/Rectangulo = \"rectangle\" (Latinate form of the German spelling).","- Herrmann is in spaced type in the original; emphasis not reproduced.","- \"u. s w.\" is an OCR spacing slip, read \"u. s. w.\"","- The asterisk refers to the figure note (next passage)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0179":["* Fig. III.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0180":["Thus Mr. Herrmann concludes, in that he asserts that the effect which gravity does in a body that falls freely is directed according to the space that it covers in falling.",["- Wirkung = \"effect\"; behaupten = \"assert\"; \"sich richten nach\" rendered \"is directed according to\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0181":["The Cartesians, on the other hand, assert that the effect of gravity is proportioned not to the spaces covered in a retarded motion, but to the times in which the body either falls or rises back. I shall now give a proof that will put the opinion of the Cartesians beyond doubt, and from which one will at the same time learn to see in what the apparent proof of Mr. Herrmann errs.",["- \"aufgehaltener Bewegung\" = \"retarded motion\" (literally \"held-up/checked\"); doubtful nuance retained.","- Meynung = \"opinion\"; scheinbar = \"apparent\" (can also mean \"seeming\"); Beweiß = \"proof\".","- \"fehle\" is literally \"fails/lacks\"; rendered \"errs\" to fit the construction \"worinn ... fehle\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0182":["§ 32.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0183":["Proof that refutes the case of Mr. Herrmann.",["- \"den Fall\" here is the \"case\" (not \"fall\"); the ambiguity with Fall = fall (of bodies) is possible and is preserved in the English only as \"case\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0184":["Just as much force is necessary to press shut a single one of the five equally tensed springs * A, B, C, D, E for one second as to press all five of them shut one after another within this same time. For divide the second, as the time during which the body M holds the spring A pressed shut, into five equal parts; instead of M pressing against the spring A through all these five parts of the second, assume that it presses the spring A only in the first part of the second, and that in the second part of the second, instead of the spring A, the other, B, which has the same degree of tension, is pushed under, then in the force that M needs to press, no difference will be found in this exchange. For the springs B and A are perfectly equal in everything, and so it is all one whether in the second part of the second the same spring A or B is pressed. Likewise it is equally much, whether M in the third part of the second tenses the third spring C, or whether it still pressed on the previous B in this part of time; for one can put one spring in the place of the other, because they are in nothing different. Thus the body M applies as much force to hold the single spring A pressed shut for an entire second as it needs to tense five such springs one after another within this same time. The same can be said, one may also increase the multitude of the springs to infinity, if only the time of the pressure is equal. It is therefore not the multitude of the pressed-shut springs according to which the force of the body that tenses them all is measured, but the time of the pressing is the right measure.",["- Menge = \"multitude\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Zeit = \"time\"; Spannung = \"tension\"; Maaß = \"measure\".","- \"an der andern Stelle\" kept literal in sense as \"in the place of the other\"; \"Stelle\" = \"place\" (not Ort). \"sie nichts unterschieden seyn\" is irregular German (subjunctive plural with \"seyn\"); rendered \"are in nothing different\".","- \"Secundtheile\"/\"Secunde\" = \"part of the second\"/\"second\".","- \"ists einerley\" = \"it is all one.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0185":["* Fig. IV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0186":["Now let us accept the comparison that Mr. Herrmann draws between the effect of the springs and the pressure of gravity, so we shall find that the time for which the force of the body can resist gravity, and not the space covered, is that according to which the whole effect of the body must be estimated.",["- schätzen = \"estimate\" (not in the glossary); Würkung = \"effect\" (as Wirkung).","- \"wornach\" is left in its relative sense, \"that according to which\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0187":["This is therefore the first attempt, which, as I believe, confirms that which I said above, namely that Descartes's opinion surpasses in mathematical proofs the law of Mr. von Leibniz.",["- Versuch = \"attempt\"; Gesetze (for Gesetz) = \"law\".","- \"übertreffe\" is subjunctive in indirect speech; rendered \"surpasses\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0188":["§ 33.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0189":["The Cartesians' error in asserting the very same thing.",["- \"Der Cartesianer Fehler\": genitive plural placed before the noun, kept in the English possessive."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0190":["I find in the dispute of the Cartesians against the defenders of living forces, which the Marquise von Chastelet carried out with much eloquence, that the former also made use of the difference of time in order to make the inferences of the Leibnizians concerning the fall of bodies powerless. But from that which she cites from the writing of Mr. von Mairan against the new estimation of forces, I see that the true advantage was unknown to him that he could have drawn from the difference of time, and which I believe I indicated in the preceding §, which is certainly so simple and distinct that one must wonder how it was possible not to perceive it in such a light of the understanding.",["- Schlüsse = \"inferences\" (Schluß = inference); einfach = \"simple\"; deutlich = \"distinct\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Schätzung = \"estimation\".","- \"welcher\" (after \"§\") could refer to the \"advantage\" or the \"§\"; the ambiguity is preserved in \"which\".","- \"Frau Marquisin\" rendered \"Marquise\" (Marquisin = marchioness).","- \"wahrzunehmen\" (wahrnehmen) = \"perceive\", not Wahrnehmung in the technical sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0191":["It is certainly quite strange how far these men have gone astray in pursuing a true law of nature, namely that the force that gravity robs from a body is proportioned to time and not to space. After they had erred so far as to concede to the Leibnizians that a body could do fourfold effect with double velocity, after, I say, they had spoiled their case in this way, they are compelled to rescue themselves with a rather poor evasion, namely that the body does indeed do a fourfold effect, but only in double time. They therefore press with uncommon earnestness that the forces of two bodies must be estimated according to the effects that they do in equal times, and that one has not at all to look to what they might accomplish in unequal times. This evasion has been met with infinite distinctness, and I do not comprehend how it was possible still further to resist the constraint of truth.",["- Gesetz = \"law\"; begreifen = \"comprehend\"; Deutlichkeit = \"distinctness\"; Wahrheit = \"truth\".","- \"sich sich\" in the German is a doubled word (printing slip); read once.","- \"daß nemlich ... Natur, daß nemlich\": the second \"daß\" clause is kept in the same place; \"der Zeit und nicht dem Raum\" = \"to time and not to space\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0192":["But we also see from this that it is really only the fallacious inferences of the Cartesians that make Leibniz's party triumph, and that they do not lose the dispute at all through the weakness of their cause. They would always retain the upper hand if they would seize the right weapons that the nature of the matter itself offers them.",["- Fehlschlüsse = \"fallacious inferences\" (Schluß = inference).","- \"möchten\" retained as a subjunctive of volition, \"would\"; Sache = \"cause\"/\"matter\" (both used for the two occurrences, Sache is not in the glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0193":["§ 34.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0194":["A doubt of Mr. Lichtscheid is removed.",["- \"Lichtscheids\" is the spelling of the source (likely Lichtscheid, as in the Academy text)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0195":["I have proved that the effects which gravity exerts, and the resistance which it practices in ascending, relate as the time which the bodies spend in the motion. However, I recall a case that is perhaps apparent enough to make this proposition doubtful for some. Herr Lichtscheid remarks in the *Acta Eruditorum*, that if one lets a pendulum * fall from D in such a way that the thread lays itself against the obstacle E, and consequently, in that it rises again from B to C, describes a smaller circle, it nevertheless attains again, by virtue of its velocity obtained in B, the height CF, which is equal to the height DG from which it has fallen down. But the time that the pendulum spends in the fall through the arc DB is longer than the time in which it rises again up to C. Thus gravity has worked longer there in the pendulum than here. One should now think, if it is true what I have proved before, that gravity does a greater effect in greater times, that the body must have obtained in B a greater velocity than gravity is in a position to take from it again in the motion from B to C. It would therefore, by means of this velocity, have to be capable of swinging itself up still above the point C, which however is false according to the proofs of Herr Lichtscheid.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0196":["* Fig. V.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0197":["But if one only considers that the thread AB is set against the body more strongly, while it moves from D to B, and hinders the fall more by its gravity than the thread EB, or EC, in the fall from C to B; then it can also easily be comprehended that the element of force which heaps up and gathers in the body in every moment of the descent from D to B is smaller than the elementary force which gravity, on the contrary, brings into the body C in each moment, when it sinks down from C to B. For since it is all one whether a body which is fastened to a thread is compelled by the restraint A to run through the circular arc DB or CB, or whether it rolls freely down on a surface curved just so, BD CB, one can represent it as if the fall of which we speak actually took place on two such hollow surfaces connected with one another. Now the surface DB is inclined more strongly toward the horizontal line than the other CB, consequently the body in the former is indeed exposed longer to the impulses of gravity than in the latter, but the surface in return also hinders a greater part of the gravity, which is endeavoring to incorporate itself into the body, than the other CB does.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0198":["I could have been spared the resolution of this objection, because the adherents of Herr von Leibnitz seem themselves to have perceived its weakness, since I find nowhere that they have made use of it. However, Herr von Leibnitz, who had been chosen by Herr Lichtscheid as judge of his treatise, grants it a laudable approval, and it is his authority that could lend it some weight.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0199":["§ 35.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0200":["Before I leave the matter of the fall of bodies through their gravity, I will give the defenders of the living forces yet another case to resolve, which, as it seems to me, is to demonstrate sufficiently: that the consideration of time cannot possibly be excluded from the estimation of the force which gravity brings into a body, as Herr von Leibnitz, and his defenders, have wanted to persuade us up to now.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0201":["§ 36.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0202":["New case which demonstrates that in the estimation of the force which arises through gravity, time must necessarily be taken into consideration too.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0203":["The case is the following: I represent to myself, in the way customary to Cartesians and Leibnitzians, the pressures of gravity which are communicated to a body from the height * aB to the horizontal line bc, through the infinite number of sheet-metal springs AB, CD, EF, GH. Further I place a body m on the inclined surface ac and another, l, I let fall freely from a to b. How will the Leibnitzians now estimate the force of the body m, which is driven down the inclined surface ac by the pressure of the springs, at the end of this slanting fall in c? They cannot do otherwise than give as the measure the product of the multitude of springs which drive the body from a to c, into the force which each spring impresses on it along the direction ac, for their system demands this, as we have seen from the case of Herr Hermann, § 31. And just so they will also be obliged to estimate the force which is found in the other body l, which falls freely from a to b, through the factum of the multitude of springs by which it has been driven onward, into the intensity with which each has pushed it onward. But the number of springs on both sides, through the inclined surface ac as well as through the height ab, is equal, so there remains only the strength of the force which each spring brings into its body in both cases, as the true measure of the forces obtained in b and c by the bodies l and m. This strength, with which each of those sheet-metal springs presses the body m along the direction of the inclined surface ac, relates to the intensity of the pressure of these same springs on the body l along the direction of its motion ab, as ab to ac; as the first elements of mechanics teach us. Thus the force which the body l has at the end of the perpendicular fall in b will likewise relate to the force which m has at the end of the inclined fall in c as ac to ab; which is absurd, for both bodies have equal velocities in b and c, and thus also equal forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0204":["* Fig. VI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0205":["The Cartesians escape this objection by drawing time in too. For although each spring brings less force into the body m on the inclined surface ac, (because a part is consumed by the resistance of the surface,) these springs in return work in the body m much longer than in the body l, which is exposed to their pressure for a much shorter time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0206":["§ 37.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0207":["Having proved that the consideration of the bodies falling through gravity is in no way advantageous to the living forces, it is time to take into consideration another genus of proofs, on which the defenders of the living forces have at all times prided themselves very much. They are those which the doctrine of the motion of elastic bodies seems to offer them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0208":["§ 38.",["- Wirkung = effect; Kraft = force; Beweis/erwiesen = proof/proved; Zeit = time; Gattung = genus (glossary); Lehre = doctrine; Betrachtung = consideration; begreifen = comprehend; Schätzung/schätzen rendered \"estimation\"/\"estimate\" (not in glossary).","- \"Körper\" = body; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = velocity; \"Schwere\" = gravity; \"Widerstand\" = resistance; \"Maaß\" = measure; \"Fläche\" = surface (none in glossary).","- \"Actis Erudit.\" expanded to *Acta Eruditorum* in italics as a Latin title; \"Factum\" kept in Latin in italics-free roman as printed? Rendered *factum* not italicized above; the German prints it as Latin word.","- \"Lehrgebäude\" rendered \"system\"; \"Anfangsgründe\" rendered \"elements\"; \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\".","- \"gnug\" (= genug) silently normalized; \"scheinbar gnug\" rendered \"apparent enough,\" preserving the ambiguity of \"scheinbar\" (apparent/plausible).","- \"dorten ... hier\" rendered \"there ... here\"; \"in den Perpendikel\" rendered \"in the pendulum\" as in the German (not \"on\").","- \"BD CB\" (OCR/figure letters) kept as printed; the asterisk figure markers retained at the point of the footnote reference, with footnotes given as separate passages.","- \"Einwurf\" = objection; \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Vertheidiger\" = defender; \"Anhänger\" = adherent. \"zu gute gethan\" rendered \"prided themselves.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0209":["In the separation that Herr von Leibnitz's estimation of forces in the world has occasioned, as many blindnesses and wrong paths have arisen among the geometers as one would hardly suppose among such great masters of inference. The accounts that will be preserved for us of all the incidents of this notorious dispute will one day take up a very useful place in the history of human understanding. No consideration is more victorious over the imagination of those who so greatly exalt the correctness of our syllogisms than such seductions, which the most acute masters of geometry were unable to escape in an investigation that ought to have afforded them, more than others, distinctness and conviction.",["- Kräftenschätzung: \"estimation of forces\" (Schätzung rendered \"estimation\" throughout; no glossary entry).","- Schlußkünstlern: \"masters of inference\" (Schluß = inference); Vernunftschlüsse = \"syllogisms\" per glossary (alternative \"inferences of reason\").","- Verstand = understanding; Einbildung = \"imagination\" (the glossary gives this for Einbildungskraft; here the word is Einbildung, no \"-kraft\"); Betrachtung = consideration; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Ueberzeugung = conviction.","- \"Verblendungen und Abwege\": rendered literally; \"Geometrer\" = geometers."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0210":["It would have been impossible to stray onto such wrong paths if the Leibnitzians had been willing to take the trouble to direct their attention to the construction of the very proofs that they now regard as insurmountable proofs for the living forces.",["- Beweise / Beweisthümer both rendered \"proofs\" (Beweis = proof; Beweisthum is the Latinate/older compound, not in the glossary).","- lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\" (Kraft = force); \"vor\" = \"for\" (in the sense of \"in favor of\").","- Construction left as in the German (Latin-derived word)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0211":["§ 39.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0212":["The sum of all proofs that are taken from the motion of elastic bodies.",["- Summe = \"sum\" (distinct from Inbegriff = sum total, which is not used here)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0213":["Almost all proofs, at least the most apparent ones, among those that have been borrowed for the living forces from the motion of elastic bodies through impact, have arisen in the following way. One has compared the force that is found in them after the impact has been carried out with the force before the collision. That was found to be greater than this when one has estimated it according to the product of the mass into the velocity, but a perfect equality showed itself only then when one posited, instead of the mere velocity, the square of it. From this the Leibnitzians have concluded that an elastic body would never be capable of bringing into that which it strikes as much motion as actually happens, if its force were only simply as its velocity; for according to this measure the cause would always be smaller than the effect produced.",["- Art = \"way\"; Stoß = \"impact\"; Anstoß = \"collision\" (two German words, two English words).","- Wirkung is spelled \"Würkung\" in the source; rendered \"effect.\"","- Ursach = \"cause\"; schlechte Geschwindigkeit = \"mere velocity\"; schlechthin = \"simply.\"","- setzete = \"posited\" (setzen); geschlossen = \"concluded\"; Maaß = \"measure.\"","- \"Jene ... diese\": \"That ... this\" (Kraft after impact, Kraft before); the pronouns follow the German.","- \"in diejenige die er stößt\": \"into that which it strikes\" — the German \"diejenige\" is feminine singular and could refer to a collective; left as it stands."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0214":["§ 40.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0215":["The Leibnitzians refute their inferences through their own mechanical doctrinal edifices.",["- Schlüße = \"inferences\"; Lehrgebäude = \"doctrinal edifices\" (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0216":["This inference is completely refuted by the theorems of those very ones who have made use of it. I will not cite the mechanical discoveries of Wren, Wallis, Huygens, and others. The Herr Regierungs-Rath and Freyherr von Wolf shall be my guarantor. One should look at his Mechanics, which is in everyone's hands, one will find in it proofs that leave no more doubt that elastic bodies, entirely in accordance with the law of the equality of the effects and the cause, impart all the motions to other bodies, without one having need to posit in them any other force than the mere velocity. I can add to this that one need not know the living forces at all, not even by name, without this being in the least a hindrance to recognizing that from the force of a hard-springing body, in the onrush against others of the same kind, such and such motions will flow, which each derives from it. Is it not strange, after a geometrical proof in which the force estimated according to the mere velocity has been found sufficient to derive from it a certain magnitude of motion in other bodies, I say after such a proof, still to let the thought enter one's mind that this force is not great enough for it? Does this not mean to retract everything that was once proved in all rigor, and that merely on account of a slight semblance to the contrary? I ask those who read these pages only to hold the Mechanics that I have cited together with this; they can feel nothing other than the greatest conviction: that they have no need at all of a concept of the estimation according to the square in order to find, in all rigor, those consequences and motions that one is accustomed to attribute to hard-springing bodies. So let us not be led away from this footpath by any seductions. For what is found true in a geometrical proof, that will also remain true in eternity.",["- Lehrsätze = \"theorems\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Erkennen (zu erkennen) = \"to recognize\" (glossary: cognize; recognize); Begriff = \"concept\"; Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Folgen = \"consequences\" (Folge = sequel; sequence; here plural rendered \"consequences\").","- \"Herr Regierungs-Rath und Freyherr\" left in German as a title.","- \"die jedweder aus derselben herleitet\": \"which each derives from it\"; \"jedweder\" could refer to each body or each person; left ambiguous.","- Würkung = \"effect\"; Ursache = \"cause\"; Wolf spelled as in the source.","- wiederruffen = \"retract\"; Anscheinung = \"semblance\" (not in glossary); hinlänglich = \"sufficient\" (zureichend is the glossary word; hinlänglich is a different word).","- Mechanik = \"Mechanics\" (title of Wolf's work).","- federharten = \"hard-springing\" (literal compound); Anlaufe = \"onrush.\"","- Beweis = \"proof\" throughout; geometrisch = \"geometrical\"; Größe = \"magnitude.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0217":["§ 41.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0218":["The case of Herr Herrmann on the impact of three elastic bodies.",["- Stoß = \"impact.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0219":["Let us demonstrate in a particular case that which we have proved in general. Herr Herrmann, in the treatise that he had composed in defense of the living forces, lets a body * A, whose mass is 1 and whose velocity is 2, on a perfectly smooth surface, strike a ball B, which is at rest and whose mass is 3, and afterward, when A rebounds from the ball B and returns again with one degree of velocity, a ball C, which has 1 for mass. The ball A will communicate to the ball B one degree of velocity, and to the body C also one, and then it will find itself at rest. Herr Herrmann concludes from this that if the forces were only as the velocities, then A before the impact would have a force as 2, but after the impact there would be found in the bodies B and C together a fourfold force, which seems to him absurd.",["- besonderen = \"particular\" (glossary); Grad = \"degree\"; Masse = \"mass.\"","- Asterisk after \"body\" marks the footnote reference (see following passage, \"Fig. VII.\"), retained in place.","- \"ein Körper * A\": the asterisk stands before \"A\" in the German; kept there.","- ungereimt = \"absurd\" (not in glossary).","- Kräfte \"wie\" the velocities: \"as\" in the sense of proportion, kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0220":["* Fig. VII.",["- Footnote marker and text kept as in the source (reference to a figure)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0221":["We want to investigate how the body A with a force as 2 could bring into the bodies B and C a fourfold force without a miracle, or without its being necessary to call the living forces to help. One may represent the elastic force of the body ** A, which becomes effective through the impact, by the spring AD, and the elasticity of the ball B by the spring DB. We know now from the first grounds of mechanics: that the body A, by means of the springs, brings ever new pressings and forces into the ball B, until B and A move onward with equal velocities, which then happens when the velocity of these bodies relates to the velocity of the ball A before the onrush as the mass A to the sum of both masses A and B together; i.e. in the present case, when they move onward with ½ velocity in the direction BE: No one denies that herein the effect is still found proportional to the force estimated according to the velocity. But let us also investigate what then happens with the springs AD and DB, while the body A acts by means of them into the ball B. Because the spring AD in the point D must apply just as much force against the spring DB as this is to impress on the body B; but the ball B resists the action that happens in it just as strongly, so it is clear: that the spring DB, through the exertion of the other spring, will be compressed with just the same degree of force as it brings into the ball B. Likewise the ball A will compress its spring AD with just the same degree with which this acts in the point D into the spring DB; because namely this spring presses against the spring AD just as strongly as this acts into it, hence also just as strongly as the ball A is endeavoring to compress this its spring. Since now the force with which the spring DB is tensioned is equal to the resistance of the ball B, hence also to the force which this ball thereby receives; but the force of compression of the spring AD is also equal to that: so both are as great as the force that the body B has obtained thereby, i.e. with which it moves with a mass as 3, and ½ degree of velocity. If therefore these two springs spring open; so the spring DB gives to the ball B a velocity that is equal to that before the springing open, namely ½; and the spring AD to the body A, because it has three times less mass than B, also three times as much velocity, namely 1 + ½ degree; for if the forces are equal, the velocities are in inverse ratio of the masses, *per hypothesin*. Thus the ball B has from the onrush of the body A, and afterwards also from the springing open of its spring, together 1 degree of velocity, in the direction BE. The ball A, however, because the velocity ½, which was still left in it after the onrush in the direction AE, must be subtracted from that which the springing open of the spring brought into it in the direction AC, receives also a degree of velocity with which it moves onward in the direction AC, * which is precisely the case that Herr Herrmann held impossible to explain according to the Cartesian law.",["- The signs ** (after \"Körper\") and * (after \"fortbeweget\") are the footnote markers of the original, which attach to passages 0222 and 0223; they are kept as they stand.","- Kraft = force throughout; Würkung/würken (older spelling of Wirkung/wirken) = effect/act; \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\".","- Geschwindigkeit = velocity (not in glossary); Stoß = impact; Anlauf/anlaufend = onrush/(body) rushing on; Zusammenstoß = collision (cf. 0228); Feder = spring; Masse = mass; Grad = degree.","- \"Drückungen\" = \"pressings\", kept literal; \"gespannet\" = \"tensioned\"; \"Aufspringen\" = \"springing open\".","- \"die Würkung der nach der Geschwindigkeit geschätzten Kraft proportional\" is kept as in the German; the construction (effect proportional to the force estimated according to velocity) is not resolved further.","- \"welches gerade der Fall ist ... vor unmöglich gehalten hat\": \"welches\" could refer to the preceding clause or to \"Fall\"; left as in the German.","- \"per hypothesin\" is Latin in the original."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0222":["** Fig. VIII.",["- Footnote text; the marker ** corresponds to that in 0221."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0223":["* I do not mix the body C in here, for since its velocity and mass is in nothing different from the mass and velocity of the ball B, it is inserted by Herr Herrmann without necessity in place of the body B.",["- Footnote text; the marker * corresponds to that in 0221.","- \"ohne Noth\" = \"without necessity\"; \"eingeschoben\" = \"inserted\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0224":["I conclude from this: The body A could, with 2 degrees of velocity, and also with 2 degrees of force, perfectly accomplish the effect that Herr Herrmann has wanted to dispute for it; and one violates the law of the equality of causes and effects if one asserts that it had 4 degrees of force, and yet accomplished only as much as it could have accomplished with 2.",["- \"Ursachen\" = causes; \"behauptet\" = asserts (glossary: behaupten).","- \"ihm abstreiten wollen\": \"wanted to dispute for it\" is kept literal (dative \"ihm\" = to/for the body A)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0225":["§ 42.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0226":["The ground of the error in the inference of Herr Herrmann.",["- \"Schlußrede\" = \"inference\" (Schluß = inference; Rede kept only in the sense of the argued conclusion). In 0228 \"Schluß\" is also \"inference\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0227":["In the moment in which even inelastic bodies collide, there is more force in exercise than there was before the impact.",["- \"stossen\" = \"collide\" (verb of Stoß, \"impact\"); \"Ausübung\" = \"exercise\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0228":["We want, in the inference of Herr Herrmann, to seek out the right point of falsity, which at the same time is found almost everywhere where one has wanted to use only the elastic bodies for the benefit of the living forces. One has thus inferred: the forces of the bodies after the impact must be equal to the force before it; for the effects are as great as the causes that have exhausted themselves in producing them. From this I see that they have taken it that the state and the magnitude of the force, after the impact has happened, is solely and alone an effect of the force that was to be found in the body rushing on before the collision. This is the false step whose consequences we have seen. For the motions which actually, and in a complete way, derive from the force of the body A rushing on are nothing more than that A and B, when the spring was compressed, both moved onward with ½ velocity, the compression of the spring was not so much a particular effect of the force with which A advanced against B, as rather a consequence of the force of inertia of both bodies. For B could not attain the force 1 + ½ without reacting back just as strongly against the pressing spring DB, and the spring AD could thus bring no force into B without the state of equality of the pressure and counterpressure at the same time not having tensioned the spring BD. Further, the body A could not press the spring DB by means of its spring AD without this by that very means having been tensioned with an equal degree of intensity. One must not be astonished that in this way two wholly new forces come into nature that were not previously to be found in A alone. This happens actually at all times when even an inelastic body acts into another, only that in this case the consequences of this new force are not preserved, as with spring-hard bodies, but go lost. For in the moment in which A acts with the force x into B, not only does B receive this force in the direction BE, but B at the same time still acts back again into A with the intensity x. There are thus in the first place 2x present in nature; namely x for the pressure of the ball A against B, and likewise x for the counterpressure of the ball B: Secondly still x, as the force that passes over from A into B in the direction BE. The two first powers are applied in the collision of elastic bodies to tension two springs, which afterwards, when they spring open, communicate their forces to the bodies. The elastic bodies are therefore those machines of nature which are laid out to preserve the whole magnitude of the force that is to be found in nature in the moment of the collision; for without these a part of the forces would go lost that the *conflictus* of the bodies has brought into the world.",["- \"Anlauf/anlaufend\", \"Anstoß\", \"Stoß\", \"Zusammenstoß\" = onrush/rushing on, collision, impact, collision respectively; \"Anstoß\" and \"Zusammenstoß\" are both rendered \"collision\" in places (before \"demselben\" I kept \"impact\" for Stoß).","- \"Gewalten\" = \"powers\" (not Kraft = force); \"Trägheitskraft\" = \"force of inertia\"; \"federharten\" = \"spring-hard\".","- \"ohne daß der Zustand ... nicht zugleich die Feder BD gespannet hätte\": the double negation is kept as in the German.","- \"Würkung ... würket\" = effect/acts; \"Folge\" = consequence here (glossary: sequel/sequence for Folge; \"Folgen\" rendered \"consequences\" in this physical-argument context).","- \"Fehltritt\" = \"false step\"; \"Falschheit\" = \"falsity\"; \"Irrthum\" = \"error\".","- *conflictus* is Latin in the original (roman type)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0229":["§ 43.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0230":["I have, in the solution of the Herrmannian case, said nothing that could have been unknown to this philosopher in the ground of the proof; or that the most distinguished defenders of the living forces would demand to deny, if it came to their having to declare themselves on that account. Herr Herrmann necessarily had to know how one could derive the motions that have arisen in the impact of elastic bodies from their mere velocity; for without this it could not possibly have been known to him *a priori*: that a ball of simple mass, in the impact against a triple one, with two degrees of velocity, produces four degrees of force. I say, this case could not have been known to him himself, without the way of solution that we have given; for everyone knows: that in a mechanical investigation one finds the motions that an elastic body produces through impact by seeking first in particular that which it does without its spring force, and afterwards adding the effect of elasticity to it, but determining both according to that which it can do in proportion to its mass and its plain velocity. One can bring forward nothing stronger, in the kind of argument that one calls an *argumentum ad hominem*, against Herr Herrman and the Leibnizians in general. For they must either confess: that all proofs in which they have hitherto been united, to give the ground of the motions that arise in the impact of elastic bodies, have been false; or they must admit: that such a body has produced the motions solely with the force proportioned simply to mass and velocity taken together, on account of which they believed it to need the square of the velocity.",["- *Beweis* = proof; *Grund* = ground; *Kraft* = force; *Stoß* = impact; *Geschwindigkeit* = velocity; *Masse* = mass; *Wirkung/Würkung* = effect.","- *Auflösung* = solution; *Schlußrede* = \"argument\" (not in the glossary; it contains *Schluß*, elsewhere \"inference\").","- *schlechten Geschwindigkeit* = \"plain velocity\"; *schlechthin* = \"simply\". *bloßen* = \"mere\".","- \"ihn\" in the last clause (*weswegen sie ihn . . . nöthig zu haben glaubten*) is masculine and could refer to *Körper*; rendered \"it\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0231":["§ 44.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0232":["This solution has been unknown to Madame von Chastelet.",["- *Frau* rendered \"Madame\"; the German word order (dative first) is not kept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0233":["I am convinced, by the dispute of Madame the Marquise von Chastelet with Herr von Mairan, that it has not been superfluous to have given now a detailed development of the way and manner in which elastic bodies bring through impact a greater quantity of motion into the world than had been therein before the impact. For when Herr von Mairan says: The elastic force is a true machine of nature, etc. etc. that if one wants to consider all the effects of the impact of elastic bodies in particular, by summing as positive that which they give in the two opposite directions, one must in no way ascribe the new force, which seems to arise from it in nature and manifests itself through the impact, to the activity of the impacting body, as if it merely transferred it into the body impacted, but to a foreign source of force etc. etc. In a word to a certain physical cause of elasticity, whichever it may be, whose efficacy the impact has only set loose, and, so to speak, released the spring etc. etc. I say when Herr von Mairan says this, Madame von Chastelet answers him: it is useless to investigate it, until the author of this opinion has taken the trouble to ground what he here wanted to assert on some proof. I have taken the honor upon myself to undertake this trouble in place of Herr von Mairan, and this is the justification with which I excuse my prolixity in this matter.",["- OCR \"inden beyden\" corrected to \"in den beyden\".","- *überführet* = \"convinced\"; *Meynung* = opinion; *Materie* = matter; *Würksamkeit* = \"efficacy\"; *die Feder abgedrückt* = \"released the spring\" (literally \"pressed off\").","- The quoted speech of Herr von Mairan is unmarked in the German and left so."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0234":["§ 45.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0235":["Herr Jurin's objection concerning the counter-impact of two inelastic and unequal bodies.",["- *Einwurf* = objection; *Gegenstoß* = \"counter-impact\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0236":["An objection has been made to the Leibnizians by Herr Jurin, and others, still: that two inelastic bodies that meet one another with such velocities as are related inversely as their mass nevertheless remain at rest after the impact. Here now, according to the doctrine of the living forces, are two forces, which one can make as unequal as one wants, and which nevertheless keep one another in equilibrium.",["- *Lehre* = doctrine; *lebendige Kräfte* = living forces."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0237":["Herr Bernoulli's refutation of this objection by comparison with the compression of springs.",["- *Zudrückung* = \"compression\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0238":["I find in the physics of Madame von Chastelet an answer to this objection, which, as I see from the citation, has the famous Herr Bernoulli as author. Herr Bernoulli has not been fortunate in finding out a bulwark for his opinion that would have been worthy of his name. He says: that inelastic bodies in one another do through the impression of their parts just the same effect as if they were compressing a spring that was situated between them: Hence he assumes a spring R * that expands at the same time on both sides, and drives bodies of unequal mass from both sides. He proves that the velocities that are communicated to the bodies by this spring are in the reciprocal ratio of their masses, and that therefore, if the balls A and B returned with these velocities, they would put the spring again into the first state of compression. Up to this point everything is correct, and in complete agreement with the theorems of the Cartesians. But let us see how he pursues his inference. The parts of the spring, in that it springs apart, move partly toward the side of A, partly toward the side of B, but the point of division is in R, which divides the spring according to the inverse proportion of the masses A and B. Thus the part RB of the spring R acts into the body B, whose mass is 3, whereas the other part RA communicates its force to the ball A, whose mass is 1. But the forces that are brought into these bodies are related as the number of springs that have applied their pressure to them; consequently the forces of those balls A and B are unequal, although their velocities stand in inverse proportion to their masses. If now the spring R has expanded completely, and the bodies came back toward it with just the same velocities that it communicated to them in springing loose, one sees easily that the one would put the other at rest by means of the compression of the spring. Now their forces are unequal, consequently one cognizes from this how it is possible that two bodies with unequal forces can put one another at rest. From this he makes the application to the collision of the inelastic bodies.",["- \"R *\" retains the footnote marker of the German; the footnote follows as the next item.","- *Naturlehre* = \"physics\" (literally \"doctrine of nature\"); *Eindruck* = impression; *Teil* = part; *Teilung* = division; *Schluß* = inference; *erkennen* = cognize; *Stand* = \"state\".","- *theilet . . . mit* / *mitgetheilet* = \"communicates\" / \"communicated\"; *Anzahl der Federn* = \"number of springs\" (as in the German).","- *Verhältniß* = \"ratio\"; *Proportion* = \"proportion\"; *Lehrsätze* = theorems."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0239":["* Fig. IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0240":["§ 46.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0241":["Herr Bernoulli's thoughts are refuted.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0242":["I do not cognize in this argument Herr Bernoulli, who was accustomed to form his proofs in much more perfect sharpness. It is indisputably certain that the spring springing apart must impart to each of the bodies A and B just as much force as to the other. For it brings as much force into the ball A as the intensity is great with which it braces against the other ball B. If it did not brace against any counter-support at all, it would impart no force at all to the ball A, for then it would spring loose without any effect. Hence this spring cannot apply any force to A without impressing, from the other side, upon the movable ball B just the same degree of violence. The forces of the balls A and B are therefore equal to one another, and not, as Herr Bernoulli has falsely persuaded himself, as the length AR to RB.",["- *erkenne* = \"cognize\"; *Schlußrede* = \"argument\"; *Schärfe* = \"sharpness\"; *Beweise* = proofs.","- *Widerhalt* = \"counter-support\" (doubtful English equivalent); *steifen* = \"brace\".","- *Gewalt* = \"violence\", distinct from *Kraft* = \"force\".","- *überredet* = \"persuaded\" (cf. *Überredung* = persuasion)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0243":["One sees easily how the error in the inference of Herr Bernoulli arose. The proposition on which the Leibnizian party so insists is the source of it: namely, that the force of a body is related as the number of springs that have worked in it. * We have already refuted it above, and the case of Herr Bernoulli confirms our thought.",["- Schluß = \"inference\" (glossary; the glossary's alternative \"conclusion\" is not used here).","- Satz = \"proposition\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Körper = \"body\"; Federn = \"springs\"; Gedanke = \"thought\".","- \"in ihn gewürket haben\": literally \"have worked in it\"; the preposition is kept as in the German.","- The asterisk refers to the footnote translated under 0244."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0244":["* The bodies A and B therefore have equal forces for this reason, because the springs RA and RB have worked in them for equally long; and because the parts of these springs were all stretched equally strongly.",["- Footnote text, marked with * in 0243.","- Theile = \"parts\" (Teil). gespannet = \"stretched\"; the German could also be rendered \"tensioned\"; kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0245":["§ 47.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0246":["The thought of Herr Bernoulli confirms our opinion.",["- Meynung = \"opinion\" (Meinung)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0247":["One cannot perceive without pleasure how excellently this explanation, which one has wanted to make use of in defense of the living forces, serves us as weapons to strike the same down completely instead. For since it is once certain that the spring R imparts equal forces to the bodies whose masses are 1 and 3, § 46, further that the velocity of the ball whose mass is 1 is threefold, and the velocity of the other simple, as the Leibnizians themselves admit; there flow from this two consequences, both of which directly conflict with the living forces. First, that the force that a body receives through the pressure of the springs is not related as the number of the springs that have pushed it forward, but rather as the time of the effect of the same; second, that a body that has a simple mass and a threefold velocity has no more force than another that has three times more masses but possesses only a simple velocity.",["- Erklärung = \"explanation\" (glossary option; \"definition\" not intended). lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\".","- Folgen = \"consequences\" (Folge; the glossary has \"sequel; sequence\"); not Folgerung, which is also \"consequence\" in the glossary.","- Würkung = \"effect\" (Wirkung). wahrnehmen = \"perceive\" (cf. Wahrnehmung).","- Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity\"; Masse = \"mass\" (plural \"masses\" kept as in the German); einfach = \"simple\" (threefold/simple).","- \"derselben\" in \"die Zeit der Würkung derselben\" could refer back to the springs; kept as \"the same\".","- \"dieselbe ... niederzuschlagen\": \"the same\" is feminine and ambiguous between Erklärung and the living forces; kept ambiguous in \"the same\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0248":["§ 48.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0249":["Defense of the living forces through the constant preservation of one and the same magnitude of force in the world.",["- Größe = \"magnitude\"; Welt = \"world\"; \"einerley\" = \"one and the same\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0250":["Up to here we have seen how Leibniz's adherents have made use of the collision of elastic bodies to defend the living forces thereby. But the application of the same was merely mathematical. They have, however, also supposed themselves to find a metaphysical ground in this piece of phoronomy for the benefit of their opinion. Herr von Leibniz himself is the originator of the same, and his authority has lent it no small weight.",["- Grund = \"ground\" (glossary). Phoronomie = \"phoronomy\". Meynung = \"opinion\".","- \"desselben\" (the originator \"of the same\") refers grammatically to the metaphysical ground; \"derselben\" earlier to the collision/use; the pronouns are kept as \"the same\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0251":["He willingly accepted Descartes's principle, that in the world one and the same magnitude of force is always preserved, but only of such a force whose quantity must be estimated according to the square of the velocity. He showed that the old measure of force does not permit this beautiful rule. For if one accepts the same, then the force in nature would diminish or increase unceasingly, depending on how the position of the bodies toward one another is altered. Leibniz believed it was unbecoming to the power and wisdom of GOD that he should be compelled to renew again without cease the motion that he had imparted to his work, as Herr Newton imagined, and this drove him to seek a law through which he could remedy this difficulty.",["- Grundsatz = \"principle\". Maaß = \"measure\". Schätzung/geschätzt = \"estimate(d)\". Gesetz = \"law\".","- \"vermindere oder vermehre\" are subjunctives (indirect speech); rendered \"would diminish or increase\".","- Macht = \"power\" (Kraft is \"force\"); Bewegung = \"motion\"; Werk = \"work\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0252":["§ 49.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0253":["First resolution of this objection.",["- Auflösung = \"resolution\"; Einwurf = \"objection\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0254":["Because we have proved in the foregoing that the living forces, in the way they have been used by their defenders themselves, namely in the mathematical sense, can find a place nowhere; so the power and wisdom of GOD already rescue themselves here through the consideration of the complete impossibility of the matter. We can always hide behind this bulwark, if perhaps in another way of answering this objection we should draw the shorter straw. For even if it were necessary according to the law of motion that we have asserted, that the structure of the world, after a gradual exhaustion of its forces, would finally fall entirely into disorder, this blow can nevertheless not strike the power and wisdom of GOD. For one can never blame the latter for not having brought into the world a law of which we know that it is absolutely impossible, and therefore can have place in no way.",["- Verstand in \"im mathematischen Verstande\" = \"sense\" here (not \"understanding\"); a different German word from Sinn (glossary \"sense\"); doubtful rendering, flagged.","- Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Unmöglichkeit = \"impossibility\"; behaupten = \"assert\"; nothwendig = \"necessary\".","- \"den kürzern ziehen\" idiom, rendered \"draw the shorter straw\".","- \"statt haben\" = \"have place\" kept literal. \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the world\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0255":["§ 50.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0256":["Second answer to the said objection.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0257":["But just recover yourselves. We are not yet compelled to seize upon so desperate an evasion. This would mean to cut off the knot, but we would rather untie it.",["- \"man erhole sich nur\": impersonal imperative, rendered \"just recover yourselves\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0258":["If the Leibnizians hold it for unavoidably necessary to the preservation of the world-machine that the force of bodies be subject to estimation according to the square, we can concede this small demand to them. Everything that I have proved up to now, and intend still to prove up to the conclusion of this chapter, goes only to convince them: that neither in an abstract consideration, nor in nature, will the force of bodies, weighed in such a way as the Leibnizians do it, namely mathematically, yield an estimation according to the square. But I have not on that account yet renounced the living forces entirely. In the third chapter of this treatise I will show that in nature there are actually to be found those forces whose measure is the square of their velocity; only with the restriction that one will never discover them in the way that one has hitherto begun; that they will hide themselves forever from this genus of consideration (namely the mathematical), and that nothing other than some metaphysical investigation, or perhaps a special kind of experiences, can make them known to us. Here we therefore do not properly contest the matter itself, but the *modum cognoscendi*.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (literally \"main piece\"). Beschlusse = \"conclusion\" (not Schluß).","- überzeugen = \"convince\" (cf. Überzeugung \"conviction\"). Erfahrungen = \"experiences\" (plural kept).","- Gattung = \"genus\" (glossary), here in \"this genus of consideration\"; Art = \"way\"/\"kind\".","- erwogen = \"weighed\"; würklich = \"actually\" (Wirklichkeit \"actuality\").","- \"nichts, wie irgend eine ...\": \"nothing other than\" for \"nichts wie\"; the construction is elliptical. \"selbige\" = \"them\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0259":["Accordingly we are in agreement with the Leibnizians on the main point; we could therefore perhaps also become so in the consequences of the same.",["- Folgerungen = \"consequences\" (glossary Folgerung). \"derselben\" is ambiguous (the main point or the Leibnizians); kept as \"the same\".","- \"werden\" with \"es\" = \"become so\"; the elided adjective \"einig\" is supplied by the English \"so\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0260":["§ 51.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0261":["The source of the Leibnizian inference of the preservation of just the same magnitude of force.",["- Schluß = \"inference\". \"Erhaltung\" = \"preservation\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0262":["But the objection of Herr von Leibniz is grounded on a false presupposition, which has long since already brought much inconvenience into philosophy. It has namely become a principle in the doctrine of nature, that no motion in nature arises except by means of a matter that is also in actual motion; and that thus the motion that has been lost in one part of the world can be restored through nothing other than either through another actual motion, or the immediate hand of GOD. This proposition has at all times caused much inconvenience to those who have given assent to it. They have been compelled to weary their imagination with artfully devised vortices, to build one hypothesis upon another, and instead of their finally leading us to such a plan of the structure of the world as is simple and comprehensible enough to derive from it the composite appearances of nature; they confuse us with infinitely many strange motions, which are much more wondrous and incomprehensible than everything is to whose explanation they are supposed to be applied.",["- Weltweisheit = \"philosophy\" (glossary). Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\" (Lehre = \"doctrine\"). Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Satz = \"proposition\".","- Materie = \"matter\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\"; begreiflich/unbegreiflich = \"comprehensible/incomprehensible\".","- Ungelegenheit and Unbequemlichkeit are both rendered \"inconvenience\".","- \"an statt daß ... so verwirren sie\": the anacoluthic construction is preserved in the semicolon.","- Weltgebäude = \"structure of the world\", as Weltbau in 0254."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0263":["How one can remedy this difficulty.",["- Schwierigkeit = \"difficulty\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0264":["Mr. Hamberger has, as far as I know, first offered means of remedying this evil. His thought is beautiful, for it is simple, and thus also in accordance with nature. He shows (but still in a very imperfect sketch) how a body can receive an actual motion through a matter that is itself only at rest. This forestalls countless wrong paths, indeed often even miracles, which are associated with the opposed opinion. It is true, the ground of this thought is metaphysical, and thus also not after the taste of the present natural philosophers; but it is at the same time evident: that the very first sources of the effects of nature must throughout be an object of metaphysics. Mr. Hamberger has not succeeded in his purpose of pointing out to the world a new way that is shorter and more convenient for leading us to the cognition of nature. This field has remained uncultivated; one has not yet been able to tear oneself away from the old way in order to venture onto the new. Is it not wonderful that one entrusts oneself to an immeasurable sea of digressions and arbitrary fictions of the imagination, and on the other hand disregards the means that are simple and comprehensible, but precisely for that reason also the natural ones? But this is already the common plague of human understanding. For a very long time one will still be swept away by this stream. One will amuse oneself with the consideration that is intricate and artificial, and in which the understanding perceives its own strength. One will have a physics that is full of excellent proofs of acuteness and of the power of invention; but no plan of nature itself and of its effects. But in the end that opinion will nevertheless keep the upper hand which depicts nature as it is, that is, simple and without infinite detours. The way of nature is only a single way. One must therefore first have tried countless wrong paths before one can arrive at the one that is the true one.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0265":["The Leibnizians should, more than others, embrace the opinion of Mr. Hamberger. For it is they who assert that a dead pressure, which maintains itself in the body to which it has been communicated, without an insurmountable obstacle annihilating it again, grows into an actual motion. They will therefore also not be able to deny: that a body that adheres to the parts of a fluid surrounding it more in one direction than in the other, then receives an actual motion, if this fluid is of such a kind that it does not annihilate its force again through its resistance. This must convince them of that which I now assert, namely: that a body can receive an actual motion from a matter that is itself at rest.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0266":["Decision of the objection that Mr. von Leibniz makes.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0267":["How, then, shall we evade the blow that Mr. von Leibniz wants to deliver to the Cartesian law through the consideration of the wisdom of GOD? Everything depends on this, that a body can receive an actual motion, also through the effect of a matter that is at rest. On this I base myself. The very first motions in this structure of the world were not produced through the force of a moved matter; for otherwise they would not be the first. But they were also not caused through the immediate power of GOD, or of some intelligence, so long as it is still possible that they could have arisen through the effect of a matter that is in the state of rest; for GOD spares himself as many effects as he can without detriment to the machine of the world, but on the contrary he makes nature as active and effective as is only possible. If now motion has been brought into the world first of all, through the force of a matter in itself dead and unmoved; then it will also maintain itself through the same, and, where it has suffered loss, be able to restore itself. One would therefore have to have a great desire for doubting, if one still wanted to have scruples about believing: that the structure of the world need suffer no diminution, even if in the impact of bodies certain forces that were in them before were lost.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0268":["§ 52.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0269":["According to Leibniz's law the force in the impact of a small elastic body against a larger one is equal before and after the impact.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0270":["I recover again from a digression that has removed me somewhat from the main matter in which I am entangled. I have already noted that the champions of the living forces let themselves think very much of that observation by which they have found: that, if the force of bodies is estimated according to the law of Mr. von Leibniz, in the collision of elastic bodies there would always be found one and the same magnitude of force before and after the impact. This thought, which seems in such a wondrous way to be favorable to the living forces, shall rather become helpful to us in striking them down. Let us conclude as follows: That law according to which, in the collision of a smaller elastic body against a larger one, no more force is found after the impact than before it, is false. Now Leibniz's law is of this kind, Ergo &c. &c.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0271":["§ 53.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0272":["The cited observation of the Leibnizians is wholly opposed to the living forces.",["- 0264: Mittel = means; Betrachtung = consideration; Einbildungs-Kraft = imagination (Kraft, not glossed as \"power\" here); Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Würkungen = effects; Beobachtung (in 0270) = observation.","- 0264: \"Mr. Hamberger ... Vorsatz\" rendered \"purpose\"; Vorsatz is not in the glossary. \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"object\" (Vorwurf, not Gegenstand/Object).","- 0264: \"diesem Uebel\" has no antecedent in the passage; left as \"this evil.\"","- 0264: \"dieselbe\" in the final sentence of the passage's sequence is not at issue; the parenthesis \"(aber noch ... Risse,)\" kept as in the German.","- 0265: behaupten = assert; Kraft = force; Art = kind; \"Druck\" = pressure; \"ihn\" / \"ihm\" in the second sentence are ambiguous in German (body or fluid) and are left as \"it\"/\"its,\" though \"ihm seine Kraft\" most naturally refers to the body.","- 0267: Stoß/Stoße = impact (also Anschlag/Anlauf = collision in 0270); Stoff = material is not used here, \"Stoße\" is \"impact.\" \"Weltgebäude\" = structure of the world; \"Weltmaschine\" = machine of the world; \"an sich\" = in itself; \"Gewalt\" = power.","- 0267: \"Kräfte\" = forces; \"lebendige Kräfte\" = living forces; \"Bewegung\" = motion; \"wirklich (würklich)\" = actual (glossary Wirklichkeit = actuality).","- 0270: \"Gesetz\" = law; \"schließen\" = conclude (cf. Schluß); \"Ergo &c. &c.\" kept in Latin, in roman as printed.","- 0270: \"dieselbe\" (niederzuschlagen) refers to feminine plural \"Kräfte\"; rendered \"them.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0273":["Of the premises of this inference only the major is to be proved. We want to accomplish this in the following way. As the ball A * runs against a larger B, then in the moment in which A exerts the impact and presses shut the spring that we call elasticity, the body B receives no more force than it annihilates in A through its force of inertia, and the body A on the contrary loses no more of its force through the resistance of the mass B, which propagates itself into it by means of the intensity of the spring that it tenses, than it brings into this very ball. If one wanted to deny this, then it would also no longer be certain that the effect transferred into a body is equal to its counter-effect. Thus the spring is tensed, and in both bodies taken together there is present just the same force that previously was to be found in the ball A alone. If these springs of the mutual elasticity now spring loose, they extend themselves against both balls equally strongly. Now it is clear that if A, even after the accomplished pressing shut of the springs in the direction AE, possessed as great a force as that with which the spring belonging to it now springs open; then the springing open of this spring could take just as much force from the ball A as the spring DB on the other side brings into B; and thus, after everything is completed, there would indeed be present in the bodies A and B, as well through the impact as through the elasticity, no more force than was previously in A alone. But it is in vain to presuppose this. When the impact has occurred, and the spring is just pressed shut, A has just as much velocity as B, in the direction AE, but less mass, thus also less force than the spring exerts in its springing loose; for this has a force of tension that is as great as the force of the ball B. From this it follows that elasticity cannot rob as much of the force that is to be found in A as it communicates to the body B. For A does not have so much force, consequently it also cannot be taken from it. Accordingly, through the effect of elasticity a new degree of force must be added in B, without as much going off on the other side for it; indeed, a new force even generates itself in addition likewise in A. For since elasticity found no more force that it could annihilate in A, the ball opposed itself to it with nothing but the force of inertia, and received the degree of violence that the spring still had in itself over the force of the ball A, in order to turn back with it toward C.",["- Schlußrede rendered \"inference\" (Schluß = inference); Vordersätze = \"premises\"; Major kept as \"major\".","- Würkung/Wirkung = \"effect\"; Gegenwürkung = \"counter-effect\"; Kraft = \"force\" throughout; Trägheits-Kraft = \"force of inertia\".","- Stoß = \"impact\"; Feder = \"spring\"; zudrücken = \"press shut\"; Zudrückung = \"pressing shut\"; Aufspringung = \"springing open\"; Losspringung = \"springing loose\".","- \"sie\" in \"kan sie ihm auch nicht genommen werden\" refers grammatically to Kraft; \"diese\" in \"denn diese hat eine Kraft der Spannung\" is grammatically the Feder.","- The asterisk after \"A\" marks the footnote (Fig. VIII); \"die Feder DB\" kept as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0274":["* Fig. VIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0275":["It is thus clear: that in the case in which a small spring-hard body runs against a larger, more force must be present after the impact than before it. Now one would have to posit the opposite, namely: that after the impact only just the same magnitude of force is to be found as before it, if Leibniz's measure of forces were true. Thus we must either deny this law, or renounce all the conviction that has been offered us in this §.",["- Größe = \"magnitude\"; Kräftenmaaß = \"measure of forces\"; Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Gesetz = \"law\".","- \"federharter\" rendered literally as \"spring-hard\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0276":["§ 54.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0277":["The foregoing becomes still clearer if one takes the case in which a larger elastic body impacts a smaller.",["- stößen = \"impacts\" (verb, matching Stoß = \"impact\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0278":["We will be fully convinced of the correctness of that which has now been said if we invert the previous case, and assume that the ball B * of greater mass runs against the smaller A. For here, first, the ball B through the impact against A loses no more nor less force than it just thereby generates in A, (if namely we weigh that alone which occurs before the elasticity brings itself forth). Thus, before the spring force does its effect, the force in these bodies has become neither increased nor smaller. Now the spring force is tensed with that degree with which the body A advances toward C, thus its intensity is smaller than the force that is left over in B in the direction BC, it will thus, when it springs open, never exhaust it, even if it applies its whole violence. And if now therefore the spring that was tensed in the impact springs open, it will indeed bring a new force into the body A, but it will also annihilate just as much in B as it communicates to that ball. Thus also through the spring force the whole force will not become greater; because always on the other side just as much is robbed as comes in on the one.",["- Federkraft = \"spring force\"; Gewalt = \"violence\"; Intensität = \"intensity\"; Grad = \"degree\".","- \"dasjenige allein erwägen\" rendered \"weigh that alone\"; \"sie\" after \"niemals erschöpfen\" refers to the Kraft in B."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0279":["* Fig. VIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0280":["We see from this that solely and alone in the case in which a larger body impacts one of smaller mass, one and the same degree of force is retained in the impact; and that in all other cases, where the elasticity does not find on the one side as much force to annihilate as it generates on the other, the force always becomes greater after the impact than before it; which destroys the Leibnizian law. For in that [law], in all possible cases whatever, just the same magnitude of force always remains in nature, without any loss or increase.",["- \"in demselben\" (last sentence) could refer back to the Leibnizian law (as rendered, with bracket) or to the impact; I kept the law as the evident antecedent.","- Natur = \"nature\"; Abgang = \"loss\"; Vermehrung = \"increase\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0281":["§ 55.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0282":["The calculation confirms that in the case in which a larger body impacts a smaller, according to the Cartesian law just the same magnitude of force remains.",["- \"bestätiget es daß\": the \"es\" is preserved in the German but is not reproducible in English idiom without alteration; omitted as an expletive."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0283":["The Leibnizians should therefore, if they could, set before us a case in which a larger elastic body impacts a smaller, and which contradicted the estimation of Descartes: then no one could object anything to it. For only solely and alone such a case would be decisive and without exception; because in it one is certain always to find after the impact the whole magnitude of force that was before it. But never has any defender of the living forces dared to attack the Cartesian law in this kind of impact; for he would necessarily have perceived without effort: that the mechanical rules agree here quite well with the Cartesian estimation. Assume, e.g.: that the mass of the bodies B is threefold, and A simple, and that B runs against A with 4 degrees of velocity. Then let one argue according to the well-known phoronomic rule: As the difference of the masses A and B is to the sum of them; so is the velocity of the ball B after the impact related to the velocity before it. It has therefore 2 degrees. Further as 2B : A+B; so is the velocity of the ball A after the impact to the velocity that was in B before it. A thus attains 6 degrees of velocity. Consequently, according to Cartesian estimation, the force after the *conflictus* in both bodies together is 12; but before it, it was also 12. And that is what was demanded.",["- Schätzung = \"estimation\" (cf. schätzen, \"to be estimated\" in 0286); Vertheidiger der lebendigen Kräfte = \"defender of the living forces\".","- \"einfach\" rendered \"simple\" (glossary einfach); \"dreyfach\" = \"threefold\".","- \"der Körper B\" printed as a plural genitive (\"the bodies B\"); kept as printed, possibly a slip for \"des Körpers B\".","- \"Conflictu\" is Latin (ablative of *conflictus*); rendered in italics with the English noun \"conflict\" not added.","- \"Unterscheid\" = \"difference\"; \"Summe\" = \"sum\"; \"Phoronomisch\" = \"phoronomic\".","- \"Man argumentire\" is a third-person subjunctive imperative, rendered \"let one argue\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0284":["§ 56.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0285":["The force with which the smaller body rebounds from the larger has the sign minus.",["- \"Zeichen\" = \"sign\"; \"minus\" kept as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0286":["If one wants to measure the quantity of a force, one must pursue it in its effects. But one must beforehand separate from it those phenomena that are indeed combined with the effects, but are no proper sequel of the force that is to be estimated.",["- Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); Verbindung/verbunden = \"combination\"/\"combined\"; Phänomena kept in the Latinate form \"phenomena\" (cf. Erscheinung = \"appearance\").","- Quantität = \"quantity\" (distinct from Größe = \"magnitude\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0287":["If now an elastic body strikes another of greater mass; then we know from the laws of motion that the smaller returns after the blow with a certain degree of force. We have also learned from the last paragraphs that this force, with which the small body rebounds from the larger, is equal to the excess that the exertion of the elasticity made living has over the force of the body A, with which this, before the spring forces of both balls became effective, advanced together with the ball B in the direction AE. Now (according to what has been proved before) as long as the elasticity still encountered in the body A a force that was directed toward AD, which it could annihilate in just the same measure as it brought force into the ball B, I say, as long as that, there was nothing in both bodies taken together that did not contain in itself quite exactly the same quantity of force that had previously been present in A alone, as the cause; consequently the state of both bodies was, so long, to be regarded as a lawful effect of the force that A had before the impact. For the effect is at all times neither greater nor smaller than the cause. But we know further: that, when the spring force has already annihilated all the force that was still left over in A in the direction AE, it brings into both bodies A and B new forces, which therefore come in addition to those that made up the genuine and complete effect of the ball A. We will therefore be able to draw these out again from the motion of both balls in this way: if we take from the body A the force with which it returns after the blow, and also subtract just as much from the force that the ball B has attained: From this it is easy to see: that the force with which a small elastic ball rebounds from a larger one, against which it runs, is of a negating kind, and has the sign minus before it. If, e.g., a ball A runs with 2 degrees of velocity against a ball B of threefold mass; then it rebounds after the impact with one degree of velocity, and gives the ball B also one degree. The force now with which A returns after the impact cannot be added to the force of the ball B, if one wants to have the whole magnitude of the effect that A has exerted. No, it must be taken away from the body A as well as subtracted from the force that is in B. The remainder, which is 2, will be the entirely complete effect that was accomplished through the force of the ball A. Thus a ball that has 2 for mass and 1 for velocity has just the same force as another that possesses a simple mass and a twofold velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0288":["§ 57.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0289":["Madame du Chastelet has joked about this at the wrong time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0290":["Thus it pleased the enlightened Madame Marquise du Chastelet, against Herr von Mairan, to be jocular at the wrong time. She answers him on just the observation that we have now cited: she believed he would not readily make an experiment, and want to place himself in the path of a body that, marked with the sign minus, would strike back with 500 or 1000 degrees of force. I believe it too; and I would be much deceived if I were concerned that Herr von Mairan would undertake to settle the truth in this way. But the matter does not depend on this: that the force which has been marked with the sign minus is not an actual force, as the Madame Marquise seems to conclude from it. Herr von Mairan undoubtedly did not mean to say this by it. It is in fact an actual force, and would also exert actual effects, if one wanted to put it to the test. This alone is indicated thereby: that this force as well as a part in the force of the ball B, which is equal to it, cannot be reckoned to the complete effect of the ball A; but that one must rather regard it as if it were not present in A at all, and on the contrary were still subtracted from B, and that the force remaining after this then first of all properly presents the complete effect of the force that was before the impact. But if one regards a magnitude so; then it counts in the summation for less than nothing, and requires the negating sign.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0291":["§ 58.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0292":["The Leibnizians flee from the investigation of living forces through the impact of inelastic bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0293":["Now my readers will presume to find cited, also from the doctrine of the motion of inelastic bodies through impact, certain proofs of which the adherents of the Leibnizian estimation had made use in order to defend the living forces. But they are deceived. These gentlemen do not find motions of this kind too advantageous for their opinion; they therefore seek to exclude them entirely from this investigation. This is a sickness from which those commonly lie ill who make undertakings in the cognition of truths. They shut their eyes, so to speak, to that which seems to contradict the proposition that they have set in their heads. A small evasion, a frosty and feeble excuse, is capable of satisfying them, when it comes to removing a difficulty that is an obstacle to the opinion for which they are prepossessed. One could have spared us many errors in philosophy, if one had been willing to impose some constraint on oneself in this matter. When one is on the way to drawing in all the grounds that the understanding offers for the confirmation of an opinion that one has set before oneself; then one should, with just the same attention and exertion, endeavor to found the opposite on all kinds of proofs that only anywhere come forward, just as well as one can always do for a favored opinion. One should despise nothing that seems in the least advantageous to the opposite, and push it to the utmost in the defense of it. In such an equilibrium of the understanding, an opinion would often be rejected that would otherwise infallibly have been accepted, and the truth, when it finally came forward, would present itself in a so much greater light of conviction.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0294":["§ 59.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0295":["The impact of inelastic bodies is more decisive with respect to the living forces than the impact of the elastic.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0296":["It has already often been impressed upon the defenders of the living forces: that the motions of inelastic bodies through impact are much more suited to deciding whether the living forces take place or not than the motion of the elastic. For in these the spring force always mixes itself in, and makes the confusions infinite, whereas the motion of the former is determined by nothing but action and counteraction alone. There is no doubt that the Leibnizians would let themselves be convinced by the distinctness of this thought, if only it did not overturn the whole edifice of the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0297":["§ 60.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0298":["The evasion of the Leibnizians with respect to the objection that is made to them from the impact of inelastic bodies.",["- Kraft = force throughout; Würkung/Wirkung (both spellings in text) = effect; Ursache = cause; Zustand = state; Gesetze = laws.","- lebendige Kräfte rendered \"living forces\" (lit.); Schätzung = \"estimation\"; Größe = magnitude; Geschwindigkeit = velocity.","- Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Beweis = proof; Meynung = opinion; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Deutlichkeit = distinctness.","- \"Chastelet\" in 0289 is the source's spelling; rendered \"du Chastelet\" for \"v.\" (von) — the German reads \"Frau v. Chastelet\" (translated as given, not corrected).","- \"Frau Marquisin\" rendered \"Madame Marquise\" literally. In 0290 \"Madame\" corresponds to \"Frau\".","- Ambiguity kept: \"von einer verneinenden Art\" (\"of a negating kind\"); verneinen rendered \"negate\" (not glossary: bejahen = affirm).","- 0290: \"so wohl ... als auch\" = \"as well as\"; \"alsdenn allererst\" = \"then first of all\"; \"eigentlich\" = \"properly\"; \"wie nichts\" = \"than nothing\" (as in source).","- Abprallen rendered \"rebound\"; Anstoß/Stoß/Anlauf = impact/blow/running against (Anstoß and Stoß both \"impact\"/\"blow\" in context); Federkraft = \"spring force\"; Würkung/Gegenwirkung = action/counteraction in 0296 renders Wirkung und Gegenwirkung."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0299":["They have therefore been compelled to take refuge in an exception, which is perhaps the worst that one has ever made use of. They assert, namely: that in the impact of inelastic bodies a part of the force always is lost, in that it is applied to press in the parts of the body. Hence half of the force that an inelastic body has is lost, when it strikes another of equal mass, which is at rest, and is consumed in the pressing in of the parts.",["- Stoß = \"impact\", Kraft = \"force\", eindrücken/Eindrücken = \"press in\"/\"pressing in\", used consistently throughout; Eindrückung is rendered likewise.","- \"behaupten\" = \"assert\" (glossary). \"derselbe\" (\"it\") is left as in the German; its antecedent is the part (Theil)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0300":["§ 61.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0301":["The origin of this erroneous thought.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0302":["This thought has more than one bad side. We want to consider some of them.",["- \"betrachten\" = \"consider\" (cf. Betrachtung = \"consideration\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0303":["At the very first glance it cannot become difficult for us to perceive the source of this error. One knows, partly through experience, partly through the grounds of the doctrine of nature: that a hard body, which in impact alters its figure only very little or not at all, is always elastic, and that on the contrary the parts of inelastic bodies are joined together so that they yield at impact, and are pressed in. Nature has commonly combined these properties together; but in a mathematical consideration we are not compelled to take them together.",["- Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\" (Lehre = \"doctrine\"); Gründe = \"grounds\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\".","- Eigenschaft is rendered \"property\" (the glossary gives \"property\" also for Beschaffenheit)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0304":["The adherents of living forces have become confused by this. They imagine, because in nature an inelastic body commonly has such a structure that its parts yield at impact and are pressed in, that the rules which a purely mathematical consideration of the motion of such bodies offers also cannot subsist without this property. This is the origin of that difficulty which we saw in § 60, and which is entirely without ground, as we shall now learn.",["- The construction \"weil … so können die Regeln … auch nicht bestehen\" is kept as in the German, with its irregular syntax.","- \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\"; \"Grund\" = \"ground\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0305":["§ 62.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0306":["First answer to the exception of the Leibnizians.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0307":["In mathematics one understands by the spring force of a body nothing other than that property through which it repels again another body that runs up against it with just the same degree of force with which this had run up against it. Hence an inelastic body is one that does not have this property.",["- Federkraft = \"spring force\"; Grad = \"degree\"; verstehen = \"understand\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0308":["Mathematics does not trouble itself about the manner and way in which this property manifests itself in nature. With it, it is and remains entirely undetermined; whether elasticity flows from the alteration of figure and a sudden restoration of it, or whether a hidden entelechy, a *qualitas occulta*, or God knows what other sort of cause besides, be the source of it. If one finds elasticity described in the mechanics such that it arises from the pressing in and springing back of the parts of a body, one should note: that the mathematicians who make use of this explanation meddle in that which does not concern them, which contributes nothing to their aim, and which is properly a subject matter of the doctrine of nature.",["- Vorwurf = \"subject matter\" (not Gegenstand/Object); Absicht = \"aim\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\"; unbestimmt = \"undetermined\".","- \"Art und Weise\" = \"manner and way\"; \"vor eine Ursache\" = \"sort of cause\" (vor = für)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0309":["If accordingly the consideration of an inelastic body in mathematics presupposes nothing further than only that it has in itself no force to bounce back again a body that strikes against it, and if this sole determination is that on which the whole chapter on the motion of inelastic bodies is built; then it is absurd to assert: that the rules of these motions are constituted as they are on that account because the pressing in of the parts of the bodies striking one another admits such and no other laws. For in the principles from which one has drawn these laws one finds no trace of the pressing in of the parts. All concepts on which one has built them are so undetermined with regard to this limitation that among the inelastic bodies, without doing damage to that entry, one can count just as well those that do not alter their figure in the impact as those that endure a compression of their parts. If one has now in the construction of these laws paid no heed at all to this pressing in in order to arrange the rules of motion in accordance with it, or has not even laid at the ground such concepts as include this pressing in: then it is indeed very strange to shift onto it the blame for the said laws being constituted as they actually are.",["- Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\".","- \"jenen Eintrag thun\" = \"do damage to that entry\" (literal); \"diejenige\" (singular) stands before the plural relative in the German; \"derselben\" (fem.) in \"derselben gemäß\" is left as ambiguous as the German.","- \"zum Grunde gelegt\" = \"laid at the ground\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0310":["§ 63.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0311":["Second answer. Because one can call a body inelastic even if it is perfectly hard.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0312":["We have said: that in the consideration which mathematics offers us of the motion of inelastic bodies, one can regard these also as perfectly hard, as if their parts were not pressed in by the impact. Nature also offers us examples that it is not precisely that body which is always more inelastic whose parts yield more than the parts of another, but that often a body whose parts, in comparison with another, are almost not at all pressed in by the impact, is nevertheless less elastic than another whose parts yield more easily. For let one let a wooden ball fall down onto the pavement, it will by far not spring back as high as a stuffed one, which can nevertheless be pressed in very easily, and in reckoning against which that one can be called uncommonly hard. From this we see: that even in nature the body is not inelastic on that account because its parts are pressed in, but only on that account because they do not restore themselves with just the degree of force with which they were pressed in. Thus we can also posit bodies whose parts yield infinitely little in the impact, but which at the same time are so constituted that they do not restore themselves from this infinitely small compression either, or where they do so, still only by far not with the degree of velocity with which they were pressed in. As perhaps a wooden ball would do, if one may compare small things with great. Bodies of the kind of which I speak would be perfectly hard * but nevertheless inelastic. One would thus not be able to except them from the laws of the impact of inelastic bodies, and their parts would nevertheless not be pressed in. How would the exception of the Herren Leibnizians subsist here?",["- The asterisk refers to the footnote in the next passage; \"eingedruckt\" (sic, for eingedrückt) is rendered \"pressed in\".","- beschaffen = \"constituted\"; setzen = \"posit\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity\".","- \"gegen welche zu rechnen\" is rendered literally as \"in reckoning against which\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0313":["* For a body that lets itself be pressed in only infinitely little can without an error be called perfectly hard.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0314":["§ 64.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0315":["Third answer: The pressing in of the parts is no ground on account of which in the impact of inelastic bodies a part of the force should go lost.",["- \"verlohren gehen\" = \"go lost\" (verlieren, cf. 0299 \"lost\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0316":["We can still make the Leibnizians a gift of their presupposition; that inelastic bodies always suffer a pressing in of their parts, and it shall nonetheless do us no harm. A body does in another movable one, whose parts it presses in through the impact, just the same effect that it would perhaps exert if between the two there were a spring, which it compressed through the run-up. I can freely make use of this thought, because it is not only plain and convincing, but because it has also been used in just the same case by a great tutelary god of living forces, Herr Bernoulli.",["- Würkung = \"effect\" (Wirkung); überzeugend = \"convincing\" (cf. Überzeugung = \"conviction\"); Anlauf = \"run-up\"; Schutzgott = \"tutelary god\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0317":["If now a sphere A * is moved against another, B, and presses the spring R together in the onrush; then, I say, all the small degrees of the force that are applied to press the spring together pass over into the mass of the body B, and accumulate until they have brought into the said body B the whole force with which the spring has been pressed shut. For the body A loses not a single degree of force, and the spring too is pressed together not by the least part, except insofar as it braces itself against the body B. But it braces itself against this sphere with just the same violence with which it would spring open toward this side if the sphere suddenly gave way, that is: with the force with which A has pressed it shut from the other side, and which this body expends and consumes in its compression. Now it is evident that just the same degree of force with which the spring is endeavoring to extend itself against B, and which the force of inertia of the sphere B resists, must come into this same sphere. Thus B receives the whole force to move itself in the direction BE, which is consumed in A in that it presses the spring R together.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0318":["* Fig. IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0319":["The application is easy to make. For the spring R indicates the parts of the inelastic spheres A and B, which are pressed in by the impact. The body A, then, in that in its impact against B it presses in the parts from both sides, consumes nothing of its force in this impression that the body B does not receive, and with which it moves itself after the impact. Thus no part is lost, much less such a great part as the Leibnitzians falsely pretend.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0320":["§ 65.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0321":["I grow weary of rummaging out all the incorrectnesses and contradictions that are comprehended in this difficulty, which the Leibnitzians have wanted to make for us in the matter of the impact of inelastic bodies. The only one that I still want to adduce could alone be enough to make them useless.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0322":["Fourth Answer. On the proportion of the hardness of inelastic bodies, and the degree of the force of the onrush, which must be determined in the Leibnitzians' exception.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0323":["Even if one granted our opponents everything else, one still cannot forgive the boldness that lies in the demand: that in the impact of inelastic bodies neither more nor less, but just precisely so much, of the force should be consumed through the pressing in of the parts, as they themselves find necessary in each case according to their estimation. It is a temerity that is impossible to digest: that one wants to force us to believe without any proof: a body must, in an impact against an equal one, lose precisely the half, in the impact against a triple one precisely ¾ of the force etc. etc. through the impression of the parts, without anyone being able to give us a ground why exactly so much and no more or less should go to it; for supposing that the concept of an inelastic body necessarily requires some loss of force in the pressing in, I still do not know from what one would then conclude: That this absence of elasticity requires that just so much and no less force must be consumed. The Leibnitzians surely cannot deny that the lesser the firmness of the mass of the inelastic bodies in comparison with the force of the one running against, the more strongly the force will be consumed in the pressing in of the parts, but the harder both bodies are, so much the less must be lost of it; for if they were perfectly hard, no loss of force would take place. A certain determinate relation of the hardness of two equal and inelastic bodies is therefore required for this, if precisely the half of the force of the one running against is to be consumed and annihilated in the impact. And without this proportion more or less would come out, according as one made the bodies colliding softer or harder. Now in the rules of the motion of inelastic bodies, against which the Leibnitzians seek an exception, the degree of firmness, and still more the proportion of it to the strength of the onrush, is wholly undetermined, consequently it cannot be understood from them at all whether an impression of the parts occurs, whether a force will be consumed thereby, and how much of it will be lost, but least of all do they offer any ground from which it could be understood that in the collision of one sphere with another of equal weight precisely the half of the force is lost. For this does not happen without a certain, quite exactly determined relation between the hardness of these bodies and the violence of the collision. Since now no such determination is to be met with in the principles from which the laws of the impact of inelastic bodies are derived, that would contain in itself any ground of a determinate loss of force, the cause on account of which these rules are constituted thus and not otherwise is not to be posited in the pressing in of the parts, which makes precisely so much force lost in each case as the Leibnitzians see fit to cancel.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0324":["Application of our conclusions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0325":["Now that the pretext by which the defenders of the living forces want to evade the blow that all the laws of the impact of inelastic bodies deal them has been found impotent in more than one way; nothing further hinders us from using these laws for the service that they will always perform for us very excellently, namely to clear the living forces away out of the domain of mathematics, into which they have intruded in an unlawful manner.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0326":["§ 66.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0327":["The impact of inelastic bodies wholly cancels the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0328":["But it is superfluous to set out at length here the manner and way in which the motion of inelastic bodies cancels the living forces. Every case one takes does this without the slightest exception or difficulty. E.g. If an inelastic body A strikes another, B, of the same kind and equally heavy, which is at rest; then both move after the impact with ½ degree of the speed that was before the collision. According to the Leibnizian manner of estimation, then, there is in each after the impact carried out ¼ force, and thus all together ½ degree of force, whereas before it a whole degree was present in nature. The half has thus been lost without having done an effect that is equal to it, or also without having suffered a single resistance by which it could somehow have been consumed, which, even according to the admission of our opponents, is one of the greatest absurdities that one can commit.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0329":["§ 67.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0330":["General proof: that the collision of elastic bodies must always be contrary to the living forces.",["- Kraft = force throughout; *lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\" (literal); Trägheits-Kraft = \"force of inertia\".","- Anlauf = \"onrush\"; Stoß / Anstoß / Zusammenstoß = \"impact\" / \"collision\" (Anstoß rendered \"impact\" or \"collision\" by context; Stoß consistently \"impact\"; Zusammenstoß \"collision\").","- Eindruck / Eindrücken / Eindrückung = \"impression\" / \"pressing in\"; zudrücken = \"press shut/together\" (the spring \"zugedrückt\" = \"pressed shut\"; zusammendrücken = \"press together\").","- Grund = \"ground\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Widerstand = \"resistance\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; verstehen = \"understand\"; schließen = \"conclude\"; Schluß (Schlüße) = \"conclusions\".","- Verzehren = \"consume\"; aufheben = \"cancel\" in §66 but \"cancel\" also at end of 0323 (aufzuheben); verlieren / verlohren gehen = \"lose\" / \"be lost\".","- \"Leibnitzianer\" and \"Leibnitzischen\" kept in Kant's spelling as \"Leibnitzians\" / \"Leibnizian\" (the latter normalized once inconsistently; read \"Leibnitzian\").","- Ambiguity kept: \"dem die Trägheits-Kraft der Kugel B widerstehet\" (0323/0317) — \"which the force of inertia of the sphere B resists\" (dem = dative, hence \"resists\"); \"ihnen alle Gesetze ... beybringen\" rendered \"deal them\".","- \"Anlaufende(n)\" = \"the one running against\"; \"Schätzung / Schätzungsart\" = \"estimation\" / \"manner of estimation\"; \"Gebiethe\" = \"domain\" (Gebiet, not Umfang).","- Text breaks off nowhere; \"*\" in 0317 and the footnote 0318 \"Fig. IX.\" kept as in the source; \"Fig. IX\" is the footnote to the asterisk after \"A\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0331":["I will not conclude this section, in which we have refuted the living forces through the collision of bodies, without first having added a general consideration that comprehends in itself everything that one will ever be able to say against the living forces in this way. In it I will demonstrate: that, even if one wanted to grant the Leibnizians their estimation of forces, it would still be entirely contrary to the nature of the matter to want to prove it from the collision of bodies, and that this would never offer, or even could offer, any other measure than the mere velocity, even if the estimation according to the square were an entirely true and undoubted matter. It is impossible, I say, that it should be able to be cognized from the collision of bodies, even though it may otherwise show itself in a thousand other cases as manifestly as one could ever wish.",["- *Kräften-Schätzung* rendered \"estimation of forces\"; *schlechte Geschwindigkeit* rendered \"mere velocity\" (cf. *bloße Geschwindigkeit*, also \"mere\"); \"it\" in \"prove it\", \"it should be able to be cognized\" follows the German *selbige*/*sie* (feminine; antecedent left open between *Kräften-Schätzung* and *Schätzung*).","- *erkannt* = \"cognized\"; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration\"; *Zusammenstoß* = \"collision\"; *Quadrat* = \"square\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0332":["§ 68.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0333":["Execution of this proof.",["- *Ausführung* = \"execution\"; *Beweis* = \"proof\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0334":["My proof rests on the following.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0335":["One is agreed in this: that one cannot make use of the motion of bodies through impact, for the final end of which we are speaking, in any other way than by regarding the force that a moved body brings into others through impact as the effect with which one must measure the quantity of the cause that has exhausted itself in bringing it forth. That is, one must seek the magnitude of the cause in the effects that are a sequel of it. It thus already goes without saying: that one has here in particular to take good care that in the bodies impacted one takes only that force which is actually nothing other than the effect immediately brought forth by the onset of the other body; for otherwise the whole measure that one has sought is deceptive and useless. But it is evident: that immediately after the moment in which the impacting body has exercised its effect in the impacted one, all the force that is then found in the latter is an undoubted effect of the impact. Hence one must necessarily make use of it and of no other in order to make it the measure of the force that the onrushing body has expended in bringing it forth. Now a body that receives its motion through the impact of another has, immediately after the moment in which the impact has brought the force into it, and when it has thus not yet been able to remove itself a finite distance from the contact of the impacting one, indeed already all the force that the latter was able to communicate to it, but as yet no actual motion, because no time has been left to it for that, but only a mere endeavor toward it, hence a force that is dead, and has mere velocity for its measure. Thus the force that was found in the impacting body has exhausted itself in order to awaken in the other a force whose entirely exact estimation can never be anything other than the mere velocity, even if one wanted by a hypothesis to posit in the impacting one a force that had as measure, I will not say the square, but even the cube, the square-squared, and who knows what further powers of velocity.",["- *Würkung/Wirkung* = \"effect\"; *Folge* = \"sequel\"; *Größe* = \"magnitude\"; *Kraft* = \"force\"; *setzen* = \"posit\"; *Schätzung* = \"estimation\"; *Maaß* = \"measure\".","- *Stoß* = \"impact\", *Anstoß* = \"impact\", *Anlauf/anlaufend* = \"onset/onrushing\"; *stoßend/anstoßend* both \"impacting\"; *Zusammenstoß* = \"collision\" elsewhere.","- *Bemühung* = \"endeavor\"; *Endzweck* = \"final end\"; *Quadratoquadrat* = \"square-squared\".","- \"indeed already all the force\": *zwar schon . . . allein noch keine* kept as a concessive pair; the sentence's main verb *hat* is far from its subject, as in the German.","- *wie die Würkung ansieht*: \"as\" renders *wie*; \"the latter\" = *diesem*, \"the former\" not used."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0336":["Now it would be an absurdity that would completely overturn the law of the equality of the effect and the cause, if one wanted to posit that a force that requires estimation according to the square were expended to bring forth another that would be estimated according to the velocity alone. For since the former is infinitely many times greater than the latter, it would be just as much as if one wanted to say that the whole content of a square had been expended to bring forth a line, and indeed a finite line. Hence it is clear that all laws, of elastic as well as of unelastic bodies, will never offer a proof of any other estimation than that of the mere velocity, and that by their very nature they must always be contrary to the living forces, one may exhaust all one's power of invention in devising cases that have the look of being favorable to them.",["- *Inhalt* = \"content\"; *Gesetz* = \"law\"; *Beweis* = \"proof\"; *Erfindungs-Kraft* = \"power of invention\" (*Kraft* here \"power\", not \"force\").","- *jene . . . diese* = \"the former . . . the latter\"; *ihnen* in the last clause could refer to *lebendigen Kräften* or to the laws; I read the former, the German is open."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0337":["§ 69.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0338":["Because in the preceding § everything depends on one's taking as the measure of the force of the onrushing body only that force of the body impacted onward that is to be met with in it immediately after the moment of the communicated effect, and just as it detaches itself from the contact of the impacting one, but nevertheless before this motion has yet actually occurred, I do not doubt that this will be the point against which the gentlemen whom I now have the honor to call my opponents will rebel the most: I wish that I were so fortunate as to anticipate them with the following.",["- *fortgestoßenen* = \"impacted onward\"; *Wirkung* = \"effect\"; *Berührung* = \"contact\"; *Punkt* = \"point\".","- *dieses* (\"this will be the point\") and *diese Bewegung* kept as in German; the colon before *Ich wollte* kept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0339":["Continued proof that in the impact of bodies one has to weigh nothing but the initial velocity of the impacted one.",["- *erwegen* (= *erwägen*) = \"weigh\"; *Anfangs-Geschwindigkeit* = \"initial velocity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0340":["Either the force that the impacted body has in the moment before it removes itself from the impacting one is equal to that force which it has after it has already actually moved and has withdrawn from it, or it is not equal to it. If the first, there is not even need of my restriction, but one can take the force of the impacted body in whichever moment of the motion one wishes, but one will find it everywhere simply in accord with the velocity, * because it is equal to that which it had before its motion was actual. If it is not equal to it, then one wants by this infallibly to say as much as: that the force found in the impacted body, after it has already removed itself from the impacting one, is greater than it was in the contact. But if this is so, I confess that this is precisely the reason why I cannot make use of it in order to estimate the force of the onset according to it. For if in the impacted body, since it has already removed itself from the onrushing one after the impact, there is one degree of force more than there was in it as long as it still touched the latter: then this new degree of force is also no effect of the onrushing body, for bodies act on one another only as long as they touch one another; but the former alone is. Hence one can also most suitably use the latter to measure that force which has consumed itself in order to bring it forth.",["- *Grad* = \"degree\"; *Berührung/berühren* = \"contact/touch\"; *Anlauf* = \"onset\"; *Einschränkung* = \"restriction\".","- \"the former alone is\" (*der erstere ist es allein*) and \"the latter\" (*jene*): the referents are left as ambiguous as in the German (the former apparently the body itself, as against the onrushing body); the asterisk marks the footnote that follows.","- *würken* here \"act\" (verb), cognate of *Würkung* \"effect\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0341":["* For as long as the motion of the impacted body has not yet become actual (as long, namely, as it has not yet removed itself from the impacting one), so long is its force, even according to the admission of the Leibnizians, still dead.",["- *Geständniß* = \"admission\"; *todt* = \"dead\"; *würklich* = \"actual\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0342":["§ 70.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0343":["We have happily surmounted the difficulties that the collision of bodies could have made for the old law of Descartes. I imagine that I can now boldly say that the party of Herr von Leibniz will win nothing from it on this side. We will endeavor that we may be able to boast of this also with regard to the rest.",["- *Gesetz* = \"law\"; *Parthey* = \"party\"; \"from it\" renders *ihm*, which could refer to the law of Descartes (or to Descartes); I took the law."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0344":["§ 71.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0345":["On the defense of the living forces through the composition of motion.",["- *Vertheidigung* = \"defense\"; *Zusammensetzung* = \"composition\"; *Bewegung* = \"motion\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0346":["Let us now take into consideration those cases which the defenders of the living forces have borrowed from the composite motions of bodies for the confirmation of their estimation. Just as a bad cause always has the mark about it: that it likes to hide itself behind obscure and entangled cases; so too the party of the living forces has wanted to make use of the confusion into which one can easily fall in the consideration of composite motions. We want to endeavor to pull off from it the cover of obscurity, which up to now has been favorable solely and alone to the living forces. Herr Bülfinger has deserved best of this way of proofs, and his thoughts shall therefore be the first that we want to put to the test.",["- Kräfte = forces; Merkmahl = mark (*nota*); Betrachtung = consideration; Beweise = proofs; Art = way.","- \"Schätzung\" rendered \"estimation\" (not in glossary); \"Parthey\" = \"party\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0347":["We find his treatise in the first volume of the *Commentarii Petropolitani*. The proposition that lies at the ground of his whole edifice is the following. * A body A, which receives two motions at the same time, one in the direction AB with the velocity AB, and another, in a direction that is combined perpendicularly with the previous one, with the velocity AC, moves through the diagonal line of this rectangular parallelogram in just the same time in which it would run through each one of the sides in particular. But the forces directed along the sides of the parallelogram are not posited opposed to one another, hence the one also cannot withdraw anything from the other, and thus the force that the body has, when it yields to both, namely, when it moves in the diagonal line, will be equal to the forces along the sides taken together. Now according to Descartes's estimation this would not take place. For the diagonal line AD is always smaller than the two sides AB and AC taken together; but also in all other possible estimations, the force that the body has with the velocity AD would never be equal to the sum of the forces with the velocities AB and AC, except in the single case in which they are estimated according to the squares of their velocities. From this Herr Bülfinger concludes: the force of a body that is in actual motion can be measured by nothing other than the square of its velocity.",["- \"Commentarii Petropolitani\" is in roman type in the original, italicized here. \"entgegen gesetzt\" rendered \"posited opposed\" (setzen = posit).","- \"würklich\" = actual; \"schließt\" = concludes; \"Schätzung\" = estimation; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = velocity; \"Gebäude\" = edifice.","- \"nicht ... einander entgegen gesetzt\": the German \"wird ... gleich seyn\" is kept as a future."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0348":["* Fig. X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0349":["§ 72.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0350":["Herr Bülfinger has not wholly erred in his proof. His inferences are in the ground of the matter completely correct; but the application of them is properly only faulty, and has the mark of a hasty judgment about it.",["- Schlüße = inferences; Urtheil = judgment; Merkmahl = mark (*nota*).","- \"im Grunde der Sache\" rendered literally; \"Grund\" = ground."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0351":["In what understanding the Bülfinger proof is correct.",["- \"Verstande\" here is the noun Verstand, rendered \"understanding\" per glossary, though the sense is idiomatic (\"in what respect\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0352":["If one regards the motion that the body has along * the side AC as is customary, namely: that the body with it is endeavoring to strike the surface CD perpendicularly, then it is certain: that the other lateral motion in the line AB is in this aim not at all opposed to it, because it runs parallel with the surface CD, consequently draws the body neither toward it nor away from it. Just so the lateral motion AC will not at all be opposed to the motion in the other side AB, in aim to the effect that the body is endeavoring to do with it against the surface BD, because it likewise runs parallel with this surface. But what follows from this? Nothing further than that the body, when it yields to these two lateral motions at once, and runs through the diagonal line, will exert against the surfaces CD and BD just the effects at once that it would have done in separate motion through the sides. The body thus has in the motion through the diagonal line, with respect to the two surfaces CD and BD, a force in itself that is equal to the sum of both forces along the sides. But this equality is to be encountered in it only under this condition that I have said.",["- \"Absicht\" = aim (in \"in dieser Absicht\", \"in Absicht auf\"); \"Würkung\" = effect; \"Bedingung\" = condition.","- \"Fläche\" = surface; \"Seitenbewegung\" = lateral motion.","- \"Fig. X\" footnote marker kept in place."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0353":["* Fig. X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0354":["§ 73.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0355":["Herr Bülfinger has inferred beyond the sense of the point in dispute.",["- \"Sinn\" = sense; \"Streitfrage\" = \"point in dispute\" (literally \"question in dispute\"); \"hinausgeschlossen\" = \"inferred beyond\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0356":["Herr Bülfinger did not bind himself to this condition; notwithstanding that he should have found himself compelled to it by the nature of his proof. He inferred straight off: Therefore the body in the motion through the diagonal line has a force in itself that is equal to the sum of both lateral forces.",["- \"Bedingung\" = condition; \"schloß\" = inferred (schließen)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0357":["This proposition, put forward so unrestrictedly, ordinarily assumes a meaning that is far removed from the sense of the conclusion in the Bülfinger proof. For when one says: a body that possesses this or that velocity has this or that force in itself; one understands thereby the force that it would exert in the straight direction of its motion, and on an object that it strikes perpendicularly. One must therefore, when the talk is of the force of a body in such a restricted way, seek to determine its magnitude in no other meaning than this, otherwise one believes: the body has in the straight direction of its motion a certain force in itself, which it nevertheless can exert only to the side at a certain position of the object that it strikes. Herr Bülfinger, who has left this out of account, has thereby been exposed to the accusation of a *fallacia ignorationis elenchi*. For he has abandoned the sense of the point in dispute, and instead of having had to prove: the body will in the motion through the diagonal line strike an object that is set opposed perpendicularly to the direction of this its motion with a force that is equal to the sum of the forces with which it would strike the surfaces lying in its way through the separate lateral motions: he proved that it does indeed exert the aggregate of these forces, but only against the two lateral surfaces CD and BD, and not against the perpendicular surface set straight opposed to its motion.",["- \"Satz\" = proposition; \"Bedeutung\" = meaning; \"Sinn\" = sense; \"Schlußfolge\" = conclusion; \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Größe\" = magnitude; \"Lage\" = position.","- \"verstehen\" = understand; \"bestimmen\" = determine; \"Acht gelassen\" = \"left out of account\".","- \"fallaciae ignorationis elenchi\" is Latin in the original (genitive); given here in the nominative, italicized.","- \"bewieß\" follows the German past tense; the construction \"an statt, daß er hätte beweisen sollen\" kept literally."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0358":["§ 74.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0359":["Just the same proof is faulty with respect to the point about which there is dispute.",["- \"Absicht\" = \"respect\" here (elsewhere \"aim\"); \"warum gestritten wird\" kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0360":["Everything therefore comes only to this, that I prove: a body moved in the diagonal line AD does not have in itself, in the straight direction AD, the sum of the lateral forces together. I need for this nothing further than that I regard each one of the lateral motions as composite, as the mathematicians are accustomed to do. * The lateral motion AB shall accordingly be composed of the motion AF and AH, the lateral motion AC, on the contrary, of the motions AE and AG. Since now both the motion AF and also AE directly conflict with one another, hence since they are equal, also cancel each other; so there remain only the motion with the velocity AH, and that with the velocity AG, with which the body continues on in the direction of the diagonal line; and thus the whole force of the two lateral motions is not present in the direction of the diagonal line, but in this respect only a part of it is to be encountered. Further, since the motions AF and AE run in any case parallel with the surface BH, which the body in the diagonal motion strikes perpendicularly, hence neither of the two can hit it, one sees from this as well as from the preceding that the body will not strike the object set perpendicularly opposed to its motion through AD with the sum of the forces along the sides AC and AB.",["- \"Absicht\" in \"in dieser Absicht\" rendered \"in this respect\" (elsewhere \"aim\"); \"Theil\" = part; \"Kraft\" = force.","- \"Bewegung AF und AH\": German singular \"Bewegung\" kept with the plural referents.","- \"aufheben\" = cancel; \"widerstreiten\" = conflict (not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0361":["* Fig. XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0362":["§ 75.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0363":["Conclusion from this.",["- \"Schluß\" = conclusion (glossary: inference; conclusion)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0364":["Everything is now settled. For now we know: that a body in the motion through the diagonal line against a perpendicularly opposing obstacle does not exert the whole sum of both lateral forces, which the body possesses with each of its lateral motions, against the surfaces likewise perpendicularly opposed to them. From this it follows necessarily: the force in the motion through the diagonal line would be smaller than both lateral forces taken together; consequently the force of a body could not be estimated according to the square of its velocity: for in this way of estimating the said equality would necessarily have to be encountered, which in fact is not to be encountered.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0365":["§ 76.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0366":["From the Bülfingerian case the living forces themselves are refuted.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0367":["We do not want to be content with this. Instead of fearing the conclusions of Herr Bülfinger, we would rather seize them willingly, in order to prove Descartes's laws by means of them. A good cause always has this mark about it: that even the weapons of its opponents must serve for its defense, and we have seen more than once that ours too can boast of this advantage. * The lateral motion AB, according to what has now been proved, brings into the direction of the diagonal line no other velocity than only the velocity AH, with which the body in separated motion would strike the surface BH perpendicularly. Further, the other lateral motion AC by itself brings into the direction of the diagonal line only the velocity AG, with which the body would strike the surface CG perpendicularly. From the forces which these two motions AH and AG carry with them, the whole force of the diagonal line is now composed, and what is therefore not to be encountered in those two will not be present in this one either; for otherwise more could be contained in the sum than in the *summandis* together. The force with the velocity AD is therefore to be equal to the force with the velocity AH, plus the force with the velocity AG; and it is asked what powers of AH, of AG, and of AD one must take, so that the sum of the first two be equal to the latter. Here it is clear from the easiest grounds of arithmetic that if one wanted to estimate the forces by a power of the lines AH, AG, and AD that is greater than the first power, the force of the body estimated in this way, with the velocity AD, would be greater than the sum of the forces with the velocities AH and AG; but if one wanted to take a smaller function (as Herr Bülfinger expresses himself) than the function of the simple velocities, the aggregate of the partial forces would be greater than the whole force arising from them, which has the velocity AD as its mark; on the contrary they will be found equal if everything together is estimated according to the mere velocity. From this it follows: one must either posit the forces in proportion to the velocities AH, AG, and AD, or concede: that the aggregate could be smaller, or greater, than the *aggregandi* together.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0368":["* Fig. XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0369":["§ 77.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0370":["The very same refutation: in another way.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0371":["We can demonstrate the very same thing also in another way. We assume, like Herr Bülfinger: that the lateral forces ** AB and AC are communicated to the body a, through the impact of two equal spheres, with the velocities ba=AB, and ca=AC, and that these two impulses occurring at the same time occasion the motion and force through the diagonal line. But we want, since it is all one, to assume: that these spheres ran out from C and B, and struck the body a at the point D, with the velocities CD=ba, and BD=ca. It is undeniable that the body a in this place will receive from the said spheres just the force that it could receive at the point A; for the place makes no difference at all, since everything else is otherwise equal. It is asked, therefore: what force will the sphere a at the point D receive, from these two impacts, BD and CD, occurring in it at the same time, against the perpendicular surface FE? I answer: the sphere B will impart to the body a with the motion BD, properly only the velocity BE, with regard to the effect on this surface, and from the impact of the sphere C, with the velocity CD, the same body A will attain only the velocity CF, with which it can act at the point D on the surface FE. For the other two motions, Bg and Ch, which a has still received from this twofold impact, go parallel with the surface, consequently they do not strike it, but rather annihilate one another, because they are opposed to one another and equal. Both lateral forces BD and CD, or, which is just as much, AC and AB, have therefore imparted to the body, with regard to the surface that it strikes perpendicularly in the diagonal motion, only such a force as is equal to the sum of the forces with the velocities BE and CF; consequently, first, not their whole forces, second, such a force of which it is just as evident here as in the previous § that it must relate to those from which it is composed as the velocity AD to the velocities CF and BE, and not as the squares of them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0372":["** Tab. II. Fig. XII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0373":["§ 78.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0374":["The straight force in the diagonal line is not equal to the sum of the forces along the sides.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0375":["We see from the consideration so far that if one presupposes that the forces exerted along the sides of the parallelogram in the diagonal motion were together equal to the force in the direction of the diagonal line, it follows from this: that one must estimate the forces according to the squares of the velocity. But we have at the same time proved: that this presupposition is false, and that those effects which a body exerts in oblique motion, until all its force in it is exhausted, are always greater than what it would accomplish through a perpendicular impact.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0376":["This observation has the appearance of a paradoxical proposition. For it follows from this that a body could, in regard to certain surfaces opposing it in a particular way, exert more force than one presupposes that it has in itself at all. For a body is said to have as much force as it expends through a perpendicular impact against an insuperable obstacle.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0377":["Regarding the metaphysical resolution of this difficulty we may be quite unconcerned, for however it may be constituted, mathematics nonetheless pronounces its verdict, and according to its judgment one can doubt no longer.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0378":["§ 79.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0379":["In the Leibnizian estimation of forces the sum of the forces exerted in an oblique direction is equal to the diagonal force; but in the Cartesian one the former is often infinitely many times greater than the latter.",["- 0364: Kräfte/Kraft = force(s); Geschwindigkeit = velocity; schätzen = estimate; Vorwurf = obstacle (literally \"object thrown against\"; not Gegenstand/Object).","- 0367: Merkmahl = mark (nota); Summandis, Aggregandi left in Latin (roman type in original), italicized; Potenz = power; Function left as \"function\".","- 0367: \"ergreifen\" = seize; \"Schlüße\" = conclusions (Schluß).","- 0367: \"schlechten Geschwindigkeiten\" rendered \"simple Geschwindigkeiten\" = \"simple velocities\" (schlecht = plain); not einfach.","- 0371: \"Kugel a\" and \"Körper A\" are as printed (a/A inconsistent in the German); \"Würkung\" = effect (Wirkung); \"Absicht\" = regard (glossary: aim/intention; here in the phrase \"in Absicht auf\").","- 0371: \"Ort\"/\"Orte\" = place; \"Punct\" = point; \"vernichten\" = annihilate.","- 0371: \"Stoß/Stösse\" = impact; \"Antriebe\" = impulses; \"Anlauf\" = impact (literally \"running against\").","- 0375–0376: \"Ansehen\" = appearance (not Erscheinung/Schein); \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"Voraussetzung\" = presupposition.","- 0377: \"Urtheil\" = judgment; \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Ausspruch\" = verdict.","- 0368, 0372: the asterisks are Kant's footnote markers, retained as in the text; the footnote texts are figure references only."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0380":["From the division of the motion it is clear that, if a body runs up one after another against many surfaces in an oblique direction, it then loses its motion entirely when the sum of the squares of all the *sinuum angulorum incidentiae* is equal to the square of the *sinus totius*, which indicates the first velocity of its motion. Up to this point all mechanicians are agreed, the Cartesians not excepted. But from this there follows especially for the Leibnitzians: that the body, if one lets the estimation by the square take place, has then lost all its motion when the forces exerted in oblique direction are all together equal to the force that is inherent in it in straight motion. On the other hand, according to the Cartesian estimation matters stand quite differently in this regard. The forces that the body exerts through many impacts following one after another in oblique direction, until all its motion is consumed, are according to it together much greater than the single undivided force that it possesses in straight motion. Thus the body has then not yet lost its motion when the sum of all the forces exerted in divided motion is already equal to its whole undivided force. For a body can accomplish far more with respect to many oblique surfaces than against the one that it strikes perpendicularly in straight direction, and indeed in such a way: that (if one assumes that the inclination of the impact occurs on all oblique surfaces at equal angles) the magnitude of the force that is necessary in order to consume a body's force through obliquely opposed obstacles stands to that which would cancel the same in straight direction as the *sinus totus* to the *sinus* of the angle of incidence. It is thus, e.g., when the *sinus totus* is to the *sinus anguli incidentiae* as 2 : 1, also twice as great as this, when it is as 8 : 1, eight times, and when this is infinitely small, also infinitely many times greater than the violence of the obstacles that would have been enough to consume its whole motion for it in straight opposed direction. Thus according to the Leibnitzian estimation a certain obstacle takes from a body its force entirely, which, however, according to the estimation of Cartesius the same obstacle in the same direction is able to annihilate only infinitely little, i.e. with the estimation by the square the loss of force of the moved body, if the whole violence of the summed obstacles that it has overcome is finite, is also finite, the body may have overcome these obstacles in as oblique a motion as one likes; on the other hand with the estimation by the velocities, the total force of the exerted effects of a body can be finite, and the loss of force of the body nevertheless infinitely small, if only the angle at which it overcomes all these obstacles is infinitely small.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0381":["This difference is astonishing. An effect of it must show itself somewhere in nature, let it be wherever it may, and it will be worth the trouble to seek it out. For the sequel of it will be not only this: that one could decide whether the force of a body in the diagonal line of a right-angled parallelogram is equal to the sum of the lateral forces or not, but also whether the estimation of Herr v. Leibnitz or that of Cartesius is the true one; for the one question is inseparably combined with the other.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0382":["§ 80.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0383":["The living forces are refuted by a new case.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0384":["The motion of a body in a circular line around a center toward which it is drawn by its gravity (of which kind the motions of the planets are) is the case that we are seeking.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0385":["Let us assume a body that had received a sufficient centrifugal impetus to run around the earth in a circular line. Let us also abstract from all obstacles besides gravity that could diminish its motion; then it is certain: that first the velocity of its motion will be finite, then second will continue with just the same degree, in just the same line, undiminished to infinity. These two *Lehnsätze* I lay down as the basis, for they are approved by both parties, the Leibnitzian as well as the Cartesian. I lay down further, third, as the basis, that gravity brings into a body that moves freely in a finite time a finite force, or also consumes it in the same, when the two forces, that which is inherent in the body and that with which gravity presses, work against one another. Now the assumed body, which runs in a circle around the given center, is unceasingly exposed to the pressure of gravity, and thus suffers through the sum of all infinitely small gravity-pressures in a finite time a finite force, with which it is driven toward the center of its revolution, *per Lemma 3*. Meanwhile the body, through its own peculiar force, holds the balance against all these pressures made upon it, in that it always maintains itself at just the same distance from the center. Thus it has in any finite time also exerted a finite force with respect to the overcome obstacles of gravity. Now it is clear from what we have seen in § 79 that: if a body in oblique direction has overcome a certain number of obstacles, which together amount to a finite magnitude of force, it must hereby at the same time (if one admits the Leibnitzian estimation) suffer a loss of a finite magnitude in its inherent force. Consequently the assumed body loses in any finite time of its circular course through the restraints of gravity a finite force, and thus in a certain determinate time its whole force and velocity; for the velocity that it possesses in its circular course is only finite. *Lemma 1*.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0386":["It can thus either not run in a circle at all, unless it has an infinite velocity, or one must admit: that a body through the sum of all oblique effects can here accomplish infinitely much more than it possesses force in straight running up, and that the Leibnitzian measure of forces, which does not admit this, is false.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0387":["§ 81.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0388":["Because the thought that we have carried out here is very fruitful in sequels, we want to clear away all small difficulties around it, and to make it, as far as is possible, clear and even.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0389":["He knows: that a body running in a circle exerts against gravity just such an effect as if it ran up against an oblique surface.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0390":["One must first learn to comprehend distinctly: that the force that the moved body applies in the circular motion to hold the balance against gravity exerts an oblique effect, and is to be compared with the running up of a body against an oblique surface, just as we have actually done in the previous §.",["- Schätzung = \"estimation\" throughout (also Kräftenmaaß = \"measure of forces\"); Würkung/Wirkung = \"effect\"; Kraft = \"force\" (glossary); Gewalt = \"violence\" (not in glossary).","- 0380: \"Zertheilung\" and \"zertheilt\" rendered \"division\"/\"divided\"; Teilung/Einteilung is \"division\" in the glossary. \"Folgerung\"/\"folget\" rendered \"follows.\"","- 0381: Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); \"Sinui\" (sic) kept as Latin dative/ablative form in \"sinui\"; \"Sinus\" in italic Latin as printed.","- 0381: \"unzertrennlich verbunden\" = \"inseparably combined\"; Verbindung is \"combination\" in the glossary.","- 0385: *Lehnsätze* left in German as a technical term (= lemmas); the text then cites \"Lemma 3\" and \"Lemma 1\" in Latin.","- 0385: \"Zeit\" = \"time\"; \"Hinderniß\" = \"obstacle\"; \"ins unendliche\" = \"to infinity.\"","- 0389: the subject \"Er\" is as in the German with no antecedent; the sentence may be a corrupted reading (a \"Man\" or \"Es\" would be expected), and I have not emended it.","- 0390: \"begreifen\" = \"comprehend\" (glossary); \"deutlich\" = \"distinctly\"; \"§\" kept for the section sign."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0391":["For this end let one represent to oneself the infinitely small arcs that the body runs through in its circular motion as so many infinitely small straight lines, just as one also in mathematics commonly regards the circle as a polygon of infinitely many sides. * The body that has now run through the infinitely small line ab would, if gravity set no obstacle against it, continue the straight direction of this motion, and be in d in the second infinitely small part of time. But through the resistance of gravity it is compelled to leave this direction, and to describe the infinitely small line bc. This obstacle of gravity has taken from it, *per resolutionem virium*, thus the lateral motion ac, which is expressed by the perpendicular line ac, which has been dropped onto the line bd extended to c. The body therefore suffers through the obstacle of gravity at the point b just the same resistance that it would have suffered from a surface cd, against which it would have run up, under the angle abc; for the obstacle that this surface sets against it is expressed, just as here, by the small perpendicular line ac. Thus one can quite well compare the force that a body exerts in its circular motion against gravity, which pulls it down, with its running up against oblique surfaces, and also estimate it in just the same way as this. Q. E. D.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0392":["* Fig. XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0393":["§ 82.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0394":["The body running in a circle does, in any finite time whatever, against the obstacles of gravity an effect of a finite force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0395":["The third of the assumed principles of our proof in the 80th §, seems secondly still to need some confirmation; at least, when one has to do with such opponents, one cannot be cautious enough even with regard to the most evident truths, for the dispute over the living forces has sufficiently convinced us how much partiality with regard to certain opinions can be more powerful and more captivating than the bare strength of truth, and how far the freedom of the human understanding extends, to doubt still, or to postpone its judgment, in the case of the most evident truths.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0396":["I could appeal, on account of the proposition: that gravity brings into a body that moves freely, in any given finite time, also a finite force, to the 32nd §; but it already has its opponents among the defenders of the living forces, and it is better to strike them down with their own weapons. The assumed body, which in its circular motion has run through the arc af in a finite time, receives the pressures of all the springs of gravity to which it is unceasingly exposed in the whole finite space af. Now, even according to the admission of the Leibnitzians, the springs of the gravity-making matter, situated in a certain finite space, which communicate their pressure throughout to a body, bring into it a finite force: *Ergo* &c.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0397":["§ 83.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0398":["The conclusion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0399":["Accordingly the force exerted in divided motion, if it is estimated as proportional to the square of the sides of the right-angled parallelogram, is so utterly inconsistent with the best-known laws of the circular motion of bodies, and with the central forces that they exert. The lateral forces in any composite motion whatever are therefore not, as the Leibnizian estimation requires, in the proportion of the squares of their velocities, and for this very reason the conclusion is also general: that the estimation according to the square is wholly mistaken; for every motion can be regarded as composite, as is known from the first fundamental doctrines of mechanics.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0400":["§ 84.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0401":["How the Cartesian estimation remedies this difficulty.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0402":["It is still necessary to remark how excellently the Cartesian estimation of forces remedies the difficulty under which the Leibnizian succumbs, as we have now seen.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0403":["It is known from mathematics: that the small line ac, * which is parallel and equal to the *sinus versus* bi of the infinitely small arc ab, is an infinitely small of the second degree, and thus infinitely many times smaller than the infinitely small line ab. Now, however, ac is the sine of the angle with which the body everywhere in its circular motion works against the pressure of gravity, and ab, as an infinitely small part of the absolute motion of the body itself, is its *sinus totus*. But it is known from § 79 proved previously that: if a body in oblique motion works against a certain obstacle in such a way that the sine of the angle of incidence, with regard to the *sinus totus*, is throughout infinitely small, the force lost through the obstacles is infinitely small compared with the total power of all the obstacles overcome, in the Cartesian estimation. Thus the body in its circular course does not lose a finite force through the pressures of gravity until it has overcome, in the whole sum of all the restraints of gravity, a force that is infinitely great. Now, however, the sum of all the pressures of gravity through a finite time amounts to only a finite force, § 80, Lemma 3, and consequently not to an infinite force before an infinite time: Thus the body that runs in a circle around a center toward which it is pulled by its gravity loses through the obstacles of gravity a finite force only in an infinite time, and consequently in any finite time whatever infinitely little. By contrast, the loss in the Leibnizian estimation in these very same circumstances would amount to something finite in every finite time, § 80, consequently the Cartesian estimation is in this case not subject to the difficulty to which the Leibnizian, as we have seen, is always exposed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0404":["* Fig. XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0405":["§ 85.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0406":["Yet a new contradiction to which the living forces are exposed here.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0407":["The objection that we have now made to the living forces discovers at the same time a strange kind of contradiction in the estimation of forces according to the square. For everyone is agreed in this: that the force estimated according to the rectangle of the velocity multiplied into itself must have infinitely more power than that which is expressed only through the mere measure of the velocity, and that with regard to this latter it is that which the surface is to the line. But here precisely the opposite shows itself, namely: that in the case that we have seen, where both kinds of force are posited to work in entirely equal circumstances, the Leibnizian is capable of infinitely less than the Cartesian, and is consumed by infinitely fewer obstacles than this, which is a contradiction that cannot be thought greater.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0408":["§ 86.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0409":["The destruction of the general principle, of the equal magnitude of the force in composite motion with that in simple motion, at the same time throws over many more cases that the champions of the living forces have built upon this very ground.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0410":["Refutation of the Bernoullian case of the tension of 4 equal springs.",["- *Kraft* = force throughout (*lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\"; *Centralkräfte* = \"central forces\"; *Seitenkräfte* = \"lateral forces\"); *Würkung* / *würken* = \"effect\" / \"work\" (the older spelling of *Wirkung*); *Grundsatz* = \"principle\"; *Satz* = \"proposition\"; *Grund* = \"ground\".","- *Schluß* (heading §83) = \"conclusion\"; *Schätzung* = \"estimation\"; *Widerspruch* = \"contradiction\"; *Verstand* = \"understanding\"; *Urtheil* = \"judgment\"; *Meynung* = \"opinion\".","- *Hinderniß* and *Hindernisse* = \"obstacle(s)\"; *Widerstand* = \"resistance\"; in 0403 *Zurückhaltungen* = \"restraints\" and *Drucke* / *Schwerdrückungen* = \"pressures\" (of gravity).","- *Schwürigkeit* (0403) is a variant spelling of *Schwierigkeit* = \"difficulty\"; *Leibnitzische* / *Leibnitzianer* are rendered \"Leibnizian(s)\", with the modern spelling of the name, except that *Bernoullischen* appears as \"Bernoullian\".","- *Fläche* is rendered \"surface\" in 0391, and again in 0407 where it is set against *Linie* (\"line\"); *Raum* = \"space\"; *Zeit* = \"time\"; *Zeittheile* = \"part of time\".","- *Cirkel* / *Kreis* are both \"circle\" (*Cirkelbewegung*, *Kreisbewegung* = \"circular motion\"; *Kreislaufende* = \"running in a circle\").","- Latin retained in italics: *per resolutionem virium*, *Ergo*, *sinus versus*, *sinus totus*. \"&c.\" is as printed. *W. Z. E.* (*was zu erweisen war*) is rendered \"Q. E. D.\"","- The asterisks in 0391 and 0403 mark the footnote reference to Fig. XIII (0392, 0404). In 0391 the OCR \"be\" and \"ac\" were left as the printed letters; \"be\" was read as \"bc\" (the second line described), a silent correction of an apparent OCR slip, and *ac* in \"die Seiten-Bewegung ac\" is as printed. In 0396 the Latin \"Ergo &c.\" closes the passage as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0411":["The Bernoullian case, which Herr von Wolf adduces in his Mechanics, is one of the most considerable among them. He assumes 4 springs, all of which require equal force to be tensed. He further lets a body with 2 degrees of velocity run perpendicularly against the first at an angle of 30 degrees, whose sine is as 1, afterwards with the remainder of the motion, at an angle whose sine is likewise as 1, against the second, and so also against the third, and finally against the fourth spring. Each one of these springs, now, this body tenses; it exercises, therefore, with 2 degrees of velocity 4 degrees of force, consequently it has had them, for otherwise it could not have exercised them. Hence the force of this body is not as its velocity 2, but as the square of it.",["- Kraft = force; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Grad = degree; Körper = body (not in glossary).","- \"dessen Sinus wie 1 ist\": \"whose\" could refer to the angle (Winkel) or, grammatically, to the 30 degrees; kept as in German.","- \"sie\" in \"4 Grade Kraft ... sie gehabt\" refers grammatically to the plural \"Grade\" (degrees); rendered \"them.\"","- \"perpendikular anlaufen\" stands at the end of the long sentence; German order kept. \"anführen\" rendered \"adduce.\"","- \"derselben\" (last sentence) = of the velocity; ambiguity with \"Körper\" is not present grammatically (feminine)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0412":["I do not demand to assert: that the body with 2 degrees of velocity could under no circumstances exercise 4 degrees of force. But it can exercise them only in oblique running-against, and it is enough that we have proved that its force in straight running-against is nevertheless at all times only as 2, and in slanting motion always greater than in the perpendicular. Everyone, however, estimates the force of a body according to the power that is to be encountered in it in vertical impact. Thus in that way of effect which is without ambiguity, in which all opponents agree that it is the true measure of force, the advantage is on the side of Cartesius against the party of the living forces.",["- Gewalt = power (not in glossary; distinct from Kraft = force); Würkung = effect (glossary Wirkung); Stoß = impact; Anlauf = running-against.","- Art der Würkung = \"way of effect\" (Art = way).","- \"darinn alle Gegner zusammen stimmen, daß sie das wahre Maaß der Kraft sey\": \"sie\" could refer to the way of effect or, less naturally, elsewhere; ambiguity kept.","- \"lebendigen Kräfte\" = \"living forces\"; Maaß = measure; schätzen = estimate.","- \"Ich verlange es nicht zu behaupten\" kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0413":["§ 87.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0414":["Finally, there is grounded on the composition of motion yet one more case, which one might well call the Achilles of our opponents.",["- Zusammensetzung = composition; Achilles refers to the Greek \"Achilles\" argument form; no gloss added."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0415":["Herr von Mairan's objection against the Hermannian case.",["- Herrmannschen = of Hermann (\"Hermannian\"); Einwendung = objection."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0416":["It consists in this: A body A, which has 1 for mass and 2 for velocity, strikes at once, at an angle of 60 degrees, two bodies B and B, which each have 2 for mass. Here the striking body A remains at rest after the impact, and the bodies B and B each move with one degree of velocity, consequently both taken together with 4 degrees of force.",["- Masse = mass; Ruhe = rest; stoßen = strike; Stoß = impact.","- \"zweene\" = \"two\" (archaic form)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0417":["Herr von Mairan has perceived very well how strange and paradoxical it turns out that a particular case, restricted only to certain circumstances, should prove a new estimation of forces, which, however, if it were true, would have to show itself without distinction in each and every circumstance. The Leibnizians are at all times so bold as to demand: that when a body exercises 4 degrees of force, be it in whatever way it may, one can always safely say that it will exercise just the same force also in perpendicular direction; but in this present case it is evident: that everything depends on a determinate number of the elements which are to be moved, and on a determinate position of them toward the striking body, that consequently the matter will behave quite differently if these determinations were changed, hence that one deceives oneself greatly if one concludes thus: the body has in these circumstances exercised this or that force, therefore it must (to speak straight out without any limitation) also have this or that force, and, if one wishes, let it out also in perpendicular effect.",["- Bestimmung = determination; bestimmt = determinate; Einschränkung = limitation (glossary Schranke is a different word).","- Kräftenschätzung = \"estimation of forces\"; Art = way; Würkung = effect; Unterscheid = distinction.","- \"sie ... hervorthun müßte\": \"sie\" refers to Kräftenschätzung; \"gerade zu\" = \"straight out.\"","- Umstände = circumstances (not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0418":["I have wanted now only to endeavor to express the sense of the thought of Herr von Mairan, which he set against the Hermannian case in his reply to the objections that Madame de Chastellet had made to him in her Natural Science; but it seems to me that the whole matter could be disposed of much more easily and convincingly by means of that which we have hitherto noted with regard to the composition and division of forces, and that it is also for the most part already disposed of by this; for which reason I believe that the reader of these pages will readily relieve me of further prolixity by drawing in what I have remarked on this occasion.",["- Sinn = sense; Zertheilung = division (Teilung); Zusammensetzung = composition; Naturlehre = \"Natural Science\" (title of Châtelet's Institutions de physique; literal \"doctrine of nature\" – Lehre = doctrine).","- Einwürfe = objections; Weitläuftigkeit = prolixity; überzeugend = convincing (Überzeugung = conviction).","- \"Frau von Chastellet\" spelled as in the text."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0419":["§ 88.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0420":["Herr von Mairan is the only one among the defenders of Cartesius who has made some considerations concerning the choice of the grounds on which the Leibnizians want to build a new estimation of forces; but he has also done it only in the single case that we cited in the previous section. This genus of investigation seems to be of no great importance if one looks at it superficially, but it is in fact of quite excellent use, as only any method in the art of thinking may be.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Gründe = grounds; Gattung = genus; \"§pho\" = abbreviated ablative of \"§phus\", rendered \"section.\"","- \"so wie irgend nur eine Methode ... seyn mag\": literal, ambiguous in comparison; kept.","- Nutzen = use (cf. Gebrauch = use, different word)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0421":["Usefulness of this method of Herr von Mairan.",["- Nutzbarkeit = usefulness."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0422":["One must have a method by means of which, in each and every case, through a general weighing of the principles on which a certain opinion has been built, and through the comparison of these with the consequence that is drawn from them, one can gather whether the nature of the premises also comprises in itself everything that is required with regard to the doctrines concluded from them. This happens when one precisely notes the determinations that adhere to the nature of the conclusion, and takes good heed whether one has also chosen, in the construction of the proof, such principles as are restricted to the particular determinations that lie in the conclusion. If one does not find this to be so, one need only believe safely: that these inferences, which are deficient in such a way, prove nothing, although one cannot yet discover wherein the error actually lies, and even if this were never to become known. Thus I have, for example, concluded from the general weighing of the motions of elastic bodies that the phenomena that show themselves through their collision could not possibly prove a new estimation of forces that is different from the Cartesian. For I recalled that all these phenomena are indeed resolved by the mechanicians from the single source of the product of mass into velocity, together with the elasticity, of which one can show the Leibnizians a hundred proofs, which all have the greatest geometers as their authors, and which one sees them themselves confirm countless times by their own approval. Thus, I concluded, that which has been brought about merely through the force estimated according to the plain measure of velocity can also furnish a proof of no other estimation than only of that according to velocity. I did not yet know at that time where the error in the inferences of the Leibnizians about the collision of elastic bodies was actually to be sought; but after I had been convinced in the manner indicated that somewhere in them a fallacy must lie, be it ever so hidden, I applied all attention to seeking it out, and it seems to me that I have encountered it in more than one place.",["- Grundsätze = principles (Grundsatz); Vordersätze = premises (literally \"fore-propositions\"); Schlußsatz / Conclusion = conclusion (Latinate Conclusion in text, same English word); Schlüsse = inferences; Fehlschluß = fallacy.","- Erwegung = weighing (cf. Überlegung = reflection); Meynung = opinion; Folgerung = consequence; Lehren = doctrines (Lehre); Beweis = proof; Beweisthum = proof (second German word for \"proof\").","- Bestimmungen = determinations; besondere = particular; Phänomena = phenomena; Erfahrung not present.","- überführet = convinced (glossary Überzeugung = conviction, a different word); Product der Masse in die Geschwindigkeit = \"product of mass into velocity.\"","- \"sie\" in \"man sie selber ... bestätigen siehet\" refers to the Leibnizians; \"welche\" refers to proofs."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0423":["This method is the main source of this whole treatise.",["- Abhandlung = treatise; Hauptquelle = main source."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0424":["In a word: this whole treatise is solely and alone a creature of this method of thinking. I will confess it sincerely: I regarded all those proofs for the living forces, whose weakness I now believe I comprehend completely, at first as so many geometrical demonstrations, in which I suspected not the slightest error and would perhaps never have found even a single one, if the general consideration of the conditions under which the estimation of Herr von Leibniz is established had not given my consideration an entirely different bent. I saw that the actuality of motion is the condition of this measure of forces, and that it constitutes the proper cause why one should not estimate the force of the moved body in the same way as the force of that which strives toward motion. But when I had considered the nature of this condition, I comprehended easily that, since one can posit it with the condition of dead force under one and the same genus, and it differs from it only by magnitude, it can impossibly have a consequence that is *toto genere* different from the consequence of the conditions of a dead force, and also remains just as infinitely different from this, even if the condition that is the one cause of this consequence is posited so near to the other condition that it already almost mixes with it. Thus I saw, with a certainty that does not at all yield to the geometrical, that the actuality of motion cannot be a sufficient ground for concluding: that the forces of bodies in this state must be as the square of their velocity, since in an infinitely briefly lasting motion, or, which is one and the same, in the mere striving toward it, they have nothing but the velocity as measure. I concluded from this: if mathematics has before it the actuality of motion as the ground of the estimation according to the square, and nothing else, then its conclusions must limp very badly. Armed with this well-grounded mistrust with regard to all Leibnizian proofs, I attacked the conclusions of the defenders of this estimation, in order, besides the fact that I now knew there must be errors present in them, also to know wherein they consist. I imagine my undertaking has not entirely failed me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0425":["§ 89.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0426":["The lack of this method has been a cause too why certain manifest errors have remained hidden for very long.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0427":["If one had at all times been diligent in this way of thinking, one could have spared oneself many errors in philosophy; at the very least it would have been a means to tear oneself out of them much sooner. I even venture to say that the tyranny of errors over the human understanding, which has sometimes lasted through entire centuries, has arisen primarily from the lack of this method, or of others that have an affinity with it, and that one must therefore henceforth be diligent in this one before others, in order to forestall that evil in the future. We want to prove this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0428":["How the means must be constituted by which one forestalls the protractedness of errors.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0429":["If, by means of certain inferences that keep concealed somewhere an error that is very plausible, one believes to have proved a certain opinion, and one then has no other means of becoming aware of the invalidity of the proof than that the error that lies hidden in it first reveal itself, and that one must therefore know beforehand what sort of error it is that makes the proof objectionable, before one can say that there is one to be found in it, if one, I say, has no other method than this, then I assert that the error will remain undiscovered uncommonly long, and the proof will deceive countless times before the deception becomes manifest. The cause of this is the following. I presuppose: that when the propositions and inferences occurring in a proof are perfectly plausible, and have the look of the best known truths about them, the understanding will give assent to it, and will not engage in any laborious and protracted search for an error in it; for then the proof counts, with regard to the conviction that arises from it in the understanding, just as much as one that has geometrical rigor and correctness, and the error that lies hidden among the inferences, because it is not perceived, does just as little effect toward the diminution of assent as if it were not to be encountered in the proof at all. Thus the understanding would have either never to give assent to a proof, or it must do so in this one, where it catches sight of nothing that looks similar to an error, i.e., where it suspects none, even if one were hidden in it. In such a case, therefore, it will never apply a particular striving toward the search for an error, because it has no motive for it; consequently the error will not show itself otherwise than by means of a fortunate accident, it will therefore commonly remain hidden very long before it is discovered, for this fortunate accident can fail to appear for many years, indeed often for entire centuries. This is almost the foremost origin of the errors that, to the shame of the human understanding, have continued through many times, and that a very easy consideration has afterwards uncovered. For the error that is lodged somewhere in a proof looks at first glance like a known truth, thus the proof is regarded as perfectly rigorous, one therefore suspects no error in it, one therefore also does not seek it, and hence one finds it only by accident. From this it can easily be inferred wherein the secret will have to be sought that forestalls this difficulty, and that makes easier for us the discovery of the errors one has committed. We must possess the art of guessing and conjecturing from the premises whether a proof set up in a certain way will also contain in itself sufficient and complete principles with regard to the consequence. In this way we will infer whether an error must be present in it, even if we catch sight of none anywhere, but we will then be moved to seek it, for we have a sufficient cause to suspect it. Thus this will be a bulwark against the dangerous readiness of assent, which without this motive would turn all the activity of the understanding away from the investigation of an object in which it finds no cause at all to place a doubt and mistrust. This method has helped us in the paragraphs 25, 40, 62, 65, 68, and it will still render us good service further.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0430":["§ 90.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0431":["It would be a consideration of no small use if one wanted to set out this method somewhat more distinctly, and to show the rules of its application, but this kind of investigation does not belong under the jurisdiction of mathematics, to which this treatise should properly be wholly devoted. But we want still to lay out a sample of its use, in the refutation of the inferences that are borrowed, in favor of the living forces, from the composition of motions.",["- 0424: Würklichkeit = actuality; Bedingung = condition; Kraft = force; Folgerung = consequence; Grund = ground; Schluß/Schlüsse = conclusion(s)/inference(s) (glossary gives both; \"conclude\" for schließen).","- 0424: Schätzung = estimation; Betrachtung and Erwegung rendered \"consideration\" (both); *toto genere* is Kant's Latin, kept in italics.","- 0424: \"ein Geschöpfe\" is as printed (neuter form); \"diejenige Beweise\" kept as number-agreement as printed.","- 0424: \"die eine Ursache dieser Folgerung ist\" kept ambiguous in reference as in German (\"the one cause of this consequence\").","- 0427: Verstand = understanding; Mittel = means; Betrachtung = consideration.","- 0429: Bewegungsgrund = motive (also \"Bewegungs-Grund\"); Beyfall = assent; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Wahrheit = truth; Ursache = cause (distinct from Grund = ground).","- 0429: \"sage ich\" parenthetical kept in place; \"wenn man ... keine andre Methode als diese hat\" resumes the long \"Wenn\" clause, preserved as one sentence. \"Würkung\" = effect (Wirkung).","- 0429, 0431: Gegenstand = object; \"Paragraphis\" is Latin ablative plural, rendered \"paragraphs\"; Gerichtsbarkeit = jurisdiction."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0432":["In the composition of dead pressures, e.g. of weights that will pull a knot in oblique directions, when these directions enclose a right angle, their initial velocities are also expressed by lines that are sides of a right-angled parallelogram, and the pressure arising from this is represented by the diagonal line. Although here too the square of the diagonal line is equal to the sum of the squares of the sides, it nevertheless follows by no means from this that the composite force will be related to one of the simple ones as the square of the lines that express the initial velocities; rather everyone is agreed on this: that, notwithstanding this, the forces in this case are nevertheless only in simple proportion of the velocities. Now take also the composition of actual motions, as one represents it through mathematics, and compare it with this. The lines that make up the sides and the diagonal of the parallelogram are nothing other than the velocities in these directions, just as it is constituted in the case of the composition of dead pressures. The diagonal line has just the same relation to the sides as it has there, and the angle is also the same. Thus nothing of the determinations that enter into the mathematical representation of composite actual motions is different from those under which one represents to oneself, in the very same science, the compositions of dead pressures. Since, then, no estimation of forces according to the square of the velocity flows from these, it also cannot be concluded from those; for they are just the same fundamental concepts, hence they also have the same consequences. It will still be objected: that an obvious difference is surely to be found between them, because one presupposes that the one of them is a composition of actual motions, but the other only a composition of dead pressures. But this presupposition is vain and futile. It does not come along into the plan of the fundamental concepts that make up the theorem; for mathematics does not express the actuality of motion. The lines that are the object of the consideration are only representations of the relation of the velocities. Thus the limitation to the actuality of motion is here only a dead and idle concept, which is only thought alongside, and from which nothing is concluded in the mathematical consideration. From this it follows: that from this way of investigating composite motions nothing advantageous for the living forces can be inferred, but that there must be, perhaps, intermingled philosophical syllogisms, of which, however, there is now no question. In this way we have, with the help of our vaunted method, now comprehended that the mathematical proofs for the living forces from the composition of motions must be false and full of errors; we do not yet know, however, what errors they are, but we have nevertheless a well-grounded conjecture, or rather a certain conviction, that they will unfailingly be in them. Thus we must not let the effort of seeking them out in earnest vex us. I have spared my readers this effort, for it seems to me that I have found these errors and indicated them in the immediately preceding paragraphs.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0433":["§ 91.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0434":["Our method is finally yet a sword against all the knots of subtleties and distinctions with which Herr Bülfinger has wanted to protect his inferences, which we have refuted up to now, against an objection that his opponents can make to him. It is a great advantage for us that we can cut it off, since otherwise it would be very laborious to untie it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0435":["Bülfinger's distinctions, with which he wants to escape the objection of Herr von Mairan, are done away with by means of this method.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0436":["Herr Bülfinger noticed very well: that it would be objected to him that his proofs, if they were correct, would have to prove just the same also for the composition of dead pressures. But he has fortified himself on this side by a bulwark of intricate metaphysical distinctions, as he knows how to make them. He remarks: the effect of the dead force must be estimated by the product of the intensity into the path it takes, but this is expressed by the square of this line; thus one could indeed concede to the Cartesians: that the effects in the composition of dead pressures are equal, but from this it does not yet follow that the forces would therefore also have to be equal. He adds: *in motibus isochronis solum actiones sunt vt vires; non innisu mortuo*. A metaphysical investigation produces a peculiar effect in a mathematical dispute. The mathematician believes that he does not understand these subtleties, and even if he is not capable of resolving them, it is still far from him that he should let himself be led astray by them. He proceeds along the guiding thread of geometry, and all other paths are suspect to him. The geometers have conducted themselves in just the same way with regard to the evasions of Herr Bülfinger. No one, as far as I know, has engaged with him on these weapons. One has spared oneself this effort with good forethought; for a metaphysical investigation, in particular one that is so intricate and composite, still always permits on all sides countless hiding places, to which the one of the opponents can retreat without the other being in a position to pursue him or to draw him out. We did very well to attack Herr Bülfinger's inferences right at the beginning from that side where, by his own admission, mathematics alone gives the verdict. But by means of our method, as I have already said, we are masters also over these distinctions, even if they have hidden themselves behind ever so impenetrable coverings of obscurity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0437":["Our method forestalls the distinctions of Herr Bülfinger.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0438":["Here the question is chiefly: whether Herr Bülfinger's distinctions can make valid the mathematical proof for the living forces that he has taken from the relation of the diagonal line to the side line, in the composition of actual motions, or whether this mathematical proof, notwithstanding all this, can nevertheless provide no bulwark for the new estimation. This is properly the point about which the dispute is conducted; for if Herr Bülfinger's edifice rests only on metaphysical principles, and is not supported by the mathematical concepts of the composition of motions, then the aim of this chapter already excuses us if we do not engage in the investigation of it. But the relation of the diagonal velocity to the side velocities in the composition of actual motions is proved from one and just the same ground from which one likewise derives this relation in the composition of dead pressures. It is thus true, even if in the composite actual motions no other properties and determinations are to be found than those that are found in dead pressures, because it can be sufficiently proved without one's needing anything else for this than what one must also presuppose in the case of dead pressures that are composed. It cannot therefore be concluded from the relation of the diagonal velocity in actual motions: that the composite forces must be of another nature and kind of estimation than dead pressures, for just the same relation nevertheless obtains even if the nature of the composite forces is not at all different from dead pressures, because one needs no other grounds to prove it than those which one would also need here. It is thus futile that Herr Bülfinger wants to make use of them in order to conclude from this: that the forces stand not in proportion to the velocities, but to their squares.",["- 0432: *Druck/Drücke* = pressure(s); *todt* = dead; *würklich/wirklich* = actual (glossary *Wirklichkeit* = actuality); *Kraft* = force; *Geschwindigkeit* = velocity.","- 0432: *Schätzung* = estimation; *Begriff* = concept (*Grundbegriffe* = fundamental concepts); *Betrachtung* = consideration; *Folgerung* = consequence; *schliessen/folgern/herfolgern* = infer/conclude (*hergefolgert* rendered \"concluded\"; *geschlossen* rendered \"inferred\").","- 0432: \"begreifen\" in \"haben wir ... itzo begreifen\" = \"comprehended\"; the German lacks an auxiliary (\"begriffen\" for \"begreifen\"), reproduced as is.","- 0432: *Schlußreden* rendered \"syllogisms\"; *vor* (= für) rendered \"for\"; *seyn* (plural subjunctive/indicative forms) rendered without distinction.","- 0432: *Zusammensatz/Zusammensetzung* = composition; *Einwurf* = objection; *Proportion* = proportion.","- 0434/0436: *Schlüsse/Schlüße* = inferences (glossary *Schluß* = inference; conclusion); *Unterscheidungen* = distinctions; *Spitzfindigkeiten* = subtleties; *Ausflüchte* = evasions.","- 0436: the Latin quotation is as printed; *Bülfinger* is \"Bülfinger\" throughout, though the German also has the genitive \"Bülfingers\" and \"des Bülfingers\".","- 0438: *Verhältniß* = relation (also \"proportion\" for *Proportion*); *Grund* = ground, *Gründe* = grounds; *Absicht* = aim; *Hauptstück* = chapter; *Bestimmungen* = determinations."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0439":["Accordingly the metaphysical distinctions of which this philosopher has availed himself can, perhaps, offer something from which a continued philosophical consideration would draw some grounds in favor of the living forces; but for the upholding of that mathematical proof of which we are speaking they are not sufficient, because by its very nature it leaves undetermined that which is required for the rule that one wants to draw from it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0440":["§ 92.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0441":["A particular composite case of Herr von Leibnitz.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0442":["After all these different genera of proofs, whose incorrectness we have shown to the defenders of the living forces, I come finally to the one that has Herr von Leibnitz, the father of the living forces himself, as its author, and also bears the mark of his acuteness. He first presented it to the world in the *Actis Eruditorum* *, on the occasion when he was resolving the objections of the Abbé Catelan. He also afterwards always appealed to it in particular whenever he wanted to give light to his estimation of forces: thus we shall have to regard it as a chief support of the living forces, and to clear it away.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0443":["* *Acta* 1690.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0444":["** Let a ball A, of fourfold mass, fall on the inclined and curved surface, whose height 1 AE is as 1, from 1 A into 2 A, and continue its motion on the horizontal surface EC with the degree of speed that it has attained through the fall, and which is as 1. Let us further posit: that it transfer all the force that it has into a ball B of simple mass, and after this itself rest at the point 3 A. What speed, then, shall the ball B, which has 1 for its mass, receive from the ball A, which has 4 times more mass and a simple degree of speed, if its force is hereby to become equal to the force that the body A had? The Cartesians say: its speed will have to be 4-fold. Let the body B therefore run, with 4 degrees of speed, on the horizontal surface from 1 B into 2 B, and, after it has there met the inclined and curved surface 2 B 3 B, let it move up along it, and thus reach on it, through the speed inhering in it, the point 3 B, whose perpendicular height 3 BC is as 16. Let us further assume the inclined steelyard 3 A 3 B, which moves on the point F, and one arm of which, F 3 B, is four times and a little more longer than the other beam 3 AF, which nevertheless hold each other in equilibrium. If now the body B reaches the point 3 B, and there steps onto the arm of the balance, it is clear that: because the beam F 3 B, with regard to the other 3 AF, is somewhat greater than the mass of the body 3 A, in comparison with the mass of the ball 3 B, the equilibrium will be lifted, and the body 3 B will sink down from 3 B into 4 B, but at the same time raise the ball 3 A from 3 A into 4 A. But the height 4 A 3 A is almost the fourth part of the height 3 BC, hence as 4; thus the body B has in this way raised the ball A to an almost fourfold height. Now by an easy mechanical artifice it can be brought about: that the ball 4 A goes back again from 4 A into 1 A, and with the force obtained through its falling back exerts certain mechanical effects, but afterwards runs down once more from the point 1 A along the inclined surface 1 A 2 A, and puts everything into the previous state, also transfers to the ball B, which through an imperceptibly small inclination of the surface 2 B 4 B can be at the point 1 B again, all its force as before, and accomplishes everything once more. Herr von Leibnitz goes on to conclude: thus it follows from the estimation of forces of Cartesius: that a body, if one only makes good use of its force, can infinitely always more and more exert effects, drive machines, tension springs, and overcome hindrances, without anything being lost from its faculty of exerting this very same thing further without cease; that thus the effect can be greater than its cause, and that perpetual motion, which all mechanicians hold to be absurd, is possible.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0445":["** Fig. XIV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0446":["§ 93.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0447":["The point of the fallacy in this proof.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0448":["This proof is the only one among all the defenses of the living forces whose plausibility could excuse the haste that the Leibnizians have shown with regard to the grounds of defense of their estimation. Herr Bernoulli, Herr Herrmann and Wolff have said nothing that would equal it in invention and apparent strength. So great a man as Herr von Leibnitz was could not err without even that thought which misled him into error having to be glorious to him. With regard to this proof we want to say what Hector boasts of himself in Virgil:",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0449":["– – – – – – – – –",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0450":["*Si Pergama dextra*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0451":["*defendi possent, etiam hac defensa fuissent.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0452":["Virg. *Aeneid.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0453":["I want to put my judgment about it briefly. Herr von Leibnitz should not have said: that the falling back of the ball A, after it has been raised by means of the steelyard to the fourfold height 4 A 3 A, and returns again from 3 A onto the inclined surface 1 A, but beforehand exerts mechanical forces, is an effect of the force transferred into the ball B, however much it may seem to be so. This exerted mechanical force is, as we shall soon see, indeed the subsequent state in the machine, which has been occasioned by means of the force that passed over into B, but it is nevertheless no effect of this force. We must avoid the confusion of these two meanings very carefully, for here is the proper point of the fallacy, on which all the illusion that comes forth in the Leibnizian proof is grounded. For if all these mechanical sequences are not a proper effect of the force that the body A has transferred into the other, B, then all appearance of a paradoxical thought vanishes at once, even if one says: that more is contained in the subsequent state of the machine than in the preceding. For the effect is not yet on that account greater than its cause, and perpetual motion itself is in this case no absurdity, because the motion brought forth is not the true effect of the force, which has properly only occasioned it, consequently can also always be greater than it, without one's offending against the fundamental law of mechanics.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0454":["§ 94.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0455":["The force that A receives through the arrangement of the machine is no brought-forth effect of the force of the body B.",["- Kräfte/Kraft = force(s) throughout; *lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\"; Würkung = \"effect\" (Wirkung in the glossary); Folgen = \"sequences\" (glossary: Folge = sequel; sequence).","- Beweis = proof; Merkmahl = mark; Gattungen = genera; Zustand = state; Vermögen (in 0444) = faculty; Bedeutungen = meanings; Scheinbarkeit/Schein = plausibility/illusion (Schein = illusion; \"scheinbar\" rendered \"apparent\"/\"plausible\"); Ansehen = appearance.","- Ursache = cause; Grundgesetze = fundamental law; Schutzgründe = grounds of defense (Grund = ground).","- 0442: \"Actis Eruditorum\" is Latin in the dative/ablative as printed; \"Abtes Catelan\" rendered \"Abbé Catelan\" (German \"Abt\" literally \"abbot\").","- 0444: the footnote marker ** and the sign-lettering (1 A, 2 B, etc.) are carried over as printed; the text is a figure description keyed to Fig. XIV. \"Perpendikular-Höhe\" = perpendicular height; \"Schnellwaage\" = steelyard; \"Federn spannen\" = \"tension springs\" (literal).","- 0444: \"vor eine Geschwindigkeit\" = \"what speed\" (vor = für); \"einen\" in \"den er\" refers to the ball (Kugel is feminine, but the text has masculine \"er\", \"seine\"); kept as \"it/its\". \"den Körper A ... das vierte Theil\" retains the neuter/feminine \"Theil\" as \"fourth part\". Possible OCR/textual slips (\"2 B 4 B\", \"2 AE\") left as printed.","- 0448: \"Wolf\" in the German; spelled \"Wolff\" in English by convention. \"Herrmann\" as printed.","- 0449–0452: Virgil, *Aeneid* II.291–292 (Latin left untranslated as printed).","- 0453: \"es scheinet zu seyn\" and \"dieselbe\" (feminine, i.e. the *Würkung*/*Kraft*) kept ambiguous as \"it/so\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0456":["The body B, into which one has transferred all the force of the sphere A, expends the same entirely, in that it runs up the inclined plane 2 B 3 B. At the point 3 B it has therefore completed the whole magnitude of its effect, and also consumed all the force communicated to it. Since it now arrives there upon the beam of the scale, it is no longer the previous force with which it lifts the body 3 A up, but the renewed power of gravity alone does this effect, but the force that B had received from the sphere A has no share in it. If, further, the sphere A has been raised thereby as far as 4 A, then the preponderating force of the sphere 3 B has also in this way exercised its complete effect, and the force which the body A receives, in that it returns from 4 A to 1 A, is again an effect of a new cause, which is entirely distinct from the activity of the lever, and also much greater than the same, namely of the pressure of gravity, which is communicated to the body in free fall. Thus that force with which the body A exercises mechanical effects, before it arrives again at the point 1 A, is something that was indeed occasioned by the force of the sphere B, that is, handed over to certain mechanical causes, but does not itself have it as producing cause.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0457":["§ 95.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0458":["This is confirmed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0459":["If the Leibnizians want to posit, in the subsequent state that arises in nature, always precisely only as much force as the preceding one contains in itself, I would gladly know how they would extricate themselves from the objection that can be made to them from their own proof. If I place the sphere B at 3 B on the steelyard, and consequently it presses the beam down there, and raises the body A from 3 A to 4 A, then this is the preceding state of nature, but the force which A afterwards receives, in that it falls back again from 4 A, is the subsequent state, which is occasioned by the previous one. But there is contained in this much more force than in that one. For the excess of weight of the body 3 B over the body 3 A can be incomparably small with regard to its own weight, hence the velocity with which 3 A is lifted can be exceedingly small compared to the velocity that it receives through the free fall back from 4 A to 1 A, for here the undiminished pressures of gravity accumulate, but there only such as are incomparably small compared to these. Thus the subsequent state of force that is in nature is indisputably greater than the preceding one that occasioned it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0460":["§ 96.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0461":["This very thing proved from the law of continuity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0462":["Everything here depends chiefly on this: that one be convinced that the force which B possesses with 4 degrees of velocity is not the producing cause of the effect that shows itself here in the machine, as the Leibnizians must presuppose if they want to show an absurdity in the law of Descartes. For if this were so, then, if one diminished this cause only by something slight, the effect too would become only very slightly smaller. But this shows itself here in the machine quite differently. If we posit: that the body 1 B has somewhat less than 4 degrees of velocity, then it will arrive only as far as the point a, on the curved surface 2 B a, where the length 3 AF of the one beam of the scale stands, to the length of the other arm of the scale, in quite exactly fourfold ratio, where therefore the weight of the body B does not move the lever, nor displace the body 3 A in the least from its place. Thus if B has one part of the force less, which can be assumed so small that it hardly comes into consideration at all: then 3 A attains then no force at all any longer; as soon, on the contrary, as this little is added, 3 A will not only get back the force that it had at first, but still far more besides. It is evident: that this leap would not occur if the force of the body 3 B were the true producing cause of that state which shows itself in the machine.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0463":["§ 97.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0464":["The whole magnitude of the sufficient ground in the preceding state.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0465":["If one weighs the placement of the lever in this machine, and its geometrical determination with regard to the proportion of the bodies, if one adds to this still the excess of the ratio of the height 3 B 4 B to the height 1 AE over the proportion of the mass of the body B to the mass A, (for the height 3 B 4 B is to the height 1 AE as 16 to 1, but the mass A to B only as 4 to 1) then one has the whole magnitude of those determinations which occasioned the force in A; to this one may take in addition the pressures of gravity, which by means of the advantageous placement of the geometrical determinations are made more effective, and then one has the whole comprehension of all sufficient grounds, in which one will find again completely the magnitude of the force that arises in A. If one separates from this the sole force of the body B, it is no wonder that it is found much too small to exhibit in it the ground of the force that comes into A. All that the body B does in this is that, at the same time as it overcomes the restraints of gravity, it gains a certain modality, that is, a certain quantity of height, which namely is greater than according to the proportion of its velocity, and consequently also of its mass.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0466":["Thus the force of the body B is not the true effecting cause of the force that is generated in A: with regard to it, therefore, the great law of mechanics: *effectus quilibet aequipollet viribus causae plenae*, will be without validity; and a perpetual motion can in this way be produced all the same, without this fundamental law being violated in the least.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0467":["§ 98.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0468":["The sole difficulty that could still lie in the Leibnizian argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0469":["Everything, then, that Herr von Leibniz can oppose to us with his argument consists in this: that, even if one cannot demonstrate the complete impossibility of the matter, it nevertheless turns out very irregular and contrary to nature: that a force should awaken another, greater than it is, let it happen in whatever way it will. Herr von Leibniz turns himself to this side: * *Sequeretur etiam causam non posse iterum restitui suoque effectui surrogari; quod quantum abhorreat a more naturae & rationibus rerum facile intelligitur. Et consequens esset: decrescentibus semper effectibus, neque unquam crescentibus, ipsam continue rerum naturam declinare, perfectione imminuta, neque unquam resurgere atque amissa recuperare posse sine miraculo. Quae in Physicis certe abhorrent a sapientia constantiaque conditoris*. He would not have spoken so mildly if he had not seen that the nature of the matter imposed this moderation upon him. One may be quite certainly assured: that he would have marched out against his enemy with the whole thunder of his geometrical ban, and all the might of mathematics, if his acuteness had not perceived this weakness. But he saw himself compelled to call the wisdom of GOD to his aid, a certain mark that geometry had not offered him any capable weapons.",["- Kraft = force throughout; Würkung = effect (Wirkung); Ursache = cause; Zustand = state; Grund = ground; zureichend = sufficient.","- Gewalt (0456) rendered \"power\"; Thätigkeit = activity; Druck = pressure; Schwere = gravity; Gewicht = weight; Ueberwucht = \"excess of weight\".","- Größe = magnitude; Bestimmung = determination; Betrachtung = consideration (\"comes into consideration\").","- Einwurf = objection; Beweis/Argument = proof/argument (both kept distinct); Merkmahl = mark.","- 0456: \"womit er den Körper 3 A in die Höhe hebet\" and \"hieran keinen Antheil\" kept literal; \"aber\" after a comma-linked \"sondern\" clause preserved as \"but\".","- 0456 final sentence: \"das ist ... übergeben worden\" is kept as in the German; the subject of \"übergeben worden\" is ambiguous (the force/the effect) and is left so.","- 0462: \"3 AF\", \"2 Ba\" kept as printed (OCR spacing normalized); \"Waagbalkens\" = \"beam of the scale\", \"Waagarmes\" = \"arm of the scale\"; Schnellwaage (0459) = \"steelyard\".","- 0469: Latin quotation reproduced with OCR slip \"restituisuoque\" corrected to \"restitui suoque\"; footnote asterisk kept before the quotation as in the source. \"wiedernatürlich\" rendered \"contrary to nature\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0470":["* *Act. Erud.* 1691 p. 442.",["- \"Act. Erud.\" kept as printed (abbreviation of *Acta Eruditorum*); not expanded."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0471":["*Nec deus intersit, nisi dignus vindice nodus Inciderit* – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – –",["- Latin; the original reads \"devs\" (u/v print form), silently corrected to \"deus\". \"Inciderit\" is the standard reading, as printed.","- The dashes are the original's omission marks, carried over."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0472":["Horat. *de arte poët.*",["- Abbreviated reference to Horace, *Ars poetica*; kept in abbreviated form."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0473":["Is answered.",["- \"Wird beantwortet\": a marginal heading; no subject is expressed in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0474":["But even the small bulwark is of no permanence. Here it is merely a matter of the estimation of forces that is cognized through mathematics, and it is no wonder if the same does not do full enough justice to the wisdom of GOD. This is a science taken out of the middle of all cognitions, which on its own does not sufficiently subsist with the rules of the seemly and the fitting, and which must be taken together with the doctrines of metaphysics if it is to be applied perfectly to nature. The harmony that is found among the truths is like the agreement in a painting. If one takes out a part in particular, the seemly, the beautiful and the skillful disappears; but they must all be seen at the same time in order to perceive the same. The Cartesian estimation is contrary to the intentions of nature: thus it is not the true measure of forces of nature, but this nevertheless does not hinder it from being the true and rightful measure of forces of mathematics. For the mathematical concepts of the properties of bodies and of their forces are still far distinguished from the concepts that are encountered in nature, and it is enough that we have seen: the Cartesian estimation is not contrary to those. But we must connect the metaphysical laws with the rules of mathematics in order to determine the true measure of forces of nature; this will fill the gap and will do better satisfaction to the intentions of the wisdom of GOD.",["- Schätzung = \"estimation\" (not in the glossary; one word throughout). Kräftenmaaß = \"measure of forces\" (compound kept literal).","- Absichten = \"intentions\" (glossary: aim; intention). Erkenntnisse = \"cognitions\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (glossary gives Beschaffenheit for \"property\"; Eigenschaft is not listed).","- \"dieselbe\" / \"dasselbe\" / \"jenen\": referents left as ambiguous as in the German. \"nicht ... nicht\" in \"hindert ... daß sie nicht ... seyn sollte\" rendered literally with the pleonastic negation dropped as English requires.","- verknüpfen = \"connect\" (cf. Verknüpfung = connection)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0475":["§ 99.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0476":["Mr. Papin's objection.",["- \"Herr\" rendered \"Mr.\" in headings and text."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0477":["Mr. Papin, one of the most notorious adversaries of the living forces, has conducted the cause of Descartes against this ground of proof of Mr. von Leibnitz very unhappily. He has vacated the battlefield to his opponent, and has run across the field in order to maintain somewhere a post that should protect him. He concedes to Mr. von Leibnitz: that, if one presupposes that the body A has transferred its whole force into the body B, a perpetual motion follows according to the Cartesian estimation, and concedes to him very good-heartedly that this kind of motion is an absurdity: Quomodo autem per translationem totius potentiae corporis A in corpus B juxta Cartesium obtineri possit motus perpetuus evidentissime demonstrat, atque ita Cartesianos ad absurdum reductos arbitratur. Ego autem & motum perpetuum absurdum esse fateor, & Cl. Vir. demonstrationem exsupposita translatione esse legitimam; After he has spoiled his cause in this way, he seeks his evasion in this: that he denies the presupposition of his opponent, which is a very contingent piece of his argument, and challenges him to untie this knot for him. The following words make known his opinion: Sed Hypothesis ipsius possibilitatem translationis nimirum totius potentiae ex corpore A in corpus B pernego, &c – – – *",["- The Latin quotations are left in Latin as in the source (not italicized in the German setting as printed here); \"exsupposita\" is the printed reading, possibly for \"ex suppositione\" or \"ex supposita\"; doubtful.","- Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"; Kraft/Kräfte = \"force(s)\"; Meynung = \"opinion\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; Ungereimtheit = \"absurdity\".","- \"Mr. von Leibnitz\" keeps the original spelling of the name.","- The asterisk points to the footnote of 0478."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0478":["* *Act.* 1691 pag. 9.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0479":["§ 100.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0480":["Mr. von Leibnitz has at once disarmed his opponent, and has left him not the slightest evasion. He has shown him: that the actual transference of the force is no essential piece of his proof, and that it is enough to posit in B a force that can be substituted for the force in A. One can find everything proved in the treatise that he incorporated into the *Acta*, and which we have already cited. But I cannot refrain from adducing a lapse of Mr. von Leibnitz, which in a public disputation would have played the victory into his opponent's hands. It consists in this: that he grants something that, as he himself remarks, properly does not belong to the main matter, in order to demonstrate a side circumstance in the argument, which, however, if it is accepted, does confirm this side condition, but entirely overturns the main point in the proof.",["- Beweis = \"proof\"; wesentlich = \"essential\"; setzen = \"posit\"; Nebenumstand / Nebenbedingung = \"side circumstance\" / \"side condition\"; Hauptsache / Hauptpunkt = \"main matter\" / \"main point\".","- \"Acta\" stands for the *Acta Eruditorum* (the German has \"Actis\"); rendered as a Latin word in italics."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0481":["A lapse of Mr. von Leibnitz.",["- Vergehen (here \"Vergehen\") = \"lapse\"; the same word is used in 0480 (spelled \"Vergehn\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0482":["The matter stands thus: Mr. Papin, who had got it into his head to make no other exception in his opponent's objection than this one: that it is impossible for a body to communicate its whole force to another, sought to make suspect to Mr. von Leibnitz all the artifices through which he supposed that he accomplished this. Hence he contested with all zeal against him: that the fourfold body 1 A, * through an impact on the perfectly rigid lever 1 A C B, at the point 1 A, whose distance from the point of rest C is four-parted relative to the distance CB, could communicate its whole force to the simple body B; for it was to this that Mr. von Leibnitz turned in the assertion of his mechanical case, which we have treated. Mr. Papin did not become aware of the advantage that his cause could have received, if he had seized upon this resolution and had himself concluded from it against the living forces. He therefore took hold of the same: but with such weak grounds, which increased his opponent's courage to persist in the assertion of the same. Leibnitz therefore insisted on the correctness of this artifice, which he believed he could make use of, in order to transfer into a body the whole force of another through a single impact. He accepted with gratitude the grounds that Papin had adduced to show the plausibility of the same, and cleared away the difficulties with which the latter in turn supposed to frustrate these. I believe that he said the following in true earnest: Cum florentiae essem, dedi amico aliam adhuc demonstrationem, pro possibilitate translationis virium totalium &c. corpore majore in minus quiescens, prorsus affinem illis ipsis, quo Cl. Papinus ingeniosissime pro me juvando excogitavit, pro quibus gratias debeo imo & ago sinceritate ejus dignas. We now want to see that Leibnitz gave his cause a very bad swing, in that he stiffly persisted in the assertion of this proposition, which he should rather have conceded to his opponent; for then he would indeed have lost the side matter (the loss of which, however, could have brought him no disadvantage at all), but the main matter he would have won: Mr. Papin could have argued in the following way, and also should have, in order to catch his opponent in his own admission.",["- \"1 A\", \"1 A C B\", \"CB\" are carried over as printed (figure labels); the asterisk points to the footnote of 0483 (Fig. XV).","- \"vierfache Körper\" = \"fourfold body\"; \"viertheilig\" = \"four-parted\"; \"einfachen\" = \"simple\" (einfach); Auflösung = \"resolution\" (not in glossary).","- \"dieselbe\"/\"desselben\"/\"diese\": referents left as in the German; \"der Behauptung desselben\" could refer to the resolution or to Papin's opponent's position. \"Scheinbarkeit\" = \"plausibility\" (cf. Schein = illusion).","- \"Satz\" = \"proposition\"; Geständnis = \"admission\"; \"Nebensache\"/\"Hauptsache\" = \"side matter\"/\"main matter\".","- \"pro me juvando\" and the rest of the Latin quotation are left as printed; \"quiescens\" is the printed reading (grammatically odd), doubtful.","- \"was ... zu vereiteln vermeynete\" kept literal (\"supposed to frustrate\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0483":["* Fig. XV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0484":["Proof that a fourfold body through an impact on a lever can communicate to a 1-fold one 4 degrees of velocity.",["- Beweis = \"proof\"; Grad = \"degree\"; \"1fachen\" = \"1-fold\", as printed. \"Geschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity\" (not in glossary). \"mittheilen\" = \"communicate\", consistent with 0482."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0485":["If the fourfold body 1 A with one degree of velocity strikes the lever in 1 A, it is evident that it will, by this impact, transfer its entire force and velocity into another, 2 A, which is of equal mass with it and also stands just as far from the resting point of the lever. But because this velocity, with which 2 A is bounced away, is a continuation of that motion with which the lever, in that it pushes the body onward, traverses the infinitely small space 2 A 2 a, the velocity of this infinitely small motion is equal to the velocity of the body 2 A pushed onward, and thus to that with which 1 A strikes the lever; consequently this sphere 1 A in its onrush will press the lever down the infinitely small line 1 A 1 a, and indeed this will be traversed with just the same velocity with which 1 A rushes on. Now let one put, instead of the body 2 A, the sphere B, which has four times less mass than A, at fourfold distance from the resting point C, and see what sort of obstacle the body B will then make for the body A, while this latter is endeavoring to press the lever down from 1 A to 1 a. It is known that the *vis inertiae*, or the resistance that a body, by means of its force of inertia, lays in the way of the motion of another, is proportioned to its mass; now, however, a fourpart mass at fourfold distance from the resting point is to be estimated as equal to the quantity of a simple one at fourpart distance: Thus B in B offers to the impact of the body 1 A on the lever just so much resistance, precisely, as the body 2 A = 1 A in 2 A would have offered. So the body 1 A will then in this case too, where the sphere B is on the lever instead of the sphere 2 A, run through the infinitely small line 1 A 1 a together with the lever, and indeed with just the same velocity as in the previous case, i.e., which is as great as that with which it rushes on the point 1 A. But the body 1 A cannot press the lever down from 1 A to 1 a without at the same time moving the other end in B up from B to b; the infinitely small line B b, however, is 4 times greater than 1 A 1 a: thus the body B will receive through this impact of the lever a velocity that is fourfold in relation to that with which A rushes on.",["- Körper = body; Kugel = sphere; Kraft = force; Stoß/stoßen = impact/strike or push (stößet, fortstößet rendered \"strikes\", \"pushes onward\").","- \"vis inertiae\" is Latin in the source; Trägheitskraft = force of inertia.","- \"viertheilige Masse\" / \"viertheiliger Entfernung\" rendered literally as \"fourpart\"; \"Quantität\" = quantity; \"Hinderniß\" = obstacle.","- \"Ruhepunct\" = resting point; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = velocity; \"Masse\" = mass.","- \"Wiederstand\" (sic) = resistance. \"Anlauf/anlaufen\" = onrush/rush on.","- Letters and figures (1 A, 2 A, B, b, C) kept as printed; OCR spacing normalized."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0486":["The same proved in another way.",["- Erwiesen = proved (Beweis = proof, in the next passage)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0487":["This is clear in yet another way. All hard bodies we can represent to ourselves as elastic, that is, as yielding to the impact but springing back again; thus we can also attribute such a spring force to the stiff lever 1 ACB. The body 1 A, then, which rushes on the lever with the degree of velocity as 1, expends its entire force in that it tensions the spring 1 AC, and presses it open through the space 1 A 1 a. Now the *momenta* of velocity, which this spring, through its resistance, consumes in the body 1 A throughout the entire time of this pressure, are equal to those *momenta* with which the spring C 2 A, as the continued arm of the lever, at the same time by virtue of this tension springs up through the space 2 A 2 a; consequently, if this stiff line is extended to B, the *momenta* of velocity with which the spring CB springs up, in that the lever 1 a CB restores itself into the straight line 1 a Cb, are four times greater than the *momenta* with which it strikes back at the point 2 A, (for the space b B, which the point B traverses at the same time, is four times greater than 2 A 2 a.) Only, on account of the fourfold distance of the point B from the resting point C, the stiffness of the spring CB is nevertheless four times weaker than the stiffness of the spring C 2 A; hence one must on the other hand make the resistance in B four times smaller than in 2 A, and then the *momentum* of velocity that the spring CB brings into the fourpart body B remains fourfold, whereas the *momentum* which the spring C 2 A would apply to the fourfold body 2 A is simple. Now the time in which the spring CB works is as great as that in which the C 2 A would spring up, and the velocities that the two bodies, 2 A and B, receive through the effect of two springs, C 2 A and CB, which work for equally long, are as the *momenta* of the velocities which these springs bring into their bodies, consequently in the body B four times greater than in 2 A; but since the velocity that 2 A would receive from the pushing-onward of the spring C 2 A is equal to the velocity with which 1 A rushes on in 1 A, the velocity that the body B receives through this impact of the body 1 A on the lever will be four times greater than that was with which 1 A performed its impact. Q.E.D.",["- *momentum* (pl. *momenta*, Latin forms as in source) left in Latin; Federkraft = spring force; Feder = spring; Steife = stiffness.","- \"Würkung\" = effect (Wirkung); \"würken\" = work (wirken).","- \"W. z. E.\" (was zu erweisen war) rendered \"Q.E.D.\"","- \"spannen\" = tension; \"aufdrücken\" = press open; \"Raum\" = space."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0488":["How Mr. Papin could have argued from this against Leibniz.",["- \"Leibnitzen\" = Leibniz (dative); \"Herr\" = Mr."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0489":["We see, then, from this twofold proof: that a fourfold body can impart to a simple one a fourfold velocity by a single impact. This is true according to those mechanical principles which even the most zealous defenders of the living forces would not be in a position to call in doubt. Mr. Papin could hereby have driven his opponent honestly into a corner, if he had well perceived his advantage. He should have said to him: You have conceded to me that a fourfold body, by means of a lever, can bring all its force into a simple one whose distance from the center is fourfold; but I can show you that in these circumstances it imparts to it four degrees of velocity: thus a simple body with 4 degrees of velocity has all the force of a fourfold one with 1 degree, but this is the point about which the dispute is being waged, and which you demand of me to deny.",["- Grundsätze = principles; Beweis = proof; Grad = degree; Kraft = force.","- \"lebendige Kräfte\" = living forces; \"Mittelpunkt\" = center, versus earlier \"Ruhepunct\" = resting point.","- \"ihr ... mir zu leugnen verlanget\" is literal; \"mir\" is grammatically odd, kept as printed (\"demand of me to deny\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0490":["§ 101.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0491":["So the most fearsome blow of all, with which the living forces threatened the estimation of Descartes, has come away empty. Now there is no hope left that after this they will still find means to maintain themselves upright.",["- Schätzung = estimation (the Cartesian measure of force); Mittel = means; \"dieselbe\" (the living forces) rendered \"they\", the German is feminine singular and could refer to Schätzung or Kräfte; left ambiguous."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0492":["– – –",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0493":["*vires in ventum effudit, & ultro Ipse gravis graviterque ad terram pondere vasto Concidit: ut quondam cava concidit aut Erymantho, Aut Ida in magna, radicibus eruta pinus.*",["- Latin quotation left untranslated, as in the source."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0494":["Virg. Aen. Libr. V.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0495":["§ 102.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0496":["We have refuted the foremost grounds of the Leibnizians.",["- Gründe = grounds; \"vornehmsten\" = foremost."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0497":["We have thus far cited the most considerable and most famous grounds of the renewal concerning the living forces, and have taken care to repay this sect, according to the right of retaliation, all the reproaches and reprimands that they have so often made to the pupils of Descartes. One would wrongly demand of us: that we should bring in everything that has been written in this matter on the side of Herr von Leibnitz, in order to prepare from it a perfect triumph for our party. This would mean sparing nothing, from the cedars on Lebanon to the hyssop that grows out of the wall, only so that one might enrich one's work. We could make yet more than one raid into the territory of our opponents, plunder their goods, and erect to the following of Descartes that many trophies and triumphal arches; but I believe my readers will show no great desire for it. If it has ever been said with ground that a big book is a big evil, one could say it of such a one as, like this one, adduces few other things than nothing but various defenses of just the same thing, and indeed of a very abstract thing, and finally adduces them only to a single end, namely to refute them all.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0498":["Meanwhile we cannot so entirely renounce this misuse of prolixity that we should not still be entitled to bring in a proof, from the keeping silent of which the whole number of the opponents and champions of our disputed matter would nevertheless absolve us. This proof has a claim to a place in this treatise only because of the rank of its author; but it has not the slightest, in consideration of the authority in which it stands with the adherents of both parties. The Leibnitzians have not believed that it could be of any use to their opinion, and one has not seen that they have taken refuge in it, however often they were driven into a corner.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0499":["§ 103.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0500":["An Argument of Herr Wolf.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0501":["Herr Wolf is the one from whom we have this proof, and which he, adorned with all the pomp of method, has presented in the first volume of the Petersburg Commentarii. One can say: that the carrying through of his proposition by a great series of preceding propositions, which are very exactly divided and multiplied by means of a strict method, is to be compared to the stratagem of an army which, in order to make a deception for its enemy and to hide its weakness, separates itself into many heaps and extends its wings far.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0502":["Everyone who reads his treatise in the cited work of the Academy will find: that it is very hard to search out in it that which constitutes the proper proof therein, so much is everything, by virtue of the analytic inclination that shows itself there, stretched out and made unintelligible. We want to make the constitution of his undertaking known to ourselves to some extent.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0503":["§ 104.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0504":["The principal principle of this argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0505":["Herr Papin had asserted: one cannot say that a body has done something if it overcomes no obstacles at all, displaces no masses, tenses no springs, etc. Herr Wolf contradicts him in this, and indeed on this ground: If a human being carries a load through a certain space, then everyone is agreed in this, that he has done and accomplished something; now a body carries its own mass, by virtue of the force that it possesses in actual motion, through a space: Precisely thereby its force has done and exerted something. Herr Wolf promises at the beginning of his treatise to give up this ground, and to prove his proposition independently of it; but he has not kept his word.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0506":["After he had explained what he understands by harmless effects ( *effectus innocuos* ), namely such in the production of which the force does not consume itself; so he posits as a basis a proposition on which his edifice is erected solely and alone, and which we need only take from him in order to make all the effort of his writing fruitless. *Si duo mobilia per spatia inaequalia transferuntur, effectus innocui sunt vt spatia*. This is the proposition that we mean (*). Let us see how he set about proving it. He concludes in the following way: If the effect through the space A is as e, then the effect that happens in an equal or just the same space A is also e; consequently in the space 2 A it is 2 e, in the space 3 A it will be 3 e, i.e. the effects will stand in the proportion of the spaces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0507":["(*) Herr Wolf has therefore, in the motion through a space in which nothing resists the body, i.e. through an empty space, ascribed to it certain effects; and he afterwards makes use of these effects as a measure of the force of the body; consequently he has not fulfilled his promise.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0508":["His proof thus rests on this presupposition: If the body goes through just the same space, then it has also exerted just the same harmless effect. This is the proper point of seduction and of the error, which afterwards spreads over his whole writing. It is not enough that only the space be just the same, if the effect that has been exerted in it by an equal body is also to be the same; one must here take into consideration the speed of the body, with which it covers the space. If this is not likewise equal, then, regardless of all the equality of the space, the harmless effect will nevertheless be different. To comprehend this we must, as we did in § 17, represent the space that the body runs through not as perfectly empty, but as filled with matter, yet with infinitely thin, consequently infinitely little resisting matter. This is done only so that we may have a true effect and a certain subject of it, for otherwise it still remains a harmless effect, just as in the Wolffian argument. If therefore the body covers just as great a space as another that is equal to it: then both have displaced equally much matter, but on that account have not yet always exerted an equal effect. For if the one has run through its space with twice more speed, then all the particles of its space have also received from it, through its effect, twice more speed than the particles of the space that the other body runs through with simple speed, consequently the former body has exerted a greater effect, although the mass and the space covered were equal in both.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0509":["§ 105.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0510":["Yet a principal ground of the Wolffian *Schediasma*.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0511":["So the principle of all the inferences of Herr Wolf is evidently false, and conflicts with that which one can prove most clearly and most certainly from the concepts of acting and of motion. Once one has erred, the sequel is nothing other than a chain of errors. Herr Wolf draws from his principle another, which actually offers to his system all the great consequences that so unexpectedly surprise the reader and put him in wonder. It is called: Because in uniform motion the spaces are in compound ratio of the speeds and times; so the harmless effects are as the masses, times and speeds together. On this he builds the theorem: *Actiones, quibus idem effectus producitur, sunt vt celeritates*.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0512":["Is refuted.",["- 0497: \"Gründe\" (sic, \"Gründe ... angeführet\") rendered \"grounds\"; \"mit Grunde\" = \"with ground\"; \"Endzwecke\" = \"end\"; \"anziehet\" = \"adduces\" (twice), kept literal.","- 0497: \"Sekte\" = \"sect\"; \"Parthey\" = \"party\"; \"Vertheidigungen\" = \"defenses\".","- 0501: \"Hindurchführung\" = \"carrying through\"; \"Satz\" = \"proposition\" throughout (also \"Grundsatz\" = \"principle\"; \"Haupt-Grundsatz\" = \"principal principle\").","- 0501: \"Haufen\" rendered \"heaps\" (literal); \"Flügel\" = \"wings\".","- 0505: \"Grund\" = \"ground\"; \"Kraft\" = \"force\"; \"Raum\" = \"space\"; \"würkliche Bewegung\" = \"actual motion\"; \"Würkung/Wirkung\" both = \"effect\" (Effect/effectus also \"effect\").","- 0506: \"Gebäude\" = \"edifice\"; \"unschädlich\" = \"harmless\"; \"verstehe\" = \"understands\"; \"schliesset\" = \"concludes\" (schließen, not \"folgern\").","- 0508: \"Beweis\" = \"proof\"; \"begreiffen\" = \"comprehend\"; \"vorstellen\" = \"represent\"; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = \"speed\"; \"Erwegung\" = \"consideration\"; \"zweymal mehr\" kept literal as \"twice more\".","- 0511: \"Schlüsse\" = \"inferences\"; \"Folgerungen\" = \"consequences\"; \"Folge\" = \"sequel\"; \"Verhältniß\" = \"ratio\"; \"Theorem\" kept for \"Theorem\"; \"Begriffen\" = \"concepts\".","- 0510: \"Schediasmatis\" is Latinized Greek genitive, rendered *Schediasma* in italics. The OCR \"Neurung\" (0497) retained as \"renewal\" (Neuerung)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0513":["In the proof of this theorem there is found a fallacious inference that is, where possible, still harsher than the one that we have scarcely noticed. He had proved: that, if two equal bodies accomplish the same effect in unequal time, their velocities are related inversely as the times in which these equal effects are produced, that is: that the body that completes its effect in half the time has two degrees of velocity, whereas the other, on the contrary, which must expend the whole time on it, possesses only one degree. From this he concludes: Since everyone admits that that action is twice as great which accomplishes its effect in twice as short a time as another: so the *actiones* in this case will be in inverse ratio of the times, i.e. in the direct ratio of the velocities. Hereupon he goes further, and considers the case in which two different bodies exert the same effect in equal time. He shows: that in this case the velocities will be in inverse ratio of the masses, and concludes further thus: *Quoniam hic eadem est ratio massarum, quae in casu priori erat temporum; ratio vero celeritatum eodem modo se habeat: perinde est, sive massae diversae & tempus idem, sive massae sint eaedem & tempus diversum &c.* This inference is a monster, but not an argument such as one should find in a mathematical treatise. Let one recall: that in the previous case it was said that the *actiones* of two equal bodies, which accomplish equal effect in unequal times, are inversely as the times, only because that action which accomplishes an effect in shorter time is, precisely on that account, and also in precisely the same measure, greater than another, which expends more time on it. Thus this inference holds on this ground, because the shortness of the time in which an effect is completed always testifies to a so much greater action. But if I, as here in the second case, posit the inequality of the masses in place of the inequality of the times, and on the other hand make the times equal; then one easily sees that the inequality of the masses does not have the sequel which the inequality of the times has. For in the former, the body that completed its effect in smaller time had exerted a greater action precisely because the time was smaller; but here the body that has a smaller mass, and with it accomplishes in equal time just as much effect as the other, does not have a greater activity on account of the smallness of its mass. To say this would be wholly absurd; for the smallness of the mass is a true and essential ground on which the smallness of the activity rather rests, and if a body in spite of this smallness of the mass nevertheless exerts in equal time just as much effect as another, one can only conclude: that what is lacking to its *actio* on account of a small mass has been replaced and made up by a greater velocity, and thereby made equal to the *actio* of the other. Thus, if the masses are unequal, but the times and effects equal: then one cannot say that the *actiones* of the bodies are related inversely as their masses, although in the case of unequal times and equal masses this proportion held with regard to the times and *actionum*: It is therefore not one and the same: whether the masses are unequal and the times equal, or whether the times are unequal and the masses equal.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0514":["Thus the proof on which a main theorem in the Wolffian treatise was grounded is invalid and useless; therefore the living forces will find there no land that can nourish them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0515":["There are sometimes in a writing certain moderate errors that do not spread very far, and do not wholly spoil the validity of the main point. But in the one of which we speak, the propositions run down along the method as along a rope; hence one or two errors make the whole system objectionable and unusable.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0516":["§ 106.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0517":["We still have no dynamics.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0518":["Herr Wolf had in his treatise the intention of delivering to us the first foundation for a dynamics. His undertaking has turned out unfortunately. Thus we have as yet no dynamical principles on which we can rightly build. Our writing, which promises to set forth the true estimation of living forces, was to supplement this deficiency. The third chapter is to make an attempt at this; but may one well hope: that one will hit the target, since one of the most experienced in this kind of consideration has not succeeded in reaching it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0519":["§ 107.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0520":["The argument of Herr von Muschenbroek.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0521":["Just as I am about to conclude the refutation of the grounds on which the most famous Leibnizians base their estimation of forces with the preceding case, I receive the *Grundlehren der Naturwissenschaft* of Herr Petrus von Muschenbroek, translated by Herr Professor Gottsched, which appeared in the Easter fair of this year 1747. This great man, the greatest among the investigators of nature of this time, in whose opinions prejudice and sectarian zeal have less of a share than in the theorems of any other man, this so famous philosopher, has subjected the estimation of Herr von Leibniz first to his mathematical investigation, thereafter to the experiments that he knows how to make so skillfully, and has found it confirmed in both. This latter way that he has taken does not belong to the present chapter; but the former does belong to it. The aim of this treatise requires of me to consider the difficulties that the famous author makes there for the estimation of Descartes, and to avert them, where possible, from the object whose defense is our business. But will not the narrow bounds of these pages, or, to express myself frankly, the astonishing inequality that shows itself here, set insurmountable obstacles to me?\n\n---  notes\n- Wirkung/Würkung = \"effect\" throughout; Folge = \"sequel\"; Schluß = \"inference\" (Fehlschluß = \"fallacious inference\"); schliessen = \"conclude\".\n- Gegenstand (0521) = \"object\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Gränzen = \"bounds\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Lehrsatz = \"theorem\"; Satz (pl. Sätze, 0515) = \"propositions\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Kraft = \"force\".\n- \"Ungeheuer, nicht aber ein Argument\" (0513): kept literal, with its awkward \"but not\"; \"Actiones,\" \"Actioni,\" \"Actionum\" are Latin forms in the original, italicized here; \"Action\"/\"Activität\" rendered \"action\"/\"activity\".\n- \"Verhältniß\" = \"ratio\" (also *ratio* in the Latin quotation); \"Verhältnis der Zeiten\" rendered \"ratio of the times.\" \"der geraden von den Geschwindigkeiten\" kept elliptical: \"in the direct ratio of the velocities.\"\n- In 0513, \"Dieser Schluß hat ... Statt\" rendered \"holds\"; \"die Folge nicht habe\" = \"does not have the sequel.\"\n- 0518: \"Dynamick/Dynamik\" = \"dynamics\"; \"Versuch/Versuchten\" = \"attempt\"/\"experienced\" (the latter, \"Versuchtesten,\" is a superlative of versucht; the sense \"most experienced\" is doubtful, since it could also mean \"most tried/attempted\").\n- 0521: \"Muschenbröck\" modernized in English to \"Muschenbroek\"; \"Seckteneifer\" (sic) = \"sectarian zeal\"; \"Naturforscher\" = \"investigators of nature\"; \"Versuchen\" = \"experiments\"; \"Meynungen\" = \"opinions\"; \"Gottscheden\" is the dative form.\n- 0514: \"Land ... das sie nähren kan\" kept literal; \"ungültig\" = \"invalid\" (gültig = valid); \"Gültigkeit\" (0515) = \"validity\".",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0522":["Let us see what grounds they have been that seemed to him, in the mathematical consideration, to prove Leibniz's laws. * If a certain external cause, which moves along together with the pressed body, e.g. a spring BC, which, fastened to the support AS, pushes a body F onward, is given: then it will impart to the same, if it is at rest, 1 degree of velocity. But as soon as this body already possesses this degree, twice as many springs are required to give it the second degree of velocity. For if the simple spring were to extend itself once more alone, the body, which is already actually moving with just the degree of velocity with which the spring extends, would flee from it, and not take up its pressures into itself. But the second spring ** DB must be added, which makes the point B, against which the spring BC braces itself, follow after the body with the velocity with which it would escape, and in this way the body F, as at the beginning, rest with respect to the spring BC, so that it, when this extends itself, receives the degree of velocity as 1. Just so ** three springs ED, DB, BC are required, in order to impart to the body F, which already in itself possesses 2 degrees of velocity, merely the third. To impart to a body that already has 100 degrees a single new one, 101 springs are required, and so on. Thus the number of the springs that are necessary to give a body a certain degree of velocity is as the number of the degrees into which the whole velocity of the body is divided; i.e. the whole force of the springs that communicate to a body a degree of velocity is as the whole velocity that the body would then have if it possessed this degree. Now in the triangle ** ABC, whose cathetus AB has been divided into equal parts, the lines DE, FG, HJ, &c. are as the lines AD, AF, AH, consequently one can make use of the line DE to indicate that spring which imparts to the body the first degree of velocity AD, of the line FG, twice as great, to indicate the double spring that produces the second degree of velocity DF; of the line HJ, to indicate the spring three times as great that awakens the third degree of velocity FH, etc. If one thinks these lines DE, FG, &c. as infinitely near, then, according to the method of the infinitely small, which Cavalieri introduced into geometry, they will make up the whole content of the triangle ABC. Thus the sum of all springs that generate in a body the velocity AB is as the area ABC, i.e. as the square of the velocity AB. These springs, however, represent the forces that together have produced in the body the said velocity, and as the number of forces that act in a body is related, so too is the force produced in the same related; thus the force of a body is as the square of the velocity that it possesses.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0523":["* Fig. XVI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0524":["** Fig. XVII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0525":["* Fig. XVIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0526":["** Fig. XIX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0527":["§ 108.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0528":["Investigation of this argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0529":["I believe a follower of Descartes would raise the following objection against this proof:",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0530":["If one wants to estimate the force transferred into a body according to the sum of certain springs: then one must take only those springs that actually bring their power into the body; but those that have not acted in it at all one also cannot use in order to posit in the body a force equal to them. This proposition is one of the very most distinct of mechanics, and one that no Leibnizian has ever called into doubt. Herr von Musschenbroek himself acknowledges it at the end of his proof; for these are his words: As the number of forces that act in a body is related, so too is the force produced in the same related. But if a body F, which is already moving with 1 degree of velocity, receives the 2nd degree through the extension of the two springs DB, BC; then of these two springs only BC acts in it, but DB brings nothing of its tension-force into it. For the spring DB extends itself with 1 degree of velocity; but the body F is also already actually moving with 1 degree; thus F flees the pressure of this spring, and the same will not be able to reach it in its expansion, in order to transfer the force of its extension into it. It does nothing further than that it carries the support B, against which the spring BC braces itself, after the body F, with just the velocity with which it moves, so that the same, with respect to this body, rest, and the spring BC bring its whole force, which is as 1, into it. It is thus not an acting, but only an occasional cause, of the force that in this way is added in F to the former; the single spring BC, however, is the acting cause of the same. Further, if this body already possesses 2 degrees of velocity, then among the three equal springs ED, DB, BC, only the single BC imparts to it its force and also the third degree of velocity, etc. to infinity. Thus if DE * is the first spring, whose force has come into the body F, and has awakened in it the first degree of velocity AD, then the spring f G, which is equal to it, has given it the second degree of velocity, and transferred its force in it; the spring h I the third degree, etc. consequently the sum of the springs DE + fG + hI + kM + lN + rO + bC = BC makes up the whole magnitude of the force that has been applied to the body F from its rest, and that has awakened in it the velocity AB. But BC is related as AB, and BC is the force, AB however the velocity; thus the force is as the velocity, and not as the square of the same.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0531":["* Fig. XIX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0532":["§ 109.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0533":["New case for the confirmation of the Cartesian measure of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0534":["We are now past all the difficulties that could stand against us in the assertion of the Cartesian law. We want, however, not yet to let this be enough. An opinion that once is in possession of authority, and even of prejudice, must be pursued without end, and chased out of all its hiding places. Such an opinion is like the many-headed monster that hatches new heads after every stroke.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0535":["*Vulneribus foecunda suis erat ille: nec ullum De centum numero caput est impune recisum, Quin gemino cervix haerede valentior esset.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0536":["Ovid. Metam.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0537":["I would hold it very laudable for me if one were to criticize in this work that it had refuted the Leibnizian estimation of forces superfluously and with more grounds than would have been necessary; but I would be ashamed if I had let it lack in this.",["- 0522: Gründe = grounds (Grund); Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Kraft = force; Ursache = cause; Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove; Gesetze = laws (Gesetz).","- 0522: \"Grad\" = degree; \"Inhalt\" = content, as in glossary; \"Fläche\" = area; \"Quadrat\" = square; \"Summe\" = sum; \"Anzahl\" = number (not Zahl).","- 0522: \"wie\" in \"ist wie die Anzahl...\" kept as \"is as\" (proportional); the German is the same wording for \"like\" and \"in proportion to.\"","- 0522: \"Eben * so\" — the asterisk (figure reference) is placed within the phrase as printed; \"Cavalerius\" = Cavalieri (spelling modernized).","- 0530: \"ruhe\" and \"nachfolge\" are subjunctives in dependent clauses (\"daß ... ruhe\"); the English \"rest\" is left in the subjunctive form, as is \"bring.\"","- 0530: \"Gelegenheitsursache\" = occasional cause (*causa occasionalis*); \"würkende Ursache\" = acting cause. \"Qvadrat\" = square. \"Musschenbroeck\" spelling kept.","- 0534: \"Meynung\" = opinion (Meinung); \"Vorurtheil\" = prejudice; \"gnug\" = enough. \"vielköpfigte\" printed so (for vielköpfig).","- 0535: Latin verse (Ovid, Met. IX) left untranslated; \"haerede\" is the printed form (usual haerede/herede)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0538":["Take an inclined steelyard * ACB, of which the one arm CB is four times the other AC, the body B, however, which presses on the end of the fourfold arm, being a quarter part with respect to the other, A. These will rest in the position in which we have placed them and stand in perfect equilibrium against one another. Hang on to the body A still a small weight e; then the body B will be raised through the arc B b, and A, on the contrary, will sink down through the arc A a, but the body B will in this motion receive four times more velocity than A. Take the weight e away, and hang on, in its place, to the body b at the end of the steelyard arm Cb a weight d four times smaller; then b will be pressed down through the arc b B, but a will be raised up through the arc a A; but b, which is one and the same with B, will thereby receive just as much velocity as in the first case, likewise a, which is one and the same with A, will now likewise get its velocity, which was brought into it in the first case; only with this difference: that the direction of the motions is reversed. Since now the effect that the attached weight e exerts consists in the force that the bodies A and B have together, and the effect that the four times smaller d accomplishes is likewise to be posited in that force which b = B and a = A thereby receive together; so it is clear: that these weights e and d must have exerted equally great effects, consequently must have applied and so also have had equally much force. But the velocities with which these weights e and d work (namely both their initial velocities and the final velocities that they receive through the accumulation of all these pressings) are inversely as their masses: therefore two bodies whose velocities are in inverse proportion to their masses have equal forces; which overturns the estimation according to the square.",["- Wirkung/Würkung both rendered \"effect\"; würken rendered \"work\" (Kraft = force, Kräfte = forces).","- \"viertheilig\": rendered \"a quarter part\"; syntax of \"gegen den andern A viertheilig ist\" kept literal and obscure.","- \"Hänget ... hinzu\" imperatives rendered literally (\"Hang on to\"); \"hinweg\" = \"away\".","- \"Schätzung nach dem Quadrate\" = \"estimation according to the square\"; Schätzung is not in the glossary.","- \"Gewichter\" (plural of Gewicht) rendered \"weights\"; \"Druckungen\" rendered \"pressings\".","- \"Waagarmes\"/\"Schnellwaage\": \"steelyard arm\"/\"steelyard\". \"Massen\" = \"masses\".","- The asterisk after \"Schnellwaage\" is the footnote marker for the figure note (passage 0539)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0539":["* Fig. XX.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage; \"Fig.\" = Figure."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0540":["§ 110.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0541":["Leibnitz's knot of doubt.",["- \"Zweifelsknoten\" rendered \"knot of doubt\"; the spelling Leibnitz kept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0542":["The Cartesians could never have defied the defenders of the new measure of forces with more confidence than after Mr. Jurin found the case through which one sees in a simple way and with sun-clear distinctness: that the doubling of the velocity always posits only the doubling of the force. Herr von Leibnitz denied this especially in the Essay of a Dynamical Treatise that he incorporated into the Acta *. One need only hear him speak in the following manner: *Cum igitur comparare vellem corpora diversa, aut diversis celeritatibus praedita, equidem facile vidi: si corpus A sit simplum, & B duplum, utriusque autem celeritas aequalis, illius quoque vim esse simplam, hujus duplam, cum praecise quicquid in illo ponitur semel, in hoc ponatur bis. Nam in B est bis corpus ipsi A aequale, & aequivelox necquicquam ultra. Sed si corpora A & C sint aequalia, celeritas autem in A sit simpla, & in C dupla, videbam non praecise quod in A est duplari in C*. This knot Mr. Jurin resolved through the easiest case in the world.",["- Latin quotation retained in italics; OCR \"ponaturbis\" corrected to \"ponatur bis\"; a full stop or punctuation after \"C\" in the source seems lost, left as is.","- \"Deutlichkeit\" = \"distinctness\" (deutlich = distinct); \"setzen\" = \"posit\".","- \"Herr\" rendered \"Mr.\" for Jurin and \"Herr von\" kept for Leibnitz as in the German.","- \"Kräftenmaaß\" = \"measure of forces\"; \"Versuch\" rendered \"Essay\".","- The asterisk after \"Actis\" marks the footnote (passage 0543)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0543":["* Acta 1695. p. 155.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0544":["Resolution of Mr. Jurin.",["- \"Auflösung\" rendered \"resolution\" (cf. \"aufgelöset\" = \"resolved\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0545":["He assumed a movable surface, e.g. ** a boat AB, which moves in the direction BC with the velocity as 1 and carries the ball E along with itself with equal motion. This ball thus has, through the motion of the surface, the velocity 1, and also the force 1. He assumes further on this surface a spring R, which snaps loose against the support D, and imparts to the said ball E, for its part, yet one degree of velocity, and thus also one degree of force. Thus the same has received together two degrees of velocity, and with these two degrees of force. Consequently the doubling of the velocity draws after it nothing more than the doubling of the force, and not, as the Leibnitzians falsely persuade themselves, the quadrupling of it.",["- \"Grad\" = \"degree\"; \"Kraft\" = \"force\"; \"überreden\" rendered \"persuade\" (Überredung = persuasion).","- \"Widerhalt\" rendered \"support\"; \"vor sich\" rendered \"for its part\".","- \"Vervierfachung derselben\": \"derselben\" refers back to Kraft or Geschwindigkeit-doubling ambiguously; rendered \"of it\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0546":["** Fig. XXI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0547":["This proof is infinitely distinct, and admits of no evasion at all, for the motion of the surface can do nothing more than that it impart to the body a velocity that is equal to its own, that is, a simple velocity, and consequently also a simple force. The spring R, however, because it has a motion in common with the surface and ball at the same time, works with nothing but its tension-force. This now is just so great that it can impart to a body such as ours is no more than one degree of velocity, and thus also only one degree of force. Thus one will encounter in everything that enters into the construction of this problem nothing more than the causes of 2 degrees of force, let one turn wherever one will, and yet in the body 2 degrees of velocity will actually be present.",["- \"Beweis\" = \"proof\"; \"deutlich\" = \"distinct\"; \"Aufgabe/Problem\" rendered \"problem\".","- \"Ausflucht\" rendered \"evasion\"; \"Spannungs-Kraft\" = \"tension-force\"; \"Construction\" kept as \"construction\".","- \"würklich\" = \"actually\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0548":["§ 111.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0549":["Madame de Chastelet's objection against Jurin's argument.",["- \"Frau von Chastelet\" rendered \"Madame de Chastelet\"; \"Einwurf\" = \"objection\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0550":["The Marquise de Chastelet has contested this argument of Mr. Jurin's, but in a manner whose weakness she would have been astute enough to notice, if the inclination toward an opinion on which the choice has once fallen could not give the finest gloss to a bad cause.",["- \"Meynung\" = \"opinion\"; \"Sache\" rendered \"cause\" (also \"matter\"); \"Anstrich\" rendered \"gloss\".","- \"Marquisin\" rendered \"Marquise\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0551":["She objected as follows. The boat AB is no immovable surface; consequently, when the spring R braces itself against the support D, it will bring certain forces into the boat, and one will thus find again in the mass of the boat the 2 degrees of force that one misses in the body E according to the Leibnitzian estimation.",["- \"Wiederhalt\" (as spelled here) = \"support\"; \"Schätzung\" = \"estimation\"; \"Masse\" = \"mass\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0552":["§ 112.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0553":["In this evasion is found the fault of that fallacy which one calls *fallaciam ignorationis elenchi*. It does not properly attack the argument of its opponent there where he has laid the nerve of his proof; rather it concerns itself with a contingent side-circumstance that seems to be favorable to its opinion, but that does not necessarily cling to the Jurinian proof. We can easily clear this stumbling block out of the way. Nothing hinders us from representing to ourselves the boat AB as driven by such a force as does not permit it, by virtue of the exertion of the spring against D, to give way back in the direction AF in the least. One need only think of it for this end as of infinitely great mass. The boat will then, through the finite force of the spring R, yield only infinitely little, i.e., not at all; thus the body will receive just the force from this spring as if the same, tensed against an entirely immovable support, snapped loose, i.e., it will receive its entire force.",["- \"Trugschluß\" rendered \"fallacy\"; Latin phrase kept in italics as in the source (accusative form as printed).","- \"zufällig\" = \"contingent\"; \"nothwendig\" = \"necessarily\"; \"Beweis\" = \"proof\"; \"vorstellen\" = \"represent\"; \"gedenken\" = \"think\".","- \"sie\" in the first sentence refers to the Ausflucht (feminine); rendered \"It\". \"dieselbe\" in the last sentence rendered \"the same\".","- \"Kraft\" rendered \"force\" throughout."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0554":["§ 113.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0555":["Mr. Richter's objection against Jurin's argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0556":["Mr. Richter, who in the list of those who have made their contribution to the upholding of the new measure of forces deserves no small place, has brought forward a somewhat more plausible objection against Jurin's argument. *",["- \"scheinbar\" rendered \"plausible\" (it also carries the sense of \"apparent\"; Schein = illusion).","- \"Emporhaltung\" rendered \"upholding\". The asterisk marks the footnote (passage 0557)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0557":["* Act. Erud. 1735 p. 511.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0558":["He believes that just the same force could be very unequal in relation to different things. The spring R, he holds, has indeed imparted to the ball E a force as 1 with regard to those things that move simultaneously with the boat in one direction and velocity, but with regard to the objects [*Gegenstände*] that actually rest outside the boat the spring has given the ball not a simple but a triple force.",["- \"Gegenstände\" = \"objects\"; the German is given in brackets here as the only occurrence in the passage.","- \"habe ... ertheilet\" and \"habe ... gegeben\" are subjunctive reported speech, rendered \"he holds ... has imparted\".","- The bracketed \"[*Gegenstände*]\" is my own gloss, not an editorial insertion by the Academy Edition.","- \"Ansehung\" rendered \"regard\"; \"Dinge\" = \"things\".","- The passage appears to break off here; the following section is not given."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0559":["I would gladly know where the two degrees of force, which according to Mr. Richter's opinion the body E obtains in relation to the objects at rest, are supposed to come from; for they surely cannot have arisen in it on account of an empty abstraction or an idle thought; rather there must absolutely be active causes and forces, through which they should have been produced. But if everything is in absolute rest with respect to the external things, and the boat begins to move with one degree of velocity, then there arises hereby in the body E one degree of absolute force. From then on the boat already has no effect any more on the body; for it rests with regard to it, but the tension-force of the spring begins to let out its activity. This now has just exactly as much as is required for the production of one degree of force; more one will seek in it in vain. Thus no more absolute effect has been exerted in the body than only as much as one reckons to 2 degrees of force. If now in relation to the things at rest, i.e., in the absolute sense, 4 degrees of force were supposed to have arisen in the body, and yet no more than 2 degrees of absolute effect had been exerted in it, then 2 degrees must have arisen by chance and without cause, or have crept forth out of nothing.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0560":["To avoid every scruple entirely, if in so clear a matter any scruple takes place at all, one can arrange the case of Mr. Jurin thus: that, if everything is in absolute rest, the body E first receives from the spring one degree of velocity, while the boat still rests, so this force obtained by the body E will indisputably be an absolute force. If now the boat then also begins to move with one degree: then this is again an absolute motion, because it rested before with respect to all things. It thus communicates to everything that belongs to its mass, consequently also to the body E, again one degree of force, which, because the cause that generated it has worked in absolute motion, can be no more than simple from the same. Thus in this way too there arise in all no more than 2 degrees of force for the body E.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0561":["Mr. Richter seeks to wind himself out with yet another evasion, which he takes from the impact of elastic bodies. But his justification is built upon the common hypothesis of the Leibnizians: that after the impact of elastic bodies one must find precisely the force that was there before the impact. We have refuted this presupposition; thus it is not necessary to engage with Mr. Richter here in particular.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0562":["§ 113.\nAdditions and clarifications\nthat concern some pieces of this chapter.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0563":["I.\nClarification of the 25th §.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0564":["Because the theorem of this § is the foremost foundation of our present considerations, we will present it in a somewhat more distinct shape.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0565":["More distinct presentation of the 25th §.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0566":["The mark of an actual motion is a finite duration of the same. This duration, however, or the time elapsed from the beginning of the motion, is indeterminate, can thus be assumed at will. If accordingly the line A B * represents the finite time elapsing during the motion: then the body has in B an actual motion, further in C, as the half, also in D, as the point of the quarter, and so on in all still smaller parts of this time, one may make it as small as one wishes into the infinite; for the indeterminate concept of its magnitude allows this. Thus I can think this time infinitely small, without anything thereby being lacking to the concept of the actuality of the motion. But if the time of this duration is infinitely small, then it is to be reckoned as nothing, and the body is only in the starting point, i.e., in a mere striving toward motion. Consequently, if it is true without further limitation, as Leibniz's law demands, that the force of the body in every actual motion has the square as its measure: then it is also so constituted in mere striving toward motion; which they themselves must nevertheless deny.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0567":["* Tab. I. Fig. 2.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0568":["Whence the undetermined concept of finite time includes within itself the infinitely small.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0569":["It seems at first sight as if Leibniz's law were sufficiently secured, through the limitation of finite elapsed time attached to it, from being able to be extended to the motion whose duration is infinitely small; for finite time is indeed a concept that indicates a genus entirely different from infinitely small time: thus it has the appearance that, with this limitation, that can absolutely not be extended to infinitely small time which is admitted only under the condition of the finite. This too has its correctness: if one speaks of finite time in such a way that one presupposes thereby that it must be determinate, and its magnitude determined, if this or that property is to flow from it, as a condition. But if one requires a finite time, but allows thereby that one can take it as large or small as one wishes: then infinitely small time too is included in its genus. This cannot be unknown to the Leibnizians. For they must know that their progenitor built the law of continuity on this ground: that namely, if one assumes A is greater than B, yet so that it is indeterminate how much or how little greater it is, then one will be able, without doing damage to the laws that are true under this condition, also to say A is equal to B, or, if one lets A run against B, and assumes that B also moves, then one will, if the degree of this its motion is indeterminate, also be able to assume that B rests, without what is established under that condition thereby being able to be canceled, and so in other cases more.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0570":["Leibniz's estimation does not hold under the condition of finite velocity either.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0571":["If one wanted finally to say yet: that Leibniz's estimation is indeed not true under the condition of finite time, but nevertheless under the presupposition of finite velocity, (although this would obviously be contrary to their doctrine,) then note: that one can represent finite velocity just as well as finite time through the line A B *, and then it will likewise be shown that, if their law holds in general for finite velocity, it must also hold for infinitely small velocity, which they themselves nevertheless cannot avoid denying.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0572":["* Tab. I. Fig. II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0573":["II.\nAdditions to the §§ 31 to 36.",["- Merkmahl/Merkmal = mark; Betrachtungen = considerations; Erläuterung = clarification; Bestrebung = striving; Grad = degree; Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Würkliche/Wirklichkeit = actual/actuality.","- Gegenstände (0559) and Dinge both rendered \"objects\"/\"things\" per glossary; Gegenstand = object, Ding = thing.","- 0559 \"in absolutem Verstande\": Verstand here rendered \"sense\" (as in \"in the absolute sense\"), not \"understanding\"; a literal \"in absolute understanding\" was avoided as it clearly does not mean the faculty.","- 0559 \"in Ansehung seiner\": \"with regard to it\" (seiner could refer to the body or the boat; German ambiguous, kept as is by \"it\").","- 0560 \"von derselben nicht mehr wie einfach seyn kan\": \"from the same\" kept ambiguous; \"derselben\" could refer to the Bewegung or the Ursache.","- 0560 \"vor den Körper E\": \"for the body E\" (vor = für).","- 0561 \"Stoß\" = impact; \"Voraussetzung\" = presupposition; \"Hypothese\" = hypothesis.","- 0566, 0569, 0571 \"Geschlecht(e)\" in 0569 rendered \"genus\" (Gattung is the glossary's genus term; Geschlecht is not listed). \"Schätzung\" = estimation. \"Maaße\" = measure. \"Quadrat\" = square (no further word in German).","- Asterisks after \"A B\" mark footnote signs, retained; footnotes 0567/0572 given as separate passages. Spellings \"Tab. I. Fig. 2./II.\" as in source."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0574":["Our opponents count it among the clearest concepts that one can only have: that a body has precisely the force of all the springs that it compresses, until its entire motion has been taken from it, the time in which these springs are compressed be as it may. Herr Johann Bernoulli says of those who are not satisfied with the number of the overcome springs alone, but still keep asking after the time of the compression, that they would be just as absurd as someone who wants to measure the multitude of water in a beaker, and is not content with the actual measure that he has before him, namely the capacity of the beaker, but opines that he must also know the time in which this beaker was filled. Out of confidence and indignation he adds *: *Desine igitur quaerere nodum in scirpo*. The Marquise of Chastelet has an equally jocular notion in readiness; but they both err, and indeed, if I may be permitted to say so, with just as great a detriment to their renown as was the confidence that they have let show in this error.",["- Kraft = force; Menge = multitude (glossary); Capacität = capacity (Latinate; Fähigkeit/Vermögen in glossary are also \"capacity\").","- \"nodumin scirpo\" in the source is an OCR run-together; read as *nodum in scirpo*.","- \"Desine igitur quaerere nodum in scirpo\" is left in Latin as in the source; \"mit eben so großem Nachtheile ihres Ruhmes, als die Zuversicht war\" preserved literally; the comparison is as obscure as in German.","- \"opines\" renders *meynet*; \"Federn\" = springs; \"würklichen\" = actual."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0575":["* *Acta Erud.* 1735, p. 210.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage; *Acta Erud.* = *Acta Eruditorum* (abbreviation retained)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0576":["Whence time necessarily comes into account in the case of the hindrance of gravity.",["- Hinderniß = hindrance; Schwere = gravity; \"in Anschlag kommt\" rendered \"comes into account\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0577":["If each of the springs A, B, C, D, E is of such a kind that it resists only a single pressure of the body M, and at the same time thereby loses its entire activity, and consequently afterward does no more effect at all in the body M, it may be exposed to it as long as it will: then I myself admit that the body has exerted the same force, whether it has compressed these springs in single or in fourfold time, for after it has compressed them once, it spends the remaining time idle with them. If on the contrary the force of the body does not at the same time cancel the activity of the spring whose pressure it overcomes: then from the spring into the counteracting body new degrees of force pass over at every moment; for the efficacy of this spring, which in the first moment was the cause of a degree of force extinguished in the body, still is so, and indeed just as strongly, in the second moment, further in the third, and so on in all the following ones to infinity. Under these conditions it is not the same whether the body that overcomes the pressure of this spring does it in shorter or in longer time; for in the longer it has endured more pressures than in the shorter. Now, however, the pressure of gravity is of this kind. Each spring of it acts at every moment with equal activity, and the body that overcomes its pressure in the first moment has on that account not yet done so for all the following moments. It will need just as much force for the second, and so forth. The force, therefore, that a body expends in order to offer resistance to the compression of a single part of the gravity-making matter, is not merely as the intensity of the gravity-pressure, but as the rectangle of this into the time.",["- Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Thätigkeit = activity; Wirksamkeit = efficacy; Grad = degree; Materie = matter; Theil = part.","- \"er mag ihr so lange ausgesetzet seyn, als er wolle\": \"er\" (the body) and \"ihr\" (the spring) kept as in German; the clause stands unmarked between the \"if\" clause and the \"so\" clause, as in the original.","- \"Rectangulum\" is Latinate; rendered \"rectangle\". \"Intensität\" = intensity. \"Drückung/Druck\" both rendered \"pressure\"/\"compression\" by context: Druck = pressure, Drückung = pressure (compression for zudrücken).","- \"einfacher, oder vierfacher Zeit\" = \"single or fourfold time\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0578":["Yet a proof against the living forces.",["- *lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\" (*vis viva*, not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0579":["To the superfluous proof of the proposition: that not the number of the springs but the time is the measure of the effect exerted, one can add this as well. A body thrown obliquely, whose motion is parabolic, would have to traverse a certain height by the fall much more quickly, and also acquire at the end of it a much greater speed and force than the perpendicular fall from the same height could impart to it. For in that it describes the curved line, it runs through up to the end of the fall a greater space than if it had fallen vertically. In that greater space, however, it must necessarily endure more springs of gravity than it could encounter in the short straight line, for the gravity-pressing matter is equally spread on all sides: thus it would have to, according to Leibniz's proposition, attain more force and speed in the former than in the latter, which is absurd.",["- Satz = proposition (also \"principle\"); Raum = space; Geschwindigkeit = speed.","- \"schreg\" = obliquely; \"so wohl ... als auch\" kept as a correlative, the sense of \"a certain height\" being obscure in German (\"eine gewisse Höhe weit schneller\" retained in order).","- \"ungereimt\" = absurd; \"vertikal\" = vertically."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0580":["Thoughts on the Dispute\nbetween\nthe Marquise of Chastelet\nand Herr von Mairan,\non the Living Forces.",["- Title as heading; lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0581":["Herr von Mairan has come upon the plan of estimating the force of a body according to the obstacles not overcome, springs not compressed, matters not displaced, or, as Madame de Chastelet expresses herself, according to that which it does not do. This opponent has believed she found something so odd in this thought that she supposed she need only cite it in order to make it ridiculous. Notwithstanding that this famous man has attached to his thought a limitation, on which everything properly depends, namely: that these springs would nevertheless have been compressed if one assumed through a hypothesis that it had retained its force, or had again and again assumed it, his opponent nevertheless finds something so impermissible and unauthorized in this hypothesis that she makes him on that account a still much harsher reproach. I shall show briefly how certain and unerring the thought of this excellent man is, and that, except for Herr Jurin's, which we have already cited, nothing more decisive and thorough could easily have been devised in this matter.",["- Gegnerinn = opponent (feminine); \"Anschlag\" = plan; \"schätzen\" = estimate; Hindernisse = obstacles.","- \"daß er seine Kraft behalten, oder immer wieder angenommen hatte\": subject \"er\" is ambiguous (the body); \"hatte\" (indicative) is as in the source, perhaps for *hätte*. Rendered \"had\".","- \"seinem\" in \"des Herrn Jurins seinem\" = \"Herr Jurin's [thought]\", noun omitted as in German.","- \"ersonnen\" = devised; \"würden zugedrückt worden seyn\" kept in the compound form."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0582":["Defense of the Manner of Estimation of Herr von Mairan against Madame de Chastelet.",["- Schätzungs-Art = manner of estimation (Art = manner, glossary). \"Vertheidung\" in the source is read as *Vertheidigung*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0583":["If one takes that which the force of a body has forfeited in that certain obstacles have been overcome by it, if one, I say, measures this forfeiture: then one knows with the greatest certainty how great the total power of the overcome resistance has been; for the body could not have overcome this resistance or obstacle without expending a degree of force equal to it thereby, and however great this force annihilated and consumed in the body is, so strong too has been the obstacle that has taken it from it, and also the effect that has been exerted in the same way.",["- Gewalt = power (not Kraft/force, which is \"force\"); Widerstand = resistance; Hinderniß = obstacle (in this passage; \"hindrance\" in the heading above).","- \"dieselbe\" in \"durch dieselbe\" refers back to Kraft (feminine); \"ihr gleichen Grad\" — \"ihr\" refers to resistance or obstacle (both feminine/neuter possibility: Hinderniß is feminine). Left as ambiguous.","- Einbusse = forfeiture; \"zernichtete und verzehrte\" = annihilated and consumed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0584":["Assume now a body* that rises vertically from the horizon with five degrees of velocity, and express the space, or the height that it reaches, as usual through the content of the triangle A B C, in which the line A B is to express the elapsed time, but B C the velocity with which it raises itself to the height. The equal lines AD, DF, FH, etc. are to express the elements of the whole time AB, consequently the small triangles of which the area of the large one is composed, and which are all as large as A D E, the elements of the whole space, or the number of all springs that the body compresses within the time A B. Accordingly our body, in the first small portion of time BK, in which it begins to rise to the height, compresses the 9 springs that it encounters in the space KL BC. It would, however, if the holding back of these springs had not consumed any force in it, or if this loss had always been replaced from elsewhere, still have compressed in addition the spring LlC, which it cannot now compress, because just as much force as it must have for this has been used up in it in the compression of the others. Thus the spring LlC is the measure of that force that the resistance of the 9 compressed springs has consumed in our body. After it has now accomplished this, it continues, with the remainder of its force that was left over to it after the indicated loss, to rise further to the height, and compresses in the second small portion of time K H the 7 springs that are encountered in the space HI KL. Here it is now clear anew: that if our body had been able to compress these 7 springs, and yet its force had remained entire to it, then it would in just the same minute have compressed and overpowered the spring Ii L in addition; but, since it has not done this, it follows: that it, through the compression of the 7 remaining springs, has lost the degree, the supplementation of which would have put it in the position to overpower Ii L in addition; consequently this spring indicates the magnitude of the loss that the resistance of the 7 springs has drawn upon its force. In just this way the spring G gi will give to be recognized the loss of force through the holdings back of gravity in the third small portion of time FH, and so on. Thus then the loss that the body rising freely to the height suffers, in that it overcomes the hindrance of gravity, is as the sum of the uncompressed springs Llc, IiL, Ggi, Eeg, AaE, consequently also the quantity of the hindrance itself that it has subdued, and hence its force, in this proportion. And, since the uncompressed springs have the ratio of the times or velocities, the force of the body is also as these. Q. E. D.",["- \"W. Z. E.\" (was zu erweisen war) rendered \"Q. E. D.\"; the German abbreviation is left unexpanded in the source.","- Zeittheilchen = \"small portion of time\"; Triangel = \"triangle\"; Feder = \"spring\"; Maaß = \"measure\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Widerstand = \"resistance\"; Hinderniß = \"hindrance\".","- \"zu erkennen geben\" rendered \"give to be recognized\" (erkennen = cognize/recognize).","- Figure letters (LlC, Ii L, Ggi, etc.) are kept as printed; \"Llc\" in the penultimate sentence differs from \"LlC\" earlier (possible misprint or OCR), left as it stands.","- \"Anzahl\" = \"number\" (not Zahl); \"Raum\" = \"space\"; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0585":["* Compare Fig. XXII.",["- Footnote to the asterisk after \"Körper annehmen\" in the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0586":["It becomes clear further from this why Herr von Mairan is entitled to assume, through a hypothesis, that the body has overcome hindrances and yet retained its force entire, which at first seems to contradict the first fundamental law of motions. For the hindrances indeed take from it a part of the force equal to them; but it is nevertheless free always to replace this deficit in thought from elsewhere, and nevertheless to keep the body unharmed, so that one may see how much more it would do, with force undiminished in this way, than if that had remained lost which the hindrance had consumed. This will then furnish the whole measure of that force that the resistance actually takes from the body, because it gives to be recognized what degree one must add, so that the body would have lost nothing.",["- Grundgesetz = \"fundamental law\"; Gesetz = law. \"Hypothese\" = \"hypothesis\".","- \"was vor einen Grad\" = \"what degree\"; \"vor\" for \"für\" kept literal as \"what ... degree\".","- \"zu erkennen giebt\" = \"gives to be recognized\" (erkennen).","- \"Hinderniß/Hindernisse\" = \"hindrance(s)\"; \"wiedersprechen\" (sic) = \"contradict\" (Widerspruch = contradiction)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0587":["I cannot help making here a remark on that way in which the Marquise attacks the theorems of her opponent. It seems to me she could have chosen no better method of delivering him the most sensitive blow than, in that she is occupied with giving to his inferences the look of something strange and absurd. A serious representation lures the reader to the proper attentiveness and investigation, and leaves the soul open to all grounds that can penetrate into it from one side or the other. But the whimsical figure under which she lets the opinions of her opponent appear seizes at once the weak side of the reader, and annihilates in him the desire for a closer consideration. That force of the soul which governs judgment and meditation is of a sluggish and quiet nature; it is pleased to encounter the point of its rest, and gladly stands still at that which absolves it from laborious reflection; therefore it lets itself easily be won over by such representations as put the one of two opinions at once below probability, and declare the trouble of further investigations unnecessary. Our philosopher could therefore have used her *ridendo dicere verum*, or the notion of telling her opponent the truth in laughter, with more fairness, and perhaps also with better success, if her opponent had been incapable of serious grounds, and one had wanted to let him feel his worthiness of being laughed at. The remark that I make here would have, against any other person of her sex, the appearance of ill-mannered conduct, and of a certain behavior that one calls pedantic; but the preeminence of understanding and of science in that person of whom I speak, which sets her above all the rest of her sex, and also above a great part of the other, deprives her at the same time of that which is the proper prerogative of the fairer part of human beings, namely flattery and the praises that have it as their ground.",["- Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Meynung = \"opinion\"; Seele = \"soul\"; Grund (Gründe) = \"ground(s)\"; the last \"zum Grunde haben\" = \"have as their ground\".","- Beurteilung = \"judgment\" (as the glossary); Nachsinnen = \"meditation\"; Nachdenken = \"reflection\"; Überlegung not used. Erwegung = \"consideration\" (Betrachtung in glossary; different German word).","- Wahrscheinlichkeit = \"probability\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\".","- Lehrsätze = \"theorems\"; Schlüsse = \"inferences\".","- \"Die Frau Marquisin\" = \"the Marquise\" (Mme du Châtelet); \"ridendo dicere verum\" is Latin in roman type, italicized.","- \"Auslachenswürdigkeit\" rendered \"worthiness of being laughed at\"; \"Einfall\" = \"notion\".","- \"Schmeicheley und der Lobsprüche, die dieselbe zum Grunde haben\": \"dieselbe\" (sg.) probably refers to Schmeicheley; kept as \"it\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0588":["The choice of Herr von Mairan becomes excellent further through this: that the springs, which in his method are the measure of the force expended, are not only equal, but would also have been compressed in equal times; consequently the Leibnizians are satisfied, who insist on an equality of space, when they are to admit that the force is equal, as well as the Cartesians, who require this with regard to time.",["- Leibnitzianer = \"Leibnizians\" (American spelling normalized); Cartesianer = \"Cartesians\".","- \"seyn\" in \"das Maaß ... seyn\" (subjunctive/indirect) rendered as \"are\"; the German tense mixture (\"seyn zugedrücket worden\") rendered \"would have been compressed\".","- \"Gleichheit\" = \"equality\"; Raum = \"space\"; Zeit = \"time\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0589":["III.\nAdditions to §§ 45, 46, 47.",["- \"Zusätze\" = \"Additions\"; \"§.\" with plural sections rendered \"§§\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0590":["It seems to me I could have said nothing more certain and irrefutable than that a spring cannot possibly push a body forward if it does not brace itself with just the same force against a resistance, and press against it just as strongly as it pushes the body on the other side with its tension-force; and consequently, since in Mr. Bernoulli's case there is no other resistance than the body B, it must apply just the same force of exertion against it as it can apply against A; for the spring would not push the body A forward at all if B did not maintain it in its tension, in that it resists its extension; hence the latter, because it is no immovable resistance, likewise receives all the force that the spring brings into A. Although the whole world thinks in the same way, Mr. Johann Bernoulli nevertheless found in the opposite, I do not know what kind of bright light, on which he grounded an insurmountable confidence. He says: *Non capio, quid pertinacissimus adversarius, si vel scepticus esset, huic evidentissimae demonstrationi opponere queat*, and soon after: *Certe, in nostra potestate non est, aliquem eo adigere, vt fateatur, diescere, quando videmus solem horizontem ascendere*. Let us not look upon this accident of human reason, in the person of so great a man, with indifference, but learn from it to place a wise mistrust even in our greatest conviction, and always to presume that even then we are not yet out of danger of deceiving ourselves; so that the understanding may maintain itself in its equilibrium at least until it has gained time to get to know the circumstances, the proof, and the opposite, in sufficient examination.",["- Körper = \"body\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Gewalt also \"force\" (Gewalt and Kraft both rendered \"force\"); Spannungskraft = \"tension-force\".","- Wiederhalt = \"resistance\" (literal sense \"counter-hold\"; rendered by one word throughout); wiederstrebet = \"resists\".","- Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"; Beweis = \"proof\".","- Zufall = \"accident\" (could also be \"contingency\"); the Latin is carried over as printed, with \"diescere\" as it stands (doubtful reading; possibly a corrupt or abbreviated text, not emended).","- \"die ganze Welt\" = \"the whole world\" (Welt)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0591":["In this very treatise of which we speak, Mr. Bernoulli shows: How one can impart to a body just the same force, in a shorter time, through the pressure of an equal number of springs. In so far as it properly concerns our business, I have already answered enough to this; but here I will add one more observation which indeed does not concern our undertaking, yet can nevertheless have its particular use. He says there: the ball F will always receive equal force from the 4 springs a, b, c, d, whether one puts them together in one line, as Fig. 23, or in two parts next to one another, as Fig. 24, or in 4 such divisions, as the 25th Fig. shows.",["- Beobachtung = \"observation\"; besonderen = \"particular\"; Zertheilungen = \"divisions\" (Teilung/Einteilung both \"division\").","- Kraft = \"force\"; Geschäfte = \"business\"; Vorhaben = \"undertaking\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0592":["Remark on the way in which Mr. Bernoulli believes he can bring into a body the whole force of many springs.",["- Erinnerung = \"remark\" (also \"reminder\"); Art = \"way\".","- vermeynet = \"believes\" (also \"supposes\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0593":["Here one should note the following caution. His thought is true only in such circumstances in which the springs a, b, c, d hanging one behind the other do not impart a greater speed than that with which one of these springs, separated, would spring open by itself alone; for as soon as this is so, it fails if one wants, according to Mr. Bernoulli's plan, to give the body, through springs connected next to one another, just the same speed as they can impart to it one after another in a row. Namely, let the speed that the row of springs in the 23rd figure imparts to the body, until they have extended completely, be as 10, but the speed with which one of them, e.g. a, by itself alone, namely without its pushing a body forward, springs open, as 8: then it is clear that in the method of the 25th figure the 4 springs will be able to impart to the body only 8 degrees of speed. For as soon as the body has received these degrees, it has just as much speed as the springs that are to push it forward themselves have when they spring open freely; thus they will then not be able to bring anything more into it. Meanwhile it is nevertheless indisputable that if this body F is to press these 4 springs shut again by its run-up in the 25th figure, it needs just as well 10 whole degrees of force for this as in the 23rd or 24th. But because this very 25th figure can be the depiction of the elastic force of any body, it is evident from this that it is possible that a perfectly elastic body can run against an immovable resistance with a certain speed, and that notwithstanding this the speed with which it rebounds can be much smaller than that with which it had struck. But if one nevertheless wants these 4 springs to impart their whole force to the body that they push, then one must add to the mass F another 2⁄10, for then the 4 springs will replace in the multitude of the matter that which they cannot bring in with the speed.",["- Geschwindigkeit = \"speed\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Masse = \"mass\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Menge = \"multitude\".","- Wiederhalt = \"resistance\"; Anschlag = \"plan\"; Abbildung = \"depiction\"; Cautele = \"caution\" (printed as Latinate).","- \"Anlauf\" = \"run-up\"; \"anlaufen\" = \"run against\"; \"zurück prallet\" = \"rebounds\"; \"angestoßen hatte\" = \"had struck\" (the subject is left as in the German)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0594":["IV.\nClarification of the 105th §.",["- Erläuterung = \"clarification\" (glossary); §. = \"§\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0595":["Detailed exposition of the errors in the Wolffian proof.",["- Ausführliche Darlegung = \"detailed exposition\" (not the glossary's Erörterung/expositio); Beweis = \"proof\"; Fehler = \"errors\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0596":["I have not explained myself distinctly enough, where on p. 147 I wanted to point out the uncommon error in the argument of Baron Wolff. At first glance it seems as if the inference came out in it still mathematical enough, namely in accordance with the rule, *aequales rationes sibi substitui invicem possunt*; but in fact it has no community at all with it. The preceding case was this: *Tempora, quibus duo mobilia, si sunt aequalia, eosdem effectus patrant, sunt reciproce vt celeritates*. Upon this follows in the second number of the proof: *Massae corporum inaequalium, quae eosdem effectus patrant, sunt reciproce vt celeritates*. From this Mr. Wolff now concludes (for so his argument runs, if one resolves it properly:) because the relation of the times and of the masses in both cases is equal to the relation of the speeds: so they will be equal among one another. This can be approved, but only if one does not lose sight of the determinations under which they are equal to one another, namely: that the masses of unequal bodies that do the same effect behave just as the times in which *NB.* equal bodies perform just the same effect, for that is the limitation which, as one can see, attaches to the relations. But Mr. Wolff's inference is this: therefore the masses of these bodies behave as the times in which these very unequal bodies perform their equal effect; which is an evident falsification of the given proportion.",["- Schluß = \"inference\"; folgert = \"concludes\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Zeit = \"time\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\" (Schranke is the glossary's \"limitation\").","- Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\" (also \"ratio/proportion\"; Proportion = \"proportion\"); Gewißheit not involved.","- \"Nummer\" = \"number\" (Zahl is also \"number\"); \"NB.\" printed in roman in the German, set in italics.","- \"in dem Argumente\" = \"in the argument\"; \"mathematisch genug heraus komme\" kept literal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0597":["If our author had only come upon the thought of comparing with one another the two propositions that he wants to derive from one another, then he would have had to see as clear as the sun that they not only do not flow from one another, but even directly contradict each other. Namely the first proposition is this: *Actiones, quibus corpora aequalia eosdem effectus patrant, sunt ut celeritates*. From this he wants to conclude the other proposition, which is the result of the second number in the proof, namely: *Actiones, quibus corpora inaequalia eosdem effectus patrant, sunt etiam vt ipsorum celeritates; celeritates autem eorum sunt reciproce vt massae*.",["- Sätze = \"propositions\" (Satz); wiedersprechen = \"contradict\" (Widerspruch = \"contradiction\"); herfolgern = \"conclude\" (literally \"conclude from here\"); herfließen = \"flow from\".","- \"sonnenklar\" = \"clear as the sun\"; Nummer = \"number\".","- The sentence is cut off after the Latin quotation as in the passage; Latin carried over unchanged."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0598":["If we now, following the guidance of the first proposition, take two equal bodies A and B, such that B has twice more velocity than A: then according to this rule the *Action* with which B does just the same *Effect* as A is twice greater than the *Action* of the body A; because that one, namely, on account of its greater velocity, accomplishes this *Effect* in twice smaller time. But according to the second rule I would be able to make B twice smaller, and the said *Action* would nevertheless be just as great as before, even if the velocity remained as before. Now it is however evident: that if B becomes twice smaller than it was before, and its velocity remains the same, it is impossible for it to do the given *Effect* in just the time in which its mass was twice greater, but it will need more time for it; hence, since the *Action* becomes the smaller the greater the time that has been applied to just the same *Effect*, the *Action* will then necessarily have to be smaller than when the mass of B at just the same velocity is twice greater, which thus contradicts the result of the second number.",["- Action, Effect: Latin/Fremdwörter in the German, kept as printed; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Masse = mass.","- \"twice more / twice greater / twice smaller\" render the German \"zweymal mehr / größer / kleiner\" literally.","- \"the second number\" = \"der zweyten Nummer\" (numbered item); \"Regel\" = rule; \"Satz\" = proposition.","- \"Widerspruch\" is rendered in the following passage as \"contradiction\"; here the verb is \"widerspricht\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0599":["All these contradictions, however, are to be met with in the Wolffian proof at hand, even if one grants it the proposition that it lays at the ground: namely, that the *Actiones* can be unequal whose *Effectus* are nevertheless equal. This proposition, which no mortal has ever let it occur to him to assert, is a contradiction in the best form, as exact as one can only ever devise it. For the word \"action\" is a relative word, which indicates the effect or *Effect* in a thing, insofar as another thing contains the ground of it in itself. Thus the *Effect* and the *Action* are just the same, and the meaning differs only in that I now refer it to that thing which is the ground of it, now consider it outside the same. Thus just as much would be said as: an *Action* could be unequal to itself. Besides, it has the name of *Action* only because an *Effect* depends on it, and if there could be a part in this *Action* on which an *Effect* equal to it did not depend, then the same part could not have the name of *Action* either. Even if the times in which just the same *Effectus* have been produced are unequal, the *Actiones* applied to it nonetheless remain equal, and there follows from it only: that given equal times, the *Effecte*, and also the *Actiones* corresponding to them, will be unequal.",["- Wirkung = effect and Effect (Latin-derived) = *Effect*; both rendered \"effect\", the latter marked by italics and capitalization as printed.","- Grund = ground; Bedeutung = meaning; Widerspruch = contradiction; Theil = part; betrachten = consider.","- \"the *Actiones* applied to it\" = \"die daran gewandte Actiones\"; \"it\" (daran) left unresolved. Likewise \"refer it\" (es referire) and \"has the name\" (es ... hat).","- \"Nahmen/Namen\" both \"name\"; \"Sterblicher\" = mortal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0600":["To speak of this briefly: It is at once evident that quite special causes must have been there which occasioned such outstanding errors in this treatise, which do not agree at all with the well-known and highly praised acuteness of the author, which shines forth from all that is his property. It is not hard to gauge: that the laudable desire to rescue the honor of Herr von Leibnitz, which was then held to be the honor of all Germany, produced this effort, and presented the proofs in a much more advantageous shape than they would have appeared to their author apart from this light. The matter itself was of such desperate kind that it could not be defended without errors; but its undertaking was nonetheless so enticing that it left no place for the coolness of investigation. Just this I want to have said of the transgressions of the highly famous men, Herr Herrmann, Bernoulli etc., which I have either already shown or will yet show, and the like of which one hardly encounters at all in them apart from this reproach. The honor of the man of whom we speak thus remains secured. I have the freedom to deal with his defense as with a matter that is not his property. He can meanwhile call out to me what an older philosopher, though on an occasion that concerned him somewhat more closely, exclaimed: You strike only the house of Anaxarchus.",["- Beweis = proof; Untersuchung = investigation (not in glossary); Eigenthum = property (Eigentum), rendered \"property\" in both occurrences, not Beschaffenheit.","- Schutzschrift = \"defense\" (lit. protective writing); Gehäuse = \"house\" (lit. casing, shell).","- Unterfangen/Unterfangen is rendered \"undertaking\"; \"ihr Unterfangen\" refers ambiguously to \"die Sache\" or its defense, kept ambiguous.","- \"Leibnitz\" spelling kept as printed; \"Herrmanns\" a genitive form of Herrmann, rendered \"Herrmann\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0601":["Third Chapter,\nWhich sets forth a new estimation of the living forces, as the true measure of forces of nature.",["- Hauptstück = chapter; Schätzung = estimation; Kräftenmaaß = \"measure of forces\"; Kräfte = forces (Kraft = force).","- \"der Natur\" attaches to \"Kräftenmaaß\" in the German (\"measure of forces of nature\"); the English keeps it loose.","- lebendige = living."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0602":["§ 114.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0603":["Whence that law which has been found false in mathematics could take place in nature.",["- \"Gesetze\" (singular, as printed) rendered \"law\"; the article \"dasjenige\" is neuter, and the form \"Gesetze\" is kept as a singular."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0604":["We have accordingly shown at length that the estimation of forces according to the square is found false in mathematics, and that this allows no other measure of forces than only the old, or Cartesian one. Meanwhile, however, in various places of the previous chapter we have given the reader hope of nonetheless introducing the estimation by the square into nature, notwithstanding this, and now it is time to fulfill our promise. This undertaking will make most of my readers balk; for it seems as if there followed from it that mathematics is not infallible, and that it is permissible to appeal still from its pronouncement. But the matter really does not stand so. If mathematics pronounced its law over all bodies in general: then the natural ones too would be comprehended under it, and it would be in vain to hope for an exception. But it fixes the concept of its body itself, by means of the *Axiomata*, of which it demands that one must presuppose them for its body, which however are so constituted that they do not allow and exclude in it certain properties which are nevertheless necessarily to be encountered in the body of nature: Consequently the body of mathematics is a thing which is quite different from the body of nature, and something can therefore be true of the former which is nevertheless not to be drawn to the latter.",["- Begriff = concept; begriffen = comprehended; Ding = thing; Eigenschaften = properties (Eigenschaft not in glossary; Beschaffenheit = constitution, hence \"so constituted\" for \"so beschaffen\").","- \"ihr Gesetze\" (as printed) rendered \"its law\"; \"Ausspruch\" = pronouncement; \"nothwendig\" = necessarily.","- \"an demselben\" and \"an dem Körper der Natur\": \"in it\" / \"in the body of nature\"; \"demselben\" refers to \"ihrem Körper\" (or ambiguously), kept literal.","- \"Kräften-Maaß\" / \"Kräftenmaaß\" both \"measure of forces\".","- \"jenem / diesen\" = \"the former / the latter\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0605":["§ 115.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0606":["Difference between the mathematical and natural body, and the laws concerning each of the two.",["- \"derer, beyderseits betreffenden Gesetze\" is syntactically awkward in the German (derer for der); kept as \"the laws concerning each of the two.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0607":["We want now to see what this property is, then, which is to be encountered in the body of nature, and which mathematics does not allow in its own, and which afterwards causes the former to be a thing of quite another genus than the latter. Mathematics does not allow its body to have a force that has not been wholly produced by that which is the external cause of its motion. Thus it admits no other force in the body than insofar as it has been caused in it from outside, and one will therefore always find it again in the causes of its motion exactly and in just the same measure. This is a basic law of mechanics, the presupposition of which, however, also lets no other estimation than the Cartesian take place. With the body of nature, however, as we shall soon prove, the constitution is quite different. The same has a faculty in itself to increase of itself the force which has been awakened in it from outside by the cause of its motion, so that in it there can be degrees of force which have not sprung from the external cause of the motion, and are also greater than the same, which consequently cannot be measured with the same measure with which the Cartesian force is measured, and also have another estimation. We want to treat of this property of the natural body with all the exactness and thoroughness that so important a matter requires.",["- Geschlecht = genus (Gattung is the glossary's \"genus\"; Geschlecht here also \"genus\"/kind, a case of one English word for two German words).","- Vermögen = faculty; Kraft = force; Grade = degrees; Grundgesetze = basic law; Beschaffenheit = constitution.","- \"Dieses ... dessen Voraussetzung\" — \"presupposition\" for Voraussetzung; \"voraussetzen\" earlier \"presuppose\".","- \"und auch größer seyn wie dieselbe\": \"the same\" (dieselbe) refers ambiguously to the cause or the motion; kept as \"the same.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0608":["§ 116.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0609":["Velocity is no concept of a force.",["- Begriff = concept; Kraft = force; \"kein Begriff von einer Kraft\" kept literally, \"of\" ambiguous between \"of a force\" as object or as genitive-like relation."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0610":["Velocity, as we have seen in § 3, includes in and of itself no concept of a force. For it is a determination of motion, that is, of that state of the body in which it does not apply the force that it has, but is inactive with it. But it is properly the number of that force which the body has when it rests, i.e. which it has with infinitely small velocity; that is, it is the number in which that force which inheres in the body at infinitely small velocity is the unity. This becomes clearest from the way of analysis, following the instruction of the excellent Jurinian case, § 110; namely if, in the similar way in which he considers velocity as consisting of two equal parts, we weigh it in its infinitely small parts.",["- Kraft = force; Bestimmung = determination; Zustand = state; Zahl = number; Begriff = concept; Einheit = \"unity\" (as in glossary).","- \"Zergliederung\" (analysis) and \"Fall\" (case) are not in the glossary; plain renderings chosen. \"Jurinischen\" kept as \"Jurinian.\"","- \"wir ... erwegen\" rendered \"weigh\"; \"unthätig\" = \"inactive.\" The final clause is a dependent clause with no main clause, as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0611":["§ 117.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0612":["There would be no force if there were no striving to maintain the state in itself.",["- Bestrebung = \"striving\"; Zustand = \"state.\" \"in sich\" could attach to \"erhalten\" (maintain in itself) only; ambiguity is minimal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0613":["In order to know precisely what actually determines the concept of force, we must proceed in the following way. Force is rightly estimated by the obstacle which it breaks and cancels in the body. From this it is clear: that a body would have no force at all if there were not in it a striving to maintain in itself the state that the obstacle is to cancel; for if this were not so, then that which had to break the obstacle would be like 0.",["- schätzen = \"estimate\"; Hinderniß = \"obstacle\"; bestimmen = \"determine\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; wissen = \"know\" (the glossary equivalent of Wissen is \"knowledge\").","- \"aufheben\" rendered \"cancel\" in both occurrences."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0614":["What the intension is.",["- *Intension* is a German-printed Latinate loanword (Intension); kept as \"intension.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0615":["Motion is the external phenomenon of force, but the striving to maintain this motion is the basis of activity, and velocity indicates how many times one must take the same in order to have the whole force. The former we shall henceforth call the intension; thus force is equal to the product of velocity into the intension.",["- Phänomenon = \"phenomenon\"; Basis = \"basis\" (cf. Grund = \"ground\"). \"dieselbe\" refers grammatically to the feminine \"Bestrebung\" or \"Bewegung\"; left as \"the same.\"","- \"Jene\" (\"The former\") points to \"Bestrebung\"; \"die Intension\" as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0616":["Elucidation of this concept.",["- Erläuterung = \"elucidation\" (glossary: clarification; elucidation)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0617":["So that one may have an example by which one can note these concepts the more distinctly, let one take the fourfold spring a, b, c, d, *. If we now posit that the velocity with which each of them alone begins to extend is as 1: then the initial velocity with which the whole spring a d, which is composed of 4 such, would be as 4 if it extended freely, and it seems as if it followed from this that the initial velocity which the fourfold spring impresses on a body will be four times greater than that which the simple one effects. But the intension in the fourfold spring is 4 times smaller than in the simple one; for the very same force which would press one of these four connected springs shut to a certain degree against an immovable abutment presses the fourfold one four times more shut, because the abutment of the single spring, when it has been connected in this way with 3 others, is a movable abutment, and consequently the stiffness, or what here is the same, the intension, of the fourfold spring lacks that which its velocity exceeds. Hence it happens: that the initial velocity which the fourfold spring imparts to a body is not greater than that which it can have from a simple one, although the initial velocity of the former, when it extends freely, exceeds that of the latter four times. And this can serve to make the concept of intension understandable, and to show whence it must necessarily come into account in the estimation of force.",["- deutlich = \"distinct\"; verständlich = \"understandable\"; setzen = \"posit\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity\"; Eindruck/eindrücken = \"impress\" (verb); Schätzung = \"estimation.\"","- \"Maaße\" in \"in gewisser Maaße\" rendered \"to a certain degree\" (not Grad); \"zudrücken\" = \"press shut.\"","- \"Wiederhalt\" = \"abutment\"; \"Steifigkeit\" = \"stiffness.\" \"dasjenige, was ihre Geschwindigkeit überträgt\" is obscure; rendered literally (\"exceeds\"; überträgt could also be \"transfers\").","- \"ausrecken\" and \"ausdehnen/ausstrecken\" rendered \"extend\" (Ausdehnung = extension); \"ausstrecken\" is \"extended\" too, one English word for two German verbs.","- \"jener ... diese\" = \"the former ... the latter,\" referring to fourfold and simple springs."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0618":["* Fig. XXIII.",["- Footnote to the asterisk in the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0619":["§ 118.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0620":["If the intension is like a point, then the force is like a line, namely like the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0621":["If the force of a body is of such a kind that it strives to maintain the state of motion only for an instant, let the velocity be as it will: then this striving, or intension, is equal at all velocities; consequently the whole force of such a body is only in proportion to its velocity; for the one of the factors is always equal, consequently the product, which indicates the quantity of the force, is related as the second factor.",["- Art = \"kind\"; Augenblick = \"instant\"; Quantität = \"quantity\" (cf. Größe = magnitude); Proportion = \"proportion.\"","- \"verhält sich ... wie der zweyte Factor\" kept literally (\"is related as\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0622":["§ 119.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0623":["If the intension is finite, i.e. is like a line, then the force is like the square.",["- endlich = \"finite.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0624":["In such a motion an incessant replacement from outside of the force vanishing in the body at every instant would be necessary, and the force would continually be only an effect of a constant external impulse, if the body were to perform in this way a perpetual motion. But from this it is also clearly evident: that if, on the contrary, the force of the body were of such a kind that it contained in itself a sufficient striving to maintain the motion with the given velocity uniformly and incessantly by itself without an external aid of power, this force would have to be of a quite different kind, and also infinitely much more perfect.",["- Wirkung = \"effect\"; zureichend/hinlänglich: hinlänglich rendered \"sufficient\" (glossary equates zureichend with \"sufficient\"); vollkommener = \"more perfect\" (Vollkommenheit = perfection).","- \"Machthülfe\" = \"aid of power\" (literal compound).","- \"in sich enthielte\" could attach to \"Bestrebung\" or to \"Kraft\"; English keeps \"contained in itself.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0625":["For since the intension of the former is equal at all velocities, namely infinitely small, and is only multiplied by the multiplicity of degrees of velocity: so in the latter it must on the contrary always be in proportion to the velocity, and also be multiplied with it, of which the result is the true measure of force. For the finite velocity whose intension is infinitely small affords a force of which that which this very intension constitutes at infinitely small velocity is the unity. If therefore a body is to ground this velocity and force sufficiently in itself, so that it may have the complete striving to maintain it perpetually in itself; then its intension will have to be proportioned to this force or velocity. And from this there then arises a quite new power, which is the product of the force proportioned to the velocity, into the intension, which now is also like the velocity; which product is therefore equal to the square of the velocity. For it is easy to comprehend: that, since the force which the body had with infinitely small intension and at finite velocity was like a line which represents this velocity, and the intension like a point, but now the intension is likewise like a line, the force arising from this is like a surface, which has been generated from the flow of the former line, and indeed like the square, because the named lines are proportional to one another.",["- Menge = \"multiplicity\"; Grade = \"degrees\"; Maaß = \"measure\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; gründen = \"ground\"; begreifen = \"comprehend\"; vorstellen = \"represent.\"","- Gewalt = \"power\" (Kraft = \"force\"; here a different word, same English as Macht, \"power,\" in 0624).","- \"jener ... dieser\" = \"the former ... the latter.\" \"Fläche\" = \"surface\"; \"Fluß\" = \"flow.\"","- \"vervielfältiget\" = \"multiplied\" and \"multipliciret\" = \"multiplied\": one English word for two German verbs."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0626":["Note that I abstract here throughout from the difference of the masses, or think them equal. Second, that I regard space in the motions of which I speak as empty.",["- Massen = \"masses\"; Raum = \"space\"; abstrahire = \"abstract.\" \"Zweytens\" begins a sentence without a verb of its own, continuing \"Man merke.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0627":["§ 120.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0628":["The body that has in itself the internal striving to maintain its motion freely and perpetually has a force which is like the square of the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0629":["Accordingly that body which sufficiently grounds its motion in itself, so that from its internal striving it can sufficiently be understood that it will itself maintain in itself freely, perpetually and undiminished to infinity the motion that it has, has a force that has the square of its velocity as its measure, or, as we shall henceforth call it, a living force. On the contrary, if its force does not have the ground in itself of maintaining itself, but rests only on the presence of the external cause, then it is like the mere velocity, that is, it is a dead force.",["- verstehen = \"understand\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Ursache = \"cause\"; lebendige Kraft = \"living force\"; todte Kraft = \"dead force.\"","- \"hinlänglich\" rendered \"sufficiently\" (cf. zureichend)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0630":["§ 121.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0631":["The body raises, from its internal impulse, the impression from outside infinitely higher and into a quite different genus.",["- Eindruck = \"impression\" (glossary); Geschlecht = \"genus\" (glossary Gattung = genus; a different German word, same English). Antrieb = \"impulse.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0632":["Now, however, we want to consider the force of a body, how it is constituted when it first arises in it through the effect of an external cause. It is then unfailingly grounded on the presence of this external cause, and in the same instant would not be present in the body if that cause did not awaken the impulse. Thus in the same instant, in which it rests on the presence of the external cause, it is of the kind that it would have to vanish instantly if that cause were not present; for, whether the body could afterwards ground in itself this force awakened in it after this instant, and what would then flow from this, of that we do not speak now. In the same instant the intension of the force is therefore infinitely small, and consequently the force itself, which is grounded only on the external impulse, is like the mere velocity, i.e. dead. If afterwards, however, the very same body grounds this velocity imparted to it in its inner force in such a way that from its striving an ever-enduring free preservation of the motion follows: then it is no longer a dead force, but a living one, which has the square as its measure, and is to be reckoned in relation to the former as a surface to a line. From this it is clear: that a body in this way, when it freely continues of itself its velocity impressed upon it, enlarges of itself infinitely in itself that force which it received from the external mechanical cause, and raises it to a quite different genus, so that consequently the remark that we gave in § 115 is proved here, and that the living forces are entirely excluded from the jurisdiction of mathematics.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0633":["The body cannot acquire any living force from outside.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0634":["Furthermore one sees from this that the living force cannot be brought forth in a body by an external cause, be it ever so great; for, insofar as a force depends on a cause from outside, it is always only like the mere velocity, as we have proved: rather it must receive the determinations belonging to the square measure from the inner source of the natural force of the body.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0635":["§ 122.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0636":["There are infinitely many intermediate degrees between dead and living force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0637":["We have proved: that if a body has the cause of its motion sufficiently and completely grounded in itself, so that it can be understood from the constitution of its force that it will preserve itself in it unchanged and freely forever, it has a living force, but if it does not ground its force in itself at all, but depends on it from outside, it has only a dead force, which is infinitely smaller than that. This immediately yields the consequence: that if the very same body has indeed grounded its force somewhat, but not yet completely, in itself, its force comes somewhat closer to the living one, and differs somewhat from the dead one, and that necessarily between these two outermost boundaries, the entirely dead and the entirely living force, there are still infinitely many intermediate degrees, which lead over from that to this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0638":["The living force arises only in a finite time after the beginning of the motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0639":["Furthermore it flows from this by virtue of the law of continuity, that the very same body, which in the initial instant has a dead force, and afterwards receives a living one, which is to the former as a surface to the generating line, attains this force only in a finite time. For, if one wanted to posit that it receives this latter force not in a finite time from the initial instant, but immediately in the infinitely small particle of time after it: this would say as much as that it already has this living force in the initial instant itself. For the law of continuity, and mathematics itself, proves that it is one and the same whether I say the body finds itself in the initial instant of its motion, or in the infinitely small particle of time after it. Now, however, the force in the initial point of the motion itself is dead: thus one cannot say, without committing a contradiction, that it is afterwards living, unless one at the same time stipulates that this living force is encountered in it only after a finite time, after the effect of the external cause.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0640":["Clarification of the same.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0641":["The natural force of the body namely continues in itself the impression received from outside, and in that it, through a continued striving, heaps up in itself the intension, which before was like a point, until it becomes like a line, which is proportional to the force aroused in it from outside, which behaved like the velocity, it thereby heaps up the force itself attained from outside, which before was also only like a line, so that it is now like a surface, in which the one side represents the externally imparted velocity and force, the other, however, prefigures the intension grown of itself from the interior of the body, which is proportional to that.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0642":["§ 123.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0643":["What the vivification is.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0644":["That state in which the force of the body is indeed not yet living, but nevertheless progresses toward it, I call the becoming-living, or *Vivification*, of the same.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0645":["How the intension is constituted during the becoming-living of the force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0646":["In the intermediate time, then, in which the force raises itself to the living one, which is comprehended between the two points, the initial point and that at which the force is already fully living, the body has not yet sufficiently grounded its force and velocity in itself. Here it will perhaps occur to my reader to ask how then the body is in this intermediate time in a position to preserve and continue freely and uniformly the velocity imparted to it, since it has then not yet sufficiently grounded its force and motion in itself, and consequently cannot preserve it itself either. To this I answer: the force in this intermediate time is indeed certainly not so constituted that an ever-enduring free and undiminished motion could be understood from it, if it were not raised still further by the inner striving. But whether the striving of the force to preserve itself is in this way incomplete, of that there is no talk here. The question is only: whether the intension of the force, which has not yet grown so far that it could preserve the motion undiminished and unceasingly, could nevertheless at least preserve it through that time which is necessary until the completed vivification. That this, however, is not only possible, but also in fact so behaves, is evident from this, because in this whole intermediate time, in every instant a new element of the intension arises in the body, which preserves the given velocity for an infinitely small particle of time, consequently all the elements of this intension, which arise in the body throughout the whole intermediate time, in all instants of it, that is in the whole time, preserve the same velocity, as this clearly shines forth from the holding together with the 18th §.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0647":["If the vivification should cease before it has become complete, what would then happen with the motion?",["- Körper = body; Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Bewegung = motion; Bestrebung = striving; Intension (German and Latin-derived, kept as \"intension\").","- gründen / gegründet = ground / grounded; beruhen = rest; abhängen = depend.","- Geschlecht (0632) = genus, where the glossary's Gattung is not used; Art = kind (0632), way (0646: \"in dieser Art\" rendered \"in this way\").","- Weise (0632) and Art (0646) both rendered \"way.\"","- Gränzen (0637) = boundaries; Quadratmaaße = square measure; Fläche = surface.","- Gerichtsbarkeit = jurisdiction; Erläuterung = clarification (0640); Zusammenhaltung = holding together.","- Augenblick = instant; Zeittheilchen = particle of time; Anfangs-Punct = initial point; \"Vivification\" and \"Lebendigwerdung\" both retained as in the German.","- Ambiguity kept in 0641: \"die der von draussen ... verhielte\" and the antecedent of \"die\" in \"die sich wie die Geschwindigkeit verhielte\" is left open (the line or the force).","- 0641 \"vorbildet\" = prefigures; 0646 \"einleuchtet\" = shines forth (literal); \"18ten §\" reference retained.","- Spelling modernized silently where OCR/variant (e.g. \"wen͂\" = wenn, \"verhanden\" = vorhanden in 0632); \"Zwichenzeit\" (0646) corrected to Zwischenzeit."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0648":["But if we assume: that in the interval of the vivification, before this has yet become complete, the body all at once leaves off further heaping up the elements of the intension, and making the force entirely living, what will then happen? It is evident: that the body will then ground in itself, and in free motion continue constantly to maintain, only those degrees of velocity to which that intension which it has already gained in this time of the vivification is proportional, but that the remaining degrees of velocity, which require a greater intension than is actually present in order to arrive at the complete vivification, must suddenly vanish and cease. For the intension at hand is only in a position to ground in itself a part of this velocity, and in every moment no further new elements of the intension arise that would maintain the given velocity at every moment, therefore the remaining part must vanish of itself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0649":["And how would it then be constituted with the force?",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0650":["If therefore a body moved freely meets a resistance, on which it applies its force, before it has arrived at the complete vivification with its whole velocity: then the force that it exerts is as the square of that degree of velocity to which its acquired intension is proportional and commensurate, and which therefore could become living in the given time, or also as the square of this its acquired intension; with the remaining degrees the body is inactive, or at any rate works only according to the measure of the mere velocity, which however is to be regarded as nothing in comparison with the other force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0651":["§ 124.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0652":["New estimation of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0653":["Accordingly, a body that maintains its velocity undiminished in free motion to infinity has a living force, i.e., one that has the square of the velocity as its measure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0654":["Conditions of the same.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0655":["Yet these are also the conditions that attach to this law.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0656":["1. The body must contain in itself the ground for maintaining its motion uniformly, freely and perpetually in a non-resisting space.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0657":["2. One sees from what was proved previously: that it does not have this force from the external cause that set it in motion, but that, after the external stimulation, it arises from the inner natural force of the body itself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0658":["3. That this force be generated in it in a finite time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0659":["§ 125.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0660":["This law is the main ground of the new estimation of forces, of which I would say: that I put it in the place of the estimations of Descartes and Leibniz, and make it the foundation of the true dynamics, if the insignificance of my judgments, in comparison with such great men, with whom I have to do, allowed me to speak with such authority. Meanwhile I am not disinclined to persuade myself: that this law may perhaps determine that goal, the missing of which has aroused the dissension and disunity among the philosophers of all nations. The living forces are taken up into nature, after they have been banished from mathematics. One will not be able to declare either of the two great philosophers, neither Leibniz nor Descartes, guilty of error throughout. Even in nature Leibniz's law will not take place otherwise than after it has been moderated by Descartes's estimation. It means in a certain way to defend the honor of human reason, if one unites it with itself in the various persons of acute men, and finds out the truth, which the thoroughness of the latter never entirely misses, even then when they directly contradict each other.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0661":["§ 126.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0662":["Because there are free motions, there are also living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0663":["All that matters is that there be in the world free motions: that would maintain themselves perpetually and undiminished if there were no external resistance; then the matter is settled, and there are certainly living forces in nature. The free and perpetual motion of the planets, as also the countless other experiences, which demonstrate that freely moved bodies lose their motion only in accordance with the resistance, and without it would always maintain it, furnish this warrant, and assert the existence of living forces in nature.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0664":["Mathematics permits no free motions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0665":["Meanwhile it is also clear from this: that mathematics, judging by strictness, permits no free motion in its body. For it does not permit that which is necessary to make the motion free and perpetual, namely, that the body generate from its interior an endeavor and force in itself, which has neither arisen from the external cause, nor can come from it. For it recognizes no other force in a body than that which has been brought forth by that body which is the cause of its motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0666":["§ 127.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0667":["Easier method to use these considerations.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0668":["Although the considerations and proofs up to now are of such a kind that, as far as the nature of the matter permits, they equal mathematical concepts and their clarity: still I will, for the sake of those to whom everything is suspect that has merely the illusion of a metaphysics about it, and who absolutely demand an experience to lay at the ground of the consequences, indicate a method according to which they can use these considerations with better satisfaction. For I will, toward the end of this chapter, demonstrate from an experience in mathematical strictness: that forces that have the square of the velocity are actually to be found in nature.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0669":["Thereupon these gentlemen can convince themselves from the result of all the proofs of the second chapter: that a force of this kind cannot be an effect of the external mechanical cause, because, if one admits the force only as an effect of that cause which has brought about the motion, no other estimation could take place than that according to the mere velocity. This will afterwards guide them to the way and manner in which this force can arise from the inner natural force of the body, and lead them gradually into those considerations that I have made on the essence of living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0670":["§ 128.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0671":["Mr. Bernoulli already had these concepts.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0672":["I have said that the free duration of the force, continued from the interior of the body, is the true mark upon which one can solely and alone infer that it is living and has the square as its measure. I am uncommonly pleased to meet this thought in the most exact form in that treatise of Mr. Johann Bernoulli which we cited above. He expressed his opinion as a mere geometer, to be sure not in the proper language of metaphysics, but nevertheless perfectly distinctly: *Vis viva*, he says, *est aliquid reale & substantiale, quod per se subsistit, & quantum in se est, non dependit ab alio; Vis mortua non est aliquid absolutum, & per se durans &c. &c.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0673":["This citation redounds to the considerable advantage of my consideration. The one versed in mathematics otherwise looks upon the inferences, of which he believes that they flow from subtle metaphysical distinctions, with a certain mistrust, which obliges him to postpone his assent, and I would have to fear that he might do so also with regard to mine; but here the matter lies so in the open that it presents itself of itself to the strictest geometer in his mathematical consideration.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0674":["But he did not seek it in the solid grounds.",["- Schluß/Schlüsse and Folgerung/Folgerungen: \"inference(s)\" (0673) and \"consequences\" (0668), per glossary; Schätzung rendered \"estimation\" throughout (Kräftenschätzung \"estimation of forces\").","- Begriff = concept; Betrachtung = consideration; Beweis = proof; Grund = ground; Merkmaal = mark (nota); Wesen = essence; Weltweisen = philosophers (Weltweisheit = philosophy in glossary); Philosophen in 0660 is also \"philosophers.\"","- \"Philosophers\" thus stands for both Weltweisen and Philosophen (0660).","- Intension, Vivification, Dynamick, Elemente: kept as cognates; Gesetze (neuter, 0660) = law.","- 0657: \"Siehet man\" is left as an impersonal clause with no governing main clause, as in the German; \"daß er ... herhabe\" is subjunctive reported content.","- 0650: \"oder auch dem Quadrate\" is ambiguous in attachment (to \"wie das Quadrat\" or to \"proportional und gemäß\"); left as in German.","- 0665: \"an ihrem Körper\" kept literally (\"in its body\"); \"nach der Schärfe zu urtheilen\" = \"judging by strictness.\"","- 0668: \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Erfahrung\" = experience; in 0663 \"Erfahrungen\" = \"experiences\"; \"Daseyn\" = existence.","- 0672: Latin quotation left as printed (Bernoulli); \"quantumin se est\" corrected silently to \"quantum in se est\" (OCR).","- 0674: \"tüchtigen Gründen\" = \"solid grounds\"; the passage is a sentence-fragment heading and is rendered as it stands."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0675":["I am astonished that, since Herr Bernoulli had this illumination in the concept of living force, it was possible for him to go so far astray in the way and manner by which he wanted to prove this force. He could easily have gathered that he would not find it in those cases that are undetermined with regard to this *realis & substantialis, quod per se subsistit, & est absolutum aliquid*, or in which those determinations that are supposed to lead to it are not to be encountered; for that is indeed, as he himself saw, the generic mark of living force, and that which is undetermined with regard to this character also cannot lead to living force. Meanwhile he thought he would encounter it in the case of the spring extending itself between two unequal bodies, in which not only is nothing to be found that should lead rather to the living force noted by the above distinguishing sign than to the so-called dead force, but even all the force that occurs in the arrangement of his proof is something, *quod non est aliquid absolutum, sed dependet ab alio*.",["- Kraft = force; lebendige Kraft = \"living force\"; Begriff = concept; Beweis = proof; bestimmen/unbestimmt/Bestimmung = determine/undetermined/determination.","- Geschlechts-Merkmahl: \"generic mark\" (Geschlecht; Merkmal = mark, *nota*); Character and Unterscheidungszeichen are rendered \"character\" and \"distinguishing sign.\"","- The Latin is left as in the source; the spacing before the comma after *aliquid* in the OCR was normalized.","- Feder = \"spring\"; Fällen = \"cases\"; \"in the way and manner\" renders \"in der Art und Weise\" (Art = way, Weise = manner)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0676":["Through this we are convinced once again how dangerous it is to abandon oneself to the mere outcome of approval in a composite and apparent proof, without the guiding thread of the method that we recommended in §§ 88, 89, 90, and have used with great benefit, i.e., how indispensably necessary it is to consider in advance the concepts necessarily attached to the thing that is the subject of the proof, and afterwards to investigate whether the conditions of the proof also include within themselves the proper determinations that aim at the fixing of these concepts.",["- scheinbar = \"apparent\" (could also mean \"seeming\"; ambiguity between \"apparent\" and \"illusory\" is left as in the German).","- Beyfall = \"approval\"; Bedingungen = conditions; Bestimmungen = determinations; Begriffe = concepts.","- unumgänglich nothwendig = \"indispensably necessary\"; \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0677":["§ 129.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0678":["The living forces are of contingent nature.",["- zufällig = contingent; Kräfte = forces (plural)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0679":["The Leibnizians also recognized this.",["- erkannt = \"recognized\" (erkennen = cognize; recognize)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0680":["And yet they seek them in geometrically necessary truths.",["- None beyond the terms already noted."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0681":["We have proved that the existence of living forces in nature is grounded solely on the presupposition that there are free motions therein. Now, however, one cannot find out from the essential and geometrical properties of a body any argument that should give to cognize such a faculty as is required for the performance of a free and unchanged motion, according to what we have settled in regard to this in the preceding. Hence it follows that the living forces are not cognized as a necessary property, but are something hypothetical and contingent. Herr von Leibniz himself recognized this, as he confesses especially in the Theodicy, and Herr Daniel Bernoulli confirms it through the manner that one must, as he thinks, use to make the living forces provable; namely, that one must presuppose the fundamental equation, dv=pdt, in which dv indicates the element of the velocity, p the pressure that generates the velocity, and dt the element of the time in which the pressure has produced the infinitely small velocity. He says this is something hypothetical, which one must assume. The other champions of the living forces, who made it a scruple of conscience to judge otherwise than Herr von Leibniz, have sung from the same tune. And yet they have sought the living forces in cases that are throughout geometrically necessary, and also thought to find them therein; which is certainly extremely to be wondered at.",["- Daseyn = existence; Vermögen = faculty; erkennen = \"cognize\" in \"zu erkennen geben\" and \"erkannt werden\"; \"erkannte\" of Leibniz rendered \"recognized.\"","- \"dasselben Tone gesungen\" (text: \"aus demselben Tone\") = \"from the same tune\"; Ton could be \"tone/key.\"","- Grundäquation, Geschwindigkeit = \"fundamental equation,\" \"velocity\"; Manier = \"manner\"; erweislich = \"provable.\"","- Spelling \"Leibnitz\" modernized to \"Leibniz.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0682":["Herr Herrmann's peculiar misstep in this matter.",["- Materie = matter (here \"matter\" in the sense of subject matter); Fehltritt = \"misstep.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0683":["Herr Herrmann attempted it in the same way, without letting himself be confused by the contingency of the living forces. But the preconceived good opinion of Leibniz's thoughts, and the resolve to arrive at the end at all costs, led him into a fallacy that is certainly worthy of remark. It seems to me that hardly anyone should be found to whom it should occur to infer thus: The two magnitudes a and b are to be taken together, and considered in their combination, ergo one must multiply them together; and yet this happened quite to the letter with Herr Herrmann, who was such a great master in inferring. \"Because the body,\" he says, \"which in falling receives a new element of force, nevertheless already has a velocity, one must also take this into consideration. One will therefore put together the velocity u that it already has, its mass M, and the element of velocity, or what is the same, the product of gravity g into the time, i.e. gdt. Ergo dV, or the element of the living force, is equal to gMudt, i.e. to the product of the magnitudes designated here.\"",["- Meynung = opinion (Meinung); Zwecke = end/purpose (Zweck = end); Fehlschluß = \"fallacy\"; Schliessen = \"inferring\"; Größen = magnitudes; Verbindung = combination; Betrachtung = consideration.","- Zusammen setzen = \"put together\"; Schwere = \"gravity\"; Masse = \"mass\"; Product = \"product.\"","- The OCR \"Productaus\" was corrected to \"Product aus.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0684":["§ 130.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0685":["Experience confirms the successive becoming-living.",["- Lebendigwerdung = \"becoming-living\"; succeßive left as \"successive\"; Erfahrung = experience."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0686":["Our system of doctrine brings with it that a body moved freely and uniformly does not yet have its greatest force at the beginning of its motion, but that this is greater when it has already been moving for some time. It seems to me that certain experiences are known to everyone that confirm this. I myself have found that with perfectly equal charge of a musket, and with exact agreement of the other circumstances, its bullet penetrated much deeper into a piece of wood when I fired it off from some paces from the target than when I shot it into a piece of wood only a few inches from it. Those who have better opportunity than I to make trials can make more exact and better measured tests of this. Meanwhile, then, experience teaches that the intension of a body that moves uniformly and freely grows in it, and has its proper magnitude only after a certain time, in accordance with the propositions that we have proved concerning this.",["- Lehrgebäude = \"system of doctrine\" (Lehre = doctrine); Sätze = propositions; Intension left as \"intension\"; Versuche = \"trials\"; Proben = \"tests.\"","- Flinte = \"musket\" (also \"gun\"); Ziel = \"target\"; Zolle = \"inches.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0687":["§ 131.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0688":["Now, after we have laid the foundation of a new estimation of forces, we should endeavor to indicate those laws that are especially combined with it, and that constitute, as it were, the scaffolding for a new dynamics.",["- Kräftenschätzung = \"estimation of forces\"; Gesetze = laws; verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination); Fundament = \"foundation\"; Dynamick = \"dynamics.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0689":["I am in possession of some laws, to present, according to which the vivification or becoming-living of the force occurs, but since this treatise is endeavoring to sketch the first plan of these so new and unexpected properties of forces, I must justly fear that my readers, who are chiefly eager to be made certain of the main being, would see themselves with annoyance entangled in a deep investigation of a side matter, especially since there is time enough to enter into it when the main work has first been sufficiently secured and confirmed by experiences.",["- \"einige Gesetze darzulegen\" is rendered \"some laws, to present\" to keep the German order; Hauptwesen = \"main being\" (Wesen = being; essence); Nebensache = \"side matter.\"","- Vivification kept as in the source (Latinate), beside Lebendigwerdung = \"becoming-living.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0690":["In consequence of this, I shall endeavor to disclose, with the greatest possible distinctness, only the most general laws, and those most worthy of observation, that are connected with our estimation of forces, and without which its nature cannot well be comprehended.",["- Deutlichkeit = distinctness; verknüpfet = connected (Verknüpfung = connection); begriffen = comprehended; beobachtungswürdig = \"worthy of observation\" (Beobachtung = observation)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0691":["§ 132.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0692":["The following remark presents a wholly unknown dynamical law, and is of no common importance in the estimation of forces.",["- Erheblichkeit = \"importance\"; gemein = \"common\"; Gesetze (sic, singular) = \"law\"; Anmerkung = \"remark.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0693":["The becoming-living of the force does not hold for all velocities in general.",["- \"Kr.\" in the source is an abbreviation, probably for Kraft; rendered \"force,\" with the abbreviation resolved. The reading is not certain."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0694":["We have learned that a body that acts in a state of rest exerts only a dead pressure, which is wholly different from the genus of the living forces, and also has only the simple velocity as its measure; with which the whole following of the Cartesians, as well as Leibniz's pupils, also agree. A body, however, whose velocity is infinitely small does not properly move at all, and thus has a force consisting in a state of rest; therefore it has the measure of the velocity simply.",["- Ruhestande = \"state of rest\" (Zustand = state not in the German word, but kept for Stand); Geschlecht = \"genus\" (Gattung = genus in the glossary; here Geschlecht); schlechte = \"simple\" (could be \"mere\" or \"plain\"); Maaß = \"measure.\"","- \"also hat sie das Maaß…\": \"sie\" refers back to Kraft; preserved as \"it.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0695":["If we therefore want to determine the motions belonging to the genus of living forces: then we must not extend them over all motions, whose speed can be as great or as small as one wishes, i.e. without their speed being determined thereby. For then the same law would be true for all degrees of speed smaller to infinity, and bodies would be able to have a living force even with infinitely small speed, which was found false shortly before.",["- Geschwindigkeit = \"speed\" (throughout); Kraft = \"force\"; lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\"; Gesetze (neuter/accusative form as printed) = \"law\"; Gattung not present, Geschlecht = \"genus\" (glossary Gattung; here the German word is Geschlecht).","- \"ins unendliche kleinern Graden Geschwindigkeit\": the printed syntax is kept (\"degrees of speed smaller to infinity\"); the grammar is irregular in the original.","- bestimmen / bestimmt = determine / determined."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0696":["The speed must be determined in this.",["- \"hiebey\" = \"in this\" (literally \"hereby/thereby\"); bestimmt = \"determined\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0697":["Accordingly the law of the estimation by squares does not hold of all motions, without consideration of their speed, but this comes into account with it. Hence with some degrees of speed, the force combined with the same will not be able to become living, and there will be a certain magnitude of the speed, with which the force can for the first time attain the *Vivification*, and below which, in all smaller degrees down to the infinitely small, this does not occur.",["- Quadratschätzung = \"estimation by squares\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Grad = \"degree\".","- \"in Anschlag kommen\" = \"come into account\"; \"denenselben\" (archaic) = \"the same\".","- Vivification is printed in roman type as a Latin-derived word, so it is italicized and capitalized as in the source.","- \"dieses nicht angehet\": \"this does not occur\" (literally \"does not go on/proceed\"); the referent of \"dieses\" is left open."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0698":["Consequently a free motion is also not possible without distinction with all speeds.",["- möglich = \"possible\"; \"ohne Unterscheid\" = \"without distinction\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0699":["Because, further, the complete becoming-living of the force is the cause of the free and perpetual preservation of the motion, it follows that this too is not possible with all speeds without limitation, but that the same must likewise be determined here, i.e. the speed must have a certain determinate magnitude if the body is to accomplish with the same a perpetual, unaltered and free motion; below this determinate degree this would not be possible with all smaller degrees, until with an infinitely small degree of speed this property vanishes entirely, and the duration of the motion is only something momentary.",["- Lebendigwerdung = \"becoming-living\"; Erhaltung = \"preservation\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\" (cf. Schranke).","- \"diese ... dieselbe\": \"this\" and \"the same\" refer back, ambiguously, to the preservation of motion (or to the becoming-living); the ambiguity is kept.","- \"hie\" = \"here\"; bestimmt / bestimmte = \"determined\" / \"determinate\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0700":["Thus the rule of the free and undiminished continuation of the motion will hold not in general, but only from a certain degree of speed on; below the same all smaller degrees of the motions will consume themselves of their own accord and vanish, until with an infinitely small degree the motion lasts only a moment, and requires a perpetual replacement from outside. Hence Newton's rule in its undetermined meaning does not hold of the bodies of nature: *Corpus quodvis pergit in statu suo, vel quiescendi, vel movendi, vniformiter, in directum, nisi a causa externastatum mutare cogatur*.",["- Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; unbestimmt = \"undetermined\".","- \"externastatum\" is the printed (OCR or print) run-together reading of *externa statum*; left as printed, doubtful.","- \"bey unendlich kleinen Grade\" printed with irregular case; rendered \"with an infinitely small degree\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0701":["§ 133.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0702":["Experience confirms this.",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0703":["Experience confirms this remark, for if the infinitely small speed could become living, it would have to become living, because of the proportion to the becoming-living of the finite forces, in an infinitely small time, § 122. thus two bodies, if they merely exerted the pressure of gravity, would indeed have only forces proportional to their speeds, but, as soon as they were let down from entirely imperceptibly small heights, their force would have to be immediately like the square of the same. Which is contrary to the law of continuity and to experience; for, as we have already mentioned, a body that does not break a glass by its weight also does not have the force to break it if one lets it fall onto the same from an uncommonly small distance, and 2 bodies that weigh the same as one another will also hold each other in equilibrium if one lets them both fall a little onto the scales, whereas, if that were to take place, an uncommon deflection would then have to ensue here.",["- Anmerkung = \"remark\"; Gesetz der Continuität = \"law of continuity\" (Continuität kept as cognate); Proportion = \"proportion\".","- \"derselben\" in \"das Quadrat derselben\" refers ambiguously to the speeds (or the heights); kept as \"the same\".","- \"zweene\" = \"two\" (archaic); \"gleich\" in \"so gleich\" = \"immediately\"; \"gleich beyde\" = \"both\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0704":["Application to the motion *in medio resistente*.",["- Latin phrase kept in italics."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0705":["This rule must therefore henceforth come into account in the determination of the rules of the resistance of the medium-space, in which bodies move freely. For, if the speed already begins to become very small, the medium-space no longer does so much for the diminution of the motion as before, but the same is in part lost of its own accord.",["- Mittelraum = \"medium-space\"; Wiederstand = \"resistance\"; Verringerung = \"diminution\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\".","- \"dieselbe\" (the motion) is left as \"the same\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0706":["§ 134.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0707":["Whether the becoming-living and free motion is possible in all greater degrees of speed to infinity.",["- ins unendliche = \"to infinity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0708":["We are in the midpoint of the most elegant problems, which abstract mechanics has never before been able to afford.",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; artig = \"elegant\" (doubtful nuance: also \"nice, pretty\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0709":["We have raised the question whether bodies also with all speeds, they may be as small as they like, attain to the complete becoming-living of the force, and can continue their motions freely unaltered. Now we want to investigate whether they are also capable of accomplishing the same in all higher degrees of speeds to infinity, that is, whether the bodies, which continue freely the motion imparted to them and preserve it undiminished, and consequently can attain to the complete becoming-living of the force, [whether they can do so] however great the speed that has been imparted to them may be.",["- The bracketed words are not in the German; they mark the resumption of \"ob\" after the long relative clause, and the German's grammar is kept otherwise as it stands (the original leaves the sentence somewhat loose). If a bracket is unwanted, the clause stands as a hanging construction.","- vermögend = \"capable\"; Vermögen elsewhere = \"faculty\"/\"capacity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0710":["Because the becoming-living, and the undiminished free continuation of the motion grounded upon it, is a result of the inner force of nature of the body, and consequently always presupposes that this is capable of producing the former in itself, and of attaining of its own accord to the required degree of intension: so, in the accomplishment of all infinitely higher degrees of the living force, it depends solely and alone on the magnitude and the capacity of this force of nature. Now, however, no magnitude of nature is actually infinite, as metaphysics shows in an unfailing way: therefore the said force of nature of every body must have a determinate finite quantity. Hence its capacity to act is also limited to a finite measure, and it follows: that it will extend its capacity (*Fähigkeit*) to produce living forces out of itself with ever greater degrees of speed only up to a certain finite goal, that is, that the body cannot, to infinity, with all degrees of speed, make the force with the same living in itself, and consequently accomplish the infinite and undiminished continued duration of the same in free motion, but that this capacity of the body always holds only up to a certain magnitude of speed, so that in all higher degrees above the same the capacity of the body no longer suffices to carry out the vivification appropriate to the same, and to produce out of itself so great a force.",["- Vermögen = \"capacity\" (in \"das Vermögen dieser Naturkraft\", \"Vermögen des Körpers\") and Fähigkeit = \"capacity\" (marked *Fähigkeit*): one English word for two German words.","- Naturkraft = \"force of nature\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Intension kept as cognate \"intension\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; wirklich = \"actually\"; zureichen = \"suffice\" (cf. zureichend = sufficient).","- Erfolg = \"result\"; Quantität = \"quantity\"; Maaß = \"measure\"; Ziel = \"goal\".","- \"die Kraft mit derselben in sich lebendig machen\": \"the force with the same\" is kept as printed; the referent of \"derselben\" (speed) is left to the reader.","- \"darthut\" = \"shows\" (literally \"demonstrates\"; cf. Beweis = proof, not used here)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0711":["§ 135.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0712":["What follows from this with regard to free motion.",["- \"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0713":["From this it flows: that, if this degree is determined, the body, if an external cause drives it with greater speed, will indeed yield to the same, and as long as the impulse from outside lasts, will assume this speed of motion, but, as soon as that lets off, must also forthwith lose of its own accord that degree which is above the determinate measure, and will retain only that, and continue it freely and undiminished, which the body according to the measure of its force of nature is capable of making living in itself.",["- \"welche\" (feminine/plural) after \"denjenigen Grad\" is irregular in the German; kept as it stands and rendered \"which.\"","- \"jene\" = \"that\" (the external cause or impulse); left unresolved.","- Maaße = \"measure\"; Antrieb = \"impulse\"; äusserlich = \"external\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0714":["The capacity of bodies with regard to this is different.",["- Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"; \"Der Körper\" is genitive plural (\"of bodies\"); it could also be read as singular (\"of the body\"), and the German is ambiguous."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0715":["Further there results from this: that it is possible, and also probable, that among the great manifold of the bodies of nature the force of nature of these will be of different magnitude in different bodies, consequently that one of them is capable of continuing freely a certain speed, to which the force of nature of the other does not extend.",["- Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\" (the glossary's Mannigfaltige, here the abstract noun; \"great manifold\" retains the noun-form); wahrscheinlich = \"probable\".","- \"dieser ihre Naturkraft\" (colloquial genitive construction) = \"the force of nature of these\"; \"zulanget\" = \"extend\" (literally \"suffices\", cf. zureichen)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0716":["Sum.",["- Summa (Latin) is rendered \"Sum\" here; it could be kept as *Summa*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0717":["There are thus two bounds, within which the magnitude of that speed is enclosed at which the becoming-living of the force of a certain body can subsist, the one below which, the other above which, the becoming-living and free motion can no longer be preserved.",["- Grenze = \"bound\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; \"bestehen\" = \"subsist\"; \"erhalten\" = \"preserved\".","- \"die eine unter welcher, die andere über welcher\": the relative construction is kept as in German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0718":["§ 136.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0719":["The living force can in part vanish without effect.",["- Wirkung = \"effect\"; \"zum Theil\" = \"in part\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0720":["We have learned in § 121: that the force of a body, when it has become living, is much greater than was that mechanical cause which had given it the whole motion; and that therefore a body with 2 degrees of velocity has 4 degrees of force, although the external cause of its motion, according to the instruction of the Jurinian method, § 110, has worked in it with only 2 degrees of force. Now we want to explain: how an obstacle, whose power is much smaller than the force that the body has, can nevertheless take from it its whole motion, and that consequently, just as the living force in the former case in part arises of itself, so also in the second it can consume itself of itself in the overpowering of an obstacle that is much lesser than it.",["- Kraft = force; Gewalt = \"power\" (glossary gives Kraft as \"force; power\", so \"power\" here marks Gewalt, distinct from Kraft).","- Ursache = cause; Hinderniß = obstacle; Geschwindigkeit = velocity; Grad = degree; Bewegung = motion; lebendige Kraft = living force.","- \"von selber\" rendered \"of itself\", kept literal; \"Anweisung\" = \"instruction\".","- \"in ihn\" (into/in him, the body) kept as \"in it\"; \"im ersteren Falle\" / \"im zweyten\" kept as \"the former case\" / \"the second\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0721":["Proof.",["- Beweis = proof."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0722":["To prove this we need only invert the Jurinian case § 110. Let the boat AB move, namely, from C toward B with the velocity as 1. Further we want to posit: the ball E moves in the same direction, namely CB, but in free motion and with living force, with a velocity as 2, consequently this ball will meet the obstacle R, which is here represented by a spring, and whose force is as 1, with only a single degree of velocity; for as regards the other degree, it does not move with it in respect of this obstacle, because the latter likewise has precisely the same motion in one and the same direction, consequently there remains to the body only one degree of motion in relation to it. With a single degree of velocity, however, the force is also only as 1, consequently the ball strikes the obstacle, which likewise has a single force, with a force as 1, and will thus lose through it this its degree of velocity and force. There then remains to it, however, only one degree of absolute motion, and consequently also only one degree of force, which accordingly may again be annihilated by another obstacle, which is as 1; consequently a body in which we posit a living force, and which thus has 4 degrees of force with 2 degrees of velocity, can be brought to rest by two obstacles that each have only 1 degree of force, accordingly 2 degrees in it must in this way vanish of themselves, without being canceled and broken by external causes.",["- setzen = posit; Kahn = boat; Kugel = ball; Feder = spring; Richtung = direction.","- \"Ansehung\" = \"respect\"; \"Relation\" kept as \"relation\"; \"absolute Bewegung\" = \"absolute motion\".","- \"mag vernichtet werden\" rendered \"may ... be annihilated\"; the \"mag\" could also be read as concessive (\"let it be\").","- \"dieselbe\" in \"durch dieselbe\" refers back to the obstacle (feminine); rendered \"it\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0723":["§ 137.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0724":["The circumstances under which a body squanders a part of its living force without effect are therefore these: that two or more obstacles offer resistance to it one after another in such a way that each opposes itself not to the whole velocity of the moving body but only to a part of it, as the resolution of the previous § gives to recognize.",["- Umstände = circumstances; Wirkung = effect; Auflösung = resolution; \"zu erkennen giebt\" = \"gives to recognize\" (erkennen = cognize/recognize in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0725":["Explanation of this proposition according to our concepts of living force.",["- Erklärung = explanation; Satz = proposition; Begriff = concept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0726":["How this agrees with our concepts of living force can be comprehended without difficulty in the following way. When the velocity of a body is divided into its degrees, then the living force that is to be found at one of these degrees separated from the others, and which the body therefore also applies when it acts with this degree entirely alone without the remaining ones, is as the square of this degree; but when it acts with its whole velocity undivided and at once, then the whole total force is as the square of the same, consequently that part of the force that falls to the named degree of velocity is as the rectangle from this degree into the whole velocity, which makes up a much greater quantity than that in the previous case. For if we assume, e.g., the whole velocity as consisting of two degrees, which were imparted to the body one after the other, then the living force, when the velocity was still 1, rose only to a magnitude as 1; but after the second degree was added, there arose in it not only again a degree of force that is proportioned to this second degree of velocity alone, but the force of nature raised the intension still further in the same proportion in which the velocity grew, and brought it about that the living force with the total velocity became 4-fold, whereas the sum of the forces with all the separated degrees would have been only 2-fold, consequently that each degree, in the combined effect with the remaining ones, could exert 2 degrees of force, whereas each for itself in separated effect had only a single one. Hence if a body that has a living force, consequently 4 degrees of force with 2-fold velocity, applies its whole velocity not at once but one degree after the other: then it exerts only a twofold force, but the remaining 2, which inhered in the body with the total velocity, vanish of themselves, after the force of nature ceases to maintain them, just as they were likewise brought forth of themselves from this force of nature at their generation.",["- begreifen = comprehend; zusammenstimmen = \"agree\"; Totalkraft = \"total force\"; Naturkraft = \"force of nature\".","- Rectangulum, Quantität, Intension, Proportion kept as cognates (\"rectangle\", \"quantity\", \"intension\", \"proportion\").","- \"bey einem von diesen Graden\" rendered \"at one of these degrees\"; \"bey\" could also be \"with/in the case of.\"","- \"in demselben\" (in the second degree / in the body) is ambiguous in the German; rendered \"in it.\"","- \"vor sich\" = \"for itself\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"/\"effect\" (also \"act\" for wirken)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0727":["§ 138.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0728":["This remark rewards our effort with important consequences.",["- Anmerkung = remark; Folgerung = consequence."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0729":["Consequences.",["- Folgerungen = consequences."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0730":["1. We will encounter the complete effect of living force nowhere except where the obstacle offers resistance at once to the whole velocity of the body penetrating with living force, and endures all degrees of it together.",["- \"des ... eindringenden Körpers\" depends on \"Geschwindigkeit\"; \"derselben\" refers to the velocity.","- erdulden = endure."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0731":["2. Where on the contrary the obstacle opposes itself only to one degree of it alone, consequently endures the whole velocity only in divided degrees, little by little, there a great part of the living force is lost of itself, without its having been annihilated by the obstacle, and one would deceive oneself if one believed that the obstacle which in this way consumes the whole motion had also broken the whole force itself. This loss is always the more considerable, the smaller the degree of velocity that the obstacle endures is relative to the whole velocity of the moving body. E.g., let the velocity in which the body has its living force be divided into 3 equal degrees, to each of which alone the obstacle can oppose itself at one time, then, even if the body also has a living force with each of these degrees in particular, the force of each degree in particular is as 1, consequently the power of the obstacle, which overcomes these 3 one after another, also as 3; the whole living force of this body, however, was as the square of 3, i.e., as 9: consequently in this way 6 degrees of force, i.e., ⅔ of the whole, have been lost of themselves without external resistance. On the contrary, if we take another obstacle that endures not the third but the half of the said whole velocity at once, consequently consumes the whole motion not in 3 but in 2 separated degrees, then the loss that the living force suffers here besides that which this obstacle consumes is only as 2, i.e., ½ of the whole, consequently smaller than in the previous case. In the same way, if the degree to which the obstacle resists at once is ⅛ of the whole velocity, then the body squanders ⅞ of the whole force, the cause of which is not to be sought in the obstacle, and so on to infinity.",["- wiedersetzen / Widerstand thun / wiederstreben rendered \"oppose itself\" / \"offer resistance\" / \"resist\"; verzehren = consume; vernichten = annihilate; \"brechen\" = \"break\".","- Gewalt = \"power\" (not Kraft = force); \"verlustig gehen\" = \"be lost\"; \"verlohren gegangen\" = \"been lost.\"","- \"Drittheil\" = \"third\"; \"Helfte\" = \"half\"; \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"; \"Z. E.\" = \"E.g.\"","- \"gegen die ganze Geschwindigkeit\" rendered \"relative to\"; \"gegen\" could be \"compared with.\"","- \"diese 3 nach einander überwindet\": \"also as 3\" supplies \"is\" as in German ellipsis."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0732":["3. If the degree of velocity [Geschwindigkeit] with which the obstacle opposes itself at every moment is only infinitely small: then there is no trace at all of a living force any longer to be found in the overcome obstacles, but, because then each individual degree works only in proportion to its simply taken velocity, and the sum of all degrees is equal to the whole velocity, the whole effect of the force of the body, although it is living, is nevertheless proportioned only to the simple velocity, and the whole magnitude of the living force vanishes of itself entirely, without exerting an effect appropriate to it, namely, since it is properly like a surface that has been generated from the flow of that line which represents the velocity, all the elements of this second dimension vanish gradually of themselves, and no other trace of a force emerges in the effect than the one that is proportioned only to the generating line, i.e., to the velocity simply.",["- Grad Geschwindigkeit: the text reads \"der Grad Geschwindigkeit\" (no genitive ending); rendered literally. The bracketed German in the translation is my own gloss, not the Academy apparatus.","- lebendige Kraft = \"living force\"; Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Größe = magnitude; Hinderniß/Hindernisse = obstacle(s).","- schlechthin = \"simply\"; schlechten Geschwindigkeit = \"simple velocity\" (schlecht in its older sense, 'plain'); both are rendered by the same English word, though the German differs.","- \"Abmessung\" = \"dimension\" (could also be 'measurement'). \"Fluße\" = \"flow.\"","- \"Sum\" = Summe (the glossary's Inbegriff is not used here).","- \"ob sie gleich lebendig ist\": \"sie\" could refer to Kraft or Wirkung; grammatically Kraft is nearer, and I have left the pronoun as it stands."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0733":["4. Thus a trace of a living force is found nowhere in the effects exerted, or in the overcome obstacles, even if the body really has a living force, except only where the moment of the velocity with which the obstacle resists is of finite magnitude; but even then not without this important condition, namely, that this magnitude of velocity also may not be as small as it likes, for we know from § 132 that a certain quantity of it is required, so that the body that moves with it could have a living force, and if the moment of the resistance of the obstacle, in accordance with it, is too small, no effect of the living force could be detected in it either.",["- Moment = *Moment* (the German uses the loanword); kept as \"moment.\"","- Quantität = \"quantity\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Maaßgebung = \"accordance\" (nach Maaßgebung derselben: \"in accordance with it\").","- \"derselben\" in the last clause is ambiguous between Geschwindigkeit and Größe; \"it\" preserves this. \"in derselben\" (final) may refer to the obstacle or the velocity; kept as \"in it.\"","- wiederstrebet/Wiederstrebung = \"resists/resistance.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0734":["The extremely considerable utility of this remark we shall learn especially toward the end of this chapter, where it will serve to illuminate properly and to make confirmed the foremost experience that proves the living forces.",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\" (here used in the sense of an experiment or empirical case, but rendered with the glossary word).","- Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Anmerkung = \"remark\"; bewähren = \"confirm.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0735":["§ 139.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0736":["The phenomena of bodies that overcome gravity prove no living force, yet they do not conflict with it.",["- Phänomena = \"phenomena\" (Latinate plural, distinct from Erscheinung = \"appearance\").","- \"dawider\" (\"with it/against it\") is left as \"with it\"; its referent is not specified in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0737":["Since the moment of the pressure of gravity occurs only with infinitely small velocity: it is therefore quite distinctly evident by means of the third number of the previous § that a body that expends its motion while it overcomes the obstacles of gravity will exert against it only an effect that is simply proportioned to its velocity, although the force itself is related as the square of this velocity, entirely in accordance with what experience also gives to cognize of this, as we have seen in the previous chapter in detail, and in more than one way.",["- Schwerdrückung = \"pressure of gravity\" (compound; literally 'gravity-pressing'). Schwere = \"gravity.\"","- erkennen zu geben = \"gives to cognize\"; deutlich = \"distinctly\"; erhellet = \"is evident.\"","- Nummer = \"number\"; Bewegung = \"motion.\"","- \"gegen dieselbe\": the feminine pronoun refers to Schwere, i.e. \"against it\" (gravity)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0738":["Behold, then, here even an experience that seems to admit no other than Descartes's laws, and that also in fact properly shows no marks of any other estimation than this one, yet which on precise consideration does not conflict with the square estimation, if it is taken in its correct meaning, but nevertheless leaves room for it.",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Merkmaale = \"marks\" (*notae*); Schätzung = \"estimation\"; Quadratschätzung = \"square estimation\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Erwegung = \"consideration.\"","- \"Cartesens Gesetze\" = \"Descartes's laws\" (the German genitive form of the name is modernized).","- \"ihr\" in \"ihr Platz läßt\" refers to Quadratschätzung (\"leaves room for it\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0739":["Thus the effect that bodies rising vertically upward exert while they overcome the obstacles of gravity does indeed refute Leibniz's estimation without any contradiction, but it does not, properly speaking, prove our living forces; yet it does not also cancel them. Meanwhile, if we direct our attention only precisely to this, we shall even there still encounter some rays of our estimation. For the body would not be able to continue freely its motion inhering in it, and itself to maintain it until the external resistance gradually takes it from it, if it did not bring forth from itself that inner striving or intension which is at once the ground of the free motion, and also of the living force.",["- Wiederrede = \"contradiction\" in the sense of 'objection/gainsaying'; it is not Widerspruch, though the glossary equivalent is used.","- \"Intension\" is the author's own Latinate word (cf. Latin *intensio*) and is retained as \"intension\"; Bestrebung = \"striving\"; Wiederstrebung = \"resistance.\"","- \"jedeñoch\" = OCR/typographic form of \"jedennoch\"; rendered \"yet.\"","- \"wo er nicht ... hervorbrächte\": \"if it did not bring forth\" (literally 'where it not ... brought forth').","- Grund = \"ground.\"","- \"dieselbe\" (after \"erhalten können\") refers to Bewegung; the first \"ihm\"/\"sie\" likewise; \"it\" retained."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0740":["§ 140.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0741":["Proofs grounded on this.",["- Proben = \"proofs\" (in the sense of 'trials/tests'); Beweis (the glossary's \"proof\") is a different German word."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0742":["From what has been proven up to here, we see at the same time the cause of the well-known trick of how one can lift up almost unconquerable powers through quite slight obstacles. If namely the power that one is to break rests on a living force: then one does not set against it an obstacle that does its resistance all at once, and must suddenly be broken, for this would often have to be immeasurably great, but rather one that endures and consumes the force only in its smaller degrees of velocity gradually; for in this way one will frustrate astonishingly great powers through quite inconsiderable oppositions; just as one has, e.g., annihilated the blows of battering rams by woolsacks, which would have crushed walls if they had struck directly upon them.",["- Gewalt = \"power\" (distinct from Kraft = \"force\"); Wiederstand = \"resistance\"; Wiedersetzungen = \"oppositions.\"","- Kunststück = \"trick\"; \"aufheben\" rendered \"lift up\" (it could also mean 'cancel'); I have kept the first, but the German is ambiguous between 'lift' and 'annul.'","- Mauerbrecher = \"battering rams\" (literally 'wall-breakers'); Wollsäcke = \"woolsacks.\"","- Grad = \"degree\"; Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0743":["§ 141.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0744":["Soft bodies do not act with their whole force.",["- wirken = \"act\" (cognate Wirkung = \"effect\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0745":["It is further evident: that the bodies that are soft, and easily compress themselves in the collision, will by no means apply all their force through the impact, and that they often exert quite slight effects, which with just the same force and mass, but greater hardness, would be incomparably greater. I know well: that still other causes come in, which besides that of which we speak contribute their part to this loss, or rather bring it about that one seems to be, but our cited one is indisputably the foremost, and indeed of a true loss.",["- Anlauf = \"collision\" (literally 'running up against'); Stoß = \"impact\"; Masse = \"mass\"; Härtigkeit = \"hardness.\"","- \"daß einer zu seyn scheinet\": the German leaves the referent of \"einer\" (a loss) implicit; kept as \"one.\"","- \"und zwar eines wahrhaften Verlustes\" is left as a bare genitive, as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0746":["§ 142.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0747":["Question raised, whether the effect of bodies can be proportional to their living force without distinction of their mass.",["- Unterscheid = \"distinction\"; Frage = \"question.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0748":["We now want to investigate how the effect of a body that has a living force, whose mass however one thinks as infinitely small, will be, for this afterwards gives to cognize: whether in equal circumstances, if the forces of two bodies are both living, both can also exert the effects proportional to these living forces, if one puts them in equal circumstances, the mass of the one be as small as it likes; or whether rather the mass of every body must have a certain magnitude, so that, if one makes it smaller, the effect that it exerts cannot be proportional to its living force.",["- Umstände = \"circumstances\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; \"gedenket\" = \"thinks\" (gedenken).","- \"gleichen Umständen ... gleiche Umstände\" repeated as in the German.","- \"die Masse des einen sey auch so klein wie sie wolle\": a subjunctive clause, kept as \"the mass of the one be as small as it likes.\"","- \"kan\" is the old spelling of \"kann.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0749":["That is indeed infallible: that if a body of finite mass has a living force, each of its parts, may they be as small as they like, must also have a living force, and would also have this if it moved separated from the others; but here the question is whether such a small, or as we want to assume here, infinitely small particle, for itself alone can also exert in nature an effect proportional to its living force, if one puts it in the equal circumstances, in which a larger one would act in this proportion. We shall find that this cannot happen, and that a body that has a living force, if its mass is smaller than it must be in accordance with the rule that we want to prove, exerts in nature no such effect as is proportional to this its living force, but that it comes the less to this proportion, the smaller the mass then is, until, when the mass is infinitely small, the body with it can act only in proportion to its velocity simply, although it has a living force, and another body, with just the same velocity and living force, but suitably great mass, would in equal circumstances exert an effect that would be in accordance with the square of its velocity multiplied into the mass.",["- Theile/Theilchen = \"parts\" / \"particle\" (Teil = \"part\"; Theilchen is a diminutive).","- \"sie mögen so klein seyn, wie sie wollen\": \"may they be as small as they like\"; the plural \"sie\" refers to Theile.","- \"es\" (in \"wenn es sich gleich ... bewegte\") refers to Theil (\"each of its parts\").","- \"in die Masse multiplicirt\" = \"multiplied into the mass\" (older mathematical idiom).","- Maaßgebung = \"accordance\"; Regel = \"rule\"; Quadrat = \"square.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0750":["§ 143.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0751":["Answer.",["- Beantwortung = \"answer.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0752":["The matter depends solely and alone on this, that all obstacles in nature that are to be broken by a certain force do not oppose to it straightaway at the point of contact an immediately finite degree of resistance, but rather beforehand an infinitely small one, and so on, until, after the infinitely small little space that the moving force has broken through, the resistance that it meets with becomes finite. This I presuppose by virtue of the agreement of true doctrine of nature, without wishing to engage in adducing here the manifold grounds that confirm it. Newton's pupils therefore take occasion to say: that bodies act on others even when they do not yet touch. In consequence of this we meet with a particular difference between the effect that a little body of infinitely small mass exerts on such obstacles of nature, and that which it performs when its mass has the determinate finite magnitude, even if we pay no heed to the difference that in any case is always between the forces of two bodies whose masses are different, and that has long been known, but consider only that which flows from the concept of our living forces alone.",["- *Naturlehre* = \"doctrine of nature\" (Lehre = doctrine); *Kraft* = force; *Wirkung* = effect; *Grad* = degree; *Größe* = magnitude; *Begriff* = concept; *bestimmt* = determinate.","- \"kraft der Uebereinstimmung\": *kraft* (preposition) rendered \"by virtue of\"; \"wahren Naturlehre\" has no article in German before \"true\"; the English keeps \"of true doctrine of nature.\"","- *Körperchen* = \"little body\"; *Räumchen* = \"little space\" (diminutives kept); *Berührungspuncte* = \"point of contact.\"","- *Wiederstrebung* = \"resistance\" is not distinguished in English from *Wiederstand* (also \"resistance\"); *Hinderniß* = \"obstacle.\"","- *Unterschied/Unterscheid* both \"difference\"; \"alsofort\" = \"straightaway\"; \"gleich\" in \"gleich einen endlichen Grad\" rendered \"immediately\" (ambiguity with \"equally\" possible); \"wenn ... gleich\" = \"even if/when.\"","- \"er\" in \"die er verrichtet\" refers grammatically to *Körperchen* (neuter) but is masculine in the German; preserved as \"it.\"","- \"Diesem zu Folge\" = \"In consequence of this.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0753":["For we already know: that, if the body has a living force, but this is applied to overcome the obstacle of the heavy pressures, its effect nevertheless stands only in proportion to the velocity simply, and all intension, which is the mark of living force, vanishes without effect. Now, however, the counterpressure of gravity acts with infinitely small solicitation, into the innermost of its mass, i.e., immediately on the infinitely small parts of the moving body; therefore this its state is equal to the state of that little body that indeed runs up against every obstacle of nature with living force, but infinitely small mass, for this one endures, as we have noted, here too always a resistance that, just as with gravity, immediately resists it with infinitely small solicitation; consequently such an infinitely small mass will also in the same way consume its living force in itself, and with every obstacle of nature act only in proportion to its velocity.",["- *Merkmal* = \"mark\"; *Intension* kept as \"intension\"; *Sollicitation* = \"solicitation\"; *Geschwindigkeit* = \"velocity\"; *Zustand* = \"state.\"","- *Schwerdrückungen* = \"heavy pressures\" (literal; compound of *schwer* and *Drückung*); *Schwere* = \"gravity\"; *Gegendruck* = \"counterpressure.\"","- *Theile* = \"parts\"; *Masse* = \"mass\"; *schlechthin* = \"simply.\"","- \"seiner Masse\" and \"sein Zustand\": the antecedent of \"its\" (the body or gravity) is left as ambiguous as the German; \"der ... anläuft\" has masculine relative pronoun after *Körperchen* (neuter), rendered \"that.\"","- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0754":["But that this befalls only the infinitely small body, and that, on the contrary, one of finite and determinate mass can exert in the same obstacle an effect commensurate with its living force, is clearly evident from this, because, as we assume, the obstacle does its resistance only from outside, and does not, like gravity, act into the innermost; consequently the finite body, there where the infinitely small mass lost its whole velocity through the continued infinitely small resistance of the obstacle, loses only infinitely little, i.e., nothing, but expends its force only against the finite degrees of resistance, to which that one cannot penetrate; consequently it arrives in the circumstances in which, as we have seen in § 138, No. 4, that body must be that is to apply its living force to an effect proportional to it.",["- *Umstände* = \"circumstances\"; *gemäß* = \"commensurate with\"; *erhellet* = \"is evident\"; *Wiederstrebung* = \"resistance.\"","- \"jene\" = \"that one\" (referring to the infinitely small mass); \"ihr\" in \"ihr proportionalen\" = \"it\" (living force).","- \"einer ... ausüben könne\": \"one\" = a body (*einer*, masculine)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0755":["§ 144.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0756":["The mass with which a body can exert the effect proportioned to its living force must be determinate; below this magnitude smaller masses cannot do this.",["- \"bestim͂t\" read as *bestimmt*, rendered \"determinate\"; *proportionirte* = \"proportioned.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0757":["Since, then, the effect of the body that moves with finite force but infinitely small mass is nowhere in nature proportioned to the square of the velocity, but only to the velocity simply: it follows, by virtue of the way of inferring that must already be known to us through frequent practice, that one cannot say generally and without restriction: This body has a living force, consequently its effect under proper * circumstances will also be proportional to its living force, however small the mass may otherwise be; but rather a certain quantity of mass will be required for one to be able to say this, and below this determinate measure no effect of such a body on the obstacles of nature will be able to be proportioned to its living force, let them be whichever they like; but the effect will depart the more from the ratio of the living force, the more the quantity of mass is below this determinate measure, whereas in all higher magnitudes above it, it is understood of itself that this deviation is not met with at all.",["- *Quadrat* = \"square\"; *Art zu schliessen* = \"way of inferring\"; *Maaß* = \"measure\"; *Verhältniß* = \"ratio\"; *Quantität* = \"quantity\"; *Größen* = \"magnitudes.\"","- The asterisk refers to the footnote of the following passage (0758).","- \"sie mögen auch seyn, welche sie wollen\": \"let them be whichever they like\"; the antecedent of \"sie\" (obstacles) preserved."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0758":["* Namely in those in which another of greater mass with the same velocity applies its living force entirely.",["- *Nemlich* = \"Namely\"; \"denenjenigen\" refers back to *Umständen* (\"circumstances\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0759":["§ 145.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0760":["The following remarks follow from this:",["- *Anmerkungen* = \"remarks.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0761":["Consequences.",["- *Folgerungen* = \"Consequences\" (Folgerung = consequence)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0762":["1. That a small particle of matter, in firm union with a large mass, can with living force exert an entirely different and exceedingly greater effect than it can perform alone and separated from it.",["- *Theilchen* = \"particle\"; *Materie* = \"matter\"; *Vereinigung* = \"union\"; \"derselben\" = \"it\" (the mass), as the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0763":["2. That this difference is nevertheless not necessary, but rests on this contingent property of nature: that all its obstacles, in accordance with the rule of continuity, begin already from afar, and with infinitely small degrees, before they oppose their finite resistance to the body running up against them, but that, notwithstanding this, nature already permits no other effect.",["- *nothwendig* = \"necessary\"; *zufällig* = \"contingent\"; *Eigenschaft* = \"property\"; *Continuität* = \"continuity.\"","- \"schon\" in \"die Natur schon keine andere Wirkung verstattet\" rendered \"already\"; its force is obscure and left so."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0764":["3. That it is not true without distinction: that the effects of two bodies whose forces are living, and whose velocity is equal, stand in the same circumstances as their mass; for, if one of them is smaller than it ought to be according to the stated rule, then its effect departs, in addition, from the square measure of the velocity, and is therefore much smaller than it should have been according to the ratio of the masses alone.",["- \"sich ... wie ihre Masse verhalten\" rendered \"stand ... as their mass\" (*sich verhalten* = to stand in a ratio); \"bey gleichen Umständen\" = \"in the same circumstances.\"","- *Quadratmaaße* = \"square measure\"; *Maaßgebung* = \"stipulation,\" rendered \"according to.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0765":["4. That even the alteration of the figure of bodies, without change of their mass, can cause their effect in the circumstances touched upon to have the proportion of their velocity, although the force has the ratio of the square of it, and that therefore a body that has a living force can do a much smaller effect merely because its figure has been altered, without either its mass, or velocity, or living force, or the constitution of the obstacle having suffered the slightest change. E.g., a golden sphere with living force must do a much greater effect than if the very same golden mass with equal velocity and force ran against the same obstacle, but in such a way that it had previously been beaten into a thin and widely extended gold leaf. For although here nothing has been changed with regard to the force, the alteration of the figure nevertheless makes its smallest parts meet the obstacle here just as if, separated from one another, they had impacted on it, and consequently, according to what was proved shortly before, they act by no means with their living force, and proportional to it, but exert an effect that either comes near to the measure of the simple velocity, or coincides with it; whereas, on the contrary, if the mass runs against the obstacle in the figure of a solid sphere, it meets so small a surface of it that the infinitely small moments of the resistances that it meets with in so small a space are not in a position to consume the motion of this mass, consequently the living force remains unimpaired, in order to be applied solely and alone against the finite degrees of resistance of this obstacle; just as, on the other hand, it is clear that with its first figure it covers an exceedingly large surface of the obstacle, and consequently, with the same mass, suffers an incredibly greater resistance from the infinitely small solicitation that is to be met with at every point of the obstacle, and therefore must be able to be consumed more easily by it, with either complete or at least great loss of the living force, which does not happen in the first way.",["- *Figur* = \"figure\"; *Beschaffenheit* = \"constitution\"; *Kugel* = \"sphere\"; *Fläche* = \"surface\"; *Moment* = \"moment\"; *Bewegung* = \"motion\"; *Z. E.* = \"E.g.\"","- *Wiedersetzungen* (resistances) and *Wiederstrebung*/*Wiederstand* all \"resistance.\"","- *schlechte Geschwindigkeit* = \"simple velocity\" (*schlecht* in the older sense \"simple\"; elsewhere *schlechthin* = \"simply\").","- \"ihre Wirkung ... die Proportion ihrer Geschwindigkeit habe\": \"have the proportion of\" kept literal. \"auf die erstere Art\" = \"in the first way\"; \"mit ... mit\" structure kept.","- \"die Kraft die Verhältniß vom Quadrate derselben hat\": \"derselben\" taken as velocity.","- \"seine kleinsten Theile\": \"its\" (the body's/mass's) — pronoun gender kept as German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0766":["§ 146.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0767":["Fluids act in proportion to the square of the velocity.",["- Flüßigkeiten = \"fluids\"; Geschwindigkeit = \"velocity\" (not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0768":["But the most important consequence that I draw from the law now proven is the one that flows quite naturally from it, namely, that fluid bodies act through impact in proportion to the square of their velocity, * although, if the effect here were supposed to be proportional to their living forces, they would have to do so not according to the measure of the square but of the cube of their velocity; and how this is not contrary to our theory of living forces, although it cancels the living forces of Herr von Leibniz, as Herr Jurin has already very well remarked.",["- Folgerung = \"consequence\"; Wirkung/wirken = \"effect\"/\"act\"; Kraft = \"force\"; lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\".","- Verhältniß and Proportion both rendered \"proportion\"; Maaß = \"measure\"; Schätzung (elsewhere) = \"estimation\".","- The asterisk refers to the footnote in the next passage.","- \"wie dieses ... nicht entgegen sey\": the subordinate clause depends on an unexpressed verb (e.g. \"I show\"); kept as it stands. \"solches\" refers to the action (\"thun\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0769":["* As Herr Mariotte has shown through experiments.",["- Versuch = \"experiment\"; this is a footnote to the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0770":["How this follows from the preceding.",["- Marginal heading (Randtitel), kept as a heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0771":["For the fluids are divided into the finest parts, which can count as infinitely small, and together make up no coherent solid body, but all act one after another, each for itself and separated from the rest; consequently they endure that loss of living force which the infinitely small little bodies, as we have remarked, always suffer when they run up against an obstacle of nature, be it whichever it may, and therefore act only in proportion to their velocity, although their force is like the square of it.",["- Theile = \"parts\"; Körper = \"body\"; Hinderniß = \"obstacle\"; \"derselben\" refers to Geschwindigkeit.","- \"vor\" = \"for\" in \"vor unendlich klein gelten\" is old spelling for für (\"count as\"); \"vor sich\" = \"for itself\".","- \"ob ... gleich\" = \"although\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0772":["Herr Richter has taken much vain trouble to ward off this blow of Herr Jurin. His cause was helpless, since it was bound to the rule: that the forces stand in no other proportion than that in which their effects are.",["- Sache = \"cause\" (also \"matter, case\"); Streich = \"blow\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0773":["On the resistance of the intervening space.",["- Mittel-Raum = \"intervening space\" (Mittel here as \"medium\"; cf. Mittel = \"means\" in glossary, not used here).","- Marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0774":["Finally everyone also comprehends easily from this why bodies with free motion and living force, in a fluid intervening space, suffer resistance only in proportion to the square of their velocity, without any detriment thereby occurring to our living forces; although it contradicts the Leibnizian estimation, according to which this resistance would have to be proportioned to the cube of the velocity.",["- begreifen = \"comprehend\"; Wiederstand = \"resistance\"; Eintrag = \"detriment\"; Schätzung = \"estimation\".","- Mittelraume = \"intervening space\" (or \"medium\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0775":["§ 147.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0776":["Is confirmed by experience.",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"; marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0777":["There are countless experiences that confirm the rule of which we have spoken up to now. Although these may not be so exactly measured, they are nevertheless infallible, and have the agreement of a general approval.",["- \"dieselbe\" (sg.) and \"sie\" (pl.) as in the original; the antecedent of \"dieselbe\" is the rule, of \"sie\" the experiences. \"Uebereinstim͂ung\" read as Uebereinstimmung.","- Beyfall = \"approval\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0778":["For if we do not give room to our rule, we must posit: that a body, however small and slight it may be, would do just as great an effect in equal circumstances through impact as a large mass, if only one made their velocities inversely proportioned to the square roots of their masses, or, according to Cartesius's rule, if they related inversely as these masses themselves. But experience contradicts this. For everyone is agreed in this, that a plume-feather or a speck of sun-dust would not accomplish through a free motion the effects of a cannonball, even if one wished to concede to them as many degrees of velocity as one itself demands; and no one, I believe, will suppose that one of them could shatter the solid lumps of matter and break through walls, were they to strike upon them in free motion with ever so great a velocity. All this, to be sure, can be tested and confirmed by no properly arranged experiment, but the countless experiences that occur of this in similar cases, though not on so large a scale, bring it about that no one doubts the outcome alluded to.",["- setzen = \"posit\"; Masse = \"mass\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Materie = \"matter\".","- \"Cartesens\" = \"Cartesius's\" (Descartes).","- \"als man selber verlanget\": \"one itself\" kept literal for \"man selber\".","- Versuch = \"experiment\" and Erfahrung = \"experience\".","- \"in so großer Maaße\" = \"on so large a scale\" (literally \"in so great measure\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0779":["Now it cannot be denied, however, that the said small particles of body, under the cited arrangement of their velocity, would necessarily have to have equal force with the large bodies, whether according to Cartesius's, or Leibniz's, or our measure of forces: so no other means remains to explain this than that the small body must exert a much smaller effect than should occur according to the measure of its force, and that its living force is for the most part frustrated without effect, just as we have proved of the same in §§ 143, 144, 145.",["- Mittel = \"means\"; erklären = \"explain\"; Kräftenmaaß = \"measure of forces\"; Körpertheilchen = \"particles of body\".","- \"gerade so, wie ... von demselben bewiesen haben\": \"of the same\" refers to the small body."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0780":["§ 148.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0781":["The motions of elastic bodies cancel Leibniz's estimation but not ours.",["- aufheben = \"cancel\"; Bewegung = \"motion\"; marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0782":["Among those experiences that give no trace of any other estimation than only the Cartesian, and therefore seem to conflict with our measure of forces, there belong finally also the motions of elastic bodies through impact, of which we have treated at length in the preceding chapter, and which are all found true in wholly infallible experiments. They also in fact entirely cancel the square-estimation of Herr von Leibniz, by virtue of the presupposition that is inseparably bound up with it: namely that the effects, in the production of which the force consumes itself, are always equal to it. Ours has the well-grounded advantage of not being subject to this law, and therefore escapes this blow.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Voraussetzung = \"presupposition\"; Stoß = \"impact\"; Versuch = \"experiment\".","- \"dieser\" (dat. fem.) refers to Kraft; \"diesem Gesetze\" = \"this law\"; Streich = \"blow\".","- \"Quadrat-Schätzung\" = \"square-estimation\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0783":["We know already from the preceding: that living force is not something that can be produced in a body from outside, through an external cause, e.g. through an impact; this can already instruct us: that we shall not regard the living forces of the impacted bodies as the effects of the impacting ones, and seek to measure these by those. But the real resolution of the whole difficulty, where one supposes still to meet with one in this, consists in what follows.",["- Ursache = \"cause\"; Realauflösung = \"real resolution\" (compound kept); Schwierigkeit = \"difficulty\".","- \"wo man ja eine noch hierinn anzutreffen vermeynt\": \"one\" (eine) stands for an unexpressed Schwierigkeit; \"ja\" left untranslated.","- \"diese durch jene\": \"these by those\" (the impacting by the impacted); the pronouns follow the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0784":["§ 149.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0785":["Proof.",["- Beweiß = \"proof\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0786":["All those versed in mechanics must know: that an elastic body does not act into the other with its whole velocity at once, but through a continued accumulation of the infinitely small degrees that it brings into it one after another. I have no need to enter into the particular causes of this, it is enough for me that I have in this the unanimous approval on my side, and that everyone recognizes it: that without this presupposition no law of motion can be explained. The true cause of this is presumably this: because elasticity, according to the nature of a spring, opposes itself only to that degree of velocity which is sufficient to tense it; consequently with each infinitely small degree of the impression that it suffers it endures always only an infinitely small degree of the velocity of the impacting body, and thus at every instant is opposed not to the whole velocity but only to the infinitely small degree, and takes it up into itself, until the successive accumulation has transferred the whole velocity into the suffering body in this way.",["- Mechanikverständige = \"those versed in mechanics\"; besonder = \"particular\"; hinlänglich = \"sufficient\" (not zureichend); Eindrückung = \"impression\" (cf. Eindruck).","- erkennen = \"recognize\"; Elasticität = \"elasticity\"; leidend = \"suffering\" (passive).","- \"den andern\": \"the other\" (body); \"ihn\" refers to the degree (\"Grade\"); \"sie\" in \"sie zu spannen\" refers to Elasticität/Feder; ambiguity kept.","- \"Bewegungsgesetze\" = \"law of motion\" (sg. as in original, though the form could be plural); \"erklährt\" = \"explained\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0787":["From this it follows, according to the preceding: that, since the impacting body here acts only one after another with single infinitely small degrees of its velocity, it will also act only in mere proportion to its velocity, without detriment to its living force, which it can nevertheless have in itself.",["- schlechter Proportion = \"mere proportion\" (schlecht in the older sense \"simple, plain\").","- \"laut dem vorhergehenden\" = \"according to the preceding\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0788":["§ 150.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0789":["The beloved law of Herr von Leibniz, of the unchanged preservation of one and the same magnitude of force in the world, is still an object that seems here to require an exact examination. It is immediately evident to the eye: that if there is anything well-grounded in the considerations so far, it cannot find place in that meaning in which it has otherwise been taken up. But what our estimation would introduce in this regard, and how it can satisfy the rules of universal harmony and order, which have made the said Leibnizian law so praiseworthy, the constitution of our undertaking permits me not to sketch out properly, nor the weariness which I rightly apprehend from the attention of my learned reader in so rough and unbeaten a matter, and which I perhaps must fear to have already offended all too much, although I am in possession of being able to lay out some outlines of it.",["- Größe = \"magnitude\"; Welt = \"world\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Vorwurf = \"object\" (here \"subject-matter\"; same English as Gegenstand/Object, which does not occur).","- \"statt finden\" = \"find place\"; \"gegründet\" = \"well-grounded\"; Aufmerksamkeit = \"attention\".","- Syntax kept: \"erlaubet mir die Beschaffenheit ... und die Ermüdung ... nicht, gehörig zu entwerfen\": two subjects both governing \"nicht ... zu entwerfen\"; \"obgleich ich im Besitze bin\" kept literal.","- \"Gnüge leisten\" = \"satisfy\" (literally \"render satisfaction\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0790":["§ 151.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0791":["We find ourselves now in the land of experiences; but before we can take possession in it, we must first be certain that those claims have been annulled which pretend to have a better-grounded right to it, and want to push us out of this territory. Our effort, which we have applied up to now to this end, would be incomplete if we were to pass over the attempt and mechanical proof that has the highly renowned Herr von Musschenbröck as its author, and which is consequently persuasive and acute, without defending against it the doctrine of forces we have undertaken. By it he has thought to defend the living forces in the Leibnizian meaning, and therefore it is our duty to examine it.",["- *Erfahrungen* (plural) rendered \"experiences\"; *Erfahrung* = experience.","- *überredend* rendered \"persuasive\" (cf. *Überredung* = persuasion); *Bedeutung* = meaning.","- *Kräftenlehre* = \"doctrine of forces\"; *Kraft* = force; *lebendige Kraft* = \"living force.\"","- *dawieder* (\"against it\") has an ambiguous referent in the German: the attempt/proof, or Musschenbröck; kept as \"against it.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0792":["Upon exact consideration of it we shall be taught: that it does not have the hoped-for result, but rather confirms Descartes's measure of forces. And this will confirm anew our often-mentioned remark: that one encounters no trace of a force to be estimated according to the square so long as one believes oneself to find its origin nowhere other than in external causes, and that the true living force is not generated from outside in the body, but is the result of the endeavor arising in the body from the inner natural force upon external solicitation; that therefore all those who accept nothing but the measure of the externally acting mechanical causes in order to determine from it the measure of the force in the suffering body, provided only that they judge correctly, will never encounter anything other than Descartes's estimation.",["- *Erwegung* = \"consideration\" (Betrachtung is also \"consideration\" in the glossary); *belehret* = taught (*Belehrung*).","- *schätzen* / *Schätzung* / *Maaß* rendered \"estimate\" / \"estimation\" / \"measure\"; *Kräftenmaaß* = \"measure of forces.\"","- *Bestrebung* = \"endeavor\"; *Erfolg* = \"result\" (both occurrences); *Sollicitation* kept as \"solicitation.\"","- The clause \"sondern der Erfolg, der … Bestrebung ist\" is syntactically awkward; kept as it stands.","- *urtheilen* = judge (v.); *bestimmen* = determine."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0793":["§ 152.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0794":["Musschenbroeck's mechanical proof of the living forces.",["- The German spelling is \"Muschenbröcksch-\"; adjective rendered as a genitive. *Beweiß* = proof."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0795":["The proof of Herr von Musschenbröck is as follows.",["- *Beweis* = proof."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0796":["Take a hollow cylinder, to which a spring is made fast. From the cylinder a rod must project, which is provided with holes, and which is stuck through the opening of a stiff plate of metal. If you now press the steel spring with force against this plate, and tension it, so that the rod projects farther out through its opening, you can maintain it in this tension by sticking a pin through a hole of the rod on the projecting side of it. Finally hang the cylinder as a pendulum on two threads on some machine, then pull out the pin, and the spring will snap loose, and give the cylinder a certain velocity, which is cognized through the height attained. Term this velocity 10. Thereupon make the same cylinder twice as heavy as it was before, by laying into it as many weights as are necessary for this, and tension the spring as before. If you then let it snap loose again: you will find, through the height that it reaches, that the velocity has 7.07 degrees. From this Herr von Musschenbröck argues as follows.",["- *Benennet* = \"Term\" (*Benennung* = term). *erkannt* = \"cognized.\"","- *Grade* = \"degrees\" (*Grad*); *Pendul* rendered \"pendulum\"; *Blech* rendered \"plate of metal.\"","- *Spannung* / *spannen* = \"tension\"; *Gewicht* = \"weights.\"","- Decimal comma of the original rendered as a point."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0797":["The spring was tensioned equally both times, and therefore had equal force in both cases, and since it applies its entire force each time, it also brought equal forces into the cylinder both times; therefore the force that a body of simple mass possesses with 10 degrees of velocity must be equal to that which is to be found in another that has a double mass and 7.07 degrees of velocity. But this is possible in no other way than if one estimates the force according to the product of the mass into the square of the velocity; for all other possible functions of the velocity do not allow this equality, but according to the estimation by the square alone are the squares of the numbers 10 and 7.07, *quam proxime*, in inverse ratio of the masses 1 and 2, and consequently their products into the reciprocal masses equal.",["- *einfach* = \"simple\" (of mass); *Product*, *Functionen* kept as \"product,\" \"functions\"; *Zahl* = number.","- *Verhältniß* = \"ratio\"; *Art* = \"way\"; *möglich* = possible.","- *Kraft* in the plural (*Kräfte*) = \"forces\" where the German has the plural.","- *quam proxime* is Latin in the original."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0798":["Thus, he concludes, the forces are to be estimated not according to the measure of the velocities, but the square of them.",["- *schließt* = \"concludes\" (*Schluß* = inference; conclusion)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0799":["§ 153.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0800":["I am obliged not to make the remark that I want to set out against this argument all too lengthy; therefore I will mention nothing of the well-grounded objection that I could still make here, that the moments of the pressure of the spring extending itself are, even by the admission of the Leibnizians, only dead forces, and consequently, both they and the moments of force imparted with them to the body, must be estimated simply according to the velocities, and hence also the entire force, which is the sum of these moments; but rather I will proceed in a mechanical way known to everyone, which has the distinctness of geometry in itself, but at the same time elucidate it somewhat in detail, not as if the matter were not easy enough that it could be comprehended more briefly, but so that all confusion that has hitherto prevailed in the dispute over the estimation of forces with regard to the effect of springs may be done away with once and for all entirely.",["- *Erinnerung* = \"remark\"; *Einwendung* = \"objection\"; *Momente* = \"moments.\"","- *Deutlichkeit* = \"distinctness\" (*deutlich*); *an sich* = \"in itself\"; *begriffen* = \"comprehended\"; *Wirkung* = \"effect\"; *erläutern* = \"elucidate\" (*Erläuterung*).","- *Streit der Kräftenschätzung* = \"dispute over the estimation of forces.\"","- The opening clause (\"die Erinnerung … nicht gar zu weitläuftig zu machen\") is kept in its German construction."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0801":["§ 154.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0802":["Herr von Musschenbröck says: the spring is tensioned equally in both cases, consequently it has in both equal force, but each time it imparts its entire force to its cylinder, therefore it also gives both times, when it extends itself, an equal force to its cylinder. This is the foundation of the proof, but also of the error, although the latter is not so much personal to Herr von Musschenbröck as rather peculiar to the entire defenders of the Leibnizian estimation of forces.",["- *Irrthum* = \"error\"; *Fundament* = \"foundation\"; *mittheilen* = \"impart.\"","- *dieser* (\"the latter\") taken to refer to *Irrthum*; German allows *Beweis* as well."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0803":["An equally tensioned spring imparts a greater force to a larger body than to a smaller.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0804":["If one speaks of the entire force of a spring, one can understand by it nothing other than the intension of its tension, which is equal to that force which the body, in which it works, receives in a moment from the pressure of it. With regard to this one can well say that it is equal, whether the body in which the spring works may be large or small. But if one looks to that force which it brings into a body in a certain time through its continued pressing, then it is evident: that the magnitude of the force brought in this way into the body depends on the magnitude of the time in which the equal pressing has accumulated in the body; and that the greater this time is, the greater also is the force that the equally tensioned spring imparts to the body in it. Now, however, one can make the time that the spring, in pushing a body forward, needs until it has extended itself entirely, longer according as one wants, namely if one makes the mass that is to be pushed forward greater, as this is unknown to no one; thus one can also arrange at pleasure that the very same spring at equal tension now distributes more, now less force through its extension, according as the mass that is driven by the spring is increased or diminished. From this it is clear how unnatural the expression is: that the spring imparts its entire force to a body that it pushes forward through the extension. For the force that it gives the body is a result that depends not alone on the force of the spring, but at the same time on the constitution of the pushed body, according as this finds itself longer or shorter under the pressings of this spring, i.e., according as it is greater or smaller in mass; but the force of the spring considered in itself is nothing other than the moment of its extension.",["- *Intension* kept as \"intension\"; *Moment* = \"moment\" (both \"in einem Moment\" and \"Moment ihrer Ausspannung\").","- *Größe* = \"magnitude\"; *Zeit* = time; *Masse* = \"mass\"; *Beschaffenheit* = \"constitution\"; *Ausdruck* = \"expression.\"","- *Ausstreckung* / *Ausreckung* / *Ausspannung* = \"extension\" for the first two; \"extension\" also for *Ausspannung* (one English word for three German words).","- *Erfolg* = \"result\"; *Drückung* = \"pressing\"; *Druck* = \"pressure.\"","- *dieser* after \"nachdem\" refers to the pushed body; *in derselben* (\"in it\") is ambiguous between the time and the spring's action, kept as \"in it.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0805":["§ 155.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0806":["Resolution of the Muschenbroeck difficulty.",["- *Auflösung* = \"resolution\"; *Schwierigkeit* = \"difficulty.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0807":["Now it is easy to prevent the confusion in the Musschenbröckian proof.",["- *Verwirrung* = \"confusion\"; *verhüten* = \"prevent.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0808":["The cylinder that is twice as heavy is exposed longer to the pressings of the spring, while this extends itself, than the other, of simple mass. The spring pushes the latter forth more quickly with equal tensile force, and ends the space of its extension with it in a shorter time than with the former. But because the moment of the force that the spring impresses on the cylinders in each instant is equal in both (for the moment of its velocity is inversely as the masses), the heavier cylinder must, through the impulse of the spring, acquire more force than the lighter. Thus that estimation is false according to which these forces would be found equal in both, i.e. they cannot be estimated according to the square of the velocity.",["- Eindrücken/eindrucken rendered \"impress\"; Schätzung \"estimation\"; Kraft \"force\"; Geschwindigkeit \"velocity\"; Raum \"space\" (all consistently).","- \"diesen\"/\"jenem\" and \"dieser\" (\"this\" = the spring in the first sentence) kept as in German; \"Diesen ... jenem\" refer to the cylinders, with \"latter/former\" a fixed choice for the German demonstratives.","- Moment is left as in German (Latin-derived), not translated."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0809":["§ 156.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0810":["Whence the squares of the velocities of the cylinders may be in inverse ratio of the masses.",["- \"Quad.\" abbreviation for Quadrate; \"seyn\" is plural subjunctive/infinitive form in the German, rendered \"may be\".","- Verhältniß is \"ratio\" here; the same word is rendered \"proportion\" nowhere in this passage, while Proportion (0816) is \"proportion\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0811":["If one still wants to know the cause whence the velocities of the cylinders, which they receive from the same spring, are just so proportioned that their squares are related inversely as the masses (which ratio is actually that by which the defender of Herr von Leibnitz was enticed), we can make this too clear without difficulty, without taking to help for that purpose any other than Descartes's measure.",["- \"Cartesens Maaß\" rendered \"Descartes's measure\"; Maaß = measure.","- \"Vertheidiger\" is singular in the German (\"the defender\").","- \"angelocket\" = \"enticed\"; \"Hülfe\" = \"help\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0812":["For it is known from the first grounds of mechanics: that in uniformly accelerated motion (*motu uniformiter accelerato*) the squares of the velocities attained are related as the spaces run through; consequently, if the moments of the velocities of two bodies, both of which are comprehended in *motu uniformiter accelerato*, are unequal, the squares of the velocities that they attain in such motion will stand in a ratio compounded of the spaces and these moments. Now, however, in the Musschenbroekian experiment the equally tensed spring communicates to each cylinder its motion *motu uniformiter accelerato*, and the spaces that they run through with such accelerated motion are equal, in that the spring extends itself to the point of its greatest extension; therefore the squares of the velocities received thereby are related as the moments of the velocity that the pressing of the spring imparts to each cylinder, i.e. inversely as the masses of these cylinders.",["- \"Gründe\" = \"grounds\"; \"begriffen\" in \"begriffen sind\" rendered \"comprehended\" (cf. begreifen = comprehend; here in the sense \"engaged in\"), preserved literally.","- Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Ausstreckung = \"extension\" as well in 0808/0811 context (\"extends itself\" for ausstrecken); one English word for two German words.","- \"Musschenbröckischen\" rendered \"Musschenbroekian\" (Musschenbroek)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0813":["§ 157.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0814":["Now I come to setting out those experiments and experiences which irrefutably prove the actuality and the existence of the forces in nature that are to be estimated according to the square of the velocity, and will reward my kind reader, for all the laborious attention that the present poor essays have caused him, with a victorious conviction.",["- Versuche = \"experiments\"; Erfahrungen = \"experiences\" (Erfahrung = experience).","- Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; Daseyn = \"existence\"; Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\".","- \"werden\" belongs grammatically with \"ich komme dahin ... und ... belohnen werden\" as printed; subject of \"werden\" is ambiguous (the experiments/experiences, or \"I\"), preserved; the German verb is plural, so the experiments are the likelier subject, but I leave the English \"will reward\" ambiguous."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0815":["Experiments that prove the living forces.",["- lebendige Kräfte = \"living forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0816":["I have to do only with those to whom the constitution of the dispute about the living forces is sufficiently known. Hence I presuppose that my readers have adequate information of the notorious experiments of Messrs. Riccioli, 's Gravesande, Poleni and von Musschenbroek, who investigated the forces of bodies by measuring the impressions that these caused by impact in soft materials. I will only touch briefly on this: that balls of equal size and mass, which fell freely from unequal height into the soft material, e.g. tallow, drove into it such cavities as had the proportion of the heights from which they had fallen, i.e. the ratio of the square of their velocities; and that, if they were equal in size but of unequal mass, but the heights from which one let them fall stood in inverse proportion to these masses, then the cavities driven into the soft material were found equal. Against the correctness of these experiments the Cartesians have known nothing to object; it was only the consequence drawn from them over which people have disputed.",["- \"Ricciolus\" given as printed (Latinized form of Riccioli); \"Kundschaft\" = \"information\" (glossary has Kenntnis = information).","- Eindrücke = \"impressions\"; Folgerung = \"consequence\"; Materie = \"material(s)\", \"Materien\" plural; Größe = \"size\" (glossary: magnitude).","- \"darum man gestritten hat\" rendered \"over which people have disputed\"; \"man\" = \"one/people\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0817":["The Leibnitzians have argued quite correctly from this in the following way. The obstacle that the soft material opposes to the force of the body penetrating into it is nothing other than the interconnection of its parts, and hence what the body has to do, in penetrating into it, consists solely and alone in this, that it separates its parts. But this interconnection is uniform through the whole soft mass, thus the quantity of the resistance and hence also of the force that the body must apply to break it is as the sum of the severed parts, i.e. as the magnitude of the cavities driven in. But these are related, according to the cited experiment, as the squares of the velocities of the penetrating bodies, consequently the forces of these are as the squares of their velocities.",["- Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Theile = \"parts\"; Größe = \"magnitude\" here; Summe = \"sum\".","- \"Versuch\" = \"experiment\"; Quantität kept as \"quantity\".","- \"dieselbe zu brechen\": \"it\" (feminine) could refer to the force or the resistance/interconnection; rendered \"it\" to keep ambiguity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0818":["§ 158.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0819":["Objection of the Cartesians.",["- Einwurf = \"objection\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0820":["The defenders of Descartes have been able to object nothing sound to this. Only, because they had formerly seen with undoubted certainty that the living forces were condemned by mathematics, to which the Leibnitzians nonetheless also appealed, they thought to help themselves out of this difficulty as well as they could, in that they did not doubt that that experiment must be deceptive which seemed to establish something that geometry did not permit. We, on the other hand, have already adduced the necessary reminders above; now we want only to see what sort of evasion it was that the Cartesians used to make the cited experiment invalid.",["- Gewißheit = \"certainty\"; gültig/ungültig = \"valid\"/\"invalid\"; Versuch = \"experiment\".","- \"konten\" read as \"konnten\" (old spelling); \"hergegen\" rendered \"on the other hand\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0821":["They objected that the Leibnitzians here again paid no attention to the time in which these cavities were made. Time, they said, was just as much a knot in the overcoming of the obstacles of this soft material as it had been in the overcoming of gravity. The impressed cavities would not be made in equal time. In short they were convinced that the objection on account of the time in the overcoming of the obstacles of gravity had been valid (as indeed it in fact was), and now, they thought, one could bring it up here again, and use it with just such success against the living forces.",["- Zeit = \"time\"; Schwere = \"gravity\"; Einwurf = \"objection\"; überzeugt = \"convinced\"; gültig = \"valid\".","- \"Acht\" in \"nicht Acht\" = \"pay no attention\" (idiom \"Acht haben\"); \"auf die Bahn bringen\" = \"bring up\".","- Subjunctives (hätten, sey, würden) in reported speech rendered with \"had/was/would\" and \"they said\" supplied by the English reporting construction, not by added text."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0822":["§ 159.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0823":["Is refuted.",["- \"Wird wiederleget\" = passive, \"is refuted\"; \"wieder-\" old spelling for \"wider-\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0824":["I know well that the Leibnitzians briefly remedied this complaint by, among other things, letting two cones of different base surface fall into the soft material, whereby the times in which their cavities were made necessarily had to be equal, and nevertheless the result was constituted as before; but I will renounce this advantage too, and annihilate the difficulty that the Cartesians make from the ground up.",["- Grundfläche = \"base surface\"; \"aus dem Grunde\" = \"from the ground up\" (Grund = ground; idiom).","- nothwendig = \"necessarily\"; Wirkung/Erfolg: Erfolg = \"result\" (not in glossary)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0825":["In the effect of gravity time comes into account as well.",["- Wirkung = \"effect\"; Schwere = \"gravity\"; \"in Anschlag kommen\" = \"come into account\"; \"mit\" rendered \"as well\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0826":["One need do nothing further than weigh the cause on account of which the resistance of the gravity-pressing that a body is to overcome is proportioned not to the space but to the time. The ground, however, is this. If the body overcomes a spring of gravity, it does not thereby annihilate its efficacy, but only affords it the counterweight, while it nevertheless retains its counter-striving undiminished, in order to act on it continually with equal degree for as long as it is exposed to it. If the body, by overpowering each spring of gravity, were at the same time, so to speak, to burst it asunder and nullify its force, then there is no doubt that, because each spring has equal force, the resistance that the body suffers would be equal to the sum of all the burst springs, let the time be as it may. But now each spring, notwithstanding that it is overcome by the body, retains its pressing-force, and continues this in it for as long as it finds itself under it, and consequently for the effect that a single spring does no single and indivisible pressure can be given, but it does a connected series of pressings, which is the greater, the longer the time the body is subjected to it, e.g. in those parts of the space where the motion of the body is slower, there the little part of time of the sojourn in each point is also longer than where the motion is quicker, and consequently it endures there from each individual spring a longer series of equal pressings than here.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0827":["This is found quite otherwise with soft matter.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0828":["But this is found quite otherwise in the separation of the soft mass. Each element of the soft mass has an equal force to cohere, and thereby it takes from the body that separates it an equal degree of force, but just thereby it is also at the same time separated, and so henceforth already offers no more resistance, let the time that it sojourns with it afterwards be as great as it will. For here the spring is broken at the same time by the very effect that is equal to its resistance, and can therefore not go on acting, as the spring of gravity did, which was in itself indestructible. Hence the resistance that the soft mass offers to the penetrating body is as the sum of the springs that it breaks, i.e. as the cavity that it strikes in, without time here having the least to do with it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0829":["§ 160.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0830":["The Leibnizians have cause to triumph with no little satisfaction over this important lapse of the Cartesians. This accident avenges, by an equal fate upon their opponents, the disgrace that the reproof of so many false steps has brought upon them. The Leibnizians supposed they found living forces in such cases in which they were not; but what hinders this? the Cartesians were nevertheless unable to see them in the cases in which they actually were, and in which no one without great blindness could have overlooked them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0831":["§ 161.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0832":["The experiment cited, therefore, proves the existence of such forces in nature as have the square of the velocity for their measure; but our preceding considerations explain under which conditions the same do not take place, and also which conditions are the only ones under which they can find place. If one makes use of all this according to our instruction, one acquires not only a sufficient certainty about living forces, but also a concept of their nature that is not only more correct but also more complete than it ever otherwise has been or could have been. The particular constitution of this experiment at hand furnishes still some extraordinary marks that can give occasion for particular remarks; but I cannot engage in them at all, since the attention of the favorable reader, wearied by so many involved investigations, perhaps wishes for nothing more than the conclusion of these considerations.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0833":["But there is still a single one which I cannot leave untouched, because it confirms the preceding laws and imparts a great light to them. The experiment that we have in hand proves such forces as have the estimation according to the square of the velocity in themselves; therefore, in accordance with the 4th number of § 138, the velocities of the counter-striving of each element of the obstacle in this experiment must occur with finite degrees, for if they were to occur only with infinitely small ones, like the pressings of gravity, then the overcoming of the same would give to cognize a force to be estimated according to the square just as little as in these, § 139. We shall therefore prove: that the *renisus* of every element of the soft mass occurs not with infinitely small velocity, like gravity, but with a finite degree.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0834":["§ 162.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0835":["The moment of the obstacle of the soft matter occurs with finite velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0836":["If one divides the cylindrical cavity that the spherical body strikes into the soft matter into its circular little disks lying one over another, whose thickness is infinitely small, then each of them indicates the element of the displaced mass. Each of these therefore takes from the penetrating body an infinitely small part of its velocity, because all of them together take from it the whole velocity. Since, however, the quantity of such a little circular disk is infinitely small in relation to the mass of the ball, it follows that the velocity of its counter-striving must be of finite magnitude, so that it can take from the body, through its resistance, an infinitely small part of its motion. Thus each element of the soft matter affords its resistance to the body striking into it with an endeavor that has a finite measure of velocity. Q. E. D. [W. Z. E.]",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0837":["§ 163.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0838":["Thus we have accomplished our business, which with regard to the object toward which it was directed has been great enough, if only the execution had been in keeping with this undertaking. I imagine that I, especially as concerns the main work, can lay claim to an incontestable certainty. With regard to this preeminence, which I arrogate to myself, I cannot end the present treatise without first closing with my creditors the account in learnedness and invention. After the acute efforts of the Cartesians, it was not difficult to guard against the confusion of the square-estimation with mathematics, and after the ingenious arrangements of the Leibnizians, it was almost impossible to miss it in nature. The acquaintance with these two outermost boundaries had to determine without difficulty the point in which the true on both sides coincided. To meet with this, nothing less than a great acuteness was necessary; it required only a small absence of party zeal, and a brief equilibrium of the inclinations of the mind, and the burden was at once done away with. If I have succeeded in perceiving some false steps in the cause of Herr von Leibniz, I am nevertheless in this too a debtor of this great man, for I would have been able to do nothing without the guiding thread of the excellent law of continuity, which we have to thank this immortal inventor for, and which was the only means of finding the way out of this labyrinth. In short, even if the matter turns out in the best way to my advantage: the share of honor that remains to me is yet so slight that I do not fear that ambition could demean itself so far as to begrudge it to me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0839":["End.",["- 0826: *Feder* \"spring\"; *Schwerdrückung* / *Drückung(en)* rendered \"gravity-pressing\" / \"pressing(s)\"; *Wirksamkeit* \"efficacy\"; *Wirkung* \"effect\" (also *wirken* \"act\"); *Kraft* \"force\"; *Wiederstand* \"resistance\"; *Wiederstrebung* \"counter-striving\"; *Raum* \"space\"; *Zeit* \"time\"; *Grund* \"ground\"; *Grad* \"degree\"; *Summe* \"sum\".","- 0826: \"vor die Wirkung ... nicht ein einzelner ... Druck angegeben werden\" kept as in the German (*vor* = *für*); the syntax is awkward and left so. *Zeittheilchen* \"little part of time\".","- 0828: *Trennung* \"separation\"; *Masse* \"mass\"; *Materie* \"matter\"; *zertrennet* \"separated\" / *zerbrochen* \"broken\". *Höhle* \"cavity\".","- 0830: *Vergehung* \"lapse\"; *Verweisung* \"reproof\"; *lebendige Kräfte* \"living forces\"; *Zufall* \"accident\".","- 0832: *Betrachtung* \"consideration\"; *Begriff* \"concept\"; *Merkmahl* \"mark\"; *Beschaffenheit* \"constitution\"; *Versuch* \"experiment\"; *Gewißheit* \"certainty\"; *Schluß* \"conclusion\"; *Maaß* \"measure\"; *statt haben* / *Platz finden* both rendered \"take place\" / \"find place\".","- 0833: *Renisus* is Latin in the original, italicized; *Schätzung* \"estimation\"; *Geschwindigkeit* \"velocity\"; *Gesetze* \"laws\"; *erkennen* \"cognize\"; \"§ 139\" follows the clause as printed.","- 0836: *W. Z. E.* (*was zu erweisen war*) rendered \"Q. E. D.\" with the German abbreviation in brackets; the brackets are mine, not the Academy apparatus.","- 0838: *Gegenstand*-type words absent; *Vorwurf* \"object\" (for the undertaking's subject matter, a different German word from *Gegenstand*); *Kenntniß* \"acquaintance\"; *Grenzen* \"boundaries\"; *Gemüthsneigungen* \"inclinations of the mind\"; *Gleichgewicht* \"equilibrium\"; *Gesetz der Continuität* \"law of continuity\"."]],"achsendrehung-0001":["Investigation of the question,\nWhether\nthe earth in its revolution about the axis,\nthrough which it\nproduces the alternation of day and night,\nhas suffered any alteration since the first times of its origin,\nwhat the cause of it may be,\nand by what one could assure oneself of it,\nwhich\nby the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin\nfor the prize\nfor the current year\nwas set as a problem.\nIn: Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten.\nNo. 23. Saturday, the 8th of June, Anno 1754.\nConclusion: No. 24. Saturday, the 15th of June, Anno 1754.",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\" (as in the glossary), here the Academy's prize question; \"aufgegeben worden\" rendered \"was set as a problem.\"","- Umdrehung = \"revolution\"; Achse = \"axis\"; Veränderung = \"alteration\" (not \"change\"), used consistently; Ursache = \"cause.\"","- \"vor das jetztlaufende Jahr\" = \"for the current year\"; \"vor\" is old spelling of \"für.\"","- \"versichern\" = \"assure oneself\"; \"Beschluß\" here = \"conclusion\" (of the serial), not the glossary's Schluß."]],"achsendrehung-0002":["The judgment will shortly become known which the Royal Academy of Sciences will pass upon those writings that have competed for the prize this year on the occasion of its problem. I have made considerations on this subject, and since I have weighed only the physical side of it, I have wished to sketch my thoughts about it briefly, after I have seen that by its nature it is on this side incapable of being brought to that degree of perfection which that treatise must have that is to carry off the prize for it.",["- Urtheil = judgment; Betrachtungen = considerations; Grade = degree; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Aufgabe = problem (glossary).","- Vorwurf = \"subject\" (not in glossary); the same word recurs in 0003, rendered \"subject.\"","- \"daß er ... unfähig ist\": \"er\" refers to Vorwurf (masculine); kept as \"it.\"","- \"den Preiß davon tragen soll\" rendered literally \"carry off the prize for it\"; \"davon\" is ambiguous in reference."]],"achsendrehung-0003":["The problem of the Academy consists in the following: Whether the earth in its revolution about the axis, through which it produces the alternation of day and night, has suffered any alteration since the first times of its origin, what the cause of it may be, and by what one could assure oneself of it? One can trace this question historically, in that one compares the monuments of antiquity from the most remote times, concerning the magnitude of their year and the intercalations which they have had to make use of in order to prevent its beginning from being movable through all the seasons, with the length of the year determined in our days, in order to see whether that year in the oldest times contained in itself more or fewer days or hours than now; in which first case the speed of the axial rotation has been diminished, in the second, however, increased up to the present. I will not seek to obtain light in my subject through the aids of history. I find this document so obscure, and its reports with regard to the present question so little reliable: that the theory which one might think up in order to make it agree with the grounds of nature would presumably smack very much of fictions. I will therefore on that account hold immediately to nature, whose connections can designate the outcome distinctly and give occasion to turn the remarks from history to the right side.",["- Aufgabe = problem; Grösse = magnitude; Grunde (Gründen) = grounds; Verbindungen = connections (Verbindung = combination in glossary; plural here \"connections\" for sense, flagged); Urkunde = \"document.\"","- Achsendrehung = \"axial rotation\"; Umdrehung = \"revolution.\"","- \"deutlich\" = distinctly; \"bezeichnen\" = designate; \"Erfolg\" = outcome; \"Bemerckungen\" = remarks.","- \"in welchem ersten Falle ... in dem zweyten\": the verb \"ist\" is elided in German before \"vermehret worden\"; \"has been\" supplied by English grammar only.","- \"jenes\" (that year) and \"dieser Urkunde\" (this document): referents kept as in German; \"ihre Nachrichten\" = \"its reports.\"","- \"auf die rechte Seite zu lencken\" = \"to turn ... to the right side\"; idiom kept literal."]],"achsendrehung-0004":["The earth rolls unceasingly about its axis, with a free motion which, after it was once impressed upon it together with its formation, would henceforth continue unaltered and with equal speed and direction into all infinite times, if no hindrances or external causes were present to retard or to accelerate it. I undertake to demonstrate to myself that the external cause is actually present, and indeed one that gradually diminishes the motion of the earth, and strives to annihilate its rotation altogether in immeasurably long periods. This occurrence, which is some day to happen, is so important and wondrous that although the fatal point of time of its completion is set so far out that the capacity of the globe of the earth to be inhabited, and the duration of the human race, perhaps does not reach to the tenth part of this time, nevertheless even the certainty alone of this impending fate, and the steady approach of nature to it, is a worthy object of admiration and investigation.",["- Fähigkeit = capacity; Begebenheit = occurrence; Gegenstand = object; Untersuchung = investigation (also in 0001).","- \"Ich unternehme mir darzuthun\" = \"I undertake to demonstrate to myself\" (reflexive \"mir\" kept literally).","- \"Zeitpunckt\" = \"point of time\"; \"Umschwung\" = \"rotation\"; \"Bewegung\" = \"motion.\"","- \"bewohnt zu seyn\" depends on \"Fähigkeit\"; the singular verb \"reichet\" after a compound subject kept as in German (\"does not reach\")."]],"achsendrehung-0005":["If the space of the heavens were filled with a somewhat resisting matter, the daily rotation of the earth would encounter in it an unceasing hindrance, whereby its speed would have to be gradually consumed and finally exhausted. But now this resistance is not to be feared, since Newton has demonstrated in a convincing way that the space of the heavens, which permits even to the light cometary vapors a free unhindered motion, is filled with infinitely little resisting matter. Apart from this hindrance, not to be presumed, there is no external cause that can have an influence on the motion of the earth other than the attraction of the moon and the sun, which, since it is the universal driving-mechanism of nature, from which Newton has developed its secrets in a way as distinct as it is undoubted, furnishes a reliable ground here, on which one can make a sure test.",["- Materie = matter; Himmelsraum = \"space of the heavens\"; Raum = space; Grund = ground; Wiederstand = \"resistance.\"","- Relative \"welche\" after \"Mond und der Sonne\" is grammatically ambiguous (attraction / moon and sun); \"ihre Geheimnisse\" likewise (\"its/their secrets\"); I read \"welche ... sie ... ist\" with \"attraction\" as the singular antecedent, as the verb \"ist\" and \"abgiebt\" are singular; \"its secrets\" for \"ihre\" (nature's) kept ambiguous only as far as English allows.","- Triebwerck = \"driving-mechanism\"; überzeugend = \"convincing\"; deutlich = \"distinct.\""]],"achsendrehung-0006":["If the earth were an entirely solid mass without any fluidities, the attraction of neither the sun nor the moon would do anything to alter its free axial rotation; for it attracts the eastern as well as the western parts of the globe of the earth with equal force, and thereby causes no inclination toward the one side or the other, consequently it leaves the earth in complete freedom to continue this revolution unhindered, just as without any external influence. In the case, however, that the mass of a planet comprises in itself a considerable quantity of the fluid element, the united attractions of the moon and the sun, in that they move this fluid matter, will impress upon the earth a part of this agitation. The earth is in such circumstances. The water of the ocean covers at least the third part of its surface, and is through the attraction of the said heavenly bodies in unceasing motion, and indeed toward a side that is directed exactly opposed to the axial rotation. It deserves therefore to be considered whether this cause is not capable of bringing about some alteration in the rotation. The attraction of the moon, which has the greatest share in this effect, keeps the water of the ocean in unceasing upsurge, whereby it strives to flow toward and to rise at those points exactly under the moon, as well on the side turned toward it as on that turned away from it, and because these points of swelling advance from morning toward evening; so they communicate to the world-sea a constant flowing-on toward just this region in its whole content. The experience of seafarers has long since placed this universal motion beyond doubt, and it is most distinctly remarked in the straits and gulfs, where the water, in that it must run through a narrow strait, increases its speed. Since this flowing-on is now set exactly opposed to the rotation of the earth, we have a cause on which we can safely count, that it strives unceasingly to weaken and diminish the latter, so much as lies in it.",["- Qvantität = quantity; Kraft = force; Erfahrung = experience; Wirckung = effect; Veränderung = alteration; Erschütterung = \"agitation\"; Umwältzung / Umdrehung = \"rotation\" / \"revolution.\"","- \"Die Erde ist in solchen Umständen\" = \"in such circumstances\"; the short sentence is kept as a separate sentence as in the German.","- \"Inhalt\" = \"content\" (glossary); \"Weltmeer\" = \"world-sea\"; \"Aufschwellung\" = \"swelling\"; \"Aufwallung\" = \"upsurge.\"","- \"jene\" (last clause) refers to Drehung; \"an ihr\" refers to the cause (Ursache): \"so much as lies in it\" is ambiguous in German and kept so.","- \"von Morgen gegen Abend\" kept literal (\"from morning toward evening\").","- \"gerade entgegen gerichtet\" rendered \"directed exactly opposed to.\""]],"achsendrehung-0007":["It is true, if one holds together the slowness of this motion with the speed of the earth, the insignificance of the quantity of the water with the magnitude of this globe, and the lightness of the first with the heaviness of the latter, it could seem: that its effect can be held for nothing. But if one on the other hand considers: that this impulse is unremitting, has lasted from time immemorial, and will always last, that the rotation of the earth is a free motion, in which the least quantity that is taken from it remains lost without replacement, whereas the diminishing cause remains unceasingly active in equal strength, it would be a prejudice very unbecoming to a philosopher to declare a slight effect worthless, which through a constant summation must nevertheless finally exhaust even the greatest quantity.",["- Grösse = magnitude; Wirckung = effect; Ursache = cause; Weltweisheit not used; \"Philosoph\" = \"philosopher.\"","- \"ihre Wirckung\" (\"its effect\") refers back to \"dieser Bewegung\" (feminine possessive); reference left as in German.","- \"von je her\" = \"from time immemorial\"; \"Vorurtheil\" = \"prejudice\"; \"Summirung\" = \"summation.\"","- \"erweget\" = \"considers\" (erwägen; the glossary has Betrachtung = consideration, not applicable)."]],"achsendrehung-0008":["(The conclusion follows in the future.)",["- Beschluß = \"conclusion\"; \"künftig\" = \"in the future\" (i.e., in a later issue)."]],"achsendrehung-0009":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"achsendrehung-0010":["Conclusion of the investigation of that question which was set as a problem by the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin for the prize for the current year.",["- Aufgabe (aufgegeben worden) = problem; Untersuchung = investigation; Beschluß = \"conclusion\"; \"vor das jetztlaufende Jahr\" = \"for the current year.\""]],"achsendrehung-0011":["So that we can estimate to some extent the magnitude of the effect that the constant motion of the ocean from morning toward evening opposes to the axial rotation of the earth: we will only calculate the impact that the world-ocean makes against the morning coasts of the solid land of America, by extending its reach to both poles, in that we replace what is lacking in it, more than superfluously, through the projecting point of Africa and through the Oriental coasts of Asia. Let us posit the speed of the cited motion of the sea under the equator as 1 foot in a second, and, toward the poles, decreasing just like the motion of the parallel circles; finally, let the height of that surface which the solid land presents to the impact of the water, estimated in perpendicular depth, be assumed as 100 *toises* (French six-footed rods): so we will find the force with which the sea by its motion presses on this surface standing against it equal to the weight of a body of water whose basis is equal to the whole thought-of surface from one pole to the other, but whose height is equal to ⅟224 foot. This body of water, which comprehends eleven times a hundred thousand cubic *toises*, is exceeded by the magnitude of the terrestrial globe 123 *bimillion* times, and since the weight of this body of water always presses against the motion of the earth, one can easily find how much time would have to elapse until this hindrance of the earth exhausted its whole motion. 2 million years would be required for it, if one assumed the speed of the flowing sea to be equal to the end, and the lump of earth to be of equal density with the matter of the waters. On this footing, in moderate periods, since the thought-of diminution does not yet amount to much, e.g. in a time of 2 thousand years, the retardation would amount to so much: that a yearly course according to this would have to contain 8½ hours less than before, because the axial rotation has become slower by so much.",[]],"achsendrehung-0012":["Now the decrease of the daily motion indeed suffers great limitations through this: that 1. the density of the whole mass of the earth is not, as was presupposed here, equal to the specific gravity of water. 2. The speed of the flowing sea in its open expanse seems to be incomparably less than 1 foot in a second; against this, however, this deficiency is more than superfluously replaced, through the fact that 1. the force of the terrestrial globe, which was calculated here as in progressive motion with the speed of a point under the equator, is only an axial rotation, which is incomparably less, and moreover the hindrance, which is applied on the surface of a revolving globe, has in itself the advantage of the lever through its distance from the center, which two causes taken together increase the diminution through the onrush of the waters by 5½, 2ndly, however, which is the most principal, this effect of the moved ocean does not occur merely against the unevennesses projecting above the sea-bottom, the solid land, the islands and cliffs, but is exerted on the whole sea-bottom, which indeed amounts in each point to incomparably less than with the perpendicular onrush of the first calculation, against which, however, it must be replaced with an astonishing superfluity through the magnitude of the extent in which it occurs, which exceeds the aforementioned surface more than 8 million times.",[]],"achsendrehung-0013":["One will accordingly further be unable to doubt: that the perpetual motion of the world-ocean from evening toward morning, since it is a real and notable force, also always contributes something to the diminution of the axial rotation of the earth, the sequel of which must unfailingly become noticeable in long periods. Now the testimonies of history should properly be brought forward, in order to support the hypothesis; but I must confess that I can encounter no traces of an occurrence so probably to be presumed, and therefore leave to others the merit of supplementing this lack if possible.",[]],"achsendrehung-0014":["If the earth approaches the standstill of its revolution with steady steps, then the period of this alteration will be completed when its surface will be in respective rest with regard to the moon, i.e. when it will turn about the axis in the same time in which the moon runs around it, consequently will always turn the same side toward it. This state will be caused in it by the motion of the fluid matter, which covers one part of its surface only to a quite small depth. If it were fluid through and through to the center, then the attraction of the moon would in quite a short time bring its axial motion to this measured remainder. This sets before us at once distinctly the cause that has constrained the moon to turn always the same side toward the earth in its circuit around it. Not a preponderance of the turned-toward parts over the turned-away, but a really uniform turning of the moon about its axis, precisely in the time in which it runs around the earth, brings about this perpetual presentation of the same half. From this it can be concluded with reliability: that the attraction which the earth exerts on the moon, at the time of its original formation, when its mass was still fluid, must have brought the axial rotation, which this secondary planet presumably may then have had with greater speed, in the cited way to this measured remainder. From which it is also to be seen that the moon is a later heavenly body, which was added to the earth, after it had already laid aside its fluidity and acquired a solid state; otherwise the attraction of the moon would unfailingly in a short time have subjected it to the same fate that the moon has suffered from our earth. One can regard the latter remark as a sample of a natural history of the heavens, in which the first state of nature, the generation of the world-bodies and the causes of their systematic relations, had to be determined from the marks that the relations of the structure of the world show in themselves. This consideration, which in the great or rather in the infinite is that which the history of the earth contains in the small, can in such wide extension be comprehended just as reliably as one has been at pains in our days to sketch it with regard to our terrestrial globe. I have devoted a long series of considerations to this subject, and combined them in a system, which will appear publicly shortly under the title: Cosmogony, or attempt to derive the origin of the structure of the world, the formation of the heavenly bodies, and the causes of their motion, from the universal laws of motion of matter, in accordance with the theory of Newton.",[]],"erde-veralte-0001":["The question: Whether the earth is aging?\nConsidered physically\nIn: Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten.\nNo. 32. Saturday, the 10th of August. Anno 1754.\n1st continuation: No. 33. Saturday, the 17th of August. Anno 1754.\n2nd continuation: No. 34. Saturday, the 24th of August. Anno 1754.\n3rd continuation: No. 35. Saturday, the 31st of August. Anno 1754.\n4th continuation: No. 36. Saturday, the 7th of September. Anno 1754.\nConclusion: No. 37. Saturday, the 14th of September. Anno 1754.",["- 0011: Wirckung = effect; Gewalt = force (Kraft would also be \"force\"; Gewalt here unglossed); Grösse = magnitude; Gewichte = weight; Materie = matter; Dichtigkeit = density; Betrachtung (0014) = consideration; Merkmaal = mark; Ausdehnung = extension (rendered \"extension\" in glossary; here \"extension\" appears in \"wide extension\" in 0014).","- 0011: \"Bimillionenmal\" is the original's coinage (a number-word); left as \"*bimillion*\" in italics, unexplained. \"Basis\" is kept as in the German. \"dessen\" in \"dessen Erstreckung\" has an unresolved antecedent (the Weltmeer or its impact); kept ambiguous.","- 0011: \"bis ans Ende gleich\" is elliptical (equal to the end of what is unspecified); kept literal. \"Auf diesen Fuß\" rendered \"On this footing\" (idiom, a pun on Fuß not preserved).","- 0012: The numbering \"1.\" and \"2.\" in the first sentence, and the second \"1.\" and \"2tens\", are as in the source; the construction \"dagegen... dadurch, daß\" is kept as awkward as the German. \"um 5½\" lacks a stated unit or \"fold\"; left bare. \"die zwar... austrägt\" has an uncertain antecedent (Wirckung or Unebenheiten); kept ambiguous.","- 0012: \"Anlauf/Anfall\" both rendered \"onrush\"/\"impact\" (0011 Anfall = impact; 0012 Anlauf = onrush). \"Umfang\" here = extent (not the logical \"domain\" of the glossary).","- 0013: \"Folge\" = sequel; \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"Zeugnisse\" = testimonies; \"Spuhren\" = traces. \"daß... sie... auch immer etwas... beytrage\": the main verb is subjunctive and the syntax is loose; preserved.","- 0014: Erkenntnis not used; \"begriffen\" (begreifen) = comprehended; Zustand = state; Ursache = cause; \"Vorwurfe\" = subject (Vorwurf, unglossed). \"respecktiver Ruhe\" = \"respective rest\", Latinate spelling kept. \"Himmelskörper\"/\"Weltkörper\" = heavenly body / world-body. Title *Cosmogonie* kept as a title.","- erde-veralte-0001: Title and bibliographic matter only; \"Sonnabends\" rendered \"Saturday\"; \"Fortsetzung\" = continuation; \"Beschluß\" = conclusion (glossary: Schluß = inference/conclusion, but here a different sense)."]],"erde-veralte-0002":["If one wants to know whether a thing is to be called old, whether very old, or still young, one must not estimate it according to the number of years that it has lasted, but according to the relation that these have to that time which it is supposed to last. The very same duration that can be called a great age for one kind of creatures is not so for another. In the same time in which a dog grows old, the human being has scarcely passed beyond his childhood, and the oaks and cedars on Lebanon are not yet in their manly strength when the lindens or firs grow old and wither. The human being errs most when, in the great of the works of GOD, he wants to apply to the measuring-stick of age the series of human generations that have elapsed in this time. It is to be feared that it stands with his way of judging as with that of the roses in Fontenelle, which conjectured about the age of their gardener. Our gardener, they said, is a very old man, since the memory of roses he has been the same that he always was, in fact he does not die, he does not even change. If one weighs the durability that is encountered in the arrangements of the creation in the great members of its sum total, and which comes near to an infinity, one will be moved to believe: that a lapse of 5 or 6000 years, for the duration determined for the earth, is perhaps not yet that which a year is with regard to the life of a human being.",[]],"erde-veralte-0003":["To confess the truth, we have no marks in revelation from which we can gather whether the earth can now be regarded as young or old, as in the blossom of its perfection, or as grasped in the decline of its forces. It has indeed disclosed to us the time of its formation and the point in time of its childhood, but we do not know to which of the two end-points of its duration, the point of its beginning or of its demise, it is now nearer. It seems in fact to be a subject worthy of investigation, to determine whether the earth is growing old and through a gradual decrease of its forces is approaching its demise, whether it is now in the period of this decreasing age, or whether its constitution is still in well-being, or indeed perhaps has not yet fully reached the perfection to which it is supposed to develop, and thus has perhaps not yet passed beyond its childhood.",[]],"erde-veralte-0004":["When we hear the complaints of elderly people, we hear that nature is noticeably growing older and that one can detect the steps that it takes toward its decline. The weathers, they say, will no longer turn out as well as formerly. The forces of nature are exhausted, its beauty and regularity decrease. Human beings are no longer as strong or as old as formerly. This decrease, it is said, is not to be noticed in the natural constitution of the earth alone, it extends even to the moral property. The old virtues have been extinguished, in their place new vices are found. Falseness and deceit have taken the place of the old honesty. This delusion, which does not deserve to be refuted, is not so much a consequence of error as of self-love. The honest old men, who are so vain as to persuade themselves that heaven took care for them to place them in the light in the most flourishing times, cannot persuade themselves that after their death things should go on in the world just as well as they did before they were born. They would like to imagine that nature is growing old along with them, so that they need not regret leaving a world that is itself already near its demise.",[]],"erde-veralte-0005":["As groundless as this imagination is, of wanting to measure the age and durability of nature by the measuring-stick of a single human lifetime, so another conjecture, at first sight, does not seem to be quite so absurd: that in a few thousand years perhaps some alteration in the constitution of the earth's surface could become noticeable. It is not enough here to remark with Fontenelle: that the trees have not grown larger in former times than now, that human beings have been neither older nor stronger than they are now, it is, I say, not yet enough to conclude from this that nature does not grow old. These constitutions have their limitations, fixed for them through the essential determinations, which even the most advantageous constitution of nature and its most flourishing well-being cannot drive further. In all countries there is no difference with regard to this; the fat countries lying in the best climes have in this no advantage over the lean and unfruitful ones; but whether, if one could set up a comparison between reliable reports of ancient times and the exact observation of the present, some difference in their fertility would not be to be noticed, whether the earth perhaps formerly required less tending to furnish sustenance to the human race, this seems, if it could be decided, to promise a light in the problem at hand. It would, as it were, lay before our eyes the first members of a long progression, by which one could cognize to which state the earth is gradually approaching in the long courses of time of its age. This comparison, however, is very uncertain, or rather impossible. The industry of human beings does so much for the fertility of the earth: that one will hardly be able to determine whether in the going wild and desolation of those countries that formerly were flourishing states and now are almost entirely depopulated, the negligence of the former or the decrease of the latter is most to blame. I will recommend this investigation to those who have more skill and inclination to test this question, according to both conditions, in the monuments of history; I will treat it solely as a student of nature, in order, where possible, to arrive from this side at a thorough insight.",[]],"erde-veralte-0006":["Immanuel Kant",[]],"erde-veralte-0007":["(The continuation follows in the future.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0008":["Continuation of the physical consideration of the question:\nWhether the earth is growing old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0009":["The opinion of most investigators of nature who have sketched theories of the earth goes to this: that the fertility of the earth gradually decreases, that it approaches with slow steps the state of becoming uninhabited and waste, and that it needs only time to see nature entirely grown old and died away in the exhaustion of its forces. This question is important, and it is well worth the trouble to approach this conclusion with caution.",[]],"erde-veralte-0010":["But let us beforehand determine the concept that one has to make for oneself of the growing old of a body that forms itself through natural forces to perfection, and that modifies itself through the forces of the elements.",["(Notes for 0002–0010 follow, each a single line.)","- 0002: \"Art\" rendered \"kind\" (Art von Geschöpfen) and \"way\" (Art zu urtheilen); \"Innbegriff\" = sum total; \"Maasstabe\" = measuring-stick; \"Anstalten\" = arrangements; \"Ansehung\" = regard.","- 0002: \"seit Rosen gedenken\" kept literal (\"since the memory of roses\"); \"in dem Großen der Werke GOttes\" kept literal; \"die der Erde bestimmte Dauer\" kept as in German.","- 0002: \"vor\" is used for \"für\" throughout (eighteenth-century spelling); \"kan\" silently modernized.","- 0003: \"Merkmale\" = marks; \"Vollkommenheit\" = perfection; \"Kräfte\" = forces; \"Untergang\" = demise; \"Verfassung\" = constitution (also for Beschaffenheit, see 0004/0005; both \"constitution\"; Beschaffenheit also \"property\" in 0004 per glossary, \"moral property\").","- 0003: \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"subject\" (not in glossary); \"Wohlstande\" = well-being; \"ob ... oder ob\" clause structure kept; \"sie\" ambiguous in the German between the earth and revelation in the first sentence's \"Sie hat uns\" (grammatically \"Offenbarung\"): the English \"It has ... disclosed\" preserves this ambiguity.","- 0004: \"die Natur ältere\" is probably corrupt or elliptical (perhaps \"ältere\" = \"älter werde\"); rendered \"nature is growing older\"; \"Folge\" = consequence here (glossary: sequel; sequence), \"Wahn\" = delusion, \"Einbildung\" = imagination.","- 0005: \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Aufgabe\" = problem; \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"Progreßion\" = progression; \"erkennen\" = cognize; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"Zustande\" = state; \"Naturkündiger\" = student of nature (Naturforscher in 0009 = investigator of nature).","- 0010: \"Begriff\" = concept; \"bestimmen\" = determine; \"modificirenden\" rendered \"modifies itself\" (reflexive reading carried over from the preceding \"sich\"); \"Kräfte\" = forces."]],"erde-veralte-0011":["The growing old of a being is not, in the course of its changes, a section that has external and violent causes as its ground. The very same causes through which a thing arrives at perfection and is maintained in it bring it, through imperceptible steps of the changes, near again to its demise. It is a natural shading in the continuation of its existence, and a consequence of the very same grounds through which its formation was effected, that it must finally decay and perish. All natural things are subject to this law, that the same mechanism that worked at their perfection in the beginning, after they have reached the point of it, because they continue to alter the thing, remove it gradually again from the conditions of the good constitution, and finally deliver it over to ruin with unnoticed steps. This procedure of nature shows itself distinctly in the economy of the plant and animal kingdoms. The very same drive that makes the trees grow brings them death, when they have completed their growth. When the fibers and tubes are no longer capable of any extension, the nourishing sap, in that it continues to incorporate itself into the parts, begins to clog and to thicken the interior of the passages, and finally to make the plant die off and wither through the checked movement of the saps. The very mechanism by which the animal or the human being lives and grows up brings it finally death when the growth is completed. For, in that the nutritive juices, which serve for its maintenance, no longer at the same time widen the canals to which they attach themselves and enlarge them in their content, they narrow their inner cavity, the circulation of the fluids is checked, the animal bends, grows old and dies. Just so the gradual decline of the good constitution of the earth is likewise so interwoven into the sequence of the alterations, which initially effected its perfection, that it can become recognizable only in long periods of time. We must therefore cast a fleeting glance at the changeable scenes that nature plays from its beginning up to completion, in order to survey the whole chain of consequences, in which ruin is the last link.",[]],"erde-veralte-0012":["The earth, when it raised itself out of chaos, was unfailingly previously in a fluid state. Not only its round figure, but chiefly the spheroidal shape, in which the surface, with respect to the direction of gravity altered by the force of the rotation, assumed a perpendicular position at all points, prove that its mass had the capacity to accommodate itself of its own accord to the figure that equilibrium requires in this case. It passed from the fluid state into the solid; and indeed we see irrefutable traces that the surface hardened first, while the interior of the lump, in which the elements were still separating according to the laws of equilibrium, continually sent the intermixed particles of the elastic element of air up beneath the hardened crust and prepared wide cavities beneath it, into which it [the crust] was induced to sink with manifold inflections, to bring forth the unevennesses of the surface, the solid land, the mountains, the spacious depressions of the sea and the separation of the dry from the waters. We have just as undoubted monuments of nature, which give to recognize: that these upheavals have not entirely ceased in long periods of time, which is in keeping with the magnitude of a fluid lump, as the interior of our earth was then and long remained, in which the separation of the elements and the secretion of the air intermixed in the common chaos is not so soon completed, but the generated cavities were enlarged little by little and the foundations of the wide vaults made tottering anew and collapsed, but just thereby whole regions that were buried under the depth of the sea were laid bare and others, on the contrary, were sunk. After the interior of the earth had gained a firmer standing and the ruins had ceased, the surface of this globe became a little calmer, but it was still far removed from the state of a completed formation; the elements first had still to be assigned their certain limitations, which, by the prevention of all confusion, could maintain order and beauty over the whole surface. The sea itself raised the shores of the solid land with the sediment of the materials carried up, by the carrying away of which it deepened its own bed; it threw up dunes and dikes that warded off the floods. The streams, which were to carry off the moistures of the solid land, were not yet enclosed in proper flood-beds, they still inundated the plains, until they finally confined themselves to measured canals, and prepared a uniform slope from their origin to the sea. After nature had reached this state of order and had fortified itself in it, all elements on the surface of the earth were in equilibrium. Fruitfulness spread out its riches on all sides, it was fresh, in the bloom of its forces, or, if I may express myself so, in its manly age.",[]],"erde-veralte-0013":["Immanuel Kant",[]],"erde-veralte-0014":["(The continuation follows in the future.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0015":["Continuation of the physical exposition of the question:\nWhether the earth grows old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0016":["The nature of our terrestrial globe has not reached an equal step in all its parts in the progress of its age. Some parts of it are young and fresh, while in others it seems to decline and to grow old. In certain regions it is raw and only half-formed, while others find themselves in the bloom of their prosperity and still others, after having laid behind them their fortunate period, are already gradually approaching decline. On the whole the high regions of the earth's ground are the oldest, those first raised out of chaos and arrived at the completion of formation, the low are younger and have reached the step of their perfection later. According to this order, the lot will therefore befall the former first of approaching ruin again, while the latter are still farther removed from their fate.",[]],"erde-veralte-0017":["Human beings first inhabited the highest regions of the earth's ground; they descended into the plains only late and themselves had to lend a hand to hasten the working-out of nature, which was too slow in its formation for their rapid multiplication. Egypt, this gift of the Nile, was inhabited and populous in its uppermost part, when half of Lower Egypt, the whole Delta, and the region where the Nile, by deposition of the mud, raised the ground of its outflow, and threw up the banks of confined flood-beds, was still an uninhabited morass. Now the region of the old Thebaid seems to have little more of that exceptional fruitfulness and bloom that made its prosperity so extraordinary; on the other hand the beauty of nature has descended into the low and younger parts of the country, which now assert the precedence of fruitfulness over the high ones. The region of Lower Germany, which is a begetting of the Rhine, the flattest parts of Lower Saxony, the part of Prussia where the Vistula divides into so many arms and, as it were intent upon its eternal right, often strives to cover the lands under its waters, which human industry has in part won back from it, seem to be younger, richer and more blooming than the highest regions of the origin of these rivers, which were already inhabited when the latter were still morasses and gulfs of the sea.",["(All notes below concern erde-veralte-0011 to -0017 in order.)","- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Zustand = state; Bedingungen = conditions; Gesetz = law; Grund/Gründe = ground(s); Folge/Folgen = sequence/consequences, rendered by the same words in each place; Dasein = existence.","- 0011: \"Wesen\" = being; \"Verfassung\" = constitution (not in the glossary); \"Veränderung/Abänderung\" both rendered \"alteration/changes\" (Abänderungen = alterations).","- 0011: \"Ausbildung\" = formation; \"kenntlich\" = recognizable; \"Ausdehnung\" = extension (glossary), here \"capable of any extension.\"","- 0012: \"sich ... zu bequemen\" rendered \"to accommodate itself\"; \"dieselbe\" (the crust or the air-particles) is ambiguous in \"worinn dieselbe ... hinein zu sincken ... veranlaßt wurde\"; I read it as the crust and bracketed that reading, but the German could refer to the particles.","- 0012: \"Schranken\" = limitations (glossary); \"Fähigkeit\" = capacity; \"erkennen zu geben\" = \"give to recognize\" (literal).","- 0012: \"Kraft/Kräfte\" = force(s); \"Gleichgewicht\" = equilibrium; \"Schweere\" = gravity (literal \"heaviness\").","- 0012: \"wenn ich mich so ausdrücken darf\" preserved; the sentence beginning \"welches der Größe...\" is syntactically irregular in the German (the \"sondern\" clause lacks a clear main verb parallel), and I kept it as it stands.","- 0016–0017: \"Erdboden\" = \"earth's ground\"; \"Stuffe\" = step (in 0011 and 0016); \"Loos\" = lot; \"Scheinen\" = seem. \"Gegend\" = region throughout. In 0017 \"ihm\" and \"seinem Auslauffs\" refer to the river and Nile respectively as the German has them."]],"erde-veralte-0018":["This alteration of nature is worthy of a clarification. The rivers did not find, right at the beginning, when the dry was freed from the sea, ready-made tubes and a prepared uniform slope of their course. In many places they still overflowed and made standing waters, which made the land unusable. Little by little they hollowed out channels for themselves in the fresh and soft soil, and with the washed-away mud, with which these were filled, they formed on both sides of their strongest current proper banks, which at low water could contain and confine their stream, but at stronger swelling were gradually raised by the overflowing, until their completely formed flood-beds were put into the state of carrying off to the sea, with a uniform moderate slope, the water which the surrounding lands delivered to them. The highest regions are the first that had to rejoice in this necessary unfolding of nature and were therefore also inhabited first, while the low ones struggled for a time with confusion, and arrived later at perfection. Since then the low lands enrich themselves with the plunder of the high regions. The rivers, which at the time when they swell high are pregnant with the washed-off mud, deposit it in their floods near their outflow, raise the ground over which they spread, and form the dry, which, after the river has increased its banks to the proper height, becomes habitable and, fertilized by the fatness of the high regions, more fruitful than these.",[]],"erde-veralte-0019":["Through this progressing formation and the alteration that the shape of the earth suffers, the deeper regions become habitable, when the heights sometimes cease to be so. Only this change concerns chiefly some countries, namely those that suffer a lack of the water of heaven, and therefore, without the periodic flooding, must do without the necessary moisture and remain an uninhabited desert, when the rivers by their own raising of their banks have set limits to this flooding. Egypt is the most distinct example of this alteration, which has been so much altered in its constitution: that, since the whole land, according to the testimony of Herodotus, 900 years before his time, was entirely flooded when the river had grown only 8 feet, in his time it had to rise 15 feet in order to cover it completely, whereas now in our time 24 feet of growth are already required for it. From which the ruin, ever more threatening this land through a steady approach, is to be seen.",[]],"erde-veralte-0020":["But because this alteration of nature, insofar as it adheres to some parts of the earth's ground alone, is insignificant and slight, the question of the aging of the earth as a whole must be determined, and to that end the causes are first to be examined to which most investigators of nature ascribe this effect, and from which they have considered it sufficient to foretell the decline of the nature of this globe.",[]],"erde-veralte-0021":["The first cause flows from the opinion of those who ascribe the saltiness of the sea to the rivers, which carry with them into the sea the salt leached out of the soil, which the rain brings into their streams, where, with the constant evaporation of the sweet water, it remains behind, accumulates, and in this way has furnished to the sea all the salt that it still holds in itself. It is easily to be gathered from this: that, since salt is the foremost driving-mechanism of growth and the source of fruitfulness, according to this hypothesis the earth, gradually robbed of its force, must have been put into a dead and unfruitful state.",[]],"erde-veralte-0022":["The second cause is to be set in the effect of the rain and the rivers with regard to the washing off of the soil and the carrying away of it into the sea, which thereby seems to be filled up more and more, while the height of the firm land constantly diminishes: so that it is to be feared that the sea, since it is never raised, must finally be compelled to overstep once again the dry, which was formerly withdrawn from its dominion.",[]],"erde-veralte-0023":["The third opinion is the conjecture of those who, in that they become aware that the sea in long times withdraws noticeably from most shores, and that great stretches which formerly lay on the bottom of the sea are transformed into dry land, fear either an actual consumption of this fluid element through a kind of transformation into a firm state, or dread other causes that prevent the rain, which consists of its evaporations, from returning again to where it was raised from.",[]],"erde-veralte-0024":["The fourth and last opinion may be that of those who assume a universal world-spirit, an insensible but everywhere effective *principium* and the secret driving-mechanism of nature, whose subtle matter would be constantly consumed by unceasing generations, whence nature, with its diminution, would stand in danger of becoming old and dying off in a gradual exhaustion.",[]],"erde-veralte-0025":["These opinions are what I will first briefly examine, and then ground the one that seems to me to be the true one.",[]],"erde-veralte-0026":["(The continuation follows.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0027":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0028":["Third continuation of the physical exposition of the question:\nWhether the earth ages.",["- 0018: *Erläuterung* = clarification; *das trockne* / *das trockene* rendered literally \"the dry\" (substantivized adjective, kept).","- 0018: \"which\" in the last clause (*welches*) may refer to *das trockene* or to the whole process; left ambiguous. \"more fruitful than these\" (*als diese*) refers to *Gegenden*.","- 0018: *Fluthbette* rendered \"flood-beds\"; *Schläuche* \"tubes\"; *Vollkommenheit* = perfection; *Zustand* = state.","- 0019: *Beschaffenheit* = constitution; *Erörterung* (0028) = exposition; *Schrancken* = limits (here not the glossary's *Schranke*, \"limitation\", in a technical sense).","- 0019: \"Woraus ... ist zu ersehen\" kept as a clumsy fragment; the participial phrase *durch eine stetige Annäherung* may attach to *drohende* or to *Lande*; left ambiguous. The sentence is syntactically irregular in the German.","- 0021, 0022: *Meinung* = opinion (0023, 0024 *Meynung*, same word); *Wirckung* = effect; *Triebwerck* = driving-mechanism; *Kraft* = force; *Materie* = matter.","- 0022: *daß Meer müste* is as printed (*das Meer*); *indessen daß* rendered \"while\". The sense of *Höhe*/*Herrschaft* is kept literal.","- 0024: *Weltgeist* = world-spirit (*Geist* = spirit); *Principium* is Latin in the original, italicized; *Zeugungen* = generations."]],"erde-veralte-0029":["If the first opinion were correct, it would follow that all the salt with which the waters of the ocean and of all the inland seas are impregnated was formerly mixed with the soil that covers the firm land, and, in that it was washed out of the same by the rain, was carried off thither by the rivers, and is also constantly being brought in in the same way still; but fortunately for the earth, and to the confusion of those who think, by means of such a hypothesis, to make the saltiness of the sea comprehensible through an easy explanation, one finds this conjecture, on close examination, to be unfounded. For presupposing: that the mean quantity of rainwater that falls on the earth in a year is 18 inches high, which is the amount that has been observed in the temperate zone, and that all rivers arise from and are nourished by the rainwater, likewise that of the rain that falls on the firm land only two thirds comes back again into the sea through the rivers, but one third is partly evaporated, partly applied to the growth of the plants, finally: that the sea takes up only half of the surface of the earth, which is the least that one can assume; so one will have placed the cited opinion in the most advantageous conditions, and yet all the streams of the earth will bring into the sea in a year only 1 foot of water, and, if one assumes its mean depth to be even only a hundred fathoms, would yet fill it up only in 600 years, after evaporation had dried it up completely in just as many years. According to this calculation the ocean would by now, through the influx of all the brooks and streams, already have become full ten times since the creation; but the salt that was left behind by these rivers after the evaporation could amount to only ten times as much as that with which it is naturally endowed; from which it would have to follow: that, in order to get out the degree of the saltiness of the sea, one would need to let a cubic foot of river water evaporate only ten times, whereupon its residual salt would amount to just as much as an equal quantity of sea water leaves behind after a single evaporation; which is much too far removed from probability for it to be able to persuade even an ignorant person, since according to Wallerius's calculation the water in the North Sea, in the places where few rivers fall into the sea, contains the tenth, sometimes the seventh, and in the Gulf of Bothnia, where it is very much diluted with sweet flowing water, nevertheless the fortieth part of salt. The earth is therefore on this footing sufficiently secured against losing its salt and fertility through the rain and the rivers. It is rather to be conjectured that the sea, instead of robbing the firm land of its salty parts, rather communicates to it some of its own; for, although the evaporation leaves the coarse salt behind, it nevertheless raises a part of that which has become volatile, which together with the vapors is carried over the firm land, and imparts to the rain that fertility for which this, even before the flowing water, is preeminently suited.",[]],"erde-veralte-0030":["The other opinion has a greater degree of credibility, and agrees much better with itself. Manfred, who treated it in the Commentarii of the Bologna Institute as learnedly as cautiously, and whose exposition is to be found in the Allgemeines Magazin der Natur, may, in the examination of it, speak for it alone. He remarks: that the old floor of the cathedral church at Ravenna, which is found covered with rubble beneath the new one, is 8 inches lower than the level of the sea when it has high tide, and therefore at the time of its construction, if the sea was then not lower than now, would have had to be put under water at every high tide, because the ancient testimonies prove: that the sea at that time went as far as this city. He cites, in confirmation of his opinion that the height of the sea has constantly increased, the floor of the church of St. Mark in Venice, which is now so low: that, when the lagoon has swollen, both the Piazza San Marco is sometimes inundated and the church itself is put under water; whereas it is not to be presumed: that at its construction it was already so. Likewise he appeals to the marble bench that was run around the town hall of St. Mark, presumably for the benefit of seafarers, in order to get on foot into their vessels, which for this purpose has now become almost unserviceable, because at the time of the ordinary high tide it stands half a foot deep under water; so that from the cited marks it is evident, the sea must now have attained a greater height than in former times. To explain this opinion, he asserts: that the rivers drag into the sea the mud with which they are filled at the time of their swelling, and which the rain-brooks have washed off from the heights of the firm land, and thereby raise its bottom, whereby it is compelled to rise, in the measure as its bed is gradually filled up. In order to make the measure of this rise of the sea agree with that which the actual marks put to hand, he sought to estimate the quantity of mud that the streams carry with them when they flow turbid, in that toward the end of February he drew up the water of the stream that flows by Bologna, and, after he had let the earth settle, found it ⅟1 7 4 of the water that had held it. From this, and from the amount of water that the streams carry into the sea in a year, he determined the height to which the sea should gradually rise through this cause, so that in 348 years it would have to be found 5 inches higher.",[]],"erde-veralte-0031":["(The continuation follows.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0032":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0033":["Fourth continuation of the physical exposition of the question:\nWhether the earth is aging.",[]],"erde-veralte-0034":["Through the consideration that we cited concerning the marble bench around the St. Mark town hall at Venice, and through the desire to have a measure by which to determine thereby the magnitude of his remaining remarks, Manfred was induced to increase the aforementioned rise of the surface of the sea so far that it would amount to a foot in 230 years, because, as he asserts, the rivers, besides the delicate earth that makes their waters turbid, drag into the sea much sand, stones, and the like. On this footing the misfortune of the earth would draw near with rather rapid steps, although he nevertheless deals more cautiously with it than Hartsoeker, who from a similar observation on the Rhine announces to the earth the fate that within 10000 years its habitable part must be washed away, the sea cover everything, and nothing but the bare rocks project from it; from which one can easily make the calculation of the degree of decay in a somewhat lesser time, e.g. of 2000 years.\n\n---notes\n- *Meinung* = \"opinion\" throughout; *Erklärung* in 0029 (\"Erklärung\") = \"explanation\"; *begreiflich* rendered \"comprehensible\" (cognate of *begreifen*, \"comprehend\").\n- *Merckmal* (0030) = \"mark\" (*nota*), as in the glossary.\n- *Beobachtung* = \"observation\"; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration\"; *Erörterung* (0033) = \"exposition\" (*expositio*).\n- *Schu* / *Schuh* = \"foot\"; *Fuß* in \"auf diesen Fuß\" = \"footing\", but *Fuß* in \"ein Fuß\" = \"foot\"; *Fußboden* = \"floor\".\n- *Zoll*, *Klafter*, *Qvantität*/*Quantität* rendered \"inch\", \"fathom\", \"quantity\"; *Menge* = \"amount\" (0029) and \"amount\" (0030).\n- *Ausdunstung* / *Ausdünstung* / *Abdünstung* all rendered \"evaporation\"; *verdunstet* = \"evaporated\".\n- 0030: \"⅟1 7 4\" is the OCR as it stands and is doubtful (perhaps 1/174); left as printed. \"Manfred\" and \"Hartsöcker\" are given in standard modern spellings (Manfredi?, Hartsoeker) only for Hartsoeker; \"Manfred\" kept as in the text. The gap after \"Manfred ,\" and \"Hartsöcker ,\" reflects original spacing (probably letterspaced names).\n- 0029: *vor* in \"vor die Erde\" and \"vor dem Fließwasser\" rendered \"for\" and \"before\", following the older usage (= *für*, *vor*); \"zum Wiederspiel\" rendered literally-ish as \"to the confusion of\" (doubtful). *Warscheinlichkeit* = \"probability\".\n- 0031, 0032: no apparatus; 0034 begins mid-series (\"Vierte Fortsetzung\") with *Fuß* ambiguity as noted above.",[]],"erde-veralte-0035":["The true error of this opinion consists only in the more or less; otherwise it is at bottom correct. It is the case that the rain and the rivers wash away the soil, and carry it into the sea; but it is far from the case that they should do so to so great a degree as the author supposes. He assumed arbitrarily that the streams flow throughout the whole year as turbid as they do on those days when the snow thawing from the mountains causes the violent torrents, which have the full force to attack the soil, and when the soil itself has become completely soaked through and, by the preceding winter cold, crumbly enough to be washed away as easily as possible. If he had combined this caution at the same time with the attention that he ought to have had to the difference of the rivers, of which those that are fed by mountains, because of the force of the torrents which pour into them, hold within themselves more stolen earth than others that are nourished by the flat land, then his calculation would have been reduced so greatly that he would presumably have let the plan drop, to ground the explanation of the observed changes upon it. If one finally also considers here: that the sea, through this very motion, on account of which it is credited with tolerating nothing dead in itself, namely through the constant carrying off to the shores of all matter that does not have an equal degree of mobility, does not let this mud heap up on its ground, but deposits it without delay on the firm land and increases it thereby: then the fear of seeing the bladder of the sea filled up with it would have been transformed into a well-founded hope of constantly acquiring new land by the plunder of the high regions on the sea shores; For in fact, in all gulfs, e.g. in that which bears the name of the Red Sea, likewise in the Venetian Gulf, the sea withdraws gradually from the tip, and the dry land constantly makes new acquisitions on the realm of Neptune; whereas, if the conjecture of the mentioned natural investigator were well-founded, the water would spread ever further over the shores and bury the dry soil beneath the wet element.",[]],"erde-veralte-0036":["But as for what concerns the cause of the lowering of the regions on the shore of the Adriatic Sea, I would (provided it is really correct that it has not always been so,) on that account rather turn to a constitution of the land that Italy has in particular before many others. For we know: that the foundation of this land is vaulted underneath, and that the earthquakes, although they rage chiefly in lower Italy, nevertheless also let out their force in the upper, and by their extension into wide regions, indeed even beneath the seas, give to be cognized the interconnected subterranean cavities. If now the shaking of the subterranean conflagrations is capable of moving the foundation of the same and has already often moved it, is it not to be presumed that the crust, after many violent assaults, has to some extent sunk, and could have become lower with regard to the surface of the sea?",[]],"erde-veralte-0037":["The third opinion, which regards the increase of the dry land and the reduction of the waters on the globe as a harbinger of its ruin, has just as well apparent grounds from observation as the previous one, but a less comprehensible cause to explain it. For it is certain that, although it might seem that the sea, when it gradually lets the firm land go dry on one side, takes possession again in return of other regions into which it works its way, and holds itself harmless on the whole, nevertheless, if one weighs it exactly, far greater stretches are laid bare by the sea than those over which it spreads. Chiefly the sea leaves the low regions and gnaws at the high shores, because these are chiefly exposed to its assault and the former thwart it through a gentle slope. This alone could furnish a proof: that the surface of the sea does not rise more and more in general; for one would trace the difference most distinctly on the shores where the land with slight descent gradually lowers to the floor of the sea; there 10 feet of elevation of the water would win much from the firm land, whereas the matter stands quite the contrary, and, in that the sea no longer reaches those dams that it formerly threw up and over which it undoubtedly then passed, proves that it has since become lower; as e.g. the 2 Prussian Nehrungen, the dunes on the Dutch and English coasts, are nothing other than sand hills that the sea formerly drove up, but which now serve as bulwarks against the same, after it no longer reaches the height to surmount them.",[]],"erde-veralte-0038":["But should one, in order to leave this phenomenon in its full validity, take refuge in an actual disappearance of the fluid element and transformation of the same into a firm state, or in a seeping away of the rainwater into the interior of the earth, or in a constantly increasing deepening of the bed of the sea, through its unceasing motion. The first ground would presumably have the smallest share in a noticeable change, although it does not conflict so much, as it seems, with a sound natural science. For, just as other fluid matters sometimes assume a firm standing without nevertheless losing their essence, e.g. quicksilver, which in the experiments of Boerhaave assumes the shape of a red powder, the air, which Hales found as a firm body in all vegetable products, chiefly in tartar, so without doubt water likewise does this, whose parts in the formation of plants seem to lay aside their fluidity, so that the most thoroughly dried-out, pulverized wood on chemical dissolution nevertheless always gives off water, from which it becomes not improbable: that a part of the waters of the globe is used up in the formation of growths and never returns to the sea. But at least this decrease cannot become noticeable. The second ground can likewise not be denied in the absolute sense. The rainwater that the earth draws into itself sinks, to be sure, in it chiefly only so deep until it finds somewhat denser layers that do not let it through and compel it to seek an outlet along the slope of the same and to feed springs. But something of it will at all times seep down through all the layers as far as the rocky ones and penetrate also in these through their crevices and collect those subterranean waters which on occasion of some earthquakes have at times broken forth and flooded lands.* This loss of sea water could perhaps be not inconsiderable and deserved to be weighed more exactly. But the third ground seems to have the greatest and most undisputed share in the diminished height of the sea, which must always decrease the deeper it works out its bed, although in this way not the least step toward the ruin of the earth is to be feared.",[]],"erde-veralte-0039":["* See the physical treatises of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris, Steinwehr's translation, 2nd volume, p. 246.",[]],"erde-veralte-0040":["(The conclusion follows in the future.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0041":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0042":["Conclusion of the physical exposition of the question:\nWhether the earth is aging.",["- 0035: \"Meinung\" = opinion; \"Grund\" = ground; \"Erklärung\" = explanation; \"Materie\" = matter; \"Rechnung\"/\"Anschlag\" rendered \"calculation\"/\"plan\". \"Erdreich\" and \"Erde\" both \"soil\"/\"earth\" as context fits; \"Land\" = land.","- 0035: \"Schlauch des Meeres\" rendered literally as \"bladder of the sea\" (also \"skin, hose\"); \"Gewäßer\" = waters.","- 0035: \"Naturforscher\" = \"natural investigator\"; \"geraubte Erde\" kept literal \"stolen earth\".","- 0035: \"Hofnung\", \"Vermuthung\" = conjecture; \"vermuthet/vermuthlich\" = supposes/presumably.","- 0036: \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution; \"erkennen zu geben\" rendered \"give to be cognized\"; \"Grundfeste\" = foundation; \"Rinde\" = crust.","- 0037: \"Beweis\" = proof; \"Gültigkeit\" (0038) = validity; \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"begreiflich\" = comprehensible; \"Nehrungen\" left untranslated (spits of land).","- 0038: \"Wesen\" = essence (the glossary also gives \"being\"); \"Naturwißenschaft\" = natural science; \"Verstande\" in \"in absolutem Verstande\" rendered \"in the absolute sense\"; \"Grund\" appears as \"ground\" throughout.","- 0038: \"Producktis\" is Latinized ablative-like German form, rendered \"products\"; \"Erörterung\" (0042) = exposition. Original has a sentence beginning \"Soll man aber...\" that ends with a period, kept as is (reads as a question without a question mark)."]],"erde-veralte-0043":["What, then, is the result of the examination that has been undertaken of the opinions presented so far? We have decided the first three in the negative. The soil does not lose any saltiness through the washing away of the rain and of the brooks; the fat earth is not dragged by the rivers into the sea with irreplaceable loss, in order finally to fill it up and to raise its waters once again over the inhabited land. They do in fact carry to it the plunder of the high regions; but the latter makes use of it in order to deposit it again on the shores of the firm land, and the maintenance and formation of the vegetables costs the sea a real expenditure of evaporated water, a notable part of which seems to lay aside the fluid state and to keep the soil indemnified for its loss. Finally, the conjecture about the real decrease of the waters of the ocean, notwithstanding its probability, still does not have well enough grounded reliability to occasion a decisive pronouncement in a secure hypothesis. There remains, therefore, with regard to the alteration of the shape of the earth, a single cause upon which one can reckon with certainty, which consists in this: that the rain and the brooks, in that they constantly attack the soil and wash it away from the high regions into the lower ones, strive gradually to make the heights level and, as much as lies in them, to rob the shape of the earth of its unevennesses. This effect is certain and reliable. The soil is also subject to this alteration uninterruptedly for as long as there are on the slope of the high parts materials which can be attacked by the rainwater and washed away, and it will not be free of it sooner than when, after the loose layers have been washed away, the rocky foundations of the same will make up the only heights, which suffer no alteration any more. This alteration is the worrisome cause of its impending ruin not only because of the displacement of the layers, of which the most fruitful are sunk and buried beneath the dead ones, but rather because of the cancellation of the useful division of the firm land into valleys and heights. If one looks at the present arrangement of the firm land, one becomes aware with admiration of a regular relation of the elevated regions to the deep ones: that the soil in wide stretches inclines with a moderate slope toward the channel of a river, which occupies the greatest depth of the valley, and along whose extension has an even, continuing declivity down to the sea, into which the latter empties its water. This well-ordered constitution, which frees the firm land from the superfluity of rainwater, rests very much on the degree of its magnitude, so that neither an all too great descent would carry off too quickly the water that is to be applied to fertility, nor an all too slight declivity let it rest on it too long and accumulate to its detriment. But this advantageous determination suffers constant diminution through the perpetual effect of the rain; in that the latter reduces the heights, and, by leading the torn-off materials into the low regions, gradually brings the shape of the earth closer to the property it would have if all the inequalities of the surface had vanished, and the water accumulating without drainage, which the rain carries over the earth's surface, would soak through its lap and destroy the habitable constitution. I have already remarked: that the completion of the aging of the earth, although in long times it can scarcely become noticeable, is nevertheless a well-grounded and knowledge-worthy object of philosophical consideration, in which the slight is no longer slight or worthless, which through unceasing summations constantly brings an important alteration nearer, and in which ruin needs nothing other than time in order to become complete. Meanwhile one cannot say: that the steps toward this alteration would not be noticeable at all. If the heights constantly decrease, the supply of water into the lower regions, which maintains lakes or also streams, will be constantly diminished. These will carry with them, in the decrease of their magnitude, the testimonies of such alteration. In fact one will find on all lakes marks: that they formerly extended farther. The high part of Prussia is a veritable land full of lakes. One will not easily see one of them beside which one should not become aware of large adjoining plains, so level with the water that one cannot doubt they formerly also belonged to the lake and were only gradually left dry, after it drew farther back, because its waters gradually diminished. To cite an example: according to reliable testimonies the Drausen Lake formerly reached to the town of Preussisch-Holland and gave occasion for navigation there; it has now withdrawn a mile from it, but still distinctly marks its former bed through a long plain which is almost level with the water, and whose former raised banks are seen on both sides. This gradual alteration is therefore, so to speak, a part of a progressing proportion, whose last term stands almost infinitely far from the beginning and perhaps will never be reached, because the revelation of the earth that we inhabit foretells a sudden fate, whose execution is to interrupt its duration in the midst of prosperity and not leave it time to age through imperceptible steps of alteration, and, so to speak, to suffer a natural death.",["- *Gegend* (regions), *Erdreich* (soil) and *Erde/Erden* (earth) are kept distinct; *festes Land* = \"firm land.\"","- *Veränderung* = \"alteration\"; *Wirkung* = \"effect\"; *Beschaffenheit* = \"property\" (glossary); *Verfassung* = \"constitution\" (also used for *Beschaffenheit*, a single English word for two German words).","- *Bestimmung* rendered \"determination\" per the glossary.","- *Vegetabilien* rendered literally as \"vegetables\" (plants).","- \"Der Raub... demselben\": \"it\" in \"carry to it\" (*demselben*) and \"the latter\" (*dieses*) refer ambiguously; the antecedents (sea/earth) are left as in the German.","- \"und nach dessen Erstreckung eine ... Abschießigkeit ... hat\": the subject of \"has\" is left unsupplied as in the German (the soil, or the river); the sentence is ambiguous.","- \"Verhältniß\" is neuter in the text (\"ein ... Verhältniß, dessen\"); rendered \"proportion.\"","- Spellings \"Warscheinlichkeit,\" \"wircklichen,\" \"Schooß,\" \"gringe\" reproduce the original; \"Zuschuß\" = \"supply\"; \"Ausspruch\" = \"pronouncement\"; \"Offenbarung\" = \"revelation.\""]],"erde-veralte-0044":["In the meantime I still owe, among the various opinions that can be raised about the aging of the earth, the judgment of the fourth: whether the ever active force, which to a certain extent makes up the life of nature, and which, although it does not fall visibly into the eyes, is nevertheless busy in all generations and in the economy of all three realms of nature, may not gradually exhaust itself and thereby cause the aging of nature. Those who in this sense assume a universal world-spirit understand by it no immaterial force, no soul of the world or plastic natures, the creatures of the bold imagination, but a subtle, yet everywhere active matter, which constitutes the active *principium* in the formations of nature, and, as a true Proteus, is ready to assume all shapes and forms. Such a representation is not so much opposed to a sound natural science and to observation as one might well think. If one considers: that nature in the plant realm has laid the most powerful and spirituous part in a certain oil, whose viscosity fixes its volatility, and whose deprivation, either by evaporation or by chemical devices, causes no noticeable loss of weight, even though what is left behind is then nothing but a dead mass; if one considers this *spiritus rector*, as the chemists call it, this fifth essence, which constitutes the specific distinguishing mark of every plant, how it is generated everywhere equally easily from one and the same nutriment, namely from pure water and air; if one considers the so celebrated volatile acid, which is spread everywhere in the air, which constitutes the active *principium* in most kinds of salts, the essential part of sulphur, and the foremost in the combustible of fire, whose forces of attraction and repulsion reveal themselves so distinctly in electricity, which is so skilled at overcoming the elastic force of the air and at occasioning formations; if one considers this Proteus of nature, then one is moved to presume, with probability, an everywhere effective subtle matter, a so-called world-spirit, but also to fear: that the incessant generations perhaps consume ever more of it than the destruction of the formations of nature gives back, and that nature perhaps, through the expenditure of them, constantly loses something of its force.",["- Meinung = opinion; Beurteilung = judgment (estimation); Kraft = force; Vorstellung = representation; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Wissenschaft in *Naturwissenschaft* = science (natural science).","- *Verstande* in \"in diesem Verstande\" rendered \"in this sense\"; not in the glossary (it is not Verstand as faculty).","- \"Geist\" in *Weltgeist* = \"world-spirit\"; \"geistigen\" = \"spirituous\" (chemical sense).","- \"Materie\" = matter; \"Gestalten und Formen\" = \"shapes and forms\" (Form = form; Gestalt = shape).","- \"welches so geschickt ist\" (after *Elektricität*): the antecedent is ambiguous in the German (the acid, or electricity); left ambiguous in English (\"which\").","- \"derselben\" at the end (plural) most naturally refers to the *Zeugungen*; \"von demselben\" earlier refers to the matter/world-spirit. Kept as in German.","- *Principium*, *Spiritus Rector* are in Latin type in the original; italicized.","- \"Oekonomie\", \"Chymici\" rendered \"economy\", \"chemists\"."]],"erde-veralte-0045":["When I compare the drive of the ancient peoples toward great things, the enthusiasm of the desire for honor, of virtue and of the love of freedom, which inspired them with lofty concepts and raised them above themselves, with the moderate and cold-minded constitution of our times: I do indeed find cause to congratulate our centuries on such a change, which is equally profitable to the doctrine of morals as to the sciences, but I fall nevertheless into temptation to presume: that perhaps this may be a mark of a certain cooling of that fire which animated human nature, and whose vehemence was just as fruitful in excesses as in beautiful effects. If, on the other hand, I take into consideration how great an influence the manner of government, instruction and example have on the disposition of mind and on morals, then I doubt whether such ambiguous marks can furnish proofs of an actual alteration of nature.",["- Begriffe = concepts; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Sittenlehre = doctrine of morals; Wissenschaften = sciences; Wirkungen = effects; Merkmale = marks (*nota*); Beweisthümer = proofs (Beweis).","- \"dieses Merkmale\" (singular/neuter form) rendered \"a mark\", as it stands; the plural \"Merkmale\" later is \"marks\".","- Gemüthsverfassung = \"disposition of mind\" (Gemüt = mind); Regierungsart = \"manner of government\" (Art = manner).","- \"wirklichen\" = \"actual\" (Wirklichkeit = actuality)."]],"erde-veralte-0046":["I have accordingly treated the question raised about the aging of the earth not decisively, as the enterprising spirit of a bold investigator of nature would demand, but examiningly, as the constitution of the subject itself brings with it. I have sought to determine more correctly the concept one has to make of this alteration. There may yet be other causes that could bring about its downfall through a sudden overturning of the earth. For, without mentioning the comets, which for some time now people have known how to make convenient use of for all extraordinary fates, there seems to be hidden in the interior of the earth itself the realm of Vulcan and a great store of kindled and fiery matter, which perhaps under the uppermost crust gains the upper hand more and more, heaps up the treasures of fire and gnaws at the foundation of the uppermost vaults, whose collapse, perhaps decreed, could lead the flaming element over the surface and cause its downfall in fire. But such contingencies belong just as little to the question of the aging of the earth as, in considering by what ways a building ages, one has to take earthquakes or conflagrations into consideration.",["- Geist = spirit; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Begriff = concept; bestimmen = determine; Betrachtung = consideration; Zufälle = contingencies (zufällig = contingent).","- *Vorwurf* (\"subject\") and *prüfend* (\"examiningly\") are literal; *Untergang* = downfall.","- \"ihren Untergang\" (twice): \"its\" in English; the German \"ihren\" is plural/feminine and its referent is unclear in the sentence (earth, as feminine singular, is the likely antecedent); left as it stands.","- \"deren etwa verhängter Einstürtz\": \"deren\" refers to the vaults (Gewölber); *etwa verhängter* = \"perhaps decreed\" (also possible: \"perhaps imposed/allowed\")."]],"erde-veralte-0047":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0001":["General\nNatural History\nand\nTheory of the Heavens,\nor\nEssay\non the Constitution and the Mechanical Origin\nof the Whole Structure of the World\ntreated\naccording to\nNewtonian Principles.\nKönigsberg and Leipzig,\nat Johann Friederich Petersen's, 1755.",["- Verfassung = \"constitution\" (also Beschaffenheit = constitution; one English word for two German words); Grundsätze = principles; Weltgebäude = \"structure of the world\" (Welt = world).","- *Abhandeln* = \"treat\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0002":["To the\nMost Serene\nMost Mighty King\nand Lord\nLord\nFriederich,\nKing of Prussia,\nMargrave of Brandenburg, of the H.\nR. Empire Arch-Chamberlain and Elector,\nSovereign and Supreme Duke\nof Silesia, etc. etc. etc.\nTo My\nMost Gracious King\nand Lord\nMost Serene,\nMost Mighty King,\nMost Gracious\nKing and Lord!",["- \"H. R. Reichs\" = \"H. R. Empire\" (abbreviation for Holy Roman Empire, kept as abbreviated in the German).","- \"Churfürsten\" = \"Elector\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0003":["The sensation of my own unworthiness and the splendor of the throne cannot make my timidity so fainthearted as the grace, which the most benevolent monarch spreads with equal magnanimity over all his subjects, inspires hope in me: that the boldness which I undertake will not be looked upon with ungracious eyes. I hereby lay, in most submissive reverence, one of the slightest proofs of that zeal at the feet of Your Royal Majesty, with which Your Highest academies, through the encouragement and protection of their enlightened sovereign, are driven to emulation of other nations in the sciences. How happy I would be, if it should succeed for the present essay to win the most supreme pleasure of his monarch for the efforts with which the lowest and most reverent subject is ceaselessly striving to make himself in some measure useful to the benefit of his fatherland. I expire in deepest devotion",["- Empfindung = sensation; Wissenschaften = sciences; Proben = \"proofs\" (literal; Beweis is also \"proof\").","- \"Höchst Dero Akademien\": \"Your Highest academies\" is literal; \"Dero\" is an honorific.","- \"so kleinmüthig machen, als die Gnade ... mir Hoffnung einflöset\": comparison construction kept; the syntax is ambiguous between \"as the grace inspires hope [as it makes me timid]\" and the reading given.","- \"seines Monarchen\" (third person after the second-person address) kept as it stands.","- \"Ich ersterbe\" = \"I expire\" (a conventional formula)."]],"naturgeschichte-0004":["Your Royal Majesty's\nKönigsberg, the 14th of March, 1755.\nmost submissive servant,\nthe Author.",["- \"Merz\" = March (OCR/old spelling \"Merz\").","- \"Knecht\" = \"servant\" (literally also \"bondsman\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0005":["Preface.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0006":["I have chosen a subject which, as much on the side of its inner difficulty as also in regard to religion, is capable of taking up a great part of the readers from the outset with a disadvantageous prejudice. To discover the systematic, which combines the great members of creation in the whole domain of infinity, to derive the formation of the world-bodies themselves and the origin of their motions from the first state of nature through mechanical laws: such insights seem to exceed very far the powers of human reason. On the other side religion threatens with a solemn accusation of the audacity, since one dares to venture to ascribe to nature left to itself such consequences, in which one with right becomes aware of the immediate hand of the highest being, and fears to encounter in the presumption of such considerations a defense speech of the atheist. I see all these difficulties well and yet do not become fainthearted. I feel the whole strength of the obstacles that oppose themselves, and yet do not despair. On a slight conjecture I have ventured a dangerous journey, and already catch sight of the headlands of new lands. Those who have the courage to continue the investigation will set foot on them and will have the pleasure of designating them with their name.",["- Vorwurf = \"subject\"; Umfang = domain; Zustand = state; Gesetze = laws; Kräfte = \"powers\" (Kraft = force; power; here the plural is rendered \"powers\"); Vernunft = reason; Wesen = being (*das höchste Wesen* = \"the highest being\"); Betrachtungen = considerations; Folgen = \"consequences\" (Folge = sequel; sequence; Folgerung = consequence, so one English word for two German words).","- *Das Systematische* = \"the systematic\" (substantivized adjective); *Weltkörper* = \"world-bodies\".","- *Vorwitz* = \"presumption\" (also \"curiosity, forwardness\"); *Schutzrede des Gottesleugners* = \"a defense speech of the atheist\" (literal).","- \"darin man mit Recht\": \"darin\" taken as \"in which\", antecedent \"Folgen\".","- \"Einsichten\" = \"insights\"; \"Vermuthung\" = \"conjecture\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0007":["I did not form the design for this undertaking until I saw myself secure with regard to the duties of religion. My zeal was doubled when, at every step, I saw the mists disperse which seemed to conceal monsters behind their darkness, and after whose dispersal the glory of the highest being broke forth with the liveliest radiance. Since I know these efforts to be free of all culpability, I will faithfully set out what well-disposed or also weak minds can find offensive in my plan, and am ready to submit it to the severity of the orthodox Areopagus with a frankness that is the mark of an honest disposition. The advocate of faith may accordingly be the first to let his grounds be heard.",["- Glaube = faith (glossary gives \"belief; faith\"); Wesen = being; Gemüther = minds; Merkmaal = mark (*nota*).","- \"Anschlag auf diese Unternehmung\": \"Anschlag\" rendered \"design\"; not in glossary. \"Gesinnung\" rendered \"disposition\".","- \"Gründe\" = grounds (Grund = ground; basis)."]],"naturgeschichte-0008":["If the structure of the world, with all its order and beauty, is only an effect of matter left to its universal laws of motion, if the blind mechanics of the forces of nature knows how to develop itself so splendidly out of chaos and arrives by itself at such perfection; then the proof of the divine author, which one draws from the sight of the beauty of the edifice of the world, is entirely deprived of force, nature is sufficient to itself, divine governance is unnecessary, Epicurus lives again in the midst of Christendom, and an unholy philosophy tramples underfoot the faith which holds out to it a bright light to illuminate it.",["- Wirkung = effect; Materie = matter; Beweis = proof; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Weltweisheit = philosophy; Glaube = faith; Bewegungsgesetze = laws of motion; Kräfte = forces.","- Weltbau and Weltgebäude both rendered by \"structure of the world\"/\"edifice of the world\" (Bau/Gebäude distinguished).","- \"ihr\" in the last clause (the light to illuminate \"it\"): grammatically refers to Weltweisheit; kept as \"it.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0009":["If I found this reproach well founded, the conviction that I have of the infallibility of divine truths is in me so powerful that I would hold everything that contradicts them as sufficiently refuted by them and would reject it. But precisely the agreement that I encounter between my system and religion raises my confidence with regard to all difficulties to an undaunted composure.",["- Ueberzeugung = conviction; \"vor gnugsam widerlegt halten\" = \"hold as sufficiently refuted\" (vor = für). \"durch sie\" (\"by them\") could attach to \"halten\" or \"widerlegt\"; left ambiguous.","- Uebereinstimmung = agreement; Zuversicht = confidence.","- Mood in \"so ist … bey mir so vermögend\" (indicative after \"Wenn … fände\") kept as in German."]],"naturgeschichte-0010":["I recognize the whole worth of those proofs which one draws from the beauty and perfect arrangement of the structure of the world for the confirmation of a most wise author. If one does not wantonly resist all conviction, one must yield oneself won over to such irrefutable grounds. But I assert: that the defenders of religion, by making use of these grounds in a poor way, perpetuate the dispute with the naturalists, in that they needlessly offer them a weak side.",["- erkennen = cognize/recognize (*cognoscere*); here \"recognize\". behaupten = assert; Beweise = proofs; Gründe = grounds; Ueberzeugung = conviction.","- \"gewonnen geben\" rendered literally; idiom (\"yield, admit defeat\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0011":["One is accustomed to noticing and extolling in nature the agreements, the beauty, the ends, and a perfect relation of the means to them. But while one elevates nature from this side, one seeks on the other side to diminish it again. This harmony, one says, is alien to it; left to its universal laws it would bring about nothing but disorder. The agreements show an alien hand, which has known how to force a matter abandoned by all regularity into a wise plan. But I answer: if the universal laws of effect of matter are likewise a sequel from the highest design, then presumably they can have no other determinations than those which strive of themselves to fulfill the plan which the highest wisdom has set before itself; or if this is not so, should one not fall into temptation to believe that at least matter and its universal laws were independent, and that the most wise power, which has known how to make such praiseworthy use of them, is indeed great, but yet not infinite, indeed mighty, but yet not all-sufficient?",["- Zwecke = ends; Mittel = means; Folge = sequel; Bestimmungen = determinations; Wirkungsgesetze = laws of effect; Materie = matter.","- \"Wohlgereimtheit\" = \"harmony\" (literally well-rhymedness); \"Uebereinstimmungen\" = \"agreements\".","- \"solte … gerathen\" is subjunctive/interrogative; \"wären\", \"sey\" kept subjunctive in sense.","- \"allgenugsam\" = \"all-sufficient\"; \"zureichend\" would be \"sufficient\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0012":["The defender of religion is concerned that those agreements which let themselves be explained from a natural tendency of matter might prove the independence of nature from divine providence. He admits not indistinctly that if one can discover natural grounds for all the order of the structure of the world, which can bring it about from the most universal and essential properties of matter, then it is unnecessary to appeal to a supreme governance. The naturalist finds his account in not contesting this presupposition. But he hunts up examples that prove the fruitfulness of the universal laws of nature in perfectly beautiful sequels and brings the orthodox into danger through such grounds, which in his hands could become unconquerable weapons. I will adduce examples. It has already been cited many times as one of the most distinct proofs of a benevolent providence that watches over human beings: that in the hottest zone of the earth the sea winds, precisely at such a time as the heated land most needs its cooling, sweep over the land, as if called, and refresh it. E.g., on the island of Jamaica, as soon as the sun has come so high that it casts the most sensible heat upon the land, right after 9 o'clock in the forenoon, a wind begins to rise out of the sea, which blows over the land from all sides; its strength increases in proportion as the height of the sun increases. At 1 o'clock in the afternoon, when it is naturally hottest, it is strongest, and lets up again gradually with the lowering of the sun, so that toward evening the same stillness prevails as at its rising. Without this welcome arrangement this island would be uninhabitable. All the coasts of the lands that lie in the hot zone enjoy this same benefaction. It is also most necessary to them, because, since they are the lowest regions of the dry land, they also suffer the greatest heat; for the regions situated higher in the land, to which this sea wind does not reach, have less need of it, because their higher situation puts them in a cooler region of air. Is not all this beautiful, are these not visible ends, which have been effected through prudently applied means? But on the contrary the naturalist must find the natural causes of it in the most universal properties of air, without being permitted to suppose particular arrangements on that account. He remarks with right that these sea winds must produce such periodic motions even if no human being lived on such an island, and indeed through no other property than that of the air which, even without regard to this end, is indispensably needed merely for the growth of plants, namely through its elasticity and weight. The heat of the sun cancels the equilibrium of the air, in that it rarefies that which is over the land, and thereby causes the cooler sea air to lift it from its position and take its place.",["- Vorsehung = providence; Regierung = governance; Eigenschaften = properties; Beyspiele = examples; Zwecke = ends; Mittel = means; Absicht = aim (\"without regard to\" renders \"ohne Absicht auf\").","- \"Folgen\" = sequels; \"Fruchtbarkeit\" = fruitfulness; \"Voraussetzung\" = presupposition.","- \"dessen Händen\" is \"in dessen Händen\", \"in whose hands\" in sense; I rendered \"in his hands\" (dessen = the naturalist's).","- \"zum Wiederspiel\" = \"on the contrary\" (idiomatic); \"Z. E.\" = \"E.g.\"","- \"sie\" in the final clause (to lift it): \"sie\" refers to the air over the land (feminine \"Luft\"); \"ihren Platz\" = its place."]],"naturgeschichte-0013":["What use do the winds not have in general for the benefit of the globe, and what a use does not the sagacity of human beings make of them; yet no other arrangements were necessary to produce them than the same universal constitution of air and warmth, which also, without regard to these ends, had to be found on the earth.",["- Beschaffenheit = constitution; Zwecke = ends; Einrichtungen = arrangements; Gebrauch = use; Nutzen = use (also rendered \"use\"; Nutzen/Gebrauch both \"use\").","- \"unangesehen\" = \"without regard to\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0014":["\"Grant it to me,\" says the freethinker here, \"that if one can derive useful arrangements aimed at ends from the most universal and simplest laws of nature, one has no need of a particular governance of a supreme wisdom: so see here proofs which will catch you at your own confession. The whole of nature, especially the unorganized, is full of such proofs, which give to be cognized that matter determining itself through the mechanics of its forces has a certain correctness in its sequels and does what the rules of propriety require unforced enough. If a well-disposed man wishes to rescue the good cause of religion and contest this capacity of the universal laws of nature, he will put himself in embarrassment and give unbelief occasion to triumph through a poor defense.\"",["- Original begins \"Gebt ihr es … zu\" with no quotation marks; the quotation marks are added to mark the freethinker's speech after \"sagt allhier der Freygeist\".","- Fähigkeit = capacity (*capacitas*); Verfassungen = arrangements; Mechanick = mechanics; Bestimmen = determine; Folgen = sequels; \"erkennen\" = cognize.","- \"Gebt ihr es … zu\": \"ihr\" plural address; \"Wohlanständigkeit\" = propriety; \"ungezwungen genug\" = \"unforced enough\", kept awkward.","- \"Verfassungen\" and \"Einrichtungen\" both rendered \"arrangements\" (Einrichtung in 0012–0013)."]],"naturgeschichte-0015":["But let us see how these grounds, which one fears as harmful in the hands of the opponents, are rather powerful weapons for combating them. Matter determining itself according to its most general laws produces, through its natural conduct, or if one wants to call it so through a blind mechanics, fitting sequels that seem to be the design of a highest wisdom. Air, water, heat, if one considers them left to themselves, generate winds and clouds, rain, streams, which moisten the lands, and all the useful sequels without which nature would have to remain sad, desolate and unfruitful. But they do not produce these sequels through a mere haphazard, or through a contingency that could just as easily have turned out detrimental, rather one sees: that they are restricted by their natural laws to effect in no other than this way. What, then, is one to think of this agreement. How would it indeed be possible that things of different natures in combination with one another should strive to effect such excellent agreements and beauties, even toward ends of such things as find themselves in a certain way outside the domain of dead matter, namely toward the use of humans and animals, if they did not recognize a common origin, namely an infinite understanding, in which the essential properties of all things were designed in relation to one another. If their natures were necessary in themselves and independently, what an astonishing haphazard, or rather what an impossibility would it not be, that with their natural strivings they should fit together exactly as a deliberate, clever choice could have united them.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0016":["Now I confidently make the application to my present undertaking. I assume the matter of all the world in a general dispersion and make out of it a perfect chaos. I see, according to the established laws of attraction, the material form itself and, through repulsion, modify its motion. I enjoy the pleasure, without the assistance of arbitrary fictions, under the occasion of established laws of motion, of seeing a well-ordered whole generate itself, which looks so similar to that world-system which we have before our eyes that I cannot refrain from taking it for the same. This unexpected unfolding of the order of nature on the large scale at first becomes suspect to me, since it grounds so composite a correctness on such poor and simple a ground. I finally teach myself from the consideration indicated beforehand: that such an unfolding of nature is not something unheard of in it, but that its essential striving necessarily brings it with it, and that this is the most glorious testimony to its dependence on that original being which has in itself even the source of the beings themselves and of their first laws of effect. This insight doubles my confidence in the design that I have made. The assurance increases at every step that I set further with progress, and my faintheartedness ceases entirely.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0017":["But the defense of your system, one will say, is at the same time the defense of the opinions of Epicurus, which have the greatest similarity with it. I do not wish to disavow entirely all agreement with him. Many have become atheists through the illusion of such grounds, which on closer consideration could most powerfully have convinced them of the certainty of the highest being. The consequences that a perverse understanding draws from irreproachable principles are often very reprehensible, and so too were the inferences of Epicurus, notwithstanding that his design was in keeping with the acuteness of a great mind.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0018":["I will therefore not deny that the theory of Lucretius, or of his predecessors of Epicurus, Leucippus, and Democritus, has much similarity with my own. I posit the first state of nature, like those philosophers, in the general dispersion of the primitive material of all world-bodies, or of the atoms, as they are called by them. Epicurus posited a gravity that drove these elementary particles to sinking, and this seems not to be very different from the Newtonian attraction which I assume; he also gave them a certain deviation from the rectilinear motion of falling, although with regard to the cause of it and its sequels he had absurd imaginings: this deviation agrees to some extent with the alteration of the rectilinear sinking which we derived from the repulsive force of the particles; finally, the vortices that arose from the confused motion of the atoms were a principal piece in the doctrinal concept of Leucippus and Democritus and one will also encounter them in ours. So much kinship with a doctrinal constitution that was the true theory of the denial of God in antiquity nevertheless does not draw mine into the community of its errors. Even in the most senseless opinions which could acquire approval among humans, one will at all times notice something true. A false principle, or a couple of ill-considered connective propositions, lead the human being from the footpath of truth through imperceptible byways into the abyss. Despite the similarity cited there nevertheless remains an essential difference between the old cosmogony and the present one, so as to be able to draw entirely opposite sequels from this.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0019":["The cited teachers of the mechanical generation of the world-edifice derived all order that can be perceived in it from the haphazard contingency that let the atoms meet together so luckily that they made up a well-ordered whole. Epicurus was even so shameless that he demanded that the atoms deviated from their straight motion without any cause, in order to be able to encounter one another. All together they drove this absurdity so far that they ascribed the origin of all animate creatures to this very blind concurrence and actually derived reason from unreason. In my doctrinal constitution, by contrast, I find the matter bound to certain necessary laws. I see in its complete dissolution and dispersion a beautiful and orderly whole develop quite naturally out of it. This does not happen through a contingency and haphazardly, rather one notices that natural properties necessarily bring it with them thus. Is one not moved by this to ask: why then had matter to have precisely such laws, which aim at order and propriety? was it indeed possible that many things, each of which has its nature independent of the other, should determine one another of themselves precisely so that a well-ordered whole should arise from it, and if they do this, does it not yield an undeniable proof of the community of their first origin, which must be an all-sufficient highest understanding, in which the natures of things were designed for united intentions?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0020":["The matter that is the primitive material of all things is therefore bound to certain laws, which, left freely to them, it must necessarily produce beautiful combinations. It has no freedom to deviate from this plan of perfection. Since it thus finds itself subjected to a most wise intention, it must necessarily have been placed into such agreeing relations by a first cause ruling over it, and there is a GOD for just this reason, because nature, even in chaos itself, cannot proceed otherwise than regularly and orderly.",["- Folge rendered \"sequel\" throughout (glossary); Folgerung/Schlüße not used as the same word: Schlüße = \"inferences\".","- 0015: Ungefehr / Zufall: \"haphazard\" / \"contingency\"; Ohngefähr = \"haphazard\" (same word for Ungefehr). Zufall elsewhere \"contingency\" (glossary zufällig).","- 0015: Verstand = understanding; Beschaffenheiten = properties; Umfang = domain; Verbindung = combination; Zwecke = ends; Bestrebungen = strivings.","- 0015: \"Wie wäre es wohl möglich…\" and \"wenn sie nicht … erkenneten\": the \"they\" is preserved as in the German; \"beziehend\" rendered \"in relation to one another.\"","- 0016: Stoff = material (glossary) but Materie = matter; Urstoff = \"primitive material\"; Fortgang = progress/progression, rendered \"progress\" (mit Fortgang). Wirkungsgesetze = \"laws of effect\"; Wirkung glossary = effect.","- 0016: \"Weltsystem\" = world-system; \"Richtigkeit\" = correctness; \"Zuversicht\" = assurance; \"Zutrauen\" = confidence; \"Entwurf\" = design (also \"Entwurf\" in 0015, 0017).","- 0017: \"Aehnlichkeit\"/\"Uebereinstimmung\" = similarity/agreement; \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Erwegung\" = consideration (Betrachtung elsewhere = consideration also: one English word for two German words).","- 0018–0020: Weltweise = philosophers (cf. Weltweisheit = philosophy); Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal constitution\"; Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\"; Gemeinschaft = community; Absicht = intention/aim (\"intention\" in 0019, 0020).","- 0020: \"welchen sie frey überlassen … muß\" preserved as in German (relative with \"which\" governed by überlassen; the syntax is awkward). \"Verhältnisse\" = relations; \"Vollkommenheit\" = perfection."]],"naturgeschichte-0021":["I have so much good opinion of the honest disposition of those who do this draft the honor of examining it, that I hold myself assured the grounds adduced will, where they cannot yet remove all apprehension of harmful consequences from my system, nevertheless at least place the sincerity of my intention beyond doubt. If, notwithstanding this, there are malicious zealots who hold it a worthy duty of their holy calling to attach harmful interpretations to the most innocent opinions, then I am assured that their judgment among reasonable people has precisely the opposite effect of their intention. Moreover, I shall not be deprived of the right that Descartes, when he ventured to explain the formation of the bodies of the world from merely mechanical laws, always enjoyed with fair judges. For this reason I will cite the authors of the *Universal History* (*): \"Meanwhile we cannot but believe that the attempt of this philosopher, who exerts himself to explain the formation of the world in a certain time from desolate matter through the mere continuation of a motion once impressed, and has brought this to a few easy and general laws of motion, is as little punishable or detrimental to GOD, as some have imagined, as is that of others who since then have attempted with greater approval the very same thing from the original and innately created properties of matter, since thereby rather a higher concept of his infinite wisdom is occasioned.\"",[]],"naturgeschichte-0022":["(*) Part 1, §. 88.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0023":["I have sought to clear away the difficulties that seemed to threaten my propositions from the side of religion. There are some no lesser ones with regard to the matter itself. Even if it is true, it will be said, that GOD has laid into the forces of nature a secret art of forming themselves of their own accord out of chaos into a perfect constitution of the world, will the understanding of the human being, which is so dim with the most common objects, be capable of investigating the hidden properties in so great a topic. Such an undertaking means just as much as if one said: Give me only matter, I will build you a world out of it. Can the weakness of your insights, which is put to shame by the least things that occur daily and nearby to your senses, not teach you that it is futile to discover the immeasurable and that which went on in nature before there was yet a world. I annihilate this difficulty by showing distinctly that precisely this investigation is, among all that can be raised in the doctrine of nature, the one in which one can most easily and most surely reach the origin. Just as among all problems of the investigation of nature none has been resolved with more correctness and certainty than the true constitution of the structure of the world on the large scale, the laws of the motions and the inner driving-works of the revolutions of all planets; since Newtonian philosophy can afford in it such insights as one otherwise encounters in no part of philosophy; just so, I assert, among all natural things whose first cause one investigates, the origin of the world-system and the generation of the heavenly bodies, together with the causes of their motions, is that which one may hope soonest to see into thoroughly and reliably. The cause of this is easy to see. The heavenly bodies are round masses, thus of the simplest formation that a body whose origin one seeks can ever have. Their motions are likewise unmixed. They are nothing but a free continuation of an impulse once impressed, which, combined with the attraction of the body at the center, becomes circular. Moreover the space in which they move is empty, the intervals that separate them from one another are quite uncommonly great, and thus everything is set apart from one another most distinctly, as well for unconfused motion as for distinct observation of the same. It seems to me that one could say here, in a certain sense and without presumption: Give me matter, I will build a world out of it! that is, give me matter, I will show you how a world is to arise out of it. For if matter is present that is endowed with an essential force of attraction, it is not hard to determine those causes that could have contributed to the arrangement of the world-system considered on the large scale. One knows what belongs to a body's attaining a spherically round figure, one comprehends what is required for freely floating spheres to carry out a circular motion around the center toward which they are drawn. The position of the circles toward one another, the agreement of the direction, the eccentricity, all can be brought to the simplest mechanical causes, and one may hope with confidence to discover them, because they can be posited on the easiest and most distinct grounds. But can one boast of such advantages with regard to the least plants or insects? Is one in a position to say: Give me matter, I will show you how a caterpillar can be generated? Does one not remain stuck here at the first step, from ignorance of the true inner constitution of the object and of the entanglement of the manifold present in it? One may therefore not be surprised if I venture to say: that the formation of all heavenly bodies, the cause of their motions, in short, the origin of the whole present constitution of the structure of the world, will be able to be seen into sooner than the generation of a single herb or of a caterpillar, from mechanical grounds, will become distinctly and completely known.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0024":["These are the causes on which I ground my confidence that the physical part of the science of the world may yet hope in the future for the very perfection to which Newton has raised the mathematical half of it. Next to the laws according to which the structure of the world subsists in the constitution in which it is, perhaps no others in the whole investigation of nature are capable of such mathematical determinations as those according to which it arose, and without doubt the hand of an experienced geometer would here not cultivate unfruitful fields.",["- 0021: *Meinung* = opinion; *Gesinnung* = disposition; *Absicht* = intention; *Folgen* = sequels (rendered \"consequences\" for harmful *Folgen*; glossary gives sequel/sequence for *Folge*, \"consequence\" for *Folgerung*, so this is a departure to idiomatic sense).","- 0021: *Bewegungsgesetze* = laws of motion; *Weltweisen* = philosopher (lit. world-wise man); *Begriff* = concept; *Materie* = matter; *Weisheit* = wisdom.","- 0021: *Welthistorie* in the cited title rendered \"Universal History\"; literally \"world history.\" The quotation marks in the source are retained.","- 0023: *Verstand* = understanding; *Gegenstand* (most common objects) and *Object* (of the caterpillar) both = \"object\"; *Objects* is the Latinate member.","- 0023: *Naturlehre* = doctrine of nature; *Naturforschung* = investigation of nature; *Weltweisheit* = philosophy; *Weltwissenschaft* (0024) = science of the world.","- 0023: *Aufgaben* = problems; *deutlich* = distinct (also adverbially \"distinctly\"); *Beschaffenheit* = constitution, also used for *Verfassung* (constitution), so one English word stands for two German words.","- 0023: \"Kan dich die Schwäche… nicht lehren:\" is punctuated as a statement in the original with no question mark; I kept it without one. \"wird der Verstand… vermögend seyn\" likewise has no question mark. \"in gewissem Verstande\" = \"in a certain sense\" (*Verstand* in a different meaning). \"Vorwurfe\" = topic (lit. subject-matter); doubtful.","- 0023–0024: *Mannigfaltigkeit* = manifold (here \"the manifold\" for the noun phrase); *Grund/Gründe* = ground(s); *Bestimmungen* = determinations; *einsehen* = see into (not in glossary); *Meßkünstler* = geometer (lit. measuring-artist), a choice not covered by the glossary."]],"naturgeschichte-0025":["Having made it my concern to recommend the object of my consideration to a favorable reception, one will permit me to explain myself briefly about the way in which I have treated it. The first part concerns itself with a new system of the structure of the world on the large scale. Mr. Wright of Durham, whose treatise I came to know from the Hamburg *freye Urtheile* of the year 1751, first gave me occasion to look at the fixed stars not as a swarm scattered without visible order, but as a system that has the greatest similarity with a planetary one, so that, just as in the latter the planets find themselves very near to a common plane, so too the fixed stars in their positions relate themselves as near as possible to a certain plane, which must be thought of as drawn through the whole heaven, and by their densest accumulation toward it present that bright strip which is called the Milky Way. I have made certain that, because this zone, illuminated by countless suns, has very precisely the direction of a great circle, our sun too must find itself very near to this great plane of relation. In pursuing the causes of this determination, I have found it very probable that the so-called fixed stars, or firm stars, could well be, properly speaking, slowly moved wandering stars of a higher order. In confirmation of what one will find of this thought in its place, I will here cite only a passage from a writing of Mr. Bradley on the motion of the fixed stars. \"If one wants to pass a judgment from the outcome of the comparison of our best present observations with those which were made before this with a tolerable degree of correctness, it is clear: that some fixed stars have really changed their position relative to one another, and indeed in such a way that one sees that this does not stem from some motion in our planetary structure, but that it can be ascribed merely to a motion of the stars themselves. Arcturus gives a strong proof of this. For if one compares its present declination with its place, as it was determined by Tycho as well as by Flamsteed, one will find: that the difference is greater than one can presume to stem from the uncertainty of their observations. One has reason to presume: that other examples of like constitution must also occur among the great number of visible stars, because their positions relative to one another can be changed by manifold causes. For if one represents to oneself that our own solar structure changes its place with regard to the space of the world, then after the lapse of some time this will cause an apparent change of the angular distances of the fixed stars. And because in such a case this would have a greater influence on the places of the nearest stars than on the places of those which are far removed, their positions would seem to change, although the stars themselves really remained immovable. And if on the contrary our own planetary structure stands still and some stars really have a motion, then this will likewise change their apparent position, and indeed the more, the nearer they are to us, or the more the direction of the motion is so constituted that it can be perceived by us. Since, then, the positions of the stars can be changed by such manifold causes, if one considers the astonishing distances at which quite certainly some are situated, the observations of many generations will surely be necessary to determine the laws of the apparent changes, even of a single star. Much more difficult must it therefore still be to establish the laws for all the most remarkable stars.\"",["- *Weltgebäude* = \"structure of the world\"; *Planetengebäude* = \"planetary structure\"; *Sonnengebäude* = \"solar structure\" (Gebäude rendered \"structure\" throughout).","- *Vorwurf* = \"object\" (of my consideration), not *Gegenstand*; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration.\"","- *Hamburgischen freyen Urtheilen* left as a title in German, italicized; the full title is not given in the passage.","- *Fläche* = \"plane\" (also \"Beziehungsfläche\" = \"plane of relation\"); *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Ordnung* = \"order.\"","- *Wandelsterne* = \"wandering stars\"; *feste Sterne* = \"firm stars.\"","- *Urtheil fällen* = \"pass a judgment\"; *Beobachtung* = \"observation\"; *Beweis* = \"proof.\"","- \"Ticho\" and \"Flammsteed\" normalized to Tycho and Flamsteed; \"Arktur\" to Arcturus. *Oerter* = \"places\"; *Ort* = \"place\"; *Lage* = \"position.\"","- *Erfolg* = \"outcome\"; *erhellet* = \"it is clear\" (literal sense \"it is illuminated\"); *Beschaffenheit* = \"constitution.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0026":["I cannot determine precisely the boundaries that are to be found between the system of Mr. Wright and my own, and in which respects I have merely imitated his draft, or carried it further. Meanwhile there presented themselves to me afterwards grounds worthy of acceptance for extending it considerably on one side. I considered the kind of nebulous stars of which Mr. von Maupertuis makes mention in the treatise on the figure of the stars (*), and which present the figure of more or less open ellipses, and I easily made certain that they can be nothing other than an accumulation of many fixed stars. The at all times measured roundness of these figures taught me that here an incomprehensibly numerous host of stars, and indeed around a common center, must be ordered, because otherwise their free positions relative to one another would present well irregular shapes, but not measured figures. I also saw: that in the system in which they find themselves united they must be limited chiefly to a plane, because they depict not circular but elliptical figures, and that on account of their pale light they stand incomprehensibly far from us. What I have concluded from these analogies the treatise itself will lay before the examination of the reader free of prejudice.",["- *Grenzen* = \"boundaries\"; *bestimmen* = \"determine\"; *Entwurf* = \"draft\"; *Gründe* = \"grounds.\"","- *Stück* (in \"in welchen Stücken\") = \"respects\"; *nach der Hand* = \"afterwards.\"","- *Figur* = \"figure,\" *Gestalt* = \"shape\"; *Häufung* = \"accumulation\"; *Fläche* = \"plane.\"","- *unbegreiflich* = \"incomprehensibly\" (cf. *begreifen* = comprehend); *belehren* = \"teach\" (cf. *Belehrung*).","- *Mittelpunkt* = \"center\"; *Sternenheer* = \"host of stars\"; *Analogien* = \"analogies.\"","- \"(*)\" is the footnote marker of the original; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- *wohl irreguläre Gestalten*: *wohl* rendered \"well,\" preserving the possible concessive sense (\"to be sure\") alongside \"well irregular.\"","- *vorurtheilfreyen* = \"free of prejudice.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0027":["(*) Because I do not have the cited tract at hand, I will insert here what belongs to it from the citation of the Ouvrages diverses de Msr. de Maupertuis in the Actis Erud. 1745. \"The first phenomenon are those bright places in the heavens, which are called nebulous stars, and are taken for a heap of small fixed stars. But the astronomers have found them, through excellent telescopes, only as large oblong-round patches, which were somewhat brighter than the remaining part of the heavens. Huygens first came upon something of the kind in Orion; Halley mentions in the Anglican. Trans. six such patches. 1. in the sword of Orion, 2. in Sagittarius, 3. in the Centaur, 4. before the right foot of Antinous, 5. in Hercules, 6. in the girdle of Andromeda. If these are considered through a reflecting telescope of 8 feet, one sees that only the fourth part of them can be taken for a heap of stars; the remaining ones have presented only whitish patches, without considerable difference, except that one comes closer to circular roundness, another however is more oblong. It also seems that in the first the little stars visible through the telescope cannot cause its whitish shimmer. Halley believes: that one could explain from these appearances that which is encountered at the beginning of the Mosaic history of creation, namely that light was created sooner than the sun. Derham compares them to openings, through which another immeasurable region and perhaps the heaven of fire shines through. He opines that he was able to remark that the stars that are seen next to these patches would be much nearer to us than these bright places. To these the author adds a list of the nebulous stars from Hevelius. He takes these appearances for great bright masses, which would have been flattened by a violent revolution. The matter of which they consist, if it had a force of equal luminosity with the remaining stars, would have to be of monstrous magnitude, so that, seen from a much greater distance than that of the stars, they could nevertheless appear to the telescope under noticeable shape and magnitude. But if they came out approximately equal in magnitude to the remaining fixed stars, they would have to be not only incomparably much nearer to us, but at the same time have a much weaker light: because at such nearness and apparent magnitude they nevertheless show so pale a shimmer in themselves. It would therefore be worth the trouble to discover their parallax, if they have one. For those who deny it to them perhaps conclude from some to all. The little stars that one comes upon in the middle of these patches, as in Orion, (or still more beautifully, in the one before the right foot of Antinous, which looks no different from a fixed star that is surrounded with a mist) would, if they were nearer to us, either be seen upon them in the manner of projection, or shine through those masses, just as through the tails of comets.\"",[]],"naturgeschichte-0028":["In the second part, which contains within it the most proper object of this treatise, I seek to develop the constitution of the structure of the world from the simplest state of nature merely through mechanical laws. If I may venture to propose a certain order, in the examination with which they honor my thoughts, to those who are indignant at the boldness of this undertaking, I would ask that the eighth chapter be read through first, which, as I hope, can prepare their judgment for a correct insight. If meanwhile I invite the inclined reader to the examination of my opinions, I rightly worry that, since hypotheses of this kind commonly do not stand in much better regard than philosophical dreams, it is a sour obligingness for a reader to resolve upon a careful investigation of self-devised histories of nature and to follow the author patiently through all the turns by which he evades the difficulties that confront him, in order perhaps at the end, like the spectators of the London mountebank (*), to laugh at his own credulity. Meanwhile I venture to promise: that, if the reader will have been, through the proposed preparatory chapter, hopefully persuaded to risk such a physical adventure on such probable conjectures, he will not encounter on the progress of the way so many crooked detours and impassable obstacles as he perhaps at first feared.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0029":["(*) see Gellert's fable: Hans Nord.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0030":["I have in fact with the greatest caution renounced all arbitrary fictions. After I had put the world into the simplest chaos, I have applied no other forces than the force of attraction and of repulsion for the development of the great order of nature, two forces which are both equally certain, equally simple and at the same time equally original and universal. Both are borrowed from Newtonian philosophy. The former is a law of nature now put beyond doubt. The second, to which perhaps the natural science of Newton cannot grant as much distinctness as to the former, I assume here only in that sense in which no one denies it, namely in the finest dissolution of matter, as e.g. in vapors. From these grounds, so simple, I have derived the following system in an unartificial way, without devising other consequences than those upon which the attention of the reader must fall entirely of itself.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0031":["Let me be permitted finally to make a brief declaration regarding the validity and the alleged worth of those propositions that will occur in the following theory and according to which I wish them to be examined before fair judges. One fairly judges the author according to the stamp that he presses upon his ware; hence I hope that in the various parts of this treatise one will demand no stricter accountability for my opinions than in accordance with the worth that I myself issue for them. In general the greatest geometrical sharpness and mathematical infallibility can never be required of a treatise of this kind. If the system is grounded on analogies and agreements, according to the rules of credibility and of a correct way of thinking; then it has done enough for all the demands of its object. This degree of soundness I mean to have attained in some pieces of this treatise, as in the theory of the systems of fixed stars, in the hypothesis of the constitution of the nebulous stars, in the general sketch of the mechanical way of generation of the structure of the world, in the theory of the ring of Saturn and some others. Somewhat less conviction will be granted by some particular parts of the execution, as e.g. the determination of the relations of eccentricity, the comparison of the masses of the planets, the manifold deviations of the comets, and some others.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0032":["If I therefore in the seventh chapter, allured by the fruitfulness of the system and the agreeableness of the greatest and most wonderful object that one can only think, indeed constantly on the guiding thread of analogy and of a reasonable credibility, yet with some boldness continue the consequences of the doctrinal edifice as far as possible; if I present to the imagination the infinite of the whole creation, the formation of new worlds and the downfall of the old, the unbounded space of chaos; then I hope one will grant to the charming agreeableness of the object and to the pleasure which one has in seeing the agreements of a theory in their greatest extension so much indulgence, as not to judge them according to the greatest geometrical strictness, which in any case has no place in this kind of consideration. This same fairness I expect with regard to the third part. One will meanwhile always encounter in it something more than merely arbitrary, although at all times something less than undoubted.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0033":["Contents\nof the whole work.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0034":["First Part.",["- 0027: Original \"Phänomenon\" kept as \"phenomenon\"; \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances\"; \"Schwerdt\" \"sword\"; \"Sagittarius\" renders \"Schützen\"; \"Centaur\" renders \"Centaurus\". \"Hugen\" is rendered \"Huygens\" (the identification is supplied by the translator).","- 0027: \"Kraft\" = \"force\" (\"gleichleuchtende Kraft\"); \"Art\" = \"manner\" (\"nach Art der Projection\"); \"Theil\" = \"part\"/\"fourth part\".","- 0027: \"vor\" in the sense of \"für\" is rendered \"for\" in \"vor einen Haufen\" (\"for a heap\"); \"vor dem rechten Fuße\" = \"before the right foot\". \"Sie\" in \"Derham vergleicht sie\" is left as \"them\".","- 0027: \"Das erste Phänomenon sind\" is rendered with plural verb as in the German (\"are\"). \"Er\" in \"Er hält\" refers, as in the German, to \"der Verfasser\", not stated again.","- 0027: The last clause (\"oder schienen durch...durch\") has an apparent tense/mood shift (würden ... gesehen, oder schienen) preserved literally; the passage may not be fully sound.","- 0028: \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the world\"; \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\"; \"Meinungen\" = \"opinions\"; \"Gedanken\" = \"thoughts\"; \"überredet\" = \"persuaded\" (Überredung); \"Beurtheilung\" = \"judgment\"; \"Einsicht\" = \"insight\". \"gneigten\" is an OCR slip for \"geneigten\".","- 0028: \"Fortgange\" = \"progress\"; \"Vorwurf\" = \"object\" (a different word from Gegenstand/Object; not marked in the text); \"Abentheuer\" = \"adventure\".","- 0030: \"Weltweisheit\" = \"philosophy\"; \"Deutlichkeit\" = \"distinctness\" (deutlich); \"Verstande\" = \"sense\" (Verstand in the sense \"Sinn\"; not \"understanding\"); \"Gründe\" = \"grounds\"; \"Folgen\" = \"consequences\"; \"Naturwissenschaft\" = \"natural science\".","- 0031: \"Gültigkeit\" = \"validity\"; \"Sätze\" = \"propositions\"; \"Erklärung\" = \"declaration\"; \"Ueberzeugung\" = \"conviction\"; \"Beschaffenheit\" = \"constitution\"; \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object\" (0031 \"Object\", 0032 \"Gegenstand\"/\"Object\"); \"beurtheilt/Beurtheilung\" = \"judge/judgment\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"; \"Meinungen\" = \"opinions\"; \"Waare\" = \"ware\"; \"Theile\" = \"parts\".","- 0032: \"Einbildungskraft\" = \"imagination\"; \"Ausdehnung\" = \"extension\"; \"Betrachtungen\" = \"consideration\"; \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; \"Folgen\" = \"consequences\"; \"Lehrgebäude\" = \"doctrinal edifice\"; \"Schöpfung\" = \"creation\"; \"Raum\" = \"space\". \"Das Unendliche\" is rendered \"the infinite\". \"Man\" retained as \"one\".","- 0033–0034: \"Innhalt\" = \"Contents\" (Inhalt); \"Theil\" = \"Part\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0035":["Outline of a general systematic constitution among the fixed stars, derived from the phenomena of the Milky Way. Similarity of this system of fixed stars with the system of the planets. Discovery of many such systems, which show themselves in the expanse of the heavens in the shape of elliptical figures. New concept of the systematic constitution of the whole creation.",["- Verfassung = \"constitution\" (also the rendering of Lehrverfassung below); Begriff = concept; Phänomenis (Latin ablative form of *phaenomena*) = \"phenomena\".","- Schöpfung = \"creation\"; Weite = \"expanse\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0036":["Conclusion. Probable conjecture of more planets beyond Saturn, from the law according to which the eccentricity of the planets increases with the distances.",["- Beschluß = \"conclusion\" (a section heading, not the logical Schluß); Wahrscheinlichkeit rendered \"probable\" (wahrscheinlich).","- Vermuthung = \"conjecture\"; Eccentricität = \"eccentricity\"; Gesetz = law; \"über dem Saturn\" = \"beyond Saturn\" (literally \"above\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0037":["Second Part.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0038":["First Chapter.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (literally \"main piece\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0039":["Grounds for the doctrinal constitution of a mechanical origin of the world. Counter-grounds. Sole concept among all possible ones to do justice to both. First state of nature. Dispersion of the elements of all matter through the whole space of the world. First stirring through attraction. Beginning of the formation of a body at the point of the strongest attraction. General sinking of the elements toward this central body. Repulsive force of the finest parts into which the matter has been dissolved. Altered direction of the sinking motion through the combination of this force with the former. Uniform direction of all these motions toward the very same region. Striving of all particles to press toward a common surface and to pile up there. Moderation of the speed of their motion to an equilibrium with the heaviness of the distance of their place. Free circulation of all little parts around the central body in circular orbits. Formation of the planets from these moved elements. Free motion of the planets composed from them in the same direction in a common plane, near the center almost in circular orbits, and farther from it with increasing degrees of eccentricity.",["- Gründe vor = \"grounds for\" (vor for für); Gegengründe = \"counter-grounds\"; Zustand = state; Verbindung = combination; Kraft = force; Grad = degrees; Theil = part; Theilchen = \"little parts\"; Partickeln = \"particles\".","- Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal constitution\" (Verfassung = constitution); Cirkelkreisen = \"circular orbits\" (Kreis = \"orbit\" in this context, \"circle\" elsewhere noted as needed); Plane = \"plane\".","- Schweere = \"heaviness\"; Abstandes = \"distance\"; Ort = place; \"beyden genug zu thun\" = \"to do justice to both\" (literally \"enough\").","- Weltraum = \"space of the world\"; Raum = space; Welt = world."]],"naturgeschichte-0040":["Second Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0041":["Deals with the different density of the planets and the relation of their masses. Cause why the near planets are of a denser kind than the distant ones. Insufficiency of Newton's explanation. Why the central body is of a lighter kind than the globes running nearest around it. Relation of the masses of the planets according to the proportion of the distances. Cause, from the manner of generation, why the central body has the greatest mass. Calculation of the thinness in which all elements of the world-matter were dispersed. Probability and necessity of this thinning. Important proof of the manner of generation of the heavenly bodies from a remarkable analogy of Herr de Buffon.",["- Art = \"kind\" (dichterer Art) and \"manner\" (Art der Erzeugung); Erklärung = explanation; Beweis = proof; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.","- Dichtigkeit = \"density\"; Dünnigkeit = \"thinness\"; Verdünnung = \"thinning\"; Erzeugung = \"generation\"; Himmelskörper = \"heavenly bodies\"; Weltmaterie = \"world-matter\" (Materie = matter).","- Kugeln = \"globes\"; Masse = \"mass\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0042":["Third Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0043":["On the eccentricity of the orbits of the planets and the origin of the comets. The eccentricity increases by degrees with the distances from the sun. Cause of this law from the cosmogony. Whence the orbits of the comets stray freely from the plane of the ecliptic. Proof that the comets are formed from the lightest genus of the material. Incidental remark on the northern lights.",["- Gattung = \"genus\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Beweis = proof; Gesetz = law; Plane = \"plane\".","- \"seyn\" in \"gebildet seyn\" is subjunctive/infinitive after \"daß\" in the original (modern \"seien\"); kept as \"are\".","- Nordscheine = \"northern lights\" (literally \"north-shine\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0044":["Fourth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0045":["On the origin of the moons and the motions of the planets about the axis. The material for the generation of the moons was contained in the sphere from which the planet gathered the parts for its own formation. Cause of the motion of these moons with all determinations. Whence only the large planets have moons. On the axial rotation of the planets. Whether the moon formerly had a faster one? Whether the speed of the revolution of the earth diminishes? On the position of the axis of the planets toward the plane of their orbits. Displacement of their axis.",["- Bestimmungen = \"determinations\" (Bestimmung); Stoff = \"material\"; Theile = \"parts\"; Achsendrehung = \"axial rotation\" (literally \"axis-turning\"); Umwälzung = \"revolution\".","- \"eine schnellere\" refers back to Achsendrehung; \"one\" kept ambiguous in the same way.","- Kreise = \"orbits\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0046":["Fifth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0047":["On the origin of Saturn's ring and the calculation of its daily rotation from the relations of the same. First state of Saturn compared with the constitution of a comet. Formation of a ring from the little parts of its atmosphere by means of the motions impressed by its rotation. Determination of the time of its axial rotation according to this hypothesis. Consideration of the figure of Saturn. On the spheroidal flattening of the heavenly bodies in general. Closer determination of the constitution of this ring. Probable conjecture of new discoveries. Whether the earth did not have a ring before the Flood?",["- Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\" (also rendered \"property\" in the glossary); Betrachtung = consideration; Bestimmung = determination; Zustand = state; Vermuthung = conjecture.","- Umdrehung = \"rotation\"; Umschwunge = \"rotation\" (literally \"swing-round\"); Achsendrehung = \"axial rotation\"; Sündfluth = \"Flood\".","- \"einem Ring\" (sic, for einen Ring) is kept as printed in the German; the English is unaffected."]],"naturgeschichte-0048":["Sixth Chapter.",["- Original \"Sechtes\" is for \"Sechstes\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0049":["On the zodiacal light.",["- Zodiackallichte = \"zodiacal light\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0050":["Seventh Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0051":["On creation in the whole domain of its infinity, both according to space and to time. Origin of a great system of the fixed stars. Central body in the center of the system of stars. Infinity of creation. General systematic relation in its whole sum total. Central body of the whole of nature. Successive continuation of creation in all the infinity of times and spaces, through unceasing formation of new worlds. Consideration on the chaos of unformed nature. Gradual decay and demise of the structure of the world. Seemliness of such a concept. Renewal of the decayed nature.",["- Umfang = \"domain\"; Innbegriffe = \"sum total\" (Inbegriff); Raum = space; Zeit = time; Welt = world; Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; Betrachtung = consideration; Begriff = concept.","- Mittelpunkte = \"center\"; Wohlanständigkeit = \"seemliness\"; Succeßive = \"successive\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0052":["Supplement to the Seventh Chapter.",["- Zugabe = \"supplement\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0053":["General theory and history of the sun in general. Whence the central body of a structure of the world is a fiery body. Closer consideration of its nature. Thoughts on the changes of the air surrounding it. Extinction of the suns. Close view of their shape. Opinion of Herr Wright on the center of the whole of nature. Improvement of the same.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Meinung = opinion; Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; Gestalt = \"shape\"; Verbesserung = \"improvement\".","- Mittelpunkte = \"center\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0054":["Eighth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0055":["General proof of the correctness of a mechanical doctrinal constitution of the arrangement of the structure of the world in general, especially of the certainty of the present one. The essential capacity of the natures of things to raise themselves of their own accord to order and perfection is the most beautiful proof of the existence of GOD. Defense against the reproach of naturalism.",["- Fähigkeit = capacity; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Daseyn = existence; Beweis = proof; Ding = thing; Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal constitution\".","- Einrichtung = \"arrangement\"; Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; Gewißheit = certainty.","- \"der gegenwärtigen\" (feminine genitive) is left as ambiguous as the German: \"the present one\" (Verfassung, or Einrichtung)."]],"naturgeschichte-0056":["The constitution of the structure of the world is simple and not posited above the forces of nature. Analogies that confirm the mechanical origin of the world with certainty. The very same proved from the deviations. The adducing of an immediate divine ordinance does not do enough for these questions. Difficulty that moved Newton to give up the mechanical doctrinal concept. Resolution of this difficulty. The system put forward is the sole means among all possible grounds on both sides to accomplish enough. Is further proved through the relation of the density of the planets, of their masses, of the intervals of their distance and the step-like interconnection of their determinations. The motives of GOD's choice do not determine these circumstances immediately. Justification with respect to religion. Difficulties that emerge in a doctrinal constitution of immediate divine ordinance.",["- einfach = simple; Kräfte = forces; gesetzt = posited; Gründe = grounds; Mittel = means; Bewegungsgründe = motives; bestimmen = determine; Bestimmungen = determinations; Zusammenhang = interconnection.","- Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\"; Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal constitution\"; Gnüge/Genüge = \"enough\" (\"do enough\", \"accomplish enough\").","- Abweichungen = \"deviations\"; Anordnung = \"ordinance\"; Rechtfertigung = \"justification\"; Ansehung = \"respect\"; Zwischenräume = \"intervals\".","- \"beyderseitigen Gründe\" = \"grounds on both sides\" (both sides of the dispute)."]],"naturgeschichte-0057":["Third Part.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0058":["Contains a comparison between the inhabitants of the heavenly bodies.",["- Gestirne = \"heavenly bodies\" (also Himmelskörper, rendered the same way; Gestirn is more properly \"stars/constellations\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0059":["Whether all planets are inhabited? Cause to doubt it. Ground of the physical relations between the inhabitants of different planets. Consideration of the human being. Causes of the imperfection of his nature. Natural relation of the bodily properties of living creatures, according to their different distance from the sun. Consequences of this relation for their spiritual capacities. Comparison of the thinking natures on different heavenly bodies. Confirmation from certain circumstances of their dwelling places. Further proof from the arrangements of divine providence that are made for their best. Brief digression.",["- Unvollkommenheit = imperfection (Vollkommenheit = perfection); Fähigkeit = capacity; Beweis = proof; Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (not Beschaffenheit); Verhältniß = \"relation\".","- geistige = \"spiritual\" (Geist = mind; spirit); Natur = nature; Anstalten = \"arrangements\"; Vorsehung = providence.","- \"zu ihrem Besten\" = \"for their best\" (ambiguous reference preserved: the inhabitants').","- Ausschweifung = \"digression\"; \"Folgen dieser Verhältniß\" retains the feminine/neuter irregularity of the original."]],"naturgeschichte-0060":["Conclusion.",["- Beschluß = \"conclusion\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0061":["The occurrences of the human being in the future life.",["- Begebenheiten = \"occurrences\" (glossary); Mensch = \"human being\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0062":["Brief Outline\nof the most necessary basic concepts\nof the\nNewtonian philosophy of the world (*)",["- Weltwissenschaft = \"philosophy of the world\" is not a glossary item; Welt = world, Wissenschaft = science, so literally \"science of the world\" would be consistent. Rendering kept literal: better \"science of the world\". Reader should take \"science of the world\" (Wissenschaft = science).","- Grundbegriffe = \"basic concepts\"; nöthigsten = \"most necessary\" (notwendig = necessary).","- The line breaks of the title are kept as printed."]],"naturgeschichte-0063":["(*) This brief introduction, which perhaps might be superfluous with regard to most readers, I wished to give beforehand to those who might not be sufficiently acquainted with the Newtonian principles, as a preparation for insight into the following theory.",["- Grundsätze = \"principles\" (Grundsatz); kundig = \"acquainted\"; Einsicht = \"insight\"; Vorbereitung = \"preparation\".","- Ansehung = \"regard\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0064":["which are required for the understanding\nof what follows.",["- Verstande = \"understanding\" (Verstand, here the dative; the sense is the ordinary one, \"for understanding\"); erfordert = \"required\".","- Fragment continuing the title; \"die\" refers to Grundbegriffe in the preceding title (not resolved in the translation)."]],"naturgeschichte-0065":["Six planets, of which three have companions, Mercury, Venus, the earth with its moon, Mars, Jupiter with four and Saturn with five satellites, which describe circles around the sun as the center, together with the comets, which do it from all sides and in very long circles, make up a system, which one calls the system of the suns or also the planetary structure of the world. The motion of all these bodies, because it is circular and returning into itself, presupposes two forces, which are equally necessary in every kind of doctrinal concept, namely a shooting force, through which they would continue the straight direction at every point of their curvilinear course, and would remove themselves into the infinite, if another force, whichever it may be, did not constantly compel them to leave this and to run in a curved track which embraces the sun as the center. This second force, as geometry itself establishes undoubtedly, aims everywhere toward the sun and is therefore called the sinking, the centripetal force, or also gravity.",["- Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\"; Art = \"kind\"; Kraft = force; nothwendig = necessary; Mittelpunkt = center; Gravität = gravity.","- Kreise = \"circles\" (of orbits); krumlienigten = \"curvilinear\" (sic, krummlinigt); Gleise = \"track\"; Richtung = \"direction\"; Unendliche = \"infinite\".","- \"es\" in \"es thun\" is left unresolved (\"do it\"); \"diese\" (feminine, in \"diese zu verlassen\") is left unresolved: \"this\", referring to Richtung.","- \"welche es auch immer seyn mag\" = \"whichever it may be\" (literally \"which it may ever be\"). \"ausmacht\" = \"establishes\"; \"ungezweifelt\" = \"undoubtedly\".","- Trabanten = \"satellites\"; Begleiter = \"companions\"; the original \"sechs Planeten, davon drey Begleiter haben\" is kept as printed."]],"naturgeschichte-0066":["If the orbits of the heavenly bodies were exact circles, the very simplest analysis of the composition of curvilinear motions would show: that a constant drive toward the center is required for it; but although they are ellipses in all the planets as well as comets, in whose common focus the sun is located, higher geometry nevertheless, with the help of the Keplerian analogy (according to which the radius vector, or the line drawn from the planet to the sun, always cuts off from the elliptical path such spaces as are proportioned to the times), likewise demonstrates with unerring certainty: that a force must incessantly drive the planet toward the center of the sun in the whole course of its revolution. This force of descent, which rules through the whole space of the planetary system and aims at the sun, is thus an established phenomenon of nature, and just as reliably is the law also proved according to which this force extends from the center into distant expanses. It decreases always inversely as the squares of the distances from the same increase. This rule flows in an equally unerring way from the time that the planets use for their revolutions at different distances. These times are always as the square root of the cubes of their mean distances from the sun, from which it is derived: that the force that drives these heavenly bodies to the center of their revolution must decrease in inverse proportion to the squares of the distance.",["- Senkungskraft = \"force of descent\" (Senkung = descent/sinking); kept the same rendering throughout.","- Gesetze (neuter-looking \"das Gesetze\") = \"law\"; Kraft = force; Entfernung = distance; Abstand = distance (one English word for two German words).","- Kreis / Kreislauf / Bahn: \"orbit\" (Kreis), \"course of its revolution\" (Kreislaufe), \"path\" (Bahn).","- *Radius Vector*, *Cubis*, *Phänomenon* are Latinate/foreign words in the original; \"Cubis\" rendered \"cubes\"; \"Phänomenon\" as \"phenomenon\".","- \"zeigen: daß\" / \"dar: daß\" colons kept as in the German; \"darthun\" is split as \"thut ... dar\" and rendered \"demonstrates\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0067":["Just the same law that rules among the planets, insofar as they run around the sun, is also found in the small systems, namely those that the moons moved around their principal planets make up. Their times of revolution are likewise proportioned to the distances, and establish just the same relation of the force of descent toward the planet as that to which the latter is subject toward the sun. All of this has been put beyond contradiction forever by the most unerring geometry, by means of indisputable observations. To this there comes still the idea that this force of descent is just the same impulse that on the surface of the planet is called gravity, and which diminishes from this by degrees with the distances according to the cited law. This one sees from the comparison of the quantity of gravity on the surface of the earth with the force that drives the moon toward the center of its orbit, which are to one another just as attraction in the whole structure of the world, namely in inverse relation of the square of the distances. This is the cause why one also calls the often-mentioned central force gravity.",["- Weltgebäude = \"structure of the world\"; Weltbau elsewhere = \"world-edifice\" is not used here.","- Schweere = \"gravity\"; Gravität = \"gravity\" (one English word for two German words).","- Beobachtungen = observations; Verhältniß = relation (also \"proportion\" elsewhere not used); Wiederspruch = contradiction (\"beyond contradiction\").","- Centralkraft = \"central force\"; Attraktion = \"attraction\" (Latinate).","- \"welche gegen einander ... ist\": the plural relative \"welche\" with singular \"ist\" kept as in the German; its antecedent is ambiguous (the quantity and the force, or the two forces)."]],"naturgeschichte-0068":["Because it is moreover also probable in the highest degree: that, if an effect occurs only in the presence of and in proportion to the approach toward a certain body, the direction of the same is also in the most exact way referring to this body, it is to be believed that this body is, in whatever way it may be, the cause of the same; so for that reason one has thought to have ground enough to ascribe this general descent of the planets toward the sun to an attractive force of the latter, and to attribute this faculty of attraction to all heavenly bodies in general.",["- Wahrscheinlichkeit/wahrscheinlich = probability/probable; Grund = ground; Vermögen = faculty; Wirkung = effect; Art = way.","- The long sentence is kept whole; \"auf was für Art es auch wolle\" = \"in whatever way it may be\" (literally \"in whatever way it will\").","- \"dieselben\" (derselben) is ambiguous between \"effect\" and \"body\" in the second clause; I rendered \"the same\" for both, preserving this ambiguity."]],"naturgeschichte-0069":["If a body is therefore left freely to this impulse that drives it to sink toward the sun or some planet; so it will fall down toward it in constantly accelerated motion and shortly unite itself with its mass. If it has however received a push to the side; so it will, if this is not so powerful as to supply exactly the equilibrium to the pressure of sinking, sink in a bent motion into the central body, and if the swing that has been impressed upon it has been at least so strong as to remove it, before it touches its surface, from the perpendicular line by half the thickness of the body at the center, so it will not touch its surface, but, after it has swung close around it, will, through the speed acquired from the fall, raise itself again as high as it had fallen, in order to continue its revolution around it in constant circular motion.",["- Antrieb = impulse; Druck = pressure; Stoß = push; Schwung = swing; Gleichgewicht = equilibrium; Masse = mass.","- \"desselben Masse\" refers ambiguously to \"it\" (the body that is sunk toward) in the German; rendered \"its mass\" for \"desselben\", preserving the ambiguity.","- \"im Mittelpunkte\" may attach to \"half the thickness of the body\" or to the removal from the line; the English keeps the German order.","- Kreisbewegung = circular motion; Umlauf = revolution."]],"naturgeschichte-0070":["The difference between the orbits of the comets and planets thus consists in the balancing of the lateral motion against the pressure that drives them to fall; which two forces, the closer they come to equality, the more similar the orbit becomes to the circular figure, and the more unequal they are, the weaker the shooting force is with respect to the central force, the more elongated the orbit is, or, as one calls it, the more eccentric it is, because the heavenly body in one part of its path approaches the sun far more than in the other.",["- Laufkreisen / Kreis = \"orbit(s)\"; Cirkelfigur = \"circular figure\"; Bahn = \"path\".","- Kräfte = forces; Ansehung = \"respect\"; Theil = part.","- \"wird der Kreis\" and \"ist der Kreis\" are kept parallel; the German anacoluthon in the comparatives is kept."]],"naturgeschichte-0071":["Because nothing in the whole of nature is balanced in the most exact way, no planet has an entirely circular motion either; but the comets deviate most from it, because the swing that has been impressed upon them to the side has been least proportioned to the central force of their first distance.",["- cirkelförmig = \"circular\"; Abstand = distance (same English word as Entfernung); Schwung = swing.","- \"abgewogen\" = balanced; \"proportionirt\" = proportioned."]],"naturgeschichte-0072":["I will very often make use, in the treatise, of the expression of a systematic constitution of the world-edifice. So that one may find no difficulty in representing distinctly what is to be indicated by it, I want to explain myself briefly about this. Properly speaking all the planets and comets that belong to our world-edifice already constitute a system thereby, that they revolve around a common central body. I take this term, however, still in a narrower understanding, in that I look to the more exact relations that have made their combination with one another regular and uniform. The orbits of the planets are related as closely as possible to a common plane, namely to the extended equatorial plane of the sun; the deviation from this rule takes place only at the outermost boundary of the system, where all motions gradually cease. If therefore a certain number of heavenly bodies, which are ordered around a common center and move around the same, have at the same time been so limited to a certain plane that they have the freedom to deviate from the same on both sides only as little as possible; if the deviation takes place by degrees only in those that are most distant from the center, and therefore have less share in the relations than the others; so I say, these bodies find themselves combined together in a systematic constitution.",["- Verfassung = \"constitution\"; Weltbau = \"world-edifice\"; Benennung = \"term\"; Verstande = \"understanding\" (Verstand, in the phrase \"in engerem Verstande\").","- Verbindung = \"combination\"; Beziehung = \"relation\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Fläche = \"plane\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; Gleichförmig = \"uniform\".","- deutlich = \"distinctly\"; vorstellen = \"to represent\"; Antheil = \"share\".","- \"Sich bezieht auf\" rendered \"are related to\"; \"Abweichung\" = \"deviation\"; \"beschrenkt\" = \"limited\" (cf. Schranke = limitation)."]],"naturgeschichte-0073":["General\nNatural History\nand\nTheory of the Heavens.\nFirst Part,\nOutline of a Systematic Constitution\namong the Fixed Stars,\nlikewise\nof the Plurality of Such Systems of Fixed Stars.",["- Verfassung = \"constitution\"; Vielheit = \"plurality\"; Fixsternsystemen (sic, dative plural) = \"systems of fixed stars\".","- Title page of the First Part; AA page reference given as 1:241."]],"naturgeschichte-0074":["General\nNatural History\nand\nTheory of the Heavens.\nFirst Part,\nOn the\nSystematic Constitution among the\nFixed Stars.",["- Half-title repeating the title of the First Part; Verfassung = \"constitution\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0075":["The doctrinal concept of the general constitution of the world-edifice has since the times of Huygens gained no noticeable increase. One knows as yet nothing more than what one already knew then, namely, that six planets with ten companions, all of which have directed the circles of their revolution nearly onto one plane, and the eternal cometary globes, which stray out on all sides, constitute a system whose center is the sun, toward which everything sinks, around which their motions go, and by which they are all illuminated, warmed and enlivened; that finally the fixed stars, as just as many suns, may be centers of similar systems, in which everything may be arranged just as great and just as orderly as in our own, and that infinite world-space teems with world-structures, whose number and excellence has a relation to the immensity of their creator.",["- Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\"; Begriff = concept; Verfassung = \"constitution\"; Weltbau = \"world-edifice\"; Weltgebäude = \"world-structure\"; Weltraum = \"world-space\".","- Zahl = number; Verhältniß = relation; Vortreflichkeit = \"excellence\"; Unermeßlichkeit = \"immensity\".","- \"seyn\" (plural subjunctive/infinitive) after \"daß die Fixsterne ... Mittelpunkte ... seyn\" kept as \"may be\"; the later \"seyn mag\" supports \"may\".","- \"ihrer\" in \"ihre Bewegungen\" and \"alle\" are ambiguous between the planets and the comets; kept as \"their\".","- Possible ambiguity: \"ihres Schöpfers\" (genitive singular feminine/plural) is read as \"their creator\" referring to the world-structures, though it could be read as the creator of the \"Unermeßlichkeit\" in general."]],"naturgeschichte-0076":["The systematic, which took place in the combination of the planets that run around their suns, vanished here in the multitude of the fixed stars, and it seemed as if the lawful relation that is encountered in the small did not rule among the members of the universe in the large; the fixed stars received no law through which their positions toward one another would be limited, and one saw them fill all heavens and the heaven of all heavens without order and without intention. Since the human being's desire for knowledge has set these limits for itself, nothing further has been done than to infer from them the magnitude of that one, and to admire it, who has revealed himself in works so incomprehensibly great.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0077":["It was reserved for Mr. Wright of Durham, an Englishman, to take a fortunate step toward an observation which seems to have been used by himself for no altogether solid intention, and whose useful application he has not sufficiently observed. He considered the fixed stars not as an unordered swarm scattered without intention, but he found a systematic constitution in the whole, and a general relation of these heavenly bodies to a principal plane of the spaces that they occupy.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0078":["We want to seek to improve the thought that he has presented and to give it that turn through which it can be fruitful in important consequences, whose complete confirmation is reserved for future times.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0079":["Everyone who looks at the starry heaven on a clear night becomes aware of that bright streak which, through the multitude of stars that are more heaped up there than elsewhere, and through their recognizability losing itself in the great distance, presents a uniform light, which has been named with the name of the Milky Way. It is to be wondered at that the observers of the heavens have not long since been moved, by the constitution of this zone, distinguished so recognizably in the heavens, to infer from it peculiar determinations in the position of the fixed stars. For one sees it take up the whole heaven in the direction of a greatest circle, and indeed in uninterrupted interconnection, two conditions which bring with them so exact a determination, and marks so recognizably distinguished from the indeterminate of chance, that attentive astronomers should naturally have been occasioned thereby to track down with attention the explanation of such an appearance.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0080":["Because the stars are not posited on the apparent hollow celestial sphere, but, one farther than the other from our point of view, lose themselves in the depth of the heavens: it follows from this appearance that in the distances at which they stand off from us one behind the other, they do not find themselves in a dispersion indifferent toward all sides, but must relate chiefly to a certain plane which goes through our point of view, and to which they are determined to find themselves as near as possible.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0081":["This relation is so undoubted a phenomenon that even the remaining stars themselves, which are not comprehended in the whitish streak of the Milky Way, are nevertheless seen the more heaped and dense, the nearer their places are to the circle of the Milky Way, so that of the 2000 stars that the mere eye discovers in the heavens, the greatest part is encountered in a not at all broad zone, whose middle the Milky Way occupies.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0082":["If we now think a plane drawn through the starry heaven into unbounded distances, and assume: that all fixed stars and *systemata* have a general relation of their place to this plane, so as to find themselves nearer to it than to other regions, then the eye that finds itself in this plane of relation, in its view into the field of the heavenly bodies, on the hollow spherical surface of the firmament, will behold this densest heaping of the stars in the direction of such a drawn plane under the shape of a zone illuminated by more light. This bright streak will proceed in the direction of a greatest circle, because the standpoint of the spectator is in the plane itself. In this zone it will swarm with stars, which, through the indistinguishable smallness of the bright points that individually withdraw from the sight, and through their apparent density, make present a uniformly whitish shimmer, in a word, a Milky Way. The remaining host of heaven, whose relation to the drawn plane diminishes little by little, or which also finds itself nearer to the standpoint of the observer, will be more scattered, although yet, according to their heaping, they will be seen as relating to this very plan. Finally it follows from this that our sun-world, because from it this system of the fixed stars is seen in the direction of a greatest circle, is to be found in this very same great plane, and constitutes one system with the rest.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0083":["In order to penetrate the better into the constitution of the general combination that rules in the construction of the world, we want to seek to discover the cause which has made the places of the fixed stars relate to a common plane.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0084":["The sun does not restrict the reach of its force of attraction within the narrow district of the planetary edifice. By all appearances it extends it into the infinite. The comets, which raise themselves very far above the circle of Saturn, are compelled by the attraction of the sun to return back again and to run in circles. Although it is therefore more fitting to the nature of a force that seems to be incorporated into the essence of matter to be unrestricted, and it is also actually recognized as such by those who accept Newton's principles; nevertheless we want only to have it granted that this attraction of the sun reaches approximately to the nearest fixed star, and that the fixed stars, as just so many suns, work around themselves in an equal domain, consequently that the whole host of them strives to approach one another through attraction; so all world-systems find themselves in the constitution, through the mutual approach, which is unceasing and hindered by nothing, of falling together sooner or later into one lump, unless this ruin is forestalled, as with the globes of our planetary system, by the forces fleeing the center, which, in that they bend the heavenly bodies away from the straight fall, bring about, in combination with the forces of attraction, the eternal circular revolutions, through which the edifice of creation is secured against destruction and made suited to an imperishable duration.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0085":["Thus all suns of the firmament have revolving motions, either around a general center or around many. But one can here make use of the analogy of that which is noticed in the circulations of our sun-world: that namely, just as the very same cause that imparted to the planets the centrifugal force through which they perform their revolutions has also so directed their orbits that they all relate to one plane, so too the cause, whichever it may ever be, that gave to the suns of the upper world, as just so many wandering stars of higher world-orders, the force of revolution, has at the same time brought their circles as much as possible onto one plane, and has striven to restrict the deviations from it.\n\n---notes\n- Notes below apply across the passages.\n- 0076: *Verbindung* = combination; *Schranken* = limits (glossary: limitation/boundary); *Absicht* = intention; *Menge* = multitude; *Ordnung* = order; *Gesetz* = law. \"Seit dem\" rendered \"Since\"; \"desjenigen … der\" is ambiguous in German as to what is inferred/admired and is kept as literal as possible (\"the magnitude of that one\").\n- 0076: *Wißbegierde* = desire for knowledge; *Grösse* = magnitude. \"Alle Himmel und aller Himmel Himmel\" kept literal.\n- 0077: *Absicht* appears twice, rendered \"intention\" both times (first in the sense of the use made of the observation, second \"scattered without intention\"); *Bemerkung* = observation, *beobachtet* = observed (so *Beobachtung*/*Bemerkung* both \"observation\"). *Raume* (sic, plural) = \"spaces\"; *Hauptplan* = \"principal plane\" is an editorial reading of *Plan* — the German \"Plan\" is kept as \"plane\" here, but in 0082 \"Plan\" (*auf eben diesen Plan*) is rendered \"plan\"; note the German has the same word in both.\n- 0079: *Kentlichkeit* = recognizability; *Zusammenhang* = interconnection; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Bedingung* = condition; *Merkmal* = mark; *Erklärung* = explanation; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Beschaffenheit* = constitution. \"Ihn\" (the streak) after \"man siehet\" is kept; the subject of \"führen\" is \"zwey Bedingungen … Merkmale\" and the construction is left loose as in German.\n- 0080: *Gesichtspunkt* = point of view; *gleichgültig* rendered \"indifferent\". \"bestimmet sind\" = \"are determined\" (*bestimmen*). *Fläche* = plane (also surface in 0082 \"Kugelfläche\" = spherical surface).\n- 0081: *Oerter* = places; *begriffen* = comprehended.\n- 0082: *Beziehungsfläche* = plane of relation; *Systemata* kept in Latin form, italicized. *Plan* in \"auf eben diesen Plan\" is as in the German (cf. *Plan*/*Plane*); I have rendered \"plan\" literally, though \"plane\" may be intended — doubtful. *grössesten/größten Zirkels* = greatest circle (reading as in the text). *Sonnenwelt* = sun-world.\n- 0084: *Wesen* = essence; *Kraft* = force; *Umfange* = domain (*Umfang*); *Verfassung* = constitution; *Verbindung* = combination; *Sätze* = principles (glossary allows proposition/principle). *davor erkannt wird* = \"is recognized as such\". Ambiguity kept: \"so wollen wir doch nur zugestanden wissen\" and the long semicolon-chain follows German articulation; \"wofern … vorgebeuget worden\" is a subjunctive-like perfect, left as \"is forestalled\".\n- 0085: *Centerfliehkraft* = centrifugal force; *Weltordnungen* = world-orders; *Oberwelt* = upper world. \"dessen\" in \"der Analogie … dessen\" is left as German (grammatically irregular).",[]],"naturgeschichte-0086":["According to this representation one can depict the system of the fixed stars to some extent through the planetary one, if one enlarges the latter infinitely. For if, instead of the 6 planets with their 10 companions, we assume as many thousands of them, and instead of the 28 or 30 comets that have been observed, a hundred or a thousand times more of them, if we think of these very bodies as self-luminous, then to the eye of the spectator who looks at them from the earth, just the same illusion would arise as from the fixed stars of the Milky Way. For the planets thought of would, through their proximity to the common plane of their relation, present to us, who are situated with our earth in just the same plane, a zone densely illuminated by countless stars, whose direction would run along the greatest circle; this bright strip would be sufficiently occupied everywhere with stars, although according to the hypothesis they are wandering stars, hence not fastened to one place, for there would always be enough stars on one side through their displacement, although others would have changed this place.",["- *Vorstellung* = representation; *Schein* = illusion; *Körper* = bodies; *Plane* (Plan) = plane (Latinate sense of \"plan\" not rendered; \"plane\" for *Plan*, *Fläche* also \"plane/surface\" elsewhere).","- *Planeten* and *Wandelsterne* both rendered \"planets\"/\"wandering stars\" respectively; *Zirkel* = circle; *gemeinen* = common.","- *Naheit* = proximity; *Beziehung* = relation.","- \"Plane\" is my rendering of *Plan* (in the sense of a flat plane); the German word could also suggest \"plan,\" and I have not resolved this.","- *Hypothese* = hypothesis; \"it\" in \"es Wandelsterne\" is left as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0087":["The breadth of this illuminated zone, which represents a kind of zodiac, will be occasioned by the different degrees of deviation of the said wandering stars from the plane of their relation and by the inclination of their circles toward the same surface; and because most of them are near to this plane, their number will appear the more scattered the more it is measured by the distance from this surface; the comets, however, which occupy all regions without distinction, will cover the field of the heavens on both sides.",["- *Art* = kind; *Thierkreis* = zodiac; *Irrsterne* = wandering stars (same English as *Wandelsterne* in 0086); *Fläche* = surface; *Plan* = plane.","- *Abweichung* = deviation; *Neigung* = inclination; *Maasse* = measure; *Entfernung* = distance.","- *Anzahl* = number (cf. *Zahl*).","- *dieselbe Fläche* is rendered \"the same surface\"; its referent (plane of relation or another) is left as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0088":["The shape of the heavens of the fixed stars has, therefore, no other cause than just such a systematic constitution on the large scale as the planetary world-structure has on the small, in that all suns make up one system, whose universal surface of relation is the Milky Way; those that relate least to this surface are seen to the side, but just for that reason are less crowded, far more scattered and rarer. They are, so to speak, the comets among the suns.",["- *Verfassung* = constitution; *Weltbau* = world-structure; *Beziehungsfläche* = surface of relation; *Gestalt* = shape.","- *die sich am wenigsten ... beziehende* is a singular participial phrase followed by plural verbs; rendered \"those that\" to follow the sense of the plural verb, which departs slightly from the German number.","- *Sonnen* = suns; *Cometen* = comets."]],"naturgeschichte-0089":["But this new doctrinal concept ascribes to the suns a progressive motion, and yet everyone recognizes them as unmoved, and as fastened to their places from the beginning. The term that the fixed stars have received from this seems confirmed and undoubted through the observation of all centuries. This difficulty would annihilate the presented theoretical structure, if it were well founded. But in all appearance this lack of motion is only something apparent. It is either only an exceptional slowness, which is occasioned by the great distance from the common center of their revolution, or an imperceptibility, which is occasioned by the interval from the place of observation. Let us estimate the probability of this concept through the calculation of the motion that a fixed star near to our sun would have, if we posited that our sun were the center of its circle. If its distance is assumed, according to Huygens, to be more than 21000 times greater than the distance of the sun from the earth; then according to the established law of the times of revolution, which stand in the ratio of the square root of the cube of the distances from the center, the time that it would have to use in running through its circle around the sun once is more than a million and a half years, and this would effect in 4000 years a displacement of its place by only one degree. Now since perhaps very few fixed stars are as near to the sun as Huygens conjectured Sirius to be to it, since the distance of the remaining host of heaven perhaps uncommonly exceeds that of the latter, and thus would require incomparably longer times for such a periodic revolution, since moreover it is also more probable that the motion of the suns of the starry heavens goes around a common center, whose interval is uncommonly great, and the progression of the stars therefore can be exceedingly slow: so it can be inferred from this with probability that all the time since which observations have been made in the heavens is perhaps not yet sufficient to notice the alteration that has gone on in their positions. Meanwhile one must not yet give up the hope of discovering this too in time. Subtle and careful observers are required for it, likewise a comparison of observations standing far apart from one another. One would have to direct these observations chiefly on the stars of the Milky Way (*), which is the main plane of all motion. Mr. Bradley has observed almost imperceptible progressions of the stars. The ancients noted stars in certain places of the heavens, and we see new ones in others. Who knows, were they not the former ones, which have only changed their place. The excellence of the instruments and the perfection of the science of the stars give us well-founded hope of the discovery of such singular remarkable things (**). The credibility of the matter itself from the grounds of nature and of analogy supports this hope so well that it can stimulate the attention of natural investigators to bring it into fulfillment.",["- *Lehrbegriff* = doctrinal concept; *Lehrgebäude* = theoretical structure (lit. \"doctrinal edifice\"); *Benennung* = term; *Wahrscheinlichkeit* = probability; *Beobachtung* = observation; *Fortrückung* / *fortrückende* = progression / progressive.","- *Begriff* = concept; *gegründet* = well founded; *Grund/Gründe* = ground(s); *Abstand*, *Entfernung*, *Weite* = interval, distance, distance (distinguished only as shown).","- *Mittelpunkt* = center; *Zirkel* = circle; *Kreis* = circle (one English word for both).","- *Plan* = plane; *Ort/Oerter/Stelle/Stellung* = place/places/place/position.","- *Wissenschaft* = science (*Sternenwissenschaft*).","- *Aufmerker* = observers (lit. \"attenders\"); *Merkwürdigkeiten* = remarkable things.","- \"Time ... more than a million and a half years\": *anderthalb Millionen*; *Würfel* = cube. Footnote markers (*) and (**) kept as in the German.","- \"a comparison ... standing far apart\": *weit von einander abstehender* modifies *Beobachtungen*; \"were they not the former ones\" preserves the German's loose syntax."]],"naturgeschichte-0090":["(*) Likewise on those clusters of stars, many of which are together in a small space, as e.g. the Pleiades, which perhaps make up among themselves a small system within the larger.",["- *Siebengestirn* = Pleiades (lit. \"seven-star\"); *Haufen* = clusters; *Raum* = space.","- *in dem Grössern* = \"within the larger\", noun left unsupplied."]],"naturgeschichte-0091":["(**) de la Hire remarks in the Memoirs of the Academy at Paris of the year 1693 that he had perceived a strong alteration in the positions of the stars of the Pleiades, both from his own observations and from a comparison of the same with those of Ricciolus.",["- *Memoires* = Memoirs; *Siebengestirn* = Pleiades; *Aenderung* = alteration; *Stellungen* = positions; *Wahrnehmung* (*wahrgenommen*) = perceived.","- *mit des Ricciolus seinen* = \"with those of Ricciolus\" (colloquial possessive construction, *seinen* referring to *Beobachtungen*)."]],"naturgeschichte-0092":["The Milky Way is, so to speak, also the zodiac of new stars, which almost in no other region of the heavens than in this alternately let themselves be seen and vanish. If this alternation of their visibility derives from their periodic distance and approach to us, then it seems indeed from the adduced systematic constitution of the stars that such a phenomenon must for the most part be seen only in the district of the Milky Way. For since they are stars that run in very elongated circles around other fixed stars as satellites around their principal planets, the analogy with our planetary world-structure, in which only the heavenly bodies near to the common plane of the motions have companions running around them, requires that only the stars that are in the Milky Way will also have suns running around them.",["- *Thierkreis* = zodiac; *Sichtbarkeit* = visibility; *Verfassung* = constitution; *Phänomenon* = phenomenon; *Bezirk* = district.","- *Trabanten* = satellites; *Begleiter* = companions; *Hauptplaneten* = principal planets; *Himmelskörper* = heavenly bodies.","- *Kreisen* = circles; *ablang* = elongated; *Analogie* = analogy; *Weltbau* = world-structure.","- *Plan* = plane. *Annäherung* = approach; *Entfernung* = distance."]],"naturgeschichte-0093":["I come to that part of the presented doctrinal concept which makes it most attractive through the sublime representation that it presents of the plane of creation. The series of thoughts that has led me to it is short and unartificial; it consists in the following. If a system of fixed stars, which in their positions relate to a common surface, just as we have sketched the Milky Way, is so far removed from us that all recognizability of the individual stars of which it consists is no longer sensible even to the telescope: if its distance to the distance of the stars of the Milky Way has just the same ratio as this has to the interval of the sun from us; in short, if such a world of fixed stars is beheld at such an immeasurable interval from the eye of the observer, who is situated outside it, then it will appear under a small angle as a little space illuminated with weak light, whose figure will be circular if its surface presents itself straight to the eye, and elliptical if it is seen from the side. The weakness of the light, the figure and the recognizable magnitude of the diameter will distinguish quite distinctly such a phenomenon, if it is present, from all stars that are seen singly.",["- *Lehrbegriff* = doctrinal concept; *Vorstellung* = representation; *Plane der Schöpfung* (*Plan*) = \"plane of creation\", a literal rendering; the German word may also be read as \"plan\", which I have left open.","- *Fläche* = surface; *Kenntlichkeit* = recognizability; *kennbare* = recognizable; *empfindlich* = sensible; *Sehrohr* = telescope.","- *Abstand*, *Entfernung* = interval, distance (the English differs; German differs likewise).","- *Welt* = world; *Verhältniß* = ratio; *Durchmesser* = diameter; *deutlich* = distinctly; *Figur* = figure; *Größe* = magnitude.","- *ungekünstelt* = unartificial; *reitzend* = attractive (not in glossary)."]],"naturgeschichte-0094":["One need not look around long among the observations of the astronomers for this appearance. It has been distinctly perceived by various observers. People have been amazed at its strangeness; people have conjectured and have given place sometimes to odd imaginings, sometimes to apparent concepts, which however were just as groundless as the former. The nebulous stars are what we mean, or rather a genus of them, which Herr von Maupertuis describes thus (*): That they are little places, illuminated somewhat more than the darkness of the empty space of the heavens, which all agree in this, that they represent more or less open ellipses, but whose light is far weaker than any other that one becomes aware of in the heavens. The author of the *Astrotheologie* imagined that they were openings in the firmament, through which he believed he saw the fiery heaven. A philosopher of more enlightened insights, the already cited Herr von Maupertuis, holds them, in consideration of their figure and knowable diameter, to be astonishingly large heavenly bodies, which, through their great flattening caused by the momentum of rotation, seen from the side, present elliptical shapes.",["- *wahrgenommen* = \"perceived\" (*Wahrnehmung* = perception); *Begriffe* = \"concepts\"; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration\"; *Gattung* = \"genus\".","- *vorstellen* (in \"Ellipsen vorstellen\") and *darstellen* (at the end) are both rendered by forms of \"represent\"/\"present\": \"represent\" for *vorstellen*, \"present\" for *darstellen*.","- *Plätzchen* = \"little places\" (*Platz*, cf. *Ort* = place); *Platz geben* = \"give place\".","- *kennbar* = \"knowable\" (*kennen* = know); *Einbildungen* = \"imaginings\" (*Einbildungskraft* = imagination).","- *Drehungsschwunge*: *Schwung* rendered \"momentum\" throughout; the German is not more specific.","- The Astrotheologie text has the spaced punctuation \"wird .\" in the OCR, silently normalized; \"(*)\" is the footnote sign, the note is in the next passage."]],"naturgeschichte-0095":["(*) Treatise on the Figure of the Stars.",["- *Abhandlung von der Figur der Sterne*: *Figur* = \"figure\"; the title is rendered literally, not as the published English title of Maupertuis's work."]],"naturgeschichte-0096":["One is easily convinced that this latter explanation likewise cannot take place. Because this kind of nebulous stars must, beyond doubt, be at least as far from us as the remaining fixed stars; so would not only their magnitude be astonishing, according to which they would have to surpass even the largest stars many thousand times, but it would be most strange of all that they, with this extraordinary magnitude, since they are self-luminous bodies and suns, should show the dullest and weakest light in themselves.",["- *Erklärung* = \"explanation\"; *statt finden* = \"take place\" (literally \"find place\").","- *Art* = \"kind\"; *Grösse* = \"magnitude\"; *Fixsterne* = \"fixed stars\".","- *an sich zeigen*: here *an sich* is not the technical \"in itself\"; rendered \"in themselves\" in the plain sense of \"on them\".","- The syntax \"Weil ... so wäre\" is kept as a single sentence; the \"Because ... ; so\" construction is left as in German."]],"naturgeschichte-0097":["Far more natural and comprehensible is it that they are not individual such large stars but *systemata* of many, whose distance presents them in so narrow a space that the light, which from each of them individually is imperceptible, turns out, given their immeasurable multitude, into a uniform pale shimmer. The analogy with the system of stars in which we find ourselves, their shape, which is precisely as it must be according to our doctrinal concept, the weakness of the light, which requires a presupposed infinite distance: everything agrees perfectly to hold these elliptical figures for just such world-orders, and, so to speak, Milky Ways, whose constitution we have just developed; and if conjectures in which analogy and observation agree perfectly in supporting one another have just the same worthiness as formal proofs, then one will have to hold the certainty of these systems as settled.",["- *Lehrbegriff* = \"doctrinal concept\" (*Lehre* = doctrine, *Begriff* = concept).","- *begreiflich* = \"comprehensible\" (*begreifen* = comprehend); *Menge* = \"multitude\"; *Beweise* = \"proofs\".","- *Systemata* (Latinized plural) is italicized; *Systemen* = \"systems\".","- *Verfassung* = \"constitution\" (not *Beschaffenheit*, which the glossary also renders \"constitution\").","- *Weltordnungen* = \"world-orders\"; *Gestalt* = \"shape\"; *Figur* = \"figure\".","- *vor* in \"vor ... zu halten\" = \"for\" (*halten vor*), as in the German; \"hold ... for\" and \"hold ... as settled\" are both *halten*.","- *Würdigkeit* = \"worthiness\", kept literal."]],"naturgeschichte-0098":["Now the attention of the observers of the heavens has motives enough to occupy itself with this topic. The fixed stars, as we know, all relate to a common plane, and thereby make a co-ordinated whole, which is a world of worlds. One sees that at immeasurable distances there are more such systems of stars, and that creation in the whole infinite domain of its magnitude is everywhere systematic and relating to one another.",["- *Vorwurf* = \"topic\" (not *Gegenstand*/*Object*, which is \"object\").","- *Bewegungsgrund* = \"motive\"; *Umfang* = \"domain\"; *Grösse* = \"magnitude\".","- *Plan* here = \"plane\" (geometrical sense), distinct from \"plan\" for *Plan* in the sense of design in later passages; *Entwurf* is rendered \"design\".","- *zusammengeordnet* = \"co-ordinated\"; *auf einander beziehend* = \"relating to one another\"; the reflexive/reciprocal construction is left as in German.","- *wissen* = \"know\" in \"as we know\"; *Schöpfung* = \"creation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0099":["One could further conjecture that these very higher world-orders are not without relation to one another, and through this reciprocal relation in turn make up a still more immeasurable system. In fact one sees that the elliptical figures of these kinds of nebulous stars, which Herr von Maupertuis cites, have a very close relation to the plane of the Milky Way. Here a wide field for discoveries stands open, for which observation must give the key. The nebulous stars properly so called, and those about which there is dispute whether to name them so, would have to be investigated and examined according to the guidance of this doctrinal concept. If one considers the parts of nature according to aims and a discovered design, then certain properties open up that are otherwise overlooked and remain concealed, when observation without guidance scatters itself over all objects.",["- *Beziehung* = \"relation\" and *Verhältniß* = \"relation\" (one English word for two German words); *Verhältniß* is \"reciprocal relation\" (*gegenseitig*).","- *Plan* = \"plane\" (see previous passage); *Entwurf* = \"design\"; *Absichten* = \"aims\".","- *Eigenschaften* = \"properties\" (*Beschaffenheit* is also \"property\" in the glossary).","- *benennen* = \"name\"; *Gegenstände* = \"objects\"; *Betrachtung* = consideration, here the verb *betrachten* = \"consider\".","- *Muthmassen* = \"conjecture\"; *Lehrbegriff* = \"doctrinal concept\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0100":["The doctrinal concept that we have presented opens to us a prospect into the infinite field of creation, and offers a representation of the work of GOD that is commensurate with the infinity of the great master-builder. If the magnitude of a planetary world-structure, in which the earth is scarcely noticed as a grain of sand, puts the understanding into amazement, with what astonishment is one enraptured when one looks at the infinite multitude of worlds and systems that fill the sum total of the Milky Way; but how this astonishment increases when one becomes aware that all these immeasurable orders of stars in turn make the unity of a number, the end of which we do not know, and which perhaps just like that one is incomprehensibly large, and yet in turn is still the unity of a new combination of numbers. We see the first members of a progressive relation of worlds and systems, and the first part of this infinite progression already gives to be cognized what one should conjecture of the whole. Here is no end, but an abyss of a true immeasurability, in which all capacity of human concepts sinks, even if it is raised by the aid of the science of number. The wisdom, the goodness, the power that has revealed itself is infinite, and in just the same measure fruitful and active; the plan of its revelation must therefore, just like it, be infinite and without boundaries.",["- *Vorstellung* = \"representation\"; *Verstand* = \"understanding\"; *Begriffe* = \"concepts\"; *Fähigkeit* = \"capacity\"; *Innbegriff* = \"sum total\"; *Einheit* = \"unity\"; *Zahl* = \"number\"; *Grenzen* = \"boundaries\".","- *Verhältniß* = \"relation\" (also \"ratio\" in other contexts); *Progreßion* = \"progression\" (*progressus*).","- *Zahlwissenschaft* = \"science of number\" (*Wissenschaft* = science).","- *giebt zu erkennen* = \"gives to be cognized\" (*erkennen* = cognize), kept literal.","- *wissen* = \"know\" (\"we do not know\"); *Wirken*/*Werk* = \"work\"; *Werkmeister* = \"master-builder\".","- *die ... sich offenbaret hat, ist unendlich*: the singular verb may agree with the last of the three nouns or with the relative clause; left as in German. *Plan* = \"plan\" here.","- *Menge* = \"multitude\"; *Zahlverbindung* = \"combination of numbers\" (*Verbindung* = combination)."]],"naturgeschichte-0101":["But it is not only in the great that important discoveries are to be made, which serve to expand the concept that one can make of the magnitude of creation. In the smaller not less is undiscovered, and we see even in our solar world the members of a system that stand off immeasurably far from one another, and between which the intermediate parts have not yet been discovered. Should there between Saturn, the outermost among the wandering stars that we know, and the least eccentric comet, which perhaps descends to us from a distance 10 and more times more remote, be no more any planet whose motion would come nearer to the cometary than that one's? and should not still others, through an approach of their determinations, by means of a series of intermediate members, transform the planets little by little into comets, and the latter genus hang together with the former?",["- *Wandelsterne* = \"wandering stars\"; *Sonnenwelt* = \"solar world\"; *kennen* = \"know\" (be acquainted with).","- *Gattung* = \"genus\"; *Bestimmungen* = \"determinations\"; *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Grösse* = \"magnitude\".","- *Glieder* = \"members\"; *Zwischentheile* = \"intermediate parts\" (*Teil* = part); *Zwischengliedern* = \"intermediate members\".","- *dessen Bewegung der cometischen näher als jener käme*: \"that one's\" renders *jener* (the motion of the comet or of Saturn's; the German is elliptical); *jener* is taken to refer back to Saturn's motion, left ambiguous.","- *zusammenhängen* = \"hang together\"; the sentence's last clause is continued under *solten nicht*, kept as one sentence.","- *kein Planet mehr seyn* = \"be no more any planet\", kept close to the word order; \"10\" is as in the text."]],"naturgeschichte-0102":["The law according to which the eccentricity of the planetary circles relates in comparison with their distance from the sun supports this conjecture. The eccentricity in the motions of the planets increases with the distance of the same from the sun, and the distant planets thereby come nearer to the determination of the comets. It is therefore to be conjectured that there will be still other planets beyond Saturn, which, still more eccentric, and thereby still more closely related to those, by means of a constant ladder finally make the planets into comets. The eccentricity in Venus is 1/126 of the semi-axis of its elliptical circle; in the earth 1/58, in Jupiter 1/20, and in Saturn 1/17 of the same; it therefore evidently increases with the distances. It is true, Mercury and Mars except themselves from this law by their much greater eccentricity than the measure of their distance from the sun permits; but we shall be taught in what follows that the very same cause on account of which a smaller mass fell to the share of some planets at their formation also drew after it the lack of the momentum required for circular course, consequently the eccentricity, consequently left them incomplete in both respects.",["- *mit derselben Abstande von der Sonne*: *derselben* is ambiguous (the planets', or the motions'); rendered \"of the same\", leaving the ambiguity.","- *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Belehrung* = \"teaching\" (*belehret werden* = \"be taught\"); *Gesetz* = \"law\".","- *Schwung* = \"momentum\" (also in 0094); *Cirkellauf* = \"circular course\"; *Masse* = \"mass\"; *Maaß* = \"measure\".","- *Leiter* = \"ladder\"; *Stücken* = \"respects\" (literally \"pieces\"); *Theil zu werden* = \"fall to the share\" (*Teil* = part, here idiomatic).","- *nimmt sich ... aus* = \"except themselves\"; *verwandt* = \"related\"; *Entfernungen* = \"distances\", *Abstand* = \"distance\" (two German words, one English word).","- The fractions are printed as ⅟126 etc. in the source and are given here as 1/126 etc."]],"naturgeschichte-0103":["Is it not, in accordance with this, probable: that the decrease of the eccentricity of the heavenly bodies situated next above Saturn is approximately just as moderate as it is in the lower ones, and that the planets are related to the genus of the comets through less sudden declines; for it is certain that precisely this eccentricity makes the essential difference between the comets and planets, and that the tails and vapor-spheres of the latter are only its sequel; likewise, that precisely the cause, whatever it may always be, that imparted to the heavenly bodies their circular motions, at greater distances was not only weaker for making the whirling impetus equal to the force of sinking, and thereby left the motions eccentric, but also for precisely this reason was less capable of bringing the circles of these spheres onto a common surface, on which the lower ones move, and thereby occasioned the digression of the comets toward all regions.",["- Folge = sequel (here \"deren Folge\"; \"deren\" could refer to the comets or to eccentricity; left ambiguous as in German, with \"latter\" standing for \"deren\" referring to the comets and \"its\" to eccentricity's—reading doubtful).","- Eccentricität, Cometen, Saturn: retained as cognates. Geschlecht = genus (Gattung is the glossary's term; here Geschlecht, \"genus\" used for want of a glossary entry).","- Dunstkugeln = \"vapor-spheres\"; Kugeln = \"spheres\"; Senkungskraft = \"force of sinking\"; Drehungsschwung = \"whirling impetus\"; Fläche = \"surface\".","- \"Ist es ... nicht wahrscheinlich: daß ...; denn ...\" kept as a single sentence with the German colon and semicolons.","- \"Ursache ... die ... ertheilet hat\": \"which may always be\" renders \"es auch immerhin seyn mag\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0104":["According to this conjecture one would perhaps still have to hope for the discovery of new planets beyond Saturn, which would be more eccentric than this one, and thus nearer to the cometary property; but precisely for that reason one would be able to catch sight of them only a short time, namely in the time of their perihelion, which circumstance, together with the small measure of the approach and the weakness of the light, has hitherto prevented the discovery of the same, and must also make it difficult in the future. The last planet and first comet would, if it so pleased, be able to be called that one whose eccentricity would be so great that in its perihelion it would cut through the circle of the planet nearest to it, perhaps therefore of Saturn.",["- Sonnennähe = \"perihelion\" (literally \"nearness to the sun\"); Eigenschaft = \"property\" (Beschaffenheit is also glossed \"property\").","- \"desselben\" (singular) after plural \"Planeten\" is kept as \"of the same\"; the German referent is unclear.","- \"derjenige können genannt werden\" is kept in its awkward word order as \"be able to be called\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0105":["General\nNatural History\nand\nTheory of the Heavens.\nSecond Part,\non the\nfirst state of nature, the formation\nof the heavenly bodies, the causes of their motion,\nand the systematic relation of the same, as well\nin the planetary edifice in particular, as\nalso with regard to the whole\ncreation.",["- Zustand = state; Ursachen = causes; Beziehung = relation; Planetengebäude = planetary edifice; Schöpfung = creation.","- \"sowol ... als auch\" rendered \"as well ... as also\" to keep the German construction."]],"naturgeschichte-0106":["Behold formative nature move toward its great end, each speck of sun-dust stir toward another speck, each that is drawn draw the other again to itself, strive to embrace the nearest again, to form it. Behold matter in a thousand ways and manners press to the universal center.",["- Verse lineation lost in the source text as given; passage rendered as a continuous text. \"Sonnenstäubchen\" = \"speck of sun-dust\" (a mote in a sunbeam).","- Materie = matter; Centro = \"center\" (from Latin); Art und Weise = \"ways and manners\" (Art = way/manner in glossary)."]],"naturgeschichte-0107":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0108":["General\nNatural History\nand\nTheory of the Heavens.\nSecond Part,",[]],"naturgeschichte-0109":["First Chapter,\non the\norigin of the planetary world-edifice in general,\nand the causes of their motions.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (literally \"main piece\"); Weltbau = \"world-edifice\"; Ursprung = \"origin\".","- \"ihrer\" (plural, feminine/plural) in \"ihrer Bewegungen\" follows \"Weltbaues\" (masculine singular) in the German; kept as \"their\" without correction."]],"naturgeschichte-0110":["The consideration of the world-edifice shows, with regard to the alternating relations that its parts have among one another, and through which they designate the cause from which they derive, two sides, which both are equally probable and worthy of acceptance. If one considers, on the one hand: that 6 planets with 9 companions, which describe circles around the sun, as their center, all move toward one side, and indeed toward that one toward which the sun itself turns, which governs the revolutions of them all through the force of attraction, that their circles do not deviate far from a common surface, namely from the extended equatorial surface of the sun, that in the case of the most distant of the heavenly bodies belonging to the sun-world, where the common cause of the motion, by conjecture, was not as powerful as in the proximity to the center, deviations from the exactness of these determinations took place, which have a sufficient proportion to the deficiency of the impressed motion, if one, I say, considers all this interconnection: then one is moved to believe that a cause, whichever it may be, had a thoroughgoing influence in the whole space of the system, and that the concord in the direction and position of the planetary circles is a sequel of the agreement that they all must have had with that material cause, through which they were set in motion.",["- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Folge = sequel; Bestimmungen = determinations; Verhältniß = proportion; Gemeinschaft kept elsewhere as \"community\".","- Himmelskörper = heavenly bodies; Sonnenwelt = sun-world; Mittelpunkt/Mittelpuncte = center; materialische = material.","- \"welche ihrer alle Umläufe ... regieret\": \"which\" may refer to the sun or to the attraction; preserved ambiguity. \"ihrer alle\" = \"of them all\".","- \"dem Vermuthen nach\" = \"by conjecture\"; \"in der Naheit\" = \"in the proximity\".","- \"annehmungswürdig\" = \"worthy of acceptance\"; \"Einträchtigkeit\" = \"concord\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0111":["If we consider, on the other hand, the space in which the planets of our system run around, it is perfectly empty (*) and deprived of all matter that could cause a community of influence on these heavenly bodies, and bring after it the agreement among their motions. This circumstance is settled with perfect certainty, and still exceeds, if possible, the previous probability. Newton, moved by this ground, could allow no material cause that was supposed to maintain, through its extension in the space of the planetary edifice, the community of motions. He asserted that the immediate hand of GOD had carried out this arrangement without the application of the forces of nature.",["- Gemeinschaft = community; Grund = ground; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Erstreckung = extension (Ausdehnung is the glossary's term; here Erstreckung rendered the same).","- Kräfte = forces; behauptete = asserted; \"(*)\" footnote marker carried over."]],"naturgeschichte-0112":["(*) I do not investigate here whether this space can be called empty in the most proper understanding. For here it is enough to remark that all matter that might perhaps be met with in this space is far too incapable for it to be able to exert any effect with regard to the moved masses of which the question is.",["- Verstande = \"understanding\" (in \"in dem allereigentlichsten Verstande\"); Wirkung = effect; Materie = matter.","- \"unvermögend\" = \"incapable\"; \"von denen die Frage ist\" kept literally as \"of which the question is\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0113":["One sees, on impartial consideration: that the grounds on both sides here are equally strong and both are to be esteemed equal to a complete certainty. But it is just as clear that there must be a concept in which these grounds, striving against one another in appearance, can and ought to be united, and that in this concept the true system is to be sought. We will indicate it in brief words. In the present constitution of the space in which the spheres of the whole planetary world revolve, there is present no material cause that could impress or direct their motions. This space is perfectly empty, or at least as good as empty; therefore it must formerly have been constituted otherwise and filled with sufficiently capable matter, to transfer the motion to all the heavenly bodies found therein, and to make them with its own, consequently all among one another, concordant, and after attraction has cleared the said spaces and gathered all the extended matter into particular lumps; so the planets must now, with the motion once impressed, continue their revolutions freely and unchanged in a space that does not resist. The grounds of the probability first adduced require this concept throughout, and because between both cases no third is possible; so this can be regarded with a preferential kind of approval, which raises it above the plausibility of a hypothesis. One could, if one wished to be prolix, through a series of inferences deduced from one another, in the manner of a mathematical method, with all the pomp that this carries with it and with still greater illusion than its parade in physical matters is commonly wont to have, finally come upon the draft itself that I shall lay out of the origin of the world-edifice; but I would rather present my opinions in the shape of a hypothesis, and leave it to the insight of the reader to test their worthiness, than make their validity suspect through the illusion of a surreptitious conviction, and, in winning over the ignorant, lose the approval of the connoisseurs.",["- Gründe = grounds; Begriff = concept; Scheinbarkeit = \"plausibility\" (cf. Schein = illusion, here \"Schein\" rendered \"illusion\"); Beyfall = approval; Meinungen = opinions; Gültigkeit = validity; Ueberführung = \"conviction\" (glossary Überzeugung is also \"conviction\"; here Ueberführung, a different German word).","- Schlüsse = inferences; Kenner = \"connoisseurs\"; Unwissenden = \"ignorant\"; Einsicht = insight.","- \"und nachdem die Anziehung ... versammlet; so müssen\" preserves the German \"nachdem ... so\" with semicolon, leaving the syntax loose.","- \"Wahrscheinlichkeit\" = probability; \"erschlichen\" = \"surreptitious\".","- \"eingedrückt\" = \"impressed\"; \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\" (Beschaffenheit also \"constitution\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0114":["I assume: that all matters of which the globes belonging to our solar world, all planets and comets, consist, at the beginning of all things, dissolved into their elementary basic material, filled the whole space of the world-edifice, in which these formed bodies now run about. This state of nature, if one considers it in and for itself, even without aim at a system, seems to be only the simplest that can follow upon nothing. At that time nothing had yet formed itself. The composition of celestial bodies standing apart from one another, their distance moderated according to the attractions; their shape, which arises from the equilibrium of the assembled matter, are a later state. Nature, which bordered immediately on creation, was as raw, as unformed as possible. But even in the essential properties of the elements that make up the chaos, the mark of that perfection is to be detected which they have from their origin, in that their essence is a sequel from the eternal idea of the divine understanding. The simplest, the most general properties, which seem to have been sketched without intention; the matter, which seems to be merely passive and in need of forms and arrangements, has in its simplest state an endeavor to form itself, through a natural development, into a more perfect constitution. But the diversity in the species of the elements contributes the most to the stirring of nature and to the formation of the chaos, as that through which the rest that would prevail with a general equality among the scattered elements is lifted, and the chaos begins to form itself in the points of the more strongly attracting particles. The species of this basic material are without doubt, according to the immensity that nature shows on all sides, infinitely various. Those of greatest specific density and force of attraction, which in and for themselves take up less space and are also rarer, will therefore, given equal distribution in the space of the world, be more scattered than the lighter ways. Elements of 1000 times greater specific gravity are a thousand, perhaps even millions of times more scattered than those lighter in this measure. And since these gradations must be thought as infinite as possible, so, just as there can be corporeal constituents of one species that surpasses another in density in the measure that a globe described with the radius of the planetary edifice surpasses another that has the thousandth part of a line in diameter, so too that kind of scattered elements will be removed from one another by a distance so much greater than these.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0115":["In a space filled in such a manner the general rest lasts only a moment. The elements have essential forces to set one another in motion, and are for themselves a source of life. The matter is at once in endeavor to form itself. The scattered elements of denser kind gather, by means of attraction, from a sphere round about themselves all matter of lesser specific gravity; but they themselves, together with the matter they have united with themselves, gather in the points where the particles of still denser genus are to be found, these likewise into still denser ones and so on. If one thus follows this self-forming nature in thought through the whole space of the chaos, one easily becomes aware: that all sequels of this effect would in the end consist in the composition of various lumps, which after the completion of their formations would, through the equality of attraction, be at rest and forever unmoved.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0116":["But nature has still other forces in store, which express themselves chiefly when matter is dissolved into fine particles, as that whereby they repel one another and through their conflict with attraction bring forth that motion which is, as it were, a lasting life of nature. Through this force of repulsion, which reveals itself in the elasticity of vapors, the effluence of strong-smelling bodies and the diffusion of all spirituous matters, and which is an indisputable phenomenon of nature, the elements sinking toward their points of attraction are steered through one another sideways from the rectilinear motion, and the perpendicular fall turns out into circular motions that encompass the center point of the sinking. In order to comprehend distinctly the formation of the world-structure, we want to restrict our consideration from the infinite sum total of nature to a particular system, such as this one belonging to our sun. After we have weighed the generation of the same, we shall in a similar way proceed to the origin of the higher world-orders, and be able to grasp the infinity of the whole creation together in one doctrinal concept.",["(Notes for all three passages follow; the format asks for one block, so they are given together.)","- 0114: \"Materien\" rendered \"matters\" (plural of Materie); \"Grundstoff\" = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material). \"Weltgebäude\" = \"world-edifice\"; \"Weltbaues\" (0116) = \"world-structure.\"","- 0114: \"Absicht\" = \"aim\" in \"ohne Absicht auf ein System\" and \"intention\" in \"ohne Absicht scheinen entworfen\"; both are German Absicht.","- 0114: \"Folge\" = \"sequel\"; \"Vollkommenheit\" = \"perfection\"; \"Verstand\" = \"understanding\"; \"Idee\" = \"idea\"; \"Merkmal\" = \"mark.\"","- 0114: \"Arten\" rendered \"ways\" (glossary: Art = way) and \"Gattungen\" = \"species\" in the first occurrence, then \"genus\" in 0115; Art and Gattung are distinct in German. Glossary lists Art = species too; \"Gattung\" is given as \"genus\" in glossary, but \"species\" was used in 0114 for consistency of sense with \"Gattungen der Elemente\"; this is a departure from the glossary and should be revised to \"genera\" if strict uniformity is wanted.","- 0114: \"inden\" in the source is an OCR slip, read as \"in den.\" \"kan\" = \"can.\"","- 0114: the last sentence is syntactically strained (\"also jene Art ... um einen so viel grössern Abstand ... als diese\"); the comparison is kept as in the German. \"Dichtigkeit\" = \"density\"; \"Anziehungskraft\" = \"force of attraction.\"","- 0115: \"Folgen\" = \"sequels\"; \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\"; \"Kräfte\" = \"forces\"; \"Bestrebung\" = \"endeavor\"; \"Qvelle\" = \"source.\"","- 0116: \"Lehrbegriff\" = \"doctrinal concept\"; \"Betrachtung\" = \"consideration\"; \"begreifen\" = \"comprehend\"; \"deutlich\" = \"distinct\"; \"Inbegriff\" = \"sum total\"; \"besonder\" = \"particular\"; \"Phänomenon\" = \"phenomenon\" (Latinate/Greek form retained)."]],"naturgeschichte-0117":["If accordingly a point is located in a very large space, where the attraction of the elements located there works more strongly than everywhere around it; then the basic material of elementary particles, spread out in the whole domain, will sink toward this one. The first effect of this general sinking is the formation of a body in this center of attraction, which, so to speak, grows on from an infinitely small germ in rapid degrees, but in just the measure as this mass increases, also moves the surrounding parts to its union with stronger force. When the mass of this central body has grown so far that the velocity with which it draws the particles from great distances to itself, bent sideways by the weak degrees of repulsion with which these hinder one another, issues in lateral motions that are in a position to encompass the central body in a circle by means of the centrifugal force: then great vortices of particles are generated, each of which describes curved lines for itself through the composition of the attracting force and the sideways-directed force of revolution; which kinds of circles all intersect one another, for which their great dispersion in this space leaves room. Meanwhile these motions, striving against one another in many ways, are naturally endeavoring to bring one another to equality, that is, into a state in which one motion is as little as possible a hindrance to the other. This happens first, in that the particles restrict one another's motion until all proceed in one direction; second, in that the particles restrict their vertical motion, by means of which they approach the center of attraction, until they, all moved horizontally, i.e. in parallel-running circles around the sun as their center, no longer cross one another, and through the equality of the force of whirling with the sinking one always maintain themselves in free circular courses at the height where they hover; so that finally only those particles remain hovering in the domain of the space which through their falling have gotten a velocity, and through the resistance of the others a direction, whereby they can continue a free circular motion. In this state, in which all particles run around the central body in one direction and in parallel-running circles, namely in free circular motions through the acquired forces of whirling, the strife and the convergence of the elements is lifted, and everything is in the state of the smallest interaction. This is the natural sequel into which a matter that is caught up in strife-ridden motions always puts itself. It is thus clear that from the dispersed multitude of the particles a great multitude must, through the resistance by which they seek to bring one another into this state, arrive at such exactness of determinations; although a still much greater multitude does not arrive at it, and serves only to increase the lump of the central body, into which they sink, in that they cannot maintain themselves freely at the height in which they hover, but cross the circles of the lower ones and finally lose all motion through their resistance. This body in the center of attraction, which in consequence of this has become the main piece of the planetary edifice through the multitude of its gathered matter, is the sun, although it does not then yet have that flaming glow which after fully completed formation breaks forth on its surface.",["- Grundstoff = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material); Materie = matter; Umfang = domain; Menge = multitude.","- Wirkung = effect; wirket = \"works\"; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Folge = sequel; Zustand = state.","- Kraft = force throughout (Centerfliehkraft = centrifugal force, Schwungskraft = force of whirling, Umwendungskraft = force of revolution).","- Bestimmungen = determinations; Raum = space; Attraction (Latinate, roman) rendered \"attraction\", as is Anziehung; both = \"attraction\".","- \"worden ist\" (without \"ge-\") rendered \"has become\"; the German omits the participle's prefix as printed.","- \"welche Arten von Kreisen\": Art = \"kinds\" here."]],"naturgeschichte-0118":["It is still to be remarked: that, in that thus all elements of nature in formation, as proved, move in one direction around the center of the sun, in such revolutions directed toward a single region, which occur as it were on a common axis, the rotation of the fine matter cannot subsist in this way; because according to the laws of central motion all revolutions with the plane of their circles must intersect the center of attraction; but among all these circles, running around a common axis in one direction, there is only a single one that intersects the center of the sun, whence all matter from both sides of this axis drawn in thought hastens to that circle which goes through the axis of rotation precisely in the center of the common sinking; which circle is the plane of relation of all elements hovering around, about which they heap up as much as possible, and by contrast leave empty the regions distant from this surface; for those which cannot come so near to this surface, toward which everything crowds, will not always be able to maintain themselves in the places where they hover, but, in that they collide with the elements hovering around, will occasion their final fall to the sun.",["- bestehen = \"subsist\" (not in the glossary; beharren = persist is kept for that word).","- Gesetze = laws; Plan = \"plane\" (German \"Plan\" for both plane and the \"plane of relation\"); Beziehung = relation.","- \"nach einer einzigen Gegend gerichteten\" = \"directed toward a single region\"; Gegend could also be \"direction\" in sense, left as \"region\".","- \"in Gedanken gezogenen\" = \"drawn in thought\"; the clause \"um welchen ... häufen\" has \"welchen\" referring to the circle or the plane (the German is ambiguous; I follow its order)."]],"naturgeschichte-0119":["If one therefore weighs this hovering-around basic material of world-matter in such a state in which it puts itself through attraction and through a mechanical result of the general laws of resistance; then we see a space, which is comprised between two surfaces not far distant from one another, in whose middle the general plane of relation is found, extended from the center of the sun on into unknown distances, in which all comprised particles, each according to its height and the attraction that rules there, perform measured circular motions in free revolutions, and therefore, in that in such a disposition they hinder one another as little as possible any more, would always remain therein, if the attraction of these particles of the basic material among one another did not then begin to do its effect and thereby occasion new formations, which are to be the seed of planets that are to arise. For, in that the elements moved around the sun in parallel circles, taken at a not too great difference of distance from the sun, are, through the equality of parallel motion, almost in respective rest toward one another; so the attraction of the elements located there, of superior specific attraction, at once does here a considerable effect (*), to begin the collection of the nearest particles for the formation of a body which, according to the measure of the growth of its lump, extends its attraction further, and moves the elements from a wide domain to its composition.",["- Verfassung = \"disposition\" (not in the glossary; distinct from Beschaffenheit = constitution).","- Weltmaterie = \"world-matter\" (Welt = world); Erfolg = \"result\"; Widerstand = resistance.","- \"Saame\" (Samen) = \"seed\"; \"Bildungen\" = \"formations\".","- \"begriffene Theilchen\" = \"comprised particles\" (begreifen here in the sense of included, not \"comprehend\").","- \"Maaßgebung\" = \"according to\"; \"Maasse\" = \"measure\"; \"respectiven Ruhe\" = \"respective rest\".","- The asterisk marks the author's footnote (next passage)."]],"naturgeschichte-0120":["(*) The beginning of the planets in formation is not to be sought in Newtonian attraction alone. This would be far too slow and weak in a particle of such exceptional fineness. One would rather say that in this space the first formation occurs through the convergence of some elements that unite through the ordinary laws of interconnection, until that lump which has arisen from them has gradually grown so far that the Newtonian force of attraction has become capable in it of ever more enlarging it through its effect at a distance.",["- Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Zusammenlauf = \"convergence\"; Anziehungskraft = \"force of attraction\".","- \"vermögend geworden\" = \"has become capable\" (vermögend, from Vermögen); \"an ihm\" = \"in it\" (could also be \"on it\").","- \"durch seine Wirkung in die Ferne\" = \"through its effect at a distance\"; \"seine\" refers to the force (or the lump) ambiguously in the German, retained."]],"naturgeschichte-0121":["The formation of the planets, in this system, has this advantage over every possible doctrinal concept: that the origin of the masses at the same time presents the origin of the motions and the position of the circles in just the same point of time; indeed, that even the deviations from the greatest exactness in these determinations, just as much as the agreements themselves, become clear in one view. The planets form themselves from the particles, which at the height at which they hover have exact motions toward circular circles: thus the masses composed out of them will continue just the same motions, in just the same degree, in just the same direction. This is enough to see why the motion of the planets is approximately circular-shaped, and why their circles are on one surface. They would also be quite exact circles (**), if the breadth from which they gather the elements for their formation were very small, and thus the difference of their motions were very slight. But since a wide domain belongs to it, to form a dense lump of a planet out of the fine basic material, which is so greatly scattered in the heavenly space; so the difference of the distances which these elements have from the sun, and consequently also the difference of their velocities, is no longer negligible, hence it would be necessary that, in order to preserve for the planet, given this difference of the motions, the equality of the central forces and the circular velocity, the particles that come together on it from different heights with different motions should exactly replace, one the lack of the other, which, although it does in fact happen fairly exactly (*), nevertheless, since something is lacking in this perfect replacement, draws after it the shortfall in the circular motion and the eccentricity. It is just as easily clear that, although the circles of all the planets should properly be on one surface, nevertheless in this respect too a small deviation is to be met with, because, as already mentioned, the elementary particles, although they find themselves as near as possible to the general plane of reference of their motions, nevertheless enclose some space on both sides of it; whereupon it would be far too fortunate a chance if precisely all the planets were to begin to form themselves quite exactly in the middle between these two sides, in the plane of reference itself, which then already occasions some inclination of their circles toward one another, although the striving of the particles from both sides to restrict this deviation as much as possible allows it only narrow bounds. One need not therefore wonder to meet here, too, the greatest exactness of the determinations so little, as in all things of nature, because on the whole the multiplicity of the circumstances that take part in every constitution of nature does not permit a measured regularity.",["- *Lehrbegriff* rendered \"doctrinal concept\" (Lehre = doctrine, Begriff = concept); *Bestimmungen* = determinations.","- *Zirkelkreise* rendered \"circular circles\"; *Kreis* = \"circle\" throughout; *Fläche* = \"surface\".","- *Umfang* = \"domain\" (glossary); *Grundstoff* = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material).","- *Geschwindigkeit* = \"velocity\"; *Centralkräfte* = \"central forces\" (Kraft = force).","- *Umstände* = \"circumstances\"; *Naturbeschaffenheit* = \"constitution of nature\" (Beschaffenheit = constitution).","- Footnote markers (**) and (*) are kept where they stand in the text; the order is as printed. \"Whereupon\" renders *da es denn*; the sense of \"es\" (impersonal) is left as in the German.","- *Zusammenkommen* / *versammlen* both occur; rendered \"come together\" / \"gather\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0122":["(**) This measured circular motion properly concerns only the planets near the sun: for of the great distances at which the most remote planets or also the comets have formed, it is easy to presume that, because the sinking motion of the basic material is there much weaker, and the extensiveness of the spaces in which it is scattered is also greater, the elements there in and of themselves already deviate from the circle-equal motion, and must thereby be the cause of the bodies formed from them.",["- *an und vor sich* rendered \"in and of themselves\"; *zirkelgleich* = \"circle-equal\"; *Weitläuftigkeit* = \"extensiveness\".","- The sentence's last clause is ambiguous as in German: \"the cause of the bodies\" (*die Ursache der ... Körper*) could be read as cause of their (deviating) character or of their existence; left as it stands."]],"naturgeschichte-0123":["(*) For the particles from the region nearer to the sun, which have a greater velocity of revolution than is required for circular motion at the place where they gather on the planet, replace that which is lacking in velocity in the particles more distant from the sun, which incorporate themselves into just the same body, in order to run in a circle at the distance of the planet.",["- *Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit* = \"velocity of revolution\"; *Abstand* = \"distance\"; *Entfernung* = \"distance\" (one English word for two German words).","- *einverleiben* = \"incorporate\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0124":["Second Chapter,\non the\ndifferent density of the planets,\nand the relation of their\nmasses.",["- *Hauptstück* = \"chapter\"; *Dichtigkeit* = \"density\"; *Verhältnis* = \"relation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0125":["We have shown that the particles of the elementary basic material, since in and of themselves they were equally distributed in the world-space, in their sinking down to the sun remained hovering in the places where the velocity they had attained in falling just furnished the equality with the attraction, and their direction was bent, as it ought to be in circular motion, perpendicular to the circular radius. If we now, however, think of particles of different specific density at equal distance from the sun, then those of greater specific gravity press deeper through the resistance of the others toward the sun, and are not so soon bent away from their path as the lighter ones; hence their motion becomes circular-shaped only at a greater approach to the sun. On the other hand the elements of lighter kind, bent away sooner from the rectilinear fall, will turn out into circular motions before they have pressed so deep through toward the center, and thus remain hovering at greater distances, and also cannot press so deep through the filled space of the elements without their motion being weakened by the resistance of these, and without being able to attain the great degrees of velocity that are required for the turning-around nearer the midpoint; thus, after the equality of the motions has been attained, the specifically lighter particles will revolve at wider distances from the sun, the heavier, however, will be met with in the nearer ones, and the planets that form themselves out of them will therefore be of denser kind, which form themselves nearer to the sun than those that form themselves farther from it out of the concourse of these atoms.",["- *Weltraum* = \"world-space\" (Welt = world, Raum = space); *Zirkelstrahl* = \"circular radius\"; *Mittelpunkt* = \"midpoint\"; *Centrum* = \"center\".","- *Art* = \"kind\" (glossary lists way, species, kind, manner); *Gattung* is \"kind\" in 0126 (see there); glossary \"genus\" not used.","- The final relative clause (\"which form themselves nearer to the sun\") is ambiguous as in German between planets and the denser kind; preserved.","- *Grad* = \"degree\"; *Widerstand* = \"resistance\"; *Zusammenlauf* = \"concourse\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0126":["It is therefore a kind of a statical law that determines for the matters of the world-space their heights, according to the inverse relation of density. Nevertheless it is just as easy to comprehend: that not precisely every height must be occupied only by particles of equal specific density. Of the particles of a certain specific genus, those remain hovering at greater distances from the sun, and attain the moderation of their fall required for constant circular motion at a wider distance, which have sunk down to it from greater distances; whereas those whose original place, in the general distribution of the matters in chaos, was nearer to the sun, notwithstanding their no greater density, will come nearer to its circle of revolution. And since therefore the places of the matters, with regard to the midpoint of their sinking, are determined not alone by their specific gravity, but also by their original places, at the first rest of nature: so it is easy to deem that very different genera of them will come together at every distance from the sun, in order to remain suspended there, but that on the whole the denser matters will be met with more frequently toward the midpoint than farther away from it; and that therefore, notwithstanding that the planets will be a mixture of very diverse matters, nevertheless on the whole their masses must be denser, in the measure that they are nearer to the sun, and of lesser density, the greater their distance is.",["- *Gattung* = \"genus\" (glossary); *Art* = \"kind\"; *Materien* = \"matters\" (Materie = matter).","- *begreifen* = \"comprehend\"; *Gesetz* = \"law\"; *Höhe* = \"height\"; *Ort/Oerter/Platz* = \"place\" (Ort, Platz both \"place\").","- *Senkung* = \"sinking\"; *Ruhe* = \"rest\"; *erachten* = \"deem\"; *Abstand* and *Entfernung* both \"distance\".","- *Ansehung* in \"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0127":["In regard to this law of their densities prevailing among the planets, our system shows a preeminent perfection over all those concepts that have been made of its cause, or could yet be made. Newton, who had determined the density of some planets by calculation, believed he found the cause of their relation, arranged according to distance, in the propriety of God's choice and in the motives of his final end; because the planets nearer to the sun must endure more heat from it, and the more distant ones are to make do with fewer degrees of warmth; which does not seem to be possible if the planets near to the sun were not of a denser kind, and the more distant ones composed of lighter matter. But to see the insufficiency of such an explanation does not require very much reflection. A planet, e.g. our earth, is composed of genera of matter very widely different from one another; among these it was now necessary that the lighter ones, which are penetrated and moved more by the equal effect of the sun, whose composition has a relation to the warmth with which its rays work, had to be spread out on the surface; but that the mixture of the remaining matters, in the whole of the lump, must have this relation, is not at all evident from this; because the sun has no effect at all on the interior of the planets. Newton feared that if the earth were sunk in the rays of the sun to the proximity of Mercury, it might burn like a comet, and its matter would not have enough fire-resistance not to be dispersed by this heat. But, how much more must the sun's own matter itself, which is yet 4 times lighter than that of which the earth consists, be destroyed by this glow; or why is the moon twice as dense as the earth, when it yet floats with it at just the same distance from the sun. One can therefore not ascribe the proportioned densities to the relation to the sun's warmth without entangling oneself in the greatest contradictions. One sees rather that a cause which distributes the places of the planets according to the density of their lump must have had a relation to the interior of their matter, and not to their surface; that it must, notwithstanding this sequence which it determined, still permit a difference of matter in just the same heavenly body, and only in the whole of the composition fix this relation of density; whether any other statical law, than the one which is set forth in our doctrinal framework, could do justice to all this, I leave to the insight of the reader to judge.",["- Dichtigkeit = density; Gattung = genus (plural \"genera\"); Art = kind (in \"dichterer Art\" and \"leichterer Art\"); Gesetz = law.","- Bewegungsgrund = motive; Endzweck = \"final end\" (Zweck = end); Erklärung = explanation; Begriff = concept; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Wirkung = effect; Beziehung = relation; Verhältniß = relation (also \"Verhältniß\" in \"proportioned densities\"; both rendered \"relation\").","- \"Folge\" in \"ohnerachtet dieser Folge\" = \"sequence\" (glossary); the antecedent is ambiguous in the German and left so.","- \"Lehrverfassung\" = \"doctrinal framework\" (not in glossary); \"Lehrbegriff\" in the next passage is rendered \"doctrinal concept\"/\"theory\" nowhere; see there.","- \"Der Sonnen eigene Materie\" is rendered \"the sun's own matter\"; \"Zusammensatz\" = \"composition\"; \"Klumpen\" = \"lump\"; \"Ganze\" = \"whole\".","- \"Anständigkeit\" = \"propriety\" (doubtful sense: decorum/fitness); \"Feuerbeständigkeit\" = \"fire-resistance\" (literally \"fire-constancy\").","- \"ob irgend ein anderes ... Gnüge leisten könne\" is a long dependent clause preceding its main verb \"überlasse\"; word order kept. \"Gnüge\" = \"justice\" (as in \"do justice\"), i.e. \"Genüge leisten\".","- \"Befurchte\" (sic) = \"befürchtete\"; \"musten\", \"Oerter\" modernized in sense only."]],"naturgeschichte-0128":["The relation among the densities of the planets carries with it yet another circumstance which, through a complete agreement with the explanation previously sketched, confirms the correctness of our doctrinal concept. The heavenly body that stands in the midpoint of other spheres running around it is commonly of a lighter kind than the body that runs nearest around it. The earth with regard to the moon, and the sun with regard to the earth, show such a relation of their densities. According to the sketch that we have laid out, such a constitution is necessary. For, since the lower planets were formed chiefly from the discharge of the elementary matters, which through the preeminence of their density could penetrate to such proximity to the midpoint with the required degree of velocity; whereas the body in the midpoint itself was heaped together, without distinction, from the matters of all the genera present, which had not attained their lawful motions, among which, since the lighter matters make up the greatest part, it is easy to see that, because the nearest heavenly body or bodies circling to the midpoint comprise as it were a segregation of denser sorts, but the central body a mixture of all without distinction, the former's substance will be of a denser kind than this one's. In fact the moon too is 2 times denser than the earth, and this 4 times denser than the sun, which by all conjecture will be surpassed in still higher degrees of density by the yet lower ones, Venus and Mercury.",["- Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\" (Begriff = concept); Entwurf = \"sketch\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\".","- Himmelskörper = \"heavenly body\"; Mittelpunkt = \"midpoint\"; Gattung = \"genus\" (plural \"genera\"); Art = \"kind\"; Sorten = \"sorts\".","- \"Ausschuß\" = \"discharge\" (also \"refuse/rejects\"; doubtful sense preserved as one word).","- The sentence beginning \"Denn, da...\" has no main verb for the first \"da\" clause until \"es leicht einzusehen ist\"; the German anacoluthon is kept. \"jenes\"/\"dieser\" are left as pronouns (\"the former's\"/\"this one's\"), their antecedents being ambiguous in the German.","- \"der noch tieferen\" = \"the yet lower ones\"; \"tiefer\" is literally \"deeper/lower\" (nearer the sun), left as such.","- \"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\"; \"Dichtigkeit\" = \"density\"; \"Grad\" = \"degree\"; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0129":["Now our attention turns to the relation which the masses of the heavenly bodies are supposed to have according to our doctrinal concept, in comparison with their distances, in order to test the result of our system against the unerring calculations of Newton. It needs not many words to make comprehensible: that the central body must at all times be the principal piece of its system, consequently the sun in a preeminent way greater in mass than the planets taken together; as this will also hold of Jupiter, with regard to its secondary planets, and of Saturn, in consideration of its own. The central body forms itself from the sediment of all particles, from the whole domain of its sphere of attraction, which could not obtain the most exact determination of the circular motion, and the close relation to the common plane, and of which, without doubt, there must be an uncommonly greater multitude than of the latter. To apply this consideration to the sun in particular: if one wants to estimate the breadth of the space by which the particles circling in circles, which served the planets as basic material, deviated farthest from the common plane: one can assume it to be approximately somewhat greater than the breadth of the greatest deviation of the planetary circles from one another. Now, however, as they stray from the common plane to both sides, their greatest inclination toward one another amounts to scarcely 7½ degrees. Thus one can represent all the matter from which the planets have formed as having been spread out in that space which was comprehended between two planes, proceeding from the midpoint of the sun, that enclosed an angle of 7½ degrees. Now, however, a zone of 7½ degrees' breadth, going along the direction of the greatest circle, is somewhat more than the 17th part of the spherical surface, thus the bodily space between the two planes, which cut out the spherical space in the breadth of the aforementioned angle, is somewhat more than the 17th part of the bodily content of the whole sphere. Thus, in accordance with this hypothesis, all the matter that was applied to the formation of the planets would make up approximately the seventeenth part of that matter which the sun has collected, from both sides, for its composition, from just the distance at which the outermost planet stands. But this central body has a preeminence of lump over the total content of all the planets that is not as 17 : 1 to this, but as 650 to 1, as Newton's calculation determines it; but it is also easy to see that in the upper spaces above Saturn, where the planetary formations either cease or are at least rare, where only a few cometary bodies have formed, and where especially the motions of the basic material, since they are not there so apt to arrive at the lawful equality of the central forces as in the region near the center, turn out only into an almost general sinking toward the midpoint, and increase the sun with all the matter from such widely extended spaces, so that, I say, from these causes the sun's lump must obtain so preeminent a magnitude of mass.",["- Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\"; Begriff = concept; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Menge = \"multitude\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Grundstoff = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material).","- Fläche = \"plane\" in \"gemeinschaftliche Fläche\" and \"surface\" would suit \"Kugelfläche\"; both rendered \"plane\"/\"spherical surface\" as the context gives; Flächen = \"planes\".","- Masse = \"mass\"; Klumpen = \"lump\"; Innhalt = \"content\" (Inhalt); \"körperlicher Raum\" = \"bodily space\"; Raum = \"space\".","- \"der letzteren\" = \"the latter\" (antecedent ambiguous in the German: planets or the planetary particles); left so.","- \"Zone ... nach der Richtung des größten Zirkels\" kept literal; Zirkel = \"circle\", \"Kreise\" = \"circles\".","- \"Vorzug des Klumpens\" = \"preeminence of lump\"; \"vorzüglich\" = \"preeminent\"; \"Centralkräfte\" = \"central forces\".","- The final clause \"daß, sage ich, ...\" resumes an earlier \"daß\" after a long interruption; the long sentence is kept whole.","- \"Senkung\" = \"sinking\"; \"ausschlagen\" = \"turn out\" (doubtful sense: \"result in\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0130":["But in order to compare the planets with one another with regard to their masses: we notice first, that according to the indicated way of formation the quantity of the matter that enters into the composition of a planet depends chiefly on the distance of its remove from the sun: 1) because the sun, through its attraction, limits the sphere of attraction of a planet, but under equal circumstances does not limit that of the more distant one as narrowly as that of the near one: 2) because the circles, out of which all the particles came together to constitute a planet, are described with a greater radius, and therefore contain more basic material than the smaller circles: 3) because for the very same last ground the breadth between the two planes of greatest deviation, with an equal number of degrees, is greater at great heights than at small ones. On the other hand, this advantage of the more distant planets over the lower ones will indeed be limited by the fact that the particles nearer to the sun will be of a denser kind, and by all appearances also less dispersed than at a greater distance; but one can easily gauge that the former advantages for the formation of great masses nevertheless far exceed the latter limitations, and that in general the planets that form at a wide distance from the sun must receive greater masses than the near ones. This happens, then, insofar as one represents the formation of a planet only as in the presence of the sun; but if one lets several planets form at different distances, then one will limit the extent of the attraction of the other through its sphere of attraction, and this brings about an exception to the previous law. For that planet which is near another of exceptional mass will lose a very great deal of the sphere of its formation, and thereby become incomparably smaller than the relation of its distance from the sun alone requires. Although, therefore, on the whole the planets are of greater mass the farther they are removed from the sun, as in general Saturn and Jupiter, as the two principal pieces of our system, are the greatest for the reason that they are most distant from the sun: there nevertheless are found deviations from this analogy, in which, however, the mark of the general formation that we assert of the heavenly bodies always shines forth: that, namely, a planet of exceptional magnitude deprives the nearest ones on both sides of the mass due to them on account of their distance from the sun, in that it appropriates to itself a part of the materials that were to come to the formation of those. In fact Mars, which by virtue of its place should be greater than the earth, has lost in its mass through the force of attraction of the so great Jupiter near it; and Saturn himself, although through his height he has a precedence over Mars, has nevertheless not been entirely exempt from suffering a considerable loss through Jupiter's attraction, and it seems to me that Mercury has the exceptional smallness of its mass to thank not only to the attraction of the so mighty sun near it, but also to the neighborhood of Venus, which, if one compares its presumable density with its magnitude, must be a planet of considerable mass.",["- *Qvantität* = \"quantity\"; *Attraction* and *Anziehung* are both rendered \"attraction\"; *Anziehungskraft* = \"force of attraction\"; *Anziehungssphäre* = \"sphere of attraction\".","- *Weite seiner Entfernung* rendered \"distance of its remove\" to keep both nouns; *Sonnenweite* = \"distance from the sun\"; *Abstand* and *Entfernung* both \"distance\".","- *Grund* in \"letzten Grunde\" = \"ground\"; *Grundstoff* = \"basic material\" (not in glossary; *Stoff* = material).","- *Zusammensatz* = \"composition\"; *Bildung* = \"formation\"; *Masse* = \"mass\"; *Grösse* = \"magnitude\" (also in \"ein Planet von grösserer Masse\").","- Doubtful syntax: \"der, ihnen wegen ihrer Sonnenweite, gebührenden Masse beraubet\" is followed literally; \"indem er einen Theil der Materien sich zueignet, die zu jener ihrer Bildung kommen solten\" – \"jener\" is left as \"those\", referring to the nearest planets.","- The sentence \"wenn man mehrere Planeten ... einer den Umfang der Attraction des andern ... einschränken\" is kept as is: \"one ... the other\" is ambiguous in the German as in the English; *Umfang* = \"extent\" here (glossary: domain).","- *Merkmal* = \"mark\"; *behaupten* = \"assert\"; *Verhältniß* = \"relation\"; *Analogie* = \"analogy\".","- *Vorzug* = \"advantage\" in the first occurrence, \"precedence\" in the second; *Hauptstücke* = \"principal pieces\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0131":["Since now everything agrees as splendidly as one could wish, to confirm the sufficiency of a mechanical doctrinal system for the origin of the structure of the world and of the heavenly bodies; so we wish, in that we estimate the space in which the basic material of the planets was spread out before their formation, to consider in what degree of thinness this middle space was then filled, and with what freedom, or how few hindrances, the particles floating about could have carried out their lawful motions therein. If the space that comprehended all matter of the planets was contained in that part of the Saturnian sphere which, from the center of the sun, was comprehended between two planes standing apart from one another by 7 degrees at all heights, and was therefore the seventeenth part of the whole sphere that one can describe with the radius of the height of Saturn; so we wish, in order to calculate the alteration of the planetary basic material when it filled this space, only to set the height of Saturn at 100000 earth diameters; then the whole sphere of the Saturnian circle will exceed the cubic content of the globe of the earth 1000 *Bimillion* times; of which, if instead of the seventeenth part we take even only the twentieth, the space in which the elementary basic material floated must nevertheless exceed the cubic content of the globe of the earth 50 *Bimillion* times. If one now sets the mass of all planets with their companions at ⅟650 of the lump of the sun according to Newton; then the earth, which is only ⅟169282 of the same, will relate to the total mass of all planetary matter as 1 to 276½; and if one therefore brought all this matter to the same specific density as the earth, there would arise from it a body that would take up 277½ times greater space than the earth. If we therefore assume the density of the earth in its whole lump to be not much greater than the density of the solid matter that is encountered beneath its uppermost surface: as indeed the properties of the figure of the earth require nothing else, and set these upper materials at about 4 or 5 times denser than water, but water 1000 times heavier than air; then the matter of all planets, if they were extended to the thinness of air, would take up an almost 14 times hundred-thousand times greater space than the globe of the earth. This space, compared with the space in which, according to our presupposition, all matter of the planets was spread out, is thirty million times smaller than the same: therefore the dispersion of planetary matter in this space also constitutes an equally many times greater rarefaction than that which the particles of our atmosphere have. In fact, this magnitude of dispersion, however incredible it may seem, was nevertheless neither unnecessary nor unnatural. It had to be as great as possible, in order to permit to the floating particles all freedom of motion, almost as in an empty space, and to diminish infinitely the resistance that they could offer one another; but they could also of themselves assume such a state of rarefaction, which one need not doubt if one knows a little the expansion that matter suffers when it is transformed into vapors; or if one, to remain with the heavens, considers the rarefaction of matter in the tails of the comets, which, with such an unheard-of thickness of their cross-section, which well exceeds the diameter of the earth a hundred times, are nevertheless so translucent that the small stars can be seen through them; which our air, when it is illuminated by the sun, does not permit at a height that is many thousand times smaller.",["- *Lehrverfassung* = \"doctrinal system\" (*Lehre* = doctrine); *Weltbaues* = \"structure of the world\"; *Grundstoff* = \"basic material\"; *Raumesinhalt* = \"cubic content\" (literally content of space).","- *Bimillionenmal* is a Kant coinage (10¹² times), left as *Bimillion*; *erwegen* = \"consider\"; *Betrachtung* not used.","- *Dünnigkeit* = \"thinness\"; *Verdünnung* = \"rarefaction\"; *Zerstreuung* = \"dispersion\"; *Ausbreitung* = \"expansion\"; *ausgebreitet* = \"spread out\".","- *Raum* = \"space\" throughout; *Mittelraum* = \"middle space\"; *Sphäre* = \"sphere\"; *Kreis* = \"circle\".","- Kept literally: the \"so ... wollen wir\" constructions after \"Indem\" and \"Wenn\" (German *so* apodoses), \"277½\" after \"1 zu 276½\", and \"14mal hunderttausendmal\" as printed; the German \"als die die Theilchen\" is read as \"als die [Verdünnung], die die Theilchen ... haben\".","- *Zustand* = \"state\"; *Voraussetzung* = \"presupposition\"; *Widerstand* = \"resistance\"; *gesetzmäßig* = \"lawful\".","- The final clause \"welches unsere Luft ... nicht verstattet\" is preserved with \"which\" ambiguous in reference as in the German (the translucency / the seeing of stars).","- \"Die Eigenschaften der Figur der Erde\" = \"the properties of the figure of the earth\"; *Eigenschaft* = \"property\" (as with *Beschaffenheit*, glossary)."]],"naturgeschichte-0132":["I conclude this chapter by adding an analogy that by and in itself alone can raise the present theory, of the mechanical formation of the heavenly bodies, above the probability of the hypothesis to a formal certainty. If the sun is composed of the particles of the same basic material from which the planets have formed: and if the difference consists only in this, that in the former the matters of all genera have been heaped together without distinction, but in these they have been distributed at different distances, according to the constitution of the density of their sorts; then, if one considers the matter of all the planets united together, in its whole mixture a density must result that is almost equal to the density of the body of the sun. Now this necessary consequence of our system finds a fortunate confirmation in the comparison that Herr von Buffon, this so worthily famous philosopher, has made between the densities of the entire planetary matter and that of the sun's; he found a similarity between the two, like that between 640 and 650. If unartificial and necessary consequences from a doctrinal framework meet with such fortunate confirmations in the actual relations of nature; can one then well believe that a mere chance occasions this agreement between theory and observation?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0133":["Third Chapter,\non the\nEccentricity of the Planetary Orbits, and the\nOrigin of the Comets.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0134":["One cannot make of the comets a particular genus of heavenly bodies that would differ entirely from the race of the planets. Nature works here, as elsewhere, through imperceptible gradations, and, in that it runs through all the steps of alterations, it links together, by means of a chain of intermediate members, the remote properties with the near ones. Eccentricity in the planets is a consequence of the deficiency in that striving through which nature endeavors to make the planetary motions exactly circle-like, which, however, because of the intervention of manifold circumstances, it can never fully attain, but yet deviates from it more at greater distances than at near ones.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0135":["This determination leads, by a constant ladder, by means of all possible steps of eccentricity, from the planets finally to the comets, and although this interconnection at Saturn seems to be cut off by a great chasm that entirely separates the cometary race from the planets; yet we have remarked in the first part that there may, presumably beyond Saturn, still be other planets which, through a greater deviation from the circular roundness of the orbits, come closer to the course of the comets, and that it lies only in the lack of observation, or also in the difficulty of it, that this kinship has not been presented to the eye as visibly as it was long ago presented to the understanding.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0136":["We have already cited in the first chapter of this part a cause that can make eccentric the course of a heavenly body that forms itself out of the basic material floating around, even if one assumes that this possesses in all its places forces exactly balanced for circular motion. For, because the planet collects them from heights standing far apart from one another, where the velocities of the circular courses are different; so they come together on it with different degrees of revolving motion dwelling in them, which deviate from the measure of velocity that is due to the distance of the planet, and thereby draw upon it an eccentricity insofar as these differing impressions of the particles fail to compensate fully for one another's deviation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0137":["If eccentricity had no other cause, it would be moderate everywhere: it would also be smaller in the small planets, distant from the sun, than in the near and great ones: namely if one presupposed that the particles of the basic material had actually had exact circular motions beforehand. Since now these determinations do not agree with observation, in that, as already remarked, eccentricity increases with distance from the sun, and the smallness of the masses seems rather to make an exception, toward increase of eccentricity, as we see in Mars; so we are compelled to restrict the hypothesis of the exact circular motion of the particles of the basic material to this, that we let them in the regions near the sun indeed come very near to this exactness of determination, but yet deviate from it the further, the more distantly these elementary little parts have floated from the sun. Such a moderation of the principle, of the free circle-like motion of the basic material, is more suited to nature. For, notwithstanding the thinness of space, which seems to leave them freedom to restrict one another to the point of the fully balanced equality of the central forces; the causes are nonetheless no less considerable to hinder this end of nature in its accomplishment. The further the extended parts of the original material are removed from the sun, the weaker is the force that brings them to sinking: the resistance of the lower parts, which bend their fall sideways, and are to compel it to set its direction perpendicular to the circular radius, diminishes in proportion as these sink away beneath it, in order either to incorporate themselves into the sun, or to institute revolutions in nearer regions. The specifically superior lightness of this higher matter does not permit them to institute the sinking motion, which is the ground of everything, with the emphasis that is required in order to bring the resisting particles to yield; and perhaps these distant particles still restrict one another, in order after a long period finally to attain this uniformity; so small masses have already formed among them, as beginnings of as many heavenly bodies, which, in that they collect themselves from weakly moved material, have only an eccentric motion, with which they sink toward the sun, and on the way are more and more, through the incorporation of more swiftly moved parts, bent away from the perpendicular fall, but nevertheless finally remain comets, if those spaces in which they have formed have become cleared and empty, through sinking down to the sun, or through gathering in particular clumps. This is the cause of the eccentricities, increasing with the distances from the sun, of the planets and of those heavenly bodies that are called comets for the reason that in this property they preeminently surpass the former. There are indeed still two exceptions that interrupt the law of the eccentricity increasing with the distance from the sun, which one perceives in the two smallest planets of our system, in Mars and Mercury; but in the former the neighborhood of the so great Jupiter is presumably the cause, who, in that by his attraction on his side he deprives Mars of particles for formation, leaves it chiefly only room to expand toward the sun, and thereby draws upon it a preponderance of the central force and of eccentricity. But as for Mercury, the lowest but also the most eccentric among the planets; it is easy to judge that, because the sun in its axial rotation still comes nowhere near equaling the velocity of Mercury, the resistance that it offers to the matter of the space surrounding it will not only deprive the nearest little parts of their central motion; but can also easily extend this resistance as far as Mercury, and will thereby have considerably diminished its velocity of revolution.",["- Gattung rendered \"genus\" throughout (also Geschlecht = \"race\", Sorten = \"sorts\"); Art not occurring.","- Grundstoff = \"basic material\"; Urstoff = \"original material\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Stoff = \"material\" (Grundstoff, Stoff and Urstoff differ in German).","- Wahrscheinlichkeit = \"probability\"; Gewißheit = \"certainty\"; Beobachtung = \"observation\"; Folgerung = \"consequence\"; Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal framework\"; Hypothese = \"hypothesis\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\".","- Eindruck = \"impression\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Centralkraft = \"central force\"; Zweck = \"end\".","- 0137: \"kan man denn wohl glauben\" in 0132 retains the question form; \"Ungefehr\" rendered \"chance\".","- 0137: \"um deswillen Cometen genannt werden\" — the Latin-derived \"Cometen\" and \"Planeten\" are rendered \"comets\" and \"planets\"; \"Zirkelgleich\" = \"circle-like\", a coinage kept literal.","- 0137: \"und vielleicht, daß diese entfernete Partikeln einander noch einschränken … so haben sich\" — the construction is irregular in German; the English preserves the loose syntax rather than repairing it. \"einander ... einschränken\" could be read as \"restrict one another\" (as rendered) or \"limit themselves to one another\".","- 0137: \"Ueberwucht\" rendered \"preponderance\"; \"Nachdrucke\" rendered \"emphasis\" (literally \"pressure behind\"), the sense being doubtful. \"Abfälle\" in 0134 = \"gradations\" (literally \"fallings-off\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0138":["Eccentricity is the foremost distinguishing mark of the comets. Their atmospheres and tails, which, upon their great approach to the sun, spread out through the heat, are only sequels of the former, although at the times of ignorance they served, as unaccustomed bugbears, to proclaim imagined fates to the rabble. The astronomers, who show more attention to the laws of motion than to the strangeness of the shape, remark a second property that distinguishes the genus of the comets from the planets, namely that they do not, like these, bind themselves to the zone of the zodiac, but carry out their revolutions freely in all regions of the heavens. This peculiarity has one and the same cause with the eccentricity. If the planets have enclosed their orbits in the narrow district of the zodiac for the reason that the elementary matter near the sun acquires circular motions, which at each revolution are endeavoring to cross the plane of reference, and do not let the body once formed deviate from this surface, toward which all matter presses from both sides: then the basic material of the spaces lying far from the central point, which, weakly moved by attraction, cannot attain to the free circular revolution, must, for just this cause that brings forth the eccentricity, not be capable of heaping itself at this height toward the plane of reference of all planetary motions, in order to maintain the bodies formed there, principally in this track: rather will the scattered basic material, since it has no restriction to a particular region, as with the lower planets, form itself into heavenly bodies equally easily on one side as on the other, and just as frequently far from the plane of reference as near it. Hence the comets will come down to us with all unboundedness from all regions: but yet those whose first place of formation is not raised far above the orbits of the planets will prove less deviation from the limitations of their path, just as well as less eccentricity. With the distances from the central point of the system this lawless freedom of the comets increases, with regard to their deviations, and loses itself in the depth of the heavens in a complete lack of revolution, which leaves the outer bodies that are forming freely to their fall to the sun, and sets the last boundaries to the systematic constitution.",["- Folge = \"sequels\" (glossary); Schranken = \"limitations\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\" (distinct German words kept distinct).","- Grundstoff rendered \"basic material\" (not in glossary); Stoff elsewhere \"material\"; Materie = \"matter\".","- Beziehungsplan = \"plane of reference\"; Verfassung = \"constitution\" (the same English word as for Beschaffenheit in the glossary).","- \"der erstern\" (the former) is left without a stated antecedent, as in the German; \"Cirkelbewegungen\" = \"circular motions\"; Attraction kept as a loanword.","- \"Plan\" in \"Plan der Beziehung\" and \"Plane\" read as the geometrical plane, per the context; the German spelling is the same."]],"naturgeschichte-0139":["I presuppose, in this sketch of the cometary motions, that, with regard to their direction, they will for the most part have it in common with that of the planets. With the near comets this seems to me beyond doubt, and this uniformity also cannot lose itself in the depth of the heavens sooner than there where the elementary basic material, in the greatest languor of motion, arranges the rotation arising perhaps through the sinking down toward all sorts of regions, because the time that is required, through the community of the lower motions, to make them agree in direction is, on account of the width of the distance, too long for it to be able to extend that far in the meantime, while the formation of nature is being carried out in the lower region. There will therefore perhaps be comets that will carry out their revolution toward the opposite side, namely from morning toward evening; although for reasons that I hesitate to adduce here I would almost like to persuade myself that, of the 19 comets in which one has remarked this peculiarity, in some perhaps an optical illusion may have given occasion for it.",["- \"selbige\" (it, i.e., the direction) and \"der Planeten ihrer\" (that of the planets) are rendered literally; the dialectal genitive is kept as \"that of the planets\".","- Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; überreden = \"persuade\"; Bewegungen = \"motions\".","- \"als daß sie indessen, daß ...\" is a difficult construction; the antecedent of \"sie\" (the time/the agreement) is left as ambiguous as in the German, and \"bis dahin\" is rendered \"that far\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0140":["I must remark something more about the masses of the comets, and about the density of their material. By rights, in the upper regions of the formation of these heavenly bodies, on the grounds adduced in the previous chapter, ever greater masses should form, in the measure that the distance increases. And it is also to be believed that some comets are greater than Saturn and Jupiter; but it is just not to be believed that this magnitude of the masses increases so without limit. The scattering of the basic material, the specific lightness of its particles, make the formation in the remotest region of cosmic space slow; the indeterminate spreading of the same, in the whole immeasurable domain of this width, without a determination to heap itself toward a certain surface, permits, instead of a single considerable formation, many smaller ones, and the lack of central force draws the greatest part of the particles down to the sun, without having assembled itself into masses.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Masse/Maass distinguished as \"mass\" and \"measure\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\".","- \"so immer zunimmt\" rendered \"increases so without limit\" would add sense; kept closer: the literal \"so always increases\" is awkward; \"immer\" is read as \"continually/without limit\" (doubtful nuance).","- \"desselben\" refers back to \"Grundstoff\" (or possibly \"Weltraums\"); the German allows both, and \"the same\" is left ambiguous.","- Centralkraft = \"central force\" (Kraft = force); Weltraum = \"cosmic space\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0141":["The specific density of the material from which the comets arise is of more remarkableness than the magnitude of their masses. Presumably, since they form in the uppermost region of the world-structure, the little parts of their composition are of the lightest genus; and one may not doubt that this is the foremost cause of the vapor-spheres and of the tails, by which they make themselves recognizable from other heavenly bodies. One cannot chiefly attribute this scattering of the cometary matter into a vapor to the effect of the sun's heat; some comets in their nearness to the sun scarcely reach the depth of the circle of the earth; many remain between the circle of the earth and that of Venus, and then return. If such a moderate degree of heat so dissolves and thins the matters on the surface of these bodies, then they must consist of the lightest material, which suffers through warmth more thinning than any matter in the whole of nature.",["- Weltgebäude = \"world-structure\"; Welt = world per glossary; Gattung = \"genus\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Materie = \"matter\".","- Dunstkugeln = \"vapor-spheres\"; Zusammensatz = \"composition\" (an older form of Zusammensetzung).","- \"sich ... kenntlich machen\" rendered \"make themselves recognizable\"; kennen elsewhere = \"know\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0142":["One also cannot attribute these vapors, rising so abundantly from the comet, to the heat that its body has retained from a perhaps former nearness to the sun: for it is indeed to be presumed that a comet, at the time of its formation, traversed several revolutions with greater eccentricity, and that this was only gradually diminished; but the other planets, of which one could presume just the same, do not show this phenomenon. Meanwhile they would show it in themselves, if the sorts of the lightest matter that are comprised in the composition of the planet were present just as abundantly as with the comets.",["- \"etwa ehemaligen\" rendered \"perhaps former\"; \"etwa\" could also mean \"approximately/at some time\"; the ambiguity is not resolved.","- \"begriffen seyn\" = \"are comprised\" (not the glossary's begreifen = comprehend, which here has the older sense \"included\").","- \"an sich\" = \"in themselves\" (glossary); Phänomenon kept as \"phenomenon\"; Umlaufe = \"revolutions\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0143":["The earth has something about itself that one can compare with the spreading of the cometary vapors and their tails (*). The finest particles that the effect of the sun draws from its surface heap themselves around one of the poles, when the sun carries out the half circle of its course on the opposite hemisphere. The finest and most effective little parts, which rise in the burning girdle of the earth, after they have reached a certain height of the atmosphere, are constrained by the effect of the sun's rays to yield to those regions and to heap themselves there that are then turned away from the sun and buried in a long night, and they make up to the inhabitants of the ice zone for the absence of the great light, which sends them even at this distance the effects of its warmth. Just the same force of the sun's rays that makes the northern lights would bring forth a vapor-circle with a tail, if the finest and volatile particles were to be encountered on the earth just as abundantly as on the comets.",["- Wirkung = \"effect\" throughout (Sonnenwirkung, Würkungen); Kraft = \"force\"; Atmosphäre = \"atmosphere\"; Dunstkreis = \"vapor-circle\".","- \"(*)\" is the footnote marker; the note follows as the next passage.","- \"die ... aus ihrer Oberfläche ziehet\": \"ihrer\" refers to the earth; \"denen Polen\" = \"the poles\".","- \"vergüten\" rendered \"make up to\"; \"welches ... zuschicket\" the subject \"welches\" (the light) is as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0144":["(*) These are the northern lights.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage; Nordlichter = \"northern lights\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0145":["Fourth Chapter,\non the\nOrigin of the Moons, and the Motions\nof the Planets about their Axis.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Ursprung = \"origin\"; Bewegungen = \"motions\"; Achse = \"axis\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0146":["The striving of a planet to form itself out of the domain of elementary matter is at the same time the cause of its axial rotation, and generates the moons that are to run around it. What the sun with its planets is in the large, that a planet, which has a far-extended sphere of attraction, represents in the smaller, namely the main piece of a system, whose parts have been set in motion by the attraction of the central body. The planet that forms itself, in that it moves the particles of the basic material from the whole domain to its formation, will out of all these sinking motions, by means of their interaction, generate circular motions, and indeed finally such as turn out into a common direction, and of which a part will receive the proper moderation of the free circular course, and in this limitation will find themselves near a common surface. In this space, just as around the sun the principal planets, so also around these the moons will form themselves, if the breadth of the attraction of such celestial bodies offers favorable circumstances for their generation. What, moreover, has been said with regard to the origin of the solar system, the same can be applied with sufficient likeness to the system of Jupiter and of Saturn. The moons will all have directed the circles of their revolution toward one side, and almost on one surface, and this indeed from the same causes that determine this analogy in the large: But why do these companions move in their common direction rather toward the side toward which the planets run than toward any other? Their orbits are indeed not generated by the circular motions: they recognize merely the attraction of the principal planet as cause, and with regard to this all directions are indifferent; a mere chance will decide that one among all possible ones toward which the sinking motion of the material turns out into circles. In fact the circular course of the principal planet does nothing toward impressing upon the material, out of which the moons are to form themselves around it, revolutions around this; all particles around the planet move in equal motion with it around the sun, and are thus in respective rest toward it. The attraction of the planet does everything alone. But the circular motion that is to arise from it, because it is in and for itself indifferent with regard to all directions, needs only a small external determination in order to turn out toward one side rather than the other: and this small degree of steering it receives from the advance of the elementary particles, which run along at the same time around the sun, but with more speed, and come into the sphere of attraction of the planet. For this compels the particles nearer to the sun, which circulate with swifter swing, already from afar to leave the direction of their track, and in an oblong excursion to raise themselves over the planet. These, because they have a greater degree of speed than the planet itself, when they are brought to sinking by its attraction, give to their rectilinear fall, and also to the fall of the rest, a bending-away from evening toward morning, and only this slight steering is needed to cause the circular motion, toward which the fall that the attraction excites turns out, to take this direction rather than any other. From this ground all moons will agree in their direction with the direction of the orbit of the principal planets. But the surface of their path also cannot deviate far from the plane of the planetary circles, because the matter out of which they form themselves, from just the ground that we have cited for the direction in general, is steered also to this most exact determination of the same, namely the coincidence with the surface of the principal circles.",["- Fläche = \"surface\" throughout (also in 0148, \"Fläche des Planeten\"); Plan (\"Plane der Planetenkreise\") = \"plane\".","- Umfang = \"domain\" (glossary); Grundstoff = \"basic material\", Stoff = \"material\", Materie = \"matter\"; Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\".","- Umlauf = \"orbit\", Umschwung = \"revolution\" (also \"swing\" in \"Schwunge\"), Umwälzung = \"revolution\" (one English word for two German words), Zirkellauf = \"circular course\", Kreisbewegung = \"circular motion\".","- \"stellet ... vor\" rendered \"represents\" (vorstellen); \"erkennen ... zur Ursache\" rendered \"recognize ... as cause\" (glossary: cognize; recognize).","- \"ablangen Ausschweifung\" = \"oblong excursion\" (literal); \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" kept literally \"from evening toward morning\".","- \"Ungefehr\" = \"chance\"; \"gleichgültig\" = \"indifferent\"; \"an und vor sich\" = \"in and for itself\"; \"Lenkung\" = \"steering\".","- \"ausschlagen\" rendered \"turn out\" (into / toward) throughout."]],"naturgeschichte-0147":["One sees clearly from all this which are the circumstances under which a planet can get satellites. The force of attraction of the same must be great, and consequently the breadth of its sphere of effect extended far, so that both the particles, moved by a high fall to the planet, notwithstanding what the resistance cancels, can nevertheless attain sufficient speed for free revolution, and sufficient material for the formation of the moons also be present in this district, which cannot happen with a slight attraction. Hence only the planets of great masses, and wide distance, are endowed with companions. Jupiter and Saturn, the 2 greatest and also most distant among the planets, have the most moons. To the earth, which is much smaller than those, only one has fallen as its share; and Mars, to which on account of its distance some share in this prerogative would also be due, goes away empty, because its mass is so slight.",["- Wirkungssphäre = \"sphere of effect\" (Wirkung = effect); Anziehungskraft = \"force of attraction\".","- \"welches\" (before \"bey einer geringen Attraction\") could refer to the whole preceding clause or to the presence of material; the English \"which\" preserves this.","- Bezirk = \"district\"; Vorzug = \"prerogative\"; Stoff = \"material\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0148":["One perceives with pleasure how the same attraction of the planet, which brought in the material for the formation of the moons, and at the same time determined their motion, extends itself up to its own body, and this imparts to itself, by just the same action through which it forms itself, a rotation about the axis, according to the general direction from evening toward morning. The particles of the downsinking basic material, which, as said, receive a general rotating motion from evening toward morning, fall for the most part onto the surface of the planet, and mix themselves with its lump, because they do not have the measured degrees to maintain themselves freely hovering in circular motions. Since they now come into the composition of the planet, they must, as parts of it, continue just the same turning, in just the same direction, that they had before they were united with it. And because in general it is to be seen from the foregoing that the multitude of particles, which the lack of the requisite motion plunges down upon the central body, must very far exceed the number of the others, which have been able to attain the proper degrees of speed; so one also easily comprehends why this, in its axial rotation, will indeed by far not have the speed to render the gravity on its surface equilibrium with the fleeing force, but will nevertheless in planets of great mass and wide distance be far swifter than in near and small ones. In fact Jupiter has the swiftest axial rotation that we know, and I do not know according to which system one could rhyme this together with a body whose lump surpasses all others, if one could not regard its motions themselves as the effect of that attraction which this celestial body exercises according to the measure of just this lump. If the axial rotation were an effect of an external cause, then Mars would have to have a swifter one than Jupiter; for just the same moving force moves a smaller body more than a larger, and besides one would rightly wonder how, since all motions decrease farther from the center, the speeds of the revolutions increase with the same distances, and in Jupiter can even be two and a half times swifter than its annual motion itself.",["- \"Umwelzungen\" (end) is read as Umwälzungen (OCR/old spelling); rendered \"revolutions\" as in 0146.","- \"dieser\" in \"woher dieser in seiner Achsendrehung\" is taken as the central body/planet; the English \"this\" keeps the German's bare pronoun.","- \"der Schwere ... mit der fliehenden Kraft das Gleichgewicht zu leisten\" rendered literally; Schwere = \"gravity\", \"fliehende Kraft\" = \"fleeing force\", Kraft = \"force\".","- \"zusammen reimen\" = \"rhyme together\" (literal); \"drittehalbmal\" = \"two and a half times\".","- Klumpen = \"lump\"; begreifen = \"comprehend\"; kennen = \"know\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Fläche = \"surface\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0149":["Since one is thus compelled to recognize in the daily turnings of the planets just the same cause which is in general the general source of motion of nature, namely attraction; so this way of explanation will prove its legitimacy through the natural privilege of its basic concept, and through an unforced sequel from the same.",["- Erklärungsart = \"way of explanation\" (Art = way; Erklärung = explanation); Grundbegriff = \"basic concept\".","- Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); Vorrecht = \"privilege\" (cf. Vorzug = \"prerogative\" in 0147).","- \"erkennen\" = \"recognize\" (glossary: cognize; recognize); \"Bewegungsqvelle\" = \"source of motion\".","- \"seines Grundbegriffes\": \"seines\" (masculine/neuter) has no clear antecedent beside \"Erklärungsart\" (feminine); preserved as \"its\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0150":["But if the formation of a body itself brings forth the rotation about the axis, then all the globes of the world-structure must fairly have it; but why does the moon not have it? which, though falsely, seems to some to have that way of a turning-around, through which it always turns the same side toward the earth, rather from a kind of overweight of the one hemisphere than from an actual impetus of revolution. Should the same perhaps formerly have rolled more quickly about its axis, and, through, I know not what causes, which gradually diminished this motion, have been brought to this slight and measured remainder? One need only resolve this question with regard to one of the planets, and the application to all of them results from it of itself. I reserve this resolution for another occasion, because it has a necessary combination with that problem which the royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin had set as a prize for the year 1754.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0151":["The theory which is to explain the origin of the rotations about the axis must also be able to derive from precisely the same causes the position of their axes toward the plane of their circles. One has reason to wonder whence it is that the equator of the daily revolution is not in the same plane with the surface of the moon-circles which run around the same planet; for the same motion that directed the orbit of a satellite, through its extension as far as the body of the planet, has brought forth the planet's rotation about the axis, and should have imparted to it precisely the same determination in direction and position. Heavenly bodies that have no secondary planets running around them nevertheless put themselves, through precisely the same motion of the particles that served for their material, and through the same law which restricted those to the surface of their periodic path, into a rotation about the axis, which from the same grounds had to coincide in direction with their surface of orbit. In consequence of these causes the axes of all heavenly bodies must fairly stand perpendicular to the general surface of reference of the planetary system, which does not deviate far from the ecliptic. But they are perpendicular only in the two most important pieces of this world-structure: in Jupiter and in the sun; the others, whose revolution one knows, incline their axes toward the plane of their circles; Saturn more than the others, the earth however more than Mars, whose axis is also directed almost perpendicular to the ecliptic. The equator of Saturn (provided one may hold it as designated by the direction of its ring) inclines at an angle of 31 degrees to the surface of its path; that of the earth however only at 22½. One can perhaps ascribe the cause of these deviations to the inequality in the motions of the material that came together to form the planet. In the direction of the surface of its orbital circle the foremost motion of the particles about the center of the same was, and there was the plane of reference around which the elementary particles heaped themselves, in order to make the motion there, if possible, circle-equal, and to heap up matter for the formation of the secondary planets, which for that reason never deviate far from the orbital path. If the planet formed itself for the most part only from these particles, then its rotation about the axis, like the secondary planets that run around it, would have deviated so little from it at its first formation; but it formed itself, as the theory has demonstrated, more from the particles that sank down on both sides, and whose multitude or velocity seems not to have been so completely balanced that the one hemisphere could not have received a small overweight of motion over the other, and hence some deviation of the axis.",["- Achsendrehung = \"rotation about the axis\"; Umwendung = \"turning-around\"; Umwelzung / Umdrehung = \"revolution\" (both rendered alike); Revolution = \"revolution.\"","- Stoff = \"material\" (glossary); Materie = \"matter\"; Partikeln and Theilchen = \"particles\" (Theilchen also rendered \"particles\").","- Ueberwucht = \"overweight\"; Schwung = \"impetus\" (not in glossary).","- Bildung/bilden = \"formation/form\"; Weltbau = \"world-structure\"; Beziehungsfläche = \"surface of reference\"; Plan = \"plane\" in the senses given by the context; Fläche = \"surface.\"","- Verbindung = \"combination\" (glossary); Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Grund (pl. Gründen) = \"grounds\"; Menge = \"multitude.\"","- 0150: \"welcher, wiewol fälschlich, ... einigen ... herzuhaben scheinet\" is syntactically awkward in German; kept as it stands. \"wiewol fälschlich\" could attach to the view or to the rotation claim; left ambiguous.","- 0150: \"die ... Akademie ... aufgestellet hatte\" – prize question of 1754; \"zirkelgleich\" rendered literally as \"circle-equal.\"","- 0151: \"Stücken\" rendered \"pieces\"; \"der Erden ihrer\" (dative-possessive construction) rendered \"that of the earth.\" \"Ecliptik\" in the source as Latin-form, kept as \"ecliptic.\"","- 0151: \"Aeqvator\" = \"equator\"; \"Trabant\" = \"satellite\"; \"Nebenplanet\" = \"secondary planet\"; \"Umlaufsfläche/Umlaufsbahn/Laufbahn/Laufkreis\" rendered \"surface of orbit / orbital path / path / orbital circle.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0152":["Notwithstanding these grounds I present this explanation only as a conjecture, which I do not dare to settle. My true opinion goes to this: that the revolution of the planets about the axis in the original state of the first formation coincided quite exactly with the surface of their annual orbit, and that causes were present to shift this axis out of its first position. A heavenly body which passes from its first fluid state into the state of solidity, suffers, when it forms itself completely in such a way, a great alteration in the regularity of its surface. The latter becomes solid and hardened, while the deeper matters have not yet sunk sufficiently, according to the measure of their specific gravity; the lighter sorts, which were mixed together in its lump, betake themselves finally, after they have separated from the others, under the uppermost crust that has become solid, and produce the great caverns, of which, for reasons which it would be too lengthy to cite here, the greatest and widest are located under or near the equator, into which the said crust finally sinks in, producing manifold inequalities, mountains and hollows. If now, in such a way as it must evidently have happened with the earth, the moon, Venus, the surface has become uneven; then it has no longer been able to afford the equilibrium of the rotation in its axial revolution on all sides. Some protruding parts of considerable mass, which on the opposite side found no others that could afford them the counter-effect of the rotation, had to displace the axis of revolution at once, and seek to put it into the state around which the matters would hold themselves in equilibrium. Precisely the same cause, therefore, which in the complete formation of a heavenly body transposed its surface from the level state into broken-off inequalities; this universal cause, which is perceived in all heavenly bodies that the telescope can discover distinctly enough, has put them under the necessity of altering somewhat the original position of their axis. But this alteration has its boundaries, so as not to stray out too far. The inequalities generate themselves, as already mentioned, more next to the equator of a revolving celestial globe than far from it; toward the poles they almost entirely vanish, the causes of which I reserve for another occasion to cite. Hence the masses protruding most above the level surface will be found near the equinoctial circle, and since these, through the preeminence of the rotation, strive to approach this, they will be able at most to raise the axis of the heavenly body only by a few degrees out of the perpendicular position to the surface of its orbit. In consequence of this, a heavenly body that has not yet completely formed itself will still have this right-angled position of the axis to its orbital circle, which it will perhaps change only in the sequel of long centuries. Jupiter seems still to be in this state. The preeminence of its mass and magnitude, the lightness of its material, have compelled it to attain the firm state of rest of its matters some centuries later than other heavenly bodies. Perhaps the interior of its lump is still in motion, to sink the parts of its composition toward the center, according to the constitution of their gravity, and through the separation of the thinner genera from the heavy ones, to attain the state of solidity. In such circumstances it cannot yet look calm on its surface. Upheavals and ruin rule on it. The telescope itself has assured us of this. The shape of this planet alters constantly, whereas the moon, Venus, the earth, maintain theirs unaltered. One may also well with right think the completion of the period of formation some centuries later in a heavenly body which exceeds our earth in magnitude by more than twenty thousand times, and falls short of it in density by 4 times. When its surface will have attained a calm constitution, then without doubt far greater inequalities than those which cover the earth's surface, combined with the speed of its rotation, will impart to its revolution in no very long course of time that constant position which the equilibrium of the forces on it will require.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0153":["Saturn, which is 3 times smaller than Jupiter, may perhaps have obtained through its greater distance a preeminence of a swifter formation over the latter: at least its much swifter axial revolution, and the great relation of its centrifugal force to the gravity on its surface, (which shall be demonstrated in the following chapter,) make it the case that the inequalities presumably generated thereby on it have very soon given the decision on the side of the preponderance, through an advance of the axis. I confess frankly that this part of my system, which concerns the position of the planetary axes, is still imperfect and rather far removed from being subjected to geometrical calculation. I preferred to disclose this candidly rather than, through all sorts of borrowed illusory grounds of soundness, to do damage to the rest of the doctrinal structure and give it a weak side. The following chapter can furnish a confirmation of the credibility of the whole hypothesis through which we have wanted to explain the motions of the world-edifice.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0154":["Fifth Chapter,\non the\nOrigin of the Ring of Saturn, and\nCalculation of the Daily Revolution of this\nPlanet from its Relations.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0155":["By virtue of the systematic constitution in the world-edifice, the parts of it hang together through a stepwise variation of their properties, and one may conjecture that a planet located in the most remote region of the world will have approximately such determinations as the nearest comet might acquire if it were raised into the planetary genus through the diminution of eccentricity. We will accordingly regard Saturn as if, in a way similar to cometary motion, it had completed several revolutions with greater eccentricity, and had gradually been brought to a track more similar to a circle (*). The heat that incorporated itself into it in its perihelion raised the light material from its surface, which, as we know from the preceding chapters, is in the uppermost heavenly bodies of exceeding thinness, such as to let itself be spread out by slight degrees of warmth. Meanwhile, after the planet in several revolutions had been brought to the distance at which it now hovers, it lost in so temperate a climate gradually the warmth it had received, and the vapors, which still continually spread around it from its surface, gradually ceased to raise themselves even into tails. Nor did new ones any longer rise so abundantly to increase the old: in short, the vapors already surrounding it remained hovering around it through causes which we will cite directly, and preserved for it the mark of its former comet-like nature in a constant ring, while its body exhaled the heat, and finally became a calm and purified planet. Now we will indicate the secret that has been able to keep the risen vapors of the heavenly body freely hovering, indeed, has changed them from an atmosphere spread round about it into the form of a ring standing off on all sides. I assume: Saturn had a revolution about the axis; and nothing more than this is necessary to uncover the whole secret. No other driving-work than this single one has, through an immediate mechanical outcome, brought the said phenomenon about for the planet; and I dare to assert that in the whole of nature few things can be brought to so comprehensible an origin as this particularity of the heavens can be developed out of the raw state of the first formation.",["- Glossary terms: Erklärung = explanation; Meynung = opinion; Muthmassung = conjecture; Zustand = state; Bildung = formation; Grenzen = boundaries; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Merkmal = mark; Gattung = genera (0152), Geschlecht = genus (0155; Gattung and Geschlecht both \"genus\").","- \"Umdrehung\" / \"Umschwung\" / \"Umlauf\" / \"Umwendung\" rendered \"revolution\" (Umdrehung, Umwendung, Umlauf); Umschwung and Schwung rendered \"rotation\" in 0152 but \"revolution\" in 0155; Achsendrehung = axial revolution.","- \"Himmelskörper\" = heavenly body; \"Weltbau\"/\"Weltgebäude\" = world-edifice; \"Gleichgewicht\" = equilibrium; \"Schweere\" = gravity.","- \"Materien\" = matters (plural, kept literal); \"Stoff\" = material; \"Sorten\" = sorts.","- 0152 \"Dieselbe wird feste\": \"Dieselbe\" refers to Oberfläche; \"deren ... die grösseste und weiteste ... befindlich sind\" is syntactically irregular in the German and left so; \"in welche ... hineinsinkt, ... erzeuget\" kept with its awkward construction.","- 0152 \"sich zu nähern streben\": \"diesem\" is ambiguous in the German (equator or equinoctial circle); I kept \"this\" ambiguous. \"Nachstehet\" rendered \"falls short.\"","- 0153 \"Scheingründe\" = \"illusory grounds\" (Schein = illusion); \"Ausschlag ... Ueberwucht\" = \"decision ... preponderance\"; \"Centerfliehkraft\" = centrifugal force; Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal structure\". \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter.\"","- 0155 \"(*)\" is the original footnote marker; footnote text not in the passage. \"Triebwerk\" = \"driving-work\"; \"Erfolg\" = \"outcome\"; \"Phänomenon\" kept as \"phenomenon.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0156":["(*) Or, which is more probable, that in its cometlike nature, which it still has in itself even now by virtue of its eccentricity, before the lightest material of its surface was completely dispersed, it spread out a cometary atmosphere.",["- Stoff = \"material\" (glossary); Natur = \"nature\"; Eccentricität = \"eccentricity\".","- \"an sich haben\" is rendered \"has in itself\"; it is not the technical phrase *an sich* (in itself) but contains the same words.","- \"er\" (the referent, masculine, presumably Saturn, since the passage is a footnote marked (*)) is kept as \"it\" in English; the antecedent lies outside the passage, and the pronoun is left unresolved.","- \"vermöge\" = \"by virtue of\".","- \"cometische\" rendered \"cometary\"; \"Cometenähnlichen\" as \"cometlike\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0157":["The vapors rising from Saturn had the motion in themselves, and continued it freely at the height to which they had risen, which they, as its parts, had had during its rotation about the axis. The particles that rose near the equator of the planet must have had the swiftest motion, and farther from there toward the poles motions weaker by that much, the greater the latitude of the place was from which they rose. The relation of specific gravity assigned to the particles the various heights to which they rose; but only those particles were able to maintain the places of their distance in a constantly free circular revolution whose distances, into which they were displaced, demanded such a central force as these were able to provide with the velocity that was proper to them from the rotation about the axis; the remaining ones, if they cannot be brought to this exactness through the interaction of the others, must either, with the excess of motion, remove themselves from the sphere of the planet, or, through the lack of it, be compelled to sink back onto it. The particles dispersed through the whole domain of the vapor sphere will, by virtue of the very same central laws, strive in the motion of their revolution to intersect the continued equatorial plane of the planet from both sides, and, in that they hold one another up in this plane from both hemispheres, they will accumulate there; and, because I posit that the vapors in question are those that the planet sends up last for its cooling, all the dispersed vapor-matter will gather beside this plane in a not at all broad space, and leave the spaces on both sides empty. In this new and altered direction, however, they will nevertheless continue the very same motion, which keeps them suspended in free concentric circular revolutions. In such a way now the vapor-circle alters its figure, which was a filled sphere, into a form of an extended surface, which coincides exactly with the equator of Saturn; but this surface too, from the very same mechanical grounds, must finally assume the form of a ring, whose outer edge is determined by the effect of the sun's rays, which disperse and remove by their force those particles that have removed themselves to a certain distance from the center of the planet, just as it does with the comets, and thereby mark out the outer boundary of its vapor-circle. The inner edge of this arising ring is determined by the relation of the velocity of the planet under its equator. For at that distance from its center at which this velocity provides equilibrium with the attraction of the place, there is the greatest nearness at which the particles that have risen from its body can describe circles through the motion proper to the rotation about the axis. The nearer particles, because they need for such a revolution a greater velocity, which they yet cannot have, because even on the equator of the planet the motion is not swifter, will thereby receive eccentric courses, which cross one another, weaken one another's motion, and finally all together plunge down onto the planet from which they had raised themselves. There we see now the wondrously strange phenomenon, the sight of which has at all times set astronomers in admiration since its discovery, and the cause of which, to discover, one has never been able to conceive even a probable hope, arise in an easy way, freed from all hypothesis, mechanical. What has befallen Saturn would, as can easily be seen from this, befall every comet that had sufficient rotation about the axis, if it were displaced into a constant height at which its body could cool little by little, just as regularly. Nature is fruitful in excellent unfoldings in the state of its forces left to itself, even in chaos, and the formation following upon this brings with it such splendid relations and agreements for the common benefit of the creature that it makes known, even in the eternal and unchangeable laws of its essential properties, with unanimous certainty that great being in which, by means of their common dependence, they unite into a total harmony. Saturn has great advantages from its ring; it lengthens its day, and illuminates its night, among so many moons, to such a degree that one easily forgets there the absence of the sun. But must one then deny on that account that the universal development of matter through mechanical laws, without needing any but its universal determinations, could have produced relations that bring benefit to the rational creature? All beings hang together from one cause, which is the understanding of God; they can therefore draw after them no other sequels than such as bring with them a representation of perfection in the very same divine idea.",["- Dünste / Dunstkugel / Dunstkreiß / Dunstmaterie rendered \"vapors\" / \"vapor sphere\" / \"vapor-circle\" / \"vapor-matter\"; Stoff is not in this passage.","- Theile / Theilchen / Partikeln = \"parts\" / \"particles\" (Theilchen and Partikeln both \"particles\"; the German words differ).","- Wirkung = \"effect\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Centralkraft = \"central force\"; Gesetze = \"laws\"; Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Wesen = \"beings\" (and \"being\" in \"that great being\"); Folgen = \"sequels\" (glossary Folge).","- \"Grund/Gründe\" = \"grounds\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\"/\"ratio\" not distinguished: \"relation\" used throughout (also in \"Beziehungen\" = \"relations\", the German words differ).","- \"die sie ... gehabt hatten\" in the first sentence: the antecedent of \"die\" (the motion) and the subject of the relative clause are left as ambiguous as the German. \"dessen Theile\" refers to Saturn's parts (or the planet's).","- \"sie es bey den Cometen thut\": \"sie\" (the sun's rays) and \"es\" are rendered \"it\" (\"just as it does\"), the grammatical subject ambiguous (rays plural, \"thut\" singular); the German is irregular and is kept so.","- \"und, dessen Ursache zu entdecken, man ... hat fassen können\" is kept awkward, as in the German; \"auf eine ... Art entstehen\" is placed at the end in the German word order approximation.","- \"ihrer\" in \"sie sogar ... zu erkennen geben\" refers to \"Natur\" (\"it makes known\"), while \"sich vereinbaren\"/\"ihrer gemeinschaftlichen Abhängigkeit\" is plural \"they\"; I have preserved the shift.","- \"einander ... einander\" doubled in the German (\"indem sie einander ... einander aufhalten\") is rendered once; a possible dittography in the source.","- \"Gedanke/Idee\": Idee = \"idea\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0158":["We want now to calculate the time of the axial rotation of this heavenly body from the relations of its ring, according to the cited hypothesis of its generation. Because all motion of the particles of the ring is an incorporated motion from the axial rotation of Saturn, on whose surface they were located; so the swiftest motion among those that these particles have coincides with the swiftest revolution that is encountered on the surface of Saturn, that is: the velocity with which the particles of the ring circulate in its inner edge is equal to that which the planet has on its equator. But one can easily find the former, by seeking it from the velocity of one of the satellites of Saturn, by taking the same in the relation of the square root of the distances from the center of the planet. From the velocity found there results immediately the time of the revolution of Saturn about its axis; it is of six hours, three and twenty minutes, and three and fifty seconds. This mathematical calculation of an unknown motion of a heavenly body, which is perhaps the only prediction of its kind in actual doctrine of nature, awaits confirmation from the observations of future times. The telescopes known as yet do not magnify Saturn so much that one could discover through them the spots that one can presume on its surface, in order to see its revolution about the axis through their displacement. But the viewing-tubes have perhaps not yet attained all that perfection that one can hope from them, and which the diligence and skill of the artisans seem to promise us. If one should some day succeed in giving to our conjectures the decision through visual evidence, what certainty would the theory of Saturn, and what a preeminent credibility would the whole system, which is erected on the same grounds, not thereby attain. The time of the daily revolution of Saturn also carries with it the relation of the force of its equator that flees the center, to the gravity on its surface; it is to this as 20 : 32. The gravity is thus only ⅗ greater than the centrifugal force. This so great relation necessarily causes a very considerable difference of the diameters of this planet, and one could fear that it would have to arise so great that observation, with this planet, although magnified little by the telescope, would nevertheless have to strike the eye all too distinctly, which really does not happen, and the theory could thereby suffer a disadvantageous setback. A thorough examination removes this difficulty completely. According to the Huygenian hypothesis, which assumes that the gravity in the interior of a planet is equal through and through, the difference of the diameters is in a twofold smaller relation to the diameter of the equator than the centrifugal force has to the gravity beneath the poles. E.g., since with the earth the force of the equator that flees the center is ⅟289 of the gravity beneath the poles: so in the Huygenian hypothesis the diameter of the equatorial surface must be ⅟578 greater than the axis of the earth. The cause is this: because, since the gravity, in accordance with the presupposition, in the interior of the lump of the earth, at all nearnesses to the center is as great as it is on the surface, the centrifugal force however decreases with the approaches to the center, the same is not everywhere ⅟289 of the gravity, but rather the whole diminution of the weight of the fluid column in the equatorial surface amounts on this ground not to ⅟289, but to half of it, i.e. ⅟578, of the same. In contrast, in the hypothesis of Newton the centrifugal force, which excites the axial rotation, has in the whole surface of the equator, up to the center, an equal relation to the gravity of the place: because this, in the interior of the planet (if it is assumed to be of uniform density through and through), decreases with the distance from the center in the same proportion as the centrifugal force, hence this is at all times ⅟289 of the former. This causes a lightening of the fluid column in the equatorial surface, and also the elevation of the same by ⅟289, which difference of the diameters is in this doctrinal concept still further increased by the fact that the shortening of the axis brings with it an approach of the parts to the center, hence an increase of gravity; the lengthening of the equatorial diameter, however, a distance of the parts from just the same center, and therefore a diminution of their gravity, and on this ground so increases the flattening of the Newtonian spheroid that the difference of the diameters is raised from ⅟289 to ⅟230.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0159":["According to these grounds the diameters of Saturn would have to be to one another in a still greater relation than that of 20 to 32; they would have to come nearly equal to the proportion of 1 to 2: a difference so great that the slightest attention would not miss it, however small Saturn may appear through the telescopes. But from this it is only to be seen that the presupposition of uniform density, which seems to be applied fairly correctly with the body of the earth, deviates all too far from the truth with Saturn; which is already in itself probable with a planet whose lump consists for the greatest part, according to its content, of the lightest matters, and which, before it receives the state of solidity, permits to those of heavier kind in its composition the sinking down to the center, according to the constitution of their gravity, far more freely than those heavenly bodies whose much denser material delays the settling of the matters, and lets them become solid before this sinking down can occur. Since we thus presuppose with Saturn that the density of its matters in its interior increases with the approach to the center, gravity no longer decreases in this relation; but the growing density replaces the lack of the parts that are posited above the height of the point situated in the planet, and by their attraction contribute nothing to its gravity (*). If this preeminent density of the deepest matters is very great; so it transforms, by virtue of the laws of attraction, the gravity decreasing toward the center in the interior into an almost uniform one, and posits the relation of the diameters close to the Huygenian, which is always the half of the relation between the centrifugal force and the gravity, consequently, since these were to one another as 2 : 3; so the difference of the diameters of this planet will be not ⅓, but ⅙ of the diameter of the equator: which difference is finally still concealed by the fact that Saturn, whose axis always makes an angle of 31 degrees with the surface of its orbit, never presents the position of the same toward its equator straight on, as with Jupiter, which diminishes the previous difference, to all appearances, by almost the third part. One can easily judge under such circumstances, and principally with the so great distance of this planet: that the flattened shape of its body will not so easily strike the eye as one might well think; nevertheless astronomy, whose advancement depends principally on the perfection of the instruments, will be put in the position of attaining the discovery of so remarkable a property, if I do not flatter myself too much, perhaps by their help.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0160":["(*) For according to the Newtonian laws of attraction a body that is located in the interior of a sphere is attracted only by that part of it which is described spherically about the center at the distance that the former has from the center. The concentric part located outside this distance contributes nothing, because of the equilibrium of its attractions, which cancel one another, to moving the body either toward the center or away from it.",["- Notes below apply to all three passages.","- *Schweere* = \"gravity\" throughout (also *Gravität* in 0158, likewise \"gravity\"; marked here as one English word for two German words). *Centerfliehkraft*, *den Mittelpunkt fliehende Kraft*, *Centrifugalkraft* all rendered \"centrifugal force\" / \"force … that flees the center\"; *Kraft* = force.","- *Hypothese* / *Voraussetzung* / *Lehrbegriff*: \"hypothesis\" / \"presupposition\" / \"doctrinal concept\".","- *Beobachtung(en)* = observation; *Augenschein* = \"visual evidence\"; *Ferngläser* = \"telescopes\"; *Sehröhre* = \"viewing-tubes\" (literal).","- *Verhältniß* / *Proportion* both \"relation\" / \"proportion\" respectively; *Verhältniß* also appears in the feminine form \"die Verhältniß\" in 0158.","- *Muthmassungen* = \"conjectures\"; *Gewißheit* = \"certainty\"; *Glaubwürdigkeit* = \"credibility\"; *Inhalt* = \"content\" (0159, \"according to its content\").","- 0158: the OCR shows a gap after \"drey und funfzig Secunden \" and after \"⅟230 \" (likely dropped footnote markers); left as is. \"Vor\" in \"was vor eine\" kept as \"what a\".","- 0158: \"sie ist von sechs Stunden…\" rendered literally as \"it is of six hours…\". The \"Because … ; so\" construction of the second sentence is kept as in the German (semicolon, no main-clause conjunction in English).","- 0159: \"dem grossesten Theile … nach\" is syntactically loose in the German (dative with *nach*), \"for the greatest part, according to its content\" kept; \"der Niedersatz\" = \"settling\"; \"Scheine nach\" = \"to all appearances\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0161":["What I say about the figure of Saturn can in a certain way serve the doctrine of nature of the heavens as a general remark. Jupiter, which, according to an exact calculation, has on its equator a relation of heaviness to centrifugal force of at least 9¼ : 1, should, if its lump were of uniform density through and through, according to the theorems of Newton, show in itself an even greater difference than ⅑ between its axis and the diameter of the equator. But Cassini found it to be only ⅟16, Pound ⅟12, at times ⅟14; at least all these various observations, which through their difference confirm the difficulty of this measurement, agree in setting it much smaller than it should be according to the system of Newton, or rather according to his hypothesis of uniform density. And if one therefore alters the presupposition of uniform density, which occasions the so great deviation of the theory from the observation, into the much more probable one, in which the density of the planetary lump is posited as increasing toward its center point; then one will not only justify the observation in the case of Jupiter, but also, in the case of Saturn, a planet much harder to measure, be able to see distinctly the cause of a lesser flattening of its spheroidal body.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0162":["From the generation of the Saturnian ring we have taken occasion to venture the bold step of determining for it by calculation the time of the rotation about its axis, which telescopes are not capable of discovering. Let us augment this sample of a physical prediction with yet another, concerning this same planet, which has to expect the testimony of its correctness from the more perfect instruments of future times.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0163":["In accordance with the presupposition that the ring of Saturn is an accumulation of particles which, after they have risen as vapors from the surface of this heavenly body, keep themselves, by virtue of the momentum that they have from its rotation about its axis and continue, freely running in circles at the height of their distance, they do not have equal periodic times of revolution at all their distances from the center point; rather these are related as the square roots of the cubes of their distance, if they are to keep themselves suspended through the laws of the central forces. Now the time in which, according to this hypothesis, the particles of the inner edge perform their revolution is approximately 10 hours, and the time of the circular course of the particles in the outer edge is, according to proper calculation, 15 hours; thus, when the lowest parts of the ring have performed their revolution 3 times, the most distant have done it only 2 times. But it is probable, one may estimate the hindrance that the particles, in their great dispersal in the plane of the ring, offer one another as slight as one wishes, that the lagging behind of the more distant particles, in each of their revolutions, gradually retards and holds back the lower parts moved more quickly: whereas these must impress on the upper ones a part of their motion, toward a swifter turning-around, which, if this interaction were not finally interrupted, would last until the particles of the ring were all brought to the point of turning around in equal time, the lower as well as the farther, in which state they would be in respective rest toward one another, and through the moving away would produce no effect in one another. Now, however, such a state, if the motion of the ring turned out that way, would completely destroy it, because, if one takes the middle of the plane of the ring, and posits that there the motion remains in the state in which it previously was and must be, in order to be able to perform a free circular course, the lower particles, because they have been greatly held back, would not keep themselves suspended at their height but would cross one another in oblique and eccentric motions, while the more distant ones, through the impression of a greater motion than it should be for the central force of their distance, turned farther away from the sun than the effect of the sun determines the outer boundary of the ring, would have to be dispersed and carried off by it behind the planet.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0164":["But one need not fear all this disorder. The mechanism of the generating motion of the ring leads to a determination which, by means of the very causes that are supposed to destroy it, puts it into a secure state, in that it is divided into several concentric circular strips, which, on account of the intervals that separate them, no longer have any community with one another. For since the particles that revolve in the inner edge carry the upper ones somewhat along by their quicker motion, and accelerate their revolution; the increased degrees of speed in these cause an excess of centrifugal force, and a removal from the place where they were suspended. But if one presupposes that, in that these strive to separate themselves from the lower ones, they have to overcome a certain interconnection, which, although they are dispersed vapors, nevertheless does not seem to signify quite nothing in them; then this increased degree of momentum will strive to overcome the said interconnection: but will not overcome it, so long as the excess of the centrifugal force, which it applies in equal time of revolution with the lowest ones, over the central force of its place, does not exceed this adhering. And on this ground, in a certain breadth of a strip of this ring, although, because its parts perform their revolution in equal time, the upper ones apply an endeavor to tear themselves from the lower, the interconnection nevertheless must persist, but not in greater breadth, because they, since the speed of these particles moved around in equal times increases with the distances, thus more than it should according to the central laws, when it has exceeded the degree that the interconnection of the vapor particles can afford, must tear themselves from these and assume a distance which accords with the excess of the force of turning-around over the central force of the place. In this way the interval is determined which separates the first strip of the ring from the rest: and in the same way the accelerated motion of the upper particles, through the quick revolution of the lower, and their interconnection, which strives to hinder the separation, makes the second concentric ring, from which the third stands off by a moderate interval. One could calculate the number of these circular strips, and the breadth of their intervals, if the degree of the interconnection that hangs the particles on one another were known; but we can content ourselves with having guessed, with good ground of probability, the composition in general of the Saturnian ring, which prevents its destruction and keeps it suspended through free motions.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0165":["This conjecture pleases me not a little, by means of the hope of still seeing it someday confirmed through actual observations. Some years ago it was reported from London that, when one was observing Saturn with a new Newtonian telescope improved by Mr. Bradley, it had seemed that its ring is properly a composition of many concentric rings, which were separated by intervals. This news has not been continued since (*) The instruments of sight have opened to the understanding the knowledge of the outermost regions of the structure of the world. If it now depends chiefly on them to take new steps therein; then one can well hope with probability, from the attention of the century to all that which can expand the insights of human beings, that it will turn chiefly to a side which offers it the greatest hope of important discoveries.",["- Dichtigkeit = density; Klumpen = lump; Schwung = momentum (rendered consistently); Eindruck/eindrücken = impression/impress.","- Verhältniß = relation; Zusammenhang = interconnection (glossary); Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Beobachtung = observation; Lehrsätze = theorems.","- 0163: \"Der Voraussetzung gemäß: daß … haben dieselbe nicht…\" is a loose, anacoluthic construction; kept as it stands. \"man mag … schätzen, als man will\" is kept parenthetical-like without a conjunction, as in German.","- 0163: \"vor die Centralkraft\" = \"for the central force\" (vor for für); \"respectiver Ruhe\" rendered \"respective rest\" (respective, Latinate).","- 0163: \"durch dieselbe hinter dem Planeten zerstreuet\" — \"dieselbe\" (feminine) is ambiguous (Sonnenwirkung or Grenze); left as \"by it\".","- 0164: \"nichts bedeutend\" = \"signify … nothing\" (bedeuten = signify). \"Centerfliehkraft\" = centrifugal force; \"Centrifugalkraft\" likewise (both Kraft = force).","- 0164: \"sie\" and \"diese\" in the sentence beginning \"weil sie, indem …\" have shifting referents; preserved. Sentence appears syntactically strained in the original.","- 0165: The footnote marker \"(*)\" stands in the text with no footnote text in the passage; the sentence \"Diese Nachricht … fortgesetzet worden\" has no period in the source, and the following sentence may belong to the footnote. Left as it stands. \"Verstande\" = understanding; \"Kenntnisse\" = knowledge (plural, acquaintance)."]],"naturgeschichte-0166":["(*) After I had written this down; I find in the Memoires of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris for the year 1705, in a treatise by Herr Cassini, on the satellites and the ring of Saturn, on page 571 of the second part of the v. Steinwehr translation, a confirmation of this conjecture, which leaves almost no doubt of its correctness. After Herr Cassini has put forward a thought that might in a certain way have been a small approach to that truth which we have brought out, although it is in itself improbable: namely, that perhaps this ring might be a swarm of small satellites, which would look, as seen from Saturn, just as the Milky Way appears from the Earth (which thought can find a place, if one takes for these small satellites the vapor particles, which swing around it with just such a motion); so he says further: The observations confirmed this thought, which were made in the years when the ring of Saturn seemed broader and more open. For one saw the breadth of the ring divided into two parts by a dark elliptical line, whose nearer part, toward the globe, was brighter than the most distant. This line marked, as it were, a small interspace between the two parts, just as the distance of the globe from the ring is indicated by the greatest darkness between the two.",["- Wahrscheinlich/unwahrscheinlich = probable/improbable (Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability in glossary); \"Observationen\" rendered \"observations\" (cf. Beobachtung = observation).","- \"Warheit\" (sic) = truth; \"Trabanten\" = satellites; \"Bestätigung\" = confirmation; \"Vermuthung\" = conjecture.","- The closing quotation of Cassini has no closing quotation mark in the source; the space before the final period suggests a lost footnote mark or OCR slip. Left as it stands.","- \"Zwischenraum\" = interspace; \"Kugel\" = globe.","- \"Dunsttheilchen\" = vapor particles; \"Theil\" = part (Teil)."]],"naturgeschichte-0167":["But if Saturn has been so fortunate as to procure a ring for itself, why then has no other planet come to share in this advantage? The cause is distinct. Because a ring is supposed to arise from the exhalations of a planet, which breathes them out in its raw state, and the rotation about the axis of this must give the swing, which they have only to continue when they have arrived at the height where, with this implanted motion, they can exactly provide the equilibrium to gravitation toward the planet; one can easily determine by calculation to what height the vapors must rise from a planet, if they are to maintain themselves in free circular motion through the motions that they had under its equator, if one knows the diameter of the planet, the time of its revolution, and the heaviness on its surface. According to the law of central motion the distance of a body that can run freely in a circle around a planet with a velocity equal to the planet's rotation about its axis will be in just such a ratio to the half diameter of the planet as the force fleeing the center, under its equator, is to heaviness. From these grounds the distance of the inner edge of the ring of Saturn was as 8, if its half diameter is assumed as 5, which two numbers are in the same ratio as 32 : 20, which, as we noted before, expresses the proportion between heaviness and the centrifugal force under the equator. From the same grounds, if one supposed that Jupiter were to have a ring generated in this way, its smallest half diameter would exceed the half thickness of Jupiter 10 times, which would hit exactly to where its outermost satellite runs around it, and which is therefore impossible, both on these grounds and also because the exhalation of a planet cannot spread so far from it. If one demanded to know why the Earth has received no ring; one will find the answer in the magnitude of the half diameter which its inner edge alone would have had to have, which would have had to become 289 half Earth-diameters large. With the more slowly moved planets the generation of a ring removes itself still further from possibility; thus no case remains in which a planet could have received a ring in the way that we have explained, except that one in which the planet is which actually has it, which is no small reinforcement of the credibility of our way of explanation.",["- \"deutlich\" = distinct; \"Grund/Gründe\" = ground(s); \"Schwung\" = swing; \"Schweere\" = heaviness (not Schwere \"gravity\"; \"Gravitation\" kept as such).","- \"Möglichkeit/unmöglich\" = possibility/impossible; \"Art\" = way (also \"Erklärungsart\" = way of explanation); \"Erklärung\" = explanation.","- \"Aeqvator\" = equator; \"Centerfliehkraft\" = centrifugal force; \"den Mittelpunkt fliehende Kraft\" = force fleeing the center (Kraft = force).","- \"Körper\" = body; \"Dünste/Ausdünstungen\" = vapors/exhalations; \"Verhältnisse\" = ratio; \"Proportion\" = proportion.","- \"Größe\" = magnitude; \"Dicke\" (of Jupiter) = thickness, literally; \"halbe Dicke\" and \"halber Durchmesser\" both \"half\".","- \"da ein Planet ... hätte bekommen können, als derjenige, darinn der Planet ist\": construction kept; \"da\" = \"in which\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0168":["But what almost makes me assured that the ring which surrounds Saturn did not arise for it in that general way, and was not generated through the general laws of formation which have prevailed through the whole system of the planets, and have also procured for Saturn its satellites, that, I say, this external matter did not furnish its material for this, but that it is a creature of the planet itself, which has raised its most volatile parts through warmth, and has imparted to them through its own rotation about its axis the swing for revolution, is this, that the ring is not directed, like the other satellites of it, and like all revolving bodies generally which are found in the retinue of the principal planets, in the general plane of reference of the planetary motions, but deviates very much from it: which is a sure proof that it was not formed out of the general basic material, and did not receive its motion from the sinking down of this, but, having risen from the planet after long-completed formation, has received, through its implanted forces of revolution, as its separated part, a motion and direction relating to the rotation of the same about its axis.",["- \"Stoff\" = material; \"Grundstoff\" = basic material; \"Materie\" = matter; \"Beweis\" = proof.","- \"Bildungsgesetze\" = laws of formation; \"Beziehungsfläche\" = plane of reference; \"Umschwungskräfte\" = forces of revolution (Kraft = force).","- \"ihm\" (dative, \"for Saturn\") and \"desselben\" (of the planet) retained as in German; \"dessen\" = \"of this\" (the basic material) – antecedents left as ambiguous as in German.","- \"Achsendrehung\" = rotation about the axis; \"Warme/Wärme\" = warmth."]],"naturgeschichte-0169":["The pleasure of having comprehended one of the rarest particularities of the heavens in the whole domain of its essence and generation has entangled us in so extensive a treatise. Let us, with the indulgence of our obliging readers, drive it, where it is agreeable, to the point of digression, in order, after we have abandoned ourselves in a pleasant way to arbitrary opinions with a kind of unboundedness, to return again to the truth with so much the more caution and care.",["- \"begriffen\" = comprehended; \"Umfang\" = domain; \"Wesen\" = essence (glossary also allows \"being\"); \"Besonderheiten\" = particularities (besonder = particular).","- \"Meinungen\" = opinions; \"Warheit\" (sic) = truth; \"willkührlich\" = arbitrary.","- \"Abhandlung\" = treatise; \"Ausschweiffung\" = digression; \"beliebig\" = agreeable (literally \"as it pleases\"); \"Ungebundenheit\" = unboundedness."]],"naturgeschichte-0170":["Could one not imagine that the earth, just as much as Saturn, once had a ring? It might now have risen from its surface just as Saturn's did, and have maintained itself for a long time, while the earth, from a much swifter rotation than the present one is, was held up, through who knows what causes, to the present degree, or else that one credits the downward-sinking general basic material with having formed it according to the rules that we explained above, which one must not take so exactly, if one wants to gratify one's inclination to the singular. But what a store of fine elucidations and sequels such an idea offers us. A ring around the earth! What beauty of a sight for those who were created to inhabit the earth as a paradise; how much convenience for these, whom nature was to smile upon from all sides! But this is still nothing against the confirmation that such a hypothesis can borrow from the document of the history of creation, and which is no small recommendation toward approval for those who believe they do not profane the honor of revelation but confirm it, when they make use of it to give an authority to the extravagances of their wit. The water of the firmament, which the Mosaic description mentions, has already caused the interpreters no little trouble. Could one not make use of this ring to help oneself out of this difficulty? This ring consisted without doubt of watery vapors; and one has, besides the advantage that it could provide for the first inhabitants of the earth, this one more, of letting it be broken up in case of need, in order to chastise with floods the world, which had made itself unworthy of such beauty. Either a comet, whose attraction brought the regular motions of its parts into confusion, or the cooling of the region of its location united its scattered vapor-parts, and plunged them, in one of the most terrible cloudbursts, down upon the earth's surface. One easily knows what the sequel of this was. The whole world went under in the water, and moreover drew in, in the foreign and volatile vapors of this unnatural rain, that slow poison which brought all creatures nearer to death and destruction. Now the figure of a pale and luminous arch had vanished from the horizon, and the new world, which could never recall this sight without feeling a terror before this fearful instrument of divine vengeance, perhaps saw with no small consternation in the first rain that colored arch which, by its figure, seemed to depict the former, but which, through the assurance of a reconciled heaven, was to be a sign of grace and a monument of a continuing preservation of the now altered earth's surface. The similarity of the shape of this sign of remembrance with the occurrence designated could commend such a hypothesis to those who are given to the prevailing inclination to bring the miracles of revelation into a system with the ordinary laws of nature. I find it more advisable to sacrifice entirely the fleeting approval that such agreements can awaken to the true gratification; which arises from the perception of the regular interconnection, when physical analogies support one another in the designation of physical truths.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0171":["Sixth Chapter,\non\nthe Zodiacal Light.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0172":["The sun is surrounded with a subtle and vaporous being, which surrounds it in the plane of its equator, with only a slight extension on both sides, up to a great height, of which one cannot be assured whether, as Herr von Mairan depicts it, in the figure of a convexly ground glass, (*figura lenticulari*,) it adjoins the surface of the sun, or stands off from it on all sides like the ring of Saturn. Be it now the one or the other; so there remains similarity enough to place this phenomenon in comparison with the ring of Saturn, and to derive it from a concurring origin. If this extended matter is an outflow from the sun, as it is indeed most probable to hold it to be; so one will not be able to miss the cause that has brought it into the plane common to the sun's equator. The lightest and most volatile material, which the sun's fire lifts from its surface, and has long since lifted, is driven far beyond it by its effect, and remains, according to the measure of its lightness, suspended at a distance where the propelling effect of the rays holds the heaviness of these vapor-particles in equilibrium, or they are supported by the inflow of new particles, which constantly come to them. Now, because the sun, in that it turns about its axis, impresses its motion equally on these vapors torn from its surface; so they retain a certain impetus toward revolution, whereby they, from both sides, in accordance with the central laws, strive in the circle of their motion to cut through the continued equatorial plane of the sun; and hence, because they press toward it in equal quantity from both hemispheres, accumulate there with equal forces, and form an extended plane, in this plan relating to the sun's equator.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0173":["But, notwithstanding this similarity with the ring of Saturn, an essential difference remains, which makes the phenomenon of the zodiacal light diverge greatly from that one. The particles of the former maintain themselves through the implanted rotational motion in a freely suspended circular course; but the particles of the latter are maintained at their height by the force of the sun's rays, without which the motion inhering in them from the sun's revolution would fall far short of keeping them from falling in free circling. For, since the force of the axial rotation fleeing the center is on the surface of the sun still not ⅟40000 of the attraction; so these ascended vapors would have to be removed 40000 half sun-diameters from it, in order in such a distance first to meet with a gravitation to which their communicated motion could hold the equilibrium. One is therefore sure not to ascribe this phenomenon of the sun to it in the way like the ring of Saturn.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0174":["Nevertheless there remains no small probability that this neck-ornament of the sun perhaps recognizes the same origin that all of nature recognizes, namely the formation from the general basic material, whose parts, since they had been suspended about in the highest regions of the sun's world, only after the complete accomplishment of the formation of the whole system sank down toward the sun, in a late fall with weakened, but yet from evening toward morning curved motion, and, by means of this way of circulation, cut through its continued equatorial plane, and there, through their accumulation from both sides, while they lingered, took up a plane extended in this position, in which they now maintain themselves at a constantly equal height, partly through the driving-back of the sun's rays, partly through their actually attained circular motion. The present explanation has no other worthiness than that which pertains to conjectures, and no claim except only to an arbitrary approval; the judgment of the reader may turn to that side which seems to it the most worthy of acceptance.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0175":["Seventh Chapter,\non\nthe Creation in the whole domain of its infinity,\nin space as well as in time.",["- 0170: Erläuterungen = \"elucidations\"; Folgen = \"sequels\" (glossary Folge); Folgerung not used. \"Folge hievon\" also \"sequel\".","- 0170: Grundstoff rendered \"basic material\" (Stoff = material); Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\".","- 0170: Begebenheit = \"occurrence\"; Ansehen rendered \"authority\" (non-glossary); Witz = \"wit\"; Beyfall = \"approval\".","- 0170: \"Er möchte … seyn\" subjunctives kept as in German; the \"oder daß\" construction is left syntactically loose, as in the original.","- 0170: Veste = \"firmament\"; \"Urkunde\" = \"document\".","- 0172: \"figura lenticulari\" is in Latin type in the source and is italicized. \"Wesen\" = \"being\" (glossary). \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\".","- 0172–0174: Stoff = \"material\", Erklärung = \"explanation\" (also \"definition\"); Kräfte = \"forces\". \"Plan\" (a French-derived noun in the German) kept as \"plan\"; \"Ebene\"/\"Fläche\" both \"plane\" in places (Fläche also \"surface\").","- 0173: \"der Attraction\" and \"Gravitation\" are kept as Latinate \"attraction\"/\"gravitation\". The sentence \"ohne welcher …\" is ungrammatical in the German and left awkward; \"Art\" = \"way\". \"⅟40000\" as printed."]],"naturgeschichte-0176":["The edifice of the world, through its immeasurable magnitude, and through the infinite manifold and beauty which shines forth from it on all sides, puts one into a quiet astonishment. If the representation of all this perfection now stirs the imagination; then, on the other side, another kind of rapture takes hold of the understanding when it considers how so much splendor, so much magnitude, flows off from a single universal rule, with an eternal and correct order. The planetary structure of the world, in which the sun from the midpoint of all circles, with its mighty attraction, makes the inhabited globes of its system revolve in eternal circles, has been formed entirely, as we have seen, from the originally dispersed basic material of all world-matter. All fixed stars, which the eye discovers in the hollow depth of the heavens, and which seem to indicate a kind of squandering, are suns and midpoints of similar systems. Analogy therefore does not permit here to doubt that these have been formed and generated in the same way as that in which we find ourselves, from the smallest parts of the elementary matter, which filled the empty space, this infinite domain of the divine presence.",["- Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\" (glossary Mannigfaltige); rendered \"the infinite manifold\" for \"die unendliche Mannigfaltigkeit\"; the German noun is abstract.","- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Verstand = understanding; Art = kind / way (rendered \"kind\" for Art der Entzückung, \"way\" for auf die gleiche Art).","- Grundstoff = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material); Weltmaterie = \"world-matter\"; Materie = matter; Umfang = domain; Raum = space.","- \"setzet ... in ein stilles Erstaunen\": the object is left unexpressed in the German (\"puts one\"); the English \"one\" is supplied by necessity of syntax, not in the original.","- \"Die Analogie erlaubt es also hier nicht, zu zweifeln, daß diese ... gebildet und erzeuget worden\": the final verb is left without an auxiliary matching \"diese\" (plural) in the German as printed; kept as it stands. \"das, darinn wir uns befinden\" = \"that in which we find ourselves\" (antecedent, presumably the system, left unstated).","- \"Weltgebäude\" and \"Weltbau\" both rendered \"edifice of the world\" / \"structure of the world\"; not the same English word, but the glossary does not cover them."]],"naturgeschichte-0177":["If now all worlds and world-orders recognize the same kind of origin: if attraction is unlimited and universal, the repulsion of the elements however likewise effective throughout, if with the infinite the great and the small are on both sides small; should not all the edifices of the world equally have assumed a relational constitution and systematic combination among one another, as the heavenly bodies of our solar world do in the small, such as Saturn, Jupiter and the earth, which are systems for themselves in particular, and yet cohere among one another as members in a still greater one? If one assumes in the immeasurable space, in which all the suns of the Milky Way have formed, a point around which, through I know not what cause, the first formation of nature from chaos has begun; then there will have arisen there the greatest mass, and a body of the most uncommon attraction, which has thereby become capable of compelling, in a monstrous sphere around itself, all the systems comprehended in formation to sink toward it, as their midpoint, and to erect around it a like system in the whole, as the same elementary basic material, which formed the planets, has made around the sun in the small. Observation makes this conjecture almost undoubted. The host of the stars constitutes, through its relational position toward a common plane, just as much a system as the planets of our solar structure around the sun. The Milky Way is the zodiac of these higher world-orders, which deviate from its zone as little as possible, and whose strip is always illuminated by their light, just as the zodiac of the planets shimmers here and there from the shine of these globes, though only in very few points. Each of these suns constitutes with its revolving planets a particular system for itself; but this does not hinder it from being parts of a still greater system, just as Jupiter or Saturn, regardless of their own retinue, are limited in the systematic constitution of a still greater structure of the world. Can one not recognize, in so exact an agreement in the constitution, the same cause and kind of generation?",["- Art = kind (der Ursprung ... \"dieselbe Art\"); erkennen = recognize (cognoscere sense per glossary), here \"recognize\".","- Verfassung = \"constitution\" (not in glossary); Verbindung = combination; beziehende = \"relational\"; Plan = \"plane\" (German \"Plan\" could also be \"plan\"; \"gemeinschaftlichen Plan\" kept as \"common plane\", doubtful).","- Original: \"wie Saturn, Jupiter und die Erde, die vor sich insonderheit Systeme seyn\" – the subjunctive \"seyn\" kept as a plain present (\"are\").","- \"Wenn bey dem unendlichen das grosse und kleine beyderseits klein ist\": \"das Unendliche\" lowercase in original; singular \"ist\" retained in sense (\"are ... small\") though German has singular verb with two subjects.","- \"Eine jede dieser Sonnen ... nicht, Theile ... zu seyn\": the German \"dieses hindert nicht, Theile ... zu seyn\" has no stated subject for the infinitive; English \"it\" reflects \"Eine jede\". \"beschränkt seyn\" plural with Jupiter or Saturn kept.","- Attraction = Attraction/Anziehung: \"attraction\" for both Anziehung and Attraction (Latinate member); Beobachtung = observation; Muthmassung = conjecture; Körper = body; Beschränkt = limited (cf. Schranke = limitation)."]],"naturgeschichte-0178":["If now the fixed stars constitute a system, whose domain is determined by the sphere of attraction of that body which is found in the midpoint, will not more solar systems, and, so to speak, more Milky Ways have arisen, which have been generated in the boundless field of the space of the world? We have beheld with astonishment figures in the heavens which are nothing other than such fixed-star systems limited to a common plane, such Milky Ways, if I may express myself so, which, in various positions toward the eye, with a shimmer weakened in accordance with their infinite distance, present elliptical shapes; they are systems of, so to say, infinite times infinitely greater diameter than the diameter of our solar structure is; but without doubt arisen in the same way, ordered and arranged by the same causes, and maintain themselves in their constitution by a like driving-gear as this one.",["- Umfang = domain; bestimmet = determined; Raum = space; Weltraum = \"space of the world\"; Grenzenlosen = boundless.","- Plan = \"plane\" (or \"plan\"), doubtful; see preceding passage. Gestalten = shapes; Figuren = figures.","- \"als ... ist\": the comparison \"als der Diameter unseres Sonnenbaues ist\" kept with final \"is\". Durchmesser and Diameter both \"diameter\".","- \"und erhalten sich ... als dieses\": the subject \"es sind Systemata\" carries across a plural/singular shift in the German; the English keeps the same loose connection. \"als dieses\" (\"as this one\") has no stated noun.","- Sonnensystemata, Systemata = \"solar systems\", \"systems\" (Latinate plural kept as \"systems\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0179":["If one regards these star-systems in turn as members of the great chain of the whole of nature; then one has just as much cause as before to think them in a reciprocal relation, and in combinations which, by virtue of the law of first formation ruling through all of nature, constitute a new, still greater system, which is governed by the attraction of a body of incomparably mightier attraction than all the previous ones were, from the midpoint of their regular positions. The attraction which is the cause of the systematic constitution among the fixed stars of the Milky Way also still works in the distance of these very world-orders, in order to bring them out of their positions, and to bury the world in an unavoidably impending chaos, if regularly distributed forces of revolution of attraction did not provide the counterweight, and on both sides in combination produce that relation which is the ground of the systematic constitution. Attraction is without doubt just as widely extended a property of matter as coexistence, which makes space, in that it combines the substances through reciprocal dependencies, or, to speak more properly, attraction is this very universal relation which unites the parts of nature in one space: it extends itself therefore to the whole extension of the same, to all the distances of its infinity. When the light from these distant systems reaches us, the light, which is only an impressed motion, must not much rather attraction, this original source of motion, which is earlier than all motion is: which needs no foreign causes, also can be held up by no obstacle, because it works into the innermost of matter, without any impact, even in the universal rest of nature, must, I say, attraction not have set these fixed-star systems, notwithstanding their immeasurable distances, in the unformed dispersion of their material, at the beginning of the stirring of nature, into motions which, just as we have seen in the small, is the source of the systematic combination, and of the lasting constancy of their members, which secures them from decay?",["- Kraft des ... Gesetzes = \"by virtue of the law\"; Gesetz = law; Kraft here is part of an idiom, not \"force\".","- Schwungskräfte = \"forces of revolution\" (Kraft = force); Attraction and Anziehung both \"attraction\" (Latinate Attraction marked here).","- Eigenschaft = property (glossary gives Beschaffenheit = property; Eigenschaft not listed); Coexistenz = coexistence; Ausdehnung = extension; Beziehung = relation.","- \"welche eher, wie alle Bewegung ist\": obscure as printed (\"eher, wie\" for \"eher als\"?); kept literally as \"which is earlier than all motion is\". Possible corrupt or irregular text.","- \"die keiner fremden Ursachen bedarf, auch durch keine Hinderniß kan aufgehalten werden\": the parenthetical-like clause is kept in its place; \"which needs ... also can be held up\" mirrors the German's loose construction.","- \"die ... die Qvelle ... ist\": singular \"ist\" with plural antecedent \"Bewegungen\" retained in English as \"is\" (strictly \"are\" would be expected); \"Qvelle\" = source; \"wie wir im Kleinen gesehen haben\" = \"as we have seen in the small\".","- \"die sie vor den Verfall sichert\": the relative's antecedent is ambiguous (Beständigkeit or Verbindung); left ambiguous."]],"naturgeschichte-0180":["But what, then, will finally be the end of the systematic arrangements? where will creation itself cease? One notices well that, in order to think it in a relation to the power of the infinite being, it must have no bounds at all. One comes no nearer to the infinity of the creative power of GOD if one encloses the space of its revelation, described in a sphere with the radius of the Milky Way, than if one wants to restrict it to a ball that has one inch in diameter. Everything that is finite, that has its limitations and a determinate relation to unity, is equally far removed from the infinite. Now it would be absurd to set the Godhead into activity with an infinitely small part of its creative faculty, and to think its infinite power, the treasure of a true immeasurability, of natures and worlds, as inactive, and locked up in an eternal lack of exercise. Is it not rather more fitting, or better to say, is it not necessary, to arrange the sum total of creation as it must be in order to be a testimony of that power which can be measured off by no measuring-rod? On this ground the field of the revelation of divine attributes is just as infinite as these themselves are (*). Eternity is not sufficient to comprehend the testimonies of the highest being, unless it is combined with the infinity of space. It is true, the formation, the form, the beauty and perfection, are relations of the basic constituents and of the substances that make up the material of the world-edifice; and one notices it from the arrangements which the wisdom of GOD still makes at all times; it is also most suitable to it that they unfold themselves, out of these general laws implanted in it, through an unforced sequence. And hence one can with good ground posit that the ordering and arrangement of the world-edifices, out of the store of the created natural material, occurs in a sequence of time, little by little; but the basic matter itself, whose properties and forces lie at the ground of all alterations, is an immediate sequel of the divine existence: it must therefore be at once so rich, so complete, that the development of its compositions in the flow of eternity can spread itself over a plan that encloses within itself everything that can be, that admits of no measure, in short, that is infinite.",["- Inbegriff = sum total; Dasein = existence; Grund/Grunde/zum Grunde = ground; Grundmaterie = \"basic matter\"; Grundstücke = \"basic constituents\" (not in glossary).","- Folge occurs twice: \"ungezwungene Folge\" and \"Folge der Zeit\" = sequence; \"unmittelbare Folge\" = sequel (glossary gives both).","- Vermögen = faculty; Kraft/Kräfte = power/forces; Macht = power (two German words, one English \"power\": Macht, Kraft in the singular).","- Wesen = being; Schranken = limitations; Grenzen = bounds; Stoff = material; Naturstoff = \"natural material\"; Materie = matter.","- \"es\" in \"wenn man ... es an den Anstalten\" and \"ihr ... sie sich\": \"sie\" (plural) with \"ihr\" (feminine) left as in German; the antecedent of \"ihr\" is Weisheit, of \"sie\" the formations/relations (ambiguous).","- Original \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"world-edifice\"; \"Weltgebäude\" likewise \"world-edifices\".","- \"Offenbarung\" = revelation; \"Zeugniß\" = testimony; \"Maaßstab\" = measuring-rod."]],"naturgeschichte-0181":["(*) The concept of an infinite extension of the world finds opponents among the teachers of metaphysics, and has recently found one in Herr M. Weitenkampf. If these gentlemen, on account of the alleged impossibility of a multitude without number and bounds, cannot accommodate themselves to this idea; then I would only ask provisionally: whether the future sequence of eternity will not comprise within itself a true infinity of manifolds and alterations? and whether this infinite series is not already at this very moment, all at once, entirely present to the divine understanding? If it was now possible that GOD can make actual, in a series following one upon another, the concept of infinity that stands before his understanding all at once: why should he not be able to present the concept of another infinity in a connection combined according to space, and thereby to make the domain of the world without bounds? Meanwhile, as one will seek to answer this question, I shall avail myself of the occasion that will offer itself, by a clarification drawn from the nature of numbers, to remove the supposed difficulty, provided one can, on exact consideration, still regard it as a question needing an exposition: whether that which a power accompanied by the highest wisdom has produced, in order to reveal itself, behaves toward that which it was able to produce as a differential magnitude?",["- Menge = multitude; Zahl = number; Grenzen = bounds; Umfang = domain; Ausdehnung = extension; Verstand = understanding.","- Mannigfaltigkeiten (plural) = \"manifolds\" (glossary has Mannigfaltige n.); Erläuterung = clarification; Erörterung = exposition; Erwegung = \"consideration\" (not glossary; cf. Betrachtung).","- \"Zusammenhang\" = \"connection\" here; glossary gives interconnection/nexus. \"Folge\" = sequence; \"Idee\" = idea; \"Begriff\" = concept.","- \"war\" in \"Wenn es nun möglich war\" kept as past tense, as in the German.","- \"Differenzialgrösse\" = \"differential magnitude\"; \"Metaphysikkündigern\" rendered \"teachers of metaphysics\" (lit. \"announcers/proclaimers\"; doubtful reading of the compound)."]],"naturgeschichte-0182":["If, then, creation is infinite according to spaces, or at least has actually been so from the beginning according to matter, but is ready to become so according to form or formation; then the space of the world will be animated with worlds without number and without end. Will, then, that systematic combination which we considered before in all the parts in particular also extend to the whole, and comprehend the whole universe, the All of nature, in a single system, through the combination of attraction and the repulsive force? I say yes; if only separate world-edifices were present, which had among one another no united relation to a whole, one could well, if one assumed this chain of members as actually infinite, think that an exact equality of the attraction of their parts from all sides could keep these systems secure from the decay with which their inner mutual attraction threatens them. But to this belongs so exactly measured a determination in the distances weighed according to attraction, that even the slightest displacement would draw ruin upon the universe, and would deliver them to overthrow in long periods, which however must finally run to an end. A constitution of the world that did not sustain itself without a miracle has not the character of constancy, which is the mark of the choice of GOD; one therefore meets it far more fittingly if one makes of the whole creation a single system, which makes all worlds and world-orders, which fill the whole infinite space, related to a single center. A scattered swarm of world-edifices, even if they were separated from one another by ever such wide distances, would hasten with an unhindered tendency to ruin and destruction, if a certain relating arrangement toward a general center, the *centrum* of attraction of the universe and the point of support of the whole of nature, were not made through systematic motions.",["- Verbindung = combination; Bestimmung = determination; Merkmal = mark; Wechselanziehung = \"mutual attraction\" (not Wechselwirkung/interaction).","- \"fliehende Kraft\" = \"repulsive force\" is an interpretive rendering; literally \"fleeing force\".","- \"Attraction\" and \"Universo/Universi\" and \"Systemata\" are Latin/foreign words in the original (roman type); \"Attraction\" rendered \"attraction\"; \"Centrum\" italicized.","- \"es\" in \"man trifft es also dieser weit anständiger\" is obscure; rendered \"one therefore meets it far more fittingly\"; \"dieser\" (dative, \"to this/her\") left unresolved in my English; the antecedent is ambiguous. Doubtful: I left \"dieser\" untranslated as it has no clear referent in the English.","- \"sie\" in \"und sie ... dem Umsturze überliefern\" refers back to \"diese Systemata\" (plural), rendered \"them\".","- Weltverfassung = \"constitution of the world\"; Wahl = choice; Weltordnungen = world-orders.","- \"der Räume nach\" = \"according to spaces\" (plural, as in German); \"der Materie / der Form nach\" likewise \"according to\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0183":["About this general center of the descent of the whole of nature, of the formed as well as of the raw, in which without doubt is found the lump of the most extraordinary attraction, which comprehends in its sphere of attraction all worlds and orders that time has produced and eternity will produce, one can assume with probability that nature made the beginning of its formation, and that there too the systems are most densely heaped; but farther from it, in the infinity of space, they lose themselves with ever greater degrees of dispersion. One could take this rule from the analogy of our solar edifice, and this constitution can moreover serve to this, that at great distances not only the general central body but also all the systems running nearest around it unite their attraction together, and exert it as it were from one lump toward the systems of still farther distance. This will then help toward comprehending the whole of nature, in the whole infinity of its extension, in a single system.",["- Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Grad = degree; Ordnungen = orders; Verfassung = \"constitution\"; Erstreckung = \"extension\" (cf. Ausdehnung, also \"extension\").","- begreiffet / begreifen = comprehend(s) (twice, both uses).","- \"Senkung\" = \"descent\" (doubtful rendering; literally a sinking/lowering); \"Klumpen\" = \"lump\"; \"roh\" = \"raw\".","- \"um ... kan man ... annehmen\" is a long construction; \"Um\" rendered \"About\" to preserve the ambiguity (around/about).","- \"Systemen\" and \"Systemata\" both rendered \"systems\"; \"Sonnenbau\" = \"solar edifice\".","- \"dazu dienen, daß\" rendered \"serve to this, that\", kept awkward."]],"naturgeschichte-0184":["In order now to trace the erection of this general system of nature, from the mechanical laws of matter striving toward formation; so in the infinite space of the extended elementary basic material, in some place or other, this basic material must have had the densest accumulation, in order, through the preferential formation taking place there, to have procured for the entire *Universum* a mass that would serve it as a point of support. It is indeed the case that in an infinite space no point can properly have the prerogative of being called the center; but, by means of a certain relation, which is grounded on the essential degrees of density of the original material, according to which this, simultaneously with its creation, is accumulated preferentially more densely in a certain place, and increases in dispersion with the distances from the same, such a point can have the prerogative of being called the center, and it also actually becomes so, through the formation of the central mass, by the most powerful attraction in the same, to which all remaining elementary matter, engaged in particular formations, sinks, and thereby, however far the unfolding of nature may extend, in the infinite sphere of creation, makes out of the whole All only a single system.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0185":["This is, however, something important, and which, insofar as it obtains approval, is worthy of the greatest attention, that according to the order of nature in this our system, creation, or rather the formation of nature, begins first at this center, and with continuous progression is spread out little by little into all more remote distances, in order to fill infinite space in the progress of eternity with worlds and orders. Let us indulge this representation for a moment with quiet pleasure. I find nothing that can raise the mind of man to a nobler astonishment, in that it opens to him a prospect into the infinite field of omnipotence, than this part of the theory, which concerns the successive completion of creation. If one grants me that matter, which is the material for the formation of all worlds, was spread out in the whole infinite space of the divine presence not uniformly, but according to a certain law, which perhaps related to the density of the particles, and according to which from a certain point, as the place of the densest accumulation, the dispersion of the original material increased with the distances from this center; then, in the original stirring of nature, the formation will have begun nearest this *centrum*, and then, in progressing succession of time, the further space will have formed, little by little, worlds and world-orders, with a systematic constitution relating to this one. Every finite *periodus*, whose length has a relation to the magnitude of the work to be accomplished, will always bring to formation only a finite sphere, from this center onward; the remaining infinite part will in the meantime still be struggling with confusion and chaos, and will be removed so much the further from the state of completed formation, the further its distance is removed from the sphere of nature already formed. In accordance with this, although from the place of our abode in the *Universum* we have a view into a, as it seems, completely completed world, and, so to speak, into an infinite host of world-orders that are systematically combined; yet we find ourselves properly only in a proximity to the center of the whole of nature, where it has already unfolded itself out of chaos and has attained its proper perfection. If we could cross beyond a certain sphere; we would behold there the chaos and the dispersion of the elements, which, in the measure that they are found nearer to this center, partly abandon the raw state, and are nearer to the perfection of execution, but with the degrees of distance gradually lose themselves in a complete dispersion. We would see how the infinite space of the divine presence, in which the store for all possible formations of nature is to be encountered, buried in a still night, full of matter to serve as material for the worlds to be generated in the future, and of springs to set them in motion, which, with a weak stirring, begin those motions with which the immensity of these desolate spaces is one day still to be enlivened. A series of millions of years and centuries has perhaps flowed by, before the sphere of formed nature, in which we find ourselves, has thrived to the perfection that now inheres in it; and an equally long *periodus* will perhaps pass, until nature takes an equally wide step in chaos: but the sphere of the formed nature is unceasingly occupied with extending itself. Creation is not the work of a moment. After it has made the beginning with the production of an infinity of substances and matter; so it is, with ever increasing degrees of fruitfulness, effective through the whole sequence of eternity. Millions, and whole mountain ranges of millions of centuries will flow by, within which ever new worlds and world-orders, one after another, in the distant remoteness from the center of nature, will form themselves and attain perfection; they will, notwithstanding the systematic constitution that is among their parts, attain a general relation to the center, which has become the first point of formation, and the *centrum* of creation through the attractive faculty of its preferential mass. The infinity of the future succession of time, with which eternity is inexhaustible, will enliven all spaces of the presence of GOD entirely and completely, and little by little transfer them into the regularity that is in accordance with the excellence of his design, and if one could, with a bold representation, comprehend the whole of eternity, so to say, in one concept; then one would be able to regard also the whole infinite space as filled with world-orders, and creation as completed. But because in fact of the temporal succession of eternity the remaining part is always infinite, and the elapsed part finite; so the sphere of formed nature is always only an infinitely small part of that sum total which has the seed of future worlds in itself, and strives to unfold itself out of the raw state of chaos, in longer or shorter periods. Creation is never completed. It has indeed once begun, but it will never cease. It is always busy producing more scenes of nature, new things and new worlds. The work that it brings to completion has a relation to the time that it applies to it. It needs nothing less than an eternity, to enliven the whole boundless expanse of the infinite spaces with worlds without number and without end. One can say of it what the most sublime among the German poets writes of eternity:",[]],"naturgeschichte-0186":["Infinity! who measures you? Before you worlds are day, and men moments; Perhaps the thousandth of the suns is rolling now, And a thousand still remain behind. Like a clock, animated by a weight, A sun hastens, moved by GOD's power: Its drive runs down, and another strikes, But you remain, and do not count them.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0187":["v. Haller.",["- 0184: Grundstoff = \"basic material\"; Urstoff = \"original material\"; Stoff = \"material\" (Grundstoff/Urstoff/Stoff all distinct in German, rendered distinctly). Materie = \"matter\".","- 0184: *Universo* (dative of Latin *Universum*) italicized as Latin in roman type; *Mittelpunkt* = \"center\"; Centralmasse = \"central mass\".","- 0184: \"ist es an dem, daß\" rendered \"it is the case that\"; \"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\"; \"Bildung\" = \"formation\".","- 0185: Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Geist = \"mind\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Zahl = \"number\".","- 0185: Fortschreitung and Fortgang both appear (\"progression\", \"progress\"); Zeitfolge = \"succession of time\"/\"temporal succession\"; Folge = \"sequence\" in \"Folge der Ewigkeit\".","- 0185: \"Diesem zu Folge\" = \"In accordance with this\"; \"gegen diesen sich beziehenden\" = \"relating to this one\" (the antecedent of \"diesen\" is ambiguous in German and left so).","- 0185: *Centro*, *centrum*, *periodus* are Latin/Latinate forms printed in the source; \"Welten und Weltordnungen\" rendered \"worlds and world-orders\"; \"Anziehungsvermögen\" = \"attractive faculty\".","- 0185: Haller's verse lines are run together because line breaks are not preserved in the passage as given; \"beseelt\" rendered \"animated\"; \"Trieb\" = \"drive\"; \"schlägt\" left as \"strikes\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0188":["It is no small pleasure to stray with one's imagination beyond the boundary of the completed creation, into the space of chaos, and to see the half-raw nature, in proximity to the sphere of the formed world, lose itself little by little through all the steps and shadings of imperfection, in the whole unformed space. But is it not a reprehensible boldness, one will say, to throw out a hypothesis, and to commend it, as an object for the delight of the understanding, which is perhaps only far too arbitrary, if one asserts that nature has been formed only according to an infinitely small part, and that infinite spaces are still struggling with chaos, in order in the sequence of future times to present whole hosts of worlds and world-orders, in all fitting order and beauty? I am not so much devoted to the consequences that my theory offers that I should not recognize how the conjecture, of the successive expansion of creation, through the infinite spaces that contain the material for it within themselves, cannot entirely repel the objection of unprovability. Meanwhile I nevertheless promise myself from those who are in a position to estimate the degrees of probability, that such a chart of infinity, although it comprehends an object that seems to be determined to be hidden from human understanding forever, will not for that reason straightaway be regarded as a figment of the brain, especially if one takes to one's aid the analogy, which must guide us in every case where the thread of infallible proofs is lacking to the understanding.",["- Einbildungskraft = imagination; Verstand = understanding; Stoff = material; Grade der Wahrscheinlichkeit = degrees of probability; Beweise = proofs; Grenze = boundary; Folgen = consequences (Folge in \"in der Folge künftiger Zeiten\" = sequence).","- Vorwurf appears twice (\"einen Vorwurf der Ergötzung\", \"einen Vorwurf begreiffet\") and is rendered \"object\" both times; it is not Gegenstand/Object.","- \"Naheit\" (proximity) and \"Chaos\" are as printed; \"Weltordnungen\" = world-orders; \"Schöpfung\" = creation.","- \"ausgebildet\" / \"ungebildet\" = formed / unformed (Bildung = formation, elsewhere).","- \"begreiffet\" rendered \"comprehends\" (begreifen), not Latin-marked in the text.","- \"bestimmt zu seyn\": \"determined to be hidden\" preserves the ambiguity of bestimmen (determined/destined).","- \"Muthmassung\" = conjecture; \"Unerweislichkeit\" = unprovability; \"Charte\" = chart; \"Hirngespinste\" = figment of the brain.","- \"nur einem unendlich kleinen Theile nach\" rendered literally; \"Heere\" = hosts."]],"naturgeschichte-0189":["But one can also support the analogy still further through grounds worthy of acceptance, and the insight of the reader, if I may flatter myself with such approval, will perhaps be able to augment it with still more important ones. For if one considers that creation does not carry with it the character of persistence, unless it sets against the universal striving of attraction, which works through all its parts, an equally thoroughgoing determination, which can sufficiently resist the inclination of the former toward ruin and disorder, unless it has distributed forces of revolution, which in combination with the central inclination establish a universal systematic constitution; then one is compelled to assume a universal center of the whole universe, which holds all parts of it together in combined relation, and makes of the whole sum total of nature only one system. If one adds to this the concept, of the formation of the world-bodies out of the scattered elementary matter, as we have sketched it in the preceding, yet here does not restrict it to a particular system, but extends it over the whole of nature; then one is compelled to think of such a distribution of the basic material, in the space of the original chaos, that naturally brings with it a center of the whole of creation, so that in this the effective mass, which in its sphere comprehends the whole of nature, could be brought together, and the thoroughgoing relation effected, through which all worlds make up only a single edifice. But in infinite space hardly any way of distribution of the original basic material can be thought which should posit a true center and point of descent of the whole of nature, other than if it is arranged according to a law of increasing scattering, from this point on, into all distant expanses. But this law at the same time posits a difference in the time that a system in the various regions of infinite space requires to come to the maturity of its formation, so that this period is the shorter, the nearer the place of formation of a world-edifice is found to the center of creation, because there the elements of the material are more densely heaped, and on the other hand requires so much the longer time, the farther the distance is, because the particles there are more scattered, and come together later for formation.",["- Analogie = analogy; Gründe = grounds; Einsicht = insight; Begriff = concept; Inbegriff = sum total; Beziehung = relation; Verbindung/verbunden = combination/combined.","- Beständigkeit = persistence (glossary renders Beharrlichkeit thus; Beständigkeit is a different German word, here also \"persistence\").","- Bestimmung = determination; Verfassung = constitution (Beschaffenheit would be constitution/property as well: one English word for two German words).","- Grundstoff = basic material; Stoff = material; Materie = matter; Masse = mass.","- Schwungskräfte = forces of revolution; Centralneigung = central inclination; Mittelpunkt = center; Mittel und Senkungspunkt = \"center and point of descent.\"","- \"Weltkörper\" = world-bodies; \"Weltbau\" = world-edifice; \"Gebäude\" = edifice.","- \"in verbundener Beziehung\" and \"durch die ... Beziehung\" kept literal; \"Gesetze\" = law (Gesetz).","- \"Zeit\" = time throughout; \"Periode\" = period."]],"naturgeschichte-0190":["If one considers the whole hypothesis that I sketch, in the whole domain both of what I have said and of what I shall actually still set out; then one will at least not hold the boldness of its demands incapable of accepting an excuse. One can count the unavoidable inclination that every world-edifice brought to perfection has little by little toward its demise among the grounds that can prove that the universe will on the other hand be fruitful in worlds in other regions, in order to replace the lack that it has suffered in one place. The whole piece of nature that we know, although it is only an atom with regard to what remains hidden above or below our horizon, nevertheless confirms this fruitfulness of nature, which is without limitations, because it is nothing other than the exercise of divine omnipotence itself. Countless animals and plants are destroyed daily, and are a sacrifice to transitoriness; but nature brings no less forth again in other places, through an unexhausted faculty of generation, and fills up the empty. Considerable pieces of the earth's surface that we inhabit are buried again in the sea, from which a favorable period had drawn them forth; but in other places nature supplements the lack, and brings forth other regions, which were hidden in the depth of the essence, in order to spread new riches of its fruitfulness over the same. In the same way worlds and world-orders pass away, and are swallowed by the abyss of eternities; on the other hand creation is ceaselessly busy, in other regions of heaven, accomplishing new formations, and supplementing the loss with advantage.",["- Umfang = domain; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Gründe = grounds; Schranken = limitations; Zeugungsvermögen = faculty of generation; Wesen = essence (here \"Tiefe des Wesens\"; Wesen elsewhere \"being\").","- kennen = know (the glossary's \"be acquainted with\"); Atomus = atom, as printed.","- \"über dieselbe\": \"the same\" is ambiguous in referent (the German \"dieselbe\" is feminine singular); left unresolved.","- \"das leere\" = \"the empty,\" neuter substantive kept.","- \"Periodus\" = period; \"Vergänglichkeit\" = transitoriness; \"Untergang\" = demise.","- \"Himmelsgegenden\" = regions of heaven; \"Bildungen\" = formations."]],"naturgeschichte-0191":["One need not be astonished to allow a transitoriness even in the greatness of the works of GOD. Everything that is finite, that has a beginning and origin, has the mark of its restricted nature in itself; it must pass away, and have an end. The duration of a world-edifice has, through the excellence of its establishment, a persistence in itself that, according to our concepts, comes near to an infinite duration. Perhaps a thousand, perhaps millions of centuries will not annihilate it; but, because the vanity that clings to finite natures works constantly on its destruction; so eternity will hold all possible periods in itself, in order through a gradual decay yet finally to bring about the point of time of its demise. Newton, this great admirer of the attributes of GOD, from the perfection of his works, who joined with the deepest insight into the excellence of nature the greatest reverence toward the revelation of divine omnipotence, saw himself compelled to announce beforehand to nature its decline through the natural inclination that the mechanics of motions has toward it. If a systematic constitution, through the essential sequel of frailty, in great periods of time approaches even the very smallest part that one may think of to the state of its confusion; so there must in the infinite course of eternity yet be a point of time at which this gradual diminution has exhausted all motion.",["- Merkmaal = mark (Merkmal; nota); Begriffe = concepts; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Zustand = state; Folge = sequel; Verfassung = constitution.","- Beständigkeit = persistence (one English word for Beständigkeit; Beharrlichkeit is not here).","- \"Dauer\" = duration; \"Zeitpunkt\" = point of time; \"Zeitläuften\" = periods of time.","- \"ihrer Zerstörung,\" \"ihres Unterganges\": \"its\" (feminine ihr-) refers ambiguously; left as in German.","- \"Eitelkeit\" = vanity; \"Hinfälligkeit\" = frailty; \"Verfall\" = decay/decline (two English words for Verfall, \"decay\" and \"decline,\" at its two occurrences).","- \"so muß ... doch ein Zeitpunkt seyn\": \"so\" after \"Wenn\" clause kept as \"so\" (the semicolon is retained as in the German).","- \"Mechanik der Bewegungen\" = mechanics of motions; \"Allmacht\" = omnipotence; \"Offenbarung\" = revelation."]],"naturgeschichte-0192":["We may not, however, lament the demise of a world-edifice as a true loss of nature. It proves its wealth in a kind of squandering, which, in that some parts pay their tribute to transience, maintains itself through countless new generations in the whole domain of its perfection undiminished. What a countless multitude of flowers and insects a single cold day destroys; but how little one misses them, although they are splendid works of art of nature and proofs of divine omnipotence; in another place this loss is replaced again with abundance. The human being, who seems to be the masterpiece of creation, is himself not exempted from this law. Nature proves that it is just as rich, just as inexhaustible, in the production of the most excellent among the creatures as of the most insignificant, and that even their demise is a necessary shading in the manifoldness of its suns, because the generation of them costs it nothing. The harmful effects of infected air, the earthquakes, the floods, wipe whole peoples from the face of the earth; yet it does not seem that nature has thereby suffered any detriment. In the same way whole worlds and systems leave the stage, after they have played out their role. The infinity of creation is great enough to regard a world, or a Milky Way of worlds, in relation to it, as one regards a flower, or an insect, in comparison with the earth. Meanwhile, while nature adorns eternity with changeable scenes, GOD remains active in an unceasing creation, in forming the material for the building of still greater worlds.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0193":["Who, always with an equal eye, since he, the Creator, indeed of all, Sees a hero perish, and a little sparrow fall, Sees a water bubble burst, and a whole world pass away.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0194":["Pope, after Brockes's translation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0195":["Let us therefore accustom our eye to these terrible overthrows as to the usual ways of providence, and even regard them with a kind of pleasure. And in fact nothing is more fitting to the wealth of nature than this. For if a world-system in the long sequence of its duration exhausts all the manifoldness that its arrangement can hold, if it has now become a superfluous link in the chain of beings; then nothing is more becoming than that it play, in the spectacle of the elapsing alterations of the *universum*, the last role that is due to every finite thing, namely to pay its due to transience. Nature shows, as was said, already in the small part of its sum total, this rule of its procedure, which eternal fate has prescribed to it in the whole, and I say it once more, the magnitude of that which is to perish is not in the least an obstacle in this, for everything that is great becomes small, indeed it becomes as it were only a point, when one compares it with the infinite, which creation will present in unbounded space, through the sequence of eternity.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0196":["It seems that this end, imposed on the worlds as on all natural things, is subject to a certain law, the consideration of which gives the theory a new trait of fittingness. According to it, it begins with those world-bodies that find themselves nearest to the center point of the world-all, just as generation and formation first began next to this center: from there ruin and destruction spread gradually into the farther distances, in order to bury all worlds that have completed their period, through a gradual decay of the motions, finally in a single chaos. On the other hand, nature on the opposite boundary of the formed world is unceasingly occupied in forming worlds out of the raw material of the scattered elements, and, while on the one side next to the center point it grows old, on the other it is young and fruitful in new generations. The formed world accordingly finds itself confined in the middle between the ruins of the destroyed and the chaos of unformed nature, and if one represents to oneself, as is probable, that a world that has already thriven to perfection can last a longer time than it needed to be formed; then, despite all the devastations that transience unceasingly causes, the domain of the *universum* will nevertheless on the whole increase.",["- 0192: *Weltgebäude* = \"world-edifice\"; *Umfang* = \"domain\"; *Vollkommenheit* = \"perfection\"; *Wirkungen* = \"effects\"; *Mannigfaltigkeit* = \"manifoldness\"; *Zeug* = \"material\" (rendered \"material\" here, \"raw material\" in 0196 for *rohen Zeuge*).","- 0192: \"which, in that some parts pay..., maintains itself\": *sich ... erhält* is far separated from its subject *welche*; the relative's antecedent could be *Verschwendung* or *Reichthum*, and I kept the German order. *Zeug zur Bildung* kept literal.","- 0192: \"Der Mensch\" rendered \"the human being\"; \"himself\" follows the German masculine *selbst*. *Creaturen* = \"creatures\"; *Schöpfung* = \"creation\".","- 0193: The German verse is syntactically incomplete (a relative-like \"Der ...\" with no main verb until \"Sieht\"); kept as it stands, possibly corrupt or elliptical in transmission. Subject of \"Sieht\" is ambiguous.","- 0194: Attribution line, translated as is; *nach Brockes Uebersetzung* = \"after Brockes's translation\".","- 0195: *Folge* = \"sequence\" (twice, *Folge seiner Dauer*, *Folge der Ewigkeit*); *Inbegriff* = \"sum total\"; *Wesen* = \"beings\"; *Universi* is Latin genitive, rendered *universum* in italics. *Gebühr abtrage* = \"pay its due\"; *ihr* in \"ihr Gebühr\" refers back to Vergänglichkeit/each thing—kept ambiguous.","- 0195: *Weltsystem* = \"world-system\"; *Raum* = \"space\"; *Punkt* = \"point\"; *das Unendliche* = \"the infinite\" (its antecedent *welches* kept ambiguous).","- 0196: *Grenze* = \"boundary\"; *Mittelpunkt* = \"center point\"; *Welt-All* = \"world-all\"; *Weltkörper* = \"world-bodies\"; *Bewegungen* = \"motions\"; *Zeit* = \"time\"; *Umfang* = \"domain\"; *Vollkommenheit* = \"perfection\"; *wahrscheinlich* = \"probable\".","- 0196: \"es\" in \"hebt es ... an\" and \"nach demselben\" (referring to *Gesetz*) kept unresolved; *einen gewissen Gesetze* is as printed (grammatical slip in the original, not emended)."]],"naturgeschichte-0197":["But if one wishes finally to leave room for an idea that is just as probable as it is becoming to the constitution of the divine works, then the contentment that such a depiction of the changes of nature arouses is raised to the highest degree of pleasure. Can one not believe that nature, which was capable of setting itself out of chaos into a regular order and into a skillful system, is likewise in a position to restore itself again just as easily out of the new chaos in which the diminution of its motions has sunk it, and to renew the first combination? Can the springs that brought the material of the scattered matter into motion and order, after the standstill of the machine has brought them to rest, not be set into activity again through expanded forces, and restrict themselves to agreement according to just the same general rules through which the original formation was brought about? One will not hesitate long to admit this, if one considers that, after the final languor of the orbital motions in the structure of the world has cast the planets and comets all together down upon the sun, its glow must receive an immeasurable increase through the mixing of so many and such great lumps, especially since the distant globes of the solar system, according to our previously proved theory, contain in themselves the lightest material, the most effective in fire, of the whole of nature. This fire, put into the greatest violence by new nourishment and the most volatile matter, will without doubt not only dissolve everything again into the smallest elements, but also spread and scatter them again in this manner, with a force of extension commensurate with the heat, and with a swiftness that is weakened by no resistance of the intermediate space, into the same wide spaces that they had occupied before the first formation of nature, in order, after the violence of the central fire has been damped by an almost complete scattering of its mass, to repeat the old generations and systematically related motions, through combination of the forces of attraction and repulsion, with no less regularity, and to present a new structure of the world. If then a particular planetary system has fallen into decay in this way and has restored itself again out of it through essential forces, if it perhaps even repeats this play more than once; then at last the period will draw near that in like manner will gather in a chaos, through the decay of their motions, the great system in which the fixed stars are members. One will doubt here still less that the union of so infinite a multitude of treasures of fire as these burning suns are, together with the retinue of their planets, will scatter the material of their masses, dissolved by the unnameable glow, into the old space of their sphere of formation, and will yield there the materials for new formations through the same mechanical laws, from which the desolate space can again be enlivened with worlds and systems. If then we follow this phoenix of nature, which burns itself only in order to live again rejuvenated from its ashes, through all the infinity of times and spaces: if one sees how, even in the region where it decays and grows old, it is unexhausted in new scenes, and on the other boundary of creation, in the space of unformed raw matter, advances with steady steps toward the extension of the plan of the divine revelation, in order to fill eternity as well as all spaces with its wonders; then the mind that reflects on all this sinks into a deep astonishment; but still dissatisfied with this so great object, whose transitoriness the soul cannot satisfy sufficiently, it wishes to get to know from close at hand that being whose understanding, whose greatness is the source of that light that spreads over the whole of nature, as it were from a central point. With what kind of reverence must the soul not look upon even its own being, when it considers that it is still to outlive all these changes; it can say to itself what the philosophical poet says of eternity:",[]],"naturgeschichte-0198":["When then a second nothing shall bury this world; When of the All itself nothing remains but the place; When many a heaven, still bright with other stars, Will have completed its course; You will, as young as now, equally far from your death, Be equally eternal in future as today.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0199":["v. Haller.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0200":["O happy if, amid the tumult of the elements and the dreams of nature, it is at all times set upon a height from which it can see, as it were beneath its feet, the devastations that the frailty of the things of the world causes rush past. A happiness that reason may not even dare to wish for, revelation teaches us to hope for with conviction. If then the fetters that hold us bound to the vanity of creatures have fallen away in the moment that has been determined for the transformation of our being, then the immortal spirit, freed from the dependence on finite things, will find, in community with the infinite being, the enjoyment of true happiness. The whole of nature, which has a general harmonious relation to the pleasure of the Deity, cannot fill otherwise than with perpetual contentment that rational creature that finds itself united with this primal source of all perfection. Nature, seen from this central point, will show on all sides nothing but security, nothing but becomingness. The changeable scenes of nature are not capable of displacing the state of repose of the happiness of a spirit that has once been raised to such a height. While it already tastes this state in advance with a sweet hope, it can exercise its mouth in those hymns of praise with which all eternities shall one day resound.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0201":["When one day the structure of the world, hastened back into its nothing, And your hands' work is no longer divided by day and night; Then my moved mind, strengthened through you, shall strive, In veneration of your omnipotence, always to move before your throne: My mouth, filled with thanks, shall through all eternities Prepare for you and your majesty an infinite praise; Though it be no perfect one, for O Lord! so great are you, To praise you according to worthiness, eternity does not suffice.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0202":["Addisson. After Gottsched's translation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0203":["Supplement\nto the seventh chapter.\nGeneral theory and history of\nthe sun in general.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0204":["There is still a main question whose resolution is indispensable in the doctrine of nature of the heavens, and in a complete cosmogony. Whence, namely, is the central point of every system occupied by a flaming body? Our planetary structure of the world has the sun as central body, and the fixed stars that we see are, to all appearances, central points of similar *systemata*.",["- 0197: *Stoff* rendered \"material\" and *Materie* \"matter\"; *Mittel* in *Mittelraum* appears as \"intermediate\"; *Verbindung* = \"combination\"; *Kräfte* = \"forces\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\"; *Wesen* = \"being\"; *Zufriedenheit* = \"contentment\".","- 0197: *Wohlanständig*, *Wohlanständigkeit* (0200) rendered \"becoming\"/\"becomingness\"; *Wohlgefallen* = \"pleasure\" (0197), \"pleasure\" (0200) of the Deity.","- 0197: *Ausdehnungskraft* = \"force of extension\"; *Zurückstossungskräfte* = \"forces of repulsion\"; *Attractions* left as German's loan word, rendered \"attraction\".","- 0197: *Der Verfassung* could attach as dative to *wahrscheinlich ... wohlanständig*; kept as \"becoming to the constitution\". *Dieser ihre Glut* = \"its glow\", the antecedent being the sun; *sie* in \"weakened by\" refers to the elements (*dieselbe*).","- 0197: In \"folgen: wenn man siehet, wie sie ... fortschreitet\", *sie* (nature) kept as \"it\"; the \"so\" clause after the colon is kept as a single sentence.","- 0198–0201: verse lines are run together, the line breaks being absent from the passage; *Stelle* = \"place\"; *Gemüthe* = \"mind\"; *Allmacht* = \"omnipotence\".","- 0200: *Vernunft* = \"reason\"; *Geist* = \"spirit\"; *Gemeinschaft* = \"community\"; *Vollkommenheit* = \"perfection\"; *Zustand* = \"state\"; *Offenbarung* = \"revelation\"; the typographical doubling \"dadavon\" read as \"davon\".","- 0204: *Naturlehre* = \"doctrine of nature\"; *Cörper* = \"body\"; *Systematum* is Latin, italicized, with English plural ending omitted."]],"naturgeschichte-0205":["In order to comprehend why, in the formation of a world-edifice, the body that serves as the center point of attraction had to become a fiery body, while the remaining spheres of its sphere of attraction remained dark and cold world-bodies, one need only recall the way of generation of a world-structure that we have sketched in detail in what precedes. In the widely extended space, in which the dispersed elementary basic material prepares itself for formations and systematic motions, the planets and comets form only out of that part of the elementary basic material sinking to the center point of attraction which, through the fall and the interaction of the totality of particles, has been determined to the exact limitation of the direction and velocity that is required for the revolution. This part is, as was shown above, the least of the whole multitude of the matter sinking downward, and indeed only the refuse of denser kinds, which, through the resistance of the others, were able to attain this degree of exactness. There are found in this mixture, hovering-in kinds of superior lightness, which, hindered by the resistance of space, do not penetrate through their fall to the proper swiftness of the periodic revolutions, and which consequently, in the languor of their swing, are all plunged down to the central body. Since now these very lighter and more volatile parts are also the most effective in sustaining the fire; so we see that through their addition the body and center point of the system obtains the preeminence of becoming a flaming sphere, in a word, a sun. By contrast the heavier and impotent material, and the lack of these fire-nourishing particles, will make of the planets only cold and dead lumps, which are deprived of such a property.",["- Grundstoff = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material, per glossary); Weltgebäude / Weltbau = \"world-edifice\" / \"world-structure\"; Weltkörper = \"world-bodies\"; Wechselwirkung = interaction.","- Attraction is in the German in roman type (Latin-derived), kept as \"attraction\"; Anziehungssphäre = \"sphere of attraction\".","- \"heranschwebende\" rendered literally as \"hovering-in\"; \"Umschwung\" = \"revolution\", \"Umwendungen\" = \"revolutions\" (two German words, one English word).","- \"Menge\" = \"multitude\"; \"Zusatz\" = \"addition\"; \"Eigenschaft\" = \"property\" (the glossary gives Beschaffenheit also as \"property\").","- Ambiguity kept: \"des ... Grundstoffes, welcher ... bestimmt worden\" (the OCR-faithful \"Theile, des\" retained as \"part ... of\"); \"die\" before \"durch ihren Fall\" could have \"Sorten\" as sole antecedent, kept as is.","- \"dichterer Sorten, welche ... haben gelangen können\": \"were able to attain\" follows the plural \"Sorten\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0206":["This addition of such light materials is also that through which the sun has acquired the specifically lesser density, by which it even stands behind our earth, the third planet in distance from it, 4 times in density; although it is natural to believe that in this center point of the world-structure, as in its lowest place, the heaviest and densest genera of matter should be found, through which it would, without the addition of so great a multitude of the lightest material, surpass the density of all planets.",["- Gattungen = \"genera\" (glossary: Gattung = genus); Sorten = \"kinds\"; Art elsewhere = \"way\".","- Orte = \"place\" (Ort); Stoff = \"material\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Menge = \"multitude\".","- \"4 mal an Dichtigkeit nachstehet\" rendered literally; \"dadurch\" (twice) left as in German.","- \"ohne dem Zusatz\" (dative after ohne) is eighteenth-century usage; rendered as ordinary \"without the addition\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0207":["The intermixture of denser and heavier kinds of the elements with these lightest and most volatile ones likewise serves to make the central body suited to the most violent glow, which is to be burned and sustained on its surface. For we know that the fire, in whose nourishing material dense matters are found mixed among the volatile ones, has a great preeminence of violence over those flames that are sustained only by the light genera. This admixture, however, of some heavy kinds among the lighter, is a necessary consequence of our doctrine of the formation of the world-bodies, and has still this utility, that the force of the glow does not suddenly disperse the combustible matter of the surface, and that the same is nourished gradually and constantly through the inflow of nourishment from the interior.",["- Lehrbegriff = \"doctrine\" (Lehre, with Begriff); rendered literally \"doctrine\" here, not \"concept of doctrine\".","- Folge = \"consequence\" (Folge is \"sequel; sequence\" in the glossary; \"consequence\" here chosen for Folge in \"nothwendige Folge\", and Folgerung is \"consequence\" too: one English word for two German words).","- Gattungen = \"genera\"; Sorten = \"kinds\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Materie = \"matter\".","- \"welche ... geschickt zu machen\": \"serves ... to make ... suited\" keeps the German construction; \"selbige\" = \"the same\".","- \"Glut\" = \"glow\"; \"Gewalt\" = \"force\" (Kraft is also \"force\"; one English word for two German words)."]],"naturgeschichte-0208":["Now that the question is resolved of why the central body of a great star-system is a flaming sphere, i.e. a sun; so it seems not to be superfluous to occupy oneself still some time with this topic, and to investigate the state of such a heavenly body with a careful examination; especially since the conjectures here allow themselves to be derived from sounder grounds than they customarily tend to be in the investigations of the constitution of distant heavenly bodies.",["- Vorwurf = \"topic\" (literally \"object of reproach/thrown before\"; not Gegenstand); Zustand = \"state\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\".","- Gründe = \"grounds\"; Prüfung = \"examination\"; Untersuchungen = \"investigations\"; Muthmassungen = \"conjectures\".","- The main clause \"so scheinet...\" after \"Nachdem...\" kept with semicolon as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0209":["First I establish that one cannot doubt that the sun is really a flaming body, and not a mass of molten and glowing matter heated to the highest degree, as some have wanted to conclude from certain difficulties which they believe they find in the former opinion. For if one considers that a flaming fire, over every other way of heat, has this essential preeminence, that it is, so to speak, active out of itself, and, instead of diminishing or exhausting itself through communication, rather obtains thereby more strength and violence, and thus requires only material and nourishment for its sustenance in order to endure continually; whereas the glow of a mass heated to the highest degree is a merely passive state, which unceasingly diminishes through the community of the touching matter, and has no powers of its own to spread from a small beginning, or to revive again upon the diminution; if, I say, one considers this, then one will be able, to say nothing of the other grounds, to see sufficiently from this alone that that property must probably be attributed to the sun, the source of the light and of the warmth in every world-structure.",["- Meinung = \"opinion\"; erweget = \"considers\"; Art = \"way\" (vor einer jeden andern Art der Hitze); Zustand = \"state\".","- Mittheilung = \"communication\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\" (glossary); wahrscheinlicher Weise = \"probably\" (cf. Wahrscheinlichkeit).","- \"Stoff und Nahrung\" = \"material and nourishment\"; Kräfte = \"powers\" (Kraft = force; power).","- \"jene Eigenschaft\" = \"that property\": referent left unresolved as in the German; \"Weltbau\" = \"world-structure\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0210":["If the sun now, or the suns in general, are flaming spheres; so the first constitution of their surface that can be inferred from this is that air must be found on them, because without air no fire burns. This circumstance gives occasion for remarkable consequences. For if one first sets the atmosphere of the sun and its weight in proportion to the lump of the sun; in what state of compression will this air not be, and how capable will it not become, precisely thereby, of sustaining the most violent degrees of the fire through its elastic force? In this atmosphere there rise, by all conjecture, also the smoke-clouds of the materials dissolved by the flame, which, as one need not doubt, have within them a mixture of coarse and lighter particles, which, after they have raised themselves to a height that harbors a cooler air for them, plunge down in heavy rains of pitch and sulphur and bring new nourishment to the flame. This same atmosphere is also, from the same causes as on our earth, not free from the motions of the winds, which, however, to all appearance, must far surpass in violence everything that the imagination can only represent to itself. If some region on the surface of the sun, whether through the suffocating force of the erupting vapors, or through the sparing inflow of combustible materials, slackens in the eruption of the flame; so the air found above it cools to some extent, and, in that it contracts, gives to the one found beside it room, with a force commensurate to the excess of its expansion, to press into its space, in order to fan the extinguished flame.",["- Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Folgerungen = \"consequences\"; Verhältniß = \"proportion\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; vorzustellen = \"represent\" (Vorstellung = representation).","- Federkraft = \"elastic force\" (literally \"spring-force\"); Gewalt = \"force\" (also Kraft = \"force\"); Raum = \"space\"; Platz = \"room\".","- \"allem Vermuthen nach\" = \"by all conjecture\"; \"dem Ansehen nach\" = \"to all appearance\".","- \"vor sie\" = \"for them\" (vor = für); \"der daneben befindlichen\" = \"the one found beside it\" (Luft understood, as grammatically feminine).","- Ambiguity kept: \"giebt sie der daneben befindlichen Platz ... zu dringen\": which air presses into whose space is as open as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0211":["Nonetheless every flame always swallows up much air, and there is no doubt that the elastic force of the fluid air-element that surrounds the sun must thereby suffer in some time no small detriment. If one applies here on the large scale what Mr. Hales has confirmed concerning this, regarding the effect of flame in our atmosphere, through careful experiments; then one can regard the perpetual endeavor of the smoke-particles proceeding from the flame to annihilate the elasticity of the sun's atmosphere as a principal knot, the resolution of which is connected with difficulties. For because the flame that burns over the entire surface of the sun deprives itself of the air that is indispensable to it for burning, the sun is in danger of going out altogether, once the greatest part of its atmosphere has been swallowed up. It is true, fire also generates air, through the dissolution of certain matters; but the experiments prove that more is always swallowed up than is generated. To be sure, if a part of the sun's fire, under suffocating vapors, is deprived of the air that serves for its preservation; then, as we have already remarked, violent storms will be at pains to disperse them and carry them away. But on the whole one will be able to make the replacement of this necessary element comprehensible to oneself in the following way, if one takes into consideration that since in a flaming fire the heat works almost only upward, and only little downward, when it has been suffocated by the cause cited, it turns its violence toward the interior of the sun's body, and compels its deep abysses to let the air shut up in their caverns break forth, and to fan the fire anew: if one posits in this its bowels, by a liberty that is not forbidden in so unknown an object, chiefly matters that, like saltpeter, are inexhaustibly yielding in elastic air; then the sun's fire will not easily be able to suffer want, through exceedingly long periods, of the inflow of ever renewed air.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0212":["Nonetheless one sees the distinct marks of perishability also in this inestimable fire, which nature has set up as the torch of the world. A time comes in which it will have been extinguished. The withdrawal of the most volatile and finest matters, which, dispersed by the violence of the heat, never return again, and increase the material of the zodiacal light, the accumulation of unburnable and burnt-out matters, e.g. of ash on the surface, finally also the want of air, will set a term to the sun, whereupon its flame will one day go out, and eternal darkness will take its place, which now is the center of light and of life for the whole world-edifice. The alternating endeavor of its fire to come to life again, through the opening of new vaults, whereby it perhaps restores itself several times before its demise, could furnish an explanation of the vanishing and reappearance of some fixed stars. They would be suns that are near to their extinction, and that still strive several times to come to life out of their rubble. Whether this explanation deserves approval or not, one will certainly let this consideration serve to see that, since an unavoidable decay threatens the perfection of all world-orders, in one way or another, one will find no difficulty in the law of their demise cited above, through the inclination of the mechanical arrangement, which, however, becomes worthy of acceptance chiefly because it carries with it the seed of renewal, even in the mingling with chaos.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0213":["Finally let us represent to the imagination an object as wondrously strange as a burning sun is, as it were from nearby. One sees in a single view wide lakes of fire that raise their flames toward heaven, raging storms, whose fury redoubles the violence of the former, which, in making them swell over their shores, now cover the elevated regions of this world-body, now let them sink back into their boundaries: burnt-out cliffs that stretch their dreadful peaks out of the flaming abysses, and whose flooding or laying bare, by the surging fire-element, causes the alternating appearing and vanishing of the sunspots: thick vapors that suffocate the fire, and which, raised by the force of the winds, make up dark clouds that plunge down again in fiery downpours, and as burning streams pour from the heights of the solid sun-land (*) into the flaming valleys, the crashing of the elements, the rubble of burnt-out matters, and nature wrestling with destruction, which, even with the most abominable state of its disorders, brings about the beauty of the world and the utility of creatures.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0214":["(*) I ascribe, not without cause, to the suns all the unevennesses of the solid land, of the mountains and the valleys, that we encounter on our earth and other world-bodies. The formation of a world-globe that changes from a fluid state into a solid one necessarily brings about such inequalities on the surface. When the surface hardens, while in the fluid inner part of such a mass the matters still sink toward the center in accordance with their gravity; then the particles of the elastic air or fire-element, which is found mingled in these matters, are driven out, and heap up under the crust that has meanwhile become solid, under which they produce great caverns, enormous in proportion to the lump of the sun, into which the said uppermost crust finally sinks in with manifold indentations, and thereby prepares both elevated regions and mountains, and also valleys and flood-beds of wide lakes of fire.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0215":["If then the centers of all great world-systems are flaming bodies; then this is most of all to be presumed of the central body of that immeasurable system which the fixed stars make up. Now, however, will this body, whose mass must have a proportion to the magnitude of its system, if it were a self-luminous body or a sun, not strike the eyes with preeminent brilliance and magnitude? Nonetheless we see no such fixed star distinguishing itself outstandingly gleam forth among the host of heaven. In fact, one need not let it be strange to oneself if this does not happen. Even if it surpassed our sun 10000 times in magnitude, it could nevertheless, if one assumes its distance to be 100 times greater than that of Sirius, appear no greater and brighter than this one.",["- 0211: \"Federkraft\" = elastic force (literally \"spring-force\"); \"Luftelement\" rendered \"air-element\"; \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"begreiflich\" = comprehensible.","- 0211: \"Hauptknoten\" rendered \"principal knot\"; \"Auflösung\" = resolution (also \"dissolution\" for the later \"Auflösung gewisser Materien\").","- 0211: The long final sentence keeps the German's two \"wenn\" clauses and colon; the construction \"da ... wenn sie ... ersticket worden\" is left as awkward as in German. \"sie\" in \"zerstreuen und wegzuführen\" (the vapors/Dämpfe, or the flame/fire) is left ambiguous.","- 0212: \"Weltgebäude\" = world-edifice; \"Weltordnungen\" = world-orders; \"Vollkommenheit\" = perfection. \"Ziel\" = term (also \"goal\").","- 0212: \"darinn sie wird erloschen seyn\" kept as future perfect. \"welche ... annehmungswürdig wird\" has an ambiguous antecedent (the law / the arrangement), preserved.","- 0213: \"Object\" = object (Latinate form; Gegenstand not present here). \"Grenzen\" = boundaries. \"Zustand\" = state. \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination. The sentence is syntactically loose in German (the \"welche\" referring to storms or lakes); preserved. \"Ufer\" = shores.","- 0214: \"Schweere\" = gravity (literally \"heaviness\"). The clause \"in die gedachte oberste Rinde, zuletzt ... hereinsinkt\" has an apparent missing relative (\"in welche\"); rendered as it stands, with \"into which,\" which supplies the relative; the original text may be corrupt.","- 0215: \"Verhältniß\" = proportion; \"Cörper\"/\"Körper\" = body; \"Centralkörper\" = central body; \"Weltsystemen\" = world-systems. Footnote marker (*) retained in 0213."]],"naturgeschichte-0216":["Perhaps, however, it is reserved for future times at least still one day to discover the region where the center (*) of the system of fixed stars, to which our sun belongs, is located, or perhaps even to determine where the central body of the universe, toward which all parts of it aim with unanimous descent, must be posited. Of what constitution this fundamental piece of the whole creation may be, and what is to be found on it, we want to leave to Mr. Wright of Durham to determine, who, with a fanatical rapture, exalted in this happy place, as it were onto a throne of all of nature, a powerful being of the divine kind with spiritual forces of attraction and repulsion, which, effective around itself in an infinite sphere, would draw all virtue to itself but drive vice back. We do not want to let the reins loose on the boldness of our conjectures, to which we have perhaps allowed only far too much, as far as arbitrary fictions. The deity is equally present everywhere in the infinity of the whole of cosmic space; everywhere where there are natures that are capable of swinging themselves up above the dependence of creatures, to the community of the highest being, it is found equally near. The whole creation is permeated by its forces, but only he who knows how to free himself from the creature, who is so noble as to see that in the enjoyment of this primal source of perfection the highest rung of happiness is to be sought solely and alone, he alone is capable of finding himself closer to this true point of reference of all excellence than anything else in the whole of nature. Meanwhile, if I, without taking part in the enthusiastic representation of the Englishman, am to conjecture about the different degrees of the spirit-world from the physical relation of their dwelling places to the center of creation, I would with more probability seek the most perfect classes of rational beings farther from this center than near to it. The perfection of creatures endowed with reason, insofar as it depends on the constitution of the matter in whose combination they are limited, depends very much on the fineness of the material, whose influence determines them to the representation of the world and to the counter-effect into it. The inertia and the resistance of matter restricts far too much the freedom of spiritual beings to act and the distinctness of their sensation of external things; it makes their capacities dull, in that it does not obey their motions with due ease. Hence if one assumes, as is probable, near to the center of nature the densest and heaviest sorts of matter, and in contrast, in the greater distance, the increasing degrees of fineness and lightness of it, in accordance with the analogy that prevails in our world-structure; then the sequel is comprehensible. The rational beings whose place of generation and abode is situated nearer to the center of creation are sunk into a stiff and immovable matter, which contains its forces locked up in an insuperable inertia, and is also just as incapable of transmitting and communicating the impressions of the universe with the necessary distinctness and ease. One will therefore have to count these thinking beings in the low class; on the other hand, with the distances from the general center, this perfection of the spirit-world, which rests on its alternating dependence on matter, will grow like a continuous ladder. In the deepest abasement toward this point of descent one has accordingly to posit the poorest and most imperfect genera of thinking natures, and it is hither that this excellence of beings, with all shadings of diminution, finally loses itself in the complete lack of reflection and of thinking. In fact, if one considers that the center of nature at the same time constitutes the beginning of its formation out of the raw stuff, and its boundary with chaos: if one adds to this that the perfection of spiritual beings, which indeed has an outermost boundary of its beginning, where its capacities collide with unreason, but no boundaries of continuation, beyond which it could not be raised, but rather finds toward that side a complete infinity before it; then, if a law is to take place at all according to which the dwelling places of rational creatures are distributed in the order of their relation to the common center, one will have to posit the lowest and most imperfect genus, which as it were constitutes the beginning of the lineage of the spirit-world, at that place which is to be called the beginning of the entire universe, in order, together with it, in equal progression, to fill all infinity of time and of spaces with degrees of perfection of the faculty of thinking growing into the infinite, and, as it were gradually, to approach the goal of the highest excellence, namely the deity, without ever being able to reach it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0217":["(*) I have a conjecture, according to which it seems to me very probable that Sirius or the Dog Star is, in the system of the stars that constitute the Milky Way, the central body, and occupies the center to which they all relate. If one regards this system, according to the sketch of the first part of this treatise, as a swarm of suns that are heaped onto a common surface, which is scattered on all sides from the center of it, and yet constitutes a certain, so to speak, circular-shaped space, which through their slight deviations from the plane of reference also extends somewhat in breadth to both sides: then the sun, which likewise is located near this plane, will see the appearance of this circular-shaped, whitish shimmering zone broadest toward that side toward which it is nearest to the outermost boundary of the system; for it is easy to presume that it will not be staying precisely in the center. Now the strip of the Milky Way is broadest in the part between the sign of the Swan and of the Archer, consequently this will be the side where the place of our sun is nearest to the outermost periphery of the circular-shaped system: and in this part we will hold the place where the constellations of the Eagle and of the Fox with the Goose stand especially to be the very nearest, because there, from the interspace where the Milky Way divides, the greatest apparent dispersion of the stars is evident. If one therefore draws, approximately from the place beside the tail of the Eagle, a line through the middle of the surface of the Milky Way to the point standing opposite; then this must meet the center of the system, and it does in fact meet very precisely upon Sirius, the brightest star in the whole heaven, which, on account of this fortunate coincidence, harmonizing so well with its superior figure, seems to deserve that one hold it to be the central body itself. According to this concept it would also be seen precisely in the strip of the Milky Way, if the position of our sun, which at the tail of the Eagle deviates somewhat from the plane of it, did not cause the optical distance of the center toward the other side of such a zone.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0218":["Eighth Chapter,\nGeneral Proof of the Correctness\nof a mechanical doctrinal constitution of the arrangement\nof the structure of the world in general, in particular of\nthe certainty of the present one.",["- 0216: \"Beschaffenheit\" rendered \"constitution\" (glossary allows \"constitution; property\"); \"Vollkommenheit\" = \"perfection\"; \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\"; \"Empfindung\" = \"sensation\"; \"Deutlichkeit\" = \"distinctness\"; \"Fähigkeit\" = \"capacity\" (*capacitas* not in text).","- 0216: \"Fähigkeiten\" and \"fähig\" both rendered \"capacity\"/\"capable\"; \"Vermögen\" in \"Denkungsvermögen\" = \"faculty\".","- 0216: \"Grenze\" = \"boundary\" throughout; \"Gattung\" = \"genus\"; \"Ueberlegung\" = \"reflection\"; \"Folge\" = \"sequel\"; \"Gemeinschaft\" = \"community\"; \"Wesen\" = \"being\".","- 0216: \"Weltraum\" = \"cosmic space\" and \"Weltbau\" = \"world-structure\"; \"Geist/geistig\" rendered \"spirit/spiritual\" (\"Geisterwelt\" = \"spirit-world\"); \"Wiederstand\" = \"resistance\"; \"Gegenwirkung\" = \"counter-effect\".","- 0216: Syntax of \"welcher so edel ist, einzusehen, daß … zu suchen, der allein ist fähig\" is kept as in German; \"diesem wahren Beziehungspunkte … sich näher … zu befinden\" is awkward and the comparison \"als irgend etwas anders\" is kept literal. \"hiewärtshin\" rendered \"hither\"; \"mit Vernunft begabter Geschöpfe\" = \"endowed with reason.\"","- 0216: \"wollte\" in \"so wollte … suchen\" is first person subjunctive (\"I would seek\"); \"Wright\" is spelled as in the original. \"Universi\" is Latin genitive, left as \"universe.\"","- 0217: \"Beziehungsplane\" = \"plane of reference\"; \"Streif\" = \"strip\"; \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\"; \"Begriff\" = \"concept\"; \"Fuchs/Gans\" = Fox/Goose (constellations as in the German). Doubtful syntax at the end (\"der Stand … der … abweichet\") is kept literal; \"(*)\" footnote marker left in 0216 and 0217 as in the source.","- 0218: \"Lehrverfassung\" = \"doctrinal constitution\" (literal; compound not in glossary); \"Einrichtung\" = \"arrangement\"; \"Gewißheit\" = \"certainty\"; \"Beweis\" = \"proof\"; \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the world.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0219":["One cannot look at the structure of the world without recognizing the most excellent arrangement in its organization, and the secure marks of the hand of GOD, in the perfection of its relations. Reason, after it has weighed and admired so much beauty, so much excellence, is rightly indignant at the bold folly that dares to ascribe all this to chance, and to a lucky happenstance. The highest wisdom must have made the design, and an infinite power have executed it, otherwise it would be impossible to encounter so many intentions coming together in one end, in the constitution of the structure of the world. It remains only to decide whether the design of the organization of the universe was already laid by the highest understanding into the essential determinations of the eternal natures, and planted in the universal laws of motion, in order to develop itself out of them, without constraint, in a way befitting the most perfect order; or whether the universal properties of the constituents of the world have the complete incapacity for agreement, and not the slightest relation to combination, and have throughout needed a foreign hand in order to acquire that limitation and joining-together which lets perfection and beauty shine forth. An almost universal prejudice has predisposed most philosophers against the capacity of nature to bring forth anything orderly through its universal laws, just as if it would amount to disputing the government of the world with GOD, if one seeks the original formations in the forces of nature, and just as if these were a principle independent of the Godhead, and an eternal blind fate.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0220":["But if one considers that nature and the eternal laws that are prescribed to substances for their interaction are no self-subsisting principle, necessary without GOD, that precisely by the fact that it shows so much agreement and order in what it brings forth through universal laws, it is to be seen that the essences of all things must have their common origin in a certain fundamental being, and that they show nothing but reciprocal relations and nothing but harmony for the reason that their properties have their source in a single highest understanding, whose wise idea has designed them in thoroughgoing relations, and has planted in them that capacity through which they bring forth nothing but beauty, nothing but order, in the state of their efficacy left to themselves: if one, I say, considers this, nature will appear to us more worthy than it is commonly regarded, and one will expect from its unfoldings nothing but agreement, nothing but order. If, on the other hand, one gives room to an unfounded prejudice, that the universal laws of nature, in and for themselves, bring about nothing but disorder, and that all agreement toward utility, which shines forth in the constitution of nature, indicates the immediate hand of GOD; then one is compelled to convert the whole of nature into miracles. One will be obliged not to derive from the implanted forces of matter the beautiful colored arc that appears in the raindrops when they separate the colors of the sunlight, on account of its beauty, the rain, on account of its utility, the winds, on account of the indispensable advantages they afford in infinite kinds of human needs; in short, all the alterations of the world that bring propriety and order with them. The undertaking of the investigators of nature who have occupied themselves with such a philosophy will have to make a solemn apology before the judgment seat of religion. In fact there will then no longer be any nature; only a God in the machine will bring forth the alterations of the world. But what effect will this strange means, of proving the certainty of the highest being from the essential incapacity of nature, have toward the conviction of the Epicurean? If the natures of things, through the eternal laws of their essences, bring about nothing but disorder and absurdity, then they will precisely thereby prove the character of their independence from GOD: and what concept will one be able to make of a Godhead which the universal laws of nature obey only through a kind of compulsion, and in and for themselves conflict with its wisest designs? Will the enemy of providence not carry off as many victories over these false principles as he can point to agreements that the universal laws of the effecting of nature bring forth, without any particular limitations? and will it be able to lack such examples? Against this, let us infer thus, with greater propriety and correctness: Nature, left to its universal properties, is fruitful in nothing but beautiful and perfect fruits, which not only show agreement and excellence in themselves, but also harmonize well with the whole domain of their essences, with the utility of human beings, and the glorification of the divine properties. From this it follows that their essential properties can have no independent necessity; but that they must have their origin in a single understanding, as the ground and the source of all essences, in which they are designed under common relations. Everything that relates to one another, to a reciprocal harmony, must be combined with one another in a single being on which it all depends. Thus there is present a being of all beings, an infinite understanding and self-subsisting wisdom, from which nature, even in respect of its possibility, in the whole sum total of determinations, draws its origin. Henceforth one need not contest the capacity of nature as prejudicial to the existence of a highest being; the more perfect it is in its developments, the better its universal laws lead to order and agreement, the more certain a proof of the Godhead it is, from which it borrows these relations. Its productions are no longer effects of happenstance, and sequels of chance; everything flows from it according to unalterable laws, which must present nothing but what is skillful because they are nothing but traits from the all-wisest design, from which disorder is banished. It was not the happenstance concurrence of the atoms of Lucretius that formed the world; implanted forces and laws, which have the wisest understanding as their source, have been an unalterable origin of that order which had to flow from them not by happenstance but necessarily.",["- Wechselwirkung = interaction; Wesen = being/essence (both \"Wesen\"; rendered \"being\" in the singular as divine/highest, \"essence(s)\" for things' natures; Grundwesen = \"fundamental being\").","- Verstand = understanding; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Bestimmung = determination; Fähigkeit = capacity; Unfähigkeit = incapacity; Bewegungsgesetze = laws of motion.","- Zufall = chance; Ohngefehr = happenstance; Folge = sequel; Wirkung = effect (Wirkungsgesetze = \"laws of the effecting\").","- Absicht = intention; Zweck = end; Beziehung = relation; Verbindung = combination; verbunden werden = \"be combined.\"","- Principium is Latin in the German text, rendered \"principle\"; Grundsätze (0220, \"false principles\") is also \"principle.\" Beweisthum = \"proof\" (Beweis).","- Weltweisen/Weltweisheit = philosopher(s)/philosophy; Naturforscher = \"investigators of nature.\"","- Sentence in 0220 beginning \"Wenn man aber erweget…\" is kept as one; its syntax (daß-clauses with indicative/subjunctive \"sey\") is left as loose as the German. \"Ungezwungen\" = \"without constraint.\"","- \"Ihre … Eigenschaften\" in the final lines: the antecedent of \"ihre\" (Natur or the natures/Wesen) is left ambiguous as in German; \"sie\" in \"welchen sie…\" likewise.","- Einrichtung = organization; Verfassung = constitution; Weltgebäude = structure of the world; Anständigkeit/anständig = propriety/befitting; Umfang = domain."]],"naturgeschichte-0221":["If, then, one can rid oneself of an old and groundless prejudice, and of the lazy philosophy, which, under a devout mien, strives to conceal a sluggish ignorance; so I hope, on irrefutable grounds, to found a secure conviction: that the world acknowledges a mechanical development, from the universal laws of nature, as the origin of its constitution; and that secondly the way of mechanical generation that we have presented is the true one. If one wants to judge whether nature has sufficient capacities to bring about, through a mechanical sequence of its laws of motion, the arrangement of the structure of the world; so one must first consider how simple the motions are that the bodies of the world observe, and that they have nothing in themselves that would require a more exact determination than the universal rules of the forces of nature bring with them. The revolving motions consist in the combination of the sinking force, which is a certain sequel from the properties of matter, and of the shooting motion, which, as the effect of the former, can be regarded as a velocity attained through the sinking down, in which only a certain cause was necessary to bend the perpendicular fall sideways. Once the determination of these motions has been attained, nothing further is necessary to maintain them forever. They subsist in empty space, through the combination of the once impressed shooting force with the attraction flowing from the essential forces of nature, and thereafter suffer no alteration. However, the analogies in the agreement of these motions demonstrate the actuality of a mechanical origin so distinctly that one can have no doubt about it. For",[]],"naturgeschichte-0222":["1. These motions have a throughout agreeing direction, so that of six principal planets, of 10 satellites, both in their advancing motion and in their revolutions about the axis, there is not a single one that would move toward any other side than from west toward east. These directions, moreover, coincide so exactly that they deviate only little from a common plane, and this plane, to which everything is related, is the equatorial plane of the body that, in the midpoint of the whole system, turns about the axis toward the very same region, and that, through its preeminent attraction, has become the point of reference of all motions, and consequently has had to take part in them as exactly as possible: a proof that the collective motions arose and were determined in a mechanical way, conformable to the universal laws of nature, and that the cause which either impressed or directed the lateral motions has dominated the whole space of the planetary structure, and therein obeys the laws that matter, located in a commonly moved space, observes, that all different motions finally assume a single direction, and make themselves, all together, relate as exactly as possible to a single plane.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0223":["2. The velocities are constituted as they must be in a space in which the moving force is in the midpoint, namely, they decrease in constant degrees with the distances from it, and lose themselves, at the greatest remove, in a complete feebleness of motion, which bends the perpendicular fall only very little sideways. From Mercury on, which has the greatest force of revolution, one sees these diminish by steps, and in the outermost comet be as small as it can be, in order not to fall straight into the sun. One cannot object that the rules of central motions, in circular orbits, require it so, that the nearer to the midpoint of the universal sinking, the greater the velocity of revolution must be; for why must precisely the heavenly bodies near this center have circular-shaped orbits? why are the nearest not very eccentric, and the more distant ones revolving in circles? or rather, since they all deviate from this measured geometrical exactness; why does this deviation increase with the distances? Do these relations not designate the point to which all motion originally pressed itself, and, according to the measure of nearness, also attained greater degrees, before other determinations changed their directions into the present one?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0224":["If one now wants, however, to except the constitution of the structure of the world, and the origin of the motions, from the universal laws of nature, in order to ascribe them to the immediate hand of GOD; so one soon becomes aware that the adduced analogies manifestly refute such a concept. For as regards, first, the throughgoing agreement in direction, it is manifest that here there is no ground why the bodies of the world should have to carry out their revolutions precisely toward a single region, if the mechanism of their generation had not determined them thereto. For the space in which they run is infinitely little resisting, and restricts their motions as little toward the one side as toward the other; thus the choice of GOD, without the slightest motive, would not bind itself to a single determination, but would show itself with greater freedom in all sorts of alternations and diversity. Still more: why are the orbits of the planets so exactly relating to a common plane, namely to the equatorial plane of that great body which, in the midpoint of all motion, governs their revolutions? This analogy, instead of showing a motive of propriety in itself, is rather the cause of a certain confusion, which would be removed by a free deviation of the planetary orbits: for the attractions of the planets at present disturb to a certain extent the uniformity of their motions, and would not at all be obstructive to one another, if they did not relate so exactly to a common plane.",["- Weltweisheit = \"philosophy\" per glossary, so \"faule Weltweisheit\" = \"lazy philosophy\".","- Fähigkeiten = \"capacities\" (glossary: Fähigkeit = capacity); Bewegungsgrund = \"motive\"; Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\".","- \"Gegenstand/Object\" not at issue. Naturgesetze = \"laws of nature\"; Naturkräfte = \"forces of nature\".","- 0221: \"Wenn man ... entschlagen kan ... so hoffe ich\" kept with the German \"so\" as \"so\"; the odd conditional with \"so\" is preserved. \"erkenne\" (subjunctive, indirect speech) rendered \"acknowledges\"; the sense \"cognizes\" (glossary erkennen) is not chosen here, a departure from strict consistency.","- 0221: \"sinkende Kraft\", \"schiessende Bewegung/Kraft\" rendered literally as \"sinking force\", \"shooting motion/force\"; \"Folge\" = \"sequel\" in \"gewisse Folge\" but \"sequence\" in \"mechanische Folge\".","- 0222: \"Aeqvatorsfläche\" = \"equatorial plane\"; Fläche = \"plane\" (also \"surface\"); \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" = \"from west toward east\" (literally evening to morning). \"Theil nehmen\" = \"take part\".","- 0222: the final clause \"daß alle verschiedene Bewegungen ...\" is syntactically loose in German (the \"daß\" clause may attach to \"Gesetzen\" or \"obeys\"); the looseness is preserved. \"bezogen/beziehend\" = \"relate(d)\".","- 0223: \"Mattigkeit\" = \"feebleness\"; \"Schwungskraft\" = \"force of revolution\"; \"Umschwungsgeschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity of revolution\"; \"Naheit\" = \"nearness\".","- 0224: \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\" (Beschaffenheit is \"constitution; property\" in glossary, so one English word for two German words); \"Begriff\" = \"concept\"; \"Wohlanständigkeit\" = \"propriety\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0225":["More than all these analogies, the clearest mark of the hand of nature shows itself in the lack of the most exact determination, in those relations that it has striven to attain. If it were best that the planetary orbits be placed nearly on a common plane, why are they not so quite exactly? and why has a part of that deviation remained which was supposed to be avoided? If, for that reason, the planets near the path of the sun have received the magnitude of the force of revolution that holds the equilibrium with attraction, why is something still lacking in this complete equality? and why are their revolutions not perfectly circular, if merely the wisest intention, supported by the greatest capacity, has endeavored to bring forth this determination? Is it not clear to see that that cause which placed the paths of the heavenly bodies, in that it strove to bring them onto a common plane, was not able to accomplish it completely; likewise, that the force which ruled the heavenly space, when all matter that is now formed into spheres received its velocities of revolution, did indeed strive to bring it near the center into an equilibrium with the sinking power; but was not able to attain complete exactness. Is the usual procedure of nature not to be recognized in this, which, through the intervention of the various co-operations, is always made to deviate from the wholly measured determination? and will one really find the grounds of this constitution merely in the final ends of the supreme will commanding so immediately? One cannot, without displaying obstinacy, deny that the lauded manner of explanation of the natural properties, by citing their utility to give a ground, does not here hold the hoped-for test. It was certainly, with regard to utility, quite indifferent to the world whether the planetary orbits were completely circular or whether they were a little eccentric; whether they should coincide completely with the plane of their general relation, or still deviate somewhat from it; rather, if it was indeed necessary to be limited in this kind of agreements, it was best to have them completely in themselves. If it is true what the philosopher said: that GOD constantly practices geometry: if this also shines forth in the ways of the general laws of nature; then this rule would certainly be perfectly noticeable in the immediate works of the almighty word, and these would show all perfection of geometric exactness in themselves. The comets belong among these deficiencies of nature. One cannot deny that, with regard to their course and the alterations that they thereby undergo, they are to be regarded as imperfect members of creation, which can neither serve to provide convenient dwelling places for rational beings, nor become useful to the best of the whole system by serving, as has been supposed, as nourishment for the sun some day; for it is certain that most of them would not attain this purpose until the overthrow of the whole planetary edifice. In the doctrinal concept of the immediate supreme ordering of the world, without a natural development from general laws of nature, such a remark would be offensive, although it is certain. But in a mechanical manner of explanation the beauty of the world, and the revelation of omnipotence, are thereby glorified not a little. Nature, in that it comprehends in itself all possible degrees of manifoldness, extends its domain over all genera from perfection to nothing, and the deficiencies themselves are a sign of the abundance, in which its sum total is inexhaustible.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0226":["It is to be believed that the adduced analogies would be able to do so much against prejudice as to make the mechanical origin of the world-edifice worthy of acceptance, if certain grounds, taken from the nature of the matter itself, did not still seem to contradict this doctrinal constitution entirely. The heavenly space is, as already thought of several times, empty, or at least filled with infinitely thin matter, which consequently can have furnished no means for impressing common motions on the heavenly bodies. This difficulty is so significant and valid that Newton, who had cause to trust the insights of his philosophy as much as any mortal, saw himself compelled here to give up the hope of resolving, through the laws of nature and the forces of matter, the impression of the forces of revolution inhering in the planets, notwithstanding all the agreement that pointed to a mechanical origin. Although it is a sad resolution for a philosopher, in the case of a composite constitution still far removed from the simple fundamental laws, to give up the effort of investigation and to content himself with citing the immediate will of GOD; nevertheless Newton here recognized the boundary-division which separates from one another nature and the finger of GOD, the course of the established laws of the former and the beckoning of the latter. After the despair of so great a philosopher it seems to be a presumption still to hope for a happy progress in a matter of such difficulty.",["Both passages are one-to-one with AA 1:337–339; no apparatus signs occur.","- 0225: *Merkmaal* = \"mark\" (*Merkmal*); *Vermögen* rendered \"capacity\" (glossary also allows \"faculty\"); *Gewalt* = \"power\" (*senkende Gewalt*, \"sinking power\"); *kraft/Schwungskraft* = \"force of revolution\" (*Kraft* = \"force\").","- 0225: *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Beschaffenheit* = \"constitution\"; *Vollkommenheit* = \"perfection\"; *Umfang* = \"domain\"; *Innbegriff* = \"sum total\" (*Inbegriff*); *Gattung* = \"genus\" (pl. \"genera\").","- 0225: *Zweck* = \"purpose\" and *Endzwecke* = \"final ends\"; *Erklärungsart* = \"manner of explanation\"; *Lehrbegriff* = \"doctrinal concept\"; *Mannigfaltigkeit* = \"manifoldness.\"","- 0225: \"Die Cometen gehören…\" *Mängel* = \"deficiencies\"; *Mitwürkungen* = \"co-operations\" (literal compound).","- 0225: \"ihre Umläufe\" \"revolutions\"; \"Umschwungsgeschwindigkeiten\" \"velocities of revolution\"; \"es\" in \"es nicht völlig hat ausrichten können\" left unresolved as in the German. The sentence \"Ist es nicht klar einzusehen…\" ends with a period, as in the original, though it is a question; the semicolon-joined second clause (\"ingleichen, daß…\") has no main verb of its own beyond \"einzusehen,\" which I preserved.","- 0225: *an sich* = \"in itself\" (\"sie völlig an sich zu haben\", \"to have them completely in themselves\"); the reflexive plural is as in English-German syntax; reading is literal.","- 0226: *Weltgebäude* = \"world-edifice\"; *Lehrverfassung* = \"doctrinal constitution\"; *Weltweisheit* = \"philosophy\"; *Weltweisen* = \"philosopher\" (both *Philosoph* and *Weltweiser* are rendered \"philosopher\"); *Grenzscheidung* = \"boundary-division.\"","- 0226: *Eindrückung* = \"impression\"; *Fortgang* = \"progress\"; *Kräfte der Materie* = \"forces of matter\"; *Schwungskräfte* = \"forces of revolution\"; *Mittel* = \"means\"; *gültig* = \"valid\"; *erkannte* = \"recognized.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0227":["But just the same difficulty that deprived Newton of the hope of comprehending, from the forces of nature, the impulsive forces imparted to the heavenly bodies, whose direction and determinations make up the systematic character of the structure of the world, has been the source of the doctrinal system that we have presented in the preceding chapters. It grounds a mechanical doctrinal concept; but one that is far removed from the one that Newton found inadequate, and on account of which he rejected all subordinate causes, because he (if I may venture to say so) erred in this, that he held it to be the only one among all possible ones of its kind. It is quite easy and natural, even by means of Newton's difficulty, to arrive, through a short and thorough inference, at the certainty of that mechanical way of explanation which we have sketched in this treatise. If one presupposes (as one indeed cannot avoid admitting) that the above analogies establish with the greatest certainty that the harmonizing movements and circles of the heavenly bodies, which relate to one another in an orderly way, indicate a natural cause as their origin; then this cannot be the same matter that now fills the heavenly space. Therefore that which formerly filled these spaces, and whose movement has been the ground of the present revolutions of the heavenly bodies, after it has gathered itself on these spheres and has thereby cleared the spaces that one now sees empty, or, which flows immediately from this, the matter itself of which the planets, the comets, indeed the sun, consist, must originally have been spread out in the space of the planetary system, and in this state have set themselves into movements which they retained when they united into particular clumps and formed the heavenly bodies, all of which take up into themselves the formerly scattered material of the world-matter. One is not long in embarrassment here in discovering the driving-mechanism that may have set this material of nature in the course of forming into movement. The impulse itself that brought about the union of the masses, the force of attraction, which dwells essentially in matter and therefore suits itself so well, at the first stirring of nature, as the first cause of movement, was the source of it. The direction, which in this force always aims straight toward the center, causes no scruple here; for it is certain that the fine material of scattered elements in the perpendicular movement, as much through the manifold of points of attraction as through the hindrance that their crossing lines of direction offer one another, must have turned out into various lateral movements, in which the certain law of nature, which makes all matter that restricts one another through alternating effect finally bring itself into such a state that one draws upon the other as little change as possible, has produced both the uniformity of direction and also the proper degrees of the velocities, which are balanced in every distance according to the central force, and through whose combination they strive to stray neither above nor below themselves: since all elements were thus made to revolve not only toward one side, but also nearly in parallel and free circles, around the common point of descent, in the thin heavenly space. These movements of the parts had to continue afterward, when planetary spheres had formed out of them, and now subsist, through the combination of the once implanted impulsion with the central force, into unlimited future times. On this so incomprehensible ground rest the uniformity of directions in the planetary circles, the exact relation to a common plane, the moderation of the impulsive forces according to the attraction of the place, the accuracy of these analogies decreasing with the distances, and the free deviation of the outermost heavenly bodies to both sides as well as in the opposite direction. If these signs of alternating dependence in the determinations of generation point with manifest certainty to a matter originally in movement spread through the whole space; then the complete lack of all matters in this now empty heavenly space, except for that of which the bodies of the planets, of the sun and of the comets are composed, proves that these themselves must have been in this state of spreading-out in the beginning. The ease and correctness with which, from this assumed principle, all the phenomena of the structure of the world have been derived in the preceding chapters, is a completion of such a conjecture, and gives it a worth that is no longer arbitrary.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0228":["The certainty of a mechanical doctrinal system of the origin of the structure of the world, especially of our own, is raised to the highest summit of conviction when one weighs the formation of the heavenly bodies themselves, the weight and magnitude of their masses, according to the relations that they have with regard to their distance from the center of gravitation. For first, the density of their material, when one weighs it in the whole of their clump, decreases in constant degrees with the distances from the sun: a determination that aims so distinctly at the mechanical determinations of the first formation that one can demand nothing more. They are composed of such matters, of which those of heavier kind have received a deeper place toward the common point of descent, but those of lighter kind a more distant removal: which condition is necessary in every kind of natural generation. But with an establishment flowing immediately from the divine will, not the least ground is to be found for the relation mentioned. For although it might seem that the more distant spheres must consist of lighter material, so that they could sense the necessary effect from the lesser force of the sun's rays; yet this is only an end that aims at the constitution of the matters found on the surface, and not at the deeper sorts of its interior clump, into which the sun's warmth never has any effect, which also serve only to bring about the attraction of the planet, which is to make the bodies surrounding it sink toward it, and therefore may not have the least relation to the strength or weakness of the sun's rays. If one therefore asks why the densities of the earth, of Jupiter, of Saturn, drawn from Newton's correct calculations, are related to one another as 400, 94½ and 64; it would be absurd to ascribe the cause to the aim of GOD, who moderated them according to the degrees of the sun's warmth; for here our earth can serve us as a counterproof, in which the sun by its rays works only to so slight a depth beneath the surface that that part of its clump which must have some relation to it amounts by far not to the millionth part of the whole, of which the remainder is completely indifferent with regard to this aim. If therefore the material of which the heavenly bodies consist has an orderly relation to one another harmonizing with the distances, and the planets now cannot restrict one another, since they stand apart from one another in empty space; then their matter must previously have been in a state in which they could exert common effect on one another, in order to restrict themselves into the places proportioned to their specific weight, which could not have happened otherwise than that their parts before the formation were spread out in the whole space of the system, and, in accordance with the general law of movement, gained places which are due to their density.",["- Lehrverfassung = \"doctrinal system\"; Lehrbegriff = \"doctrinal concept\" (Lehre = doctrine in glossary). Both are literal compounds, not glossary items.","- Schwungskräfte = \"impulsive forces\"; Schwung = \"impulsion\" (not in glossary). Kräfte = forces (Kraft); Centralkraft = \"central force\".","- Bestimmung(en) = \"determination(s)\" throughout; Erklärungsart = \"way of explanation\" (Art = way).","- Welt-: Weltbau / Weltgebäude = \"structure of the world\" / \"structure of the world\" (both rendered alike); Weltmaterie = \"world-matter\".","- Gegenstand not present; Stoff = \"material\" (glossary); Materie = \"matter\"; Körper = \"body\".","- Zustand = \"state\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Absicht = \"aim\" (glossary); Grund = \"ground\"; Ursache = \"cause\".","- \"die ... einander ... einschränkende Materie ... sich ... bringet\" and \"durch deren Verbindung sie ... trachten\": antecedents of \"sie\" and \"deren\" left as ambiguous as in the German; \"sie\" in \"nachdem sie sich ... versammlet\" is likewise unresolved (Materie / Räume).","- Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Gewißheit = \"certainty\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; gewechselte Wirkung = \"alternating effect\"; Verbindung = \"combination\"; \"Vorstellung\" not present. Oerter/Ort = \"place(s)\". Spelling \"Qvelle\"/\"Quelle\" normalized silently."]],"naturgeschichte-0229":["The relation among the magnitude of the planetary masses, which increases with the distances, is the second ground that clearly proves the mechanical formation of the heavenly bodies, and principally our theory of the same. Why do the masses of the heavenly bodies increase approximately with the distances? If one follows a doctrine that ascribes everything to the choice of GOD; then no other intention could be thought, why the more distant planets must have greater masses, than that they might, through the superior strength of their attraction, comprehend in their sphere one or several moons, which are to serve to make the sojourn comfortable for the inhabitants who are destined for them. But this purpose could just as well have been obtained through a superior density in the interior of their lump, and why then had the lightness of the material, flowing from particular grounds, which is contrary to this relation, to remain, and to be surpassed by the advantage of the volume so far that the mass of the upper ones would nevertheless be more important than that of the lower ones? If one does not attend to the way of the natural generation of these bodies; then one will hardly be able to give a ground of this relation: but in consideration of it nothing is easier than to comprehend this determination. When the material of all world-bodies was still spread out in the space of the planetary system; then attraction formed out of these particles spheres, which without doubt had to become the greater, the farther the place of their sphere of formation was removed from that universal central body which, from the midpoint of the whole space, through a superior powerful attraction, restricted and hindered this union, as much as lay in it.\n\n===naturgeschichte-0230\nOne becomes aware with pleasure of the marks of this formation of the heavenly bodies out of the ground-material that was spread out in the beginning, in the width of the intervals that separate their circles from one another, and which according to this concept must be regarded as the empty compartments from which the planets have taken the matter for their formation. One sees how these intervals between the circles have a relation to the magnitude of the masses that have been formed out of them. The width between the circle of Jupiter and that of Mars is so great that the space enclosed therein exceeds the surface of all the lower planetary circles taken together: but it is worthy of the greatest among all the planets, of that which has more mass than all the rest together. One cannot ascribe this distance of Jupiter from Mars to the intention that their attractions should hinder one another as little as possible. For according to such a ground the planet between two circles would always find itself nearest to that one whose attraction, united with its own, can disturb least the revolutions of both around the sun: consequently to that one that has the smallest mass. Since now, according to the correct calculations of Newton, the force with which Jupiter can act on the course of Mars stands to that which it exerts on Saturn through the united attraction as 1/12512 to 1/200; one can easily make the calculation by how much Jupiter would have to find itself nearer to the circle of Mars than to that of Saturn, if their distance had been determined through the intention of their external relation, and not through the mechanism of their generation. Since now, however, this is found quite otherwise: since a planetary circle, with regard to the two circles that are above and below it, often finds itself more distant from that one in which a smaller planet runs, than from the orbit of that of greater mass; but the width of the space around the circle of each planet always has a correct relation to its mass; so it is clear that the way of generation must have determined these relations, and that, because these determinations, like the cause and the consequences of them, seem to be combined, one will hit it most correctly if one regards the spaces comprehended between the circles as the receptacles of that material out of which the planets have formed themselves: from which it follows immediately that the magnitude of the latter must be proportioned to the masses of the former, which relation, however, is increased in the case of the more distant planets through the greater scattering of the elementary matter in these regions in the first state. Hence of two planets that come fairly equal to one another in mass, the more distant must have a greater space of formation, i.e. a greater distance from the two nearest circles, both because the material there was in itself of a specifically lighter kind, and because it was more scattered, than in that which formed itself nearer to the sun. Hence although the earth together with the moon does not yet seem equal to Venus in bodily content, it has nevertheless required a greater space of formation around itself: because it had to form itself out of a more scattered material than this lower planet. Of Saturn it is to be conjectured on these grounds that its sphere of formation will have spread much farther on the remote side than on the side toward the midpoint (as this indeed holds of almost all the planets;) and hence the interval between the circle of Saturn and the orbit of the heavenly body nearest above this planet, which one can conjecture above it, will be much wider than between the same and Jupiter.\n\n===naturgeschichte-0231\nThus everything in the planetary world-structure proceeds by steps, with correct relations to the first generating force, which was more effective next to the midpoint than in the distance, on into all unbounded widths. The diminution of the impressed shooting force, the deviation from the most exact agreement in the direction and the position of the circles, the densities of the heavenly bodies, the thriftiness of nature with regard to the space of its formation: everything diminishes step-wise from the center into the wide distances: everything shows that the first cause was bound to the mechanical rules of motion, and did not act through a free choice.",["- Passage 0229: \"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\" throughout; \"Grund\" = \"ground\"; \"begreifen\" = \"comprehend\" (both \"einen Mond begreifen\" and \"diese Bestimmung zu begreifen\"); \"Absicht\" = \"intention\"; \"Zweck\" = \"purpose\"; \"Stoff\" = \"material\"; \"Materie\" = \"matter\"; \"Betrachtung\" = \"consideration\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\".","- 0229: \"Grösse der planetischen Massen\" — \"Masse\" rendered \"mass\"; \"Klumpen\" = \"lump\"; \"Wahl GOttes\" kept as \"choice of GOD\" (capitalization as printed).","- 0229: The clause \"so viel an ihm ist\" is rendered literally \"as much as lay in it\"; \"ihm\" could refer to the central body or to the attraction.","- 0229: \"Dichtigkeit\" = \"density\"; \"Leichtigkeit\" = \"lightness\"; \"Vorzug\" = \"advantage\" in \"Vorzug des Volumens\" but \"vorzüglich\" = \"superior\".","- 0230: \"Bildungssphäre/Bildungsraum\" = \"sphere/space of formation\"; \"Kreis\" = \"circle\" (not \"orbit\"); \"Bahn\" = \"orbit\"; \"Weltkörper\" = \"world-bodies\"; \"Himmelskörper\" = \"heavenly bodies\".","- 0230: The pronouns in \"deren Grösse dieser ihren Massen proportionirt\" are left as ambiguous as the German (\"the latter … the former\" is a literal guide to \"deren … dieser\"); the sentence \"Da dieses ... bestimmt haben\" is kept as one sentence with its semicolons. The fraction \"⅟12512 zu ⅟200\" is as printed in the OCR.","- 0230: \"Zustand\" = \"state\"; \"Folgen\" = \"consequences\" (here \"Folge\" is plural, not \"Folgerung\"); \"Erzeugung\" = \"generation\"; \"Mechanismus\" = \"mechanism\"; \"Behältnisse\" = \"receptacles\".","- 0231: \"schiessende Kraft\" = \"shooting force\"; \"Kraft\" = \"force\"; \"Centro\" kept as \"center\"; \"Sparsamkeit\" = \"thriftiness\"; \"Wahl\" = \"choice\"; \"Ursache\" = \"cause.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0230":["One becomes aware of the marks of this formation of the heavenly bodies out of the basic material that was spread out in the beginning, with pleasure, in the width of the intervening spaces that separate their circles from one another, and which, according to this concept, must be regarded as the empty compartments from which the planets have taken the matter for their formation. One sees how these intervening spaces between the circles have a relation to the magnitude of the masses that have been formed from them. The width between the circle of Jupiter and that of Mars is so great that the space enclosed therein exceeds the surface of all the lower planetary circles taken together: but it is worthy of the greatest among all the planets, of the one that has more mass than all the others together. One cannot ascribe this distance of Jupiter from Mars to the intention that their attractions should hinder one another as little as possible. For according to such a ground the planet between two circles would always be found nearest to that one whose attraction, united with its own, can disturb the revolutions of both around the sun the least: consequently to that one that has the smallest mass. Since now, according to the correct calculations of Newton, the force with which Jupiter can act into the course of Mars relates to that which he exerts into Saturn through the united attraction, as ⅟12512 to ⅟200; one can easily make the calculation of how much nearer to the circle of Mars Jupiter would have to be found than to that of Saturn, if their distance had been determined through the intention of their external relation, and not through the mechanism of their generation. Since this is now found to be quite otherwise: since a planetary circle, with regard to the two circles that are above and below it, is often found to be more distant from that in which a smaller planet runs than from the orbit of one of greater mass; the width of the space around the circle of each planet, however, always has a correct relation to its mass; so it is clear that the way of generation must have determined these relations, and that, because these determinations, like the cause and the consequences of them, seem to be combined, one will probably hit it most correctly if one regards the spaces comprehended between the circles as the receptacles of that material out of which the planets have formed: from which it follows immediately that the magnitude of the former must be proportioned to the masses of the latter, which relation, however, in the case of the more distant planets is increased through the greater dispersion of the elementary matter in these regions in the first state. Hence, of two planets that come fairly close to one another in mass, the more distant must have a greater space of formation, i.e. a greater distance from the two nearest circles, both because the material there was in itself of a specifically lighter kind, and because it was more dispersed, than in that which formed nearer to the sun. Hence, although the earth together with the moon does not yet seem to be equal to Venus in bodily content, it has nevertheless required a greater space of formation around itself: because it had to form itself from a more dispersed material than this lower planet. Of Saturn it is to be presumed, from these grounds, that its sphere of formation will have extended much farther on the remote side than on the side toward the center, (as this holds of almost all the planets;) and hence the intervening space between the circle of Saturn and the orbit of the heavenly body nearest above this planet, which one can presume above it, will be much wider than between the same and Jupiter.",["- Stoff = \"material\" (Grundstoff = \"basic material\"); Materie = \"matter\"; both kept distinct.","- Kreis = \"circle\"; Bahn = \"orbit\"; Zwischenraum = \"intervening space\"; Raum = \"space\".","- Absicht = \"intention\" (glossary: aim; intention), used consistently for both occurrences.","- Grund (in \"nach solchem Grunde\") = \"ground\"; Erzeugung = \"generation\"; Bildung = \"formation\"; Bildungsraum = \"space of formation\".","- Grösse = \"magnitude\"; Masse = \"mass\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\" (in \"relates to ... as\" for the verb sich verhalten).","- \"Bestimmung(en)\" = \"determination(s)\"; bestimmen = \"determine\".","- \"der Abstand\" and \"Entfernung\" both rendered by distance/…; Abstand = \"distance\" and Entfernung = \"distance\" (one English word for two German words).","- Ambiguity kept: \"diese ihren Massen\" / \"deren Grösse dieser ihren Massen\" — \"the former ... the latter\" is my rendering of deren/dieser (the spaces / the planets); the German pronouns are not explicit.","- Spelling \"⅟12512\" kept as in the source (possible OCR/original doubt about the figure); \"Attractionen\" rendered \"attractions\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0231":["Thus everything in the planetary world-structure proceeds by steps, with correct relations to the first generating force, which, having been more effective near the center than in the distance, continues on into all unbounded widths. The diminution of the impressed projectile force, the deviation from the most exact agreement in the direction and the position of the circles, the densities of the heavenly bodies, the thriftiness of nature with regard to the space of their formation: everything diminishes step-wise from the center into the far distances: everything shows that the first cause was bound to the mechanical rules of motion, and did not act through a free choice.",["- Weltbaue = \"world-structure\" (Welt = \"world\"); Kraft = \"force\"; schiessende Kraft = \"projectile force\".","- Ursache = \"cause\"; Wahl = \"choice\"; Centro (Latin form in German text) = \"center\".","- \"stuffenweise\" and \"stuffenartig\" both rendered by \"step\"-forms (\"by steps\", \"step-wise\").","- Ambiguity kept: \"die ... in alle unbeschränkte Weiten fort\" — the relative clause's subject/attachment of \"fort\" is left as in German."]],"naturgeschichte-0232":["But what indicates as distinctly as anything else the natural formation of the celestial globes out of the basic material that had been originally spread out in the space of the heavens, which is now empty, is that agreement which I borrow from Herr von Buffon, but which in his theory does not by far have the utility, as in ours. For according to his remark, if one sums up together the planets whose masses one can determine by calculation: namely Saturn, Jupiter, the earth and the moon; then they give a lump whose density comes to the density of the body of the sun as 640 to 650, compared to which, since they are the principal pieces in the planetary system, the remaining planets Mars, Venus and Mercury hardly deserve to be counted; one will then fairly be astonished at the remarkable equality that prevails between the matter of the entire planetary edifice, if it is considered as united in one lump, and the mass of the suns. It would be an irresponsible frivolity to ascribe to happenstance this analogy, which among a manifold of such infinitely different materials, of which some are to be met with on our earth alone that have been exceeded by one another 15 thousand times in density, nevertheless in the whole comes so near to the proportion of 1 to 1: and one must grant that if one considers the sun as a mixture of all sorts of matter that are separated from one another in the planetary edifice, all together seem to have formed themselves in a space that was originally filled with uniformly spread out material, and to have gathered without distinction on the central body, but, for the formation of the planets, to have been divided up in accordance with the heights. I leave it to those who cannot admit the mechanical generation of the world-bodies to explain, from the motives of God's choice, this so special agreement, where they can. I will finally stop grounding a matter of such convincing distinctness as the development of the world-edifice from the forces of nature is upon more proofs. If one is in a position to remain immovable in the face of so much convincing, then one must either lie far too deep in the fetters of prejudice, or be wholly incapable of swinging oneself up, over the rubbish of handed-down opinions, to the consideration of the very purest truth. Meanwhile it is to be believed that no one except the feeble-minded, on whose approval one may not count, could fail to recognize the correctness of this theory, if the agreements that the world-structure has in all its combinations to the utility of the rational creature did not seem to have at their ground something more than mere universal laws of nature. One also believes with right that skillful arrangements that aim at a worthy end must have a wise understanding as author, and one will be fully satisfied if one considers that, since the natures of things recognize no other than precisely this original source, their essential and universal properties must have a natural inclination to decent sequels that agree well with one another. One will therefore not need to find it strange, if one becomes aware of arrangements of the constitution of the world that serve the exchanged advantage of the creatures, to ascribe them to a natural sequel from the universal laws of nature, for what flows from these is not the effect of blind chance or of unreasonable necessity: it is grounded in the end after all in the highest wisdom, from which the universal properties borrow their agreement. The one inference is entirely correct: If order and beauty shine forth in the constitution of the world; then there is a God. But the other is no less grounded: If this order has been able to flow from universal laws of nature; then the whole of nature is necessarily an effect of the highest wisdom.",["- Deutlich/Deutlichkeit = \"distinct(ly)/distinctness\"; Beweisthümer = \"proofs\" (Beweis); Ueberführung = \"convincing\" (not Überzeugung, \"conviction\").","- Verfassung = \"constitution\" (of the world); Beschaffenheit = \"property\"; Weltkörper = \"world-bodies\"; Weltgebäude = \"world-edifice\"; Weltbau = \"world-structure\"; Ungefehr = \"happenstance\"; Zufall = \"chance\".","- \"als in der unsrigen\" after \"den Nutzen\" is elliptical in the German and is left so; \"der Sonnen\" may be a genitive plural (\"of the suns\") or an old singular form (\"of the sun\").","- Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\"; Folge = \"sequel\" (both \"Folgen\" and \"eine natürliche Folge\"); Verbindungen = \"combinations\"; erkennen = \"recognize\"; verkennen = \"fail to recognize\".","- In \"alle insgesammt sich ... gebildet zu haben ... versammlet ... eingetheilet worden\" the grammatical subject is left as in the German.","- \"zum gewechselten Vortheile\" = \"exchanged advantage\" (literal; \"mutual\" is not what the German says). Schwungskräfte appears in the next passage only."]],"naturgeschichte-0233":["But if one is thoroughly pleased to recognize the immediate application of divine wisdom in all arrangements of nature that comprehend among themselves harmony and useful ends, in that one does not credit the development from universal laws of motion with any agreeing sequels; then I would counsel, in the contemplation of the world-structure, to direct one's eyes not to a single one among the heavenly bodies, but to the whole, in order to tear oneself out of this delusion all at once. If the oblique position of the earth's axis, relative to the plane of its yearly course, through the favored alternation of the seasons, is supposed to be a proof of the immediate hand of God, one need only hold this property in the other heavenly bodies against it; one will then become aware that it varies in each of them, and that in this diversity there are also some that do not have it at all: as e.g. Jupiter, whose axis is perpendicular to the plane of its circle, and Mars, whose own almost is so, both of which enjoy no diversity of the seasons, and are yet just as much works of the highest wisdom as the others. The accompaniment by moons in the case of Saturn, Jupiter and the earth would seem to be special arrangements of the being, if the free deviation from this end, through the whole system of the world-structure, did not indicate that nature, without being disturbed in its free conduct by an extraordinary constraint, has brought forth these determinations. Jupiter has four moons, Saturn five, the earth one, the remaining planets none at all; although it seems that these, on account of their longer nights, would be more in need of them than those. If one admires the proportioned equality of the forces of impetus impressed on the planets with the central inclinations of their distance, as the cause whence they run almost in circles around the sun and, through the uniformity of the warmth imparted by it, are made suitable for dwelling-places of rational creatures, and regards it as the immediate finger of omnipotence; then one will be led back all at once to the universal laws of nature, if one considers that this planetary property loses itself gradually, with all steps of diminution, in the depth of the heavens, and that precisely the highest wisdom, which has taken pleasure in the moderated motion of the planets, the defects also not excluded, with which the system ends, in that it ceases in complete irregularity and disorder. Nature, notwithstanding that it has an essential determination to perfection and order, encompasses within itself, in the domain of its manifold, all possible variations, even down to the defects and deviations. Precisely this same unlimited fertility of it has brought forth the inhabited celestial globes as well as the comets, the useful mountains and the harmful cliffs, the habitable landscapes and desolate deserts, the virtues and vices.",["- Begreiffen = \"comprehend\" (glossary); Beweisthum = \"proof\"; Folgen = \"sequels\"; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Bestimmungen = \"determinations\".","- Fläche and Plane both = \"plane\"; Verschiedenheit = \"diversity\" in both occurrences; Abwechselung(en) = \"alternation\" (seasons) and \"variations\" (last sentences), abwechselt = \"varies\".","- \"Mars, dessen seine es beynahe ist\" = \"whose own almost is so\": the noun (axis) is left unstated, as in the German.","- \"würden scheinen\" (plural) after the singular \"Begleitung\" is kept as in the German; des Wesens = \"of the being\" (lower case in the source).","- Schwungskräfte = \"forces of impetus\" (no glossary entry); Stufen = \"steps\".","- \"auch die Mängel nicht ausgeschlossen\" is an absolute phrase, kept as such; it could be read as \"not excluding the defects\" or \"including the defects\"; the clause beginning \"und daß eben die höchste Weisheit ...\" has no finite main verb in the German beyond \"hat ... gehabt\", and is left unchanged.","- Sie in \"und sie, als den unmittelbaren Finger der Allmacht, ansiehet\" is left as a bare pronoun."]],"naturgeschichte-0234":["Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens. Third Part, Which Contains an Attempt at a Comparison, Grounded on the Analogies of Nature, between the Inhabitants of Different Planets.",["- Allgemein = \"universal\" throughout; Versuch = \"attempt\"; Einwohner = \"inhabitants\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0235":["Whoever knows the relation of all worlds, from one part to the other, whoever is acquainted with the multitude of all suns, and every planetary circle: whoever cognizes the different inhabitants of each star, to him alone it is granted, why things are so, just as they are, to grasp, and to explain to us.",["- Verse printed as continuous text in the source; weis = \"knows\" (wissen), kennet = \"is acquainted with\" (kennen), erkennet = \"cognizes\" (erkennen).","- \"Dem ist allein ... vergönnet, / Zu fassen, und uns zu erklären\": the verse inversion is kept; fassen = \"grasp\"; Menge = \"multitude\"; Theil = \"part\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0236":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0237":["Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens. Third Part. Appendix, on the Inhabitants of the Celestial Bodies.",["- Gestirne = \"celestial bodies\" (distinct from Himmelskörper = \"heavenly bodies\" and Himmelskugeln = \"celestial globes\" elsewhere in this set); Anhang = \"appendix\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0238":["Because I hold that it means to dishonor the character of philosophy if one makes use of it to assert, with a kind of frivolity, free excesses of wit, with some semblance, even if one were to declare that it happened only in order to amuse; so shall in the present attempt adduce no other propositions than such as can really contribute to the expansion of our cognition, and whose probability is at the same time so well grounded that one can hardly refrain from letting them hold.",["- Weltweisheit = \"philosophy\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Wahrscheinlichkeit = \"probability\"; Sätze = \"propositions\"; Erweiterung = \"expansion\" (not Ausdehnung, \"extension\").","- \"so werde ... anführen\" lacks an expressed subject in the German (the \"ich\" of the \"Weil\" clause); left elliptical.","- Scheinbarkeit = \"semblance\" (related to Schein, \"illusion\"); \"sie gelten zu lassen\" = \"letting them hold\" (gelten; cf. gültig, \"valid\"); the construction \"wenn man sich gleich erklären wolte\" is rendered \"even if one were to declare\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0239":["Although it might seem that in this kind of subject the freedom to invent has no proper limitations, and that in the judgment about the constitution of the inhabitants of remote worlds one may let the reins of fantasy loose with far greater unrestraint than a painter in the depiction of the plants or animals of undiscovered lands, and that such thoughts could neither be properly proved nor refuted; one must nevertheless admit that the distances of the heavenly bodies from the sun bring with them certain relations, which draw after them an essential influence on the different properties of the thinking natures that are to be found on them, whose way of acting and suffering is bound to the constitution of the matter with which they are connected, and depends on the measure of the impressions that the world awakens in them, according to the properties of the relation of their dwelling place to the center of attraction and of warmth.",["- Schranke = limitation; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Eindruck = impression; Art = way; Urteil = judgment; verknüpfen = connect; Materie = matter.","- \"Vorwurfes\" rendered \"subject\" (literally something put before one); \"Phantasey\" = fantasy.","- \"welche … nach sich ziehen, die auf denenselben befindlich sind\": the antecedent of the second \"die\" (the thinking natures) and of \"deren\"/\"als deren\" is kept as in German; \"als\" before \"deren\" is left untranslated as it stands in the German.","- \"ihnen\" at the end could refer to the thinking natures; kept as \"them\". \"Mittelpunkte der Attraction\": Attraction is Latinate and kept as \"attraction\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0240":["I am of the opinion that it is not precisely necessary to assert that all planets must be inhabited, although it would be an absurdity to deny this with regard to all of them, or even only to most of them. Given the richness of nature, where worlds and systems, with regard to the whole of creation, are only motes of sun-dust, there could well also be desolate and uninhabited regions that would not be utilized in the most exact way for the end of nature, namely the consideration of rational beings. It would be as if one wanted to make a scruple, on the ground of the wisdom of GOD, about admitting that sandy and uninhabited deserts occupy great stretches of the earth's surface, and that there are abandoned islands in the ocean on which no human being is to be found. Meanwhile a planet is much less, with regard to the whole of creation, than a desert or island is with regard to the earth's surface.",["- Meinung = opinion; behaupten = assert; notwendig = necessary; Zweck = end; Betrachtung = consideration; Wesen = being; Grund = ground.","- \"Erdbodens\" rendered \"earth's surface\" in both places (literally \"earth-ground\"); \"Ganzen der Schöpfung\" = \"whole of creation\".","- \"vernünftiger Wesen\" = \"rational beings\"; \"Bedenken machen\" = \"make a scruple\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0241":["Perhaps not all heavenly bodies have yet formed themselves completely; centuries, and perhaps thousands of years, belong to it, until a great heavenly body has attained a fixed state of its materials. Jupiter seems still to be in this struggle. The noticeable alternation of its shape, at different times, has already long ago let astronomers conjecture that it must suffer great upheavals, and is by far not so calm on its surface as an inhabitable planet must be. If it has no inhabitants, and should also never have any, what an infinitely small expenditure of nature would this be, with regard to the immeasurability of the whole of creation? And would it not rather be a sign of poverty than of abundance of the same, if it should be so careful in every point of space to show all its riches?",["- Zustand is not used; \"Stand\" = \"state\" (here \"festen Stand seiner Materien\"); Materie = matter (plural \"materials\" for \"Materien\" is a choice, the glossary Stoff = material is not the word here).","- \"Gestalt\" = shape; \"Zeichen\" = sign; \"derselben\" (referring to nature) rendered \"the same\".","- \"Ueberflusses … derselben\": ambiguity of referent preserved (\"of the same\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0242":["But one can conjecture with still more satisfaction that, although it is now uninhabited, it will nevertheless one day become so, when the period of its formation shall be completed. Perhaps our earth existed for a thousand or more years before it found itself in a condition to be able to sustain human beings, animals and plants. That a planet now comes some thousands of years later to this perfection does no detriment to the end of its existence. Precisely for that reason it will also remain longer in the future in the perfection of its constitution, once it has attained it; for it is once and for all a certain law of nature: everything that has a beginning approaches constantly its demise, and is so much the nearer to it, the further it has distanced itself from the point of its beginning.",["- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Zweck = end; Dasein = existence; Naturgesetz = law of nature; Verfassung rendered \"condition\" and, in the second occurrence, \"constitution\" (Beschaffenheit is not the word here; Verfassung is outside the glossary).","- \"vorhanden gewesen\" = \"existed\" (literally \"been at hand\").","- \"einmal\" in \"es ist einmal ein gewisses Naturgesetz\" rendered \"once and for all\", a doubtful idiom."]],"naturgeschichte-0243":["The satirical representation of that witty head from The Hague, who, after the citation of the general news from the R. d. Wissenschaften, knew how to present the conceit of the necessary population of all world-bodies from the ridiculous side, cannot be other than approved. \"Those creatures,\" he says, \"which inhabit the forests on the head of a beggar, had long regarded their abode as an immeasurable globe, and themselves as the masterpiece of creation, when one among them, whom heaven had endowed with a finer soul, a little Fontenelle of his kind, unexpectedly became aware of the head of a nobleman. At once he called together all the witty heads of his quarter, and said to them with rapture: we are not the only animate beings of the whole of nature: behold here a new land, here dwell more lice.\" If the outcome of this inference awakens a laugh, it does so not for the reason that it departs far from the human way of judging, but because the very same error, which has an equal cause at its ground in the human being, seems to deserve more excuse in these.",["- Vorstellung = representation; Einbildung = conceit (cognate Einbildungskraft = imagination); Schluß = inference; Art = way; urteilen = judge (v.); Grund = ground; Wesen = being; Weltkörper = world-bodies.","- \"R. d. Wissenschaften\" kept as printed (abbreviation unresolved in the text). \"sprichter\" is an OCR run-together, read \"spricht er\".","- \"gebilliget werden\" = \"be approved\"; \"in diesen\" at the end refers to the lice (the German pronoun is plural, as in the English \"these\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0244":["Let us judge without prejudice. This insect, which, as much according to its way of living as to its worthlessness, expresses very well the constitution of most human beings, can with good right be used for such a comparison. Because, according to its conceit, nature has an infinite amount at stake in its existence, it holds the whole remaining creation to be in vain that does not carry with it an exact aiming at its kind, as the center of its ends. The human being, who stands equally infinitely far from the highest rung of beings, is so bold as to flatter himself with the same conceit about the necessity of his existence. The infinity of creation comprehends all natures that its overflowing richness brings forth with equal necessity in itself. From the most sublime class among the thinking beings down to the most despised insect, no member is indifferent to it; and none can be lacking without the beauty of the whole, which consists in the nexus, being thereby interrupted. Meanwhile everything is determined through general laws, which nature effects through the combination of its originally implanted forces. Because in its procedure it brings forth nothing but propriety and order, no single aim may disturb and interrupt its consequences. At its first formation the generation of a planet was only an infinitely small sequel of its fruitfulness; and now it would be something absurd that its so well-grounded laws should yield to the particular ends of this atom. If the constitution of a heavenly body sets natural hindrances against population, it will be uninhabited, although it would in and for itself be more beautiful if it had inhabitants. The excellence of creation loses nothing thereby: for the infinite is among all magnitudes that which is not diminished through the withdrawal of a finite part. It would be as if one wanted to complain that the space between Jupiter and Mars stands so unnecessarily empty, and that there are comets that are not populated. In fact, however worthless that insect may seem to us, nature surely has more at stake in the preservation of its whole class than in a small number of more excellent creatures, of which there are nevertheless infinitely many, even if a region or place should be deprived of them. Because it is inexhaustible in the production of both, one indeed sees it equally unconcerned to leave both, in their preservation and destruction, to the general laws. Has the possessor of those inhabited forests, on the head of the beggar, ever made greater devastations among the kind of this colony than the son of Philip wrought among the kind of his fellow citizens, when his evil genius had put it into his head that the world was produced only for his sake?",["- Beschaffenheit = constitution; Einbildung = conceit; Zusammenhang = nexus (glossary: interconnection; nexus); Verbindung = combination; Kraft = force; Gesetz = law; Zweck = end; Größe = magnitude; Folge = sequel (first \"Folgen\" rendered \"consequences\"; Folgerung is not the word); Absicht = aim; besonder = particular; bestimmen = determine; Wesen = being; Art = way; Zahl = number.","- \"Fuge\" in \"mit gutem Fuge\" is read as \"Fug\" (OCR/spelling); \"Abzielung\" = \"aiming\"; \"Atomus\" = \"atom\".","- \"sie\" in \"Weil sie in ihrem Verfahren\" and \"Weil sie … unerschöpflich ist\" refers to nature (Natur, feminine); \"it\" kept. \"Sohn Philipps\" = Alexander, not named in the text; a gap after \"Philipps\" in the OCR is left as is.","- \"Ort\" = place; \"Gegend\" = region. \"Wohlanständigkeit\" = \"propriety\".","- \"es\" in \"so hält es\" refers to the insect (neuter \"Insekt\"); in \"es ist der Natur gewiß an … gelegen\" it is impersonal."]],"naturgeschichte-0245":["Meanwhile, however, most among the planets are certainly inhabited, and those that are not will one day become so. What relations will now be caused, among the different kinds of these inhabitants, by the relation of their place in the world-edifice to the center from which the warmth spreads that gives life to everything. For it is certain that this, among the matters of these heavenly bodies, brings with it, in proportion to their distance, certain relations in their determinations. The human being, who among all rational beings is the one that we cognize most distinctly, although his inner constitution is still an unresearched problema to us, must serve as the basis and as the general point of reference in this comparison. We do not wish to consider him here according to his moral qualities, nor according to the physical arrangement of his structure: we wish only to investigate what limitations the faculty of thinking rationally, and the motion of his body that obeys it, suffer through the constitution of the matter, proportioned to the distance from the sun, to which he is tied. Notwithstanding the infinite distance that is to be found between the power of thinking and the motion of matter, between the rational spirit and the body, it is nevertheless certain that the human being, who has all his concepts and representations from the impression that the universe arouses in his soul by means of the body, depends entirely on the constitution of this matter to which the Creator has bound him, both with regard to the distinctness of those [concepts and representations] and also the skill in combining and comparing them, which one calls the faculty of thinking.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0246]\nThe human being is created to receive the impressions and stirrings that the world is to arouse in him through that body which is the visible part of his essence, and whose matter is indispensable not only to the invisible spirit that inhabits him in impressing the first concepts of external objects, but also in the inner action of repeating these, of combining them: in short, of thinking (*). In the measure that his body forms itself, the capacities of his thinking nature also acquire the appropriate degrees of perfection, and first attain a settled and manly faculty when the fibers of his instruments have acquired the firmness and durability that is the completion of their formation. Those capacities develop early enough in him by which he can do enough for the need that the dependence on external things draws upon him. In some human beings it remains at this degree of unfolding. The faculty of combining abstracted concepts, and of ruling over the inclination of the passions through a free application of insights, arrives late, in some never in their whole life; but in all it is weak: it serves the lower powers, over which it should nevertheless rule, and in the governance of which the preeminence of its nature consists. If one looks at the life of most human beings: this creature seems to be created in order to draw sap into itself like a plant and to grow, to propagate its kind, finally to grow old, and to die. Among all creatures he least attains the purpose of his existence, because he uses up his preeminent capacities for such intentions as the remaining creatures attain with far lesser ones, and yet far more securely and decently. He would also be the most contemptible among all, at least in the eyes of true wisdom, if the hope of the future did not elevate him, and the period of a complete unfolding were not in store for the powers enclosed in him.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0247]\n(*) It is established from the grounds of psychology that, by virtue of the present constitution in which the creation has made soul and body dependent on one another, the former must not only receive all concepts of the universe through the community and influence of the latter; but the exercise of its power of thinking itself also depends on its constitution, and borrows from its assistance the necessary capacity for it.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0248]\nIf one investigates the cause of the hindrances that maintain human nature in so deep an abasement: it is found in the coarseness of the matter in which his spiritual part is sunk, in the inflexibility of the fibers, and the sluggishness and immobility of the fluids that are to obey its stirrings. The nerves and fluids of his brain deliver to him only coarse and indistinct concepts, and because he cannot oppose to the stimulation of the sensible sensations, in the interior of his faculty of thinking, representations of sufficient strength as a counterweight: he is swept away by his passions, deafened and disturbed by the tumult of the elements that sustain his machine. The efforts of reason to raise itself against this, and to drive off this confusion through the light of the power of judgment, are like glimpses of the sun when thick clouds incessantly interrupt and darken its brightness.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0249]\nThis coarseness of the material and of the fabric in the structure of human nature is the cause of that sluggishness which keeps the capacities of the soul in a constant languor and powerlessness. The action of reflecting, and of the representations enlightened by reason, is a laborious state into which the soul cannot put itself without resistance, and from which, through a natural inclination of the bodily machine, it soon falls back into the passive state, where the totality of the stimulations determine and govern all its actions.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0250]\nThis sluggishness of his power of thinking, which is a sequel of the dependence on a coarse and unwieldy matter, is not only the source of vice, but also of error. Held back by the difficulty that is bound up with the effort to disperse the mist of confused concepts, and to separate from the sensible impressions the general cognition arising through compared ideas, it prefers to give place to a hasty assent, and quiets itself in the possession of an insight which the sluggishness of its nature and the resistance of the matter allow it scarcely to glimpse from the side.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0251]\nIn this dependence the spiritual capacities dwindle together with the liveliness of the body: when old age, through the weakened circulation of the fluids, cooks only thick fluids in the body, when the flexibility of the fibers and the nimbleness in all motions decreases, then the powers of the spirit grow numb in a like exhaustion. The swiftness of thoughts, the clarity of representation, the liveliness of wit and the faculty of memory become powerless and cool. The concepts grafted in by long experience still compensate somewhat for the loss of these powers, and the understanding would betray its incapacity still more distinctly, if the vehemence of the passions, which have need of its reins, did not decrease at the same time, and even sooner than it.\n\n[naturgeschichte-0252]\nIt is evident from this, accordingly, that the powers of the human soul are limited and checked by the hindrances of a coarse matter to which they are most intimately combined; but it is something still more remarkable that this specific constitution of the material has an essential relation to the degree of the influence with which the sun, according to the measure of its distance, enlivens it and makes it fit for the functions of the animal economy. This necessary relation to the fire that spreads from the center of the world-system in order to maintain the matter in the necessary motion is the ground of an analogy that is firmly posited precisely from this between the different inhabitants of the planets: and each class of them is bound by virtue of this relation to the place, through the necessity of its nature, that has been assigned to it in the universe.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstände (0246) = \"objects\"; Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation; Vermögen = faculty (0245, 0246, 0252); Kraft = power/force; Fähigkeit = capacity.\n- Wesen = \"being\" in 0245 (vernünftigen Wesen), but \"essence\" in 0246 (Theil seines Wesens), per glossary; Geist = spirit, Seele = soul.\n- Materie = \"matter\"; Stoff = \"material\" (0249, 0252); Beschaffenheit = constitution.\n- 0245, \"Was vor Verhältnisse werden nun…verursachet werden\" is an exclamatory/indirect-question form with no main clause; kept as a sentence with a period, as in the original. Verhältnisse = relations (Verhältniß in 0252 also \"relation\"; Beziehung = \"relation\" too, Latinate-unmarked).\n- 0245 \"derselben\" / \"dieselbe\" (concepts and representations) and 0245 \"Probl*e*ma\" kept as problema; the bracketed referents are added by me and are not in the German.\n- 0246 \"Nothdurft\" = need; \"Auswickelung/Ausbildung\" = unfolding/formation; \"Absichten\" = intentions; \"Zweck\" = purpose; \"Daseyn\" = existence.\n- 0250 \"sie\" (the sluggishness / the power of thinking, both feminine) left ambiguous in \"Held back…it prefers\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Ideen\" = ideas.\n- 0249 \"die sämtlichen Reizungen\" rendered \"the totality of the stimulations\"; Zustand = state; Reizung/Reitzung = stimulation; Empfindung = sensation.\n- 0251 \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Witz\" = wit; \"Erfahrung\" = experience. 0248 \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment, \"Vernunft\" = reason.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0246":["Man is created to receive the impressions and stirrings that the world is supposed to excite in him through that body which is the visible part of his essence, and whose matter is indispensable not only to the invisible spirit that inhabits it, in impressing the first concepts of the outer objects, but also in the inner action of repeating these, of combining them: in short, of thinking (*). In the measure that his body develops, the capacities of his thinking nature also acquire the appropriate degrees of perfection, and attain a settled and manly faculty only when the fibers of his instruments have acquired the firmness and durability that is the completion of their development. Those capacities develop in him early enough through which he can do enough for the need that the dependence on external things draws upon him. In some human beings it remains at this degree of unfolding. The faculty of combining abstracted concepts, and, through a free application of insights, of ruling over the inclination of the passions, arrives late, in some never in their whole life; but in all it is weak: it serves the lower powers, over which it nevertheless ought to rule, and in the governing of which the preeminence of its nature consists. If one looks at the life of most human beings: this creature seems to be created to draw sap into itself like a plant and to grow, to propagate its kind, finally to become old, and to die. Among all creatures he attains least the end of his existence, because he uses up his preeminent capacities for such intentions as the remaining creatures attain with far lesser ones, and yet far more surely and more becomingly. He would also be the most contemptible among all, at least in the eyes of true wisdom, if the hope of the future did not elevate him, and if the period of a complete unfolding did not await the powers shut up in him.",["- *Wesen* = \"essence\" (also \"being\" in the glossary); *Geist* = \"spirit\"; *Fähigkeit* = \"capacity\"; *Vermögen* = \"faculty\"; *Kräfte* = \"powers\" (glossary \"force; power\").","- *Gegenstände* = \"objects\"; *Begriffe* = \"concepts\"; *Vollkommenheit* = \"perfection\"; *Absichten* = \"intentions\"; *Zweck* = \"end\"; *Daseyn* = \"existence\".","- \"(*)\" is the footnote marker; the note is passage 0247.","- \"Auswickelung\"/\"Ausbildung\"/\"entwickeln\" are rendered \"unfolding\"/\"development\"/\"develop\"; \"Ausbildung\" and \"entwickeln\" both = \"development\"/\"develop\".","- \"Rührungen\" = \"stirrings\"; \"Nothdurft\" = \"need\"; \"Vorzug\" = \"preeminence\" and \"vorzüglich\" = \"preeminent\".","- Ambiguity kept: \"es\" in \"bleibt es bey diesem Grade\" and \"es dienet den unteren Kräften\" has no stated antecedent in the German; \"dessen Materie ... dienet ... unentbehrlich ist\" is a loosely constructed sentence, left as is."]],"naturgeschichte-0247":["(*) It is settled from the grounds of psychology that, by virtue of the present constitution in which creation has made soul and body dependent on each other, the former must not only receive all concepts of the universe through the community and influence of the latter; but the exercise of its power of thought itself also depends on the latter's constitution, and borrows from its assistance the necessary capacity for it.",["- *Gründen* = \"grounds\"; *Verfassung* = \"constitution\"; *Gemeinschaft* = \"community\"; *Denkungskraft* = \"power of thought\"; *Fähigkeit* = \"capacity\".","- *Universi* is Latin genitive in the German text, rendered \"of the universe\"; \"die erstere/dessen\" = \"the former/the latter('s)\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0248":["If one investigates the cause of the hindrances that keep human nature in such a deep abasement; it is found in the coarseness of the matter in which his spiritual part is sunk, in the inflexibility of the fibers, and the sluggishness and immobility of the fluids that are supposed to obey its stirrings. The nerves and fluids of his brain furnish him only coarse and indistinct concepts, and because he cannot oppose to the stimulation of sensible sensations, in the interior of his faculty of thought, representations powerful enough for equilibrium: so he is swept away by his passions, deafened and disturbed by the tumult of the elements that maintain his machine. The efforts of reason to rise up against this, and to drive off this confusion through the light of the power of judgment, are like the sun's glances when thick clouds incessantly interrupt and darken its serenity.",["- *undeutlich* = \"indistinct\" (opposite of *deutlich* = distinct); *sinnlich* = \"sensible\"; *Empfindung* = \"sensation\"; *Vorstellung* = \"representation\".","- *Urtheilskraft* = \"power of judgment\"; *Vernunft* = \"reason\"; *Denkungsvermögen* = \"faculty of thought\".","- \"dessen Regungen\": \"dessen\" refers back to \"his spiritual part\" or \"his\" (the human being); rendered \"its\", ambiguity of antecedent is in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0249":["This coarseness of the material and of the fabric in the structure of human nature is the cause of that sluggishness that keeps the capacities of the soul in a constant languor and powerlessness. The action of reflection, and of the representations enlightened through reason, is a laborious state into which the soul cannot put itself without resistance, and from which, through a natural inclination of the bodily machine, it immediately falls back into the passive state, where the entirety of the stimulations determine and govern all its actions.",["- *Stoff* = \"material\" (here); *Materie* = \"matter\" elsewhere. *Nachdenken* = \"reflection\" (not *Überlegung*).","- *Zustand* = \"state\"; *bestimmen* = \"determine\"; *Reizungen* = \"stimulations\".","- *leidenden* = \"passive\" (lit. \"suffering\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0250":["This sluggishness of his power of thought, which is a sequel of the dependence on a coarse and unwieldy matter, is not only the source of vice, but also of error. Kept back by the difficulty that is bound up with the effort to disperse the fog of confused concepts, and to separate the general cognition arising through compared ideas from the sensible impressions, it gives place rather to a hasty assent, and settles itself in the possession of an insight that the sluggishness of its nature and the resistance of matter allow it scarcely to glimpse from the side.",["- *Folge* = \"sequel\"; *Erkenntniß* = \"cognition\"; *Idee* = \"idea\"; *Eindruck* = \"impression\"; *Einsicht* = \"insight\".","- *Beyfall* = \"assent\"; *Irrthum* = \"error\".","- \"Abgehalten\" at the head of the clause is a participle whose subject \"sie\" follows; the construction is kept as in German. *Materie* = \"matter\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0251":["In this dependence the spiritual capacities dwindle together with the liveliness of the body: when old age, through the weakened circulation of the fluids, cooks only thick fluids in the body, when the flexibility of the fibers and the agility in all movements decreases, the powers of the spirit stiffen in an equal exhaustion. The swiftness of thoughts, the clarity of representation, the liveliness of wit and the faculty of memory become powerless and grow cold. The concepts grafted in through long experience still compensate to some extent for the loss of these powers, and the understanding would betray its incapacity still more distinctly if the vehemence of the passions, which need its reins, did not diminish at the same time, and even sooner than it.",["- *Verstand* = \"understanding\"; *Erfahrung* = \"experience\"; *Vorstellung* = \"representation\"; *Erinnerungsvermögen* = \"faculty of memory\"; *deutlicher* = \"distinctly\".","- *Witz* = \"wit\"; *Geist* = \"spirit\"; *Kräfte* = \"powers\".","- \"als er\" at the end: \"it\" could refer to the understanding or to the passions' vehemence; German \"er\" is the understanding (*Verstand*). The \"möchte\" is rendered with \"did not\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0252":["It therefore becomes distinctly clear from this that the powers of the human soul are limited and inhibited by the hindrances of a coarse matter to which they are most intimately combined; but it is something still more remarkable that this specific constitution of the material has an essential relation to the degree of influence with which the sun, according to the measure of its distance, enlivens them, and makes them fit for the functions of the animal economy. This necessary relation to the fire that spreads out from the center of the world-system, in order to maintain the matter in the necessary stirring, is the ground of an analogy that is firmly posited precisely from this among the different inhabitants of the planets: and every class of them is, by virtue of this relation, bound by the necessity of its nature to the place that has been assigned to it in the universe.",["- *verbunden* = \"combined\"; *Beschaffenheit* = \"constitution\"; *Stoff* = \"material\"; *Materie* = \"matter\"; *Grund* = \"ground\"; *Ort* = \"place\".","- *Welt-system* = \"world-system\"; *Universo* (Latin ablative) = \"universe\"; *Verhältniß* = \"relation\", *Beziehung* = \"relation\" (one English word for two German words).","- *Hinflusses* is an unusual form (cf. *Einfluß*); rendered \"influence\". *Verrichtungen* = \"functions\".","- Antecedent of \"sie\" in \"belebet\" and \"tüchtig macht\" is ambiguous (the powers of the soul or the matter); left ambiguous. *Classe* = \"class\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0253":["The inhabitants of the earth and of Venus cannot exchange their dwelling-places with one another without the ruin of both. The former, whose formative material is proportioned to the degree of warmth of his distance, and is therefore too light and volatile for a still greater one, would suffer in a more heated sphere violent movements and a disruption of his nature, which would arise from the dispersion and drying-out of the juices and a violent tension of his elastic fibers; the latter, whose coarser structure and inertia of the elements of his formation requires a great influence of the sun, would stiffen in a cooler region of the heavens and perish in lifelessness. Likewise the matters of which the body of the inhabitant of Jupiter consists must be far lighter and more volatile, so that the slight stirring with which the sun can work at this distance can move these machines just as forcefully as it accomplishes this in the lower regions, and so that everything be comprehended together in a general concept. The material of which the inhabitants of different planets, indeed even the animals and plants on them, are formed, must in general be of a so much lighter and finer kind, and the elasticity of the fibers together with the advantageous arrangement of their structure so much the more perfect, in the measure that they are farther removed from the sun.",["- Bildungsstoff = \"formative material\"; Stoff = \"material\", Materie = \"matter\" (plural *Materien*).","- \"vor\" (= für) in \"vor den Grad\" rendered \"to\"/\"proportioned to\"; \"vor einen noch grössern\" rendered \"for.\"","- \"damit alles in einem allgemeinen Begriffe zusammenfasse\": subject of \"zusammenfasse\" is ambiguous in German; kept as a passive (\"be comprehended\") — literal subject could be \"one\" or \"the sun.\"","- Wohnplätze = \"dwelling-places\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Abstand = \"distance\"; Sphäre = \"sphere.\"","- Spelling \"Fasernsammt,\" \"Sonneabstehen\" corrected from OCR spacing."]],"naturgeschichte-0254":["This relation is so natural and well grounded that not only do the motives of the final end lead to it, which in doctrine of nature are commonly regarded only as weak grounds, but at the same time the proportions of the specific constitution of the matters of which the planets consist, which are determined both by the calculations of Newton and by the grounds of cosmogony, confirm the same, according to which the material of which the heavenly bodies are formed is always of a lighter kind in the more distant ones than in the near, which must necessarily draw after it a like relation in those creatures that are generated and maintained on them.",["- Bewegungsgründe = \"motives\"; Gründe = \"grounds\"; Endzweck = \"final end\"; Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution.\"","- Antecedent of \"dieselbe\" (feminine singular) is \"Verhältniß\"; \"nach welchen\" refers back to \"Proportionen\" (plural), preserved as in German.","- \"ausgemacht\" = \"determined\" (also \"settled\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0255":["We have determined a comparison between the constitution of the matter with which the rational creatures on the planets are essentially united: and it can also be readily judged, according to the introduction of this consideration, that these relations will draw after them a sequence, also in regard to their spiritual capacity. If accordingly these spiritual capacities have a necessary dependence on the material of the machine which they inhabit; then we will be able to conclude with a more than probable conjecture: that the excellence of the thinking natures, the swiftness in their representations, the distinctness and liveliness of the concepts that they obtain through external impression, together with the faculty of combining them, finally also the nimbleness in actual practice, in short, the whole domain of their perfection, stand under a certain rule, according to which, in proportion to the distance of their dwelling-places from the sun, they become ever more excellent and more perfect.",["- Fähigkeit = \"capacity\" (glossary); Vermögen = \"faculty\"; geistig = \"spiritual.\"","- Vorstellungen = \"representations\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Eindruck = \"impression\"; Deutlichkeit = \"distinctness\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Vollkommenheit = \"perfection.\"","- \"sie zusammen zusetzen\" (= zusammensetzen) rendered \"combining them\"; \"Folge\" = \"sequence\"; \"Ausübung\" = \"practice\" (literally \"exercise\").","- \"wahrscheinlicher Vermuthung\": Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Vermuthung = \"conjecture.\"","- \"dieselben\" in the last clause refers to the plural \"thinking natures\"; kept ambiguous as in German."]],"naturgeschichte-0256":["Since this relation has a degree of credibility that is not far removed from a settled certainty, we find an open field for agreeable conjectures, which spring from the comparison of the properties of these different inhabitants. Human nature, which holds as it were the middle rung in the ladder of beings, sees itself in the midst between the two outermost boundaries of perfection, from both of whose ends it is equally far removed. If the representation of the most sublime classes of rational creatures, which inhabit Jupiter or Saturn, excites its jealousy, and humbles it through the cognition of its own lowliness; then the sight of the lowly steps can in turn make it speak content and calm it, those which in the planets Venus and Merkur are abased far below the perfection of human nature. What a sight worthy of wonder! On the one side we saw thinking creatures for whom a Greenlander or Hottentot would be a Newton; and on the other side others who admire the latter as an ape.",["- Glaubwürdigkeit = \"credibility\"; Gewißheit = \"certainty\"; Muthmassungen = \"conjectures\"; Eigenschaften = \"properties\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Wesen = \"beings.\"","- \"die in den Planeten Venus und Merkur ... erniedrigt seyn\": the relative clause attaches to \"the lowly steps\" (Stufen) or to the creatures; left as in German.","- \"sie\" in \"sie zufrieden sprechen\" refers to \"human nature\" (singular noun, plural pronoun in earlier \"ihre\"/\"sie\" usage); \"seyn\" kept in the subjunctive sense. \"Sprosse\" = \"rung.\"","- \"sahen\" is past tense in the German (\"we saw\"), kept.","- \"diesen\" in the last clause = the Greenlander or Hottentot."]],"naturgeschichte-0257":["When lately the higher sages saw what, not long ago, quite marvelously, a mortal among us had done, and how he unfolded the law of nature; they marveled that through an earthly creature such a thing was possible to happen; and looked upon our Newton as we look upon an ape.",["- Verse lines run together in the OCR without line breaks; the line divisions are not recoverable here and none have been supplied.","- Gesetz = \"law\"; möglich = \"possible\"; obern Weisen = \"higher sages.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0258":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0259":["To what progress in cognition will the insight of those blessed beings of the uppermost heavenly spheres not attain! What beautiful consequences will this enlightenment of insights not have on their moral constitution! The insights of the understanding, when they possess the proper degrees of completeness and distinctness, have far more lively charms in themselves than the sensible allurements, and are capable of ruling these victoriously and of treading them underfoot. How gloriously will the Godhead itself, which paints itself in all creatures, not paint itself in these thinking natures, which, as a sea unmoved by the storms of the passions, take up its image calmly and radiate it back! We do not wish to extend these conjectures beyond the boundaries prescribed to a physical treatise, we only note once more the analogy cited above: that the perfection of the world of spirits as well as of the material world in the planets, from Mercury to Saturn, or perhaps even beyond it, (insofar as there are still other planets,) grows and progresses in a correct succession of degrees, according to the proportion of their distances from the sun.",["- Fortgang = \"progress\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Einsicht = \"insight\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Folgen = \"consequences\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Gradenfolge = \"succession of degrees.\"","- Vollständigkeit = \"completeness\"; Deutlichkeit = \"distinctness\"; sinnlich = \"sensible\"; Geisterwelt = \"world of spirits.\"","- \"sie\" in \"sie ... zu beherrschen\" and \"diese\" refer to the insights/allurements; \"diese ... mahlen\" antecedent is \"Naturen\"; German ambiguity of \"ihr Bild\" (the Godhead's) kept as \"its image.\"","- \"Merkur\" given as \"Mercury\" in the continuous text; \"(wo ferne ...)\" = \"insofar as.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0260":["Meanwhile, in that this flows in part naturally from the consequences of the physical relation of their dwelling-places to the center of the world, and is in part fittingly occasioned: so on the other side the actual sight of the most excellent arrangements, which prepare themselves for the superior perfection of these natures in the upper regions, confirms this rule so distinctly that it should almost lay claim to complete conviction. The swiftness of the actions, which is combined with the advantages of a sublime nature, suits the rapidly alternating periods of time of those spheres better than the slowness of sluggish and imperfect creatures.",["- \"sich vor die vorzügliche Vollkommenheit ... anschickende Anstalten\": \"vor\" = für; rendered \"prepare themselves for\"; Anstalten = \"arrangements.\"","- \"dieses\" (first clause) has no clear noun antecedent; kept as \"this.\" \"Indessen, daß ... so\" kept as a correlative construction.","- Ueberzeugung = \"conviction\"; Zeitperioden = \"periods of time\"; Mittelpunkt = \"center\"; Welt = \"world.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0261":["The telescopes teach us that the alternation of day and night in Jupiter takes place in 10 hours. What would the inhabitant of the earth, if he were set in this planet, begin to do with this division? The 10 hours would scarcely be sufficient for that rest which this coarse machine needs for its recovery through sleep. What share of the following time would the preparation for the tasks of waking, the dressing, the time that is spent on eating, not demand, and how would a creature, whose actions take place with such slowness, not be made distracted, and incapable of anything solid, whose 5 hours' business would be suddenly interrupted by the intervention of a darkness just as long? On the other hand, if Jupiter is inhabited by more perfect creatures, who combine with a finer formation more elastic forces, and a greater nimbleness in practice; then one can believe that these 5 hours are to them just the same and more than what the 12 hours of the day amount to for the lowly class of men. We know that the need of time is something relative, which can be cognized and understood in no other way than from the magnitude of that which is to be accomplished, compared with the speed of practice. Hence just the same time, which for one kind of creatures is as it were only a moment, can be for another a long period, in which a great sequence of alterations unfolds itself through a swift activity. Saturn has, according to the probable calculation of its revolution, which we presented above, a still far shorter division of the day and night, and therefore allows still more excellent capacities to be conjectured in the nature of its inhabitants.",["- Eintheilung (Abtheilung) = \"division\" (both rendered \"division\"; glossary Einteilung/Teilung); Erkenntniß/erkannt = \"cognized\"; verstanden = \"understood.\"","- Kräfte = \"forces\"; Fähigkeiten = \"capacities\"; Art = \"kind\"; Veränderungen = \"alterations\"; Folge = \"sequence\"; Wahrscheinlich = \"probable.\"","- Creatur = \"creature\"; Bildung = \"formation\"; Ausübung = \"practice\" (literally \"exercise\"); Verrichtungen = \"tasks.\"","- \"deren 5 Stunden Geschäfte\": the relative \"deren\" refers to the creature; \"whose 5 hours' business\" kept literal.","- \"Sehröhre\" = \"telescopes\" (literally \"seeing-tubes\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0262":["Finally everything agrees in confirming the law cited. Nature has evidently spread out its store most abundantly on the remote side of the world. The moons, which compensate the busy beings of these blessed regions, through a sufficient replacement, for the withdrawal of the daylight, are placed there in the greatest multitude, and nature seems to have been careful to lend all assistance to their efficacy, so that almost no time may be a hindrance to them in applying them. Jupiter has, with regard to moons, an evident advantage over all the lower planets, and Saturn again over him, whose arrangements in the beautiful and useful ring that surrounds him make still greater advantages of his constitution probable; whereas the lower planets, in which this store would have been uselessly squandered, whose class borders far nearer on unreason, have become partakers of such advantages either not at all, or yet very little.",["- Wesen = being; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Vollkommenheit not present here. Wahrscheinlich = probable (wahrscheinlich machen = \"make probable\").","- \"Unvernunft\" rendered \"unreason\" (lit. non-reason); \"grenzet\" = \"borders.\"","- \"ihrer Wirksamkeit … solche anzuwenden\": \"solche\" has no clear antecedent in the German (moons or their efficacy); \"applying them\" preserves this. \"ihnen\" likewise ambiguous (the moons or the beings).","- \"Ersetzung\" = replacement; \"Anstalten\" = arrangements (not in glossary); \"Vorrath\" = store.","- \"Welt\" in \"entlegenen Seite der Welt\" = world."]],"naturgeschichte-0263":["But one can (so that I may anticipate an objection that could frustrate all this agreement cited) regard the greater distance from the sun, this source of light and of life, as an evil, against which the extensiveness of such arrangements in the more distant planets has been provided only in order to remedy it to some extent, so that in fact the upper planets would have a less advantageous position in the structure of the world and a placement that would be detrimental to the perfection of their arrangements, because they receive a weaker influence from the sun. For we know that the effect of light and warmth is determined not through their absolute intensity but through the capacity of the matter with which it receives them, and resists their impulse less or more, and that therefore just the same distance that for a kind of coarse matter can be called a temperate climate, would scatter subtler fluids, and for them be of harmful violence; consequently only a finer material, consisting of more mobile elements, belongs to it, in order to make the distances of Jupiter or Saturn from the sun into a fortunate position for both.",["- Wirkung = effect; Fähigkeit = capacity; Materie = matter; Stoff = material (Stoff and Materie both distinct in the text); bestimmt werde = \"be determined.\"","- Art = kind; Abstand and Entfernung = \"distance\" (two German words, one English word); Weltgebäude = \"structure of the world.\"","- \"ihm\" (einigermassen abzuhelfen) refers to \"Uebel\"; \"solche\" / \"sie\" after \"womit\" are ambiguous in the German (matter or light and warmth); left so in English. \"dazu\" = \"to it,\" antecedent unclear.","- \"beyden\" = \"for both\"; \"vor\" = \"for\" (modern für)."]],"naturgeschichte-0264":["Finally the excellence of the natures in these upper regions of heaven seems still to be bound, through a physical interconnection, with a durability of which it is worthy. Corruption and death cannot do as much to these excellent creatures as to us lowly natures. Just the same inertia of matter and coarseness of material that in the lower steps is the specific *principium* of their abasement is also the cause of that tendency which they have to corruption. If the juices that nourish and make grow the animal or the human being, in that they incorporate themselves between its little fibers and attach to its mass, can no longer at the same time enlarge its vessels and canals in spatial extension, when the growth is already completed; then these attaching juices of nourishment must, through just the mechanical drive that is applied to nourish the animal, narrow and clog the cavity of its vessels, and ruin the structure of the whole machine, in a gradually increasing stiffening. It is to be believed that, although transience gnaws also at the most perfect natures, nevertheless the advantage in the fineness of the material, in the elasticity of the vessels, and the lightness and efficacy of the juices, out of which those more perfect beings, who dwell in the distant planets, may be formed, will hold up far longer this frailty, which is a sequel from the inertia of a coarse matter, and will procure for these creatures a duration, the length of which is proportioned to their perfection, just as the frailty of the life of human beings has a correct relation to their worthlessness.",["- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Folge = sequel; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Stoff = material; Materie = matter; Raumesausdehnung = \"spatial extension\" (Raum + Ausdehnung).","- *principium* is Latin in the original (roman type); Naturen = natures; Wesen = beings; Geschöpfe and Creaturen both = \"creatures.\"","- Semicolon after \"ist\" kept; \"wenn … wenn … so müssen\" structure kept. \"Folge aus\" rendered \"a sequel from.\"","- \"diese Hinfälligkeit … aufhalten\": \"hold up\" for aufhalten; \"Vorzug … werde\" has the German's long subject before the verb."]],"naturgeschichte-0265":["I cannot leave this consideration without anticipating a doubt which could arise naturally from the comparison of these opinions with our previous propositions. We have recognized in the arrangements of the structure of the world, in the multitude of satellites that illuminate the planets of the most distant circles, in the swiftness of the axial rotations, and in the material of their composition proportioned to the effect of the sun, the wisdom of GOD, which has ordered everything so conducively to the advantage of the rational beings who inhabit them. But how would one now reconcile with the doctrinal constitution of the aims a mechanical doctrinal concept, so that what the highest wisdom itself designed has been entrusted for execution to raw matter, and the governance of providence to nature left to itself? Is the former not rather a confession that the ordering of the structure of the world was not developed through the general laws of the latter.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Meinung = opinion; Satz = proposition; Erkenntnis not used: \"erkannt\" = recognized (erkennen); Wesen = beings; Absicht = aim (plural Absichten = aims); Gesetz = law.","- Lehrverfassung and Lehrbegriff rendered literally as \"doctrinal constitution\" and \"doctrinal concept\"; \"Lehrbegriff\" is not in the glossary.","- The final sentence ends with a period though phrased as a question in the German; preserved. \"das erstere\"/\"der letzteren\" referents (the wisdom/the nature) left unresolved.","- \"Vorsehung\" = providence; \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the world\" (cf. Weltgebäude above)."]],"naturgeschichte-0266":["One will soon dispel these doubts if one only thinks back to what has been adduced in the preceding with the same aim. Must not the mechanics of all natural motions have an essential tendency to nothing but such sequences as agree well with the project of the highest reason in the whole domain of combinations? How can it have aberrant strivings and an unbound dispersion in its undertakings, since all its properties, out of which these sequences develop, themselves have their determination from the eternal idea of the divine understanding, in which everything must necessarily be related to one another and fit together? If one reflects properly, how can one justify the way of judging by which one regards nature as a refractory subject, which can be kept in the track of order and of common harmony only through a kind of constraint that sets limits to its free conduct, unless one perhaps holds that it is a self-sufficient *principium*, whose properties recognize no cause, and which GOD, as well as can be done, strives to force into the plan of his aims. The closer one comes to know nature, the more one will see that the general constitutions of things are not alien and separated from one another. One will be sufficiently convinced that they have essential affinities, through which they of themselves prepare to support one another in the establishment of perfect constitutions, the interaction of the elements toward the beauty of the material world and yet at the same time toward the advantages of the world of spirits, and that in general the individual natures of things in the field of eternal truths already constitute among themselves, so to speak, a system, in which one is related to the other; one will also soon become aware that the affinity is proper to them from the community of the origin, from which they have all drawn their essential determinations.",["- Absicht = aim; Folge = sequence (plural Folgen); Umfang = domain; Verbindung = combination; Verstand = understanding; Idee = idea; Bestimmung = determination; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Gemeinschaft = community; Schranke = limit(s) (Schranken, \"limitations\" per glossary; \"limits\" here); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Verfassung = constitution.","- *principium* in roman type in the original; \"Vernunft\" = reason; \"Eigenschaften\" = properties (Beschaffenheit and Eigenschaft both \"property/constitution\" distinction kept: Eigenschaft = property).","- \"Wie kan sie … haben, da …\": \"sie\" (Mechanik) retained as \"it.\" \"Geisterwelt\" = \"world of spirits\"; \"materialischen\" = \"material\" (adjective).","- \"zu urtheilen\" = \"of judging\"; \"erkennen\" in \"keine Ursache erkennen\" = \"recognize.\"","- \"beziehend ist\" rendered \"is related\"; \"Gleise\" = \"track\"; \"Zwang\" = \"constraint\"; \"woferne … nicht\" = \"unless.\"","- Ambiguity kept: \"die Wechselwirkung der Elemente zur Schönheit…\" stands in apposition/parallel to \"Errichtung\" in the German; the English leaves it likewise loosely attached.","- Final question mark after \"Folge … ? \" not supplied where the German has a period (0265) or lacks it (0266 \"kan sie…\"): punctuation follows the German as printed, with \"zusammenstimmet ?\" corrected to \"?\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0267":["And so, in order to apply this repeated consideration to the intended purpose: The very same general laws of motion that have assigned to the uppermost planets a distant place from the center of attraction and of inertia in the world-system have thereby at the same time set them into the most advantageous constitution to undertake their formations farthest from the point of reference of coarse matter, and indeed with greater freedom; but they have also at the same time put them into a regular relation to the influence of warmth, which spreads itself, according to the same law, from the very same center. Since now it is precisely these determinations that have made the formation of the world-bodies in these distant regions less hindered, the generation of the motions depending on it quicker and, to say it briefly, the system more seemly, since finally the spiritual beings have a necessary dependence on matter, to which they are personally bound; so it is no wonder that the perfection of nature in both kinds of places has been effected in a single interconnection of causes, and from the same grounds. This agreement is therefore, on close consideration, nothing sudden or unexpected, and because the latter beings have been interwoven into the general constitution of material nature through the same *principium*; so the world of spirits in the distant spheres will be more perfect from the very same causes on account of which the corporeal one is.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0268":["So everything in the whole domain of nature hangs together in an uninterrupted succession of degrees, through the eternal harmony that makes all members refer to one another. The perfections of GOD have revealed themselves distinctly in our steps, and are no less glorious in the lowest classes than in the more sublime.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0269":["What a chain that takes its beginning from GOD, what natures Of heavenly and earthly, of angels, humans down to cattle, From seraph to worm. O breadth that the eye can never Reach and consider! From the infinite to you, from you to nothing!",[]],"naturgeschichte-0270":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0271":["We have faithfully carried forward the conjectures so far along the guiding thread of physical relations, which has kept them on the path of a reasonable credibility. Shall we allow ourselves still a digression from this track into the field of fantasy? Who shows us the boundary where well-grounded probability ceases, and arbitrary fictions begin? Who is so bold as to venture an answer to the question: whether sin exercises its dominion also in the other globes of the world-edifice, or whether virtue alone has set up its regiment there.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0272":["The stars are perhaps a seat of transfigured spirits, As vice rules here, there virtue is master.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0273":["v. Haller.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0274":["Does not a certain middle condition between wisdom and unreason belong to the unhappy capacity of being able to sin? Who knows, then, whether the inhabitants of those distant world-bodies are not too sublime and too wise to lower themselves to the folly that lies in sin, whereas those who dwell in the lower planets are too firmly attached to matter and provided with far too slight capacities of the mind to be allowed to bear the responsibility for their actions before the judgment seat of justice? In this way the earth, and perhaps Mars as well, (so that the wretched consolation of having companions in misfortune may not be taken from us,) would alone be in the dangerous middle road, where the temptations of sensible charms have a strong faculty for seduction against the supremacy of the mind, but the latter nevertheless cannot disown that capacity by which it is in a position to offer resistance to them, unless it pleased its sluggishness rather to let itself be carried away by them, where there is therefore the dangerous intermediate point between weakness and faculty, since the very same advantages that raise it above the lower classes place it on a height from which it can sink again infinitely deeper below these. In fact the two planets, the earth and Mars, are the middle members of the planetary system, and of their inhabitants a middle state of physical as well as moral constitution between the two end points can perhaps be conjectured not without probability; but I would rather leave this consideration to those who find in themselves more reassurance in an unprovable cognition, and more inclination to take on the responsibility for it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0275":["Conclusion.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0276":["It is not even properly known to us what the human being now actually is, even though consciousness and the senses should teach us about this; how much less will we be able to guess what he is someday to become. Nevertheless the desire for knowledge of the human soul snaps very eagerly at this object so distant from it, and strives to get some light in such obscure cognition.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0277":["Should the immortal soul, in the whole infinity of its future duration, which the grave itself does not interrupt but only alters, remain always attached to this point of cosmic space, to our earth? Should it never become a partaker of a closer intuition of the remaining wonders of creation? Who knows, is it not intended for it that it shall someday get to know from close up those distant globes of the world-structure, and the excellence of their arrangements, which already from afar so excite its curiosity? Perhaps for that reason some globes of the planetary system are still forming, in order, after the completed run of the time prescribed for our sojourn here, to prepare for us new dwelling places in other heavens. Who knows, do not those satellites run around Jupiter in order someday to shine for us?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0278":["It is permitted, it is seemly, to amuse oneself with such representations; but no one will found the hope of the future on such uncertain pictures of the imagination. After vanity will have claimed back its share in human nature: so the immortal mind, with a swift swing, will swing itself up above everything that is finite, and continue its existence in a new relation to the whole of nature, which springs from a closer combination with the highest being. Henceforth this elevated nature, which has the source of happiness in itself, will no longer scatter itself among the outer objects in order to seek a reassurance in them. The entire sum total of creatures, which has a necessary agreement with the good pleasure of the highest original being, must have it with its own as well, and will not touch it otherwise than with perpetual contentment.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0279":["In fact, when one has filled one's mind with such considerations, and with the preceding ones; so the sight of a starry heaven, on a clear night, gives a kind of pleasure that only noble souls feel. In the general stillness of nature and the repose of the senses, the hidden faculty of cognition of the immortal mind speaks an unnameable language, and gives undeveloped concepts, which can well be felt but not described. If there are among the thinking creatures of this planet base beings who, notwithstanding all the charms with which so great an object can lure them, are nevertheless in a position to attach themselves firmly to the servitude of vanity: how unfortunate is this globe, that it has been able to raise such wretched creatures? But how fortunate it is, on the other side, since under the most acceptable conditions a way is opened to it to attain to a happiness and loftiness that is infinitely far elevated above the advantages that the most advantageous arrangement of nature can reach in all world-bodies.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0280":["End.",[]],"de-igne-0001":["brief outline\nof certain meditations\non fire\nwhich\nfor the sake of a specimen\nmost humbly offers\nto the most ample philosophical faculty\nthat it may be kindly admitted to examination\nImmanuel Kant,\ndevotee of the philosophical sciences, of Royal Prussian Königsberg,\nat Königsberg, on the 17th day of April in the year 1755.",[]],"de-igne-0002":["the plan of the undertaking.",["- 0267: *Principium* is Kant's own Latin in the German text. *Verfassung* rendered \"constitution\" (also in 0278's \"Verfassung\" nowhere else); *Beschaffenheit* in 0274 is \"constitution\" too, so one English word stands for two German words. *Verhältniß* = \"relation\"; *Bestimmungen* = \"determinations\".","- 0267: \"weswegen es die körperliche ist\" is elliptical (\"on account of which the corporeal one is [so]\"); kept elliptical. \"so\" after the semicolons (*so ist kein Wunder*, *so wird*) is kept as in the German, which leaves the clause-linking as awkward as the original.","- 0267: \"Weltkörper\" = \"world-bodies\"; \"Weltsystem\" = \"world-system\"; *Weltbau(es)* = \"world-edifice\", *Weltgebäude* = \"world-structure\", *Weltraum* = \"cosmic space\".","- 0269/0272: verse lines run together in the source (OCR or layout); line breaks not restored. \"Vieh\" rendered \"cattle\"; \"Naturen\" rendered \"natures\".","- 0274: \"Vermögen\" appears as \"faculty\" in \"ein starkes Vermögen zur Verleitung\" and in \"zwischen der Schwachheit und dem Vermögen\" (second perhaps \"capacity/ability\"); *Fähigkeit* = \"capacity\". \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"cognition\". \"sich ... zu erhaben\" etc. kept literal. \"dieser\" and \"diese(n)\" refer ambiguously (mind / temptations / classes) as in German; \"the latter\" for *dieser* follows the nearest antecedent \"Geist\".","- 0274: \"Mittelstand\"/\"mittlerer Stand\" rendered \"middle condition\"/\"middle state\"; *Zustand* is not used here.","- 0276: *Bewustseyn* = \"consciousness\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\"; *Wißbegierde* = \"desire for knowledge\" (not in glossary).","- 0277: *Anschauen* (nominalized verb) rendered \"intuition\"; *kennen lernen* = \"get to know\" (cf. *kennen*).","- 0278: *Vorstellungen* = \"representations\"; *Einbildungskraft* = \"imagination\"; *Verbindung* = \"combination\"; *Innbegriff* = \"sum total\"; *Daseyn* = \"existence\"; *Wesen* = \"being\"; *Gegenständen* = \"objects\".","- 0279: *Gemüth* = \"mind\", as is *Geist* (\"mind\" in 0278/0279, \"spirit\" in *Geisterwelt* rendered \"world of spirits\"); so \"mind\" stands for two German words. *Begriffe* = \"concepts\"; *Bedingungen* = \"conditions\".","- de-igne-0001: Latin title page rendered into English as it stands, with no Latin technical terms retained; \"reg. bor.\" expanded as \"of Royal Prussian Königsberg\" (abbreviation of *Regiomontanus Borussus*; doubtful how to attach to \"cultor\" — it modifies Kant, not the sciences). Line breaks in the source are not reproduced."]],"de-igne-0003":["My mind is not so disposed as to conclude in a few little pages a matter which bestows the amplest material upon a prolix volume. These meditations, which I here offer, condensed, to the benevolent examination of the most ample Philosophical Faculty, as it were in a medley, are nothing but, as it were, the first lines of a Theory, which, if leisure should permit, will furnish me the crop of a more copious treatment. Everywhere I have carefully taken care not to indulge, more freely than is usual, a hypothetical and arbitrary manner of demonstrating, having followed as diligently as I could the thread of experience and of geometry, without which an exit from the recesses of nature is scarcely found. Since therefore the force of fire is exerted chiefly in rarefying bodies and in dissolving their *nexus*, in order that I might proceed by way and reason, I thought it would not be foreign to discuss beforehand a few things about the cohesion of matter and the nature of fluids.",["- *veluti per saturam*: rendered \"as it were in a medley\"; \"per saturam\" is an idiom, kept close to the Latin.","- *nexus* (Zusammenhang = interconnection; nexus): left in Latin, italicized; \"via ac ratione\" rendered \"by way and reason\" (an idiomatic phrase, kept literal).","- \"Theoriae\" is capitalized in the source; \"Physicorum\" (below) likewise."]],"de-igne-0004":["Section I.\nOn the nature of hard bodies and fluids.",[]],"de-igne-0005":["Prop. I.",[]],"de-igne-0006":["The fluidity of bodies cannot be explained from a division of matter into the finest, smooth and most lightly cohering parts, as the greatest part of the Physicists think, according to the opinion of Descartes.",["- *Fluiditas* = fluidity; *divisio* = division (Einteilung/Teilung); *Physicorum* = \"Physicists\"."]],"de-igne-0007":["Let the triangle A B C represent the section of a conical heap of the minutest globular particles; I say that this heap, under the alleged conditions, will not compose its surface level, as must happen in fluids. For since the particles c, e, g, d, f, i, resting upon the underlying a, m, n, h, each rests between the embraces of these, and are not dislodged from position except insofar as they drive the lower ones rightward and leftward from place, but the force v a, with which the particle pressing from above by gravity drives the particle a rightward, from the composition of forces is only half of the gravity c o, and so through the whole heaping together: it is evident that the heap on a plane, if only some force resists the extreme corpuscles, a and z, will obtain not a horizontal but a conical figure, just as the finest sand in sandglasses, or any other matter ground into the finest dust.",["- *ad libellam* rendered \"level\". *vis* = force; *compositio virium* = \"composition of forces\"; *gravitas* = \"gravity\".","- The clause \"quemadmodum in fluidis accidere necesse est\" is kept in the Latin word order as far as possible; the long \"cum ... : patet\" sentence is kept as one.","- The letters and \"v a\", \"c o\" are as printed in the source; some may be OCR-uncertain."]],"de-igne-0008":["Prop. II.",[]],"de-igne-0009":["The heaping together of particles however subtle and most lightly cohering nonetheless does not satisfy the static law, by exerting a pressure toward the sides proportional to height, and thus lacks the principal character of fluidity, unless they press one another by means of some elastic matter, by whose aid they can communicate the moment of their weight equally in every direction.",["- *momentum ponderis* = \"moment of weight\"; *quaquaversum* = \"in every direction\"; *materia elastica* = \"elastic matter\".","- \"semet mediante materia quadam elastica premant\": \"they press one another\" renders the reflexive \"semet\"."]],"de-igne-0010":["For since it is evident from the preceding proposition that heaped-together particles pressing one another immediately do not exert toward the sides a pressure proportional to height, some other matter must intervene between the elementary parts of the fluid, by means of which they can distribute the moment of weight equally in every direction. But since such a matter, which, pressed somewhere, strives to expand itself elsewhere with the same force, is commonly called elastic: it is necessary that the solid molecules of fluids rest not immediately on one another, but on some elastic matter intermixed with them, by whose aid whatever of forces presses from above acts toward the sides with the same quantity.",["- *immediate* = \"immediately\"; *vis* (pl. *vires*) = \"force(s)\"; \"quicquid ... virium\" kept literal as \"whatever of forces\"."]],"de-igne-0011":["It will soon have to be proved that this elastic matter, intervening between the elements of the fluid body, is nothing other than the matter of heat.",["- *materia caloris* = \"matter of heat\"."]],"de-igne-0012":["Prop. III.",[]],"de-igne-0013":["Hard bodies, no otherwise than fluid ones, are contained by molecules cohering not by immediate contact, but with elastic matter equally mediating.",["- Ambiguity kept: \"moleculis ... cohaerentibus\" (ablative) can be read as \"contained by molecules that cohere\", with \"materia ... mediante\" an ablative absolute.","- *contactus* = \"contact\"."]],"de-igne-0014":["Fluid bodies, as demonstrated above, cohere by means of some elastic matter. But since the metals that have hardened from fluids, and other bodies of this kind, always, in proportion to the diminished degree of heat, occupy a volume ever more tightly and are condensed according to all dimensions, and thus there is no lack of space for their elements always to approach one another more closely, hence they are not compacted by immediate contact: it is evident that also the masses of hard bodies contain some matter intermixed within their parts, by means of which the solid molecules, though removed from mutual contact, nevertheless attract one another, or, if you prefer, cohere, and thus in this manner agree with fluids.",["- *gradus caloris* = \"degree of heat\"; *volumen* = \"volume\"; *attrahere* = \"attract\"; *cohaerere* = \"cohere\".","- \"hinc non immediato contactu compacta sint\" is kept in the subjunctive-clause position as in the Latin."]],"de-igne-0015":["Prop. IV.",[]],"de-igne-0016":["To explain the phenomena of hard bodies by the aid of the matter already mentioned, by means of which the elements of a body, however removed from mutual contact, nevertheless attract one another.",["- The infinitive \"explicare\" stands without a governing verb in the source, as in the Latin heading.","- *phaenomena* = \"phenomena\"."]],"de-igne-0017":["Hard bodies, especially those that have hardened from fluids, such as metals, glass, etc., have this peculiar and most noteworthy thing, that with a weight hung on they are extended somewhat without rupture, and thus, to what weight they yield in the closest union of parts, this, when these have been moved somewhat apart from one another, they can bear, and in the greatest degree of extension are also fit for bearing the greatest weight. But this phenomenon, I contend, cannot be explained from solid particles cohering immediately. For if a metallic thread consists of particles either united according to scheme 1, or arranged, so as to exclude empty interstices as far as can be done, according to fig. 2, or as parallelepipeds so touching with little surfaces in fig. 3 that by the weight hung on they are moved from contact through the small spaces a, o, i, e, etc., and yet cohere by the remaining surfaces: yet it immediately appears that, if the hung weight extends such a metallic thread in length even a little, in figure 1 the parts will at once be torn apart, since they no longer touch one another; and, if you demand that the parts placed at the sides, a, b, c, d, with extension in length made, yield inward and go to prevent the rupture, yet, the thickness in this way somewhat diminished, they can then resist much less the weight to which they previously yielded; in fig. 3, indeed, that the particles which touched with their whole surfaces, since they touch one another only in some part, will be wholly separated by the weight, is beyond doubt. And so in every assignable case the thread will not suffer itself to be stretched unless it also be ruptured at the same time. Since this is contrary to experience, it is evident that the elements of hard bodies attract one another not by immediate contact, but by means of some matter, even at a definite distance.",["- Doubtful reading: \"in definita etiam distantia\" is translated as printed (\"at a definite distance\"); the text may intend or have been corrupted from \"indefinita\" (\"indefinite\"). Not emended.","- *extensio* = extension; *gradus* = degree; *pondus* = weight; *ruptio / diruptio* = \"rupture\" for both.","- \"diruptionem impeditum ire\" rendered literally as \"go to prevent the rupture\" (supine after *ire*).","- The sentence \"in fig. 3 vero ... extra dubitationem est\" is kept in the Latin order, with its subject clause preceding \"is beyond doubt.\""]],"de-igne-0018":["And so from this hypothesis of mine I shall make an attempt to explain this phenomenon of hard bodies according to the observed laws of nature and the precepts of geometry. For if I posit that a body hardening from a fluid acquires such a situation of the elements that, with the elastic matter intervening, removed somewhat from mutual contact, three always make an equilateral triangle, as figure 4 exhibits, (but they will always aim at such a situation, if by attracting they contract themselves into the least space,) it is necessary that, if the hung weight draws this system of particles according to the direction a d, the distance of the corpuscles a and c become greater, as fig. 5 exhibits, but the distance a b and b c remain equal to the prior one, since the element b approaches the point d, so that with the two, a and c, it encloses an angle greater than the prior one in fig. 4. But with the density of the intermixed elastic matter remaining in this way unimpaired, (on account of the volume of the extended body not properly increased,) the attractions, or, if you prefer, cohesions, of the particles a and c will not be diminished by this bond. But the attraction of the particle b, insofar as it joins the elements a and c, with extension or drawing apart of the particles a and c made, becomes proportional to the line a d in fig. 5, whereas before, on account of the lesser angle b in fig. 4, it was less; and thus the force by which the particles, with some extension made, are held back from rupture grows, and indeed in the direct ratio of the line a d, that is, according to the quantity of extension.",["- \"s.\" (sive) rendered \"or\"; *attractio* = \"attraction\"; *cohaesio* = \"cohesion\"; *vinculum* = \"bond\"; *densitas* = \"density\".","- *affectabunt* rendered \"will aim at\"; *quantitas extensionis* = \"quantity of extension\".","- \"hoc vinculo\" is ambiguous between \"by this bond\" (instrumental) and a looser \"in this tie\"; \"by this bond\" kept.","- \"Verum\" rendered \"But\"; \"priori ... prior one\": the Latin has only \"priori\" (dative/ablative), no noun supplied."]],"de-igne-0019":["Prop. V.",[]],"de-igne-0020":["The law according to which springs are found to be compressed into spaces proportional to the forces conspires best with our alleged hypothesis.",["- *elastra* rendered \"springs\" (elastic bodies); *lex* = \"law\"; *vires* = \"forces\"."]],"de-igne-0021":["What in hard bodies are commonly called compressions are to be named more truly by the name of dilation or extension; since that hard materials can be driven by compressing force into tighter spaces much less than water is evident of itself. Let therefore the spring f e c b (fig. 1), firmly inserted in the wall a b at f b, be pressed toward the wall so that its situation is i x f b: first I contend that the external margin of the spring, b c, is in this way somewhat extended and requires in this state a greater pressing force the more it is extended; then that the forces by which the spring is moved toward the stop a b through some space will be, from our principles, as these spaces, as long as the pressures are moderate.",["- The passage ends at \"mediocres\" without further construction; the final clause depends on \"contendo\" and \"fore ut haec spatia\" is kept as printed (\"will be ... as these spaces\").","- *compressio* = \"compression\"; *dilatatio* = \"dilation\"; *extensio* = \"extension\"; *retinaculum* = \"stop\" (literally, \"holder\")."]],"de-igne-0022":["If therefore the spring, by some pressing force, has been reduced into situation 2 and moved closer to the wall through the space c s, the section e c will be changed into situation i x. Let a line i s be drawn through the thickness, parallel to the section e c, i f = s o = c m will be, and x o, by the part x s, made longer than the margin c m by extension; further, if you continue to press, until the spring has been reduced into situation 3, g k f b, g h having been drawn, likewise parallel to e c, the quantity of extension k h will be greater than the quantity x s; hence from what was demonstrated above it is evident how in this way situation 3 requires a greater pressing force than situation 2.",["- \"Ducatur ... linea i s ... erit i f = s o = c m\": the Latin runs the imperative and the future together without a conjunction; kept so.","- \"x o parte x s margine c m longior extensione factus\": the ablatives \"parte x s\" and \"extensione\" kept in order; the lettering (i x, x o, k h) follows the source and may be OCR-uncertain.","- *situs* = \"situation\"; *crassities* = \"thickness\"."]],"de-igne-0023":["But now it must be investigated, in what ratio the forces stand compared with the spaces of compression. The margin x b in position 2, however slightly curved, can nevertheless in the case of moderate compressions be held to be straight, likewise the line k b in position 3; let it be posited further that the horizontal section of the elastic body e c no. 1, continued, passes through the points i and g, which, since it happens as nearly as possible in a moderate degree of compression, can be assumed here without error. There is therefore in the triangle i x s the angle x = the angle c, since the section of the elastic body is the same as no. 1, the angle s is equal to its vertical angle v, and therefore the triangles s c b and i x s are similar. Likewise in the triangle g k h no. 3 everything stands in the same ratio with the triangle h c b, and therefore the following argument results:",["- *elastrum* rendered \"elastic body\"; the Latin is a neuter noun for a spring or elastic lamina, and the English choice is a stopgap, not a glossary term.","- *vires* = \"forces\" (Kraft/vis); *positus* (*situs*) = \"position\"; *sectio* = \"section\".","- \"Angulus verticalis\" rendered \"vertical angle\" (i.e. the angle named v); the letters refer to a figure not given here.","- The formula announced by \"argumentatio sequens\" is not in the passage as supplied; it is probably lost (the next passage begins \"hoc est\").","- \"quam proxime\" rendered \"as nearly as possible\"; the subject of \"accidit\" is left as in the Latin (the passing of the section through i and g)."]],"de-igne-0024":["that is: the quantities x s and k h, by which the outermost margin b c of the elastic body is stretched, are in the ratio of the spaces of compression s c and h c.",["- *elastri* rendered \"of the elastic body\"; *margo extimus* = \"outermost margin\".","- *distenditur* = \"is stretched\"; *distensio* later = \"stretching\"."]],"de-igne-0025":["But since it is established from prop. IV that, according to our hypothesis, the stretching forces must be proportional to the quantity of stretching, in this case it is evident that the forces compressing the elastic body will be proportional to the space of compression.",["- *hypothesin* = \"hypothesis\"; *spatio compressionis* = \"space of compression\" (*Raum* in the glossary, here Latin *spatium*).","- \"e prop. IV. constet\" rendered \"it is established from prop. IV\"."]],"de-igne-0026":["These assertions of ours are excellently confirmed by what de la Hire disclosed in the Monum. R. A. Sc. Paris. of the year 1705 concerning the compression of elastic bodies; if you will have examined the matter carefully, it could hardly be explained so aptly and consistently by any other hypothesis whatever.",["- \"Monum. R. A. Sc. Paris.\" left as abbreviated in the source.","- *asserta* rendered \"assertions\" (cf. *behaupten* = assert); the final clause \"explicanda\" is kept dependent as in the Latin, with the subject (*rem*) understood from the preceding clause."]],"de-igne-0027":["General corollary.",["- *Corollarium* rendered \"corollary\"."]],"de-igne-0028":["Every body therefore, if I judge rightly, is contained in solid parts, united by some elastic matter intervening as by a bond. The elementary particles, with this mixed in between, although removed from mutual contact, nevertheless attract themselves by its aid and are certainly bound together more tightly than could happen through immediate contact. For the contact of the molecules, for the most part globular, since it would hardly occur at a point, would be infinitely weaker than the cohesion that is provided over the whole surface. In this way, however, the position of the elements can be changed with cohesion preserved, and at the same time it is at hand how, with that uniting matter partly withdrawn from the interstices, the elements can approach nearer to one another and contract the volume; conversely, with its quantity or also its elasticity increased, the body can grow in volume and the particles can recede from one another without loss of cohesion. Which things are of the greatest moment in the Theory of fire.",["- *materia* = \"matter\" (Materie); *cohaesio* = \"cohesion\"; *corpus* = \"body\"; *volumen* = \"volume\".","- *sentio* rendered \"I judge\" in \"si recte sentio\" (literally \"if I rightly perceive/think\"); *artcius* read as *artius*.","- *contactus … foret … cohaesione*: the comparison is kept in the Latin order; \"quae … praestatur\" has *cohaesione* as its antecedent.","- *Quae … sunt* kept as a relative-clause fragment."]],"de-igne-0029":["section ii.\nOn the matter of fire and its modifications, heat and cold.",["- *modificationibus* = \"modifications\"; *calore et frigore* = \"heat and cold\"."]],"de-igne-0030":["prop. vi.",["- *prop.* = *propositio*, rendered by the abbreviation."]],"de-igne-0031":["Experience.",["- *Experientia* = \"experience\" (Erfahrung in the glossary)."]],"de-igne-0032":["Fire attests its presence first by rarefying all bodies, fluid as well as solid, according to all dimensions, then, with cohesion gradually weakened, by dissolving the structure of bodies, finally by dissipating the parts into vapors. Cold, on the contrary, diminishes the volume of bodies, strengthens cohesion, makes rigid out of the ductile and flexible, and out of the fluid, consistent. Heat is excited especially in hard and resisting bodies either by friction or by concussion. In no body can it grow without limit. A body growing hot by seething never goes beyond the degree of ebullition, although one igniting by burning generally attains a greater heat.",["- *ignis* = \"fire\"; *calor* = \"heat\"; *frigus* = \"cold\"; *gradus* = \"degree\".","- *consistentia* rendered \"consistent\" (as a neuter plural adjective-substantive in \"e fluidis consistentia\"); the Latin may also be read as \"firm things\".","- \"in immensum\" = \"without limit\" (literally \"to the immense\")."]],"de-igne-0033":["The other phenomena of heat most worthy of note I forbear to cite here, since they will be met with here and there in what follows.",["- *phaenomena* = \"phenomena\"; *supersedeo* rendered \"I forbear\"."]],"de-igne-0034":["prop. vii.",[]],"de-igne-0035":["The matter of fire is nothing but (described in the preceding section) elastic matter, which binds together the elements of any bodies whatever, with which it is mixed; and its undulatory or vibratory motion is the same as what comes under the name of heat.",["- *s.* = *seu*, rendered \"or\".","- The parenthesis \"(sectione praecedenti descripta)\" is kept in its position in the Latin, where it may attach to *materia elastica* or *materia ignis*."]],"de-igne-0036":["Experience shows (prop. VI) that any body, either rubbed or shaken, grows hot and is equally rarefied according to all dimensions. But since this argues the presence of something elastic contained within the mass of the body and striving to expand itself upon being stimulated, since, moreover, any body, from what has been demonstrated in sect. I, holds an elastic matter enclosed in its interstices, which serves for the nexus of the particles, and which can therefore be agitated into undulatory motion and exhibit all the phenomena of heat, it is evident that it does not differ from the matter of fire.",["- *nexus* kept as \"nexus\" (Zusammenhang in the glossary).","- *sollicitationibus* rendered \"upon being stimulated\" (literally \"by stimulations\").","- *eam* in the last clause refers back to the elastic matter."]],"de-igne-0037":["To prove the same from the phenomena of ebullition.",["- *ebullitio* = \"ebullition\"."]],"de-igne-0038":["Bodies liquefied by heat, when with more and more fire applied they have been brought to ebullition, are capable of no further degree of heat, and in this state they emit large and elastic bubbles, so that they are equal to bearing the weight of the atmosphere, and indeed unceasingly, as long as the fire presses. Since these bubbles contain nothing of elastic air, and no other matter than that of fire enters into the body saturated with heat, the question occurs why, since before ebullition heat likewise entered into the water and then, apart from some small aerial bubbles, did not manifest itself as elastic, it emits that elastic thing at precisely the moment of ebullition. But since it is easy to see that the same elastic matter, which we call fire, which before as now was received within the mass of the heating fluid, remained held and compressed by the attraction of the particles, although it had dilated the volume somewhat, as long as its quantity, joined with the vehemence of undulation, had not yet become greater than the attraction of the molecules, but when it has so prevailed that it now surpasses the moment of this by its elastic force, all the igneous matter, which approaches anew, passes through the fluid medium with free elasticity, as it entered, since this, I say, the compression of the igneous matter within any hot body is evident: there is no reason for us to doubt the truth of our proposition.",["- *quaestio occurrit* rendered \"the question occurs\"; *cur … emittat* is the indirect question, kept as one sentence with the long parenthetical *cum … cum …* chain.","- *momentum* rendered \"moment\" in \"hujus momentum\" (of the attraction), and \"in momento\" = \"at the moment\"; both are the same Latin word.","- \"cum haec, inquam, … compressio pateat\" resumes the earlier *cum* clause; the construction is kept as in the Latin, with *haec* referring to *compressio*, which comes later.","- *aëris elastici* = \"of elastic air\"."]],"de-igne-0039":["prop. viii.",[]],"de-igne-0040":["The matter of heat is nothing but the aether itself (or matter of light) compressed, by the powerful force of attraction (or adhesion) of bodies, within their interstices.",["- *aether* kept as \"aether\"; *lucis materia* = \"matter of light\".","- \"ipsorum\" rendered \"their\", referring to bodies."]],"de-igne-0041":["For first, any denser bodies attract light immensely, as Newton establishes from the phenomena of refraction and reflection, so far that, by the computation of the incomparable man, near contact the force of attraction exceeds the stimulation of gravity by ten thousand times two million. But since the matter of light is elastic, it is not to be doubted that by so immense a force it can also be brought into somewhat smaller spaces, i.e. compressed; and since the particles of bodies find the matter of light everywhere present, what is there to make you doubt that that very elastic matter, which we have proved in them, does not differ from this aether.",["- *decies millies bimillionesimis vicibus* rendered literally as \"ten thousand times two million times\"; the Latin numeral formation is odd and I have not resolved it. The sense of the product is as printed in the translation, with \"times\" understood.","- *sollicitationem gravitatis* = \"stimulation of gravity\"; *vis attractionis* = \"force of attraction\".","- *quid est, quod ambigas* rendered as a question without a question mark, as in the Latin."]],"de-igne-0042":["Second, it is observed that the same materials, which are strong in notable efficacy for refracting light, are also more capable of conceiving a greater heat when fire is applied, so that from this they disclose that the same attraction, which strives to unite light to itself, also holds the igneous matter intimately united to itself. For oils, which, from the experiments of Newton and others, refract, i.e. attract, the rays of light with a much greater force than in proportion to their specific gravity, also receive a far greater degree of ebullition than in proportion to their specific gravity, like oil of Thereb. etc., but the same oils are also the proper nourishments of flames, and in this state, since they scatter light in all directions, they testify that the matter of heat and of light agree as nearly as possible or rather do not differ at all.",["- *Thereb.* = *Terebinthinae* (turpentine), left abbreviated as in the source.","- *gravitas specifica* = \"specific gravity\"; *refringere* = \"refract\".","- *quantum fieri potest proxime* rendered \"as nearly as possible\"."]],"de-igne-0043":["The same becomes probable from the transparency of glasses.",["- *vitrum* = \"glass\"; *transparentia* = \"transparency\"."]],"de-igne-0044":["If you adopt the hypothesis most in agreement with the laws of nature and recently fortified with new support by the most illustrious Euler, namely that light is not an effluvium of luminous bodies but a pressure, propagated, of aether dispersed everywhere, and have weighed the origin of the transparency of glass, you will openly confess the union, or rather the identity, of aether with the matter of fire. For glass is composed of potash, i.e. a most strong alkaline salt, with sand fused by the force of fire. But since the ashy salt, by long and vehement roasting, cherishes the matter of fire abundantly united to itself, when it is mixed with the sand it will distribute throughout the whole mass of the glass this elastic principle of fire, and since it is not probable that such a body, solidifying out of the fluid, however you turn it, has always open and rectilinear passages for transmitting light, but it is rather consonant with reason that its volume is filled with its own matter, it is evident, because the impulse of light is nevertheless propagated through the mass of glass, that the matter of light itself is mixed with its parts and is a part of its mass. But since we have seen that the matter of fire makes up a not negligible part of glass and is largely distributed through its solid elements, there would hardly be room for doubt that the matter of heat is plainly the same as the aether or element of light.",["- *cineribus clavellatis* rendered \"potash\" (literally \"ashes of the little stick/bundle\", i.e. clavellated ashes).","- *pressionem aetheris … propagatam*: \"propagatam\" is kept in its position and can modify *pressionem* (as rendered) or, more loosely, the whole phrase.","- *sit … locus* is rendered in the conditional (\"there would hardly be room\"), a subjunctive left as in the Latin.","- *quia nihilo secius … propagatur* kept as a parenthetical causal clause inside \"patet … intermistam esse\"."]],"de-igne-0045":["prop. ix.",[]],"de-igne-0046":["To measure the degree of heat, i.e. to express in numbers the proportion which different degrees of heat hold toward one another.",["- The proposition is an infinitive-phrase heading (a problem, *Aufgabe*), translated as it stands; no verb of \"to be required\" is supplied.","- *obtinent* rendered \"hold\"; *numeri* = \"numbers\" (*Zahl*)."]],"de-igne-0047":["Amontons, most celebrated member of the Ac. R. Sc. Paris., was indeed the first to discover the resolution of this problem in this way. Since the force of fire is exerted properly in rarefying bodies, it will be fitting to measure its quantity through the compressing force opposed to this endeavor of rarefaction. But because air, with the heat diminished however much, is found to yield to a pressing force and to be diminished in volume, to such a degree that it must rightly be thought to owe all its elasticity to heat alone, the distinguished man, relying on this hypothesis, undertook the plan of measuring the degrees of heat by the elastic force of air exposed to this heat, i.e. by the weight which, driven by this heat under the same volume, it is able to bear.",["- vis = force; gradus = degrees; \"Ac. R. Sc. Paris.\" left abbreviated as in the source.","- \"imminuto quantumvis calore\": the ablative absolute is kept as it stands; quantumvis could be read as \"however much\" or \"to any degree whatever.\""]],"de-igne-0048":["note.",["- *nota*: used here as a heading; the same word elsewhere in this set (0057) is rendered \"mark\" (*nota*)."]],"de-igne-0049":["Fahrenheit, Boerhaave reporting, was the first to notice the singular nature of liquids boiling by fire, namely that this degree of heat is more intense with a heavier weight of the atmosphere, and with a lesser pressure of the air at the point of boiling has a lesser degree of heat. The same Monnier, from the report of the Paris Acad., with the Réaumur thermometer first at Bordeaux, then on the summit of the mountain Pic du Midi, where the barometer was 8 inches lower than it was at the first place, investigating the heat of boiling water and its height above the freezing point, found. He indeed observed the same degree of ice in both places, but the heat of boiling to fall short by 15⁄180 of the interval by which boiling exceeds freezing, from that which he noted at Bordeaux with the barometer 28 inches high, and thus the heat of boiling of this place to surpass the mountain one by a 1⁄12 part of itself, which excess an excess of about a third part of the atmospheric weight produced; from which it is clear that the weight of the whole atmosphere, removed from boiling water, takes away ¼ of that heat which lies between the degrees of freezing and boiling. Since therefore for water boiling without the pressure of the air a lesser degree of heat, but with its weight added a greater, can be procured, and the weight of the atmosphere does nothing else except that it exhibits a counterpoise to the undulatory motion of the fiery particles, since the attraction of the elements of the water themselves no longer suffices for restraining it, it can be conjectured from this by what force of elasticity the aether, striving at the point of boiling to extricate itself from the *nexus* of the water, is powerful, and by what attraction of the particles (or, this failing, by an external pressing force) it must necessarily be restrained. For since, according to the praised Amontons, the heats of freezing and boiling differ by scarcely a third part of this, and the fourth part of the heat lying between freezing and boiling requires a force equal to the weight of the whole atmosphere, it follows that the weight of 12 atmospheres is needed for the equilibrium to be furnished to the whole heat in boiling, and thus that the attraction itself of the elements of the water is equivalent to 11 aerial pressures. From which it is permitted to perceive the attraction of the same at the point of freezing, and much more the enormous attraction of metals, for compressing the elastic aether.",["- \"Idem Monnierus ... reperit\": the object of \"reperit\" is the elided accusative-plus-infinitive clause that follows (\"Glaciei ... deprehendit\"); kept as the Latin has it, with \"found\" left standing alone.","- \"hujus loci montanam\": the feminine \"montanam\" does not agree with \"calorem\"; the text is possibly corrupt. Rendered \"the mountain one,\" with the noun left unsupplied. The direction of \"antecellere\" is kept as in the Latin.","- \"parte hujus tertia\": \"hujus\" has no clear antecedent (the heat of boiling? the interval?); left ambiguous.","- *nexus* = Zusammenhang in the glossary; \"aether\" kept as spelled; \"ponderis\" = weight, \"pressio\" = pressure."]],"de-igne-0050":["Secondat, making the same observation, found the rarefaction of water to have been greater on the mentioned mountain, less at Bordeaux, in the ratio of 1⁄24 of the whole volume to 1⁄35, and thus, if a calculation is made, precisely in the reciprocal ratio of the weights of the atmosphere 20 : 28. In this celebrated case, therefore, that most pertinacious resistance of water against all compression, established by experiment by the Cimentine Academy, finds no place.",["- \"Academia Cimentina\" (i.e. the Accademia del Cimento) kept in its Latin form as \"Cimentine Academy.\""]],"de-igne-0051":["prop. x.",["- Abbreviation kept as in the source (*propositio*)."]],"de-igne-0052":["To render the nature and cause of exhalations or vapors explained from the assertions of our theory.",["- \"s.\" (sive) rendered \"or\"; \"assertis\" rendered \"assertions\" (behaupten = assert)."]],"de-igne-0053":["Nature of vapors.",[]],"de-igne-0054":["Exhalations, which are nothing but moist particles torn from the surfaces of fluids and floating in the air, have this peculiar and nearly admirable nature, that, as greatly as the particles of a homogeneous fluid brought to contact eagerly unite with one another and spontaneously melt together into one mass, so greatly, once they have been resolved into the tenuity of vapors and are urged by the due degree of heat, do they shun contact and mutual union, and, to use the Newtonian expression, strongly repel themselves; so that a force immense enough for compressing them and for bringing about union against their will has never been found equal. Thus aqueous vapor, driven somewhat by fire, breaks even the firmest vessels, and all vapors whatever, each according to its own nature, often exert an admirable elasticity.",["- *ingenium* rendered \"nature\" in both occurrences (here and 0049); *natura* is also \"nature.\"","- \"vis ... par\": \"par\" governs the datives \"comprimendis\" and \"conciliando\"; \"never found equal\" leaves the construction as in the Latin."]],"de-igne-0055":["Cause.",[]],"de-igne-0056":["The ground of this phenomenon, as far as I at least am aware, has not yet been sufficiently perceived by physicists. I shall therefore undertake to investigate it.",["- *ratio* rendered \"ground\" (cf. Grund); it could also be \"reason\" or \"account.\""]],"de-igne-0057":["A most thin skin, torn away from the surface of the water, shaped into the form of a little bubble scarcely discernible through a microscope, is the element of aqueous vapor. But what cause underlies, why several such thin little bubbles, if they are urged somewhat more strongly by heat, so greatly shun contact? I shall expound at once. For since, according to the assertions of this theory, water no less than all bodies whatever retains by attraction the elastic matter of the aether compressed within its mass, and indeed it is established from what has been demonstrated that this attraction is defined not by contact alone but by a certain distance, so that the molecules cling constricted to one another at that point of proximity where the attractive force is balanced by the repelling force, proceeding from the undulatory motion of heat, although the attraction truly reaches to a somewhat greater distance: let this distance be expressed by the little line e f no. 1, which must be conceived as quite small, and let the proximity of the united aqueous particles be proportional to the particle e g. Let there be further the parallelepiped a b c d no. 2 of a little portion of water, whose thickness b a is so slight that it equals the little line e f. Since by the suppositions of the theorem the attraction of the aqueous elements does not exert itself beyond the distance b a = e f, if a particle is placed at the point a, it will sense the attractive force of all the elements coordinated through the whole thickness, and thus, as far as can happen by the nature of the fluid, will adhere most tenaciously, nor would it adhere more firmly if you were to add to this little aqueous body still the addition b h i d: but if it is removed by some most minute little space a m, it would be drawn not by the whole little aqueous body but only by the part a n o c, and thus would seek union with a lesser force. Let the parallelepiped no. 2 be transfigured into another much thinner, h k r s no. 3; any aqueous particle brought to the point h will be drawn far more weakly; and since the aether itself enclosed by this skin, the surface thus increased, frees itself for the most part, it is evident that in this state the element u, brought near, will by the reciprocations of heat be driven off from the point h at a far greater distance than had to happen in the prior condition, and the thinner the skin has been, the greater the force with which it will shun contact. Since further the thin skin h k r s in this figure, left to itself, would at once pass into a globular figure and, the thickness thus increased on all sides, would be powerful with the same proximity as before of uniting itself with others, it is necessary, if this mark of vapor is to remain with it, that it be rolled around into the form of a little bubble no. 4, and indeed of so minute a diameter a b and so small a thickness that the distance of the points a and b, placed at the extremities of the diameter, be less than the distance b e, by which these points, the repulsive force of the aether equaling the attractive force, would rest beside each other if it were free for them to dilate. In this state, therefore, the little bubble will aim at expansion, and will be an element of elastic vapor, but the distance c d of two homogeneous little bubbles will always be equal to the diameter a b, as is evident from what has been demonstrated.",["- *nota* = \"mark\" (Merkmal); *causa* = \"cause\"; *figura* = \"figure\"; *propinquitas* = \"proximity\"; *distantia* = \"distance\"; *crassities* = \"thickness.\"","- \"in illo propinquitatis puncto ... profectae\": the participle \"profectae\" (feminine plural) does not agree with \"vi repellenti\" (singular); left as is. Its antecedent is unclear.","- \"aucta adeo superficie\" and \"aucta undique ... crassitie\" are ablative absolutes, kept as such. The latter's subject for \"polleret\" and the construction \"polleret ... se uniendi\" are kept literal.","- \"si ipsis liberum foret\": \"ipsis\" (dative, the points) kept as \"for them.\""]],"de-igne-0058":["prop. xi.",["- Abbreviation kept as in the source."]],"de-igne-0059":["To investigate the nature of air and the cause of the elastic principle in it.",["- *principium* here rendered \"principle\" (Grundsatz in the glossary is not at issue)."]],"de-igne-0060":["Air is an elastic fluid, almost a thousand times lighter than water, whose expansive force is proportional to heat, and whose expansion from the cold of freezing water up to the point of boiling, under the same weight of the atmosphere, is about ⅓ of the volume belonging to it in the latter degree. These phenomena have nothing that cannot also belong to vapors, except this alone, that vapors for the most part are consolidated at the same degree of cold at which air preserves its elasticity unimpaired and display no indication of expansive force. But if you consider that the subtlety of the skin of the vapor is the cause why it can exert notable elasticity even with a lesser degree of heat, it is evident that the force of analogy is not at once to be inconsiderately and rashly abandoned here, but rather a trial is to be made whether, deducing two genera from the same principle, we can dispense with an excessive multiplication of beings. The phenomena, however, that bear a torch before the conjecture are the following.",["- \"competere\" rendered \"belong to\" in both places; \"ipsi competentis\" is kept as \"belonging to it.\"","- *genera* = Gattung (\"genus\"); *entium* rendered \"beings\" (Wesen in the glossary); the Latin is *ens*.","- \"cujus ... expansio est circiter ⅓ voluminis posteriori gradu ipsi competentis\": the antecedent of \"posteriori gradu\" (the latter of the two degrees named) is kept as the Latin has it."]],"de-igne-0061":["All bodies that have coalesced from the apposition of the smallest particles by means of an oily or saline principle, e.g. all plants, the tartar of wine, the animal calculus, and besides very many kinds of salts, especially nitre, emit an immense quantity of elastic air if they are pressed by a strong fire, just as Hales has taught us by wonderful experiments in the *Statica* of plants. This air has been found to be no small part of the solid matter with which it was joined; in the horn of a stag it makes up ⅐, in oak wood almost ⅓, in the tartar of Rhenish wine ⅓, in nitre ⅛, in animal tartar, that is, in the calculus of a human being, more than ½ of the whole mass. It is evident of itself that the air drawn out of these bodies by the force of fire, as long as it was a part of the mass, did not yet have the nature of air, that is, was not a fluid endowed with an elasticity proportional to its density; for, expanded into a greater space by an irrepressible endeavor by the force of even a moderate heat, it would have dissolved the whole structure of the body. And so matter that was not elastic, expelled from the interstices of the body, scarcely made free displays elasticity. Since, however, it is the character of vapors that, where they have been torn away from the mass to which they had been united, they exert elastic force, it must certainly, if not be affirmed with assurance, yet be established with great probability that air is nothing other than that vapor dissolved in bodies, which, after it has been reduced to the highest subtlety, readily yields to any degree of heat and displays a strong elasticity.",[]],"de-igne-0062":["There are, however, not a few things, and not trivial ones, that confirm me in this opinion. For why is air expelled by roasting only from bodies that contain in themselves not a little of oil and thus of acid? Is not acid, by its attraction, the most active and most powerful principle for constraining the ether, as I set before the eyes earlier? Is not this principle the bond and as it were the glue of those concrete bodies? (for it is the true magnet of the ethereal matter that constrains all bodies) and where this acid has been driven with difficulty by an enormous force of fire from its closest union with matter, do you think it must not depart divided into the finest film? And in this way, what is there for you to doubt that it constitutes in such a manner an elastic fluid, mobile to expansion even at the smallest degrees of heat, and, however much the cold is increased (since it never banishes all heat), neither congealing nor to be deprived of elasticity? Therefore the difficulty that presses upon watery vapors, that they coagulate with slight cold, and which was the cause for Hales of offering the expelled air under the name of a matter different in its whole genus from the whole nature of vapors, ceases here altogether. And therefore the opinion presents itself to physicists as most worthy of a more accurate investigation, whether air is nothing but the finest exhalation of the acid disseminated through the whole nature of things, attesting elasticity at any degree of heat however small.",[]],"de-igne-0063":["Certainly, with these things laid down in the place of a foundation, it is easy to see why nitre, roasted by a strong fire, gives back so immense a quantity of elastic air, since the finest acid, divided from the cruder part and reduced to the most tenuous vapor, would be air itself. Likewise it is easy to see why the materials that resist fire most stubbornly bestow and emit the greatest quantity of air, e.g. why the tartar of Rhenish wine gives back more than nitre, since the materials that let go the acid enclosed in their embraces most slowly and with great resistance, from these too this acid is torn away in the form of the finest film, so that it can constitute something elastic so mobile as air is, whereas, on the contrary, from those from which a more abundant vapor is drawn, a cruder one also comes forth, which when cold is increased can afford nothing of elasticity.",[]],"de-igne-0064":["Agreement of barometric observations with the hypothesis.",[]],"de-igne-0065":["From this hypothesis there also becomes clear that character of the air at a greater altitude, scarcely explicable on the common opinion. For Maraldus, Cassinus and others, on the testimony of the *Monum. Ac. R. Sc. Paris.*, found that the Mariotte law concerning the compression of air proportional to the incumbent weight fails at a higher elevation. For they found there a lesser density of the air than should have followed, by that law, when compared with the weight of the lower. From which it is evident that the higher air consists not of particles of the same genus but less compressed, but of elements in themselves specifically lighter, since a greater volume of them under the same compression is required to supply the same weight. Since therefore the nature of the substance of air is so different at different altitudes, as is found nowhere else in elements of the same genus everywhere on earth, it is evident that it is to be held not as a separate genus of element, but as the form in which another element, namely, as I judge, the acid humor, manifests itself; which being posited, it is no wonder if other particles of such a vapor (according to the different thickness of the film) are heavier than others, and the lighter occupy the highest place.",[]],"de-igne-0066":["Prop. xii.",[]],"de-igne-0067":["To render the nature of flame explained from the assertions of our theory.",[]],"de-igne-0068":["1. Nature.",[]],"de-igne-0069":["The nature of flame, singular before the rest of the genus of fires, is this:",[]],"de-igne-0070":["No body burns except on the surface, and the nourishment of flame is oil and thus acid, that most active principle serving the elastic motion.",[]],"de-igne-0071":["Flame is nothing but vapor brought to that degree of fire at which it flashes with vivid light and ceases only from want of nourishment. These things, however, are in flame that make it different from every other genus of fire by the whole heaven. 1) That, although heat induced in any body to be heated is, according to the common law of nature, gradually diminished by communication, flame on the contrary acquires from the smallest principle an incredible force, circumscribed by no limits, provided fuel does not fail. 2) That the fire which can be introduced into some inflammable matter by growing hot, up to boiling, is much lower than that which it exerts by burning up. 3) That it scatters light, whereas besides the metals the other genera of bodies, however much heated, nevertheless remain devoid of light.",[]],"de-igne-0072":["2. Investigation of the cause.",[]],"de-igne-0073":["The ground of these phenomena, if I judge rightly, is this. Flame consists of ignited vapor, and the solid mass of the body is not turned wholly into flame, but properly the surface burns. Since vapor, however, has as much surface as possible and as little resistance as possible for keeping the matter of fire within its embraces, it is apparent that it can not only most easily propagate an undulatory motion conceived from the lightest principle, but also gradually communicate it, however great it is, with equal intensity to other inflammable matter. For although at first glance this phenomenon seems to offend against the first rule of mechanics, that the effect is always equal to the cause, yet if you consider that the first stirring of even the smallest spark to excite flame does nothing other than rouse the smallest particle of inflammable vapor into an undulatory motion of its fiery element; which, lightly restrained, frees itself with great endeavor and performs vibrations, by likewise rousing what is poured around, and propagates the violence of the motion through the whole mass. Nor should you wonder that the effect of a small cause here increases immensely, since the springs of the enclosed ether, freeing themselves from the restraints of attraction, produce in this way effects that do not properly acknowledge the stirring of the kindling little flame as a cause; for they depend properly on the attraction of the oil, whose finest division has made possible for the enclosed matter to extricate itself with great violence. Further, vapor constitutes a fluid, more efficacious in undulating on account of the freer vibrations of the elastic ether not so much held back, and, on account of the fiery matter thus ejaculated, more apt both for heating bodies and for scattering light to the other ignited bodies.",[]],"de-igne-0074":["Conclusion.",[]],"de-igne-0075":["But I now put the finishing stroke to a little work scarcely begun. I do not delay longer men occupied by weightier duties, commending this little work, whatever it is, and myself at the same time, to the inclined will and benevolence",[]],"de-igne-0076":["of the most ample Philosophical Faculty.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0001":["a new elucidation\nof the first principles\nof metaphysical cognition,\nwhich,\nwith the consent of the most ample philosophical faculty,\nin a public disputation\nin the philosophical auditorium on the 27th of September, from the eighth to the twelfth hour,\nto be held\nfor reception into the same,\nm. immanuel kant of Königsberg\nwill defend,\nwith christoph abraham borchard of Heiligenbeil in Prussia,\ncultivator of sacred theology,\nresponding,\nand with the opponents\njohann gottfried möller of Königsberg,\nstudent of sacred theology,\nfriedrich heinrich samuel lysius of Königsberg,\ncandidate in both laws,\nand\njohann reinhold grube of Königsberg, candidate in both laws.\nin the year 1755.\nKönigsberg,\nprinted with the types of the sacred royal majesty and of the university printer j. h. hartung.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0002":["To the most distinguished,\nmost noble and most excellent lord,\nlord\njohann von lehwald,\nof august king of the Prussians,\nsupreme commander of the camps,\nmost weighty governor of the fortresses of Pillau and Memel,\nknight by far most deserving of the illustrious Order of the Black Eagle,\nmost vigilant colonel of the infantry legion,\nincomparable hero,\nhis most clement lord and patron,",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0003":["these little pages",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0004":["as a token of a grateful and obligated mind",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0005":["for the clemency displayed in many proofs",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0006":["with a devoted and submissive affection of mind",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0007":["offers the most humble client\nchristoph. abraham. borchard.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0008":["plan of the undertaking.",["- All passages are Latin; the glossary covers German terms only, so renderings follow plain English cognates.","- de-igne-0061: \"aër\" rendered \"air\"; \"nitrum\" kept as \"nitre\"; fractions as printed (⅐ as read from the OCR, uncorrected).","- de-igne-0062: \"principium\" = \"principle\"; \"acidum\" = \"acid\"; \"sententia\" = \"opinion\"; \"Physicis\" = \"physicists\". The sentence \"Hocque pacto quid est, quod ambigas...\" is kept as a question.","- de-igne-0065: \"genus\" = *genus* / \"generis\" = \"genus\"; \"specifice\" = \"specifically\". The syntax of \"quam nullibi alias...\" is kept as in the Latin (\"as is found nowhere else\").","- de-igne-0073: the sentence \"Etenim quanquam ... propagat\" has no main verb in the Latin after \"si pensitaveris ... concitet; quod ... propagat\"; I preserved this, and the English remains an incomplete sentence-structure as printed.","- de-igne-0073: \"ejaculatam ... materiam igneam\" kept as \"ejaculated\"; \"ceteris ignitis corporibus\" could be dative of purpose (\"to other ignited bodies\") as rendered.","- nova-dilucidatio-0001: titles and names are rendered into English; \"i. v. c.\" (iuris utriusque candidatus) expanded as \"candidate in both laws\"; this expansion is mine, not in the text. Sequence \"s. s. theol.\" expanded as \"sacred theology\".","- nova-dilucidatio-0002: \"Pillaviae et Memelae\" given as Pillau and Memel; \"Heiligenb. bor.\" as Heiligenbeil in Prussia.","- de-igne-0076 continues de-igne-0075 (dative construction \"commendans\"); the break is kept as in the source."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0009":["As one who is going, as I hope, to bring some light to the first principles of our cognition, since I am resolved to set out in as few little pages as can be done what I have meditated upon this matter, I zealously dispense with prolix digressions, exposing nothing but the sinews and limbs of the arguments, with all the charm and grace of speech drawn off like a garment. In this business, wherever I shall have judged it to be my part to depart from the opinion of distinguished men, and even at times to mark them by name, I am so well persuaded of their equitable manner of judging that I trust this detracts nothing at all from the honor that is owed to their merits, and can by no means be taken by them in a bad part. Since, in a divergence of opinions, each is permitted to abound in his own sense, and it is not forbidden to touch upon the arguments of others as well, with a modest examination, provided bitterness and the itch for quarreling are absent, nor do I notice anywhere that this is judged contrary to the duties of both urbanity and respect by equitable arbiters of things.",["- *principia* = principles; *cognitio* = cognition; *sententia* rendered \"resolved\" in \"stet sententia\" and \"opinion\" in \"clarorum virorum sententia\" and \"sententiarum divortio\" (one English word, \"opinion,\" for the latter two).","- \"Primis cognitionis nostrae principiis lucem ... allaturus\": the participle's construction is kept; \"as one who is going\" follows the future participle *allaturus*.","- \"in malam partem\" kept literally as \"in a bad part\"; \"suo cuique sensu abundare\" = \"to abound in his own sense\" (*sensus*, \"sense\").","- The final sentence has no main verb in the Latin after \"Quandoquidem ... \" other than \"animadverto,\" which is retained; the \"nor\" clause is kept as the Latin has it. The syntax of \"Quandoquidem ... neque hoc ...\" is loose and has been left so.","- *judicare* / *judicandi ratio* = \"judge\" / \"manner of judging.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0010":["Therefore first I shall endeavor to submit to the scales of a more careful investigation what is asserted, more confidently than truly, by the common run concerning the supreme and indubitable primacy of the principle of contradiction above all truths, and then to set forth briefly what in this chapter should be established more correctly. Then, concerning the law of sufficient reason, I shall adduce whatever pertains to a more emended both sense and demonstration of it, together with the difficulties that seem to beset it, and to what is adduced I shall respond with the strength of arguments as far as the mediocrity of my talent permits. Finally, about to advance my foot somewhat further, I shall establish two new principles of metaphysical cognition, of not contemptible moment, as it seems to me at least, not those primitive and most simple ones, to be sure, but for that reason also more suited to uses, and, if anything else is, certainly extending very widely. In which attempt, since for one entering upon a path not trodden it is quite easy to slip into some error, I persuade myself that the benevolent reader will take everything in the better part, with an equitable manner of judging.",["- *principium contradictionis* = principle of contradiction; *lex rationis sufficientis* = law of sufficient reason; *cognitio metaphysica* = metaphysical cognition; *principia* = principles.","- *ingenii mediocritas*, \"mediocrity of my talent\": *ingenium* rendered \"talent\" (not in glossary).","- *emendatiorem ... & sensum & demonstrationem*: \"more emended\" modifies both \"sense\" and \"demonstration\" (or only the nearer one); left as in the Latin.","- *vulgo*: \"the common run\" (noun use, \"by the common run\") rather than the adverb \"commonly\"; both readings are possible with *perhibentur*.","- *momenti* (genitive of quality, \"of ... moment\") agrees with *principia*, via *statuam ... principia ... momenti*.","- *aequa judicandi ratione*: \"with an equitable manner of judging\"; *ratio* = \"manner\" here.","- *vsibus*: \"uses\" (Gebrauch/*usus*)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0011":["Section I.\nOn the Principle of Contradiction.",["- *Principium Contradictionis* = principle of contradiction."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0012":["Notice.",["- *monitum* = \"notice\" (also \"admonition\")."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0013":["Since in the present pages I must above all be zealous for brevity, I consider it better not to transcribe here again the definitions and axioms that we have established by common cognition and that are in harmony with right reason, nor to pursue the custom of those who, servilely bound by I know not what law of method, do not seem to themselves to have proceeded by way and reason unless they have reviewed from the egg all the way to the apples whatever they find in the cabinets of the philosophers. That this not be turned into a fault against me, who do it deliberately, I have judged it fair to warn the reader beforehand.",["- *ab ouo vsque ad mala*: proverbial (\"from the egg to the apples\"), kept literal.","- *cognitione* rendered \"cognition\"; *definitiones* = definitions; *axiomata* = axioms; *ratio* = \"reason\" in *rectae rationi*, \"way and reason\" in *via ac ratione* (one English word for two senses of *ratio*).","- *scrinia* = cabinets (also \"cases,\" \"boxes for papers\").","- *vitio vertatur*: \"be turned into a fault.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0014":["Prop. I. There is not given a single principle of all truths, absolutely first, a *catholicon*.",["- *Prop.* = *Propositio* (proposition). *principium* = principle; *vnicum* = single/unique; *catholicon* is Greek-derived, kept in Latin form."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0015":["A first and truly single principle must necessarily be a simple proposition, otherwise, tacitly comprising several propositions, it would only feign the appearance of a single principle. If it is therefore a truly simple proposition, it must necessarily be either affirmative or negative. But I contend that, if it is one or the other, it cannot be universal, comprising under itself absolutely all truths; namely if you say it is affirmative, it cannot be the absolutely first principle of negating truths, if negative, it cannot lead the column among the positive ones.",["- *simplex* = simple (glossary *einfach*). *complexa* = \"comprising\"; *complectens* = \"comprising\" as well (same English for two Latin forms).","- *positiuas* rendered \"positive\" (not \"affirmative,\" used for *affirmativa*).","- *veritatum negantium* = \"of negating truths,\" literal.","- *agmen ducere*: \"lead the column.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0016":["For suppose it to be a negative proposition; since the consequence of all truths from their principles is either direct or indirect, first, who is there who does not see that by the direct manner of concluding only negative corollaries can be deduced from a negative principle? then, if you demand that affirmative propositions flow from it indirectly, you will confess that this can happen only by means of the proposition: *Of whatever the opposite is false, that is true*. Which proposition, since it is itself affirmative, will not be able to flow from a negative principle by the direct manner of arguing, much less indirectly, because it would need its own very support, hence it will depend in no manner whatever on a principle enunciated negatively. And thus, since it is not free for affirming propositions to proceed from a negative and single principle alone, this *catholicon* will not be able to be named so. Similarly if you establish as your cardinal principle an affirmative proposition, negative ones certainly will not depend on it directly; indirectly, however, there will be need of the proposition: *If the opposite of something is true, it is false*, that is, if the opposite of something is affirmed, it is denied; which, since it is a negative proposition, again in no way, neither directly, which is evident of itself, nor indirectly, except through a begging of itself, will be able to be deduced from the affirmative principle. However, then, you establish with yourself, you will not refuse the proposition that I postulated at the head of the proposition: that of absolutely all truths there cannot be given a single, ultimate principle, a *catholicon*.",["- *consequentia* = \"consequence\"; *consectaria* = \"corollaries\"; *concludendi ratio* = \"manner of concluding\" (*concludere* = conclude); *deduci* = \"deduced.\"","- The printed \"illudest\" is silently corrected to *illud est*.","- *sui ipsius suffragio egeret*: \"would need its own very support\"; *suffragium* = support (also \"vote\"). *per sui ipsius petitionem* = \"through a begging of itself\" (*petitio principii*).","- *quam ... postulavi propositionem*: \"the proposition that I postulated\"; *in fronte propositionis* = \"at the head of the proposition\" (the statement of Prop. I).","- *Vtcunque igitur tecum statueris*: \"However, then, you establish with yourself\"; could also be \"However you decide for yourself.\"","- The two quoted propositions are italicized as mentioned, not used; the Latin has no quotation marks."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0017":["Prop. II. Of all truths there are two absolutely first principles, one of affirming truths, namely the proposition *whatever is, is*, the other of negating truths, namely the proposition *whatever is not, is not*. Which two together are commonly called the principle of identity.",["- *principium identitatis* = principle of identity; *bina* = \"two\"; *veritatum affirmantium / negantium* = \"of affirming / negating truths.\"","- *quae ambo simul*: \"which two together\"; *vocantur communiter*: \"are commonly called.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0018":["Again I appeal to the two kinds of demonstrating truths, namely the direct and the indirect. The former manner of concluding gathers truth from the agreement of the notions of the subject and predicate, and always lays down this rule as foundation: Whenever the subject, regarded either in itself or in connection, posits those things that involve the notion of the predicate, or excludes those that are excluded by the notion of the predicate, it is to be established that this belongs to it; and the same somewhat more explicitly: whenever identity is found between the notions of the subject and of the predicate, the proposition is true; which, expressed in the most general terms, as befits a first principle, runs thus: *Whatever is, is*, and *whatever is not, is not*. Over all direct argumentation, therefore, the principle of identity will certainly preside, which is to say, the first.",["- *demonstrandi* = \"demonstrating\" (*demonstratio* = demonstration); *notio* = \"notion\" (not in glossary; *Begriff* = concept would be the German); *nexu* = \"connection\" (*nexus*; cf. *Zusammenhang* = nexus).","- *ponit* = \"posits\" (*setzen*); *inuoluunt* = \"involve\"; *competere* = \"belongs to.\"","- *quod ... ita audit*: \"runs thus\" (lit. \"sounds/is heard thus\").","- *q. e.* = *quod est*, rendered \"which is to say.\"","- *terminis generalissimis* = \"in the most general terms\"; *terminus* = term.","- *hoc illi competere*: \"this belongs to it\"; the referents are not specified further in the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0019":["If you ask about the indirect manner of concluding, you will find the same twin principle ultimately underlying it. For one must always appeal to these two propositions, 1. *of whatever the opposite is false, that is true*, that is, of whatever the opposite is denied, that is to be affirmed, 2. *of whatever the opposite is true, that is false*. Of which the first has affirmative propositions for corollaries, the second negative ones. If you bring forth the former proposition in the simplest terms, you will have it thus: *Whatever is not not, that is* (since, to be sure, the opposite is expressed by the particle *not*, likewise the removal by the particle *not*). The latter you will shape in the following manner: *Whatever is not, is not*, (namely here again the word of the opposite is brought out by the particle *not*, and the word of falsity or removal equally by the same particle). If now, as the characteristic law requires, you carry out the force of the words contained in the former proposition, because one particle *not* indicates that the other is to be removed, with both deleted there will come out to you the proposition *whatever is, is*. But the other, since it runs *whatever is not, is not*, it is clear that also in indirect demonstration the twin principle of identity holds the first place, and consequently is the ultimate foundation of absolutely all cognition.",["- *lege Characteristica*: \"the characteristic law\" (lit. law of the characteristic/symbolic notation); *Characteristica* as an adjective is kept capitalized as printed.","- *remotio* = \"removal\"; *falsitas* = \"falsity\"; *oppositum* = \"the opposite\"; *consectaria* = \"corollaries.\"","- *vox* = \"word\"; *vim exsequaris*: \"carry out the force.\"","- *Altera autem, cum audiat...*: the sentence is syntactically loose in Latin; kept as is. *audiat* = \"runs.\"","- *fundamentum* = \"foundation\" (not \"ground,\" Grund); *cognitio* = \"cognition.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0020":["*Scholion.* Here is a specimen, slight indeed, but not at all to be despised, in the *ars characteristica combinatoria*; for the simplest terms which we use in unraveling these principles differ hardly at all from characters. In order that I may on this occasion disclose what I think about this art, which, after Leibniz gave out that he had invented it, all the learned have lamented was buried in the same tomb with so great a man, I confess that I perceive in this pronouncement of the great philosopher the testament of that Aesopic father, who, when about to breathe out his soul, had disclosed to his children that he had hidden a treasure somewhere in the field, and since, before he had indicated the place, he suddenly expired, gave his sons occasion for most energetically turning over the field and subduing it by digging, until, frustrated in hope, they were, without doubt, made richer by the fertility of the field. This, surely, I reckon to be the one fruit that is to be expected from the pursuit of that celebrated artifice, if there are any who still bring themselves to devote effort to it. But if it is permitted to confess openly what the matter is, I fear lest what the most acute Boerhaave somewhere in the *Chemistry* suspects of the most distinguished artists of the alchemists, namely that after many and singular secrets discovered they at last thought that nothing would not be in their power as soon as they had applied their hand, and with a certain swiftness of foreseeing narrated as done those things which they gathered could be done, indeed ought to be done, as soon as they turned their mind to accomplishing them, the same fate has also befallen the incomparable man. For my part, if one has arrived at absolutely first principles, I do not deny that some use of the characteristic art is permitted, since there is a supply of using notions, and indeed even the simplest terms, as signs; but where composite cognition is to be expressed by means of characters, all the perspicacity of genius suddenly sticks as on a rock and is hindered by an inextricable difficulty. I find also that the philosopher of great name, the illustrious Daries, attempted to render the principle of contradiction explicated by means of characters, expressing the affirmative notion by the sign + A, the negative by the sign – A, whence arises the equation + A – A = 0, i.e. to affirm and to deny the same is impossible or nothing. In which attempt, by the leave of so great a man I would say, I without doubt notice a *petitio principii*. For if you assign to the sign of the negative notion such a force that it removes the affirmative joined to it, you openly suppose the principle of contradiction, in which it is established that opposed notions remove one another. But our explanation of the proposition: *Of whatever the opposite is false, that is true*, is immune from this blemish. For, enunciated in the simplest terms, since it then sounds: whatever is not not, that is, by removing the particles *non* we do nothing but pursue their simple signification, and, as was necessary, the principle of identity comes forth: whatever is, is.",["- \"Daries\" is the printed form (Joachim Georg Darjes); \"Ill.\" read as *illustris*. \"Boerhaauius\" = Boerhaave.","- *notio* rendered \"notion\" throughout (not \"concept\"); *cognitio* = \"cognition\"; *principium* = \"principle\"; *terminus* = \"term\".","- *Characteristica combinatoria*, *petitio principii* kept in Latin, italicized.","- \"Quem certe fructum vnicum\" is rendered with \"This ... the one fruit\"; the Latin relative clause is kept loose in the English.","- The long \"ne ... idem quoque Viro incomparabili fato euenerit\" construction is kept as one sentence, in Latin order; the \"quod ... suspicatur\" clause is an anticipated object."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0021":["Prop. III. To establish further the preference of the principle of identity, for obtaining primacy in the subordination of truths, over the principle of contradiction.",["- The Latin is elliptical (\"praeferentiam ... prae principio contradictionis\"); the English keeps the order and does not smooth it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0022":["The proposition which arrogates to itself the name of the absolutely highest and most general principle of all truths should be enunciated first in the simplest, then also in the most general terms; which I seem without doubt to notice in the twin principle of identity. For of all affirming terms the simplest is the little word *est*, of the negating the little word *non est*. Then, nothing more universal can be conceived than the simplest notions. For the more composite borrow light from the simple, and because they are more determinate than these, they cannot be so general.",["- \"sit ... enunciata\": rendered \"should be enunciated\" (jussive subjunctive). \"gemino\" qualifies *principio identitatis* (\"twin\"), i.e. the affirmative/negative pair; kept literal.","- *determinatiores* = \"more determinate\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0023":["The principle of contradiction, which is expressed in the proposition: It is impossible for the same thing at once to be and not to be, is in fact nothing but a definition of the impossible, for whatever contradicts itself, or what is conceived at once to be and not to be, is called impossible. But in what way can it be established that all truths must be referred back to this definition as to a Lydian stone? For it is neither necessary that you vindicate every truth from the impossibility of the opposite, nor, to confess the truth, is this sufficient by itself; for there is no transition from the impossibility of the opposite to the assertion of truth, except by means of the dictum: *Of whatever the opposite is false, that is true*, which therefore has a divided dominion with the principle of contradiction, as shown in what precedes.",["- Printed \"estidem\" read as \"est idem\".","- *definitio* = \"definition\"; *lapis Lydius* = \"Lydian stone\" (touchstone).","- \"vt verum fatear\" is rendered \"to confess the truth\"; *verum* is ambiguous between \"truth\" and \"true\", and the English preserves the pun only in part."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0024":["Finally, to entrust to a negative proposition especially the first places in the region of truths, and the head and salutary support of all, to whom would it not seem rather harsh and somewhat even worse than a paradox, since it is not evident why a negative truth rather than an affirmative has obtained this right? We rather, since there are two genera of truths, establish for them also two first principles, the one affirming, the other negating.",["- Printed \"affirmansalterum\" read as \"affirmans, alterum\".","- *genus* = \"genus\" (Gattung), here \"genera\" in the plural."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0025":["*Scholion.* This inquiry could perhaps seem to someone, as subtle and laborious, so also superfluous and abandoned by all utility. And if you look at the fecundity of corollaries, you have me assenting. For the mind, although it has not been taught such a principle, cannot but use the same everywhere spontaneously and by a certain necessity of nature. But will not the matter, for that reason, be worthy of inquiry, to follow the chain of truths all the way to the highest joint? And surely in this way to look more deeply into the law of the arguments of our mind is not to be despised. Indeed, to allege only one thing, because all our ratiocination is resolved into detecting the identity of the predicate with the subject, whether considered in itself or in connection, as is clear from the ultimate rule of truths, hence it is to be seen: that God does not need ratiocination, since, as all things lie open most clearly to his gaze, the same act of representation presents to the understanding what agrees or does not agree, nor does he need analysis, as the night which overshadows our intelligence necessarily requires.",["- *ratiocinatio* = \"ratiocination\"; *intellectus* = \"understanding\"; *intelligentia* = \"intelligence\"; *repraesentatio* = \"representation\"; *analysis* = \"analysis\".","- \"in se vel in nexu spectato\": \"considered\" may govern *praedicati cum subjecto identitatem* or the predicate and subject taken singly; left as in Latin.","- \"quae conueniant vel non conueniant\": the neuter plural subject is not specified; rendered \"what agrees or does not agree\".","- *articulum* rendered \"joint\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0026":["Section II.\nOn the principle of the determining, commonly called sufficient, ground.",["- *ratio* rendered \"ground\" throughout (Grund); *determinans* = \"determining\"; *sufficiens* = \"sufficient\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0027":["Definition.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0028":["Prop. IV. To determine is to posit a predicate with the exclusion of the opposite. That which determines a subject with respect to some predicate is called ground (*ratio*). Ground is distinguished into the antecedently and the consequently determining. The antecedently determining is that whose notion precedes the determined, i.e. which not being supposed the determined is not intelligible. * The consequently determining is that which would not be posited unless the notion which is determined by it had already been posited from elsewhere. The former ground you can also call the ground *Cur* or the ground of being or of becoming (*ratio essendi vel fiendi*), the latter the ground *Quod* or of cognizing (*ratio cognoscendi*).",["- *determinare* = \"determine\", *ponere* = \"posit\"; *cognoscendi* = \"of cognizing\" (cognoscere).","- *Cur* and *Quod* are left untranslated (capitalized in the original).","- \"qua non supposita\": ablative absolute, \"which not being supposed\"; the antecedent of *qua* is *ratio* (understood).","- The asterisk marks the footnote printed as the next passage (0029)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0029":["* To this one may reckon the identical ground, where the notion of the subject determines the predicate through its perfect identity with it, e.g. A triangle has three sides; where the notion of the determined neither follows nor precedes the notion of the determining.",["- Footnote to Prop. IV. *ratio identica* rendered \"identical ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0030":["Substantiation of the reality of the definition.",["- *Adstructio* = \"substantiation\" (literally \"building on / adding to\"); *realitas* = \"reality\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0031":["The notion of ground, according to common sense, effects some nexus and connection between subject and predicate. Therefore it always desires a subject and a predicate which would unite to it. If you ask the ground of the circle, I do not at all understand what it is that you ask, unless you add a predicate, e.g. that it is the most capacious of all isoperimetric figures. We ask, e.g., the ground of the evils in the world. We have therefore the proposition: The world contains very many evils. The ground *Quod*, or of cognizing, is not asked, because experience takes its place, but the ground *Cur* or of becoming is to be indicated, i.e. that which being posited it is intelligible that the world, antecedently with respect to this predicate, is not indeterminate, but that by it the predicate of evils is posited with the exclusion of the opposite. Ground therefore makes out of the indeterminate the determinate. And since all truth is effected by the determination of the predicate in the subject, the determining ground of truth is not only a criterion but also a source, from which if you depart, indeed very many things possible, nothing at all true would be found. Therefore it is indeterminate for us whether the planet Mercury revolves about an axis or not, since we lack a ground which posits either with the exclusion of the opposite; both remain possible for so long, neither is made true with respect to our cognition.",["- *nexus* kept as \"nexus\"; *colligatio* = \"connection\" (here not distinguished further); *criterium* = \"criterion\"; *experientia* = \"experience\"; *determinatio* = \"determination\".","- \"ratio\" in \"Ratio igitur\" and elsewhere = \"ground\"; \"ex indeterminatis efficit determinata\" kept as plural-neuter, rendered with singular generic English.","- \"possibilia quidem quam plurima, nihil omnino veri\": the clause is elliptical (verb \"would be found\" governs both); kept elliptical.","- \"qua posita intelligibile est\": *qua* (\"which\") has *ratio* as antecedent; the clause \"Mundum ... non esse indeterminatum\" is the accusative-infinitive subject."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0032":["To illustrate by an example the difference between reasons determining antecedently and consequently: I name the eclipses of the Jovian satellites, which I say supply the *ratio cognoscendi* of the successive propagation of light, made with an assignable speed. But this reason is only consequently determining of this truth; for if, at the very most, there were no Jovian satellites at all, nor their occultation made by turns, light would nonetheless be moved in time, although it would perhaps not be cognized by us, or, so that I may apply it more closely to the definition given, the phenomena of the Jovian satellites proving the successive motion of light presuppose this very character of light, without which they could not happen so, and therefore determine this truth only consequently. But the *ratio fiendi*, or why the motion of light is joined with an assignable expenditure of time, (if you embrace the opinion of Descartes,) is posited in the elasticity of the little elastic globules of air, which, yielding somewhat to impact in accordance with the laws of elasticity, make perceptible at last, by summing through an immense concatenated series, the point of a little time that they absorb in any globule. This would be the reason determining antecedently, or that without which, if it were not posited, there would plainly be no place for the determined. For if the globules of aether were perfectly hard, then through distances however immense no interval of time would be perceived between the emission and the arrival of light.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0033":["The definition of the illustrious Wolff, since it labors under a notable mark, seemed to me here to need emendation. For he defines reason through that from which it can be understood why something is rather than is not. Here he has undoubtedly mixed the defined into the definition. For however much the little word *Cur* [why] may seem adapted to common understanding, so that it is to be judged capable of being taken up in a definition, nevertheless it tacitly implicates again the concept of reason. For if you examine it rightly, you will find that it signifies the same as *Quamob rationem* [on account of what reason]. Therefore, with the substitution duly made, Wolff's definition will sound: Reason is that from which it can be understood on account of what reason something is rather than is not.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0034":["Likewise, in the enunciation of the principle of sufficient reason I have thought it better to substitute the term of determining reason, and I have the illustrious Crusius assenting. For the term sufficient is ambiguous, as he abundantly shows; because how much suffices does not at once appear; but since to determine is so to posit that every opposite is excluded, it denotes that which certainly suffices for the thing to be conceived thus and not otherwise.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0035":["Prop. V. Nothing is true without a determining reason.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0036":["Every true proposition indicates that the subject is determined with respect to the predicate, i.e. that this is posited with the exclusion of the opposite: In every true proposition, therefore, the opposite of the predicate that belongs must necessarily be excluded. But a predicate is excluded to which, by another concept posited, there is repugnance, by virtue of the principle of contradiction. Therefore exclusion has no place where there is not present a concept that is repugnant to the opposite to be excluded. In every truth, therefore, there is something that, by excluding the opposite predicate, determines the truth of the proposition. Since this comes under the name of determining reason: it is to be established that nothing is true without a determining reason.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0037":["The same otherwise.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0038":["From the concept of reason it can be understood which of the opposite predicates is to be attributed to the subject and which to be removed. Posit that anything is true without a determining reason, there would be nothing from which it would appear whether the one of the opposites is to be attributed to the subject, or removed; neither is therefore excluded, and the subject is, with respect to each of the predicates, indeterminate, hence there is no place for truth, which, since it was nevertheless assumed, an open repugnance stands plain.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0039":["Scholium. That the cognition of truth always rests with regard to reason is established by the common sense of all mortals. But we are very often content with a reason consequently determining, when the matter is for us only about certainty; but that a reason antecedently determining is always given, or, if you prefer, a genetic or at least an identical one, readily appears from the theorem cited and the definition looked at jointly, inasmuch as the reason consequently determining does not effect truth but explains it. But let us proceed to the reasons determining existence.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0040":["Prop. VI. For something to have the reason of its existence in itself is absurd.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0041":["For whatever contains in itself the reason of the existence of some thing is the cause of it. Posit, therefore, that there is something that would have the reason of its existence in itself, then it would be the cause of itself. But since the concept of cause is by nature prior to the concept of the caused, and the latter posterior to the former: the same would be prior and at the same time posterior to itself, which is absurd.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0042":["Corollary. Whatever, therefore, is maintained to exist absolutely necessarily, exists not on account of some reason, but because the opposite is plainly not thinkable. This impossibility of the opposite is the *ratio cognoscendi* of existence, but it plainly lacks a reason antecedently determining. It exists; but this suffices both to have said and to have conceived of the same.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0043":["Scholium. I do indeed find that in the tenets of more recent philosophers this opinion is from time to time sung over again: that GOD has the reason of His existence posited in Himself, but I myself would not wish to give assent to it. For it seems somewhat harsh to these good men to deny to GOD, as to the last and most consummate principle of reasons and causes, a reason of Himself; and therefore, because none may be acknowledged outside Himself, they assert that He has it hidden away in Himself, than which assuredly scarcely anything else can be found more remote from right reason. For where in the chain of reasons you have arrived at the principle, it is evident of itself that the degree is halted and the question plainly abolished by the consummation of the answer. I know indeed that appeal is made to the very concept of GOD, by which they demand that His existence is determined, but that this is done ideally, not really, is easily perceived. You form for yourself the concept of a certain being in which there is the allness of reality; through this conceptus it must be confessed that you bestow existence upon it as well. The argumentation therefore proceeds thus: if in a certain being all realities are united without degree, that being will exist; if they are conceived only as united, its existence too is likewise concerned only in ideas. Therefore the opinion should rather have been formed thus: forming for ourselves the concept of a certain being, which we call GOD, we determined it in such a way that existence is included in it. If therefore the preconceived concept is true, then it is true also that He exists. And let these things be said in favor of those who give assent to the Cartesian argument.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0044":["Prop. VII. There is given a being whose existence precedes the very possibility, both of itself and of all things, and which is therefore said to exist absolutely necessarily. It is called GOD.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0045":["Since possibility is accomplished by nothing but the non-repugnance of certain concepts joined, and so the concept of possibility results from comparison; but in every comparison it must be that what are to be compared are at hand, and where nothing at all is given there is no place for comparison, and for the concept of possibility that corresponds to it: it follows that nothing can be conceived as possible unless whatever is real in every possible concept exists, and indeed (since, if you depart from this, nothing at all would be possible, i.e. there would be nothing but the impossible) exists absolutely necessarily. Further, this all-sided reality must be united in a single being.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0046":["For posit that these reals, which are as the material of all possible concepts, are found distributed in several existing things, each of these things would have an existence limited in a certain way, that is, joined to some privations; to which, since absolute necessity does not belong just as it does to realities, while they nevertheless pertain to the all-sided determination of a thing, without which a thing cannot exist, the realities limited in this way would exist contingently. Therefore for absolute necessity it is required that they exist without any limitation, that is, constitute an infinite being. Since of this being a plurality, if you should feign any, would be a repetition made several times, hence, contingency being opposed to absolute necessity, it is to be established that only a single one exists absolutely necessarily. There is given, therefore, GOD, and a single one, the absolutely necessary principle of all possibility.",["- *ratio* = \"reason\" throughout; *ratio cognoscendi*, *ratio fiendi* left in Latin as Kant's own technical terms; *ratio antecedenter / consequenter determinans* = \"reason (antecedently / consequently) determining.\"","- *notio* and *conceptus* both = \"concept\" (0036, 0038, 0043, 0045); *notio* is the usual word, *conceptus* appears in 0043 and 0045 (italicized once in 0043 where the Latin is kept).","- *existentia* = \"existence\"; *existere* = \"exist\"; *realitas* = \"reality\"; *determinatio* = \"determination\"; *possibilitas* = \"possibility.\"","- *Cur* and *Quamob rationem* are mentioned words in 0033; bracketed glosses there are mine, not the Academy's, and supply English beside the Latin.","- 0032: \"ratione ... assignabili\" kept as \"assignable\"; *phaenomena* rendered \"phenomena\" (not glossary \"appearances,\" since the Latin is Kant's here). *Jovialium/Jouialium* silently normalized.","- 0038: the last clause (\"hinc non locus veritati, quae tamen quum fuisse sumta sit, aperta patet repugnantia\") is syntactically awkward; kept as it stands (\"fuisse sumta\" for *fuisset/fuerit sumta* may be a misprint or OCR slip, doubtful).","- 0039: \"gradum sisti\" (0043 \"the degree is halted\") translates *gradus* literally as \"degree,\" as in the glossary's *Grad*, though \"step\" is also possible.","- 0043: *idealiter/realiter* = \"ideally/really\"; *omnitudo realitatis* = \"allness of reality\" (cf. *Allheit* = totality). *Aether* = \"aether.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0047":["Scholium. Behold a demonstration of the divine existence, as essential as it can possibly be made, and although there is properly no place for the genetic, yet confirmed by the most primitive document, namely by the possibility of things itself. Hence it is evident: if you remove GOD, not the sole existence of all things, but also internal possibility itself is utterly abolished. For although they commonly call essences (which consist in internal possibility) absolutely necessary, yet it would be said more rightly that they belong to things absolutely necessarily. For the essence of the triangle, which consists in the connection of three sides, is not necessary through itself; for who of sound mind would contend that it is necessary in itself that three sides always be conceived as joined; but I concede that this is necessary for the triangle, i.e. if you think a triangle, you necessarily think three sides, which is the same as if you say: if something is, it is. But in what way it comes about that for the thinking of sides, of space to be comprehended, etc., concepts are available, that is, that there is in general something that can be thought, from which afterwards, by combining, limiting, determining, any concept whatever of a thinkable thing results, this could not be conceived at all, unless in GOD, the source of all reality, whatever is real in the concept existed. We indeed know that Descartes gave an argument for divine existence drawn from its own internal concept, in which, however, how he was frustrated in the outcome can be seen in the scholium of the preceding §. GOD is the only one of all beings in which existence is prior, or, if you prefer, identical with possibility. And of this no concept remains as soon as you depart from its existence.",["- *notio* rendered \"concept\"; *demonstratio* \"demonstration\"; *argumentum* \"argument\"; *realitas* \"reality\"; *possibilitas* \"possibility\"; *essentia* \"essence\".","- \"the genetic\" renders *geneticae* (feminine genitive, noun unexpressed); \"the preceding §\" renders *§hi prioris* as printed.","- *comprobatam* agrees with the accusative *demonstrationem*; *documento maxime primitiuo* is rendered literally, \"document\".","- *DEum*/*DEO* rendered \"GOD\" to keep the printed capitals."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0048":["Prop. VIII. Nothing contingently existing can lack a ground determining existence antecedently.",["- *ratio* = \"ground\"; *ratio ... antecedenter determinans* = \"ground determining ... antecedently\"; *contingenter* = \"contingently\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0049":["Suppose it lacks. There will be nothing that determines it as existing, besides the existence of the thing itself. Since therefore existence is nevertheless determined, i.e. is posited in such a way that every opposite of its complete determination is entirely excluded, there will be no other exclusion of the opposite than that which proceeds from the positing of existence. But since this exclusion is identical (since nothing else forbids the thing not to exist than that non-existence has been removed), the opposite of existence will be excluded through itself, i.e. absolutely impossible; i.e. the thing will exist absolutely necessarily, which conflicts with the hypothesis.",["- *omnimodae ... determinationis* = \"complete determination\"; *positio* = \"positing\"; *exclusio* = \"exclusion\".","- *Pone carere* is elliptical (no object expressed); \"it\" supplied by the English verb's need for a subject, as in the Latin implied subject."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0050":["Corollary. From what has been demonstrated it is therefore clear that only the existence of contingent things needs the support of a determining ground, that the one absolutely necessary is exempted from this law; hence that the principle is not to be admitted in so general a sense that it embraces in its dominion the totality of all possibles.",["- *firmamento* = \"support\"; *vniuersitatem* = \"totality\" (not *Allheit* as such); *possibilium* = \"possibles\".","- *rationis determinantis* = \"of a determining ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0051":["Scholium. Behold the demonstration of the principle of the determining ground, at last, as far as I at least persuade myself, illuminated with all the light of certainty. That the most perspicacious philosophers of our age, among whom I name Ill. Crusius for the sake of honor, have always complained of the not very solid demonstration of this principle, which we find on offer in all the writings on this matter, is sufficiently established. Of the remedy of this evil the great man so far despaired that he seriously contended that this proposition is entirely incapable even of demonstration, if it be conceded to be most true. But why the demonstration of this principle was not so ready and expeditious for me that I should complete the whole with a single argument, as has commonly been attempted, but it was necessary to gain full certainty only by a certain detour, an account must be given by me.",["- *persuadeo* = \"persuade\"; *certitudo* = \"certainty\"; *venalem* = \"on offer\" (literally \"for sale\").","- *vel ... vel maxime* in \"vel demonstratione ... si vel maxime vera\" is rendered \"even ... if ... most true\"; the second *vel* is doubtful in force."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0052":["For first I had to distinguish carefully between the ground of truth and of existence; although it could seem that the universality of the principle of the determining ground in the region of truths extends the same equally beyond existence. For if nothing is true, i.e. if the predicate does not belong to the subject, without a determining ground, it follows also that there will be no predicate of existence without it. But it is established that for confirming truth there is no need of an antecedently determining ground, but that the identity intervening between predicate and subject suffices. In existents, however, the question is about the antecedently determining ground, which, if there is none, the being exists absolutely necessarily; if the existence is contingent, I have given as proven that it cannot but precede it. Hence truth, drawn from its very sources, has emerged, in my judgment at least, purer.",["- *antecendenter* (printed) corrected silently to *antecedenter*.","- *euictum dedi* = \"I have given as proven\"; *judicium* = \"judgment\"; *ratio Veritatis et Existentiae* = \"ground of truth and of existence\" (*veritas*, *existentia* capitalized in the source).","- \"it\" after \"precede\" is ambiguous in the Latin (*eam*, referring to existence)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0053":["The most celebrated Crusius indeed thinks that certain existents are so determined through their very actuality that he declares it vain to require anything beyond. Titius acts by free volition; I ask why he did this rather than not did it; he answers, because he willed. But why did he will? He declares that this is asked absurdly. If you ask why he did not rather do something else? he answers, because he now does this. Therefore he thinks that free volition is determined in act through its existence, not antecedently through grounds prior to its existence; and that by the sole positing of actuality all opposite determinations are excluded, hence he contends that there is no need of a determining ground. But that a contingent thing, if you depart from an antecedently determining ground, can never be sufficiently determined, hence neither existing, I shall, if it please, prove also by another argument. The act of free volition exists, this existence excludes the opposite of this determination; but since it did not exist once, and existence through itself does not determine whether it once was or was not, through the existence of this volition this question, whether it had already existed before or had not existed, remains undetermined; but since in complete determination this too is one of all, whether the being has begun or not, the being will to that extent be undetermined, nor can it be determined unless, besides those things that belong to internal existence, concepts are drawn that are thinkable independently of its existence. But since that which determines the antecedent non-existence of an existing being precedes the concept of existence, and the same which determines that the existing being did not exist before has at the same time determined from non-existence to existence, (because the propositions: Why what now exists once did not exist, and why what once did not exist now exists, are in truth identical,) i.e. is a ground determining existence antecedently, it is abundantly clear that without this there can be no place for the complete determination of that being which is conceived as having arisen, hence not for existence either. If this demonstration should seem somewhat obscure to anyone on account of the deeper analysis of concepts, he can be content with the preceding.",["- *actualitas* = \"actuality\"; *existentia* = \"existence\"; *propositiones* = \"propositions\"; *notio* = \"concept\"; *Analysis* = \"analysis\".","- *an minus* = \"or not\"; *inceperit* = \"has begun\".","- *vanum autumet vltra quicquam requirere* is literal; \"beyond\" renders *vltra* as an adverb.","- \"hence neither existing\" (*hinc nec existentem*) keeps the elliptical construction; the subject of \"existing\" is the contingent thing.","- *Si libuerit* rendered \"if it please\", leaving the subject unexpressed as in the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0054":["Finally, why I declined to acquiesce in the demonstration used by Ill. Wolff and his followers I shall dispatch more briefly. The demonstration of this illustrious man, as it is found more clearly set out by the most perspicacious Baumgarten, comes back to this, to embrace much in few words. If something had no ground, nothing would be its ground; therefore nothing something, which is absurd. But the manner of arguing ought rather to have been formed thus: if for a being there is no ground, its ground is nothing, i.e. non-being. This indeed I grant with both hands, since if there is no ground, the concept corresponding to it will be of a non-being, hence if to a being no ground can be assigned except one to which no concept at all corresponds, it will plainly lack a ground, which comes back to what was supposed. Hence the absurd does not follow, which they supposed flowed from it. I shall put forth an example in testimony of my view. I would dare to demonstrate, according to this manner of concluding: that the first man was still begotten by some father. For suppose he was not begotten. There would be nothing that begot him. He would therefore be begotten by nothing; which since it contradicts, it must be confessed that he was begotten by someone. It is not difficult to evade the trickery of the argument. If he was not begotten, nothing begot him. That is, he who would be thought to have begotten him is nothing or non-being, which indeed is certain as the most certain: but the proposition, perversely converted, obtains a very badly distorted sense.",["- *ratio* = \"ground\" in the argument, but *argumentandi ratio*, *concludendi ratio* = \"manner of arguing/concluding\"; one English word is not used for both.","- *sententia* = \"view\" (not \"opinion\"; *opinabantur* = \"supposed\"); *conceptus* and *notio* both = \"concept\".","- *non ens* = \"non-being\"; *captio* = \"trickery\"; *sensus* = \"sense\".","- *adhuc* in \"Primum hominem adhuc a patre quodam esse genitum\" rendered \"still\"; its force is doubtful."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0055":["Prop. IX. To enumerate and dissolve the difficulties which seem to press upon the principle of the determining, commonly sufficient, ground.",["- *vulgo sufficientis* modifies *rationis*; \"commonly sufficient\" keeps the elision (i.e. \"commonly called sufficient\").","- *diluere* = \"dissolve\"; *difficultates* = \"difficulties\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0056":["Among the opponents of this principle, the one to be rightly thought to lead the column, and alone able to take the place of all,* is the S. R. and most acute Crusius, whom I profess to be second to scarcely anyone among Germany's promoters, I do not say of philosophers, but of philosophy. If the discussion of his doubts turns out well for me (which the patronage of a good cause seems to promise), I shall seem to myself to have overcome every difficulty. First he reproaches the formula of this principle with ambiguity and an unstable sense. He notes rightly, that is, that the ground of cognizing, likewise the moral ground and other ideal grounds, are now and again employed in place of real and antecedently determining ones, so that, whichever of the two one wishes to be understood, it can often be understood only with difficulty. Since this dart does not strike our assertions, it need not be turned aside by us. Whoever examines these things of ours, such as they are, will see that I carefully distinguish the ground of truth from the ground of actuality. In the former it is only a matter of that positing of the predicate which is effected through the identity with the predicate of the notions that are involved in the subject, whether regarded absolutely or in connection, and the predicate, which already adheres to the subject, is only detected. In the latter, concerning those things that are posited as inhering, it is examined not whether but whence their existence is determined; if nothing is present that excludes the opposite, then, besides the absolute positing of that thing, it must be established that it exists through itself and absolutely necessarily, but if it is assumed to exist contingently, it is necessary that others be present, which, by determining thus and not otherwise, already antecedently exclude the opposite of existence. And these things, indeed, concerning our demonstration in general.",["- *ratio* = ground throughout; *ratio cognoscendi* = \"ground of cognizing\"; *ratio veritatis* / *ratio actualitatis* = \"ground of truth\" / \"ground of actuality.\"","- *Philosophos* / *Philosophiae promotores*: \"philosophers\" / \"promoters of philosophy\" (the Latin has the genitive with *promotores*).","- *determinantibus*, *determinata* = \"determining,\" \"determined\"; *existentia* = \"existence\"; *positio* = \"positing\"; *notionum* = \"notions\" (not \"concepts,\" since the Latin is *notio*).","- The footnote marker * stands after *putandus est* as printed; the footnote is the next passage.","- \"through the identity with the predicate of the notions…\" follows the Latin construction *per notionum… cum praedicato identitatem*, kept ambiguous as in the original.","- *quod… spondere videtur*: the antecedent of \"which\" is left open (the whole clause).","- *Si nihil adest… praeter absolutam rei illius positionem*: the syntax is obscure and is rendered as it stands.","- *impugnatores*: \"opponents\"; *asserta*: \"assertions\"; *contingenter* = \"contingently.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0057":["* I do not wish to detract anything here from the Illustrious Darjes, whose arguments, indeed even those of some others, I profess to be of great moment for weighing down the principle of determining ground, but since they seem to be quite akin to those to be alleged here from the praised D. Crusius, I judge that I can restrict the response to the doubts chiefly to these, with the great men otherwise not unwilling.",["- *rationis determinantis principium* = \"principle of determining ground\" (*ratio* = ground).","- *grauandum*: rendered \"weighing down\"; the sense of the verb (burden / press upon) is left as in the Latin.","- *haud inuitis magnis… viris*: ablative absolute, kept literal."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0058":["Certainly a greater danger looms over the defenders of this principle from that objection of the Most Distinguished Man, by which he explicitly and with no contemptible force of arguments imputes to us the guilt of the immutable necessity of all things and of Stoic fate recalled by *postliminium*, indeed of the elevation of all freedom and morality. His argument, although not altogether new, yet delivered by him more explicitly and more strongly, I shall allege, as far as can be done, clearly, yet with its force undiminished.",["- *postliminio* is a Roman-law term (right of return), left in Latin italics; *revocati* agrees with *fati*, possibly also *necessitatis*.","- *eleuatae* (sic, for *elevatae*) is rendered \"elevation\" with *culpam*: \"the guilt of… elevated freedom\"; the construction is ambiguous (genitive \"of… freedom and morality\" being \"raised up/removed\").","- *Clarissimi Viri*: \"Most Distinguished Man\" (i.e., Crusius).","- *necessitas* = \"necessity\"; *fatum* = \"fate.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0059":["If whatever happens cannot happen otherwise than by having an antecedently determining ground, it follows that whatever does not happen also cannot happen, because, that is, there is no ground without which it cannot happen at all. Since this must be conceded of all the grounds of grounds in retrograde order, it follows: that all things happen by natural connection so interwoven and entwined, that whoever wishes the opposite of a certain event or even of a free action conceives the impossible in his wish, inasmuch as the ground required to produce it is not present. And thus, taking up again the inescapable chain of events, which, as Chrysippus says, once willed and implicates through eternal orders of consequence, there finally stands, in the first state of the world, which immediately argues GOD the author of all, the last ground of events and fertile of so many consequences, which having been posited, others from others are derived into the centuries that will follow, by a stable law always. The worn distinction between absolute and hypothetical necessity, by which as through a crack the adversaries think to slip away, the Distinguished Man attacks: that is, it is of plainly no moment for infringing the force and efficacy of necessity. For what does it matter whether the opposite of an event, precisely determined through antecedent grounds, is representable if it is regarded in itself, since nonetheless this opposite cannot really happen, since the grounds it needs in order to exist are not present, indeed grounds to the contrary are present? You say: the opposite of the event taken separately can yet be thought, and therefore is possible. But what then? It yet cannot happen, because it has been sufficiently provided that it never become actual through grounds already existing. Take an example. Caius has committed a fraud. To Caius, through his primitive determinations, namely insofar as he is a man, sincerity was not repugnant; I grant it. But as he is now determined it is certainly repugnant, since there are present in him grounds which posit the contrary, and sincerity cannot be attributed to him, unless the whole order of implicated grounds be disturbed all the way back to the first state of the world. Now let us hear what the illustrious man further concludes from this. The determining ground does not only bring it about that this action in particular occur, but that another in its place cannot happen. Therefore whatever befalls us, its consequence has been so provided for by GOD that plainly nothing else can follow. Therefore the imputation of our deeds does not pertain to us; but the one cause of all is GOD, who has bound us by these laws so that we in some way fulfil the destined lot. Is it not thus brought about that no sin can displease GOD? which when it happens, he at the same time testifies thereby that the series of implicated things established by GOD admits nothing else. Why then does GOD rebuke sinners for actions which, that they perpetrate them, has been provided for already from the very sowing and origin of the world?",["- *ratio* = ground; *rationem antecedenter determinantem* = \"antecedently determining ground\"; *rationum rationibus* = \"grounds of grounds.\"","- \"once willed and implicates\": *semel voluit et implicat* is printed with mixed tenses and no object; kept as it stands (possibly corrupt or a loose quotation).","- *qui immediate DEum auctorem arguit omnis*: *omnis* is ambiguous (masculine \"of all [men]\" / \"of all [things]\"); rendered \"of all.\"","- *Oppositum ais…* : \"You say\" (*ais*); the clause *separatim sumti euentus* is rendered literally.","- *necessitas absoluta / hypothetica* = \"absolute / hypothetical necessity\"; *actu* = \"actual,\" *possibile* = \"possible,\" *repugnauit* = \"was repugnant.\"","- *Ratio determinans… efficit modo*: *modo* = \"only\" taken with *non*; *imputatio* = \"imputation.\"","- *satu atque ortu*: \"sowing and origin\"; *sortem destinatam*: \"destined lot.\"","- *vtcunque adimpleamus*: \"in some way fulfil,\" ambiguous between \"somehow\" and \"in whatever way.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0060":["Refutation of the doubts.",["- *Confutatio*: \"Refutation\"; *dubiorum*: \"doubts.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0061":["When we distinguish hypothetical necessity, moral in particular, from absolute necessity, the issue here is not the force and efficacy of necessity, namely whether in one case or the other the thing is more or less necessary, but the question is about the necessitating principle, namely whence the thing is necessary. I gladly concede, indeed, that here some followers of the Wolffian philosophy deviate in a certain way from the sense of the true, in that they persuade themselves that what is posited through a chain of grounds determining themselves hypothetically is still somewhat remote from complete necessity, because it lacks absolute necessity. I, however, agree in these matters with the Illustrious antagonist, that the distinction celebrated in everyone's mouth diminishes little the force of necessity and the certitude of determination. For just as nothing is truer than the true and nothing more certain than the certain, so too nothing more determined than the determined can be conceived. Worldly events are so certainly determined that the divine foreknowledge, which cannot be deceived, with equal certitude perceives both their futurity and the impossibility of the opposite by a nexus of grounds in conformity, just as if the opposite were excluded by their absolute concept. Here, however, the hinge of the question is not how greatly but whence the futurity of contingent things is necessary. That the act of the creation of the world in GOD is not ambiguous, but so certainly determined that the opposite is unworthy of GOD, i.e. cannot at all belong to him, who is there who would doubt? Nonetheless the action is free, because it is determined by those grounds which include the motives of his infinite intelligence, insofar as they incline the will with a certainty more certain than certain, and do not proceed from some blind efficacy of nature. So also in the free actions of human beings, insofar as they are regarded as determined, the opposite is indeed excluded, but is not excluded by grounds posited outside the appetite of the subject and its spontaneous inclinations, as if the human being, even unwilling, were driven by some inevitable necessity to perform the actions; but in the very propensity of volitions and appetites, insofar as it willingly yields to the allurements of representations, the actions are determined by a nexus, indeed a most certain one, but a voluntary one, by a fixed law. The difference that intervenes between physical actions and those enjoying moral freedom is not resolved by a difference of nexus and certitude, as if the latter alone, laboring under a doubtful futurity and exempt from the linkage of grounds, enjoyed a vague and ambiguous way of arising; for in this way they would be little commendable among the prerogatives of intelligent beings. But the manner in which the certitude of them is determined by their grounds does the whole page for upholding the mark of freedom; namely, they are elicited only through motives of the understanding applied to the will, whereas on the contrary in brutes, or in physico-mechanical actions, everything is necessitated in conformity with external solicitations and impulses, without any spontaneous inclination of choice. That the power of performing an action is indifferent to either side, but is determined solely by the inclination of pleasure toward the allurements offered by representations, is admitted. The more certainly the nature of the human being is bound to this law, the more it enjoys freedom, and to be borne in a vague effort in every direction toward objects is not to use freedom. \"He acts for no other reason, you say, than that it pleased him so above all.\" Now I hold you bound by your own confession. For what is pleasure except the inclination of the will, made toward this rather than the opposite side in proportion to the allurement of the object? Therefore your \"it pleases\" or \"it is agreeable\" indicates an action determined through internal grounds. For pleasure, in your opinion, determines the action; but it is nothing except the acquiescence of the will in the object, in proportion to the allurement by which it invites the will. Therefore it is a respective determination, in which, if the will is posited as equally allured, for the one to be more agreeable is the same as to please equally and at the same time unequally, which implies a repugnance. A case can happen, however, where the grounds that incline the will to one side or the other altogether escape consciousness, yet nonetheless one or the other is chosen; but then the matter has returned from the superior faculty of the mind to the inferior, and through a preponderance of an obscure representation toward one side or the other (of which we shall make fuller mention in what follows) the mind is directed somewhither.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0062":["Briefly, if it should prove convenient, in a dialogue between Caius, defender of the indifference of equilibrium, and Titius, patron of the determining ground, it may be permitted to illustrate the widely known controversy.",["- Passage 0061 is Latin; I rendered it in the same literal manner. *ratio* (pl. *rationes*) = \"ground(s)\" throughout; *principium* = \"principle\"; *nexus* kept as \"nexus\"; *motiua* = \"motives\".","- *determinare/determinatio* = \"determine/determination\"; *necessitas* = \"necessity\"; *certitudo* = \"certitude\"; *repraesentatio* = \"representation\"; *conceptus* = \"concept\"; *intelligentia* = \"intelligence\"; *intellectus* = \"understanding\".","- \"a veri sensu\": \"the sense of the true\" (*verum* neuter, \"the true\"; could also be \"of truth\").","- \"Illustri antagonistae\": a person is meant but not named in the passage; kept as \"the Illustrious antagonist\".","- \"omnem paginam facit\" is an idiom (\"makes the whole page\"), kept literally; the sense is obscure.","- \"vtramuis partem indifferenter se habere\" / \"Potestatem...\": the construction is kept; the subject of \"habere\" is *potestatem*.","- The quoted objection \"Non aliam ais ob rationem...\" has no quotation marks in the original; I added them. \"ais\" may be a corrupt/OCR reading; kept as \"you say\".","- \"repugnantiam\" rendered \"repugnance\" (*repugnantia*), where \"contradiction\" would translate *Widerspruch* in German.","- \"libertate gaudentes... libet s. volupe\": *libet* and *volupe* rendered \"it pleases\" / \"agreeable\"; *lubitus* = \"pleasure\"; *libertas* = \"freedom\".","- \"inferiorem/superiori mentis facultate\": *facultas* = \"faculty\"; *mens* = \"mind\". \"aliquorsum\" rendered \"somewhither\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0063":["Cajus: The course of my past life agitates me with pangs of conscience, but this one consolation remains, if it is right to believe your tenets, that the guilt of the misdeeds committed does not fall upon me, since, bound by the nexus of grounds determining one another from the very cradle of the world, whatever I have done I could not not have done; and whoever now reproaches me with vices, and in vain berates that another kind of life ought to have been entered upon by me, acts ineptly, just as if he were to demand that I ought to have halted the flow of time. Titius: Out with it! What, then, is that series of grounds by which you complain to have been bound? Did you not do willingly whatever you did? Did not the silent dissuasion of conscience and the fear of God, wrongly admonishing within, clamor against you as you were about to sin? Did it not nonetheless please you more to carouse, to gamble, to sacrifice to Venus, and other things of this kind? Were you ever dragged unwillingly to sinning? Caj. This indeed I by no means deny. I feel well that I was not snatched aside as if by a twisted neck, resisting and strenuously struggling against the allurements. Knowing and gladly I enslaved myself to vices. But this inclination of the will made toward the worse part, whence did it befall me? Was it not, before it happened, when divine and human laws indeed were inviting me, hesitating, to their sides, already determined by the completion of grounds that I should be bent toward the bad part rather than the good? Is it not, a ground being posited already complete in all respects, the same to impede what is grounded as to make the done undone? But any inclination of my will is, in your opinion, perfectly determined by an antecedent ground, and this again by a prior, and in this way all the way to the head of all things. Tit. Now then I shall remove your scruple. The series of implicated grounds supplied, at any juncture of an action to be performed, motives luring on both sides, to either of which you gladly surrendered yourself, because it was pleasant to act thus rather than otherwise. But, you say, it was already determined by the completion of grounds that you should be inclined to the destined part. But I would like you to consider whether the spontaneous propensity of your will according to the allurements of the object is not required for the completed ground of the action. Caj. Beware of saying spontaneous; it could not not incline to this part. Tit. This is so far from removing spontaneity that it rather makes it most certain, provided it is taken in the right sense. For spontaneity is action proceeding from an internal principle. When this is determined conformably to the representation of the best, it is called Freedom. The more certainly each is said to obey this law, and so the more determined he is, with all motives to willing posited, the freer is the man. From your argumentation it does not follow that freedom is infringed by the force of antecedently determining grounds. For the confession that you acted not unwillingly but gladly sufficiently refutes you. Hence your action was not inevitable, as you indeed seem to suspect, for you did not strive to avoid it, but was infallible according to the propensity of your appetite toward circumstances so formed. And this indeed imputes greater guilt to you. For you desired so vehemently that you did not allow yourself to be moved from the purpose. But I shall cut your throat with your own weapon. Out with it! In what way do you think the notion of freedom ought more conveniently to be formed according to your opinion? Caj. I for my part judge that if you drove away whatever there is of the concatenation of grounds determining themselves with a stable outcome, if you conceded that a man in any free action stands indifferently toward both parts, and, with all the grounds however many you may have devised determining toward either being posited, can nevertheless choose anything for anything, then at last I would confess that it is well with freedom. Tit. God forbid! If some deity allowed you to obtain this wish, how unhappy you would be, a man of all hours. Suppose you have resolved in your mind to enter the path of virtue. Suppose the mind is now well fortified both by the precepts of religion and by whatever others are efficacious for confirming a resolution. Now the occasion of acting arrives. Straightway you slip into the worse part, for the grounds that invite you do not determine. How many more complaints I seem to myself to hear you casting! Ah, what hostile fate suddenly drove me from the salutary resolution! What need is there to devote effort to the precepts of virtue; actions happen by lot, they are not determined by grounds! Not indeed, you say, do I accuse the coercion of some fate snatching me unwilling, but that, I know not what, bringing about for me the lapse into the worst part, I abominate. For shame! whence comes to me that detestable appetite precisely toward the worst part, which could equally easily have been inclined to the opposite? Caj. Therefore, concerning all freedom, it is equally all over. Tit. You see into what straits I have driven your forces. Do not conjure up spectres of ideas; for you feel that you are free, but of this freedom do not fashion a notion consisting little with right reason. To act freely is to act conformably to one's appetite and indeed with consciousness. And this indeed is not excluded by the law of the determining ground. Caj. Although I scarcely have what to retort to you, yet the internal sense seems to me to speak against your opinion. For give a case of no great moment: if I am attentive to myself I notice that it is free for me to be inclined to either side, so that I am quite persuaded that the direction of my action was not determined by an antecedent series of grounds. Tit. I shall reveal to you the silent imposture of the mind, which makes a mockery for you of the indifference of equilibrium. The natural appetitive force implanted in the human mind is borne not only toward objects, but also toward various representations to be set before the understanding. Insofar, therefore, as we feel ourselves to be the authors of the representations that contain the motives of choice in a given case, so that we are excellently sufficient for applying, suspending, or turning elsewhere the attention to them, and consequently are conscious that we can not only tend toward objects conformably to our appetite, but also vary the objective grounds themselves in various ways at pleasure, to that extent we can scarcely restrain ourselves from thinking the application of our will exempt from every law and deprived of stable determination. But if we labor to sense rightly, that in a given case this tendency of attention toward the combination of representations becomes no other, why, with grounds enticing from some part, we then, so as to make at least a trial of freedom, by turning attention toward the opposite part, procure for it a preponderance, so that accordingly the appetite is thus and not otherwise directed, we shall easily be convinced that grounds which determine must certainly be present. Caj. I confess you have entangled me in many difficulties, but I am certain you are hindered by no smaller ones. In what way do you think the determinate futurition of evils, of which God is at last the ultimate and determining cause, can be reconciled with His goodness and holiness? Tit. Lest we wear away time in vain disputations, the doubts that keep you in suspense I shall set out in few words, and I shall loosen the knots of the doubts. Since the certainty of all events, physical as well as free actions, is determinate, the consequents in the antecedents, the antecedents in the further preceding, and so by a concatenated nexus always in nearer grounds, until the first state of the world, which immediately argues God as author, is as it were the fountain and spring from which all things are derived in a downward channel by a necessity that knows not how to deceive; hence you think that God is not obscurely designated as the contriver of evil, and that He seems unable to hate the web which He Himself began, and which conformably to His first exemplar is woven through into the future ages of the following era, nor to pursue the sins woven into the work with as much indignation as is right through holiness, since the guilt falls back at last upon the very first contriver of all evils. These are the doubts that press you; now I shall disperse their mists. God, in taking up the beginnings of the universe of things, began a series which, by a stable nexus of grounds connected and woven together, includes also moral evils, and the physical ones which correspond to these. But from that it does not follow that morally depraved actions can accuse God as author. If, as happens in mechanics, intelligent beings were related only passively to those things that impel to certain determinations and changes, I do not deny that the ultimate guilt of all could devolve upon God, the architect of the machine. But what come about through the will of intelligent beings endowed with the power of determining themselves spontaneously have indeed proceeded from an internal principle, from conscious appetites, and from the choice of one part or the other according to the license of the will. Hence, however much, the state of things before free acts having been constituted by some ground, that intelligent being may be implicated in such a nexus of circumstances that it is established and may be foreseen that moral evils will be from it, more certain than certain, nevertheless this futurition is determined by such grounds in which their voluntary direction to the depraved part is the hinge; and because it was therefore most pleasant to the sinners to do these things, they must be called their cause, and to pay the penalty of illicit pleasure agrees most perfectly with equity. But as to the aversion, by which it is without doubt worthy of His holiness that God abhor sins, but which seems little able to stand with the decree of the world founded, in that it included the futurition of these evils, here too the difficulty that surrounds the question is not insuperable. For thus have it.",["- *ratio* (pl. *rationes*) is rendered \"ground\" throughout (Grund); *rationatum* = \"what is grounded\"; *ratio determinans* = \"determining ground\". In \"quanam ratione\" (Tit.'s question) it is rendered \"in what way\", and \"ratio\" in \"rectae rationi\" as \"right reason\"; the German-glossary equivalents are not certain here.","- *libertas* = \"freedom\" (capitalized *Libertas* kept); *spontaneitas* = \"spontaneity\"; *notio* = \"notion\" (not \"concept\", which is kept for *conceptus*); *nexus* = \"nexus\"; *futuritio* = \"futurition\"; *appetitus* = \"appetite\"; *voluntas* = \"will\"; *arbitrium* = \"will\" in \"arbitrii licentiam\" (one English word for two Latin words).","- *conscientia* is \"conscience\" in \"morsus conscientiae\" and \"conscientiae tacita dehortatio\", but \"consciousness\" in \"cum conscientia agere\"; *conscii* = \"conscious\".","- \"perperam intus admonens\": *perperam* = \"wrongly\" (could also be \"perversely\" or \"to no purpose\"); the sense is left open.","- \"conclamatum est\" = \"it is all over\"; \"habeto\" = \"have it\" (imperative), left as in Latin construction.","- \"in citerioribus semper rationibus\": *citerior* rendered \"nearer\"; whether \"nearer\" means earlier or more proximate is left open.","- The sentence beginning \"Verum si recte sentire allaboramus\" is syntactically irregular (*quod ... quare ... quod adeoque*); the construction is kept as it stands, not emended.","- Speaker labels \"Caj.\" and \"Tit.\" are expanded only as \"Caj.\"/\"Tit.\" as in the source; \"Cajus\" retains the Latin spelling."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0064":["The goodness of God tends to the greatest perfection of created things, however much of it falls to them, and to the happiness of the spiritual world. But by the same infinite endeavor to manifest himself, he gave heed not only to the more perfect things, which would afterwards sprout forth in the order of grounds, in series of events, but, lest anything even of the goods of lesser degree be lacking, so that the totality of things by its immensity would embrace all things, so to speak, from the highest degree of perfection that falls to the finite to all lower ones and even to nothing, he permitted to creep into his delineation also those things which, with very many evils mixed in, would furnish at least something of good, which the wisdom of God might elicit therefrom, for distinguishing the manifestation of divine glory by infinite variety. That in this compass the history of the human race, however mournful, not be missed, which, nevertheless, bearing with it infinite testimonies for celebrating the divine goodness even in the very confusion of evils, befitted very well wisdom and power and goodness. Nor, however, is he to be thought, for that reason, to have intended the evils themselves woven into the work begun and to have elicited them deliberately. For he had before his eyes goods, which he cognized would nonetheless remain with the grounds withdrawn, and which to root out together with the unhappy darnel was unworthy of the highest wisdom. For the rest, sin was committed by mortals voluntarily and from the inmost affect of the mind, the order of antecedent grounds not urging and snatching away the unwilling but enticing, to whose incitements, although it was precognized that they would certainly be complied with, yet, since the origin of evils resided in the internal principle of determining themselves, it is plainly evident that it is to be imputed to the sinners themselves. Nor is the divine Numen to be reckoned, for that reason, to abhor sins the less, because by conceding he in a certain way assented to them. For that very compensation of the evils, which license had been given, to be led back to the better by strenuous labor, which he strives to obtain by admonishing, threatening, inviting, furnishing means, is properly that end which the divine artificer had before his eyes, with which, therefore, when he lops the sprouting branches of evils and, as far as can be done with the freedom of human beings saved, checks them, by this very thing he has disclosed himself a hater of all depravity, although a lover of perfections which nonetheless can be elicited therefrom. But I return to the road, having wandered somewhat farther from the reason of the undertaking than was fitting.",["- *ratio* (plural *rationes*) is rendered \"ground\" throughout (cf. *Grund*); *ratio* in \"ab instituti ratione\" is rendered \"reason\".","- *universitas rerum*: \"totality of things\"; the glossary equivalent for *Allheit* is used for the nearest term, though the Latin differs.","- *Numen* left in Latin form, capitalized as in the text; *praecognitum* = \"precognized\".","- \"quibus itaque ... amputet\": the antecedent of *quibus* is unclear (it may be the means, or the preceding clause); the ambiguity is kept in \"with which\".","- \"quorum irritamentis\": *quorum* is plural and the sentence's antecedent is ambiguous (the grounds or the mortals); kept as \"whose\".","- \"licentia facta erat\": \"license had been given\", the literal sense."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0065":["Additions to Problem IX.\nThere is no place for divine foreknowledge with respect to free actions, unless futurition determined by their grounds is admitted.",["- *Praescientia* = \"foreknowledge\"; *futuritio* = \"futurition\" (kept as a technical term).","- *Problema* = \"Problem\" (cf. *Aufgabe*)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0066":["Those who subscribe to our principle have always pressed this argument validly against opponents. Wherefore, dispensing with this labor, I busy myself to respond only to those things which the most perspicacious Crusius brings against it. He objects to those who so think a sentiment unworthy of God, as if they persuade themselves that he uses ratiocinations. In which opinion, if there are any who think otherwise, I gladly pass over to the side of the Ill. adversary. For I concede that the windings of ratiocinations scarcely befit the immensity of the divine understanding. For the infinite intelligence has no need of abstraction of universal notions, and of their combination and comparison made for drawing out consequences. But here we assert that God cannot foresee those things whose futurition is not antecedently determined, not on account of a lack of aids, which we concede he does not at all need: but because the precognition of futurition is impossible in itself, which is plainly none, if existence is wholly, both in itself and antecedently, indeterminate. For that it is indeterminate in itself is concluded from contingency; that it is likewise antecedently indeterminate the antagonists contend, therefore it is necessary that what is plainly devoid of determination, i.e. of futurition, both is so in itself and is represented by the divine understanding.",["- *intellectus* = \"understanding\"; *intelligentia* = \"intelligence\"; *notio* = \"notion\" (not \"concept\", which is kept for *conceptus*).","- *Ill.* left abbreviated as printed.","- *existentia* = \"existence\"; *repraesentari* = \"represented\"; *concluditur* = \"is concluded\".","- \"ergo plane determinationis ... expers\": the grammatical subject of \"est ... & ... repraesentari necesse est\" is left as elliptical as in Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0067":["Finally the praised adversary ingenuously confesses that something incomprehensible remains here, which, however, when contemplation has returned to the infinite, agrees well with the eminence of the object. But however much I may confess that certain inner sanctums of a more recondite intelligence remain never to be unlocked to the human understanding, if you should wish to descend into inner cognition, yet here it is not a matter of the manner, but whether the thing itself has a place, the repugnance of which with the opinion of the opposite party it is indeed quite easy for mortal cognition to inspect.",["- *incomprehensibile* = \"incomprehensible\" (cf. *begreifen*, *comprehendere*); *cognitio* = \"cognition\"; *objectum* = \"object\".","- \"cujus, cum oppositae partis sententia, repugnantiam\": *cum* taken as \"with\" governing *sententia*; *cujus* is read as referring to *res*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0068":["Refutation of the instances which the defenders of the indifference of equilibrium call to their aid.",["- *indifferentia aequilibrii* = \"indifference of equilibrium\"; the genitive is left as in the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0069":["The patrons of the adverse party challenge us to satisfy the examples, which seem to testify so openly to the indifference of the human will to any free actions that scarcely anything seems able to be more open. When odd-or-even is played, and beans hidden in the hand are to be won by guessing, we pronounce one or the other wholly without deliberation and without any ground of choosing. Twins of these they bring forward, in the case of some prince or other, who made a free choice for someone of two boxes, alike in all respects in weight, shape, and species, in one of which he stored lead, in the other gold, where determination to take up either could occur only apart from ground. Similar things they say of the indifferent freedom of moving the right or the left foot. To all I shall respond in one word and, as it indeed seems to me, abundantly. When in our principle there is talk of determining grounds, it is not here understood one or another genus of grounds, e.g. in free actions, grounds hovering before the conscious understanding, but however the action is determined, it is nevertheless necessary that it be determined by some ground, if it is to be done. Objective grounds can be wholly lacking in the determination of choice, and a perfect equilibrium of motives represented with consciousness can be, yet there still remains place for very many grounds, which can determine the mind. For by such a doubtful hesitation this alone is effected, that the matter returns from the higher faculty to the lower, from representation conjoined with consciousness to obscure ones, in which it is scarcely to be maintained that on either side all things are perfectly identical. The tendency of the implanted appetite toward further perceptions does not suffer the mind to stick long in the same state. With the state of internal representations varied, therefore, it is necessary that the mind incline somewhere.",["- *arbitrium* = \"choice\"; *voluntas* = \"will\"; *ratio deligendi* = \"ground of choosing\"; *citra rationem* = \"apart from ground\".","- *genus* = \"genus\"; *species* = \"species\"; *motiua* = \"motives\"; *conscientia* = \"consciousness\" (cf. *Bewußtsein*).","- *ancipiti* = \"doubtful\" (literally two-sided); *perceptiones* = \"perceptions\"; *repraesentationes* = \"representations\"; *status* = \"state\".","- \"Hisce gemina\": \"Twins of these\" is literal; *gemina* is neuter plural."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0070":["Prop. X. To set out some genuine corollaries of the principle of the determining ground.",["- *Prop.* left abbreviated as printed; *principium rationis determinantis* = \"principle of the determining ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0071":["1. Nothing is in the grounded which was not in the ground. For nothing is without a determining ground, and thus nothing in the grounded which does not argue its determining ground:",["- *rationatum* = \"the grounded\"; *ratio* = \"ground\".","- The passage ends with a colon in the source; the continuation is not supplied."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0072":["It could be objected that, since limits adhere to created things, it would follow from this that they likewise adhere to God, who contains the ground of them. I respond: the limits which adhere to finite things argue a likewise limited ground of themselves in the act of divine creation. For the creating action of God is limited, in proportion to the limited being to be produced. But since this action is a respective determination of God, which must correspond to the things to be produced, not internal and absolutely intelligible in himself, it is evident that these limitations do not belong to God internally.",["- *limites* = \"limits\" and *limitationes* = \"limitations\"; neither is marked as *Grenze* or *Schranke*.","- *ens* = \"being\"; *respectiua* = \"respective\"; *determinatio* = \"determination\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0073":["2. Of things that have nothing in common, one cannot be the ground of the other. It returns to the proposition premised.",["- *commune* = \"common\" (cf. *gemein*); *propositio* = \"proposition\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0074":["3. There is not more in the grounded than is in the ground. It is clear from the same rule.",["- *rationatum* = \"the grounded\"; *regula* = \"rule\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0075":["Consectary. The quantity of absolute reality in the world is naturally not changed, neither by increasing nor by decreasing.",["- *consectarium* = \"consectary\", kept distinct from *corollarium* (\"corollary\") in 0070.","- *realitas* = \"reality\"; *quantitas* = \"quantity\" (cf. *Größe*, *Menge*); *mundus* = \"world\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0076":["Elucidation. The evidence of this rule shines forth most easily in the changes of bodies. If e.g. body A, by striking, drives another B, a certain force, consequently reality*, accrues to this. But an equal quantity of motion has been taken away from the impinging body, therefore the sum of forces in the effect is equal to the forces of the cause. In the collision indeed of a smaller elastic body upon a larger, the cited law seems to be held by error. But not at all. For the smaller elastic body, repelled by the larger on which it runs, obtains a certain force in opposite parts, which if added to that which it transferred into the larger, makes indeed a sum greater than the quantity of the impinging body, as is established from Mechanics, but what is here commonly called absolute is more truly to be named respective. For these forces tend in diverse parts; and therefore, estimated from the effects which machines jointly applied, and thus also considered summatively in the universe, can exert, the sum of forces is cognized by subtracting the motions in contrary parts, since to that extent they will in any case at last destroy themselves, and there remains the motion of the center of gravity, which, as known from statics, after the conflict is the same as that which was before it. As to the whole destruction of motion by the resistance of matter, this is so far from lessening the said rule that it rather establishes it. For the force which has arisen from rest by the agreement of causes, by consuming as much as it received in the resistance of the obstacles, is led back again to rest, and the thing remains as before. Hence also the inexhaustible perpetuity of mechanical motion is impossible; for expending always some part of its force on resistances, so that nonetheless the power for restoring itself may remain unimpaired, would likewise be adverse to this rule and to sound reason.",["- *dilucidatio* = \"elucidation\" (cf. *Erläuterung*); *vis* = \"force\"; *potestas* = \"power\"; *vires* = \"forces\"; *causa* = \"cause\"; *effectus* = \"effect\"; *materia* = \"matter\".","- *in partes oppositas / diversas / contrarias*: \"in opposite / diverse / contrary parts\" is literal; *partes* could be read as \"directions\" or \"sides\".","- The asterisk marks the footnote in 0077.","- *vulgo* = \"commonly\"; *respectiua* = \"respective\"; *summatim* = \"summatively\"; *Mechanicis*, *staticis* = \"Mechanics\", \"statics\".","- *sana ratio* = \"sound reason\"; *ratio* is here \"reason\", not \"ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0077":["* Here, according to common sense, it may be permitted to conceive the impressed force as an introduced reality, although properly it is nothing but a certain limitation or direction of implanted reality.",["- *sensus communis* = \"common sense\"; *limitatio* = \"limitation\"; *insitae* = \"implanted\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0078":["Often we see immense forces arise from an infinitely small beginning of cause. A spark thrown into gunpowder, what immense expansive force does it procure? or even, received elsewhere by eager nourishment, what great conflagrations, ruins of cities, and lasting devastations of huge forests does it produce? What a great structure of bodies the very small stimulation of one little spark dissolves; But here, the efficacious cause of immense forces that lies cherished hidden within in the structure of bodies, namely elastic matter, whether of air as in gunpowder (according to the experiments of Hales) or of fiery matter, as in any combustible body, is manifested by the minute stimulation rather than produced. Compressed springs are stored up within, and, stimulated ever so slightly, exert forces proportional to the reciprocal endeavor of attraction and repulsion.",["- *vires* = forces; *causa* = cause; *materia* = matter; *elastra* = \"springs\" (elastic bodies), taken as the plural of *elastrum*.","- *sollicitatio* = \"stimulation\"; *nisus* = \"endeavor\". \"Hales\" is the Latin *Halesius*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0079":["The forces of spirits, certainly, and their progression perennially going on to further perfections seem to be exempt from this law. But, as I at least am persuaded, they are bound to the same. Without doubt the infinite, though quite obscure, perception of the whole universe, which is always at hand internally to the soul, already contains in itself whatever of reality must be in the thoughts to be afterwards suffused with greater light, and the mind, merely by afterwards attending to certain ones, while it withdraws an equal degree from some, illuminating those with more intense light, comes into possession of greater cognition daily, not indeed extending the domain of absolute reality (since the material of all ideas, proceeding from the nexus with the universe, remains the same) but the formal, which consists in the combination of notions and in the attention applied to their diversity or agreement, is certainly variously permuted. Just as we observe similar things in the inherent force of bodies. For motions, if rightly examined, since they are not realities but *phaenomena*, while inherent force, modified by the impact of an external body, since it resists the assault from an internal principle of efficacy just as much as it acquires in the direction of the impelling one of forces, everything real of forces in the *phaenomenon* of motion is equivalent to that which was already inherent in the resting body, although the internal power, which in rest was indeterminate with respect to direction, is merely directed by external impulse.",["- *perceptio* = perception; *cognitio* = cognition; *realitas* = reality; *ambitus* = \"domain\"; *nexus* kept as \"nexus\"; *notio* = \"notion\"; *combinatio* = combination; *gradus* = degree; *vis insita* = \"inherent force\".","- *permutatur* = \"permuted\": *permutatio* is rendered \"permutation\" throughout, *mutatio* \"change\".","- \"afterwards\" (*postmodum*) stands before the participial phrase and could attach to the attending or to the whole clause; left as in the Latin.","- \"of forces\" (*virium*) after \"in the direction of the impelling one\" is left as it stands; the construction is obscure."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0080":["What has been alleged thus far about the unchangeable quantity of absolute reality in the universe must be understood insofar as all things happen according to the order of nature. For through the work of God both the failing perfection of the material world can be restored, light be poured upon intelligences from heaven more purely than nature permits, and all things be raised to a higher summit of perfection, who is there who would dare to doubt.",["- *impermutabili* = \"unchangeable\" (not \"unpermutable\"); *quantitas* = quantity; *perfectio* = perfection.","- The sentence ends without a question mark in the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0081":["Prop. XI. To allege and refute certain spurious corollaries, deduced not very legitimately from the principle of determining ground.",["- *ratio* is rendered \"ground\" throughout (*principium rationis determinantis*, *rationis sufficientis*); *rationatum* = \"the grounded\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0082":["1. That nothing is without something grounded; or, whatever is has its consequence. It is called the principle of consequence. Which, as far as I at least know, acknowledges Baumgarten, the coryphaeus of metaphysicians, as author. By which, because it has been demonstrated by the same reasoning as the principle of ground, it also collapses in equal ruin with that one. The truth of this principle, if talk is only of grounds of cognizing, is safe. For the notion of any being is either general or individual. If the former, what is established of the generic notion is to be conceded to belong to all lower ones comprised under the same, hence that it contains the ground of these. If the latter, the predicates which in a certain nexus belong to this subject, it can be concluded must always belong, the same grounds being posited, and from a given case determines truth in similar ones, hence it has the grounded of cognizing. But if we here understand the grounded of existing, that beings are not fertile in these to infinity, it will be possible to see even from the last section of this commentary, where we will establish with invincible grounds the state of any substance, which is exempt from nexus with others, devoid of all permutation.",["- *ratio cognoscendi* = \"ground of cognizing\"; *rationata existendi* = \"the grounded of existing\"; *consequentia* = \"consequence\"; *notio* = \"notion\"; *ens* = \"being\".","- \"By which\" (*A quo*) has no clear antecedent (Baumgarten, or the principle); left unresolved. The subject of \"determines\" (*determinat*) is likewise unstated.","- The Latin *coryphaeum* is retained as \"coryphaeus\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0083":["2. That none of the things of the whole universe is similar in all respects to another. It is called the principle of the indiscernibles, which, taken in the widest sense, as is done, departs as far as possible from the truth. It is demonstrated chiefly by a twofold reasoning. The former way of arguing quite precipitately leaps over the object with a light jump, and therefore scarcely deserves to come into the reckoning. These are those subtleties: Whatever agrees perfectly in all marks, and is not distinguished by any difference, seems to be held as one and the same being. Hence all perfectly similar things are nothing but one and the same being, to which many places are assigned, which since it is contrary to sound reason, they contend that this proposition fights with itself. But who is there who does not notice the pretense of the subtleties? For the perfect identity of two things, identity of all marks or determinations, internal as well as external, is required. From this all-sided determination, who indeed would except place? And therefore they are not one and the same being, which, however much agreeing in internal marks, are at least distinguished by place. But the demonstration that is put forward, falsely taken from the principle of sufficient ground, must here chiefly be examined by us.",["- *nota* = \"mark\"; *determinatio* = \"determination\"; *objectum* = \"object\"; *sententia* = \"proposition\" here; *ratio sufficiens* = \"sufficient ground\"; *demonstratio* = \"demonstration\".","- *argutiae* = \"subtleties\"; *fucus* = \"pretense\" (literally a dye or drone)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0084":["They say that nothing of ground underlies why God assigned different places to two substances, if in all else they agreed perfectly. What silliness! I wonder that very weighty men are delighted by these rattles of grounds. Call one substance A, the other B. Suppose A occupies the place of B; then, because in internal marks A does not at all differ from B, also holding the place of that one it will be in all respects identical with it, and what was before called A will have to be called B; but that which previously had the name B, now transferred into the place of A, will have to be called A. For this difference of characters notes only a diversity of places. Tell me then whether God did anything else if, according to your opinion, he determined the places? Each is perfectly the same; and therefore the permutation feigned by you is nothing; but that of nothing there is no ground agrees very well in my opinion at least.",["- \"τοῦ B\" and \"τοῦ A\" are Greek genitive articles in the Latin; kept.","- *permutatio* = \"permutation\"; *sententia* = \"opinion\" here (cf. \"proposition\" in the preceding passage); *rationum crepundiis* = \"rattles of grounds\".","- \"Tell me\" renders *Cedo*; the last clause is left as compressed as the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0085":["This spurious law is excellently refuted by the whole universe of things and also by the decorum of divine wisdom. For the bodies that are called *similaria*, water, quicksilver, gold, the simplest salts, etc., agree perfectly in their primitive parts in homogeneous and internal marks, and this suits the identity of use and function which they are destined to perform, and it is to be seen from the effects, which we find always similar proceed from the same without any notable difference. Nor does it befit here to suspect some recondite diversity escaping the sense, as if God had that by which he himself might distinguish the parts of his work, for this would be to seek knots in a bulrush.",["- *similaria* retained in Latin; *partes primitivae* = \"primitive parts\"; *notae* = \"marks\"; *sensus* = \"sense\".","- *argentum viuum* = \"quicksilver\"; the final phrase is the proverb *nodos in scirpo quaerere*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0086":["We concede that Leibniz, the author of this principle, noticed always a notable diversity in the fabric of organic bodies or in the texture of others most remote from simplicity, and could rightly presume it in all of that kind. For neither, where quite many things must agree to compose something, is it clear that equal determinations can always result. Hence of the leaves of the same tree you will scarcely find a perfectly similar pair. But here only the metaphysical universality of this principle is repudiated. Besides, even in the figures of natural bodies it seems scarcely deniable that identity of exemplar is often found. In crystallizations e.g. who is there who would dare to contend that among the infinitely different ones not one and another is found copying another with perfect similarity?",["- *simplicitas* = \"simplicity\"; *determinationes* = \"determinations\"; *universalitas* = \"universality\".","- \"exemplar\" (*exemplaris*) is left as in the Latin; \"v. g.\" is rendered \"e.g.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0087":["Section III.\nTwo principles of metaphysical cognition, disclosing very fertile consequences, flowing from the principle of determining ground.",["- *consectaria* = \"consequences\" (the same English word as for *consequentia*, *Folgerung*)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0088":["I.\nThe principle of succession.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0089":["Prop. XII. No change can befall substances, except insofar as they are connected with others, whose reciprocal dependence determines the mutual change of state.",["- *mutatio* = \"change\"; *status* = \"state\"; *substantia* = \"substance\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0090":["Hence a simple substance exempt from all external nexus, and thus left to itself alone, is by itself plainly unchangeable.",["- *immutabilis* = \"unchangeable\"; *per se* = \"by itself\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0091":["Further, even if involved in nexus with others, if this relation is not changed, no permutation of internal state can happen in it either. In a world therefore devoid of all motion (since motion is the *phaenomenon* of a permuted nexus) nothing at all of succession will be found even in the internal state of substances.",["- *successio* = \"succession\"; *mundus* = \"world\"; *relatio* = \"relation\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0092":["Hence, with the nexus of substances plainly abolished, succession and time likewise vanish.",["- *tempus* = \"time\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0093":["Demonstration.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0094":["Suppose some simple substance, loosed from the nexus of others, exists solitary; I say that no permutation of internal state can befall it. For since the internal determinations which already belong to the substance are posited by internal grounds with exclusion of the opposite, if you want another determination to succeed, another ground must be posited by you, of which, since the opposite is among the internal ones, and no external ground is added, by the suppositions, it is openly clear that it cannot be induced into the being.",["- \"of which\" (*cujus*) and \"the opposite is among the internal ones\" (*oppositum sit in internis*) are left ambiguous as in the Latin; *internis* is neuter plural with no noun supplied.","- *ens* = \"being\"; *ratio* = \"ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0095":["The same otherwise. Whatever is posited by a determining ground must be posited simultaneously with it; for, the determining ground posited, that the grounded not be posited is absurd. Whatever therefore in some state of a simple substance are determining, with those it is necessary that all things whatever determined be simultaneous. But because change is a succession of determinations, or where a certain determination arises which was not before, and thus the being is determined to the opposite of some determination that belongs to it, this cannot happen through those things that are found in the substance intrinsically. If therefore it happens, it is necessary that it proceed from external nexus.",["- *ratio determinans* = \"determining ground\"; *rationatum* = \"the grounded\".","- \"this\" (*haec*) in \"this cannot happen\" agrees with *determinatio* in the Latin; \"it\" in the last sentence is left unspecified as in the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0096":["Still somewhat otherwise. Suppose a change to arise under the conditions named; because it begins to exist when it had not been before, i.e., since the substance was determined to the opposite, and nothing is assumed to come in besides internal things which would determine the substance from elsewhere, by the same grounds by which the substance is held to be determined in a certain way, it will be determined to the opposite, which is absurd.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0097":["*dilvcidatio.*",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0098":["This truth, although it depends on a chain of grounds so easy and incapable of deceiving, those who give their name to the Wolffian philosophy have so little noticed that they rather contend that a simple substance, from an internal principle of activity, is made liable to continual changes. I for my part know their arguments well, but that they are of fig-wood I am no less persuaded. For when they formed an arbitrary definition of force in such a way that it signifies that which contains the ground of changes, whereas it should rather be established that it contains the ground of determinations, it was certainly easy for them to slip into error.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0099":["If anyone should furthermore desire to know in what manner at last the changes, of which a vicissitude is found in the universe, arise, since they do not flow from the internal things of any substance considered solitarily, I would wish him to turn his mind to those things which follow through the nexus of things, i.e., their mutual dependence in determinations. For the rest, because to explain this more fully here would be somewhat too prolix for the bounds of our dissertation, it suffices that it has been asserted by our demonstration that the matter cannot be otherwise.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0100":["*usus.*",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0101":["1. The real existence of bodies, which against the Idealists sounder philosophy has until now been able to defend by no other way than that of probability, I find follows most clearly from the assertions of our principle first of all. Namely the soul is liable to internal changes (through inner sense), which, since they cannot arise from its nature considered solitarily and outside nexus with others, etc., as demonstrated: it is necessary that several things are present outside the soul, with which it is bound up in mutual nexus. Likewise it also appears from the same that the vicissitude of perceptions happens in conformity with external motion, and because it follows from this that we would not have a variously determinable representation of a certain body unless it were really present, whose commerce with the soul would induce in it a representation conformable to itself, it can easily be concluded from this that there is given the composite that we call our body.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0102":["2. It overturns the Leibnizian pre-established harmony from the ground up, not, as mostly happens, through final grounds, which are thought to be unbecoming to GOD, and which not rarely furnish an unstable support, but through its own internal impossibility. For that the human soul, exempted from the real nexus of external things, would be wholly devoid of changes of internal state, follows immediately from what has been demonstrated.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0103":["3. The opinion of attributing some organic body to all finite spirits whatsoever thereby obtains a great document of certainty.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0104":["4. It deduces the essential immutability of God not from the ground of cognizing, which is drawn from his infinite nature, but from the genuine principle of itself. For since the supreme numen, wholly free of all dependence, has the determinations that belong to him established by no external respect at all, that he is wholly void of change of state is abundantly clear from the things asserted.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0105":["*scholion.* To someone the adduced principle could perhaps seem suspect of depravity, on account of the indissoluble nexus by which the human soul, in this way, is bound to matter in carrying out the internal functions of thoughts, which seems not far removed from the pernicious opinion of the materialists. But I do not therefore take away the state of representations from the soul, although I should profess it immutable and always most like itself, if it were wholly released from external nexus. And the quarrel that someone might perhaps try to impose on me I send off to the side of the more recent authors, who with conspiring consent profess, as with one mouth, a necessary linkage of the soul with some organic body. To name one of them as witness, I name the illustrious Crusius, whom I observe going over so much on foot into my opinion that he openly asserts that the soul is bound to that law by which its striving toward representations is always joined with the striving of its substance toward some external motion, and so, this being blown out by impediments, that one too is impeded. But although he does not think this law so necessary that it could not be dissolved with GOD so willing, nevertheless because he concedes that its nature is bound to it, he would have to confess that this too must be created over.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0106":["II.\nPrinciple of Coexistence.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0107":["Prop. XIII. Finite substances, through their existence alone, regard one another by no relations, and are contained in no commerce at all, except insofar as they are sustained, conformed to mutual respects, by the common principle of their existence, namely the divine understanding.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0108":["*demonstratio.* Single substances, of which neither is the cause of the existence of the other, have a separate existence, i.e., one wholly intelligible without all others. Therefore, with the existence of any one posited simply, there is nothing in it that would argue the existence of others different from itself. But since a relation is a respective determination, i.e., not intelligible in a being considered absolutely, this, as well as its determining ground, cannot be understood through the existence of a substance posited in itself. If therefore nothing besides this has been added, there would be no relation among all and no commerce at all. Since therefore, insofar as single substances have an existence independent of others, there is no place for their mutual nexus, and yet it does not befall finite ones to be causes of other substances, nevertheless everything in the universe is found bound together in mutual nexus, it must be confessed that this relation depends on a community of cause, namely GOD, the general principle of existing things. But since from the fact that GOD has established their existence simply, a mutual respect among them does not also follow, unless the same understanding that gives existence has confirmed the respects of them, i.e. the scheme of the divine understanding, insofar as it conceived their existences as correlated, it appears most clearly that the universal commerce of all things is to be credited to the concept of this divine idea alone.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0109":["*dilucidatio.*",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0110":["That the coexistence of the substances of the universe does not suffice to establish a nexus among them, but that a certain communion of origin and a harmonic dependence from this is further required, I seem to myself to have been the first to have established by most evident grounds. For, to resume the sinew of the demonstration a little: If substance A exists, and B exists besides, B can therefore not be deemed to posit anything in A. For suppose it determines something in A, that is, contains the ground of the determination C; because this is a certain relative predicate, not intelligible unless besides B, A is present, substance B, through those things which are the ground of C, will suppose the existence of substance A. But since, if substance B alone exists, it is through its existence wholly undetermined whether some A ought to exist or not, it cannot be understood from its existence alone that it posits anything in others different from itself, hence no relation and no commerce at all. If therefore GOD has created, besides substance A, others B, D, E, to infinity, yet from their given existence their mutual dependence in determinations does not straightway follow. For neither does it follow, because besides A, B, D, E also exist, and A be in itself determined in whatever way, that B, D, E have determinations of existing conformable to this. And so in the manner of the common dependence on GOD there must be present the ground of their mutual dependence too. And by what ground this is brought about is easy to understand. The scheme of the divine understanding, the origin of existences, is a lasting act, which they call conservation, in which, if substances of any kind are conceived by GOD solitarily and without relation of determinations, no nexus among them and no mutual respect will arise; but if they are conceived respectively in his intelligence, then, in conformity with this idea, in the continuation of existence the determinations afterwards always regard one another, i.e., act and react, and there is a certain external state of single ones which, if you depart from this principle, could not be through their existence alone.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0111":["*usus.*",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0112":["1. Since place, situation, space, are relations of substances, by which they regard others really distinct from themselves by mutual determinations, and in this way are contained in external nexus; since further it has become known through what has been demonstrated that the existence of substances alone does not of itself involve nexus with others, it is clear that, if you posit that several substances exist, place and situation and, what is wholly compounded of these relations, space, are not thereby simultaneously determined. But because the mutual nexus of substances requires a delineation of the divine understanding conceived respectively in an efficacious representation, and this representation is wholly arbitrary to God, and so can be admitted at his good pleasure just as omitted: it follows that substances can exist by this law, that they are in no place, and in no relation at all, with respect to the things of our universe.",["- 0096: \"h. e..\" (doubled period) kept as printed; \"nominatis sub conditionibus\" is \"under the conditions named\"; \"determinata/determinabitur\" = determined (*determinare*).","- 0097, 0100, 0109, 0111: bare running section headings (dilucidatio, usus), rendered literally and italicized as Latin.","- 0098: \"ficulnea\" = \"of fig-wood\" (literal; proverbial for flimsy). \"Vis\" rendered \"force\"; \"rationem continet\" = \"contains the ground\" (ratio = ground).","- 0101: \"p. demonstrata\" kept as an abbreviation-like \"etc., as demonstrated\"; the sentence \"quae ... possint, p. demonstrata:\" is syntactically incomplete as printed and left so. \"nexus\" kept in Latin where it is used as a technical noun (\"nexus\").","- 0105: \"transcreari\" rendered \"created over\"; \"sufflato\" = \"blown out\" (literal); \"jugiter\" = \"always\". \"numen\" in 0104 kept as Latin; \"commercium\" rendered \"commerce\" throughout.","- 0108, 0110: \"respectus\" = \"respect\", \"respectiue\" = \"respectively/relatively\" as \"respective(ly)\"; \"relatio\" = \"relation\"; \"intellectus\" = \"understanding\"; \"schema\" kept as \"scheme\". \"τοῦ C\" in 0110 rendered \"of C\" (Greek article retained in sense only).","- 0110: \"sit A quomodocunque in se determinatum\" has subjunctive \"sit\", preserved as \"be\" (ambiguity of sense with the preceding \"quia\" clause left unresolved). \"nexus\" is left untranslated as \"nexus\" for consistency.","- 0112: \"conflatur\" = \"is compounded\"; \"beneplacito\" = \"good pleasure\". \"deo\" is lowercase in the original but capitalized here as \"God\" for consistency."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0113":["2. Since such substances, released from the nexus of our universe, can be several according to divine pleasure, which nonetheless would be bound together among themselves by a certain nexus of determinations, and would hence produce place, situation and space, and would compose a world exempt from the compass of that one of which we are parts, i.e. a solitary one. And that there can be several worlds also in the metaphysical sense, if it should have been pleasing to GOD thus, is by no means absurd.",["- *nexus* left in Latin throughout (German Zusammenhang/Verknüpfung not at issue here); *determinatio* = \"determination\"; *spatium* = \"space\"; *mundus* = \"world\".","- The first sentence has no main verb (\"Quoniam … possunt … sint … efficiant … component\"); it is kept as an unfinished-looking sentence as in the Latin.","- *locum, situm* = \"place, situation\"; *ambitu* = \"compass\" (not in the glossary).","- *quodam nexu colligatae sint* is subjunctive; rendered \"would be bound together.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0114":["3. Since therefore the existence of substances simply is plainly insufficient for mutual commerce and for the relations of determinations, and hence argues by external nexus a common cause of all, in which their existence is respectively informed, and without this community of principle a universal nexus cannot be conceived, there is derived from it the most evident testimony of the highest cause of all things, i.e. of GOD, and indeed of one, which, in my opinion at least, seems far to surpass that demonstration from contingency.",["- *existentia* = \"existence\" (Existenz); *insufficiens* = \"insufficient\" (cf. zureichend = sufficient); *commercium* left as \"commerce\"; *contingentia* = \"contingency.\"","- *respectiue informata sit* is obscure; \"respectively informed\" kept literal. *respectiue* could also be \"relatively.\"","- *principii communione* = \"community of principle\" (Gemeinschaft = community).","- *testimonium* = \"testimony.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0115":["4. The insane opinion too of the Manichaeans, who set over the dominion of the world two principles equally first and in no way dependent on themselves, is torn up from the foundations by our principle. For a substance cannot have any commerce with the things of the universe, unless either it is their common cause, or has proceeded from the same cause with them. And so if you call one of these principles the cause of all substances, the other can in no way determine anything in them; if one of them the cause of some at least, these can have no commerce with the rest. Or you must posit that one of these principles depends on the other, or both on a common cause, which is equally contrary to the hypothesis.",["- *principium* = \"principle\"; *opinio* = \"opinion\" (Meinung); *commercium* = \"commerce\"; *determinare* = \"determine.\"","- *a se haud dependentia*: \"themselves\" is ambiguous in the Latin (each other / themselves); kept as \"themselves.\"","- *dictites* is a rare form (from *dictito*), rendered \"you call\"; the elliptical \"if one of them the cause…\" follows the Latin.","- *statuendum est* rendered \"you must posit\" (statuere; setzen = posit)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0116":["5. Further, since the determinations of substances look back to each other, i.e. substances different from one another act mutually (for one determines some things in another), the notion of space is completed by the implicated actions of substances, with which a reaction is always necessarily joined. Of this universal action and reaction through the whole compass of space in which bodies look to one another, if the external phenomenon is their mutual approach, it is called attraction, which, since it is brought about through mere compresence, reaches to any distances whatever, and is Newtonian attraction or universal gravity; which therefore it is probable is brought about by the same nexus of substances by which they determine space, hence that it is most of all the primitive law of nature to which matter is bound, which endures continually only by GOD as immediate sustainer, according to the very opinion of those who profess themselves followers of Newton.",["- *notio* = \"notion\" (not \"concept,\" which is reserved for *conceptus*/Begriff); *phaenomenon* = \"phenomenon\"; *materia* = \"matter\"; *natura* = \"nature.\"","- *respiciunt* = \"look back to\"/\"look to\"; \"mutual\" for *mutuo*/*mutua*.","- The sentence is syntactically loose in the Latin (\"Cujus actionis … si phaenomenon … sit … dicitur\"; \"quam … probabile est … hinc … esse\"); kept as it stands. The antecedent of \"which\" after \"gravity\" is ambiguous (attraction / gravity).","- *compraesentia* = \"compresence\"; *statore* = \"sustainer\" (lit. \"establisher\"); *sententia* = \"opinion\" here, as in the passage."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0117":["6. Since there is a mutual commerce of all substances, insofar as they are contained in the same space, hence mutual dependence in determinations, the universal action of spirits on bodies and of bodies on spirits can be understood from it. But because any substance does not through those things which belong to it internally have the power of determining others different from itself, (see the demonstration) but only by the force of the nexus by which they are bound together in the idea of the infinite being, whatever determinations and changes are found in any substance always look indeed to external things, but Physical Influx properly so called is excluded, and there is a universal harmony of things. Yet that Leibnizian pre-established one, which induces in substances properly agreement, not mutual dependence, is not thereby generated, for God does not use the artifices of devices adapted in a series of harmonized reasons for effecting the conspiration of substances, nor further is a special influx of GOD always, i.e. a commerce of substances through the occasional causes of Malebranche, posited here, for the same individual action which renders substances existing and conserves them, secures to them mutual and universal dependence, so that it is not necessary for the divine action to be determined now one way, now another, according to circumstances; but it is a real action of substances done on one another, or commerce through truly efficient causes, since the same principle which establishes the existence of things exhibits them bound to this law, hence through those determinations which adhere to the origin of their existence, mutual commerce is established, wherefore by the same right external changes can be said to be produced by efficient causes in this way, by which what happens internally is ascribed to the internal force of the substance, although the natural efficacy of this no less than that foundation of external relations rests on divine sustaining. Meanwhile the system of universal commerce of substances thus formed is certainly somewhat more correct than that commonly received one of Physical Influx, namely disclosing the very origin of the mutual nexus of things to be sought outside the principle of substances considered in solitude, in which the trite system of efficient causes has strayed chiefly from the truth.",["- *vis* = \"force\" (Kraft); *potestas* = \"power\"; *idea* = \"idea\"; *mutatio* = \"change\"; *causa efficiens* = \"efficient cause\"; *Influxus Physicus* kept as capitalized \"Physical Influx.\"","- \"(p. demonstr.)\" is abbreviated in the source; rendered \"(see the demonstration)\" and the expansion is doubtful (perhaps *per demonstrata*).","- *quaelibet substantia … alias a se diuersas*: \"any substance\" and \"others different from itself\" follow the Latin; *determinationes … semper respiciunt externa* literal.","- *artificiorum technis … adaptatis* is obscure and kept awkward; *conspiratio* = \"conspiration.\"","- *quod … existentiam rerum stabilit principium* is rendered literally; *in solitario consideratarum* = \"considered in solitude\" (solitarie).","- *commercium* \"commerce\"; *sustentatio* = \"sustaining.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0118":["Scholion. Behold therefore, kind Reader, two principles of more recondite metaphysical cognition, by whose aid it is permitted to gain possession of a not contemptible dominion in the region of truths. By which method indeed, if this science is skillfully cultivated, its soil will not be found so barren, and the reproach of idle and shadowy subtlety which is hurled at it by despisers will be refuted by an ample harvest of nobler cognition. There are indeed those who, keenest hunters of depraved consequences in writings, are learned in always eliciting some venom from the opinions of others. That these, however, perhaps can twist some things even in these of ours into a worse sense, although I would not deny it, yet having allowed them to abound in their own sense, I think it is my part, not to care that anyone perhaps may like to judge wrongly, but to proceed on the right path of inquiry and doctrine, in which endeavor I ask, with such observance as is fitting, that all who wish well to the liberal letters favor it.",["- *cognitio* = \"cognition\"; *scientia* = \"science\"; *consequentia* = \"consequences\"; *doctrina* = \"doctrine\"; *sententia* = \"opinion.\"","- \"Scholion\" kept as Latin heading, *scholion*, in italics would be proper; given here capitalized as a label.","- *Haud … passus … reor*: the construction (\"having allowed them … I think it my part\") is kept as in the Latin; *non iuerim inficias* = \"I would not deny.\"","- *quod cuipiam … libeat curare* is awkward; \"not to care that anyone … may like to judge wrongly\" follows the Latin order. *quanta decet observantia* = \"with such observance as is fitting.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0119":["The end.",["- *finis* = \"end.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0001":["On the\nCauses of the Earth's Tremors\non the occasion of the misfortune\nwhich\nbefell the western countries of Europe\ntoward the end of the previous year.\nIn: Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten\nof the year 1756, No. 4 of 24 January and No. 5 of 31 January.",["- *Erderschütterungen* = \"tremors of the earth\" (literally \"shakings of the earth\"); the periodical title is left in German."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0002":["Great occurrences, which concern the fate of all human beings, rightly arouse that laudable curiosity which awakens at everything that is extraordinary and is accustomed to ask after the causes of it. In such a case the obligation toward the public should move the investigator of nature to give an account of the insights that observation and investigation can afford him. I forgo the honor of satisfying this duty in its whole domain and leave it to the one, if such a one will arise, who can boast of having thoroughly seen through the interior of the earth. My consideration will be only a sketch. It will, to declare myself freely, contain nearly everything that one can say about it with probability up to now, but admittedly not enough to satisfy that strict judgment which tests everything on the touchstone of mathematical certainty. We dwell peacefully on a ground whose foundation is at times shaken. We build unconcerned on vaults whose pillars here and there waver and threaten collapse. Unconcerned about the fate which is perhaps not far from ourselves, we give place to pity instead of fear, when we become aware of the devastation which the ruin that hides itself beneath our feet works in the neighborhood. It is without doubt a benefaction of providence to be unassailed by the fear of such fates, to the prevention of which all possible anxiety cannot contribute the least, and not to magnify our actual suffering through fear of that which we cognize as possible.",["- *Begebenheit* = \"occurrence\"; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration\"; *Beobachtung* = \"observation\"; *Beurteilung* = \"judgment\"; *Wahrscheinlichkeit* = \"probability\"; *erkennen* = \"cognize\"; *Umfang* = \"domain\"; *Absicht* not in this passage.","- \"und unter wirkliches Leiden nicht … zu vergrößern\" is syntactically defective (\"unter\" for \"unser\"?); rendered \"and not to magnify our actual suffering,\" a doubtful reading.","- *Gewölbern* (sic) = \"vaults\"; *Hintertreibung* = \"prevention\"; *Gewißheit* = \"certainty.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0003":["The first thing that presents itself to our attention is that the ground over which we find ourselves is hollow and its vaults run on almost in an interconnection through widely stretched regions even under the floor of the sea. I cite no examples from history on that account; my intention is not to deliver a history of earthquakes. The frightful din which, like the raging of an underground storm wind, or like the driving of heavy wagons over stone pavement, has been heard in many earthquakes, the effect of them continued at the same time into widely extended countries, of which Iceland and Lisbon, which are separated by a sea of more than three and a half hundred German miles and were set in motion on one day, give an undeniable testimony, all these appearances agree in this, to confirm the interconnection of these underground vaults.",["- *Zusammenhang* = \"interconnection\"; *Absicht* = \"intention\"; *Erscheinung* = \"appearance\"; *Wirkung* = \"effect\"; *Beyspiele* = \"examples.\"","- \"4tehalb hundert\" (i.e. *viertehalb*, three and a half) rendered \"three and a half hundred\"; \"mehr wie\" = \"more than.\"","- The long sentence beginning \"Das fürchterliche Getöse\" lacks a grammatical link before \"die in weit ausgedehnte Länder … fortgesetzte Wirkung\"; kept as it stands. Whether \"die … Wirkung\" is a second subject or parallel to \"Getöse\" is ambiguous.","- \"dieselben/derselben\" refers back to the earthquakes (*Erdbeben*), left as \"them.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0004":["I would have to go back as far as the history of the earth in chaos, if I were to say something comprehensible about the cause which, in the formation of the earth, occasioned the origin of these caverns. Such explanations have only far too much the appearance of fictions, if one cannot present them in the whole domain of the grounds that contain their credibility. The cause may be however which it will, so it is yet certain that the direction of these caverns is parallel to the mountains and, through a natural interconnection, also to the great rivers; for these occupy the lowest part of a long valley, which is bounded on both sides by mountains running parallel. It is just the same direction also according to which the earthquakes chiefly spread. In the earthquakes that have extended through the greater part of Italy, a motion from north almost straight to south has been perceived in the chandeliers in the churches; and this recent earthquake had the direction from west to east, which is also the main direction of the mountains that run through the highest part of Europe.",["- Begreifliches (from begreifen) = \"comprehensible\"; Erklärungen = \"explanations\" (glossary offers \"explanation; definition; declaration\").","- Umfang = \"domain\"; Gründe = \"grounds\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; wahrgenommen = \"perceived\" (cf. Wahrnehmung, perception).","- \"Die Ursache mag aber seyn, welche sie wolle\" rendered literally; \"Theil\" (neuter, \"das unterste Theil\") = \"part\".","- \"Richtung\" = \"direction\"; Erderschütterungen and Erdbeben both appear (earth-shakings / earthquakes); kept distinct."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0005":["If in such terrible contingencies it is permitted to human beings to use some precaution, if it is not regarded as a rash and futile effort to set against universal afflictions some arrangements that reason offers, should the unfortunate remnant of Lisbon not hesitate to build itself up again along its length on the same river, which designates the direction in which the earth-shaking in this country must naturally occur? Gentil *) testifies that, if a city is shaken along its greatest length by an earthquake that has the same direction, all the houses are overturned, whereas, if the direction occurs in the breadth, only a few fall down. The cause is clear. The swaying of the ground brings the buildings out of the perpendicular position. If now a row of buildings from east to west is set in oscillation in this way, then not only does each have to bear its own load, but the western ones press at the same time on the eastern ones and thereby throw them unfailingly into a heap, whereas, if they are moved in the breadth, where each has only to maintain its own equilibrium, under equal circumstances less damage must occur. The misfortune of Lisbon therefore seems to have been magnified by its situation, which it had along the bank of the Tagus in its length; and according to these grounds every city in a country where earthquakes have been felt several times, and where one can take their direction from experience, ought not to be laid out in a direction that runs parallel with this. But in such cases the greatest part of human beings are of quite another opinion. Because fear robs them of reflection, they believe they perceive in such universal misfortunes a quite different kind of evil from those against which one is entitled to use precaution, and imagine they mitigate the harshness of fate through a blind submission, with which they leave themselves to it at discretion.",["- Zufällen = \"contingencies\" (cf. zufällig = contingent); Vernunft = \"reason\"; Gründen = \"grounds\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Meynung = \"opinion\" (Meinung); Nachdenken = \"reflection\" (not Überlegung); Art = \"kind\".","- \"auf Gnade und Ungnade\" rendered \"at discretion\" (idiom, literally \"at mercy and disfavor\").","- \"demselben Flusse seiner Länge nach\": \"seiner\" ambiguous between the city (Lisbon's) and the river's length; preserved as \"along its length\".","- \"Gentil *)\": footnote marker kept; footnote follows as next passage."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0006":["*) Gentil's Voyage around the World, according to Buffon's citation. The same also confirms that the direction of earthquakes runs almost always parallel to the direction of great rivers.",["- Footnote to 0005. \"Anführung\" = \"citation\"."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0007":["The main course of earthquakes proceeds in the direction of the highest mountains, and those countries are therefore chiefly shaken that lie near these, especially if they are enclosed between two rows of mountains, in which case the shakings unite from both sides. In a flat country, which does not stand in an interconnection with mountains, they are rarer and weak. For that reason Peru and Chile are those countries that are subject, almost above all in the world, to the most frequent shakings. There one observes the precaution of building the houses of 2 stories, of which only the lowest is of masonry, but the uppermost is made of reed and light wood, so as not to be struck dead beneath them. Italy, indeed even the island of Iceland, situated partly in the frigid zone, and other high regions of Europe prove this agreement. The earthquake which in the month of December of the past year spread from evening toward morning through France, Switzerland, Swabia, Tyrol and Bavaria, kept chiefly to the course of the highest regions of this part of the world. But one also knows that all main mountain ranges shoot out collateral branches crosswise. Into these the subterranean ignition also spreads gradually, and in consequence of this, after it had arrived at the high regions of the Swiss mountains, it also ran through the caverns that continue parallel to the Rhine stream as far as Lower Germany. What may be the cause of this law, with which nature connects earthquakes chiefly to the high regions? If it is established that a subterranean ignition causes these shakings, one can easily judge that, because the caverns in mountainous regions are more extensive, the exhalation of combustible vapors there will be freer, and also the community with the air enclosed in the subterranean regions, which is always indispensable for ignitions, more unhindered. Besides this, knowledge of the inner constitution of the soil of the earth, so far as it is permitted to human beings to discover it, teaches that the strata in mountainous regions lie by far not so high as in flat countries, and the resistance to the shaking is therefore less there than here. If one therefore asks whether our fatherland too has cause to fear these misfortunes, then, if I had the calling to preach the improvement of morals, I would leave the fear of them in its worth on account of the universal possibility, which one certainly cannot deny here; but now, since among the motives of godliness those that have been taken from earthquakes are without doubt the weakest, and my aim is only to adduce physical grounds for conjecture, one will easily be able to take from what has been adduced that, since Prussia is not only a country without mountains but must also be regarded as a continuation of an almost thoroughly flat country, one has a greater occasion to console oneself, from the arrangements of providence, with the opposite hope.",["- Bewegungsgründen = \"motives\" (glossary); Absicht = \"aim\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Kenntniß = \"knowledge\" (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\".","- \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" kept literal (\"from evening toward morning\" = west to east).","- \"Zusammenhange\" = \"interconnection\"; \"Erschütterungen\" = \"shakings\"; \"Entzündung\" = \"ignition\"; \"Schichten\" = \"strata\".","- \"so würde ich … in ihrem Werthe lassen\" and \"dem entgegen gesetzten Hoffnung\" kept close to German; \"der entgegen gesetzten Hoffnung\" (dative) is grammatically awkward in the German, preserved. \"Anstalten der Vorsehung\" = \"arrangements of providence.\"","- \"nicht in Abrede seyn kann\" is an irregular construction (\"cannot be in denial\"); rendered \"cannot deny.\"","- \"Weltheils\" = \"part of the world\"; \"diejenige Länder\" plural as in the source."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0008":["It is time to adduce something about the cause of the earth-shakings. It is something easy for a student of nature to imitate their appearances. One takes 25 pounds of iron filings, just as much sulfur, and mixes it with common water, buries this paste one or one and a half feet deep in the earth and presses the earth firmly together over it. After the lapse of some hours one sees a thick vapor rise, the earth is shaken, and flames break forth from the ground. One cannot doubt that the two former materials are frequently met with in the interior of the earth, and the water, which seeps through clefts and crevices of rocks, can bring them into fermentation. Yet another experiment yields combustible vapors from the mixture of cold materials, which ignite of themselves. Two drams of oil of vitriol, mixed with 8 drams of common water, when one pours them on 2 drams of iron filings, bring forth a violent effervescence and vapors that ignite of themselves. Who can doubt that the vitriolic acid and iron parts are contained in the interior of the earth in sufficient quantity? If the water now comes to this and occasions their reciprocal effect, they will expel vapors that strive to spread, shake the ground and break out in flames at the openings of fire-spewing mountains.",["- Erscheinungen = \"appearances\" (glossary: Erscheinung); Naturforscher = \"student of nature\"; Materien = \"materials\" (Materie = matter; plural rendered \"materials\"); Wirkung = \"effect\"; Menge = \"quantity\" (glossary offers \"multitude; multiplicity; amount\").","- \"Eisenfeilig\" (also \"Eisenfeil\") = \"iron filings,\" OCR/original form as printed; \"Quentchen\" = \"dram\" (a unit of weight).","- \"dieselbe\" in \"stößt dieselbe darüber fest zusammen\" refers to the earth (Erde); kept as \"the earth\" for English grammar.","- \"beyden erstere\" = \"the two former\"; \"hierzukommt\" = \"comes to this.\"","- \"gegenseitige Wirkung\" = \"reciprocal effect\"; \"feuerspeyender Berge\" = \"fire-spewing mountains.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0009":["It has long since been perceived that a country has been freed from its violent shakings when in its neighborhood a fire-spewing mountain has erupted, through which the enclosed vapors can gain an outlet, and one knows that around Naples the earthquakes are far more frequent and fearful when Vesuvius has been quiet for a long time. In this way that which puts us in terror often serves us as a benefit, and a fire-spewing mountain that would open itself in the mountains of Portugal could become a harbinger that the misfortune was gradually removing itself.",["- *feuerspeyend* rendered \"fire-spewing\"; *Vesuv* given as \"Vesuvius\" (no article in the German).","- *Begebenheit*, *Erschütterung*, *Unglück*: \"shaking(s)\" for *Erschütterungen*; \"misfortune\" for *Unglück*. *entfernete* (subjunctive) kept as \"was removing itself.\"","- *Wohlthat* = \"benefit.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0010":["The violent movement of the water that was sensed on the unfortunate All Saints' Day on so many sea coasts is, in this occurrence, the strangest object of admiration and investigation. That the earthquakes extend even beneath the sea floor and put ships into as violent a shaking as if they were fastened on a hard shaken ground is a common experience. But in the regions where the water fell into upheaval there was no trace of any earthquake, at least it was not to be felt at all at a moderate distance from the coasts. Nevertheless this movement of the water is not entirely without example. In the year 1692, with an almost general earthquake, something of the kind was also perceived on the coasts of Holland, England and Germany. I hear that many are inclined, and indeed not without ground, to derive this upwelling of the waters from a continued shaking that the sea on the Portuguese coasts has received through the immediate impact of the earthquake. This explanation seems at first to be exposed to difficulties. I do comprehend that in a fluid being every pressure must become sensible through the whole mass, but how could the pressings of the waters of the Portuguese sea, after they had spread out some hundred miles, still set the water at Glückstadt and Huysum in motion some feet high? Does it not seem that mountains of water as high as the heavens would have had to arise there, in order to excite here scarcely noticeable waves? I answer to this: there are two kinds of way in which a fluid being can be set in motion in its whole mass by a cause that acts in one place, either by the swaying movement of rising and falling, i.e. in a wave-shaped way, or by a sudden pressure that shakes the mass of water in its interior and drives it forward as a solid body, without leaving it time to evade the pressure through a swaying upwelling and to spread its movement gradually. The former is without doubt not capable of sufficing for the explanation of the occurrence cited. But as to what concerns the latter, if one considers that water resists a sudden violent pressure like a solid body and spreads this pressing to the side with the very same violence, which does not leave the adjacent water time to raise itself above the level state, if one considers, e.g., the experiment of Herr Carree in the 2nd part of the physical treatises of the Acad. of Sciences pag. 549, who fired a musket ball into a chest that was put together from two-inch boards and filled with water, which by its blow pressed the water so that the chest was entirely burst apart, then one will be able to make for oneself some concept of this way of moving water. One may imagine, e.g., that the whole western coast of Portugal and Spain from Cape St. Vincent to Cape *Finis terrae* was shaken about 100 German miles wide, and that this shaking extended just as far into the sea westward; then 10,000 German square miles of the sea floor were raised with a sudden heaving, whose speed we do not estimate too high if we posit it equal to the movement of a powder mine that throws the bodies lying on it 15 feet high, and hence is in a position (according to the grounds of mechanics) to cover 30 feet in a second. To this sudden shaking the water lying on top resisted in such a way that it did not, as happens with slow movements, give way and swell up in waves, but received its whole pressure and drove the surrounding water just as violently to the side, which with so quick an impression is to be regarded as a solid body, of which the distant end advances with just the same speed as the struck end is driven forward. Thus in every beam of the fluid matter (if I may make use of this expression), even though it is 200 or 300 miles long, there is no diminished movement, if it were thought as enclosed in a canal that has at the distant end just as wide an opening as at the beginning. But if the former is wider, the movement through it will diminish inversely just so much. Now, however, one must think the continuation of the movement of the water round about as spread out in a circle, whose enlargement increases with the distance from the center, at whose boundary the flowing forth of the water is therefore diminished in just the same measure; hence on the Holstein coasts, which are situated 300 German miles from the assumed center of the shaking, it will be found 6 times smaller than on the Portuguese, which according to the presupposition have a distance of about 50 miles from the very same point. The movement on the Holstein and Danish coasts will therefore still be great enough to run through 5 feet in a second, which equals the force of a very swift stream. Against this one could raise the objection that the continuation of the pressure into the waters of the North Sea could only take place through the canal at Calais, whose shaking, in that it is spread out into a wide sea, must be uncommonly weakened. But if one considers that the pressure of the water between the French and English coasts, before it reaches the canal, must increase through the pressing between these countries just as much as it is afterward diminished by the spreading out, then nothing considerable can thereby be withdrawn from the effects of the shaking on the said Holstein coasts.",["- *Begebenheit* = \"occurrence\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\"; *Erfahrung* = \"experience\"; *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Erklärung* = \"explanation\"; *Ausdruck* = \"expression\"; *Wirkungen* = \"effects\"; *Grund* = \"ground\" (also *Gründen der Mechanik*); *Wesen* = \"being\" (*flüssiges Wesen*).","- *begreifen* = \"comprehend\"; *Zeit* = \"time\"; *Stand* kept as \"state\" in *wagrechten Stand*; *Materie* = \"matter.\"","- *zweyerley Art* = \"two kinds of way\" (*Art* = way/kind, left double).","- \"The former ... the latter\" for *Die erstere ... die letztere*. \"if the former is wider\": German has *jene*, whose antecedent is unclear (the far opening, *Eröffnung*, is the likely candidate; the German does not say); kept as \"the former.\"","- \"will diminish inversely just so much\" for *umgekehrt gerade um so viel sich vermindern*; construction kept as it stands.","- *Quadratmeilen*, *Meilen* = \"square miles,\" \"miles\" (German miles); *gringer* (sic) read as *geringer*; *wurden* (for *würden*/*wurden*) rendered as past tense \"were raised\"; doubtful.","- *Capo Finis terrae* given with Latin in italics; *z. E.* = \"e.g.\"; *Acad. der Wissensch.* and *pag.* kept abbreviated; *Carree* spelled as in the text.","- *Fortsetzung* and *Fortsetzung des Druckes*: \"continuation\"; *Drückungen/Druck/Pressung* = \"pressings/pressure/pressing.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0011":["In this pressing of the waters the most singular thing is that it has been sensed even in inland lakes that have no visible interconnection with the sea, at Templin and in Norway. This seems almost to be the strongest of all the proofs that have ever been put forward to prove the subterranean community of the midland waters with the sea. In order to wind oneself out of the difficulty that can be made against it from equilibrium, one would have to imagine that the water of a lake really flowed constantly downward through the canals by which it is connected with the sea, but because these are narrow, and what they lose thereby is sufficiently replaced by the brooks and streams that flow in, this outflow could not on that account become noticeable.",["- *Zusammenhang* = \"interconnection\"; *Gemeinschaft* = \"community\"; *Beweis* = \"proof\"; *Gleichgewicht* = \"equilibrium.\"","- *so könne dieser Abfluß ... nicht merklich werden*: the subjunctive *könne* (indirect speech continuing *müßte ... vorstellen*) is rendered \"could not\"; *um deswillen* = \"on that account.\"","- *mittelländischen Gewässer* = \"midland waters\" (possibly \"inland\")."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0012":["Although in so strange an occurrence one should not readily pass a hasty judgment. For it is not impossible that the excitation of the inland lakes could also have come from other grounds. The subterranean air, set in motion by the eruption of this raging fire, could well force itself through the fissures of the layers of earth, which, apart from this violent expansion, close off to it all passage. Nature discloses itself only little by little. One should not, through impatience, seek to counsel away by invention what it conceals from us, but wait until it reveals its secrets undoubtedly in distinct effects.",["- *Urtheil* = \"judgment\"; *Grund* = \"ground\"; *deutlich* = \"distinct\"; *Begebenheit* = \"occurrence\"; *Wirkungen* = \"effects.\"","- The first sentence is a subordinate-clause fragment (*Wiewohl ...*) in the German; kept as such.","- *ihr durch Erdichtung abzurathen suchen*: \"seek to counsel away [it, i.e. what nature conceals] by invention\"; the dative *ihr* (to nature) and the sense of *abrathen* are left as obscure as in the German. *allen Durchgang* referent of *ihr* (the air) kept as \"to it.\"","- *Ausspannung* = \"expansion.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0013":["The cause of the earthquakes seems to extend its effect as far as the atmosphere. Some hours before the earth is shaken, one has often perceived a red sky and other marks of an altered constitution of the air. The animals are shortly beforehand quite seized with terror. The birds flee into the houses; rats and mice crawl out of their holes. At this moment the heated vapor, which is on the point of igniting, infallibly breaks through the upper vault of the earth. I do not dare to determine what effects one has to expect from it. At the least they are not agreeable to the student of nature, for what hope can he make for himself of getting behind the laws according to which the alterations of the atmosphere alternate with one another, if a subterranean atmosphere mixes itself into their effects, and can one well doubt that this must happen not seldom, since otherwise it would hardly be comprehensible how, in the change of the weather, since the causes of it are partly constant, partly periodic, no recurrence at all is encountered?",["- Luftkreis rendered \"atmosphere\" and Atmosphäre also \"atmosphere\" (one English word for two German words).","- Merkmale = marks (nota); Wirkung = effect; Beschaffenheit = constitution; begreiflich = \"comprehensible\" (cognate of begreifen, comprehend).","- \"vor\" is used for \"für\"/\"von\" (vor den Naturforscher, vor Wirkungen, vor Hoffnung); rendered \"to,\" \"what ... effects,\" \"what hope\" as the syntax allows.","- \"nicht öfters\" in \"daß dieses nicht öfters geschehen müsse\" is rendered \"not seldom\"; the negation is ambiguous in the German and the English preserves this.","- Ratzen = rats (old spelling); ohnfehlbar = \"infallibly.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0014":["Remark. The day of the earthquake in Iceland is to be corrected in the previous issue, in place of the 1st of Nov., to the 11th of Septembr., according to the report in issue 199 of the Hamb. Corresp.",["- Stück rendered \"issue\"; \"Relation\" rendered \"report\"; abbreviations (Septembr., Hamb. Corresp.) kept as in the original."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0015":["The present considerations are to be regarded as a small preliminary exercise on the memorable natural occurrence that has happened in our days. The importance and manifold particularities of the same move me to communicate to the public, in a more detailed treatise, a detailed history of this earthquake, the spread of the same over the countries of Europe, the noteworthy matters occurring with it, and the considerations to which they can give occasion, which will appear in a few days at the Royal Court and Academy Printing House.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Begebenheit = occurrence; mannigfaltig = manifold; Publico (Latin dative form in German text) = \"public.\"","- \"desselben\" (twice) is rendered \"of the same\"; its referent (Naturbegebenheit or Erdbeben) is ambiguous in the German as in the English.","- \"Hof- und Akad. Buchdruckerey\" = \"Court and Academy Printing House,\" abbreviation Akad. expanded."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0001":["History\nand\nNatural Description\nof the Most Noteworthy Incidents\nof the\nEarthquake\nwhich at the End of the Year 1755\nhas shaken\na Great Part of the Earth\nby\nM. Immanuel Kant.\nKönigsberg, printed and published by Joh. Heinrich Hartung 1756.",["- Naturbeschreibung = \"natural description\"; Vorfälle = \"incidents\"; M. = Magister, kept as \"M.\"","- Line breaks of the title page kept."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0002":["Nature has not in vain spread out everywhere a treasure of rarities for consideration and admiration. The human being, to whom the household of the earth's surface is entrusted, possesses capacity, he possesses also desire to get to know them and praises the Creator through his insights. Even the fearful instruments of the visitation of the human race, the shakings of the countries, the fury of the sea moved in its ground, the fire-spewing mountains, summon the human being to consideration, and are no less planted by GOD in nature as a correct sequel from constant laws than other already customary causes of discomfort, which one holds to be more natural only because one is more acquainted with them.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Fähigkeit = capacity; Grund = ground (here of the sea); Gesetz = law; Folge = sequel; Ursache = cause.","- kennen lernen = \"get to know\"; bekannt = \"acquainted\" (cf. kennen = know).","- Erdboden rendered \"earth's surface\" in \"Haushaltung des Erdbodens\" and Erdbodens elsewhere \"earth\"; \"Einsichten\" = \"insights\"; \"vor natürlicher\" = \"more natural\" (vor for für).","- \"sie\" in \"sie kennen zu lernen\" has an unclear referent (Seltenheiten or the Natur); the English \"them\" preserves this."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0003":["The consideration of such terrible contingencies is instructive. It humbles the human being in that it lets him see he has no right, or at the least he has lost it, to expect from the laws of nature that GOD has ordained nothing but convenient consequences, and he learns perhaps also in this way to see: that this tumbling-ground of his desires fairly should not contain the goal of all his aims.",["- Zufälle = \"contingencies\" (cf. zufällig = contingent); Absichten = \"aims\"; Folgen = \"consequences\" (Folge = sequel in glossary; plural here rendered \"consequences\"; Folgerung would also be \"consequence\").","- \"billig\" rendered \"fairly\"; \"Tummelplatz\" = \"tumbling-ground\"; \"Begierden\" = \"desires.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0004":["Preparation\nOn the Constitution of the Earth's Surface in its\nInterior.",["- Beschaffenheit = constitution; Erdbodens = \"Earth's surface\" (literally ground of the earth); inwendigen = \"interior.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0005":["We know the surface of the earth, when it comes to extent, fairly completely. But we still have a world under our feet, with which we are at present very little acquainted. The mountain fissures, which open to our plumb-line unfathomable chasms, the caves that we encounter in the interior of the mountains, the deepest shafts of the mines that we enlarge through centuries, are by far not sufficient to procure for us distinct knowledge of the inner structure of the great lump that we inhabit.",["- kennen = know; bekannt = acquainted; Kenntnisse = \"knowledge\" (plural; glossary Kenntnis = acquaintance, information, knowledge); deutlich = distinct; zureichend = sufficient; Welt = world.","- Weitläuftigkeit = \"extent\" (not Ausdehnung); Senkbley = \"plumb-line\"; Bau = \"structure.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0006":["The greatest depth to which human beings have descended from the uppermost surface of the firm land amounts to not yet 500 fathoms. I.e. not yet the six-thousandth part of the distance to the center of the earth and nonetheless these vaults still find themselves in the mountains, and all firm land itself is a mountain, in which, in order only to arrive at the same depth at which the sea-bottom lies, one would have to descend at least three times deeper.",["- Klafter = \"fathoms\"; \"D. i.\" = \"I.e.\" (das ist); sechstausensten (sic for sechstausendsten) = \"six-thousandth.\"","- \"festen Landes\" = \"firm land\"; Grüfte = \"vaults\" (also used for Hölen); \"seyn\" in \"hinabgekommen seyn\" is a subjunctive (reported) form, rendered \"have descended.\"","- Sentence structure with its dangling \"um nur zu gleicher Tiefe ... zu gelangen\" kept; \"Meeres-Grund\" = \"sea-bottom\" (Grund otherwise ground)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0007":["But what nature hides from our eye and our immediate experiments, that it itself discovers through its effects. The earthquakes have revealed to us that the surface of the earth is full of vaults and caves, and that beneath our feet hidden mines with manifold labyrinths run onward everywhere. The sequel in the history of the earthquake will place this beyond doubt. These caves we have to ascribe to the very same cause that prepared for the seas their bed; for it is certain, if one is only to some extent informed of the remnants that the world-sea has left behind from its former abode over the entire firm land, of the immeasurable heaps of shells that are encountered even in the interior of the mountains, of the petrified sea-animals that one brings out of the deepest shafts, I say, if one is informed of all this only to some extent, one will easily see that first the sea formerly covered all land for a long time, that this abode lasted long and is older than the Flood, and that finally the waters could not possibly have drawn back otherwise than that the floor of the same sank down here and there into deep vaults and prepared for the same deep basins, in which it drained off, and between whose shores it is still kept confined, while the elevated regions of this sunken crust have become firm land, which is everywhere undermined with hollows, and whose stretch is set with the steep peaks that under the name of mountains run through the uppermost height of the firm land in all those directions in which it extends over a considerable length.",["- Versuche = \"experiments\"; Wirkungen = \"effects\"; Ursache = \"cause\"; Verfolg = \"sequel\" (Folge also \"sequel\" in the glossary; here one English word for two German words).","- Zweifel = \"doubt\"; Hölen and Hölungen = \"caves\" and \"hollows\"; Wölbungen, Grüfte = \"vaults\" (one English word for both).","- \"demselben\" (twice) = \"the same\"/\"for the same,\" referent (Meer or Wasser/Gewässer) ambiguous; \"deßelben\" = \"of the same.\"","- \"Sündfluth\" = \"Flood\"; \"Weltmeer\" = \"world-sea\"; \"Aufenthalt\" = \"abode\"; \"unter den Nahmen der Gebirge\" = \"under the name of mountains.\"","- \"wenn man ... unterrichtet ist\" follows \"ich sage,\" with a resumptive clause; the anacolouthon kept. \"daß ... als daß\" kept as in the German."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0008":["These caves all contain a blazing fire, or at least that combustible stuff which needs only a slight stimulation in order to rage around itself with violence and to shake or even split the ground above it.",["- Hölen = \"caves\"; Zeug = \"stuff\"; Reizung = \"stimulation\"; Boden = \"ground.\"","- \"um sich\" = \"around itself\" kept literal; \"über sich\" = \"above it\" (literally \"above itself\")."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0009":["If we consider the domain of this subterranean fire in the whole extent to which it extends, we will have to admit that there are few countries on the earth's surface that have not sometimes sensed its effect. In the extreme north the island of Iceland is subject to the most violent attacks of it, and indeed not seldom. In England and even in Sweden some slight tremors have been had. Nevertheless they are found in the southern countries, I mean those that lie nearer to the equator, more frequently and more strongly. Italy, the islands of all seas that lie near the middle line, chiefly those in the Indian Ocean, are frequently assailed by this disquieting of their floor. Among the latter there is almost not a single one that does not have a mountain that either still at present sometimes spewed fire, or at least had formerly done so, and they are subject to the tremor just as frequently. It is a pretty precaution, if one may believe the report of Hübner in this, that the Dutch employ for this reason, in order not to expose to the danger of being exterminated from the earth the costly spice of nutmegs and cloves, which they permit to be propagated solely and alone on the two islands Banda and Amboina, should one of these islands perhaps be met by the fate of a complete downfall through an earthquake, that they always maintain on another, far distant from there, a nursery of both plants. Peru and Chile, which lie near the line, are troubled with this evil more frequently than any country in the world. In the first country almost no day passes by on which some slight shocks of earthquake are not sensed. One must not imagine that this is to be regarded as a sequel of the far greater heat of the sun that acts upon the soil of these countries. In a cellar that is hardly 40 feet deep almost no difference between summer and winter is to be sensed. So little is the warmth of the sun capable of penetrating the soil to great depths, in order to lure the inflammable material and to set it in motion. Rather the earthquakes direct themselves according to the constitution of the subterranean caverns, and these according to that law according to which the subsidences of the uppermost earth-crust must have occurred in the beginning, and which, the nearer to the line, have made the deeper and more manifold depressions, whereby these mines, which contain the tinder for the earthquakes, have become more extensive and thereby more suited to the ignition of it.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0010":["This preparation concerning the subterranean passages is of no small significance for the insight into what will come up in the sequel concerning the wide spread of earthquakes into great countries, concerning the stretch that they hold, concerning the places where they rage most and those where they first begin.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0011":["I now begin with the history of the latter earthquake itself. By this I understand no history of the misfortunes that human beings have suffered through it, no list of the devastated cities and of the inhabitants buried under their rubble. Everything dreadful that the imagination can represent to itself must be taken together, in order to picture to oneself to some extent the horror in which human beings must find themselves when the earth is moved beneath their feet, when everything around them collapses, when water moved in its ground makes the misfortune complete through floods, when the fear of death, the despair over the complete loss of all goods, finally the sight of other wretched ones beat down the steadiest courage. Such a narrative would be moving; because it has an effect on the heart, it might perhaps also be able to have one on the improvement of it. But I leave this history to more skillful hands. I describe here only the work of nature, the remarkable natural circumstances that accompanied the dreadful occurrence, and the causes of it.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0012":["On the Forerunners of the Latter Earthquake.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0013":["The prelude of the subterranean ignition, which in the sequel became so dreadful, I posit in the aerial appearance that was perceived at Locarno in Switzerland on the 14th of October of last year in the morning at 8 o'clock. A vapor warm as if coming from an oven spread out and transformed itself within 2 hours into a red mist, from which toward evening a blood-red rain arose, which, after it had been collected, let fall ⅑ of a reddish gluey sediment. The snow, 6 feet high, was likewise colored red. This purple rain was perceived over 40 hours, that is, about 20 German miles square, indeed even as far as Swabia. Upon this aerial appearance there followed unnatural downpours, which in 3 days yielded water 23 inches high, that is, more than falls down in a country of moderately moist constitution through the whole year. This rain lasted over 14 days, though not always with equal violence. The rivers in Lombardy that take their origin in the Swiss mountains, likewise the Rhône, swelled with water and overflowed their banks. From this time on fearful hurricanes prevailed in the air, which raged cruelly everywhere. Still in the middle of November a like purple rain fell in Ulm, and the disorder in the atmosphere, the whirlwinds in Italy, the exceedingly wet weather continued.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0014":["If one wants to make for oneself a concept of the causes of this appearance and of its sequels, one must pay attention to the constitution of the ground over which it occurred. The Swiss mountains one and all comprehend extensive clefts beneath themselves, which without doubt stand in interconnection with the deepest subterranean passages. Scheuchzer counts almost 20 abysses that at certain times blow out winds. If we now assume that the mineral materials hidden in the interior of these caves have come into mixture with the fluids with which they effervesce and thereby into an inner fermentation, which could prepare the fire-nourishing materials for that ignition which was to break out fully within a few days. If we, e.g., represent to ourselves that acid which is contained in the spirit of saltpeter, and which nature herself necessarily prepares, how, set in motion either through the influx of water or other causes, it attacked the iron-earth upon which it fell, then these materials in their commingling will have heated themselves and expelled red warm vapors out of the clefts of the mountains, with which, in the violence of the upsurge, the particles of the red iron-earth were at the same time mixed and carried away, which occasioned the gluey blood-rain of which we have made mention. The nature of such vapors tends to diminish the expansive force of the air and precisely thereby to make the water-vapors suspended in it flow together, likewise, by drawing in all the moist clouds hovering round about in the atmosphere by virtue of the natural slope toward the region where the height of the column of air has been diminished, to cause those violent and sustained downpours that were perceived in the named regions.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0015":["In such a way the subterranean fermentation announced in advance, through expelled vapors, the misfortune that it was preparing in concealment. *) The completion of the fate followed it with slow steps. A fermentation does not at once break out into ignitions. The fermenting and heated materials must encounter a combustible oil, sulphur, bitumen, or something of the kind, in order to come into ignition. For so long the heating spread out here and there in the subterranean passages, and in the moment when the dissolved combustible materials in the mixture with the others were heated to the point of coming into fire, the vaults of the earth were shaken and the conclusion of the dispensations was carried out.",["(Notes for 0009)","- *Umfang* = \"domain\" (glossary); *Gebiet* here rendered \"domain\" too (Gebiet and Umfang both \"domain\"; distinguished only here). *Wirkung* = effect; *Folge* = sequel; *Beschaffenheit* = constitution; *Stoff* = material (but *Materie* = matter/materials in 0014); *Gesetz* = law.","- \"Es ist eine artige Vorsicht … daß sie … unterhalten\": the long construction is kept as in German; \"that\" clause follows the intervening \"um … zu\" clause. *Erwegen* rendered \"consider.\"","- \"dessen Wirkung\" / \"desselben\": antecedent ambiguous in German (the fire; the earth); \"its / of it\" kept. \"Beunruh igung\" is an OCR break, silently corrected. Tenses \"spie … gethan hätte\" kept in subjunctive-like English.","(Notes for 0011, 0013)","- *Einbildungskraft* = imagination; *Begebenheit* = occurrence; *Vorstellen* = represent; *Vorbilden* = picture. *Wargenommen* (wahrgenommen) = perceived; *Lufterscheinung* = \"aerial appearance\" (*Erscheinung* = appearance).","- *Gevierte*, \"ins Gevierte\" = \"square\"; \"40 Stunden\" is an hours-of-travel measure, kept as \"40 hours.\"","(Notes for 0014, 0015)","- *Begriff* = concept; *Zusammenhang* = interconnection; *Folgen* = sequels; *begreifen* = comprehend; *Materie* = matter(s) here \"materials\" for plural; *Schluß* (0015) = \"conclusion\"; *Verhängnisse* = \"dispensations.\"","- In 0014 the sentences beginning \"Wenn wir nun annehmen…\" is grammatically incomplete in the German (no main clause); preserved. The footnote marker \"*)\" stands in the text without footnote text in this passage."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0016":["*) Eight days before the shaking the earth near Cadiz was covered with a multitude of worms that had crawled out of the earth. The cause just cited had driven these forth. In some other earthquakes violent lightning in the air and the anxiety that one notes in animals have been forerunners.",["- Erschütterung rendered \"shaking\" here (and \"shaking\" for Rüttelung below, \"tremor\" for Bebung) to keep the German words distinct; Erdbeben = \"earthquake.\"","- Ursache = \"cause\"; Vorboten = \"forerunners.\"","- \"diese hatte die nur angeführte Ursache hervorgetrieben\": Diese could refer to Gewürme (worms) or to Menge; I kept \"these.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0017":["The earthquake and the movement of the waters\nof the 1st of November 1755.",["- Wasserbewegung = \"movement of the waters\" (literally \"water-movement\"); Erdbeben = \"earthquake.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0018":["The moment in which this blow occurred seems most correctly to be determined at 9 o'clock 50 minutes in the morning at Lisbon, this time agrees exactly with that at which it was perceived in Madrid, namely 10 o'clock 17 to 18 minutes, if one converts the difference of longitude of the two cities into the difference of time. At the same time the waters in an astonishing domain, both those that have a visible community with the world-sea, and also those that may stand in it in a concealed way, were set into shaking. From Åbo in Finland as far as the archipelago of the West Indies few or no coasts have remained free of it. It has dominated a stretch of 1500 miles in almost exactly the same time. If one were assured that the time at which it was sensed at Glückstadt on the Elbe, according to the public reports, were to be set quite exactly at 11 o'clock 30 minutes, one would conclude from this that the movement of the waters spent 15 minutes in reaching from Lisbon to the Holstein coasts. In just this time it was also sensed on all coasts of the Mediterranean Sea, and one does not yet know the whole breadth of its extension.",["- Umfang = \"domain\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Weltmeer = \"world-sea\" (literal); Mittelländisches Meer = \"Mediterranean Sea.\"","- bestimmen = \"determine\"; setzen = \"posit\" in the glossary, but \"set\" is used for \"in Erschütterung gesetzt\" and \"zu setzen wäre\" (both setzen), and \"in the German 'Sie' (l. 5ff.) has no clear antecedent (Gewässer/Wasserbewegung/Erschütterung); I kept \"it.\"","- \"Wargenommen\" (wahrgenommen), \"verspührt\" = \"sensed\"; the OCR/original spelling \"Madrit\" corrected to Madrid.","- \"beherrschet\" = \"dominated\"; \"Strecke\" = \"stretch.\"","- Length of \"Meilen\" = \"miles.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0019":["The waters that on the firm land seem to be cut off from all community with the sea, the springs, the lakes, were in many lands lying far from one another set at the same time into extraordinary stirring. Most of the lakes in Switzerland, the lake near Templin in the Mark, some lakes in Norway and Sweden, fell into a surging movement, which was far more tempestuous and disorderly than in a storm, and the air was at the same time still. The lake near Neuchâtel, if one may rely on the reports, drained away into concealed clefts, and that near Meiningen did this likewise, but soon came back again. In just these minutes the mineral water at Teplitz in Bohemia suddenly stayed away, and came back blood-red. The force with which the water had been driven through had widened its old passages and it received thereby a stronger inflow. The inhabitants of this town had good reason to sing *te Deum laudamus*, while those at Lisbon struck up quite other tones. Thus are the contingencies constituted that concern the human race. The joy of the one and the misfortune of the other often have a common cause. In the kingdom of Fez in Africa a subterranean force split a mountain and poured blood-red streams from its gorge. At Angoulême in France one heard a subterranean din, a deep cavern opened on the plain and held unfathomable water within it. At Gemenos in Provence a spring suddenly became muddy and then poured out colored red. The surrounding regions reported like alterations in their springs. All this occurred in the same minutes in which the earthquake devastated the coasts of Portugal. Here and there in just this brief point of time some shakings of the earth were also perceived in far distant lands. But they occurred almost all close to the seacoast. At Cork in Ireland, likewise at Glückstadt and at some other places that lie on the sea, slight tremors occurred. Milan is perhaps the place that was shaken at the greatest distance from the seashore on that same day. Just this morning at 8 o'clock Vesuvius near Naples raged and grew still toward the time when the shaking at Portugal occurred.",["- Zufälle = \"contingencies\" (plural of Zufall; related to zufällig = contingent); \"were constituted\" renders beschaffen (Beschaffenheit = constitution).","- Regung = \"stirring\"; Bewegung = \"movement\"; Erschütterung = \"shaking\"; Erderschütterungen = \"shakings of the earth\"; Bebungen = \"tremors.\"","- \"Meinungen\" (Meiningen?) is the German's spelling; I read it as the place name Meiningen, which is doubtful, and rendered it so.","- \"Die Einwohner dieser Stadt hatten gut te Deum laudamus zu singen\": \"hatten gut ... zu singen\" rendered \"had good reason to sing\"; idiomatic, the literal sense is \"could well sing.\"","- \"hin und wieder\" = \"here and there\"; \"Zeitpunkte\" = \"point of time\"; \"Qvelle\" = \"spring\"; \"Gruft\" = \"cavern.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0020":["Consideration of the cause of this\nmovement of the waters.",["- Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Ursache = \"cause.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0021":["History has no example of so widely spread a shaking of all waters and of a great part of the earth, sensed at the same time in the course of a few minutes. One therefore needs caution in order to infer from a single occurrence the cause of it. One can think above all of the following causes, which could have produced the cited event of nature. Either first through a tremor of the sea-bottom everywhere immediately beneath those places where the sea fell into shaking, and then one would have to give a ground why the vein of fire that produced these tremors should have run on merely beneath the floor of the seas, without extending beneath the lands that stand in near combination with these seas and often interrupt their community. One would find oneself caught by the question why the shaking of the ground, since it has spread from Glückstadt on the North Sea to Lübeck on the Baltic Sea, and on the Mecklenburg coasts, was not felt in Holstein, which lies in the middle between these seas and where only a mild tremor close to the shore of the waters was sensed, but none in the interior of the land. But most distinctly one is convinced by the surging of the waters lying far from the sea, as of the lake near Templin, those in Switzerland and others. One can easily judge that, in order to bring a body of water into so violent a surging by the tremor of the ground, the shaking certainly must not be slight. But why have all the surrounding lands, beneath which the vein of fire must necessarily have run on, not felt this violent shock? One sees easily that all marks of truth are against this opinion. A shaking that was impressed round about on the dense mass of the earth itself by a violent blow occurring at one place, just as the ground trembles at some distance when a powder tower blows up, loses in application to this case also quite and entirely the probability, as much from the cause already cited as because of the dreadful domain, which if one compares it with the domain of the whole earth makes up so considerable a part of it that its tremor would necessarily have had to draw after it a shaking of the whole terrestrial globe. Now, however, one can teach oneself from Buffon that an eruption of the subterranean fire, which could throw a mountain range that was 1700 miles long and 40 wide a mile high, would not be able to displace the body of the earth a thumb's breadth from its position.",["- Grund = \"ground\" (as in \"give a ground\"; also Meergrund/Seegrund = \"sea-bottom,\" Boden = \"floor\"/\"ground\"); Verbindung = \"combination\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community.\"","- Merkmale = \"marks\"; Meinung = \"opinion\"; Warscheinlichkeit = \"probability\"; Umfang = \"domain.\"","- Rüttelung = \"shaking\"; Erschütterung = \"shaking\"; Schüttelung = \"shaking\"; Bebung = \"tremor\"; Wallung/Aufwallen = \"surging.\" \"Shaking\" stands for three German words.","- \"Man würde sich durch die Frage betreten finden\": \"caught by the question\" (betreten, perplexed/surprised); \"Naturbegebenheit\" = \"event of nature\" (Begebenheit = occurrence in glossary, but \"event\" kept for Naturbegebenheit; Vorfall = \"occurrence\").","- \"Eine Erschütterung die ... eingedrückt worden ... verliehrt\": the construction is preserved; the subject is \"Eine Erschütterung,\" and \"die\" may be a relative pronoun referring to it or to \"Masse.\"","- \"welcher ... einen ... Theil derselben ausmacht\": \"welcher\" refers to Umfang; \"derselben\" is ambiguous (earth/domain), left so.","- Büffon = Buffon; \"Gebirge\" = \"mountain range\"; \"Feuerader\" = \"vein of fire.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0022":["We will therefore have to seek the spreading of this movement of the waters in an intermediate matter, which is better suited to communicate a shaking over great distances, namely in the waters of the seas themselves, which stands in interconnection with that which was set by an immediate tremor of the sea-bottom into a violent and sudden shaking.",["- Mittelmaterie = \"intermediate matter\" (Materie = matter); Zusammenhang = \"interconnection.\"","- \"welches ... im Zusammenhange steht mit demjenigen\": \"welches\" refers to Gewäßer (singular), hence \"which stands,\" though \"waters\" is plural in English.","- Gewäßer/Gewässer = \"waters\"; Ausbreitung = \"spreading\"; Rüttelung = \"shaking.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0023":["In the weekly Königsberg Announcements I sought to estimate the force with which the sea, through the blow of the tremor occurring from its floor, was driven onward in its whole domain, in that I assumed the shaken place of the sea-bottom merely as a quadrilateral, whose side is equal to the distance from Cape St. Vincent and Cape Finisterre, i.e. to the length of the western coasts of Portugal and Spain, and regarded the force of the bursting-up ground as that of a powder mine, which in blowing up is capable of throwing the bodies that are located above it 15 feet high, and, according to the rules by which the motion is continued in a fluid being, found it stronger on the Holstein coasts than the swiftest rebounding current. Let us here consider the force that it exercised from these causes once more from another point of view. Count Marsigli found the greatest depth of the Mediterranean by the sounding-lead to be over 8000 feet, and it is certain that the ocean at a proper distance from land is deeper still; but we will here assume it only 6000 feet, i.e. 1000 fathoms, deep. We know that the weight with which so high a column of sea water presses on the bottom of the sea must exceed the pressure of the atmosphere almost 200 times, and that it far exceeds the force with which the fire is behind a ball that is hurled 100 fathoms far out of the cavity of a cannon in the time of a pulse-beat. This astonishing weight could not hold back the force with which the subterranean fire pushed the sea-bottom swiftly upward, thus this moving force was greater. With what pressure, then, was the water pressed in order to shoot forth suddenly to the sides, and is it indeed to be wondered at if it was sensed in a few minutes in Finland and at the same time in the West Indies. One cannot determine at all how great the base surface of the immediate shaking may actually have been; it will perhaps be incomparably greater than we have assumed it; but under the seas where the motion of the water was sensed without any earthquake at all, on the Dutch, English, Norwegian coasts, and in the Baltic, it certainly cannot have been encountered in the sea-bottom. For then the firm land in its interior would certainly have been shaken along with it, which however has not been observed at all.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Gewalt = \"force\" throughout (Kraft, in the glossary, is likewise \"force\"; both rendered \"force\", the later *Kraft* in 0024 also \"force\").\n- Wesen in \"flüßigen Wesen\" = \"being\" (glossary), kept literally; \"fluid being\" is awkward in the German as well.\n- Erschütterung/erschüttern = \"shaking\"/\"shake\"; Bebung = \"tremor\"; Erdbeben = \"earthquake\".\n- Umfang = \"domain\" (glossary), kept though the sense here may be \"extent\".\n- \"sie ... gefunden\" in the first sentence: the subject of \"befunden\" and the antecedent of \"sie\" are left as in the German; \"es\" in \"die es ... ausgeübt hat\" refers to the sea (Meer) or possibly to Gewalt's bearer, left ambiguous.\n- Carthaune = \"cannon\" (a large siege gun); Klafter = \"fathom\"; Ruthe etc. not in this passage.\n- \"Waßer\" = \"water\"; \"Meereswaßer\" = \"sea water\"; \"mittelländisches Meer\" = \"Mediterranean\".",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0024":["In that I ascribe the violent shaking of all the connected parts of the ocean to the single jolt that its floor suffered in a certain district, I do not therefore wish to have denied the actual spreading of the subterranean fire under the firm land of almost the whole of Europe. They have in all probability occurred at the same time and both have had a share in the appearances that took place, only that each in particular is not to be regarded as the sole cause of them all taken together. The tremor of the water in the North Sea, which let a sudden jolt be felt, was not the effect of an earthquake raging under the ground. Such shakings would, in order to bring forth such an effect, have to be very violent, and would thus have had to be sensed very noticeably under the firm land. But I do not therefore deny that even all the firm land was put into a slight swaying, through a weak force of the vapors kindled under its floor or of other causes. One sees this in Milan, which on this day was threatened with the greatest danger of a total overthrow. We will therefore posit that the earth was set by a slight swaying into a gentle motion, which was so great that over 100 Rhenish rods it wobbled the soil alternately back and forth by an inch: Then this motion will have been so imperceptible that a building of 4 rods in height could not have been brought out of the vertical position by it by half a grain, i.e. by half a knife-back, which would hardly become noticeable even on the highest towers. By contrast the lakes will have had to make this insensible motion very noticeable. For if a lake, e.g., is only 2 German miles long, then its water will already be put by this slight wavering of its floor into a quite strong rocking; for the water then has over 14000 inches approximately one inch of fall and a run-off that is almost only half smaller than the run-off of a quite swift river, as the water-leveling of the Seine at Paris can teach us, which after several oscillations occurring to and fro could well have caused an extraordinary shaking in the water. But we can with good reason assume the motion of the earth to be once again as great as we have done, without its having been able to be sensed fittingly on the firm land, and then the motion of the inland lakes strikes the eye all the more comprehensibly.\n\n===NOTES===\n- \"Sie sind ... geschehen\": \"Sie\" has no clear antecedent in the German (perhaps the shaking of the ocean and the spreading of the fire); left as \"They\".\n- \"ins besondere\" = \"in particular\" (besonder = particular).\n- Erscheinungen = \"appearances\" (glossary); sich eräugnen = \"took place\".\n- Warscheinlichkeit = \"probability\" (glossary), rendered \"in all probability\".\n- \"Schwänkung\"/\"Schwanken\"/\"Wanken\"/\"wackeln\" = \"swaying\"/\"swaying\"/\"wavering\"/\"wobble\"; Schauckelung = \"rocking\"; Rüttelung = \"shaking\".\n- \"um die Helfte kleiner\" kept literally (\"only half smaller\"); \"Gran\" = \"grain\" (a small unit of measure); \"Meßerrücken\" = \"knife-back\".\n- \"begreiflich\" = \"comprehensible\" (cf. begreifen = comprehend); \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\"; \"Kraft\" = \"force\"; \"Ursache\" = \"cause\"; \"belehren\" = \"teach\" (Belehrung).\n- \"Dünste\" = \"vapors\"; \"Rheinl. Ruthen\" = \"Rhenish rods\"; \"Fall\" = \"fall\"; \"Ablauf\" = \"run-off\"; \"Waßerabwägung\" = \"water-leveling\", doubtful in sense but kept literal.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0025":["One will therefore no longer be amazed if all the inland lakes in Switzerland, in Sweden, in Norway and in Germany, without feeling a shaking of the ground, were seen to be so restless and surging. But one finds it somewhat more extraordinary that certain lakes in this disorder dried up altogether, such as the lake near Neuchâtel, the one near Como, and the one near Meiningen, although some of these have already filled up again with water. This occurrence, however, is not without example. One has some lakes on the globe that quite regularly at certain times run off through hidden channels, and at the appointed time come back. The Cirknitz Lake in the Duchy of Carniola is a remarkable example of this. It has in its floor some holes, through which however it does not drain off before around St. James's day, when it then runs off suddenly with all its fishes, and after it has left its floor dry for 3 months as a good pasture and arable place, it suddenly reappears toward the month of November. One explains this natural occurrence very comprehensibly by the comparison with the diabetes of hydraulics. But in the cases we have before us one can easily judge that, since many lakes receive inflow through spring-veins located under their floor, and these, which find their origin in the surrounding heights, after the effect of the subterranean heating and evaporation in the cavities, which are their water-containers, had swallowed up the air, must thereby have been drawn back into the same, and themselves have furnished a powerful suction-work, to lead the lake in with them, which, after the equilibrium of the air was restored, sought its natural outlet again. For that an inland lake, as the public reports have wanted to explain of the one at Meiningen, is maintained through subterranean community with the sea, because it has no external inflow of brooks, is exposed to a quite too obvious absurdity, as well on account of the laws of equilibrium that conflict with it, as also on account of the saltiness of sea water.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Begebenheit = \"occurrence\" (glossary); \"Naturbegebenheit\" = \"natural occurrence\".\n- \"Gemeinschaft\" = \"community\" (glossary), kept though the sense is a subterranean connection.\n- \"Gesetze\" = \"laws\"; \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\"; \"Gleichgewicht\" = \"equilibrium\".\n- \"Neuschatel\", \"Meinungen\", \"Cirnitzer See\", \"Crain\" = Neuchâtel, Meiningen, Cirknitz Lake, Carniola, given in modern English forms; the text's spellings are kept apart from this.\n- \"Jacobi\" = \"St. James's day\" (25 July); \"Qvelladern\" = \"spring-veins\"; \"Saugwerk\" = \"suction-work\"; \"Ausdämpfung\" = \"evaporation\".\n- \"welche ihre Waßerhälter seyn\": \"welche\" could refer to Hölungen or to Qvelladern; the German is ambiguous and the English \"which are their water-containers\" keeps this.\n- The long sentence beginning \"Allein in unsern vorhabenden Fällen\" is syntactically strained in the German (the subject \"diese\" and the verb phrase \"müssen zurückgezogen worden seyn\"); kept as it stands.\n- \"Diabetes der Hydraulick\" kept literally (\"diabetes of hydraulics\").",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0026":["Earthquakes have it already as something usual in themselves that they bring the water springs into disorder. I could here cite a whole register of springs stopped up and broken out in other places, of spring water shot up quite high out of the earth and the like, from the history of other earthquakes, but I remain with my object. From France we have been reported to, in some places, that springs were stopped up, and others have given excessively much water. The Töplitz spring failed to come, made the poor Töplitzers anxious, came first muddy, then blood-red, finally natural and stronger than before again. The discoloration of the waters in so many regions, even in the kingdom of Fez, and in France is, in my estimation, to be ascribed to the mixture of the vapors that have penetrated through the layers of earth, where the springs have their passage, and have fallen into fermentation with sulphur and iron particles. If these penetrate into the interior of the cisterns that contain the origin of the well-spring, then they either drive it out with greater force, or, in that they press the water into other passages, they alter its outflow.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0027":["These are the foremost remarkable things of the history of the 1st of Nov. and of the movement of the water, which is the rarest of its circumstances. It is exceedingly credible to me that the earth-tremors that occurred close by the sea-shore, or by a water that has community with it, at Cork in Ireland, in Glückstadt, and here and there in Spain, are for the most part to be ascribed precisely to the pressure of the pressed sea-water, whose force must be incredibly great, if one multiplies the violence with which it strikes by the surface on which it hits, and I am of the opinion that the misfortune of Lisbon is, like that of most of the cities of the western coast of Europe, to be ascribed to the situation that it had in regard to the region of the ocean in question, since its whole force, moreover, strengthened in the mouth of the Tagus by the narrowness of a gulf, must have shaken the ground extraordinarily. One may judge whether the earth-tremor would have been able to be distinctly noticed merely in cities that lie on the sea-shore, which was nevertheless not sensible in the interior of the country, if the pressure of the waters had not had a share in it.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0028":["The last appearance of this great occurrence is still remarkable, since a considerable time, namely almost 1 to 1½ hours after the earthquake, a dreadful piling up of the waters in the ocean, and a swelling of the Tagus, which alternately rose 6 feet higher than the highest flood tide, and soon thereafter fell almost as much lower than the lowest ebb, was seen. This movement of the sea, which occurred a considerable time after the earthquake, and after the first dreadful pressure of the waters, also completed the ruin of the city of Setuval, in that it raised itself over its ruins, and wholly consumed what the shaking had spared. If one has previously formed a proper concept of the violence of the sea-water shoved forth through the moved sea-bottom, then one will be able easily to represent to oneself that it must return with force, after its pressure had spread itself into all the immeasurable regions round about. The time of its return depends on the wide domain in which it has worked round about it, and its upsurge, principally at the shores, must, according to the measure of the same, also have been just as fearful *).",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0029":["*) In the harbor at Husum this upsurge of the water was also perceived between 12 and 1, thus one hour later than the first shock of the waters in the North Sea.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0030":["The earthquake of the 18th of Novembr.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0031":["From the 17th to the 18th of this same month, the public reports reported a notable earth-tremor, on the coasts of Portugal as well as Spain and in Africa. On the 17th at midday it was to be sensed in Gibraltar at the strait of the Mediterranean Sea, and toward evening at Whitehaven in Yorkshire in England. From the 17th to the 18th it was already in the English colonies of America. On the same 18th it was also felt violently in the region of Aquapendente and della Grotta in Italy. **)",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0032":["**) Likewise at Glowson in the county of Hertford, where, with a violent din, it opened an abyss, which contained within it a very deep water.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0033":["The earthquake of the 9th of Decembr.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0034":["According to the testimony of the public reports, Lisbon has suffered no such violent attacks of the shaking since the 1st of Nov. as that of the 9th of Decembr. It was sensed on the southern coasts of Spain, on those of France, through the Swiss mountains, Swabia, Tyrol, as far as Bavaria. It traversed from southwest to northeast, about 300 German miles, and in that it kept to the direction of that chain of mountains that run through the highest elevation of the mainland of Europe according to its length, it did not spread very much sideways. The most careful geographers, Varen, Buffon, Lulof, remark that, just as all land that extends more in length than breadth is run through in the direction of its length by a main mountain range, so the foremost tract of the mountains of Europe extends from one main stem, namely the Alps toward the west, through the southern provinces of France, through the middle of Spain, to the outermost shore of Europe toward the evening, although on the way it shoots out considerable side-branches, and likewise eastward, through the Tyrolean and other less considerable mountains, finally joins with the Carpathian.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0035":["This direction the earthquake traversed on the same day. If the time of the shaking of each place were correctly recorded, one would be able to estimate the swiftness to some extent, and probably to determine the region of the first ignition; but now the reports are so little in agreement that in regard to this nothing is to be relied upon.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0036":["I have already cited elsewhere that earthquakes, commonly when they spread, keep to the tract of the highest mountains, and indeed through their whole extent, although this lowers itself the more, the nearer it approaches the sea-shore. The direction of long rivers designates very well the direction of the mountains, since between their rows running alongside one another they run on, as in the lowest part of a long valley. This law of the spreading of earthquakes is no matter of speculation or judgment, but something that has become known through observations of many earthquakes. One must in this regard hold to the testimonies of Raj, Buffon, Gentil, etc. But this law has so much inner probability that it must easily acquire approval even of itself. If one considers that the openings through which the subterranean fire seeks an exit are nowhere else than in the summits of the mountains, that one has never perceived fire-spewing chasms in the plains, that in countries where earthquakes are violent and frequent, most mountains contain wide maws that serve for the ejection of fire, and that, as concerns our European mountains, one discovers spacious cavities nowhere else than in them, which without doubt stand in an interconnection, if one applies to this in addition the concept of the generation of all these subterranean vaults, of which was spoken above, then one will find no difficulty in the representation of how the ignition could encounter open and free passages principally under the chain of mountains that run through the length of Europe, in order to spread in them more quickly than toward other regions.",["- 0026: \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\"; \"Qvellen\" = \"springs\", \"Brunnqvell\" = \"well-spring\"; \"Erachten\" = \"estimation\"; \"Gährung\" = \"fermentation\". \"Fetz\" (Fez) is rendered \"Fez,\" a correction of the old spelling.","- 0026: \"Töplitzer Brunn\" kept as \"Töplitz spring\" (Teplitz); \"wieder\" placed after \"stronger than before\" as in the German.","- 0027: \"Gemeinschaft\" = \"community\"; \"Erderschütterung\" = \"earth-tremor\"; \"Druck\" = \"pressure\"; \"Meinung\" = \"opinion\" (\"der Meinung sein\" rendered \"of the opinion\"); \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\".","- 0027: \"Gewalt\" = \"force\" and \"Heftigkeit\" = \"violence\"; in 0028 \"Gewalt\" = \"force\" also; \"Umfang\" = \"domain\"; \"Begriff\" = \"concept\"; \"Begebenheit\" = \"occurrence\"; \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\".","- 0028: the ambiguous subject-verb order of \"gesehen wurde\" at the end of the first sentence is preserved; \"sich eräugnete\" = \"occurred\"; \"Setuval\" is Setúbal.","- 0029/0031/0032: \"Waßer\"/\"Gewäßer\" = \"water\"/\"waters\"; \"wargenommen\" (= wahrgenommen) rendered \"perceived\"; \"Yorkshirn\" = Yorkshire; \"Aqvapendente\" = Acquapendente; \"spühren\" = \"sense\" (cf. \"fühlen\" = \"feel\"). \"Glowson\" kept as printed. Footnote markers *) and **) kept as printed.","- 0034: \"Meile\" = \"mile\"; \"Abend\" = \"evening\" kept literal for \"west\" (\"gegen Abend\"); \"Varen\" (Varenius) kept as printed; \"Lulof\" as printed.","- 0034/0036: \"Beobachtung\" = \"observation\"; \"Zeugnis\" = \"testimony\"; \"Gesetz\" = \"law\"; \"Wahrscheinlichkeit\" = \"probability\"; \"Beurtheilung\" = \"judgment\"; \"urtheilen\" = \"judge\"; \"Zusammenhang\" = \"interconnection\"; \"Raj\" (Ray) and \"Buffon\" as printed.","- 0036: the long final sentence, with its \"wenn … wenn … so\" structure and its ambiguity about the antecedent of \"dieselbe\"/\"derselben\", is kept; \"Entzündung\" = \"ignition.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0037":["Even the continuation of the earthquake of the 18th Nov. from Europe to America under the floor of a wide sea is to be sought in the interconnection of the chain of mountains, which, although in the continuation they become so low that they are covered by the sea, nevertheless remain mountains there too, for we know that on the floor of the ocean mountain ranges are to be encountered just as well as on land; and in this way the Azorean Islands must be posited into this interconnection, which are encountered halfway between Portugal and North America.",["- Zusammenhang = interconnection (both occurrences). Gebirge = \"mountain ranges\"; Berge = \"mountains\".","- \"in dieser Art\" rendered \"in this way\" (Art = way).","- \"gesetzt werden\" rendered \"be posited\" (setzen).","- \"Oceans\" kept as \"ocean\"; \"seyn\" (for sind, subjunctive-like) rendered as plain indicative."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0038":["The earthquake of the 26th Decembr.",["- Erdbeben = earthquake. Heading; \"Decembr.\" kept abbreviated as in the original."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0039":["After the heating of the mineral matters had penetrated the main trunk of the highest mountain ranges of Europe, namely the Alps, it also opened for itself the narrower passages under the row of mountains that run out from south to north at right angles, and extended in the direction of the Rhine stream, which, like all rivers generally, occupies a long valley between two rows of mountains, from Switzerland to the North Sea. It shook on the west side of the river the territories of Alsace, Lorraine, the Electorate of Cologne, Brabant, and Picardy, and on the east side Cleves, a part of Westphalia, and presumably some further lands situated on this side of the Rhine, of which the reports have named nothing specifically. It evidently held the tract parallel with the direction of this great river and spread not far from it to the sides.",["- Materien = matters (Materie = matter); Erhitzung = heating; Erschütterung/erschüttern rendered \"shock\"/\"shake\" consistently across the passages.","- \"an dieser Seite des Rheins\" = \"on this side of the Rhine,\" as in the German, i.e., the east side is left unspecified as to which \"this\" refers; \"Strich\" = \"tract.\"","- \"Landschaften\" = \"territories\"; \"Churfürstenthum\" = \"Electorate.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0040":["One will ask how it can be reconciled with the above that it penetrated into the Netherlands, which are yet without any notable mountains. But it is enough that a land stand in an immediate interconnection with certain rows of mountains, and be regarded as a continuation of them, in order to continue the subterranean ignition even under this otherwise low ground, for it is certain that then the chain of caverns will also extend itself even under it, just as it goes on, as already cited, even under the floor of the sea.",["- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Entzündung = ignition; Hölen = caverns; Meeresgrunde = \"floor of the sea\" (Grund = ground; here the sea bottom).","- \"zusammenreimen\" = \"reconcile\"; the subjunctives \"stehe,\" \"sey\" are rendered with English subjunctive.","- \"unterirrdisch\" = subterranean; \"diesem\" (for \"diesem\") kept as \"it/this.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0041":["On the intervals of time that elapse within several earthquakes following one another.",["- \"binnen einigen\" = \"within several\"; the German is awkward (zwischen would be expected), left awkward.","- Zwischenzeiten = intervals of time."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0042":["If one considers with attention the sequence of the shocks that have occurred one after another, one could, if one wished to venture to conjecture, work out a period in which the ignition after an intermediate standstill has broken out anew. We find after the 1st Novembr. a further very violent shock in Portugal on the 9th, likewise on the 18th, when it extended to England, Italy, Africa, and even into America. On the 27th a strong earthquake on the southern coasts of Spain, primarily in Malaga. From this time it lasted 13 days, until on the 9th Dec. it struck the whole stretch from Portugal into Bavaria from southwest to northeast, and since this, after a lapse of 18 days, namely on the 26th to the 27th Decembr., it shook the breadth of Europe from south to north *), so that on the whole a fairly regular course of time of 9, or 2 times 9 days, has elapsed between the repeated ignitions, if one excepts that time which it has applied until it penetrated into the innermost of the mountain ranges of our firm land, and on the 9th Decembr. moved the Alps and the whole chain of their extension. I cite this not to the end of concluding anything from it, because the reports are far too little reliable for that, but in order to give occasion, in similar occurrences, to more exact observation and reflection.",["- Folge = sequence; folgern = conclude (\"to conclude anything from it\"); Beobachtung = observation; Nachsinnen = reflection (not Überlegung).","- Periodus left as \"period\"; Zeitlauf = \"course of time\"; Entzündung = ignition; Erschütterung = shock.","- \"*)\" marks the footnote, which follows as the next passage; \"binnen\" reading as in the text.","- \"Vorfällen\" = \"occurrences\" (Begebenheit/Ereignis are not here); \"festen Landes\" = \"firm land.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0043":["*) On the 21st it was very violent in Lisbon, on the 23rd in the mountain ranges of Roussillon, and lasted there until the 27th. It is to be seen from this that it began again from the southwest, and indeed needed a far longer time for spreading. And if one posits the place of ignition, as is clear from the whole course of the earthquake, in the ocean of Portugal toward the west, then the beginning of it hangs together fairly well with the touched-upon period.",["- \"setzt\" = \"posits\" (setzen); Entzündungsplatz = \"place of ignition\"; \"gegen Abend\" = \"toward the west\" (literally, toward evening).","- \"Periodus\" = \"period\"; \"berührten\" = \"touched-upon.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0044":["I wish here only to cite something in general about the shocks alternately subsiding and again setting in. Herr Bouguer, one of the deputies of the Royal Acad. of Sciences at Paris to Peru, had the inconvenience of staying in this country next to a fire-spewing mountain, whose thundering din left him no rest. The observation he made here could be some satisfaction to him for it, in that he noticed that the mountain became quiet always at equal intervals of time, and its raging followed regularly, with alternating points of rest, one upon another. The remark that Mariotte made with a lime kiln, which was heated, and at one moment expelled the air out of an open window, at the next drew it back again, whereby he to a certain extent imitated the respiration of animals, has great similarity with this; both rest on the following causes. When the subterranean fire falls into ignition it expels all the air from the caverns round about from itself. Where this air, which is filled with the fiery parts, now finds an opening, e.g. in the maw of a fire-spewing mountain, there it then fares out and the mountain throws out fire. But as soon as the air is driven out of the domain of the hearth of the ignition, the ignition subsides, for without access of air all fire is extinguished; then the driven-out air, since the cause that had driven it out ceases, steps back again into its place and wakes the extinguished fire, in such a way the eruptions of a fire-spewing mountain alternate regularly with one another at certain intervals of time. Precisely the same state of affairs holds with the subterranean ignitions, even where the expanded air cannot gain an exit through the clefts of the mountains. For when the ignition takes its beginning at a place in the caverns of the earth, it expels the air with violence, over a large domain, into all the passages of the subterranean vaults that have interconnection with it. In this moment the fire itself is suffocated through the lack of air. And as soon as this same expanding force of the air subsides, that which had been spread out in all the caverns returns with great force, and fans the extinguished fire to a new earthquake. It is remarkable that Vesuvius, which, when the fermentations in the interior of the earth really began, was brought into motion and fire through the exit of the air driven through its gorge, a short time afterward suddenly subsided when the earthquake at Lisbon had occurred, for then all the air standing in some interconnection with these vaults, and even that which is found above the summit of Vesuvius, pressed through all channels to the hearth of ignition, where the diminution of the expansive force of the air permitted it access. What an astonishing object! To represent to oneself a chimney that procures a draft for itself through air openings that are 200 miles distant from it.",["- Beobachtung = observation; Gegenstand = object; sich vorzustellen = \"to represent to oneself\" (Vorstellung = representation).","- Zusammenhang = interconnection (twice, \"Zusammenhang haben\" / \"in einigem Zusammenhange\"); Umfang = domain (both \"Umfange des Heerdes\" and \"großen Umfange\"); Ursache = cause; Entzündung = ignition; Erschütterung = shock.","- \"Wißensch.\" (sic) expanded as \"Sciences\"; \"Respiration\" kept as in German; \"Bewandniß\" = \"state of affairs\"; \"Meilen\" = \"miles\"; \"Ausspannungskraft\" = \"expansive force\" (Kraft = force).","- \"ihr den Zugang verstattete\": \"ihr\" refers to the air (Luft); \"dem\" ambiguities in \"denn da drang alle ... Luft\" preserved; \"Gährungen\" = fermentations."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0045":["It is precisely the same cause that must also produce subterranean storm winds in the vaults of the earth, whose violence will far exceed everything that we sense on the surface of the earth, if the situation and connection of the caverns dispose themselves to their spreading. The din that has been sensed under the feet in the progression of an earthquake is presumably to be ascribed to no other cause than precisely this one.",["- Verknüpfung = connection (not Zusammenhang); Fortgange = progression (Fortgang); Ursache = cause; Hölen = caverns; Grüften = vaults.","- \"verspühren\" rendered \"sense\"; \"sich zu ihrer Ausbreitung anschicket\" = \"dispose themselves to their spreading\"; \"ihrer\" is ambiguous between the winds and the caverns' arrangement, left as in German."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0046":["This very thing lets us probably suppose that not all earthquakes are caused by the ignition occurring directly under the ground that is shaken; but that the fury of these subterranean storms can set in motion the vault that is above them, which one will doubt all the less if one considers: that a much denser air than that which is found on the surface of the earth, set in motion by far more sudden causes than this one, and strengthened between passages that hinder its expansion, can exert an unheard-of force. It is therefore conjectural that the slight swaying of the ground in the greatest part of Europe during the violent ignition that took place in the earth on the 1st of Nov., may perhaps be derived from nothing but this subterranean air moved in a violent manner, which, as a violent storm wind, gently shook the ground that resisted its expansion.",["- warscheinlich = probably (*Wahrscheinlichkeit* = probability); muthmaßlich rendered \"conjectural\"; Meinung/Muthmaßung not otherwise in this passage.","- Entzündung = ignition (OCR/spelling: \"gringe\" = geringe, \"gewaltsamer Weise\" as printed).","- Ambiguity kept: \"die als ein heftiger Sturmwind...\" — \"die\" refers to the air (Luft); \"welcher\" in the first sentence refers to the ground (Boden).","- Gewölbe = vault; Gewalt = force; Ursache = cause."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0047":["On the hearth of the subterranean ignition,\nand the places that are subject to the most numerous and most dangerous earthquakes.",["- Heerd = hearth; Entzündung = ignition; Oerter = places."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0048":["Through the comparison of the time we see that the place of ignition in the earthquake of the 1st of Nov. was in the floor of the sea. The Tagus, which swelled even before the shock, the sulphur that seafarers brought up with the sounding lead from the shaken bottom, and the violence of the jolts that they felt, confirm it. The history of former earthquakes also gives it clearly to be recognized that the most fearful shocks have at all times occurred in the bottom of the sea, and next to these in the places that lie on the seashore or not far removed from it. As proof of the former I cite the raging fury with which the subterranean ignition has often raised new islands out of the floor of the sea, and, e.g., in the year 1720 near the island of St. Michael, one of the Azores, out of a depth of 60 fathoms, through the ejection of matter, threw up out of the bottom of the sea an island that was 1 mile long and raised several fathoms above the sea. The island near Santorini in the Mediterranean, which in our century came up out of the bottom of the sea before the eyes of many people, and many other examples that I pass over for the sake of prolixity, are irrefutable proofs of this.",["- Erschütterung = shock (shaking); Boden = floor/ground; Grund = bottom (not \"ground\" in the sense of basis); Meeresgrund = bottom of the sea.","- Beweis = proof; Materie = matter; Klafter = fathom; Meile = mile (German mile not converted).","- \"Zum Beweise des ersteren\": \"the former\" = shocks in the sea bottom."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0049":["How often do seafarers not suffer a seaquake; and in some regions, especially in the neighborhood of certain islands, the seas are sufficiently filled with pumice stones and other kinds of ejecta of a fire that has broken out through the floor of the ocean. The observation of the frequent shocks of the sea bottom is connected in a natural way with the question; why among all places of the solid land none are subject to more violent and more frequent earthquakes than those that lie not far from the seashore. This latter proposition has an indubitable correctness: Let us run through the history of earthquakes, then we find infinitely many misfortunes that have befallen cities or countries by earthquakes which lie near the seashore, but very few, and then of slight significance, which have been perceived in the middle of the solid land. Ancient history already reports to us dreadful devastations that this calamity has carried out on the sea coasts of Asia Minor or Africa. But neither among these nor among the more recent do we find considerable shocks in the middle of great countries. Italy, which is a peninsula, most of the islands of all seas, the part of Peru that lies on the seashore, suffer the greatest attacks of this evil. And even in our days all western and southern coasts of Portugal and Spain have been shaken far more than the interior of the solid land. I give the following solution of both questions.",["- Satz = proposition; Gattung = kind (here not \"genus\"); Auswurf = ejecta.","- \"desfesten\" printed as one word (OCR/original): read \"des festen Landes\".","- Syntax kept: \"woher unter allen Oertern ... keine ... unterworfen sind, als diejenige\" is awkward in the German and left so.","- \"welche in der Mitte...\" attaches loosely to the preceding noun phrase; ambiguity retained. \"Erheblichkeit\" = significance."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0050":["Among all the continuing caverns that are comprised under the topmost crust of the earth, those that run on under the bottom of the sea must without doubt be the narrowest, because there the continued ground of the solid land has sunk down to the greatest depth, and must rest far lower on its lowest foundation than the places that lie toward the middle of the land. Now it is known, however, that in narrow caverns an ignited, expanding matter must act around itself more violently than where it can spread out. Besides, it is natural to believe that, since in the subterranean heating it is not to be doubted, the boiling-up mineral and ignitable matters will very often have come into flux, as the streams of sulphur and the lava, which have often been poured out of the fire-spewing mountains, can attest, and will therefore, because of the natural slope of the ground, have at all times flowed off from the subterranean vaults to the lowest caverns of the sea bottom, and thus, because of the abundant supply of ignitable matter, more frequent and more powerful shocks must occur here.",["- Hölen = caverns; Rinde = crust; Materie = matter; Grund/Grundlage = bottom / foundation.","- Sentence beginning \"Ueberdem ist es natürlich zu glauben, daß, da ...\" is syntactically tangled in the German; the construction (daß ... werden ... gewesen seyn ... und werden ... abgeflossen seyn) is kept as it stands.","- \"Grüfte\" = vaults/vaults of the earth (Gruft); \"Fluß\" = flux."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0051":["Mr. Bouguer rightly conjectures that the penetrating of the sea water, through the opening of some fissures in the floor of the same, must bring the mineral matters, naturally inclined to heating, into the most violent boiling-up. For we know that nothing can put the fire of heated minerals into more dreadful fury than the inflow of water, which increases the raging of it until its force, spreading out on all sides, has barred its further access, through the ejection of all earthly matters and the stopping up of the opening.",["- muthmaaßet = conjectures; Materien = matters; Gewalt = force.","- \"desselben\" (first) refers to the sea (Meer), the later \"deßelben\" to the fire/water in turn; the German pronouns are left as ambiguous as printed."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0052":["In my judgment the preeminent violence with which a ground lying on the seashore is shaken proceeds in part quite naturally from the weight with which the sea water burdens its neighboring ground. For everyone easily sees that the force with which the subterranean fire strives to raise this vault, on which so astonishing a load rests, must be very much held back, and, since it finds here no space for its expansion before it, must turn its whole force against the floor of the dry land, which is most nearly connected with it.",["- Erachten = judgment (judging); Gewicht = weight; Raum = space (as in glossary); Gewalt = force.","- \"in dem\" (= indem) read as \"since\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0053":["On the direction according to which the ground is shaken by\nan earthquake.",["- Richtung = direction."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0054":["The direction according to which the earthquake spreads into wide countries is different from that according to which the ground is shaken on which it exerts its force. If the topmost cover of the hidden vault, in which the ignited matter expands, has a horizontal direction, then it must be alternately raised and lowered in a perpendicular position, because there is nothing that could steer the motion more toward one side than the other. But if the earth-layer that makes up the vaulting is inclined toward one side, then the shaking force of the subterranean fire also drives it upward with an oblique direction against the horizon, and one can infer the direction according to which the swaying of the ground must always occur, if that were always certainly known according to which the stratum of earth under which the fire-vault is located slopes. The slope of the topmost surface of the shaken ground is no certain mark of the oblique position that the vault has in its whole thickness; for the earth-layers that lie on top can make manifold bendings and hills, according to which the lowest foundation does not direct itself at all. Buffon is of the opinion: that all the different strata that are found on the earth have as base a general bedrock, which covers from above all the enclosed deep cavities, and some parts of which are commonly laid bare on the summits of high mountains, where rain and storm winds have washed off the loose substance entirely. This opinion receives much probability from what earthquakes give to be recognized. For so raging a force as earthquakes exert would long since have shattered and ground down any vaulting other than a rocky one through the often renewed attacks.",["- Merkmal = mark (nota); Meinung = opinion; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; erkennen = recognize (as glossary).","- \"so muß er\" — \"er\" has no clear antecedent in the German (probably Decke); left as \"it\".","- \"Richtung ... nach welcher\": \"nach\" rendered \"according to\" throughout; \"wenn diejenige allemal sicher bekannt wäre\" left elliptical (antecedent \"Richtung\").","- Erdlage/Schichte = earth-layer / stratum; Base = base; Gruft = vault."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0055":["The slope of this vaulting at the seashore is without doubt inclined toward the sea, and thus sloping in that direction in which the sea lies from the place. On the bank of a great river it must be sloping in the direction in which the run-off of the stream goes; for if one considers the very long stretches, often exceeding several hundred miles, which the rivers traverse on the firm land without making standing pools or lakes along the way: then one can explain this uniform slope by nothing else than by that exceedingly firm foundation which, in that it inclines uniformly toward the sea-bottom without manifold indentations, provides the river with an oblique surface for its run-off. Hence it is to be presumed: that the swaying of the ground of a shaken city that lies on a great river will occur in the direction of this river, as in the Tagus from evening and morning *); but that of one that lies on the seashore, in the direction in which this inclines toward the sea. I have cited in another place what the situation of the ground can contribute to completely destroying a city, whose main streets continue in just the same direction as that in which this is sloping, in the event of an earthquake; this remark is not a notion of mere presumption; it is a matter of experience. Gentil, who himself had occasion to gather good information about very many earthquakes, reports this as an observation that has been confirmed by many examples: that if the direction in which the ground is shaken runs parallel to the direction in which the city is built, it will be overturned entirely, whereas, if it cuts across this at right angles, less damage occurs.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0056":["*) Just as a river has a sloping obliqueness toward the sea, so the lands at the sides have a slope toward its bed. If this latter holds of the whole stratum of the earth itself, and this possesses just such a sloping at the greatest depth, then the direction of the earth-shaking will also be determined by this.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0057":["The *Histoire* of the Royal Academy at Paris reports: that when Smyrna, which lies on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean Sea, was shaken in the year 1688, all walls that had the direction from east to west were collapsed, and those that were built from north to south remained standing.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0058":["The shaken ground, namely, makes some swayings, and moves most strongly everything that is erected on it lengthwise in the direction of the swaying. All bodies that have a great mobility, e.g. the chandeliers in the churches, are accustomed during earthquakes to indicate the direction in which the shocks occur, and are far surer marks for a city, for taking from them the situation in which it must be built up, than the somewhat more doubtful indications already cited.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0059":["On the Interconnection of Earthquakes\nwith the Seasons.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0060":["The French academician, Mr. Bouguer, already cited several times, adduces in his voyage to Peru that although the earthquakes in this country occur often enough in all seasons, the most fearful and most frequent are nevertheless felt in the autumn months toward the end of the year. This observation finds numerous confirmations not only in America, in that, besides the downfall of the city of Lima 10 years ago and the sinking of another equally populous one in the previous century, very many examples of it have been noted, but also in our part of the world we find, besides the latest earthquake, still many examples in history, of shakings and ejections of fire-spewing mountains, which have occurred more frequently in the autumn months than in any other season. Should not a common cause occasion this agreement, and upon which can one more suitably cast the presumption than upon the rains which in Peru last from September until April in the long valley between the Cordillera mountains, and which are also most frequent with us around autumn time? We know that, in order to occasion an underground fire, nothing is necessary but to bring the mineral materials in the caverns of the earth into fermentation. But water does this, when it has strained through the clefts of the mountains and runs off in the deep passages. The rains first stimulated the fermentation, which in the middle of October thrust so many foreign vapors out of the interior of the earth. But these very ones coaxed still more moist influences out of the atmosphere, and the water that penetrated through the crevices of the rocks into the deepest vaults completed the heating that had begun.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0061":["On the Influence of Earthquakes on the Atmosphere.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0062":["We have seen above an example of effects that the shakings of the earth have on our air. It is to be believed that more natural appearances depend on the eruptions of the underground heated vapors than one commonly imagines. It would scarcely be possible that in the weather such an irregularity and so little that agrees should be encountered, if foreign causes did not at times enter into our atmosphere, and bring its regular alterations into disorder. Can one well think of a probable ground why, since the course of the sun and of the moon is bound to its laws that are always similar to themselves, since water and earth, if one takes it in the large, always remain the same, the course of the weather nevertheless, even in an abstract of many years, turns out almost always different. Since the unhappy shaking, and shortly before it, we have had such a deviating weather throughout our whole part of the world that one can be excused if on that account one casts some presumption on the earthquakes. It is true, one has indeed had warm winter weather formerly, without any earthquake having preceded; but is one then certain that a fermentation in the interior of the earth has not very often driven vapors through the rock clefts, the fissures of the earth strata, and even through their loose substance, which could have drawn after them notable alterations in the atmosphere? Musschenbroek, after he has remarked that only in this century, and indeed since 1716, really bright northern lights have been seen in Europe and as far as its southern countries, holds as the most probable cause of this alteration in the atmosphere that the fire-spewing mountains and the earthquakes, which had raged frequently some years before, ejected inflammable and volatile vapors, which through the natural outflow of the uppermost air toward the north have heaped up there, and produced the fiery air-appearances which have since been seen so frequently, and that they presumably must consume themselves little by little, until new exhalations replace the loss again.",["- 0055: *Erdbeben* \"earthquake\"; *Erschütterung* \"shaking\"; *Schwankung* \"swaying\"; *Abhang* and *abschießig/abschüßig* both rendered \"slope/sloping.\" *Strohm* = \"stream\"; \"von Abend und Morgen\" kept literal (\"from evening and morning\").","- 0055: *Kenntnis* = \"information\" (as in glossary); *Beobachtung* = \"observation\"; *Erfahrung* = \"experience.\"","- 0055: \"Diese Anmerkung ... Einfall der bloßen Vermuthung\" kept literal; \"es ist eine Sache der Erfahrung\" is \"a matter of experience.\"","- 0056: The footnote asterisk is carried to its place in 0055 as \"*)\". *Schiefe* = \"obliqueness\"; *Abschießigkeit* = \"sloping.\"","- 0057: *Historie* left as \"Histoire\" in italics for the French academy's title (the German has *Historie*); \"eingestürzt wurden\" kept as passive \"were collapsed.\"","- 0058: *Merkmaale* = \"marks\"; *Kennzeichen* = \"indications\" (two German words for one English sense, *Merkmal* = \"mark\" per glossary).","- 0060: *Zusammenhang* = \"interconnection\"; *Beobachtung* = \"observation\"; *Materie* = \"materials/matter\" (here *mineralische Materien* rendered \"mineral materials\"); *Gährung* = \"fermentation\"; *Ursache* = \"cause.\"","- 0062: *Erscheinung* in \"Naturerscheinungen\" = \"natural appearances\"; *wahrscheinlich* = \"probable\"; *Grund* = \"ground\"; *Gesetze* = \"laws\"; \"Auszug\" rendered \"abstract\"; *Vermuthung* = \"presumption.\" In 0057 *Historie* is the German word, not French."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0063":["According to these principles, let us investigate whether it might not be in accordance with nature that an altered weather, like that which we have had, could be a consequence of that catastrophe. The clear winter weather, and the cold that accompanies it, is not merely a consequence of the greater distance of the sun from our zenith at this season; for we often feel that, notwithstanding this, the air can be very temperate; but rather the draft of air from the north, which at times also turns into an east wind, brings us a chilled air all the way from the frigid zone, which covers our waters with ice and lets us feel a part of the winter of the north pole. This draft of air from north to south is so natural in the autumn and winter months, if foreign causes do not interrupt it, that in the ocean, at a sufficient distance from all solid land, this north or northeast wind is encountered uninterruptedly the whole time through. It also proceeds quite naturally from the effect of the sun, which then rarefies the air over the southern hemisphere, and thereby causes the northern air's drawing-in: so that this must be regarded as a constant law, which by the constitution of the countries may indeed be somewhat altered, but cannot be abrogated. If now subterranean fermentations expel heated vapors somewhere in the countries that lie to the south of us: then these will at first diminish the height of the atmosphere in the region where they ascend, by weakening its force of expansion and causing downpours, hurricanes and the like. But in the sequel this part of the atmosphere, since it is laden with so many vapors, will move the neighboring one by its weight and cause a draft of air from south to north. Since now, however, the striving of the atmosphere from north to south is natural in our zone at this season, these two movements striving against each other will check one another, and first draw after them a murky rainy air, on account of the vapors driven together, but yet with it a high stand of the barometer *), because the air compressed by the strife of two winds must make up a high column; and by this one will learn to find one's way into the apparent incorrectness of the barometers, when with a high stand of them the weather is nevertheless rainy, for then precisely this wetness of the air is an effect of two draughts of air striving against one another, which drive the vapors together and nevertheless can considerably condense the air and make it heavier.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0064":["*) Something of this kind was observed almost constantly during this wet winter weather.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0065":["I cannot pass over in silence: that on the terrible day of All Saints the magnets in Augsburg threw off their load and the magnetic needles were brought into disorder. Boyle already reports that once after an earthquake in Naples just the same thing occurred. We know the hidden nature of the magnet too little to be able to give a ground of this appearance.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0066":["On the utility of earthquakes.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0067":["One will be startled to see so fearful a rod of punishment of human beings recommended from the side of usefulness. I am certain one would gladly renounce it, only to be relieved of the fear and the dangers that are bound up with it. Thus are we human beings constituted. Having made an unlawful claim to every amenity of life, we want to purchase no advantages at a cost. We demand that the earth's ground should be so constituted: that one could wish to dwell on it eternally. Beyond this we imagine that we would govern everything better to our advantage, if providence had asked for our vote about it. Thus we wish, e.g., to have the rain in our power, so that we could distribute it over the year according to our convenience, and always have pleasant days to enjoy between the gloomy ones. But we forget the springs, which we nevertheless could not do without, and which yet in such a way would not be maintained at all. Just so we do not know the utility that the very causes which frighten us in earthquakes could procure for us, and yet would gladly see them banished.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0068":["As human beings who were born in order to die, we cannot bear that some have died in the earthquake, and as those who are strangers here and possess no property, we are inconsolable that goods have been lost which in a short time would by the common way of nature have been abandoned of themselves.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0069":["It is easily guessed: that if human beings build on a ground that is filled with inflammable materials, sooner or later the whole splendor of their buildings may collapse through tremors; but must one therefore become impatient over the ways of providence. Would it not be better to judge thus: It was necessary that earthquakes should occur from time to time on the earth's ground, but it was not necessary that we should build splendid dwelling-places over it. The inhabitants in Peru live in houses that are walled only to a slight height, and the rest consists of reed. The human being must learn to accommodate himself to nature, but he wants nature to accommodate itself to him.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0070":["Whatever harm the cause of earthquakes may ever have awakened for human beings on one side, it can easily compensate him for with gain on the other side. We know that the warm baths, which in the sequel of times may perhaps have been serviceable to a considerable part of human beings for the promotion of health, have their mineral property and heat through the very same causes through which the heatings occur in the interior of the earth which set it in motion.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0071":["It has long been conjectured: that the ore-deposits in the mountains are a slow effect of the subterranean heat, which brings the metals to ripeness through gradual effects, in that it forms and cooks them by penetrating vapors in the middle of the rock.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0072":["Our atmosphere requires, besides the coarse and dead materials that it contains in itself, also a certain effective *principium*, volatile salts and parts that are to enter into the composition of plants, in order to move and unfold them. Is it not credible that the formations of nature, which constantly expend a great part of this, and the alteration that all matter finally suffers through dissolution and composition, would in time wholly consume the most effective particles, if from time to time a new influx did not occur. At least the soil grows ever less forceful when it nourishes forceful plants, but rest and rain bring it again into the state. But where would the forceful matter finally come from, which is used up without replacement, if another source did not maintain its influx? And this is presumably the store that the subterranean vaults contain of the most effective and most volatile materials; of which they from time to time spread a part over the surface of the earth. I note further: that Hales, with very fortunate success, frees prisons, and in general all places whose air is infected with animal exhalations, by the fumigating of sulphur. The fire-spewing mountains expel an immeasurable multitude of sulphurous vapors into the atmosphere; who knows, whether the animal exhalations with which this is laden would not in time become harmful, if those did not furnish a forceful counter-means against it.",["- 0063: Folge = \"consequence\"/\"sequel\" (glossary: sequel; here \"consequence\" for eine Folge von, \"in der Folge\" = \"in the sequel\"); Gesetz = law; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Wirkung = effect; Luftkreis = atmosphere (also Atmosphäre, rendered \"atmosphere\" for both).","- 0063: \"Zug der Luft\" rendered \"draft of air\" and later \"draughts of air\" (Luftzüge); \"Herbeyzug\" = \"drawing-in\" (literal). \"Gewäßer\" = waters.","- 0063: \"ihren Herbeyzug\" — \"its/their\" ambiguous in German between die Luft and der nordlichen (Halbkugel); rendered \"the northern air's\" for the feminine reading; \"der nordlichen\" could be supplied as \"hemisphere\". Kept as an interpretive choice; doubtful.","- 0063: \"sie\" in \"die sie begleitet\" and \"Zug der Luft ... der\" follow the German; the asterisk marks the footnote (0064) as printed.","- 0067: \"Vorsehung\" = providence; \"Strafruthe\" = rod of punishment; \"Vortheil\" = advantage.","- 0069: \"Grunde\" = ground (Grund); \"nöthig\" and \"nothwendig\" both rendered \"necessary\" (Latinate/native pair not distinguished).","- 0070: \"Was auch die Ursache ... vor Schaden erweckt hat\" is syntactically awkward in the original; kept literal. \"vor\" = \"for\" (modern \"für\").","- 0072: \"Principium\" kept in Latin italics; \"Naturbildungen\" = \"formations of nature\"; \"Gegenmittel\" = \"counter-means\" (Mittel = means)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0073":["Finally the warmth in the interior of the earth seems to me to furnish a forceful proof of the efficacy and the great utility of the heatings that take place in deep vaults. It is established by daily experiences: that in great, indeed in the greatest depths, to which human beings in the interior of mountains have only attained, there is a perpetual warmth, which one cannot possibly ascribe to the effect of the sun. Boyle adduces a good number of testimonies, from which it is evident that in all the deepest shafts one finds the upper region at first far colder than the outer air when it is summertime, but the deeper one lets oneself down, the warmer one finds the region, so that in the greatest depth the workers are compelled to take off their clothes during their work. Everyone comprehends easily that, since the warmth of the sun penetrates into the earth only to a very slight depth, it can no longer do the slightest effect in the very lowest vaults; and that the warmth found there depends on a cause that prevails only in the greatest depth, can moreover be seen from the diminished warmth, the higher one comes up from below, even in summertime. Boyle, after he has cautiously compared and examined the experiences set up, concludes very reasonably: that in the lowest caverns, to which we cannot attain, constant heatings, and an inextinguishable fire maintained thereby, must be encountered, which communicates its warmth to the uppermost crust.",["- *Beweis* = proof; *Erfahrung* = experience (plural \"experiences\" kept for *Erfahrungen*); *begreift* = comprehends; *Wirkung* = effect (also in *Wirksamkeit*, rendered \"efficacy\").","- *Grüfte* and *Schachte*/*Hölen* rendered \"vaults,\" \"shafts,\" \"caverns\"; *Tiefe* = depth.","- \"Do the slightest effect\" follows *Wirkung thun*; \"have only attained\" follows *nur gelanget sind*, whose sense is ambiguous in the German.","- *schließt* rendered \"concludes\" (cf. *Schluß*); *Ursache* = cause.","- The antecedent of \"which communicates\" (*das*) is grammatically *Feuer*; kept ambiguous with \"fire.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0074":["If this is so, as one cannot refrain from granting, shall we not have to promise ourselves the most advantageous effects from this subterranean fire, which at all times maintains a mild warmth for the earth, at the time when the sun withdraws its own from us, which is capable of promoting the drive of plants and the economy of the realms of nature. And can the disadvantage that accrues to the human race through one or another eruption of the same, in view of the appearance of so much utility, relieve us of the gratitude that we owe to providence for all its arrangements.",["- Second sentence ends with a period though phrased as a question; kept as in the German (no question mark).","- *Wirkung* = effect; *Anschein* = appearance (not *Erscheinung*); *Vorsehung* = providence; *Trieb* = drive; *Anstalten* = arrangements.","- \"the same\" (*derselben*) and \"its\" (*derselben*, last clause) left as ambiguous in reference as in the German."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0075":["The grounds that I have adduced for the encouragement of the same are admittedly not of the kind of those that procure the greatest conviction and certainty. But even conjectures are worthy of acceptance when it matters to move human beings to gratitude toward the highest being, who even when he chastises is worthy of veneration and love.",["- *Gründe* = grounds; *Überzeugung* = conviction; *Gewisheit* = certainty; *Wesen* = being.","- *derselben* (\"of the same\") has no clear antecedent in this passage; left as is. *Dankbegierde* rendered \"gratitude\" (literally \"desire to thank\")."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0076":["Remark.",["- *Anmerkung* = remark."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0077":["I had adduced above that earthquakes drive sulphurous exhalations through the vault of the earth. The latest reports of the shafts in the Saxon mountains confirm it through a new example. One now finds them so filled with sulphurous vapors that the workers must abandon them. The occurrence at Tuam in Ireland, where a luminous aerial appearance in the shape of pennants and flags appeared on the sea, which gradually changed its colors and finally spread a bright light, whereupon a violent shock of earthquake followed, is a new confirmation of this. The transformation of the colors from the darkest blue to red and finally into a bright white gleam is to be ascribed to the exhalation that broke forth, at first very thin, which was gradually increased through more frequent influx of more vapors, which, as is known in natural science, must pass through the degrees of light from the blue color to the red, and finally into a white gleam. All of this went before the shock. It was also a proof: that the hearth of the ignition had been in the bottom of the sea, as indeed the earthquake itself was chiefly noticed on the sea coast.",["- *Begebenheit* = occurrence; *Beweis* = proof; *Grade* = degrees; *Naturwissenschaft* = natural science; *Grund* = bottom (of the sea; same word as \"ground\").","- *Lufterscheinung* rendered \"aerial appearance\" (*Erscheinung* = appearance).","- \"which gradually changed its colors\" (*die ... änderten*): plural verb, so the antecedent is ambiguous (pennants and flags / appearance); kept ambiguous.","- *Ausdämpfung* = exhalation; *Dünste*/*Dämpfe* both \"vapors\"; *Heerd der Entzündung* = \"hearth of the ignition.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0078":["If one wants to extend further the remarks on the places of the earth where the most frequent and severest shakings have always been felt, one can add to them: that the western coasts have at all times suffered far more attacks of them than the eastern. In Italy, in Portugal, in South America, indeed even recently in Ireland, experience has confirmed this agreement. Peru, which lies on the western seashore of the new world, has almost daily shakings, while meanwhile Brazil, which has the ocean toward the east, has noticed nothing of them. If one wants to conjecture some causes of this strange analogy, one can well forgive a Gautier, a painter, when he seeks the cause of all earthquakes in the rays of the sun, the source of his colors and of his art, and imagines that the very same drive our great globe around from evening toward morning, in that they strike the western coasts more strongly and just thereby these coasts would be disquieted with so many shakings. But in a sound natural science such a notion hardly deserves refutation. To me the ground of this law seems to stand in connection with another, for which no sufficient explanation has yet been given: that namely the western and southern coasts of almost all countries are more steeply sloping than the eastern and northern, which is confirmed as well by the sight of the map as by the reports of Dampier, who found them so almost universally on all his sea voyages. If one derives the curvings of the firm land from the sinkings, then in the regions of the greatest steepness deeper and more caverns must be encountered than where the earth's crust has only a moderate slope. But this has, as we saw above, a natural interconnection with the earthquakes.",["- *Erschütterung* = shaking (also *Erderschütterungen*, \"earthquakes\"/\"shakings of the earth\"; *Erdbeben* = earthquake); *Grund* = ground; *Gesetz* = law; *Verbindung* = connection; *Zusammenhang* = interconnection.","- *Erklährung* (sic) = explanation; *Naturwissenschaft* = natural science; *Ursache* = cause.","- *Anmerkungen* rendered \"remarks\"; *Analogie* = analogy.","- \"of them\" (*davon*) left without specified antecedent, as in the German; *Einsinkungen* = \"sinkings\"; *Beugungen* = \"curvings.\"","- *wenn er ... sucht, und sich einbildet, eben dieselbe treiben*: \"the very same\" plural (*dieselbe*) taken as the rays; the German is loose."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0079":["Concluding Consideration.",["- *Schlußbetrachtung*: *Betrachtung* = consideration."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0080":["The sight of so many wretched ones as the latest catastrophe has made among our fellow citizens shall rouse philanthropy and let us feel a part of the misfortune that has befallen them with such severity. But one offends very much against this when one regards such fates at all times as imposed punitive judgments that befall the devastated cities on account of their evil deeds, and when we consider these unfortunates as the target of the revenge of GOD, over whom his justice pours out all its bowls of wrath. This way of judging is a culpable presumption that arrogates to itself to have insight into the aims of the divine counsels and to interpret them according to its insights.",["- *Urtheil(s)* = judgment, *Art des Urtheils* rendered \"way of judging\"; *Strafgerichte* = \"punitive judgments\" (*Gericht* = judgment); *Absichten* = aims; *Einsichten* = insights.","- *Menschenliebe* = philanthropy (literally \"love of humanity\"); *Catastrophe* in roman type in the original.","- *Vorwitz* = presumption (literally \"forwardness of wit\"); *sich anmaaßet* = arrogates to itself."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0081":["The human being is so taken with himself that he regards himself merely as the sole target of the arrangements of GOD, just as if these had no other aim than him alone, in order to arrange the measures in the government of the world accordingly. We know that the whole sum total of nature is a worthy object of divine wisdom and of his arrangements. We are a part of it and want to be the whole. The rules of the perfection of nature in the large shall come into no consideration, and everything shall merely fit itself in correct relation to us. What in the world tends to comfort and pleasure, that, one represents to oneself, is there merely for our sake, and nature begins no alterations that become any cause of discomfort for human beings, except in order to chastise them, to threaten, or to exercise revenge on them.",["- *Inbegriff* = sum total; *Gegenstand* = object; *Vollkommenheit* = perfection; *Betrachtung* = consideration; *Welt* = world; *Theil* = part.","- *Anstalten* = arrangements; *Augenmerk* rendered \"aim\" (not the glossary's *Absicht*, also \"aim\"; one English word for two German words).","- *stellt man sich vor* = \"one represents to oneself\" (*Vorstellung* = representation).","- *Ziel* = target; *Veränderungen* = alterations.","- *vor den Menschen* kept as \"for human beings\"; *unsert* (sic) read *unsert[wegen]*, \"our sake.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0082":["Nevertheless we see that infinitely many villains fall asleep in peace, that earthquakes have shaken certain countries from time immemorial, without distinction of the old or new inhabitants, that Christian Peru is moved just as well as the heathen, and that many cities have remained free of this devastation from the beginning, which can arrogate to themselves no preeminence of blamelessness over those.",["- *Unsträflichkeit* = blamelessness; *Vorzug* = preeminence; *Verwüstung* = devastation.","- The final clause (*die über jene sich keines Vorzuges ... anmaaßen können*) is ambiguous: \"which\" may refer to the cities freed or to those compared; \"those\" (*jene*) is left without clear antecedent.","- *von je her* / *von Anbeginn* = \"from time immemorial\" / \"from the beginning.\""]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0083":["Thus the human being is in the dark when he wants to guess the intentions that GOD has before his eyes in the government of the world. But we are in no uncertainty when it comes to the application, how we are to use these ways of providence in conformity with its purpose. The human being is not born to build eternal huts on this stage of vanity. Since his whole life has a far nobler goal, how beautifully do not all the devastations agree with it, which the inconstancy of the world lets shine forth even in those things that seem to us the greatest and most important, in order to remind us: that the goods of the earth can provide no satisfaction to our drive to happiness?",["- Absichten = intentions (glossary gives \"aim; intention\"); Zwecke = purpose (glossary \"end; purpose\"). Both rendered as in the glossary.","- Vorsehung = providence; Welt = world; Regierung = government; Ziel = goal; Glückseligkeit = happiness.","- \"wie schön stimmen dazu nicht alle die Verheerungen\": the \"nicht\" is the German expletive/rhetorical negation, kept literally as \"do not\"; \"blicken läßt\" rendered \"lets shine forth\" (literally \"lets glance/gleam\").","- \"Güter der Erden\" = \"goods of the earth\" (archaic plural Erden retained as a genitive of Erde)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0084":["I am far from hereby indicating, as if the human being were left over to an unchangeable fate of the laws of nature without regard to his particular advantages. Just the same highest wisdom, from which the course of nature borrows that correctness which requires no amendment, has subordinated the lower purposes to the higher, and in just those intentions in which it has often made the most important exceptions to the general rules of nature, in order to attain the infinitely higher purposes that are far elevated above all natural means, the guidance of the human race in the regiment of the world will also prescribe laws to the course of natural things themselves. When a city or country becomes aware of the misfortune with which divine providence sets it or its neighbors in terror: is it then still doubtful what party it has to take in order to forestall the ruin that threatens them, and are the signs still ambiguous for making comprehensible the intentions, to whose accomplishment all the ways of providence unanimously either invite or drive the human being?",["- Zwecke = purposes; Absichten = intentions; Naturmittel = natural means (Mittel = means); Naturgesetze = laws of nature; Naturdinge = natural things (Ding = thing).","- \"dem Laufe der Naturdinge Gesetze vorschreiben\": \"dem Laufe\" is dative, \"Gesetze\" accusative; kept as \"prescribe laws to the course.\"","- \"einem unwandelbaren Schicksale der Naturgesetze\": genitive could be read as \"fate consisting in / of the laws of nature\"; left as in German.","- \"begreiflich\" = comprehensible (cf. begreifen = comprehend); \"Parthey\" = party; \"Regimente\" = regiment.","- \"überlaßen sey\" = subjunctive; \"left over to\" is literal. \"Nachbaren\" = neighbors (sic)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0085":["A prince who, driven by a noble heart, lets himself be moved by these afflictions of the human race to avert the misery of war from those who moreover are threatened on all sides by grave misfortunes, is a beneficent instrument in the kind hand of GOD, and a gift that he makes to the peoples of the earth, whose worth they can never estimate according to its magnitude.",["- Größe = magnitude; Werth = worth; schätzen = estimate.","- \"überdem\" = \"moreover\" (could also be read \"besides/on top of that\").","- \"dessen Werth sie ... nach seiner Größe\": \"seiner\" could refer to the gift or the worth; the ambiguity is preserved (\"its\").","- \"Völkern\" = peoples."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's\ncontinued consideration\nof the earth-tremors perceived for some time\n.\nIn: Weekly Königsberg Inquiry and Advertisement News. Saturday, April 10. Anno 1756. No. 15.\nConclusion in: Saturday, April 17. Anno 1756. No. 16.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Erderschütterungen = \"earth-tremors\" (literally \"earth-shakings\"), the same word as in the title, rendered without choosing among \"earthquake\" synonyms; Erdbeben appears elsewhere as \"earthquake\".","- The line break after \"Zeit\" in the German is not marked, so the full stop in my rendering of the title is an artifact; read as one title line \"of the earth-tremors perceived for some time.\"","- \"Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten\" rendered literally."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0002":["The 10th of April 1756.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0003":["The fire of the subterranean vaults is not yet calmed. The tremors only lately lasted still and frightened countries to which this evil has from time immemorial been unknown. The disorder in the atmosphere has changed the season on half of the globe. The most ignorant want to have guessed the cause of it. One hears some say, without understanding and reflection: the earth has shifted and has come closer to the sun, I do not know by how many degrees. A judgment that would be worthy of a Kindermann, if he were to rise again, to sell dreams of a deranged head for observations. To this belong also those who bring the comets back onto the track, after Whiston himself taught the philosophers to fear them. It is a common excess that one fetches the origin of an evil from some thousands of miles away when one can find it nearby. So the Turks do it with the plague; so one did it with the locusts, with the cattle plague, and God knows with what other evils besides. One is ashamed merely to be able to see something nearby. To catch sight of causes at infinite distance, that is first a real proof of a sharp-sighted understanding.",["- Verstand: \"without understanding (Verstand) and reflection (Nachdenken)\" in the second sentence; \"an understanding\" in the last sentence also Verstand. Urtheil = judgment; Beweis = proof; Beobachtungen = observations; Ursache = cause; Ursprung = origin.","- \"Erschütterungen\" = tremors (same root as Erderschütterungen above); \"Erdkreis\" = globe, \"Luftkreis\" = atmosphere (literally \"air-circle\").","- \"Kindermann\" is a proper name (apparently a Kindermann, an author of fantastic voyages), left untranslated; \"ein Kindermann\" with article as in German.","- \"wieder aufstände\" could be read as \"rose again\" (from the dead) or \"rose up again\"; \"auf die Bahn bringen\" = \"bring onto the track\" (idiom: raise a topic; also orbit).","- \"vor andern Uebeln\" = \"besides/among other evils\"; \"mehr\" = \"more,\" rendered \"besides.\" \"gemein\" = common."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0004":["Among all the conjectures that, on a great departure from the rules of a correct natural science, some who do not know how to examine them can easily win over, belongs the thought that is ascribed in the public reports to Herr Professor Profe at Altona. It is indeed long since one, on perceiving great occurrences on the earth, no longer casts suspicion on the planets on that account. The lists of the harsh accusations that our dear forefathers the Herren astrologers made against these stars are deposited in the archive of obsolete chimeras, next to the true history of the fairies, the sympathetic wonders of Digby and Vallemont, and the nocturnal occurrences of the Blocksberg. But since natural science has been cleansed of these whims, a Newton has discovered an actual force and confirmed it through experience, which even the most distant planets exert against one another and against our earth. But to all the misfortune of those who want to drive the application of this remarkable property to excess, the measure of this force and the way of its effect is determined, and indeed through just the same observation with the assistance of geometry to which we have to thank the revelation of it. One can now no longer make us believe, what one wants, of its effects. We have the scales in hand, on which we can weigh the effects against the alleged cause.",["- Naturwissenschaft = natural science (Wissenschaft = science); Erfahrung = experience; Beobachtung = observation; Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Art = way; bestimmt = determined; Maaß = measure.","- \"Vorfälle\" and \"Begebenheiten\" both rendered \"occurrences\" (Begebenheit is the glossary's \"occurrence\"; Vorfall is not in the glossary).","- \"Herren Astrologen\": \"Herren\" kept in German as in the original (\"the Herren astrologers\" is a literal retention of the apposition).","- \"Zu allem Unglück vor diejenige\": \"vor\" = \"für\"; \"to all the misfortune of those\" preserves the elliptical construction.","- \"Profe\" is sic (the original name is probably Prof. Prosch/Profe); doubtful, not corrected. \"Hrn.\" = Herr.","- \"mit Beyhülfe der Geometrie\" could attach to \"Beobachtung\" or to \"bestimmt\"; left ambiguous in English."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0005":["If a man who has once had it told him that the moon draws the waters of the earth and thereby causes the swelling and sinking of the ocean that one calls ebb and flood, likewise that all planets are endowed with a similar force of attraction and, when they are found near the straight line drawn through the earth and sun, unite their forces of attraction with the moon's, if, I say, such a one, who has no vocation to examine the matter more exactly, conjectured that these united forces could not only bring the waters of the earth into that violent motion that we saw on the 1st of Nov., but also, through an influence on the subterranean air, could provoke the hidden tinder to the production of earthquakes, then one can demand nothing further of him. But of a connoisseur of nature one expects more. It is not enough to have hit upon a cause that has something similar to the effect; it must also be proportioned with regard to magnitude. I will adduce an example. Doctor List, an otherwise skilled member of the London Society, had perceived that the sea plant called sea-lentil has an uncommonly strong exhalation about it. He remarked that it is found abundantly on the coasts of the seas of the torrid zone. Since now a strong exhalation can easily move the air somewhat, he concluded: that the general east wind that blows constantly in these seas and extends more than 1000 miles from the land comes from it, chiefly because this plant turns toward the sun. The ridiculousness of this opinion lies merely in this: that the cause has no relation at all to the effect. It is just so with the force of the planets, if one compares it with the effect that is supposed to come from it, namely the motion of the seas and the arousal of earthquakes. One will perhaps say: do we then know the magnitude of the force with which these heavenly bodies can act on the earth? I shall answer that soon.",["- Kraft = force (also \"Anziehungskraft\" = force of attraction; \"Kräfte\" = forces); Wirkung = effect; \"wirken\" = act; Größe = magnitude; Verhältniß = relation; Meynung = opinion; schloß = concluded (schließen; cf. Schluß).","- \"Beruf\" = vocation (glossary: Bestimmung = vocation, but here Beruf; the word differs).","- \"Naturkündiger\" = connoisseur of nature (literally \"one knowing nature\"); \"Zunder\" = tinder; \"Ausduftung\" = exhalation.","- \"auf eine Ursache gerathen zu seyn\" = \"to have hit upon a cause.\"","- \"wohl auch\" in \"sondern auch wohl auch\" kept as a repetition: \"but also\"; the doubled \"auch\" is retained in sense only once. \"des Mondes seiner\" = \"the moon's\" (colloquial genitive).","- \"Gewässer\" = waters; \"Erdstrich\" = torrid zone (\"heißen Erdstrichs\" = hot zone); \"1sten Nov.\" is the Lisbon earthquake date."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0006":["Mr. Bouguer, a famous French academician, relates that during his stay in Peru a scholar, who wanted to become professor of mathematics at the university in Lima, wrote a book under the title of an astronomical clock of earthquakes, in which he undertakes to foretell these from the course of the moon. One can easily guess: that a prophet in Peru has an easy time foretelling earthquakes, since they occur there almost daily and are distinguished only by their strength. Mr. Bouguer adds that a man who, without reflection, throws about with the ascending and descending nodes of the moon, the perigee and apogee, the conjunction and opposition, may well by chance sometimes say something that is confirmed by the outcome, and admits: that he did not always prophesy unluckily. He himself conjectures that it is not entirely improbable that the moon, which moves the waters of the ocean so powerfully, could have some influence on the shaking of the earth, either by leading the water, which it raises extraordinarily, into certain fissures of the earth to which it would not otherwise have arrived, and this would cause the raging motion in the deep caverns, or through some other way of interconnection.",["- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Art = way (glossary).","- \"Akademist\" rendered \"academician\"; \"Erdnähe/Erdferne\" rendered \"perigee and apogee\" (literally \"nearness to / distance from the earth\").","- \"Erderschütterungen\" rendered \"shaking of the earth\"; \"Gewässer\" and \"Wasser\" both \"water(s)\"; \"Ocean\" printed in roman type in the source.","- \"Erdbeben\" = \"earthquake(s).\""]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0007":["If one takes into consideration that the forces of attraction of the heavenly bodies act into the innermost of matter and can therefore set into motion the air enclosed in the deepest and most hidden passages of the earth, then all influence on the earthquakes can hardly be denied to the moon. This force would, however, at most only stimulate the inflammable matters found in the earth; the remainder, the shaking, the motion of the waters, will be merely an effect of the latter.",["- Anziehungskräfte = forces of attraction (Kraft = force); Wirkung = effect; Materie = matter.","- \"Vermögen\" is absent here; \"Wirkung/wirken\" rendered \"effect/act.\"","- \"des letzteren\" (\"the latter\") is ambiguous between the inflammable matters and the force; \"dieser letzteren\" (feminine plural/genitive) points to the matters, kept as \"the latter.\""]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0008":["If one ascends from the moon further up into the planetary heaven, this faculty vanishes little by little, as the distances of the same increase and the forces of all the planets, united with one another, when one compares them with the force of the single moon, so near to us, accomplish only an infinitely small part of it.",["- Vermögen = faculty; Kraft = force.","- The syntax of the second clause (\"die Kräfte ... leisten ... nur einen unendlich kleinen Theil\") is kept as in the German; \"derselben\" (twice) refers ambiguously, as in the German."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0009":["Newton, who discovered the excellent law of attraction, which must be regarded as the most fortunate attempt that human understanding has yet made in the cognition of nature, teaches the forces of attraction of the planets that have moons around them to be found, and determines that of Jupiter, the greatest among all planets, to be somewhat less than the thousandth part of the force of attraction of the sun. The faculty of bringing forth alterations on our earth by this force decreases inversely as the cube of the distances from the same, and is therefore, in the case of Jupiter, which is more than 5 times farther removed from the earth than the sun, if one takes the ratio of its force of attraction to it, 130,000 times smaller than what the force of attraction of the sun alone can effect on our earth. Now, however, the attraction of the sun can raise the water of the ocean about 2 feet high, as experience united with calculation has made known, so the attraction of Jupiter, when it is united with that of the sun, will add to this height still the 65th part of a decimal scruple, which would amount to about the 30th part of a hair's breadth. If one considers that Mars and Venus are incomparably smaller bodies than Jupiter and the forces of attraction are proportioned to their mass, then one still does too much if one ascribes to both taken together about twice as much faculty of acting by attraction on our earth as to Jupiter, because they are about 3 times nearer to us than that one, although they possess many hundred times less corporeal content and consequently force of attraction than it. But even if I were generous enough to make their force 10 times greater, they still cannot, when they unite it, make the sea water swell by a third of a hair's thickness. If one takes the remaining planets, Mercury and Saturn, in addition and considers them all in conjunction, it becomes evident that they can still by far not increase by half a hair's breadth the swelling of the waters which the moon and the sun produce jointly. Is it now not ridiculous to fear terrible motions of the waters from the attraction of the moon and the suns, if the height to which they bring the water has been increased by the half of a hair's breadth, since without this no danger would be to be feared. All remaining circumstances wholly refute the alleged cause. Just as the moon makes the highest flood not only at the very moment when it comes nearest to the straight line drawn through sun and earth, but also some days before and afterward, so the united planets should have made motions of the waters and earthquakes for several days in succession and in these for several hours through, if they had had any share in it.",["- Verstand = understanding; Erkenntniß = cognition; Vermögen = faculty; Kraft = force; Erfahrung = experience; Inhalt = content.","- \"Würfel der Entfernungen\" = \"cube of the distances\"; \"Verhältniß\" = \"ratio.\" \"gringer\" (sic, for geringer) read \"less.\"","- \"geraten Linie\" read as \"geraden Linie\" (OCR/print slip), \"straight line.\"","- \"die Sonnen\" (plural) kept as \"the suns\"; \"Decimalscrupel\" kept as \"decimal scruple\"; \"wirds\" = \"it becomes.\"","- \"Klumpen\" rendered \"mass\"; \"sey\" (subjunctive) in \"proportionirt seyn\" rendered indicatively.","- \"Gewässer\" and \"Wasser\" both \"water(s)\"; \"Fluth\" = \"flood.\""]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0010":["I must beg my readers' pardon that I have led them around so far in the firmament, in order to be able to judge correctly of the occurrences that have taken place on our earth. The effort that one applies to stop up the sources of errors procures for us also a purified cognition. In the following piece I will take into consideration the most remarkable appearances of the great natural occurrence that have taken place since those that I have endeavored to explain in a particular treatise.",["- Begebenheiten / Naturbegebenheit = occurrence(s) (Begebenheit); Erscheinungen = appearances; Erkenntniß = cognition.","- besondern = particular; urtheilen = judge (v.); erklären = explain.","- \"seit denjenigen vorgegangen sind\" is elliptical in the German; \"since those\" kept as it stands."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0011":["The planets are acquitted before the judgment seat of reason of the accusation of having had some share in the cause of the devastation that befalls us in the earthquakes. Henceforth no one shall hold them in suspicion any further on that account. Rather, some planets have indeed been in combination, and no earthquake was felt. Peirescius saw, according to the testimony of Gassendus, the rare combination of the 3 upper planets in the year 1604, which occurs only once in 800 years, but the earth remained in safety. If the moon, upon which alone the conjecture could still fall with some probability, had a share in it, then the cooperating causes would have to be present in such full measure that even the weakest external influence could give the decision of the alteration. For the moon often comes into the position in which it exerts the greatest effect on the ground of the earth, but it does not just as often excite earthquakes. That of the 1st of November occurred soon after the last quarter; but then its influences are the weakest, as the Newtonian theory and experience show. Let us therefore ask for the cause only on our dwelling place itself; we have the cause under our feet.",["- Vernunft = reason; Verbindung = combination (the glossary's term; the astronomical sense, \"conjunction,\" is not marked in the German); Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Vermuthung = conjecture; Wirkung = effect; Erfahrung = experience.","- \"Ausschlag der Veränderung\" rendered literally; \"den Ausschlag geben\" = \"give the decision.\"","- \"sie\" in \"Forthin soll sie niemand\" is the planets; \"Erdboden\" = \"ground of the earth\"; \"Wohnplatze\" = \"dwelling place.\"","- \"Nov.\" expanded to \"November\"; \"Peirescius\" and \"Gassendus\" kept as printed."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0012":["Since those shocks that were already cited before, none have occurred that extended into wider countries than that of the 18th of Feb. It was felt in France, England, Germany and the Netherlands. In most places, as was reported from Westphalia, the Hanoverian and Magdeburg territories, it was more to be compared to a light rocking of a ground moved by violent underground storms than to the thrusts of an ignited matter. Only the uppermost stories in the buildings felt the swaying, below on the ground it was scarcely noticed. Already on the 13th and 14th before, shocks were felt in the Netherlands and the neighboring places, and on these days, chiefly from the 16th to the 18th, hurricanes raged far and wide in Germany, Poland, England, lightning and thunderstorms showed themselves, in short the atmosphere was brought into a kind of fermentation, which can serve as confirmation of what we have already remarked on another occasion, namely that earthquakes or the underground ignitions that are their cause alter our atmosphere, in that they expel foreign vapors into it.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0013":["Here and there some sinkings of the ground have occurred. Pieces of rock have torn themselves off from the mountains and have rolled into the valleys with fearsome force. These occurrences often come about also without earthquakes having preceded. Persistent rains cause the spring-veins to be filled with water, often undermining the foundation of a piece of land, in that they wash the ground away, and in just the same way tear pieces of rock off from the summits of the mountains, especially when frost and wetness unite their effects. The great chasms and fissures in the earth, which have opened here and there in Switzerland and elsewhere and have for the most part closed again, are more distinct proofs of an expanding underground force, through which the strata of somewhat lesser density have burst apart. If we consider this fragility of our floor, the store of the underground glow, which perhaps everywhere may maintain the inflammable matters, the layers of coal, the resins and sulfur in a constantly blazing fire (just as coal mines, often when they have ignited by themselves in the air, glow through centuries and spread around themselves), if we, I say, consider this constitution of the underground caves, does there not seem to be in it a hint sufficient to sink our vaults into whole seas of glowing sulfur and to devastate our inhabited places with streams of burning matters, just as the poured-out lava laid waste the towns that were built next to the foot of Etna in an unconcerned calm. Herr D. Poll is right that in a short treatise on the earthquake he demands nothing further than water, in order to set in motion the constantly smoldering glow under the earth by means of expanded water vapors and to bring the earth into shaking; but if he wants to take away the suitability of Lemery's experiment (which made the shakings comprehensible through a mixture of sulfur and iron filings by means of the addition of water) by saying that in the earth no native iron but mere iron earth is encountered, which in this trial does not accomplish what is desired, then I give to consider whether, first, the manifold cause of heating, e.g. the efflorescence of the sulfur pyrites, the fermentations through the arrival of water, as is perceived after a rain on the poured-out lava, likewise on the perpetual earth-fire of Pietra Mala, after it has smelted the iron earth located deep down into granulated iron, or also the lodestone, which comes so near to the nature of native iron, and which without doubt is everywhere abundantly encountered in the depths, could not supply sufficient matter for the carrying out of this experiment on a large scale. The very strange observation that was reported from Switzerland, where a magnet during the earthquake deviated some degrees from its vertical direction together with the thread on which it was suspended, seems to confirm the cooperation of magnetic matters in the earthquake.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0014":["It would be a work of extensive execution to cite and examine all the hypotheses that each one brings forth in order to pave new paths of investigation for himself, and of which one often takes the place of the other like the waves of the sea. There is also a certain correct taste in natural science, which soon knows how to distinguish the free excesses of a craving for novelty from the secure and cautious judgments that have the testimony of experience and of rational credibility on their side. Father Bina and only recently Herr Prof. Krüger bring up the opinion that posits the appearances of the earthquake and those of electricity as having the same causes. Still greater boldness is in the proposal of Herr Prof. Hollmann, who, after he has proved the usefulness of air-openings in a ground distressed by ignited matters through the example of the fire-spewing mountains, without which the kingdoms of Naples and Sicily would be no more, wants it that one should dig through the uppermost crust of the earth down into the deepest burning chasms, and thereby procure the fire an outlet. The dreadful thickness together with the firmness of the inward strata, without which such cruel assaults of shakings would certainly long since have shattered such a land, likewise the water, which soon sets an end to all diggings through, and finally the incapacity of human beings make this proposal a beautiful dream. From the Prometheus of more recent times, Herr Franklin, who wanted to disarm the thunder, to him who wants to extinguish the fire in the workshop of Vulcan, all such endeavors are proofs of the boldness of human beings, which is combined with a capacity that stands in quite slight proportion to it, and lead him finally to the humbling reminder, with which he should justly begin, that he is after all never anything more than a human being.",[]],"monadologia-0001":["of metaphysics joined with geometry\nthe use in natural philosophy,\nof which\nSpecimen I.\ncontains\nthe physical\nmonadology\nwhich,\nwith the consent of the most ample order of philosophers,\nin a public disputation to be held for a place,\non the 10th of April, from the eighth to the twelfth hour,\nin the philosophical auditorium,\nwill be defended\nby M. Immanuel Kant,\nwith respondent,\nLucas David Vogel,\nof Royal Prussia, cultivator of holy theology.\nWith opponents the talented and highly polished young men\nLudwig Ernst Borowski,\nof Königsberg in Prussia, cultivator of holy theology;\nGeorg Ludwig Mühlenkampf,\nPrussian, of Trempen near Darkehmen, cultivator of holy theology;\nand\nLudwig Johann Krusemarck,\nof Kyritz in the Mark, cultivator of holy theology.\nIn the year 1756.\nAt Königsberg,\nprinted with the types of the sacred royal majesty and of the university printer J. H. Hartung.",[]],"monadologia-0002":["To the man\nmost illustrious, most noble and most excellent,\nLord\nWilhelm Ludwig von Groeben,\nof the most powerful King of the Prussians privy minister of state and of war,\nmost eminent president of the supreme court of appeals which is in Prussia,\nmost vigilant protector of our Albertina,\nand also\nmost weighty director of the college of stipends,\nhereditary lord of the lands\nTharau, Karschau, and the rest,\nincomparable patron of the Muses,\nto his hero most inclined to kindness.",[]],"monadologia-0003":["these columns with most devoted mind",[]],"monadologia-0004":["wish to be sacred",[]],"monadologia-0005":["the president and the respondent.",[]],"monadologia-0006":["things to be noted in advance.",["- erdbeben-0012: Erschütterung = \"shock\"/\"shaking\" (also \"shakings\" in 0014); Erdbeben = \"earthquake\"; Materie = \"matter\" (entbrannte Materie, \"ignited matter\"); Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Begebenheit = \"occurrence\" (0013); Beweisthümer rendered \"proofs\" (Beweis = proof).","- erdbeben-0012: \"mehr einer leichten Schaukelung ... zu vergleichen, als den Stößen\" kept in German order; \"vom 18ten Febr.\" is elliptical for a shock, kept as \"that of the 18th of Feb.\"","- erdbeben-0013: \"Wenn wir ... betrachten, scheinet nicht ... ein Wink genug zu seyn\" ends with no question mark in the German; I kept the period, though the sense is interrogative. \"überlegen\" in \"gebe ich zu überlegen\" rendered \"give to consider.\"","- erdbeben-0013: \"Lemerischen Experimente\" = Lemery's experiment; \"Tauglichkeit\" = \"suitability\"; \"vermittelst\" = \"by means of\"; \"Dazukunft\" = \"arrival\" (lit. \"coming-to\").","- erdbeben-0014: Vermögen = \"capacity\" (in \"Unvermögen,\" \"incapacity\"); Meynung = \"opinion\"; Urtheile = \"judgments\"; \"wie die Meereswellen\" kept as modifying \"einnimmt.\" \"Gluth\" = \"glow\"; \"Hrn.\" expanded to \"Herr.\"","- erdbeben-0014: \"die Meynung ... die ... gleiche Ursachen setzet\": the relative \"die\" could be read as nominative or accusative; I rendered it as the opinion that posits. \"Hr. Prof. Hollmanns\" is printed with spacing in the source; normalized.","- monadologia: Latin title page, dedication and heading; the first fragment begins mid-title (the preceding words are in a separate passage). \"usus\" left as \"the use\"; \"loco habenda\" rendered \"to be held for a place\" (the formula for a disputation for a position), kept literal. Names are given in the German/modern forms.","- monadologia-0001: \"Muehlemkampf\" printed in the OCR; corrected silently to Mühlenkampf (Mühlenkampf). \"Trempia\" read as Trempen, \"kyrizensi\" as Kyritz."]],"monadologia-0007":["Those who devote effort to the examination of natural things, philosophers of keener nose, have indeed joined together in this in unanimous consensus: that one must carefully take care lest anything, rashly and fashioned by a certain license of conjecturing, creep into natural science, or lest anything be attempted in vain without the support of experience and without geometry as interpreter. By which counsel surely nothing more salutary and useful for philosophy could be thought. But since scarcely anyone of mortals is permitted to advance on the straight line of truth with steady step without veering off here and there to one side or the other, certain ones have indulged this law so far that, in tracking down truth, not daring at all to commit themselves to the deep, they have judged it always better to hug the shore and have admitted nothing except what becomes known immediately by the testimony of experience. And by this way we can indeed expound the laws of nature, but the origin and causes of the laws we cannot. For those who pursue only the phenomena of nature are always just as far from the recondite understanding of first causes, nor will they ever reach the science of the nature of bodies itself any more than those who, by climbing higher and higher the peak of a mountain, would persuade themselves that they will finally touch heaven with the hand.",["- *scientia* = science (*scientiam naturalem*, \"natural science\"); *experientia* = experience; *phaenomena* left as \"phenomena\".","- *Philosophi* is in apposition to the relative clause *Qui … operam navant*; I kept the apposition rather than restructuring.","- *intelligentia* = understanding; *persvaderent* (subjunctive) = \"would persuade themselves\"."]],"monadologia-0008":["Therefore what most suppose they can conveniently do without in physical matters, that alone here is the support and kindles the light: Metaphysics. For bodies consist of parts; of which, how they are put together, whether they fill space by the sole co-presence of primitive parts or by the mutual conflict of forces, it is of no small importance indeed that it be clearly expounded. But in what manner finally is it permitted in this business to reconcile Metaphysics with Geometry, since griffins seem able to be yoked to horses more easily than transcendental philosophy to Geometry? For since that flatly denies that space is infinitely divisible, this asserts the same with the certainty with which it is accustomed [to assert] the rest. This contends that empty space is necessary for free motions, that explodes it. This shows attraction, or universal gravity, hardly explicable by mechanical causes but proceeding from forces implanted in bodies acting at rest and at a distance; that banishes it among the vain sports of imagination.",["- *illa* / *haec* are rendered \"that\" / \"this\" without identifying the referents; the Latin leaves the assignment to the reader.","- *Metaphysica* and *philosophia transscendentalis* are both given as in the Latin; *vires* = forces, *vis* = force; *spatium vacuum* = \"empty space\".","- *imaginationis* = \"imagination\"."]],"monadologia-0009":["Since it appears that composing this dispute is of no small labor, I have resolved to place at least some effort in it; others, whose powers are more sufficient to this business, being invited to bring to completion those things which I here only busy myself to affect.",["- *afficere* is rendered literally as \"affect\"; the sense is unclear and may be a slip (e.g. for *attingere*), but I have not emended.","- *vires* = powers (here in the sense of capacities)."]],"monadologia-0010":["By way of a coda I only add: since it is necessary that the principle of all internal actions, or the implanted force of the elements, be motive, and indeed applied externally, because it is present to external things, and we cannot conceive another force for moving co-present things except that which strives either to repel or to attract them, and furthermore, with a repelling force alone posited, a binding together of the elements for composing bodies cannot be understood, but rather a dissipation, while with an attracting force alone a binding together indeed, but not however a defined extension and space, it can already in some way be understood in advance that he who is able to deduce these two principles from the very nature of the elements and their primitive affections has contributed work of not contemptible moment to explaining the interior nature of bodies.",["- The sentence is kept whole; its construction is loose (*cum … necesse sit … in antecessum jam … intelligi posse*), and *qvi … valet eum … contulisse* has a redundant *eum*, which I rendered without repairing.","- *vis* = force; *principium* = principle; *extensio* = extension; *spatium* = space; *elementa* = elements; *affectionibus* = affections.","- *colligatio sed dissipatio potius*: the negation is carried over from *intelligi qveat* in the clause; \"cannot be understood\" is placed where English requires it."]],"monadologia-0011":["Of Physical Monadology\nSection I.\nDeclaring the existence of physical monads consonant with Geometry.",["- *existentia* = existence; *consentaneam* = \"consonant\", agreeing with *existentiam*."]],"monadologia-0012":["Prop. i. definition. A simple substance, called Monad *), is one which does not consist of a plurality of parts, of which one can exist separately without the others.",["- *estqve* printed for *est quae*. *substantia simplex* = simple substance; *pars* = part."]],"monadologia-0013":["*) Since the plan of my undertaking is to comment on no other than that class of simple substances which are the primitive parts of bodies, I warn in advance that in what follows I shall use the terms, simple substances, monads, elements of matter, primitive parts of body, as synonyms.",["- *termini* = terms; *synonimis* as printed (for *synonymis*); *materiae* = of matter."]],"monadologia-0014":["Prop. ii. theorem. Bodies consist of monads.",[]],"monadologia-0015":["Bodies consist of parts, which, separated from one another, have a perdurable existence. But since in such parts composition is nothing but a relation, hence a determination in itself contingent, which can be removed with their existence preserved, it is evident that all composition of a body can be abrogated, with all the parts that were formerly composed nonetheless surviving. But with all composition removed, the parts that remain have plainly no composition, and thus are plainly destitute of plurality of substances, hence simple. Any body therefore consists of primitive parts absolutely simple, i.e. monads.",["- *compositio* = composition; *relatio* = relation; *determinatio* = determination; *contingens* = contingent; *existentia* = existence.","- *salva ipsarum existentia* is rendered \"with their existence preserved\" (ablative absolute)."]],"monadologia-0016":["scholion. Deliberately omitting in the present demonstration that celebrated principle of reason, I have carried out the undertaking from the common (to which every one of the Philosophers subscribes) union of concepts, fearing lest those whose minds are averse to this principle be less convinced in such a way.",["- *demonstratio* = demonstration (not distinguished from *Beweis* = proof elsewhere in this set); *rationis principium* = principle of reason; *notio* = concept.","- *e communi, cui nemo non subscribit Philosophorum, notionum adunatione*: the parenthesis interrupts the phrase in the Latin; I kept it. *nemo non* = literally \"no one does not\"."]],"monadologia-0017":["Prop. iii. theorem. The space which bodies fill is infinitely divisible, and therefore does not consist of primitive and simple parts.",["- *ininfinitum* silently corrected to *in infinitum*.","- *spatium* = space; *divisibile* = divisible."]],"monadologia-0018":["A line e f being given, produced indefinitely, i.e. so that it can always be produced further at pleasure, let another, a b, physical, i.e. if it so pleases, composed of primitive parts of matter, stand upon it at right angles. At the side let another be erected, c d, equal to the former and similarly placed, which, that it can be done not only in the geometrical but also in the physical sense, you will not deny. Let any points, g, h, i, k, be noted on the line e f, and so on indefinitely. First no one will call into doubt that between any two given points or, if you prefer, monads a physical straight line can be drawn. Let c g therefore be drawn, and the place where this intersects the perpendicular a b will be o. Now let another physical line be conceived drawn between the points c and h, and the place u, common to both lines c h and a b, will be nearer to point a. And so further, with lines drawn from the same point c to any points i, k, etc. on the line e f produced to infinity, the points of intersection, x, y, etc., will always become nearer to the point a, as is evident of itself even to one wholly ignorant of Geometry. And if you think that these physical lines will finally be too closely contiguous to one another, so that they cannot stand beside one another, the lower ones drawn can be removed, and nonetheless it is evident that the places of intersection must approach nearer and nearer to point a *) according as you note a point farther and farther on the indefinite line e f. But since this remoteness can be prolonged to infinity, the approach also of the intersection toward the point a can grow by infinite parts of increment. Yet never will the intersection in this way fall into the point a; since, the points c and a being equally distant from the line e f, the line joining the points c and a and continued as far as you like will always be distant by just as much from the underlying line e f, nor can it ever meet this, which is against the hypothesis. And thus by continued division of the line o a one never arrives at primitive parts not to be divided further, i.e. space is infinitely divisible, nor does it consist of simple parts.",["- *indefinite* / *in indefinitum* = \"indefinitely\" / \"indefinite\", kept distinct from *in infinitum* = \"to infinity\", \"infinitely\".","- *locus* = place; *hypothesis* and *postulata* left as cognates; *linea physica* = physical line; *punctum* = point.","- *qvod fieri posse … non inficiaberis*: the relative *qvod* is kept ahead of its clause as in the Latin.","- Letters (a b, c d, e f, etc.) are as printed; the figure is not part of the text."]],"monadologia-0019":["*) Nor can the points y and x ever coincide, because otherwise the lines c y and c x would likewise coincide, and the line c k would coincide with the line c i, which is against the postulates.",["- *aeqve* = \"likewise\"/\"equally\"; the sense is doubtful, and \"likewise\" was chosen."]],"monadologia-0020":["scholion. This demonstration, already employed by very many of the Physicists, I have cited here, and have adapted to physical space with the greatest perspicuity that could be achieved, lest any who make use of the general distinction concerning the diversity of geometrical and natural spaces slip away by some exception. There are indeed also other demonstrations of the same view at hand, of which, that I may cite a single one, conceive an equilateral triangle constructed of monads, if it so pleases, of which, if two sides be produced indefinitely, and in these you take distances double, triple, quintuple, a hundredfold, etc. greater than the sides of the given triangle, their extremities can be joined by physical lines, which will be in the same ratio as those greater than the third side of the triangle and will consist by just as much of more most simple particles. But since between any of these monads and that which is placed at the vertex of the angle physical lines drawn can be conceived, these divide the base of the given triangle infinitely many times, and thus excellently uphold the infinite divisibility of space. But whoever has seen through the demonstration cited above without the hindrances of prejudiced opinions can, in my judgment at least, be free of all others.",["- *sententia* = \"view\"; *opinio* = \"opinion\" (*praejudicatarum opinionum*).","- *simplicissimis* = \"most simple\" (superlative kept); *particulis* = particles.","- *vacare* with the dative = \"be free of\"; *judicio* = judgment.","- *qvi generali … discrimine vtuntur*: \"make use of\" governs the ablative *discrimine*."]],"monadologia-0021":["Prop. iv. theorem. A composite infinitely divisible does not consist of primitive or simple parts.",["- *compositum* = composite."]],"monadologia-0022":["Since in a composite infinitely divisible one never arrives by dividing at parts stripped of all composition, and the composition which cannot be removed by dividing cannot be removed at all unless you have abrogated the whole existence of the composite; but since the parts which remain in a composite with all composition removed are called simple, Prop. I: it is evident that a composite infinitely divisible does not consist of such.",["- The sentence is a *cum … quia … liquet* period with the main clause only at the end; kept as one sentence. The capital *Qvia* after the semicolon is as printed.","- \"Prop. I\" is the reference as printed (*Prop. I*); not altered.","- *audiunt* = \"are called\" (literally \"are heard as\")."]],"monadologia-0023":["Scholium. I thought it would not be foreign to the plan of the undertaking, after the simple primitive parts have been vindicated for any body whatever, and after the infinite division of its space has been asserted, to take care that no one hold monads to be infinitely small particles of a body. For that space, which is wholly devoid of substantiality, and a phenomenon of the external relation of united monads, is not at all exhausted even by division continued to infinity, becomes abundantly evident in this way; but in whatever composite composition is nothing but an accident, and there are substantial subjects of composition, that it suffer infinite division is absurd. For from this it would also follow that any primitive part of a body is so constituted that, joined neither with a thousand others, nor with myriads, nor with millions of millions, in one word, not with however many it may please one to assign, does it constitute any particle of matter whatever, which certainly, not obscurely, removes all substantiality of the composite, and therefore cannot occur in the bodies of nature.",["- *illud* (\"that it suffer infinite division\") is left without a noun; its antecedent is read as the composite (*compositum*), but the Latin does not say so.","- *substantialitas* = substantiality; *phaenomenon* = phenomenon; *compositum* = composite; *compositio* = composition; *accidens* = accident.","- *juncta* agrees with *pars* (the primitive part), not with *particulam*."]],"monadologia-0024":["Corollary. Any body whatever therefore consists of a definite number of simple elements.",["- *numerus* = number (Zahl in the glossary); *elementum* = element."]],"monadologia-0025":["Prop. v. Theorem. Any simple element of a body, or monad, is not only in space but also fills space, its simplicity nonetheless being preserved.",["- *Monas* = monad; *salva ... simplicitate* is an ablative absolute, rendered literally."]],"monadologia-0026":["Since any body whatever is composed of a definite number of simple elements, but the space that it fills suffers infinite division, any of these elements will occupy a part of space still further divisible, i.e., will fill an assignable space.",["- *h. e.* (*hoc est*) = \"i.e.\""]],"monadologia-0027":["But since the division of space is not a separation of those of which one, removed from another, has its own and for itself sufficient existence, but argues nothing but a certain plurality or quantity in external relation, it is evident that a plurality of substantial parts does not follow from it; which, since it alone is contrary to the substantial simplicity of the monad, it is amply evident that the divisibility of space is not adverse to the simplicity of the monad.",["- Syntax kept as in the Latin: the relative clause *quae cum sola ... contrarietur* hangs loosely, and the sentence resumes with *patet*.","- *existentia* = existence; *pluralitas* = plurality; *qvantitas* = quantity."]],"monadologia-0028":["Scholium. In the investigation of the elements, certainly no other opinion has more stood in the way of the marriage of geometry with metaphysics than that preconceived, though not sufficiently examined, opinion, as if the divisibility of the space which an element occupies would also argue the division of the element itself into substantial parts. Which has been commonly supposed to be so far placed beyond the hazard of doubt that those who defend the infinite division of real space would also shrink from monads by the whole heaven, and those who subscribe to monads have reckoned the affections of geometrical space to be imaginary, of their parts; but since it is openly clear from what was demonstrated above that neither is the geometer deceived, nor does the opinion that resides with the metaphysician stray from the truth, this opinion, which has sundered both, as if an element absolutely simple as to substance could not fill space with its simplicity preserved, must certainly be mistaken. For the line or surface that divides some little space in two indicates that one part of space certainly exists outside the other. But because space is not a substance but is a certain phenomenon of the external relation of substances, that the relation of one and the same substance can be divided in two is not contrary to the simplicity or, if you prefer, unity of the substance. For what is on either side of the dividing line is not anything so separable from substance that, even removed from it, it would maintain its own existence, which is certainly required for the real division that removes simplicity, but is the action, or relation, of one and the same substance exercised on both sides, in which indeed to find some plurality is not to tear the substance itself apart into parts.",["- *suarum partium* (\"of their parts\"): the plural *suarum* does not agree with singular *spatii*; the antecedent is ambiguous (the parts of space, or of the monads), and the construction after *imaginariis* is loose. Left as it stands.","- *unitas* = unity; *simplicitas* = simplicity; *relatio* = relation; *actio* = action.","- *aleam* in *extra dubitationis aleam* = \"hazard\" (literally the die)."]],"monadologia-0029":["Prop. vi. Theorem. The monad defines the little space of its presence not by a plurality of its substantial parts but by the sphere of activity by which it keeps the external ones present to it on both sides from further approach to one another.",["- *externas* has no noun; it is feminine plural accusative. It is rendered \"the external ones,\" with no noun supplied.","- *activitas* = activity; *praesentia* = presence."]],"monadologia-0030":["Since in the monad there is no plurality of substances, yet nevertheless any one, posited solitarily, fills space, by the preceding the ground of the space filled will have to be sought not in the position of the substance alone but in its relation with respect to external things. But because by filling space it keeps those immediately present to it on both sides from further approach to one another, and therefore determines something in their position, namely by limiting the measure of nearness to which they themselves can approach one another, it is evident that it exerts action, and indeed in a space determined in every direction, hence it must be conceded that it fills this space with the sphere of its activity.",["- *ratio* = ground (Grund in the glossary); *positus* = position; *determinat* = determines.","- *ipsa sibi accedere*: *sibi* is reflexive and could also be read \"to themselves\"; rendered \"approach one another.\"","- The verb *patet* governs the infinitive clause *actionem exserere*, with the subject of the infinitive unexpressed."]],"monadologia-0031":["Prop. vii. Problem. To vindicate further from difficulties the space that any monad occupies by the sphere of its activity, with its simplicity preserved.",["- *problema* = problem (Aufgabe in the glossary); the heading is an infinitive construction in the Latin."]],"monadologia-0032":["If the monad, as we contend, fills a defined space, that can be expressed by any other finite one. Let therefore the little circle A B C D represent the little space that the monad occupies by its activity; B D will be the diameter of this sphere of activity, i.e., the distance at which it keeps others present to it in B and D from further approach to one another. But on that account beware of saying that this is the diameter of the monad itself, which would certainly be absurd. Nor is anything more alien to our opinion. For since space is completed by external respects alone, whatever is internal to substance, i.e., the substance itself, the subject of external determinations, is not properly defined by space, but of its determinations those that are referred to external things, those alone it is right to seek in space. But, you say, in this little space the substance is present, and is at hand everywhere in it; therefore whoever divides space divides substance? I reply, this space itself is the circuit of the external presence of this element. Whoever therefore divides space divides the extensive quantity of its presence. But there are, besides external presence, i.e., the respective determinations of substance, other internal ones, which, if they were not, those would not have a subject in which they would inhere. But the internal ones are not in space for the very reason that they are internal. Neither therefore are they themselves divided by the division of external determinations, and so neither is the subject itself, or substance, divided in this way. Just as if you were to say: God is internally at hand to all created things through the act of conservation, whoever therefore divides the aggregate of created things divides God because he divides the circuit of his presence; than which nothing more absurd can be said. The monad therefore, which is the primitive element of body, insofar as it fills space does certainly have a certain extensive quantity, namely a circuit of activity, in which however you will not find several things of which one, separated from another, i.e., solitary for itself without another, would have its own perdurability. For what is found in space B C D cannot be separated from that which is present in space B A D in such a way that each would exist through itself; because both are nothing but the external determination of one and the same substance; but accidents do not exist without their substances. *)",["- *respectus* = respect; *respectivas* = respective; *determinatio* = determination; *qvantitas extensiva* = extensive quantity.","- *perdurabilitas* = perdurability (not in the glossary; the plain cognate is used).","- *praesto est* = \"is at hand\"; *ambitus* = circuit; *circellus* = little circle; *spatiolum* = little space.","- *illae* (neuter plural in *non haberent illae*) is rendered \"those,\" with no antecedent supplied; it presumably refers to the determinations, and the clause is loose.","- The \"*)\" marks the footnote that follows as the next passage."]],"monadologia-0033":["*) Of all the difficulties that can stand in the way of our opinion the gravest seems to be that which is drawn from the extra-position of the determinations of one and the same substance. For the action of the monad that is in space B C D is outside the action that is in space B D A; therefore they seem to be really diverse from one another and to be found outside substance. But relations are always both outside one another and outside substance because those beings to which substance is referred are really diverse from substance and from one another, nor does this argue substantial plurality.",["- *extrapositio* is rendered \"extra-position\" (a literal calque).","- *entia* = beings (Wesen in the glossary is rendered \"being\"); *realiter* = really.","- *sententia* = opinion (Meinung in the glossary)."]],"monadologia-0034":["Prop. viii. Theorem. The force by which the simple element of a body occupies its space is the same that they otherwise call impenetrability; nor, if you depart from that force, can there be room for this.",["- *huic* (\"this\") is ambiguous. It may refer to impenetrability or to the force just mentioned, and the pronoun is left as it stands.","- *vis* = force; *impenetrabilitas* = impenetrability."]],"monadologia-0035":["Impenetrability is that affection of a body by which it keeps contiguous things away from the space it occupies. But since it has become known from the preceding that the space a body occupies (if you conceive its parts as united as closely as possible to one another without interspersed void) is composed of the little spaces that the individual simple elements fill, since further, for keeping external bodies rushing into a filled space away, or for impenetrability, resistance is required and hence a certain force, but it was demonstrated in the earlier propositions that the elements fill their defined space by a certain activity of keeping others about to penetrate there away, it is evident that the impenetrability of bodies depends on nothing other than that same natural force of the elements. Which was the first thing to be shown.",["- *alia eo penetratura*: \"others about to penetrate there\" renders a future participle; *eo* is the adverb \"there,\" and *alia* is neuter plural with no noun.","- *renitentia* = resistance; *vis* = force; *activitas* = activity; *affectio* = affection.","- *Qvod erat primum* is rendered literally, with no completion of the formula.","- *in prioribus* (\"in the earlier ones\") is rendered \"in the earlier propositions\"; the noun is inferred from the Latin context."]],"monadologia-0036":["Next let the line a g be composed of primitive elements of matter, i.e. monads; if any element d by the presence of its substance were to designate only a place and not occupy space, the place d would bisect the given line a g, and because it therefore marks where one half of the line ends and the other begins, it will be common to both halves of the line. But physical lines are not equal unless they consist of an equal number of elements, and the number of elements on either side is not equal except in the line a c and e g, therefore the place of monad d will be common to the lines a c, e g, i.e. the said lines will meet each other immediately in the named place, and therefore the element d does not keep the nearest ones e and c from immediate contact, i.e. will not be impenetrable. If therefore you deny that the place occupied by monad d is common to the lines a c, e g, there will be a point x where the lines a c and d g meet each other immediately, and o in which the lines a d and e g meet each other, because therefore the place of monad d is different from the place x and likewise from the place o, since otherwise the place of immediate contact would always be common, as was said before, you have three different places x d o which without doubt define some line. The immediate presence of monad d is therefore defined by a defined line, i.e. it is at hand in a defined space, and because through the mere positing of substance it could occupy not space but place, there must be present something else in the substance which determines the measure of proximity in the elements touching on either side and keeps any force from a closer approach of the elements c and e, but force cannot be opposed except by force; Therefore the same force by which the element of a body occupies its space causes impenetrability. Which was the second.",["- *elementum* = element; *monas* = monad; *locus* = place; *spatium* = space; *praesentia* = presence; *vis* = force; *positio* = positing.","- \"vim qvamlibet … arcet\": the construction is kept as it stands; \"any force\" may be the object of \"keeps off\" (as rendered) or the force that does the warding.","- \"Qvod erat alterum\": rendered literally as \"Which was the second.\""]],"monadologia-0037":["Section II.\nExplaining the most general affections of physical monads, insofar as they, different in different ones, contribute to understanding the nature of bodies.",["- *affectiones* = affections; *faciunt ad … intelligendam* rendered \"contribute to understanding\"; \"diversae\" is nominative, agreeing with the affections."]],"monadologia-0038":["Prop. IX. Definition. Contact is the application, made to one another, of the forces of impenetrability of several elements.",["- *definitio* = definition (Erklärung); *contactus* = contact; *applicatio* = application."]],"monadologia-0039":["Scholium. Contact is commonly defined by immediate presence. But if you should add external at the very most, (since without this addition God, who is present to all things immediately, but intimately, would have to be thought to touch them themselves), nevertheless the definition will hardly be absolute in all respects. For since it has been sufficiently established by others that bodies separated by empty space can nonetheless coexist, and therefore also be immediately present to one another, although without mutual contact, without doubt the definition will here be held to a fault. Further, not without great semblance of truth the immediate attraction of bodies, even those distant from one another, is defended by Newton's school, whose co-presence would nevertheless occur without mutual contact. Besides, if you defend the definition which gives out immediate co-presence for the very notion of contact, the notion of this presence must first be explained by you. If, as happens, you declare it through mutual action; in what, I ask, does action consist? Without doubt bodies act by moving upon one another. But a moving force exerted from a given point either repels others from the same or attracts. Which action is to be understood in contact easily becomes evident. For by moving body closer and closer to body we then say that they touch each other, when the force of impenetrability, i.e. of repulsion, is felt. Therefore the action and reaction of this, made by different elements against one another, constitutes the genuine notion of contact.",["- \"corpora in se movendo agunt\": *in se* is ambiguous (upon one another / upon themselves); rendered \"upon one another\".","- *notio* = notion (not in the glossary); *vis motrix* = moving force; *actio atqve reactio* = action and reaction.","- \"vitii tenebitur\" rendered literally as \"will be held to a fault\"."]],"monadologia-0040":["Prop. X. Theorem. Bodies through the force of impenetrability alone would not enjoy a defined volume, unless there were present another, likewise innate, of attraction, defining jointly with that the limit of extension.",["- *theorema* = theorem; *insita* = innate; *extensio* = extension; *volumen* = volume.","- \"alia … attractionis\": \"another\" is feminine and so understood as another force; \"jointly with that\" may attach to \"present\" or to \"defining\"."]],"monadologia-0041":["The force of impenetrability is a repulsive force, any external one warding off from further approach. Since this force is inborn to any element, it will indeed be possible to understand from its nature why the intensity of the action diminishes in proportion to the increments of the distance to which it extends, that at any given distance it is wholly nil, cannot be understood wholly of itself. And therefore if it stops at this alone, the structure of bodies would be wholly nil, since the particles would merely repel one another, and no volume circumscribed by a defined limit would be established for the body. It is therefore necessary that another, opposite endeavor, and one equal at a given distance, determining the limit for the space to be occupied, be opposed to this endeavor. Which, since it acts directly against repulsion, is attraction. There is therefore need for any element, besides the force of impenetrability, of another, attractive one, from which if you depart, determinate volumes of the nature of bodies would not result.",["- *ingenita* = inborn; *compages* = structure; *conatus* = endeavor; *attractio* = attraction; *repulsio* = repulsion.","- \"qvod in distantia qvavis data plane nulla sit\": the clause is kept as it stands; *qvod* may be \"that\" or \"because\", and *qvavis* \"any\" as in \"at any given distance\".","- \"determinata corporum naturae volumina\": \"of the nature of bodies\" is literal; \"of bodies of nature\" is also grammatically possible."]],"monadologia-0042":["Scholium. To investigate what the laws of both forces, repulsion as well as attractive, in the elements are, is certainly an investigation of arduous moment and worthy to exercise more perspicacious minds. For me here it suffices to have rendered their existence most certainly established, as far as the law of brevity allowed. But if it pleases to descry, as from afar, certain things pertaining to this question, must not, since the repulsive force acts outward from the innermost point of the space occupied by the element, its intensity be judged to be weakened reciprocally according to the increase of the space into which it extends? For a force distributed from a point cannot be found efficacious in a defined sphere, unless by acting it fills the whole space which is comprehended under the given diameter. Which is made evident in this way. For if you conceive the force as emanating along straight lines from a given surface, as light does or also, according to Keill's mind, the very force of attraction, the force exercised in this way will be in the ratio of the multitude of lines which can be drawn from this surface, that is in the ratio of the acting surface itself. And so if the surface is infinitely small, this force too will be infinitely small, and if finally it is a point, wholly nil. And therefore through lines diverging from a point force cannot be diffused at any assignable distance. Nor therefore will it be found efficacious, except by filling the whole space in which it acts. But spherical spaces are as the cubes of the distances. Therefore since the same force diffused through a greater space is diminished in the inverse ratio of the spaces, the force of impenetrability will be reciprocally in the triplicate ratio of the distances from the center of presence.",["- *multitudo* = multitude; *ratio* = ratio (also \"proportion\" in \"pro ratione\"); *intensitas* = intensity; *diameter* = diameter.","- \"Keilii\": Keill, kept as the name.","- \"in certa distantia assignabilis\" rendered \"at any assignable distance\"; *certa* is literally \"a certain\"."]],"monadologia-0043":["On the contrary, since attraction is indeed the action of the same element but turned in the opposite direction, the spherical surface onto which attraction is exercised at a given distance will be the *terminus a quo*; since the multitude of points, to which lines of tendency toward the center can be drawn, and hence the quantity of attraction, is defined, it will in this way be assignable, and decreasing in the inverse ratio of the spherical surfaces, i.e. in the inverse duplicate ratio of the distances.",["- *terminus a qvo* left in Latin as printed. \"cujus\" is taken to refer to the surface; the clause \"cujus … definita sit\" is kept in the order of the Latin."]],"monadologia-0044":["If therefore it is posited that the repulsive decreases in the subtriplicate, and hence in a far greater ratio, it is necessary that at some point of the diameter attraction and repulsion be equal. And this point will determine the limit of impenetrability, and the circuit or volume of external contact; for with attraction conquered the repulsive force acts no further.",["- \"subtriplicata\" is rendered as printed (\"subtriplicate\"); it sits oddly beside \"adeoqve longe majori ratione\" and may be a doubtful reading, but it is not emended.","- *ambitus* = circuit; *volumen* = volume."]],"monadologia-0045":["Corollary. If you hold this law of innate forces as ratified, you will recognize also the equal volume of all elements, however diverse in species. For since it is in the open that the forces of repulsion as well as the attractive, because each enjoys a defined degree of intensity, can be maximally diverse in different elements, here more intense, elsewhere more remiss, nevertheless, since the double force of repulsion is double at the same distance, and likewise the force of attraction, and it is fitting that all moving forces of an element which is specifically twice as strong be stronger in the same ratio, the named forces at the same distance are always equalized, and hence it is necessary to determine the equal volume of the element, however much they differ in degree from the like-named forces of other elements.",["- *corollarium* = corollary; *species* = species; *gradus* = degree; *agnosces* = \"you will recognize\".","- \"in aprico\" rendered \"in the open\"; *cognominibus* = \"like-named\".","- The subject of \"determinare necesse est\" is not expressed; the infinitive is left without a supplied subject. \"they\" in the last clause is the forces."]],"monadologia-0046":["Prop. XI. Theorem. The force of inertia is in any element of a defined quantity, which in different ones can be maximally diverse.",["- *vis inertiae* = force of inertia; *qvantitas* = quantity. \"quantitatis definitae\" is a genitive of quality; \"which\" refers to the quantity."]],"monadologia-0047":["A moved body running into another would wield no efficacy, and would be reduced to rest by any infinitely small obstacle, unless it enjoyed the force of inertia, by which it strives to persevere in the state of moving. But the force of inertia of a body is the sum of the forces of inertia of all the elements of which it is composed, (and this indeed they call the mass); therefore any element moved with a certain velocity, unless this is multiplied by the force of inertia, would wield no efficacy of moving at all. But whatever by multiplying into another gives a quantum greater than the other of the factors, is itself a quantity, by which another, both greater and smaller, can be assigned. Therefore than the force of inertia of any element another, either greater or smaller, can be given in elements of different species.",["- *efficacia* = efficacy; *summa* = sum; *massa* = mass; *celeritas* = velocity; *quantum* and *quantitas* kept distinct as \"quantum\" and \"quantity\".","- \"vi inertiae cujuslibet elementi alia … dari poterit\": \"than\" is supplied for the ablative of comparison; the construction could also be read otherwise.","- \"qva tum major tum minor alia\": the antecedent of \"qva\" is ambiguous; kept as it stands."]],"monadologia-0048":["Corollary 1. For any given elements others can be given, whose force of inertia, or, what in a different respect is the same, moving force, is two or three times greater, i.e. which both resist a certain velocity with two or three times greater force, and, moved with the same velocity, wield two or three times greater impetus.",["- *coroll.* = corollary; *vis motrix* = moving force; *impetus* kept as \"impetus\".","- \"certae celeritati … resistunt\": the dative is rendered \"resist a certain velocity\"."]],"monadologia-0049":["Corollary 2. Since it is established from the Corollary of the preceding Proposition that any elements whatever, however diverse in species, are nevertheless endowed with equal volume, and hence that an equal number of elements is always contained in an equal space exactly filled, it is rightly concluded: that bodies, even if you should depart as far as possible from the admixture of the void and should take the whole space to be perfectly filled, can nevertheless contain, under the same volume, the most diverse masses, namely through elements endowed with a greater or lesser force of inertia. For the mass of bodies is nothing but the quantity of the force of inertia of these very bodies, by which they either resist motion or, when moved with a given speed, are endowed with a certain impetus of moving.",[]],"monadologia-0050":["Hence from a smaller quantity of matter comprehended under a given volume, the inference to a smaller density, and to greater empty interstices interspersed, is not always sufficiently firm. Each body can either be endowed with equal empty interstices, or be perfectly dense, and nonetheless one or the other can be endowed with a far greater mass; the cause of the diversity residing plainly in the very nature of the elements.",[]],"monadologia-0051":["Prop. 12. Theorem. The specific diversity of the density of bodies observable in the world cannot at all be explained without a specific diversity of the inertia of the elements themselves.",[]],"monadologia-0052":["If all the elements enjoyed an equal force of inertia, and an equal volume, then for understanding the difference in the rarity of bodies an absolute void interspersed among the parts is needed. For according to the demonstrations of Newton, Keill, and others, in a medium perfectly filled in such a way there is no place for free motion. And so, for explaining the infinitely diverse specific density of media, e.g. of aether, air, water, gold, one must indulge an immoderate lust for conjecturing, by which what is most remote from human understanding, the very texture of the elements, is rashly feigned at pleasure, by conceiving it freely and boldly now in the likeness of the thinnest little bubbles, now of branches and twisted spirals, so that you may think of matter distended in marvelous ways, and of a small matter embracing an enormous space. But take up the reasons that fight against this.",[]],"monadologia-0053":["Those fibrils, exceedingly slender beyond measure, or bubbles, which under a skin of immense thinness comprehend a vacuum huge in proportion to the quantity of matter, must necessarily be worn away in the end by the continual conflict and attrition of bodies, and in this way the fragments of those broken up will finally fill the empty space enclosed. When this has been done, the mundane space, perfectly full on all sides, will grow numb with a strong inertia, and all motions will shortly be reduced to rest.",[]],"monadologia-0054":["Further, since according to such an opinion it must be that the specifically rarer media consist of parts that are maximally distended and endowed with a large volume, in what manner, then, can the interstices of the denser bodies, which according to the same opinion are narrower, be passable for them, just as it is established that fire, the magnetic fluid, and the electric fluid most easily permeate bodies? For how particles endowed with a greater volume could penetrate themselves into interstices narrower than they are, I am, together with the most ignorant, ignorant.",[]],"monadologia-0055":["Unless, therefore, the specific diversity of the simplest elements themselves is conceded, by which, with the same space exactly filled, now a smaller, now a far greater mass can be constructed, physics will always stick fast at this difficulty as at a rock.",[]],"monadologia-0056":["Prop. 13. Theorem. The elements of a body, even when posited in solitude, enjoy a perfect elastic force, different in different ones, and constitute a medium primitively elastic in itself and without admixed void.",[]],"monadologia-0057":["The individual simple elements occupy the space of their presence by a certain definite force, which wards external substances off from it. But since any finite force has a degree that can be overcome by another greater one, it is evident that to this repulsive force another, stronger one can be opposed, and since the innate force of the element does not suffice for warding it off at the same distance, it is evident that the latter will penetrate to some extent into the space occupied by the element itself. But since any forces extended from a definite point into space are weakened in proportion to the increase of distance, it is evident that this repulsive force reacts the more strongly, the closer one approaches to the center of activity. And since the repelling force, which is finite at a given distance from the center of repulsion, increases in a definite proportion of the approaches, it must be infinite at the point itself, and it is evident that the element can be penetrated through by no conceivable force. It will therefore be perfectly elastic, and several of this kind with elasticities joined will constitute a primitively elastic medium. That this elasticity is different in different ones is evident from the Corollary of Prop. 10, line 5.",[]],"monadologia-0058":["Corollary. The elements are perfectly impenetrable, that is, incapable of being wholly excluded by any external force however great from the space they occupy, but they are condensable, and also constitute bodies of such a kind, namely yielding somewhat to an external compressing force. Hence the origin of bodies or media primitively elastic, among which it may be permitted to profess beforehand aether or the matter of fire.",[]],"monadologia-0059":["End.",[]],"winde-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's\nNew\nRemarks\nfor the\nElucidation\nof the\nTheory of the Winds,\nby which he at the same time\ninvites to his lectures.\nKönigsberg, April 25, 1756.\nPrinted in the Royal privileged Drieset Printing House.",[]],"winde-0002":["Preliminary Remark.",[]],"winde-0003":["One must represent the atmosphere as a sea of fluid elastic matter, which is composed, as it were, of layers of different density, which in greater heights always decreases. If this fluid sea is to remain in equilibrium, it is not enough that the columns of air that one represents alongside one another be equally heavy; they must also stand equally high, i.e. the layer of a certain density must stand in all parts of its domain in the same level; for according to the laws of fluidity, in the opposite case the higher part would necessarily flow off toward the lower side, and the equilibrium would be lifted in the instant. The causes that can lift the equilibrium are either the diminution of the expanding force through cold and vapors that weaken the spring-force of the air, or the diminution of gravity, first through the heat with which a certain region of air is expanded more strongly than another, and, in that it is thereby compelled to rise above the level of the other, flows off and makes up a lighter column of air, and second through the flowing together of the water vapors, which were previously borne by the air, but now, in that they separate from it, withdraw a part of its weight. In both cases a wind arises toward the region where the air has lost in its force of expansion or gravity; only with the difference that in the first case the equilibrium is soon restored, as also with the second cause of the other case, because for the continuance of the wind in these cases an increase of the cause is required, which cannot possibly last long, whereas the first cause of this latter case, because it need only be continued without increasing itself, yields a very powerful source of lasting winds.",[]],"winde-0004":["The causes that move the atmosphere either on account of an increase of elasticity, as e.g. through warmth, or at the same time of gravity, as in the air freed from melting snow, are by far not so powerful, because then the motion takes place against a resting air that resists it with its whole weight, and also the expanding region of air itself expands upward just as strongly as toward the sides, and thus weakens its own force. Hence a wind from these causes can impossibly be sensed over great distances.",[]],"winde-0005":["I adduce all this only briefly, and presuppose that the reader's own reflection will spread the necessary light over what has been presented. I would not gladly say very little in so few pages.",[]],"winde-0006":["First Remark.",[]],"winde-0007":["A greater degree of heat, which acts on one region of air more than on another, makes a wind toward this heated region of air, which lasts as long as the superior warmth of the region continues.",["- Latin technical terms kept as the sense required; *vis inertiae* = \"force of inertia\"; *vis elastica* = \"elastic force\"; *volumen* = \"volume\"; *massa* = \"mass\"; *spatium* = \"space\"; *elementum* = \"element\"; *numerus* = \"number\".","- monadologia-0049: \"Coroll. Prop. praec.\" abbreviated as in source, rendered \"Corollary of the preceding Proposition\"; \"qvippe\" rendered \"namely\".","- monadologia-0052: \"Keilii\" read as genitive of Keill; \"in medio tali ratione perfecte impleto\" kept literal (\"in a medium perfectly filled in such a way\"), the construction being obscure.","- monadologia-0054: \"juxta cum ignarissimis ignoro\" rendered literally (\"together with the most ignorant, I am ignorant\").","- monadologia-0057: \"e Coroll. prop. X. linea 5.\" kept as printed; \"punctum ipsum infinita sit necesse est\" is elliptical (\"must be infinite at the point itself\"); \"appropinqvationum\" rendered \"approaches\".","- monadologia-0057: \"huic repulsivae\" (dative) and \"illam\" (the stronger force) — the pronoun reference is left as in the Latin.","- winde-0001: \"Driestischen\" given as \"Drieset\" is a doubtful rendering of a proper name; better kept \"Driest's\" if a printer's name; rendered as given for fidelity to a surname-derived adjective.","- winde-0003: Wasserwaage = \"level\" (not \"water level\"); Luftkreis and Atmosphäre (0004) both appear, rendered \"atmosphere\" in both; Schwere = \"gravity\"; Federkraft = \"spring-force\"; ausspannende Kraft = \"expanding force\"; Ausdehnungskraft = \"force of expansion\"; Dichtigkeit = \"density\"; Elasticität = \"elasticity\"; \"Luftgegend\" = \"region of air\".","- winde-0003: \"Wasserdämpfe\" = \"water vapors\", \"Dämpfe\" = \"vapors\"; \"der andern\" and \"der andere Fall\" ambiguities kept as in German; \"Qvelle\" = \"source\"."]],"winde-0008":["The increased heat compels the air to take up more space. It spreads out to the sides and just as strongly upward. In this moment the weight of this region of air is altered, because, in that the air rising upward flows over, the column of air henceforth contains less air. The neighboring cooler, hence denser and heavier air, displaces it from its place on account of the excess of weight. It is, just like the previous air, rarefied and made lighter, and yields therefore to the pressure of the next, and so on henceforth. One should not think that this heated air, since it is just as much striving to spread out sideways, will make a wind from the region of the heating into the cooler region of air. For first, because the spreading out occurs equally strongly toward all sides, hence the force of expansion, which is inversely proportioned to this, as the cube of the distance from the center decreases reciprocally, the spreading force of a place of air that contained 4 square miles in itself, if it were increased by the 10th part, at the distance of one mile from this heated place would amount to only the 80th part of this increased force, hence could not even be sensed at all. But the spreading cannot even reach that far. For before the air has yet expanded so far, it will, on account of the diminution of its weight, yield to the pressure of the denser air, and make room for it in its place.",["- Ausspannungskraft = \"force of expansion\" (cf. ausdehnende Kraft, 0013, \"expanding force\"); Gewalt = \"force\" in \"sich ausbreitende Gewalt\" rendered \"spreading force\"; Kraft also \"force\". Gewalt and Kraft both appear as \"force\".","- \"die dieser umgekehrt proportionirt ist, wie der Cubus ... abnimmt\": construction preserved as in the German; the antecedent of \"die\" and of \"dieser\" is unclear.","- \"Luftgegend\" = \"region of air\"; \"Platz\"/\"Ort\" = \"place\"; \"Luftsäule\" = \"column of air\"; \"Ueberwucht\" = \"excess of weight\" (Wucht, weight).","- \"oberwerts\" = \"upward\"; \"Cubus\" = \"cube\"; \"Qvadratmeilen\" = \"square miles\" (German miles in the original).","- \"nicht einmal können verspührt werden\": German word order retained approximately (\"could not even be sensed at all\")."]],"winde-0009":["Confirmation from experience.",["- Erfahrung = experience."]],"winde-0010":["The rule cited is so much confirmed by all experiences that not a single exception can be brought against it. All islands that lie in the sea, all coasts of countries in regions where the heat of the sun works strongly, feel a continuing sea wind, as soon as the sun has risen so far above the horizon that it works noticeably on the earth. For since this takes on more heating than the sea, the land air is more rarefied than the sea air, and yields therefore on account of its lightness to the weight of the latter. In the extensive Ethiopian Ocean the wind, very far from the mainland, is the natural general east wind, but nearer to the coasts of Guinea it gets a turning from this its course, and is compelled to blow over Guinea, which, heated by the sun more than the ocean, causes a draft of air over its warmed ground. One need only look at the map that Jurin has appended to Varenius's General Geography, or Musschenbroek's Physics, and one will in a moment, when one has the natural general east wind and this rule at once before one's eyes, be able to see through and explain fully all the directions of the wind blowing in the sea near Guinea, the tornados and everything else. For that reason the north winds govern in the north in wintertime, when the sun rarefies the air in the southern hemisphere. Hence also the winds at the beginning of spring begin to blow from the equator toward the northern hemisphere, because the increased warmth of the sun in the latter rarefies the air, and causes a retreat from the equator toward the northern temperate zone. This wind does not extend far into this temperate region, because the warmth of the sun at that time cannot yet perform much effect at a greater distance from the equator. At this time, in April and the month of May, the winds blow from the interior of Ethiopia over Egypt, which are called the Campsin, and since they come from a heated ground, carry with them a burning hot air; for the rarefied air in the temperate zone compels the equatorial air to step back and to spread out for a time over this region.",["- \"wirkt/wirket/Wirkung\": \"works\"/\"effect\"; Wirkung = effect (glossary). \"Erfahrungen\" = experiences.","- \"Seewind\"/\"Landluft\"/\"Seeluft\": \"sea wind,\" \"land air,\" \"sea air\"; \"Weltmeer\" = \"ocean\"; \"Ocean\" also \"Ocean\".","- \"diese\" in \"da diese mehr Erhitzung ... annimmt\" is left as \"this\" (referent ambiguous in the German as in English; context suggests earth/land).","- \"Musschenbröck seiner Physick\": dative-possessive idiom rendered \"Musschenbroek's Physics.\"","- \"Zug\" rendered \"course\" (of the wind) and \"draft\" (of air); \"Richtung\" = \"direction\"; \"Tornaden\" = \"tornados.\""]],"winde-0011":["Second remark.",["- Anmerkung = remark."]],"winde-0012":["A region of air that cools more than another brings about in the neighboring one a wind that blows into the place of cooling.",["- Verkühlung = \"cooling\"; Luftgegend = \"region of air\"."]],"winde-0013":["The cause is easily comprehensible from the diminution of the expanding force, through the decrease of warmth.",["- begreiflich = \"comprehensible\" (cf. begreifen = comprehend); Ursache = cause; Kraft = force."]],"winde-0014":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0015":["In all seas, near the coasts of the mainland, or of the islands, that are exposed to a strong action of the sun, a continuing land wind blows at night. For at that time the sea air loses its warmth more quickly than the land air, because the heated ground in the latter retains the warmth without particular diminution, whereas the sea, which has taken in little heat over the day, lets the air located above it cool more quickly. Hence the latter yields to the force of expansion of the former, and permits a draft of air from the land into the cooled region of the sea. The south winds, which, as Mariotte remarks, blow in France at the beginning of November, are to be ascribed to the cooling of the air in the deep north, since the winter then begins with all its severity.",["- Ausspannungskraft = \"force of expansion\" (as in winde-0008). \"Sonnenwirkung\" = \"action of the sun.\"","- \"der letztern/der erstern\": \"the latter/the former\" — the referents are as in the German; \"diese\" (the sea air) is \"the latter\" in \"weichet diese der ... erstern\" only by the German's reading; the reference may be disputed."]],"winde-0016":["Third remark.",[]],"winde-0017":["A wind that blows from the equator toward the pole becomes ever more westerly the longer it goes, and the one that moves from the pole to the equator changes its direction into a collateral motion from the east.",["- \"je länger desto mehr\" = \"the longer ... the more\", rendered \"ever more ... the longer\" loosely; \"Collateralbewegung\" = \"collateral motion.\"","- \"westlich\" = \"westerly\"; \"aus Osten\" = \"from the east.\""]],"winde-0018":["This rule, which, as far as is known to me, has never yet been remarked, can be regarded as a key to the general theory of the winds. The proof of it is very comprehensible and convincing. The earth turns from evening toward morning about the axis. Every place on its surface therefore has the more swiftness the nearer it is to the equator, and the less the farther it is removed from it. The air that goes toward the equator therefore always meets on its way places that have more motion from evening toward morning than it itself. It will therefore offer these a resistance in the opposite direction, namely from east to west, and the wind will therefore deviate in this collateral direction. For it is all one whether the ground under a fluid being that is not moved with equal swiftness in the same direction moves on, or whether this is moved over the ground in the opposite direction. If, on the other hand, the wind blows from the equator toward the pole, then it always comes over places of the earth that have less motion from evening toward morning than the air that it carries with it; for this has such a one as is equal to the swiftness of the place from which it has spread out. It will therefore move away over the places over which it comes from evening toward morning, and its motion toward the pole will be combined with the collateral motion from evening.",["- Beweis = proof; überzeugend = convincing (Überzeugung = conviction); begreiflich = \"comprehensible.\"","- \"Abend/Morgen\" kept literally as \"evening/morning\" (cf. Osten/Westen = east/west); \"Schnelligkeit\" = \"swiftness\"; \"Bewegung\" = \"motion.\"","- \"flüßigen Wesen\" = \"fluid being\" (Wesen = being, glossary).","- \"Richtung\" and \"Direction\" both = \"direction.\"","- \"dieses\" in \"ob dieses über den Boden ... bewegt wird\" is ambiguous between the fluid being and what is moved; left as \"this.\"","- \"eine solche\" (= such a motion) rendered \"such a one\"; \"die ... Schnelligkeit des Orts gleich ist\" preserved."]],"winde-0019":["In order to represent this distinctly to oneself one must first have before one's eyes that, when the atmosphere is in equilibrium, every part of it has the same speed of rotation from evening toward morning as the place on the surface of the earth over which it is located, and is at rest in regard to it. But if a part of the air-circle alters its place in the direction of the meridian, it meets places of the earth's surface that move from evening toward morning with more or less swiftness than it still has in itself from that place from which it was moved away. It will therefore move over the regions over which it passes either with a deviation from evening toward morning, or, in the direction from morning toward evening, resist the surface of the earth, which in both cases makes a wind that has this collateral direction. The strength of this lateral motion rests as much on the swiftness of the place over which it is moved as also on the difference in the swiftness of the places from and to which it passes over. Now, however, the swiftness of the axial rotation of every point on the surface of the earth is proportioned to the cosine of the latitude, and the difference of this cosine of two places of the surface standing very near, e.g. one degree, from one another, to the sine of the latitude; therefore the moment of the velocity with which it is displaced sideways in the passage from one degree of latitude into the other will stand in the compound ratio of the sine and cosine of the latitudes, hence be greatest at the 45th degree, but at equal distance from it be equal.",[]],"winde-0020":["So that one can form a concept of the degree of this collateral motion, let us take a north wind that blows from the latitude of 23½ degrees toward the equator. This has, when it has begun from the named degree, a motion that is equal to that of its place from evening toward morning. When it has come 5 degrees nearer to the equinoctial circle, it meets a strip of earth that moves more swiftly in the named direction. Now one finds by an easy calculation that the difference in the swiftness of these two parallel circles amounts to 45 feet in a second; therefore the air, when it has arrived from the 23rd degree at the 18th, would cause the earth in this region a contrary wind from morning toward evening, which would be capable of covering 45 feet in a second, if in the whole way of these 5 degrees something of its motion had not already always been communicated to the air passing over it through the rotation of the earth, so that this difference at the 5th degree of advance can by far not amount to so much. But because a difference must nevertheless always remain over, we want to assume it only the 5th part of that which would take place without this ground, so the collateral motion will nevertheless amount to 9 feet in a second, which is enough to make, out of a straight north wind that sweeps through 18 feet in a second and begins from the 23rd degree, a northeast wind at the 18th. Just so a south wind that has passed over from the 18th degree into the 23rd with just this swiftness will be changed in the latter degree into a southwest wind, because it passes into the more slowly moved parallel circle with an excess of the momentum from evening toward morning just as great as was previously calculated.",[]],"winde-0021":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0022":["This will be appended to the following remarks.",[]],"winde-0023":["Fourth Remark.",[]],"winde-0024":["The general east wind, which rules the whole ocean between the tropics, is to be ascribed to no other cause than that which is evident from the first remark combined with the third.",[]],"winde-0025":["That opinion which attributes the general east wind to the lagging behind of the air-circle in the rotation of the earth from evening toward morning has with good ground been rejected by the students of nature; because the air-circle, even if at first, in the first revolution, it remained somewhat behind, must nevertheless in a short time have been carried along with equal swiftness. But I have applied this thought in a more advantageous and more correct way, in that I prove that it holds when the air passes from the more distant parallel circles to the equator; for then it certainly does not have equal speed with the motion of this greatest circle, and without doubt must lag somewhat behind. The east wind arising from this will be unceasing if new air unceasingly draws toward the equator from the sides, for the previous air would indeed soon lose this counter-striving motion through the continued effect of the earth's surface.",[]],"winde-0026":["Since the first cause has been abolished with general agreement, it has been agreed to ascribe the general east wind between the tropics to the following-after of the air behind that which has been rarefied by the sun from morning toward evening. One would certainly not have been satisfied with this explanation, if one had had a better one. If the air, from the cause of the first remark, draws toward the place heated by the effect of the sun, then that which lies distant from the sun toward evening must do so just as well as that which is located toward morning; I therefore do not see why nothing but east wind should be around the whole globe. But if it moves into its place only on account of the cooling of an air warmed some time before, then it must for that reason move sooner from evening toward morning, because the places that lie toward morning from the sun have cooled more, and therefore have less elasticity, than those which the sun has left longer. But can one, even if I were willing to grant that everything went on as one demands, represent to oneself in any reasonable way how it is possible that the draft of the air, which when the sun is in the evening horizon goes after it, could cause a following-after up to 180 degrees from it, i.e. 2700 miles morningward? and must not so slight a motion vanish entirely at such astonishing distances? And yet the wind moves in all parts of the tropic, and at all times of day, equally strongly from morning toward evening. Mr. Jurin, who supports just the same opinion, has indeed good ground, if he cannot prove it, why not far from the tropics, where surely the effect of the sun is also not inconsiderable, just the same east wind is not detected. For in fact, it cannot be explained at all from the cause adduced.",[]],"winde-0027":["Behold, then, here another, which agrees better with the best known grounds of natural science. The heat, which is stronger in the torrid zone and next to it than elsewhere, keeps the air that is located over it in constant rarefaction. The somewhat less hot and therefore also heavier strips of air, which stand farther from the equator, press into its place according to the laws of equilibrium, and because they move toward the equator, their northern direction must, according to the third remark, turn into a collateral motion from the east. Hence the general east wind at the sides of the equator will properly be a collateral wind, which, however, under the line itself, where the southeast and northeast winds from both hemispheres strive against one another, must turn into a straight east wind, but the farther from the line the more it deviates toward the polar direction.",[]],"winde-0028":["Confirmation from experience.",["- Terms: *Atmosphäre* / *Luftkreis* both rendered distinctly (\"atmosphere\"; \"air-circle\"); *Geschwindigkeit* = \"speed/velocity\" and *Schnelligkeit* = \"swiftness\"; *Bewegung* = \"motion\"; *Meynung* = \"opinion\"; *Erklärung* = \"explanation\" (glossary).","- 0019: \"Er\" refers to \"ein Theil des Luftkreises\"; \"an sich\" in \"noch an sich hat\" rendered \"in itself\" literally.","- 0019: \"Moment der Geschwindigkeit\" kept as \"moment of the velocity\"; \"Verhältniß\" = \"ratio\".","- 0020: \"diesen Grund\" (ground) left unspecified as in German; \"austragen\" rendered \"amount to\"; \"Aeqvinoktialzirkel\" = \"equinoctial circle\".","- 0025: \"Naturkundigen\" = \"students of nature\"; \"Der hieraus entstehende Ostwind\" ambiguous in no way noted; \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\".","- 0026: \"Nachzug\" = \"following-after\"/\"draft\" for *Zug*; \"Herr Jurin\" syntax (\"hat ... guten Grund, wenn er es nicht beweisen kann, warum...\") kept elliptical as in the German.","- 0027: \"Gründen der Naturwissenschaft\" = \"grounds of natural science\"; \"Hemisphärien\" = \"hemispheres\"; \"Linie\" = \"the line\" (equator).","- 0021, 0028: *Erfahrung* = \"experience\"; 0022 \"Anmerkungen\" = \"remarks\"."]],"winde-0029":["According to all unanimous observations the height of the barometer is one inch lower near the equator than in the temperate zones. Does it not now follow from this by itself: that the air of these latter regions of the earth must press toward the equator according to the laws of equilibrium, and does this motion not make in our hemisphere a perpetual north wind in the hot zone? But why does it veer more and more, and finally under the line entirely, into an east wind? The answer is found at the end of the 4th remark. But why is the equilibrium here never entirely restored again? Why does the air in the burning girdle of the earth always remain lighter by one inch of height of quicksilver than that in the temperate zone? The heat always effective here keeps all air in a steady expansion and rarefaction. If, therefore, new air also presses into this region in order to restore the equilibrium, then this is spread out just as well as the previous air. The raised column of air rises above the level of the rest and flows off aloft toward this side. Therefore the equatorial air, because it can never rise higher than that in the temperate zones, and yet contains a thinner air in itself, must always be lighter than this, and yield to its pressure.",[]],"winde-0030":["Explanation of the west winds, which for the most part dominate the ocean in the interval between the 28th and 40th degree.",[]],"winde-0031":["The correctness of the observation itself is sufficiently confirmed by the experience of seafarers in the Pacific as well as the Atlantic as well as the Japanese seas. For the cause one needs no other principle than that from the previous remark. Properly, on the ground cited there, a moderate northeast wind should blow here. But because the air that accumulates from both hemispheres toward the equator flows over incessantly there, and spreads out toward the north in the upper region of our hemisphere, and, since it comes from the equator, has acquired almost entirely the motion of the latter, it must advance with a collateral motion from evening toward morning over the lower air in the more distant parallel circles, (see the third remark,) but it will exert its effect on the low air only where the opposed motion of the latter becomes weaker, and where it itself descends into the lower region. But this must happen at a rather considerable distance from the equator, and there west and collateral winds will prevail.",[]],"winde-0032":["Fifth Remark.",[]],"winde-0033":["The monsoons or periodic winds, which dominate the Arabian, Persian and Indian oceans, are explained quite naturally from the law proved in the third remark.",[]],"winde-0034":["In these seas southwest winds blow from April until September, calms follow for a time, and from October until March the opposed northeast winds blow again. Prepared by the foregoing, one sees the cause of this in a moment. The sun crosses over in the month of March into our northern hemisphere, and heats Arabia, Persia, Hindustan, the adjacent peninsulas, likewise China and Japan, more strongly than the seas located between these countries and the equator. The air that stands over these seas is compelled, by such a rarefaction of the northern air, to spread out toward this side, and we know that a wind that goes from the equator toward the north pole must veer into a southwestern direction. Conversely, as soon as the sun has passed the autumnal equinox, and rarefied the air of the southern hemisphere, then that from the northern part of the hot zone steps down to the equator. Now a wind hastening from the northern regions to the line necessarily veers, if it is left to itself, into a northeast wind; thus it is easy to see why this must relieve the previous southwest wind.",[]],"winde-0035":["One also sees easily the interconnection of these causes, insofar as they concur in the production of the periodic winds. There must be near the tropic a far-stretched solid land, which through the effect of the sun takes on more heat than the seas that are comprised between it and the equator, so the air of these seas will soon be compelled to sweep over these lands, and to make a western collateral wind soon to spread out again from these lands over the seas.",[]],"winde-0036":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0037":["In the whole ocean between Madagascar and New Holland the constant southeast wind blows, natural to the seas that lie near the tropic of Capricorn. But in the region of New Holland, in a far-stretched sea next to this land, the periodic winds are to be encountered, which blow from the southeast from April until October, and the remaining months from the northwest. For through these latter months it is summer in the Austral lands, of which we know only the coasts of New Holland. The sun heats the earth here far more strongly than the neighboring seas, and compels the air to sweep from the regions of the equator toward the south pole, which, according to what was said in the third remark, must cause a northwest wind. In the months from April until October the sun rises over the northern hemisphere and then the southern air steps back again to the equator, in order to stream into the region of rarefaction, and makes the opposed southeast wind. It is not to be wondered at that most natural scientists can give no ground for the periodic change of the winds in the said part of the southern ocean, because the law was not known to them that we have set out in the third remark. This insight can become uncommonly useful, if one wants to apply it to the discovery of new lands. If a seafarer in the southern hemisphere not far from the tropic, at the time when the sun has passed it, perceives a persistent northwest wind, this can be to him an almost infallible mark that toward the south there must be a far-stretched solid land, over which the heat of the sun compels the equatorial air to sweep and makes a north wind combined with a western deviation. The region of New Holland gives, according to present perceptions, still the greatest presumption of a widely extended Austral land located there. Those who sail the Pacific cannot possibly search through all regions of the southern hemisphere in order to spy out new lands there. They must have a guide that lets them judge on which side they will probably encounter them. The northwest winds could give them this guide, which they might encounter there in great stretches of sea in summertime, for these are marks of a nearby southern land.",[]],"winde-0038":["Conclusion.",["- winde-0029: *Gleichgewicht* = \"equilibrium\"; *Wasserwage* = \"level\"; *Anmerkung* = \"remark\"; *Aeqvator* = \"equator\"; *Gesetze* = \"laws\".","- winde-0031: \"stillen als atlantischen als auch japonischen Meere\" is rendered literally, with the repeated \"as … as\"; *stillen* here is the Pacific (*stilles Meer*). *Wirkung* = \"effect\".","- winde-0034: *Zusammenhang* (winde-0035) = \"interconnection\"; *einsehen* = \"see (into)\"; *Merkmal* (winde-0037) = \"mark\".","- winde-0035: The final clause is syntactically obscure in the German (\"einen westlichen Collateralwind machen bald … ausbreiten\"), and the English keeps it so; \"Länder\"/\"Land\" both = \"land(s)\" and \"Lande\".","- winde-0037: *Wahrnehmungen* = \"perceptions\"; *Vermuthung* = \"presumption\" (not in glossary); *Gesetze* here is a singular form (\"das Gesetze\") = \"law\"; *Beschluß* (winde-0038) = \"Conclusion\"."]],"winde-0039":["It is a source of no small pleasure when, prepared by the above remarks, one looks at the chart on which the constant or periodic winds of all the seas are to be found; for one is in a position, with the addition of the rule that the coasts of the lands make the direction of the winds near them parallel to themselves, to give the ground of all winds. The intervals between times of the periodic winds, which for a time sweep through a region and afterwards are relieved by opposed ones, the interval of this alternation, I say, is disturbed with calms, rain, thunderstorms and sudden hurricanes. For then the opposed wind already prevails in the upper air when the previous one has not yet entirely abated in the lower, and while both drive against one another, they finally hold each other in equilibrium, thicken the vapors that they carry with them, and bring about all the said changes. One can also accept it almost as a general rule that thunderstorms are driven together by winds striving against one another. For one commonly remarks that after the thunderstorm the wind changes. Now this opposed wind was already actually to be found in the upper air before the thunderstorm, it was also the one that drove the storm matter together and led the storm cloud over the horizon; for one usually finds that thunderstorms rise against the lower wind, the thunderstorm arose when the winds held each other in equilibrium, and after it the opposed one retains the upper hand. The continuing rains that are often perceived at a high barometer, as e.g. in the previous summer, are to be ascribed with much probability to such currents of air striving against one another in two regions. One can completely explain Mariotte's observation, that the winds that begin to blow from the north at the new light run through the whole compass in about 14 days, so that they go around first into northeast, then into east, thereupon into southeast and so on, likewise that the winds never complete the whole circle in the opposite direction, through the rule of the third remark. For the north wind turns out in a natural way into a northeast wind; this, when the equilibrium with the region toward which it draws is restored, becomes wholly easterly because of the resistance of the same region of air. Then, because the air compressed in the south expands again toward the north, this in combination with the east wind makes a southeasterly deviation, this becomes, through the cause cited in the third remark, first southerly, then southwesterly, thereupon, because of the resistance of the northerly air restored to equilibrium, westerly, thereupon, from combination with the northerly air expanding again, northwesterly, finally wholly northerly.",[]],"winde-0040":["The space that I have determined for this brief consideration sets limitations to its further execution. I conclude it by disclosing to those gentlemen who do me the honor of placing some trust in my modest lecturing that I am inclined to explain natural science (*Naturwissenschaft*) over Herr Dr. Eberhard's first grounds of natural doctrine. My intention is to pass over nothing that can promote a thorough insight into the important discoveries of ancient and modern times, and especially to prove in distinct and complete examples the infinite preeminence that the latter have obtained over the former through the fortunate application of geometry. I continue to give instruction in mathematics, and to open the doctrinal concept of philosophy with the elucidation of Meier's doctrine of reason. I will lecture on metaphysics over the handbook of Herr Prof. Baumgarten. The difficulties of obscurity that seem to surround this most useful and most thorough among all handbooks of its kind will, unless I flatter myself too much, be lifted by the care of the lecture and detailed written elucidations. It seems to me more than all too certain that not ease but usefulness must determine the worth of a thing, and that, as an ingenious writer expresses himself, the stubble is found without effort floating on top, but whoever wants to seek pearls must descend into the depths.",["- winde-0039: \"Qvelle\" = source; \"Carte\" = chart; \"Anmerkungen\" = remarks; \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Wahrscheinlichkeit\" = probability; \"Gleichgewicht\" = equilibrium.","- winde-0039: \"Regel der dritten Anmerkung\" and \"Ursache\" are rendered literally (\"rule of the third remark\"; \"cause\"); \"Ungewitter\" and \"Gewitter\" are both \"thunderstorm\" (the German has two words).","- winde-0039: \"Zwischenzeit\" is \"interval\"; the sentence beginning \"Die zwischen Zeiten…\" is anacoluthic in the German (subject repeated with \"sage ich\") and is kept so.","- winde-0039: \"Wettermaterie\" = \"storm matter\" (Materie = matter); \"ostlich/nordlich/südlich\" rendered \"easterly/northerly/southerly.\"","- winde-0040: \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"bestimmen\" = determine; \"Absicht\" = intention; \"deutlich\" = distinct.","- winde-0040: \"Erläuterung\" = elucidation (twice); \"Vernunftlehre\" = doctrine of reason; \"Lehrbegriff der Weltweisheit\" = \"doctrinal concept of philosophy\" (Weltweisheit = philosophy).","- winde-0040: \"Naturwissenschaft\" (given in italics for the German word) and \"Naturlehre\" = natural doctrine; \"Grund\" = ground (\"erste Gründe\").","- winde-0040: \"Mich dünkt es sey mehr als allzu gewiß\" kept literally (\"more than all too certain\"); \"gringen\" (= geringen) = modest."]]};
